window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("python-for-ai", {"topics":[{"key":"python-for-ai/type-hints","sources":["van Rossum, Lehtosalo, Langa, 2014 — PEP 484: Type Hints","Official docs: typing module","Official docs: mypy"],"why":"- **Python is dynamically typed.** A name can hold anything, and a function discovers what it got only\n  when it uses it. Fine for a fifty-line script; it falls apart in agent loops and AI pipelines.\n- **AI glue code fails late.** Dicts of dicts flow from a provider SDK through five helpers into a prompt\n  template. The first symptom is `KeyError: 'content'` inside a retry loop, or a `TypeError` about\n  concatenating `str` and `NoneType` two hours into an eval run.\n- **Docstrings do not hold.** They drift, nobody checks them, and a refactor that renames a field\n  silently breaks every caller, including the hand-written tool schema your prompt sends the model.\n- **Runtime assertions everywhere are not enough.** They catch the error only on the path that executes,\n  and clutter every function with checks that belong to the contract, not the logic.\n- **One machine-readable contract serves three readers.** A checker walks the call graph without running\n  anything and flags every `Message | None` flowing into a `Message` parameter. Pydantic builds\n  validators, FastAPI builds parsers and OpenAPI schemas, and typed OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs tell your\n  editor `response.choices[0].message.content` can be `None`.","how":"### What the interpreter does with an annotation\n- **It stores metadata, nothing more.** `def f(x: int) -> str` records `int` and `str` in\n  `f.__annotations__`, a dict `{\"x\": int, \"return\": str}`.\n- **Class and module scope too.** `name: list[str] = []` stores the type in that scope's\n  `__annotations__`.\n- **No checking.** `f(\"hello\")` runs normally, because CPython performs no checking. This is deliberate:\n  near-zero runtime cost and full backward compatibility, with enforcement left to tools.\n- **Agent consequence.** A model that sends `{\"limit\": \"5\"}` to a tool typed `limit: int` is not stopped\n  by Python; only a validator built from the hint (Pydantic, or your framework) rejects or coerces it.\n\n### When the annotation is evaluated\n| Python version | Behaviour | Forward reference to a later class |\n|---|---|---|\n| Up to 3.13 | Eager, at definition time | `NameError` unless quoted (`\"Message\"`) or the module uses `from __future__ import annotations` |\n| Any version, with the future import | Postponed: every annotation stored as a string | Libraries that need the real type resolve strings with `typing.get_type_hints(obj)` |\n| 3.14 (PEP 649 and PEP 749) | Lazy: computed when something first reads `__annotations__` (or calls `annotationlib.get_annotations`) | No failure at `def` time, but can still fail later if the name is undefined when a library inspects it |\n\nCheck which version your project runs when a library (or a tool decorator building a schema) complains\nabout unresolved forward references.\n\n### What a checker does with it\n1. **Builds a type for every expression** from annotations plus inference.\n2. **Applies the gradual-typing rule.** Any unannotated value has type `Any`, and `Any` is compatible with\n   everything in both directions. This exists *because* typing had to be adoptable one function at a\n   time in a huge untyped ecosystem; a checker that refused partially typed code would never have been\n   adopted.\n3. **Pays for it with holes.** Once a value is `Any`, every downstream error is invisible. Most \"mypy\n   said it was fine\" surprises trace back to an `Any` from `json.loads`, an untyped library or a\n   missing annotation. In agents the classic source is the tool-call `arguments` string or `input` dict.\n\n### The vocabulary that matters\n- **`X | None` (older spelling `Optional[X]`)** says the value may be absent; the checker forces you to\n  narrow it with `if x is not None` before use. Provider SDK responses are full of these: text content\n  is absent on tool-call turns.\n\n| Spelling | Meaning | Why you care in agent code |\n|---|---|---|\n| `list[str]`, `dict[str, float]`, `tuple[int, ...]` | Built-in generic containers | Prefer abstract inputs (`Sequence[str]`, `Mapping[str, float]`, `Iterable[str]`) and concrete outputs (`list[str]`) so callers can pass tuples or generators |\n| `Callable[[str, int], bool]` | A function value | Typing callbacks, hooks and tool handlers in a registry |\n| `Awaitable[T]`, `Coroutine` | Async results | Matters when wrapping an async client or async tools |\n| `Any` | Opt out of checking | Every use is a hole |\n| `object` | Anything, but you may only use what every object supports until you narrow it (for example with `isinstance`) | Choosing `object` over `Any` for genuinely unknown inputs keeps the hole small |\n| `Annotated[T, meta]` | Extra data on a type without changing it | Pydantic's `Field` and FastAPI's `Depends` carry runtime constraints; tool frameworks read descriptions from it |\n| `TYPE_CHECKING` | Import a type only for the checker | Breaks import cycles; avoids loading torch or transformers just for an annotation |\n| `cast(T, value)` | \"Trust me\", no runtime effect | Escape hatch; use sparingly |\n| `reveal_type(x)` | Prints what the checker thinks `x` is | Fastest way to debug an inference surprise |\n\n### Narrowing: how the checker learns more inside a branch\n- **A declared type is an upper bound.** After `if msg.content is not None:`, `msg.content` is `str` in\n  that block, *because* the checker treats the test as evidence.\n- **Tests that narrow:** `isinstance(x, ToolCall)`, `if not x: return`, `assert x is not None`, and a\n  `match` case. You pay one explicit check and the rest of the block is precise, which is what makes\n  `X | None` usable rather than a nuisance.\n- **Helpers do not narrow by default.** The checker cannot see inside `def is_tool_turn(m) -> bool`.\n  Annotate it `-> TypeIs[ToolTurn]` (3.13, or `typing_extensions`) or the older\n  `-> TypeGuard[ToolTurn]` so a call narrows like an `isinstance`.\n- **Narrowing ends** when the name is reassigned. Checkers are conservative about carrying it into a\n  nested function or lambda that runs later, which is why you sometimes copy `msg.content` into a local\n  first.\n\n### Where types come from for libraries you did not write\n- **Inline types** with a `py.typed` marker file (PEP 561).\n- **Stub packages** such as `types-requests` or `types-PyYAML`, containing signature-only `.pyi` files.\n- **Neither:** every import from the package is `Any`, silently. mypy reports \"missing library stubs or\n  py.typed marker\"; silencing it with `ignore_missing_imports` hides the error and keeps the hole.\n- **In practice:** the major LLM SDKs ship inline types; many older data and ML packages are partially\n  typed or not at all, so that boundary is where your own typed wrapper goes.\n\n### Why runtime libraries read them\n- **Who reads them:** Pydantic, dataclasses and `attrs` at class creation; FastAPI when a route is\n  registered; libraries such as `instructor` reuse Pydantic's models; agent frameworks read a tool\n  function's signature when it is registered.\n- **What it buys:** a `BaseModel` with `temperature: float = 0.7` becomes a validator that coerces `\"0.7\"`\n  to `0.7` and rejects `\"hot\"`.\n- **The bridge:** the annotation you wrote for the checker becomes the schema that validates a model's\n  JSON output and the JSON Schema you hand to a tool-calling API. Write the type once, get all three\n  behaviours; that is the main practical payoff in agent work.","example":"### A typed tool: one hint, schema plus validation\nThe argument model is the single source for the schema the model sees and the check on what it sends\nback. The shape matches the Anthropic `tools` entry (`name`, `description`, `input_schema`).\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Annotated, Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationError\n\nclass SearchArgs(BaseModel):\n    query: Annotated[str, Field(min_length=1, description=\"What to search for\")]\n    limit: Annotated[int, Field(ge=1, le=20)] = 5\n    source: Literal[\"docs\", \"tickets\"] = \"docs\"\n\nTOOL = {\"name\": \"search\", \"description\": \"Search internal docs or tickets.\",\n        \"input_schema\": SearchArgs.model_json_schema()}\n\ndef search(args: SearchArgs) -> list[str]:\n    return [f\"{args.source}:{args.query}:{i}\" for i in range(args.limit)]\n\ndef run_tool_call(name: str, raw_input: dict[str, object]) -> str:\n    if name != \"search\":\n        return f\"error: unknown tool {name}\"\n    try:\n        args = SearchArgs.model_validate(raw_input)     # raw model JSON becomes a typed object\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        return f\"error: invalid arguments: {e.errors()[0]['msg']}\"   # fed back so the model retries\n    return \"\\n\".join(search(args))\n\nprint(run_tool_call(\"search\", {\"query\": \"kv cache\", \"limit\": \"3\"}))   # \"3\" coerced to 3\nprint(run_tool_call(\"search\", {\"query\": \"kv cache\", \"limit\": 50}))    # error: ... less than or equal to 20\n```\n\n### A typed wrapper around a chat completion\nThe checker flags any caller that forgets `content` can be `None`, and `Annotated` carries a constraint\nPydantic enforces at runtime.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom collections.abc import Sequence\nfrom typing import Annotated, Any, Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nRole = Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]\n\nclass Message(BaseModel):\n    role: Role\n    content: str\n\nclass GenConfig(BaseModel):\n    model: str\n    temperature: Annotated[float, Field(ge=0.0, le=2.0)] = 0.7\n    max_tokens: Annotated[int, Field(gt=0)] = 512\n\ndef to_wire(messages: Sequence[Message]) -> list[dict[str, str]]:\n    # Sequence accepts list or tuple; the return is a concrete list for the SDK.\n    return [m.model_dump() for m in messages]\n\ndef first_text(response: Any) -> str | None:\n    # SDK responses are typed in the real SDK; Any here stands in for \"unknown JSON\".\n    choice = response[\"choices\"][0]\n    return choice[\"message\"].get(\"content\")   # may be None for tool-call turns\n\ndef reply(messages: Sequence[Message], cfg: GenConfig, response: Any) -> str:\n    text = first_text(response)\n    if text is None:                 # the checker forces this narrowing\n        raise ValueError(\"model returned no text content\")\n    return text.strip()\n\nraw = {\"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\", \"temperature\": \"0.2\"}       # e.g. parsed from a YAML config\ncfg = GenConfig.model_validate(raw)                        # \"0.2\" coerced to 0.2 by Pydantic\nprint(cfg.temperature, type(cfg.temperature))            # 0.2 <class 'float'>\n# GenConfig(model=\"x\", temperature=3.0) raises a ValidationError: le=2.0\n```\n\n### Worked experiment: the `Any` hole in one file\nRun `mypy` on the file above after each edit.\n\n| Edit | mypy result | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Delete the `if text is None` branch | Error: the optional string has no attribute `strip` | The value proposition, delivered before the first API call |\n| Keep it deleted and also delete `first_text`'s return annotation | Silent | A missing return annotation defaults to `Any` (a fully unannotated function is not checked at all); `Any.strip()` is accepted |\n| Call `GenConfig(model=\"x\", temperature=\"0.2\")` directly | Error: `str` where `float` expected (the call still runs, because Pydantic coerces) | A checker that understands Pydantic's `__init__` through `dataclass_transform` flags the `str` argument |\n| Same call with Pydantic's mypy plugin enabled | Silent by default | The plugin deliberately types `__init__` parameters as `Any` unless you set `init_typed = True` |\n\n- **The first error is the whole point:** `str | None` has no attribute `strip`, reported before any\n  tokens are spent. On a tool-call turn that `None` is the normal case, not an edge case.\n- **Lesson 1:** the annotation on the function that touches raw JSON matters more than any other.\n- **Lesson 2:** untyped input goes through `model_validate` at the boundary, not through `__init__`.\n\n### Narrowing helper with `TypeIs`\nWithout `TypeIs` the checker cannot see inside the helper, so `turn.tool_name` would still be an error.\nThis is the shape of the dispatch step in an agent loop.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing_extensions import TypeIs   # typing.TypeIs on 3.13+\n\n@dataclass\nclass TextTurn:\n    text: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass ToolTurn:\n    tool_name: str\n    args: dict[str, object]\n\ndef is_tool_turn(t: TextTurn | ToolTurn) -> TypeIs[ToolTurn]:\n    return isinstance(t, ToolTurn)\n\ndef describe(turn: TextTurn | ToolTurn) -> str:\n    if is_tool_turn(turn):\n        return f\"call {turn.tool_name}\"    # narrowed to ToolTurn\n    return turn.text                       # narrowed to TextTurn in the else branch\n\nprint(describe(ToolTurn(\"search\", {\"q\": \"kv cache\"})))   # call search\n```","patterns":["**Generate tool schemas from types, never by hand.** Define each tool's arguments as a Pydantic model (or let a framework decorator read the signature) and send `model_json_schema()` as the tool schema. Hand-written JSON drifts from the handler. Monitor the tool-argument validation-error rate per tool; a spike after a prompt or schema change means the model and contract disagree.","**Strict at the edges, gradual inside.** Set `strict = true` under `[tool.mypy]` (it is a global-only option, so it cannot be scoped to one package), then add `[[tool.mypy.overrides]]` with a `module` pattern for legacy code that relaxes it, for example `ignore_errors = true` or `disallow_untyped_defs = false`. SDK wrappers and tool handlers stay strict. Track the count of overridden modules and shrink it each sprint.","**Validate at the boundary.** Parse every `json.loads` result, raw SDK dict or tool-call input with `Model.model_validate(...)` (or `TypeAdapter(list[Message]).validate_python(...)`) in the first function that receives it. Monitor validation-error rate per provider; a spike means an API changed shape.","**Hunt `Any` leaks in CI.** Enable `warn_return_any` and `disallow_any_generics`, and run `mypy --strict` on new packages. Watch the error count; a jump after adding a dependency usually means missing stubs, so install the `types-*` package rather than `ignore_missing_imports`.","**Enable the Pydantic mypy plugin deliberately.** In `pyproject.toml` add `plugins = [\"pydantic.mypy\"]` under `[tool.mypy]` and `init_typed = true` under `[tool.pydantic-mypy]` if you want constructor arguments checked. Without that setting the plugin treats `__init__` parameters as `Any`, so `GenConfig(temperature=\"0.2\")` passes the checker.","**Keep heavy imports behind `TYPE_CHECKING`.** Wrap torch or transformers imports used only in annotations in `if TYPE_CHECKING:`, so an agent worker or CLI starts fast. Measure import time (`python -X importtime`) before and after."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent loop is glue between untyped JSON (model tool calls, tool\n  results, SDK responses) and your code. Hints catch `None` content and wrong field names before a\n  thirty-minute eval run, and they are the source the tool schema is generated from.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangChain's `@tool` decorator and the OpenAI Agents SDK's\n  `@function_tool` read a function's annotations and docstring to build the tool schema; LangGraph state\n  is usually a `TypedDict` with `Annotated` reducers; Pydantic AI and `instructor` use them for outputs.\n- **What you control.** Annotate every tool handler and the function that receives raw model JSON,\n  choose `X | None` honestly where the API can omit a field, and run a checker in CI.\n- **What you can skip.** Variance theory and the full PEP history. Know `Any` versus `object`,\n  narrowing, `Annotated` and when annotations are evaluated; that covers agent code.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool schemas from signatures.** Agent frameworks read a handler's annotations and docstring to build\n  the schema the model sees, so the hint is the contract the model must satisfy.\n- **Typing the raw-JSON boundary.** The function that receives provider responses declares `X | None`\n  where content can be missing, so the checker forces the tool-call-only branch to be handled.\n- **Refactoring an agent core safely.** Renaming a field in a message or step type surfaces every stale\n  caller in CI instead of in a long eval run.\n- **Typed graph state.** LangGraph-style state declared with `TypedDict` and `Annotated` reducers lets\n  the checker flag nodes that return keys the graph does not have.","mini_project":"**Type-check a small tool-calling loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** take an untyped agent loop with two tools and make the checker catch the bugs before a run.\n- **Steps:** write the loop with plain dicts and plant two bugs (a misspelled message key and an\n  unhandled `None` content); annotate the tool handlers, the message type and the response parser; run\n  mypy or pyright in strict mode and fix what it reports; generate each tool's schema from its signature.\n- **Done when:** the checker reports both planted bugs before any model call, the fixed loop passes clean,\n  and the generated tool schemas match the annotated parameters."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Tool signature with hints\"] --> I[\"CPython: store only\"]\n    A --> C[\"Static checker: mypy, pyright\"]\n    A --> R[\"Runtime: Pydantic, tool decorator\"]\n    C -->|\"declared type plus inference\"| N[\"Narrowing in branches\"]\n    N --> E[\"Errors before tokens spent\"]\n    C -.->|\"value is Any\"| H[\"Hole: errors invisible\"]\n    R --> S[\"JSON Schema sent to model\"]\n    R --> V[\"Validator on tool arguments\"]\n    V -->|\"error text\"| M[\"Model retries the call\"]","caption":"One annotation, three readers: the interpreter only stores it in `__annotations__`, a checker reads it\nbefore the run, and runtime libraries turn it into the tool's JSON Schema and the validator for the\nmodel's arguments (coerce `\"0.7\"`, reject `\"hot\"`). The dotted `Any` edge is where checking stops."},"interview":[{"q":"What do type hints actually do at runtime in Python, and why were they designed that way?","a":"- **Almost nothing.** CPython stores annotations in `__annotations__` and never checks them, so a\n  function typed `-> int` can return a string without complaint.\n- **Deliberately so:** near-zero runtime cost and full backward compatibility, with enforcement left to\n  static checkers and to libraries such as Pydantic that choose to read the annotations."},{"q":"How do type hints on a tool function become something the model can use, and what still has to happen at runtime?","a":"- **Schema generation.** A framework decorator or a Pydantic argument model reads the annotations (plus\n  `Annotated` descriptions and constraints) and emits the JSON Schema sent with the request.\n- **Runtime validation is separate.** The model can still send `\"5\"` or omit a field, so validate the\n  call's input with the same model before running the handler.\n- **Close the loop:** return the validation message to the model as the tool result so it can retry,\n  and count those errors per tool as a signal of a confusing schema."},{"q":"You call `if is_tool_turn(turn):` and mypy still says `turn` has no attribute `tool_name`. Why, and what is the fix?","a":"- **The checker does not look inside helpers.** A `-> bool` return says nothing about the argument, so\n  `turn` keeps its declared union type inside the branch.\n- **Annotate the helper `-> TypeIs[ToolTurn]`** (3.13, or `typing_extensions`). It narrows like\n  `isinstance`: to `ToolTurn` in the true branch and to the remaining members in the else branch.\n- **`TypeGuard` is the older form:** it narrows only the true branch, and does not require the guarded\n  type to be a subtype of the input, so prefer `TypeIs` for plain type tests."},{"q":"When would you choose `object` over `Any` for an unknown input?","a":"- **`object` keeps checking on.** You may only use what every object supports until you narrow it with\n  `isinstance`, so mistakes are still caught.\n- **`Any` turns checking off** in both directions for that value and everything derived from it.\n- **Use `Any` only** as a temporary escape hatch at an untyped library seam, and wrap it quickly."},{"q":"How would you introduce type checking into a 50,000-line untyped agent service without stopping feature work?","a":"- **Gradual by design:** unannotated code is `Any`, so you can start with zero errors and tighten module\n  by module.\n- **Order:** type the boundaries first (SDK wrappers, tool handlers, serialized models and agent\n  state), set `strict = true` globally, and relax untouched legacy modules with `[[tool.mypy.overrides]]`\n  (`strict` itself cannot be set per module).\n- **Track progress** by the number of overridden modules and the `Any` surface, not by the share of\n  functions with annotations."},{"q":"Why might a forward reference to a class break at import time, and how does Python 3.14 change that?","a":"- **Up to 3.13 annotations are evaluated eagerly** at definition time, so naming a class defined further\n  down raises `NameError` unless quoted or the module uses `from __future__ import annotations`.\n- **3.14 evaluates lazily** (PEP 649 and PEP 749), on first read of `__annotations__`, so the `def` no\n  longer fails, but a library inspecting the annotation can still fail if the name is undefined then."}],"tradeoffs":["**Early detection vs annotation effort.** You catch shape bugs across the call graph before running, but pay effort up front. The payoff flips negative for throwaway notebooks and one-off scripts whose whole life is a single run, where the runtime error *is* the cheapest check.","**Gradual adoption vs leaks.** You can adopt incrementally, but every `Any` leak silently disables checking downstream, so a codebase \"typed\" at 60% often catches far fewer bugs than it looks.","**One source of truth vs library coupling.** Annotations double as Pydantic, FastAPI and tool schemas, saving duplicated validation code. But your static types are now coupled to a library's interpretation (for example, how a `float` field coerces strings), which can surprise you on a major version change.","**Precise SDK types vs verbosity.** They make the editor useful and catch `None` cases, but deeply nested unions make simple code verbose. Wrapping them in your own small models pays off once the same response shape is consumed in more than one place, as it is in any agent loop.","**Rich tool types vs what the model handles.** Nested unions and many constrained fields make an exact schema but a harder one for the model to fill. Flat argument models with a few `Literal` choices tend to produce fewer invalid calls; measure on your eval set."],"pitfalls":["**Over-specific parameters.** Annotating `list[str]` when callers pass tuples or generators gives a wall of checker errors at call sites. Fix: accept `Sequence[str]` or `Iterable[str]`.","**Treating annotations as enforcement.** A function typed `-> int` happily returns a string at runtime because nothing checked it; you find out much later in a `TypeError`. A tool handler called with model-supplied arguments is exactly this case unless a validator sits in front of it.","**Letting `json.loads` output (typed `Any`) flow untouched.** Symptom: a clean mypy run, then `KeyError` in production, because `Any` suppressed every downstream error. Fix: validate at the boundary with Pydantic or a `TypedDict` so the type becomes concrete.","**Forward-reference `NameError` at import time** (Python 3.13 and earlier) when one class annotates a field with a class defined below it. Fix: quote the name or use `from __future__ import annotations`, and know some runtime libraries then need `get_type_hints` to resolve the strings.","**Expecting a `bool` helper to narrow.** After `if is_valid(x):` with `is_valid(x) -> bool`, you get the same \"may be None\" error. Fix: annotate the helper with `TypeIs`/`TypeGuard` or inline the test.","**Reading `Optional[X]` as \"optional argument\".** `Optional` only means \"may be None\"; without a default, the checker still requires the argument be passed. In a tool schema the field is then required too, so the model must send it."],"when":"- **Always annotate boundaries:** public module functions, anything taking or returning provider SDK\n  objects, tool handlers an agent calls, and data structures that get serialized into prompts or traces.\n- **Skip inside tight private helpers:** inference is usually enough.\n- **Run a checker in CI once annotations exist:** hints without a checker are documentation that rots.\n- **Measure the `Any` surface:** mypy's `--strict` flag or `warn_return_any` shows where typing leaks.\n- **Day to day:** type hints let you refactor a prompt-assembly pipeline or tool registry with confidence\n  and make Pydantic models and tool schemas possible at all.\n- **Interviews:** know that annotations are inert at runtime, how gradual typing and `Any` interact, and\n  how Pydantic turns an annotation into a validator and a JSON Schema.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes typed wrappers around SDK calls and tool handlers so mypy catches `None` content and wrong field names before an eval run burns tokens.","architect":"Sets the policy: which boundaries must be fully typed, whether CI blocks on the checker, and that shared message and tool schemas live in one typed package.","fde":"Reads a customer's untyped integration code and adds annotations at the seams first, turning vague \"it sometimes breaks\" reports into concrete checker errors within a day."},"check":{"q":"A colleague says \"we added type hints everywhere and mypy passes, but we still get\n`KeyError` and `AttributeError: 'NoneType'` in production from our LLM pipeline\". What is the most\nlikely cause and how would you fix it?","a":"Passing mypy with those runtime errors almost always means `Any` is leaking. The provider\nresponse or `json.loads` output enters as `Any`, so every attribute access and key lookup\ndownstream is unchecked; the pipeline is \"typed\" syntactically but not semantically. The fix is to\nmake the boundary concrete: parse the raw response into a Pydantic model or a `TypedDict`\nimmediately, with `content: str | None` where the API can omit it, then let the checker force\nnarrowing. Enable `warn_return_any` and `disallow_any_generics` to find remaining holes, and check\nthat third-party stubs are installed so SDK calls are not silently `Any` either."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does CPython do with the annotation in `def f(x: int) -> str` when `f(\"a\")` is called?","options":["Raises TypeError because \"a\" is not an int","Coerces \"a\" to int if possible, otherwise raises","Nothing; the annotation is stored in `f.__annotations__` and never checked","Emits a RuntimeWarning about a type mismatch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Annotations are inert metadata (evaluated at definition time up to 3.13, lazily from 3.14). Enforcement is the job of static checkers or libraries that explicitly read the annotations, never the interpreter itself."},{"q":"Why can a fully \"type-hinted\" codebase still let shape bugs through mypy?","options":["mypy only checks functions that have docstrings","Values typed `Any` (from `json.loads`, untyped libraries or missing stubs) disable checking downstream","mypy ignores `Optional` types by default","Type hints are only checked inside classes, not module-level functions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gradual typing makes `Any` compatible with everything, so once a value is `Any` every operation on it is accepted. Untyped JSON and missing stubs are the usual sources."},{"q":"Which annotation best fits a parameter that will only be iterated over, so callers may pass a list, tuple or generator?","options":["`list[str]`","`Any`","`Iterable[str]`","`dict[str, str]`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`Iterable[str]` is the least demanding contract that still guarantees iteration, so all those callers type-check. `list[str]` rejects tuples and generators; `Any` discards checking entirely."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/protocols-generics-structural-typing","sources":["Levkivskyi, Lehtosalo, Langa, 2017 — PEP 544: Protocols: Structural subtyping (static duck typing)","Traut, 2022 — PEP 695: Type Parameter Syntax","Official docs: typing module"],"why":"- **Python code targets behaviour, not names.** \"Anything with an `embed(texts)` method\" is a complete\n  contract. An OpenAI client, a sentence-transformers model and a test stub can all satisfy it without\n  sharing a base class. The same holds for \"anything with `run(args)`\" as an agent tool.\n- **Nominal typing forces bad choices.** Originally a value matched `Embedder` only if its class\n  inherited from it. You either write adapter classes that exist only to inherit, or type the parameter\n  `Any` and lose checking exactly at the seam with a vendor's code.\n- **Without generics, element types vanish.** `first(xs)` must be typed `list[Any] -> Any`, so the\n  checker cannot tell you `first(messages).content` is wrong when `messages` holds tool-call records.\n  One copy per element type is absurd; you need a placeholder bound per call.\n- **The design goal was Pythonic.** Protocols (PEP 544) and type variables keep runtime behaviour\n  unchanged and make existing stdlib duck-typed idioms (`Iterable`, `Sized`, file-like objects)\n  expressible, rather than forcing Python into a Java-shaped type system.","how":"### Structural versus nominal compatibility\n| Question asked | Nominal | Structural |\n|---|---|---|\n| Compatibility rule | \"Is this class a declared subclass?\" | \"Does it have the required members with compatible types?\" |\n| Example | an `abc.ABC` subclass | `collections.abc.Iterable`: any class with `__iter__`, no registration |\n\nA `Protocol` class lets you define your own structural interfaces:\n\n```python\nfrom collections.abc import Sequence\nfrom typing import Protocol\nclass Embedder(Protocol):\n    dim: int\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]: ...\n```\n\n- **Member by member.** Any class with an `int` attribute `dim` and a compatible `embed` is an\n  `Embedder`. The checker compares attribute types, then each method's parameter and return types.\n- **Parameter names are checker-dependent.** pyright rejects an implementation that renames `texts`,\n  because a caller might pass `texts=` by keyword; mypy deliberately ignores positional parameter names\n  when matching protocol methods. Declare parameters positional-only with `/` and both agree.\n- **Errors show up at use sites.** The match is checked where an object is used as an `Embedder`,\n  *because* the implementation never declared intent. Adding `impl: Embedder = MyEmbedder()` in a test\n  moves the error earlier.\n\n### `@runtime_checkable`\n- Makes `isinstance(obj, Embedder)` work.\n- It checks only that the named attributes exist, not signatures or types, *because* a full structural\n  check at runtime would be slow and ambiguous.\n- Treat it as a cheap plugin-discovery check (for example, finding tool objects in a plugin module), not\n  validation.\n\n### Protocol versus ABC\n| | ABC (`abc.ABC` plus `@abstractmethod`) | Protocol |\n|---|---|---|\n| Compatibility | Nominal | Structural |\n| Enforcement | At instantiation: a subclass missing a method cannot be constructed | Static only |\n| Shared default implementations | Yes | No behaviour |\n| Use when | You own every implementation | Implementations come from outside |\n\n### What a protocol member actually promises\n- **A bare attribute is read-write.** `dim: int` lets a consumer assign `e.dim = 768`, so a class exposing\n  `dim` as a read-only `@property` does not satisfy it.\n- **Settable means exact.** For the same reason as `list` invariance (below), a settable attribute must\n  match its type exactly, not a subtype.\n- **Read-only consumers should say so.** Declare `@property def dim(self) -> int: ...`, which accepts\n  plain attributes, properties and subtypes alike. Ask for the least you need, here as everywhere; a\n  tool's `name` is the typical case.\n\n### Generic protocols and callback protocols\n- **Generic protocol:** `class Store[T](Protocol): def get(self, key: str) -> T | None: ...` (or\n  `Protocol[T]` on older versions). A Redis-backed `Store[Session]` and an in-memory `Store[Session]` stand\n  in for each other while the checker still knows what `get` returns. Agent memory and checkpoint stores\n  fit this shape.\n- **Callback protocol:** when an interface is one call with keyword arguments or overloads that\n  `Callable[...]` cannot express (a streaming callback taking `token: str, *, final: bool`), a protocol\n  defining only `__call__` types it exactly.\n\n### Generics: type variables\n- **A `TypeVar` is a placeholder bound per use.** `def first[T](xs: Sequence[T]) -> T` (PEP 695 syntax,\n  Python 3.12+), or the older `T = TypeVar(\"T\")` plus `def first(xs: Sequence[T]) -> T`, says the return\n  type equals the element type.\n- **Generic classes carry the binding across methods.** With `class Index[T]:` or\n  `class Index(Generic[T]):`, `Index[Document].nearest(...)` returns a `Document`.\n- **Bound:** `T = TypeVar(\"T\", bound=BaseModel)` allows any subclass of `BaseModel`, so the checker\n  allows `.model_dump()` on it. A `parse_output[T: BaseModel](text, schema: type[T]) -> T` helper for\n  structured outputs uses exactly this.\n- **Constrained:** `TypeVar(\"N\", int, float)` means exactly one of those types, which is how `sum`-like\n  helpers avoid accepting strings.\n\n### Variance, or why `list[Dog]` is not a `list[Animal]`\n1. `def rename(xs: list[Animal]) -> None` may legally `xs.append(Cat())`.\n2. If the checker accepted a `list[Dog]` there, the caller's list would now contain a `Cat`.\n3. So the rules are:\n\n| Kind | Variance | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mutable containers | Invariant | `list[Dog]` is compatible only with `list[Dog]` |\n| Read-only views | Covariant | `Sequence[Dog]` is a `Sequence[Animal]`, because nothing can be inserted |\n| Function parameters | Contravariant | A callback accepting `Animal` works where one accepting `Dog` is required |\n\n- **Most common \"it obviously should work\" error.** The fix is almost always to accept `Sequence[T]` or\n  `Iterable[T]` for inputs; in agents it appears as `list[ToolMessage]` rejected for `list[Message]`.\n- **Declaring variance.** With the old syntax you must state it (`TypeVar(\"T_co\", covariant=True)`); PEP\n  695 syntax infers it from how the parameter is used.\n\n### ParamSpec and Self\n- **The problem:** every retry, tracing and caching decorator wraps arbitrary functions. Typed as\n  `Callable[..., Any]`, it erases the wrapped signature and every call through it goes unchecked.\n- **`ParamSpec`** captures \"whatever parameters the wrapped function has\", so\n  `Callable[P, R] -> Callable[P, R]` preserves argument and return types through the wrapper.\n- **`Self`** (3.11) fixes the related problem of fluent builders and `copy`-style methods returning the\n  subclass type rather than the base.","example":"### An agent loop typed against Protocols, driven by a fake LLM\nThe loop depends only on `LLMClient` and `Tool`. A scripted test double and a plain class satisfy them\nwith no base class, so the loop is testable without network or vendor mocks. This passes\n`mypy --strict` on Python 3.10+.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass\nclass Reply:\n    text: str | None = None\n    tool: str | None = None\n    args: dict[str, object] = field(default_factory=dict)\n\nclass LLMClient(Protocol):\n    def complete(self, messages: list[dict[str, str]], /) -> Reply: ...\n\nclass Tool(Protocol):\n    @property\n    def name(self) -> str: ...                # read-only: plain attributes also satisfy it\n    def run(self, args: dict[str, object], /) -> str: ...\n\nclass Clock:                                  # a tool; no base class\n    name = \"clock\"\n    def run(self, args: dict[str, object], /) -> str:\n        return \"2026-10-22T09:00Z\"\n\nclass ScriptedLLM:                            # test double: satisfies LLMClient structurally\n    def __init__(self, replies: list[Reply]) -> None: self.replies = replies\n    def complete(self, messages: list[dict[str, str]], /) -> Reply:\n        return self.replies.pop(0)\n\ndef run_agent(llm: LLMClient, tools: dict[str, Tool], task: str, max_steps: int = 5) -> str:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}]\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        r = llm.complete(messages)\n        if r.tool is None:\n            return r.text or \"\"\n        result = tools[r.tool].run(r.args) if r.tool in tools else f\"unknown tool {r.tool}\"\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"content\": result})\n    return \"stopped: step limit\"\n\nllm = ScriptedLLM([Reply(tool=\"clock\"), Reply(text=\"It is 09:00 UTC.\")])\nprint(run_agent(llm, {\"clock\": Clock()}, \"What time is it?\"))   # It is 09:00 UTC.\n```\n\n### Structural embedder, generic index, typed decorator\nTwo unrelated classes satisfy `Embedder`, the index remembers what it stores (the retrieval half of a\nRAG tool), and the decorator keeps the signature. On Python 3.12+ the `Generic[T]` base can be replaced\nby `class Index[T]:`.\n\n```python\nimport functools, zlib\nfrom collections.abc import Callable, Sequence\nfrom typing import Generic, ParamSpec, Protocol, TypeVar, runtime_checkable\n\n@runtime_checkable\nclass Embedder(Protocol):\n    dim: int\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]: ...\n\nclass HashEmbedder:                       # no inheritance from Embedder anywhere\n    dim = 16\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        return [self._one(t) for t in texts]\n    def _one(self, text: str) -> list[float]:\n        v = [0.0] * self.dim              # bag of words; crc32 is stable across runs, hash() is not\n        for w in text.lower().split(): v[zlib.crc32(w.encode()) % self.dim] += 1.0\n        return v\n\nclass OpenAIEmbedder:                     # wraps a vendor SDK; also structurally an Embedder\n    dim = 1536\n    def __init__(self, client) -> None: self.client = client\n    def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        r = self.client.embeddings.create(model=\"text-embedding-3-small\", input=list(texts))\n        return [d.embedding for d in r.data]\n\nT = TypeVar(\"T\")\nclass Index(Generic[T]):\n    def __init__(self, embedder: Embedder) -> None:\n        self.embedder = embedder\n        self.items: list[T] = []                    # annotated, or the checker sees list[Any]\n        self.vecs: list[list[float]] = []\n    def add(self, item: T, text: str) -> None:\n        self.items.append(item); self.vecs.append(self.embedder.embed([text])[0])\n    def nearest(self, text: str) -> T:              # the checker knows this is a T\n        q = self.embedder.embed([text])[0]\n        dot = lambda v: sum(a * b for a, b in zip(q, v))\n        return self.items[max(range(len(self.vecs)), key=lambda i: dot(self.vecs[i]))]\n\nP = ParamSpec(\"P\"); R = TypeVar(\"R\")\ndef logged(fn: Callable[P, R]) -> Callable[P, R]:   # keeps fn's exact signature\n    @functools.wraps(fn)\n    def inner(*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs) -> R:\n        print(\"calling\", fn.__name__); return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n    return inner\n\nidx: Index[int] = Index(HashEmbedder())\nidx.add(42, \"the answer\"); idx.add(7, \"a prime\")\nprint(idx.nearest(\"answer\"))                       # 42 (int, per the checker)\nprint(isinstance(HashEmbedder(), Embedder))        # True: attribute presence only\n```\n\nWhat the checker catches when you break it:\n\n| Edit | Checker report |\n|---|---|\n| `nearest` returns `self.vecs[0]` | `list[float]` is not `T` (only because `self.vecs` is annotated; an unannotated `[]` would leak `Any` and hide the error) |\n| `idx: Index[int] = Index(object())` | `object` is missing `embed` |\n\n### Generic store protocol and a callback protocol (Python 3.12+)\nA memory store swaps for a Redis one without inheritance, and the streaming callback's keyword-only\n`final` is typed exactly (the shape of a token callback that streams an agent's answer to a UI). This\npasses `mypy --strict`.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\nclass Store[T](Protocol):\n    def get(self, key: str) -> T | None: ...\n    def put(self, key: str, value: T) -> None: ...\n\nclass OnToken(Protocol):                     # callback protocol: Callable[...] cannot say \"*, final\"\n    def __call__(self, token: str, *, final: bool) -> None: ...\n\nclass MemoryStore[T]:                        # no inheritance; structurally a Store[T]\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._d: dict[str, T] = {}\n    def get(self, key: str) -> T | None:\n        return self._d.get(key)\n    def put(self, key: str, value: T) -> None:\n        self._d[key] = value\n\ndef stream(words: list[str], on_token: OnToken) -> None:\n    for i, w in enumerate(words):\n        on_token(w, final=i == len(words) - 1)\n\ndef printer(token: str, *, final: bool) -> None:\n    print(token, end=\"\\n\" if final else \" \")\n\nsessions: Store[dict[str, str]] = MemoryStore()\nsessions.put(\"u1\", {\"lang\": \"en\"})\nprint(sessions.get(\"u1\"))                    # {'lang': 'en'}\nstream([\"hello\", \"world\"], printer)          # hello world\n```","patterns":["**Consumer-owned protocols at the provider boundary.** Define `LLMClient`, `Embedder`, `Tool` and `Store` Protocols in the agent-core package, next to the code that calls them, and keep vendor SDK and framework imports in adapter modules only. Monitor how many core modules import a vendor SDK; the target is zero.","**Scripted fake LLMs for loop tests.** Drive the agent loop in unit tests with a fake that satisfies `LLMClient` and returns a fixed list of tool calls and answers. Test step limits, unknown tools and error results deterministically; keep real-model runs for the eval suite.","**Conformance assertions in tests.** For each implementation add one line such as `_: Embedder = OpenAIEmbedder(client)` in a test module the checker covers. A signature drift then fails CI at the implementation, not at a distant call site.","**Run both checkers if both are used.** Because pyright checks protocol parameter names and mypy does not, declare protocol method parameters positional-only (`def embed(self, texts: Sequence[str], /) -> list[list[float]]: ...`) so editor and CI agree. Watch for failures that only one checker reports.","**`ParamSpec` on every shared decorator.** Retry, tracing and caching decorators around model and tool calls use `Callable[P, R] -> Callable[P, R]` with `functools.wraps`. A quick audit: `reveal_type` on a decorated function should show its real signature, not `(...) -> Any`.","**Protocol for consumers, ABC for your own implementations.** Ship an ABC with shared defaults that in-house classes inherit, and type consumers against the Protocol, so outside classes still plug in."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent loop talks to parts you swap often: model providers, tools,\n  embedders, vector stores, memory. A Protocol per seam lets a scripted fake LLM drive the loop in tests\n  and a new provider drop in without touching the core.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Framework base types (LangChain's chat-model and retriever\n  classes, LlamaIndex's) play the same role nominally; your own agent core can stay framework-free behind\n  `LLMClient`, `Tool` and `Store` Protocols, with each framework or SDK in an adapter.\n- **What you control.** Where the seams are, keeping protocols small and consumer-owned, and typing\n  retry and tracing decorators with `ParamSpec` so tool calls stay checked.\n- **What you can skip.** Deep variance theory. Remember \"accept `Sequence`, return `list`\" and that\n  `@runtime_checkable` checks names only.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Swapping model providers.** An `LLMClient` Protocol lets the agent core run on one vendor today and\n  another tomorrow, with each SDK wrapped in a thin adapter.\n- **Deterministic agent tests.** A scripted fake LLM that satisfies the same Protocol drives the loop\n  through tool calls and errors without network access or token cost.\n- **Pluggable tools and memory.** A `Tool` or `Store` Protocol lets new tools and vector stores join the\n  registry without inheriting from a framework base class.\n- **Typed retry and tracing wrappers.** `ParamSpec` keeps argument checking on every tool and model call\n  wrapped by a retry or span decorator.","mini_project":"**Build a provider-agnostic agent core (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an agent loop that depends only on small Protocols, so a real provider and a fake one are\n  interchangeable.\n- **Steps:** define `LLMClient` and `Tool` Protocols; write the loop against them; add one adapter for a\n  real SDK and one scripted fake that returns a tool call, then a final answer; type a retry decorator\n  with `ParamSpec`; run the checker in strict mode.\n- **Done when:** the same loop passes a unit test with the fake and runs a live query with the adapter,\n  the checker is clean, and a deliberately wrong argument to a decorated tool is flagged."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"Value used where Embedder is expected\"] --> M{\"Has every protocol member?\"}\n    M -->|\"no\"| E1[\"Checker error at the use site: missing embed\"]\n    M -->|\"yes\"| A{\"Attribute types match? settable means exact\"}\n    A -->|\"no\"| E2[\"Error: member dim not settable or wrong type\"]\n    A -->|\"yes\"| S{\"Method parameter and return types compatible?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| E3[\"Error: incompatible embed signature\"]\n    S -->|\"yes\"| OK[\"Accepted, no base class needed\"]\n    U -.->|\"runtime isinstance with runtime_checkable\"| RC[\"Checks attribute names only, not signatures\"]","caption":"How a checker decides whether a value can be used where a Protocol (here an `Embedder`, but equally an\nagent's `Tool` or `LLMClient`) is expected: no inheritance lookup, only a member-by-member comparison,\nand `isinstance` at runtime checks far less."},"interview":[{"q":"What is structural typing, and why did Python add Protocols instead of just using ABCs?","a":"- **Structural typing** means compatibility is decided by the members an object has, not by its declared\n  base classes; it is static duck typing.\n- **Why Protocols (PEP 544):** Python code already targeted behaviour, so nominal ABCs forced adapter\n  classes or `Any` at every vendor seam. Protocols make existing idioms such as `Iterable` and file-like\n  objects checkable without changing runtime behaviour."},{"q":"How would you structure an agent codebase so you can switch model providers and unit-test the loop without network calls?","a":"- **Define small consumer-owned Protocols** (`LLMClient.complete`, `Tool.run`, `Store.get/put`) in the\n  core, and put each SDK or framework behind an adapter that satisfies them.\n- **Test the loop with a scripted fake** that returns a fixed sequence of tool calls and answers, so step\n  limits, unknown tools and tool errors are deterministic tests.\n- **Add conformance lines** (`_: LLMClient = AnthropicAdapter(...)`) so adapter drift fails CI, and keep\n  real-model behaviour in the eval suite."},{"q":"A teammate's function takes `list[Message]` and every caller passing `list[ToolMessage]` gets an error. Explain and fix.","a":"- **Cause: invariance.** The function could append a plain `Message` into the caller's list, so the\n  checker refuses to treat `list[ToolMessage]` as `list[Message]`.\n- **Fix:** accept `Sequence[Message]` or `Iterable[Message]`, which are covariant because nothing can be\n  inserted through them.\n- **Rule of thumb:** abstract read-only types for inputs, concrete types for outputs."},{"q":"When would you not use a Protocol?","a":"- **When you own every implementation and want shared behaviour:** an ABC holds default methods and\n  refuses to instantiate a subclass missing an abstract method, which a Protocol cannot do.\n- **When you need runtime validation:** `@runtime_checkable` checks attribute names only, so it is no\n  substitute for validating untrusted objects.\n- **Common compromise:** Protocol for consumers, ABC for in-house classes."},{"q":"Your retry decorator around tool and model calls makes mypy stop catching wrong arguments. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Cause:** typing the decorator `Callable[..., Any]` erases the wrapped signature, so every call through\n  it is unchecked.\n- **Fix:** `P = ParamSpec(\"P\")`, `R = TypeVar(\"R\")`, and type it `Callable[P, R] -> Callable[P, R]` with\n  `*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs` in the inner function.\n- **Verify** with `reveal_type` on a decorated function; it should show the real parameters."},{"q":"mypy passes and pyright fails on the same protocol implementation. What is the likely difference?","a":"- **Parameter names.** pyright rejects an implementation that renames a protocol method parameter,\n  because a caller could pass it by keyword; mypy ignores positional parameter names when matching.\n- **Fix that satisfies both:** match the names, or make the protocol's parameters positional-only with\n  `/`."}],"tradeoffs":["**Decoupling vs late errors.** Protocols free your contracts from vendor classes, but mismatches surface at call sites, so without an explicit `impl: Proto = Impl()` check in tests a broken implementation is reported only where it is first used.","**Cheap runtime check vs false confidence.** `@runtime_checkable` verifies attribute names only, so using it to validate untrusted objects becomes harmful the moment signatures matter.","**Precise element types vs reading cost.** Generics make types flow through containers and pipelines, but nested type variables and variance annotations are harder to read; for a small private helper `list[Any]` can be the honest choice.","**ABC enforcement vs structural freedom.** An ABC enforces at instantiation and holds shared defaults, which a Protocol cannot; when you own all implementations and want shared behaviour, the ABC wins despite being nominal.","**Signature preservation vs boilerplate.** `ParamSpec` costs more code per decorator; skipping it turns every decorated function into `Callable[..., Any]` and silently disables checks.","**Own protocols vs framework base classes.** Your own small Protocols keep the core portable across frameworks and providers; adopting a framework's base classes gives you its integrations for free but couples the loop to its release cycle. The second wins when you lean on many of its connectors."],"pitfalls":["**Invariant inputs.** Declaring `list[Message]` gives callers \"incompatible type list[ToolMessage]\". Cause: invariance of mutable containers. Fix: `Sequence[Message]`.","**Renamed protocol parameters.** `def embed(self, docs)` is rejected by pyright and accepted by mypy, so a team using both sees CI disagree. Fix: match the names or make the protocol's parameters positional-only.","**`isinstance(x, SomeProtocol)` as a safety check.** It passes with the wrong signature, and the failure appears later as a `TypeError` inside the call, often mid-run in an agent tool step.","**A `TypeVar` used once.** In `def f(x: T) -> None` it relates nothing to anything (pyright warns about exactly this); usually `object` was intended.","**`dim: int` against read-only properties.** Symptom: \"protocol member dim is not settable\" on classes that look correct. Fix: declare it as a `@property` in the protocol.","**Decorators typed `Callable[..., Any]`.** mypy never catches wrong arguments to decorated LLM-call functions, because the signature was erased.","**Fat protocols.** An `LLMClient` with fifteen methods makes every fake and adapter expensive. Symptom: test doubles full of `raise NotImplementedError`. Fix: split by consumer; the loop usually needs one `complete` method."],"when":"- **Use a Protocol when the consumer defines the interface** and implementations come from elsewhere: an\n  `LLMClient` your agent loop calls, an `Embedder` your retriever uses, a `Store` your memory layer\n  writes to. Test doubles become trivial, because a stub only needs the methods, and vendor SDKs stay\n  out of your signatures.\n- **Use an ABC instead** when you ship a base class with shared behaviour that your own subclasses extend.\n- **Use generics** for anything container-shaped whose element type varies: `Cache[K, V]`,\n  `Pipeline[In, Out]`, `Result[T]`.\n- **Use `ParamSpec`** on every decorator in a typed codebase.\n- **Measure:** how few `Any` and adapter-only classes remain.\n- **Interviews:** explain structural versus nominal typing, why `list` is invariant and what `ParamSpec`\n  fixes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Types the LLM client, embedder and store as Protocols so a fake can replace each in tests without monkeypatching vendor modules.","architect":"Defines the handful of Protocols that form the boundary between agent core and providers, so swapping a vendor is an implementation change, not a refactor.","fde":"Fits a customer's existing classes (their search service, their auth client) into the product's interfaces by matching method shapes instead of asking them to subclass anything."},"check":{"q":"Your team has a `Retriever` abstract base class with three in-house subclasses. A customer\nwants to plug in their vendor's search client, which already exposes a compatible `search(query,\nk)` method. A teammate proposes writing an adapter subclass. Is there a better option, and what do\nyou give up?","a":"Define `Retriever` as a `Protocol` with the `search` signature; the vendor class then\nsatisfies it structurally with no wrapper, and the checker verifies the signature match at the\ncall site. What you give up is the ABC's instantiation-time enforcement and any shared default\nmethods, and you should make parameter names match or declare them positional-only (pyright checks\nnames; mypy does not). A common\ncompromise is both: a Protocol for consumers to depend on, and an ABC that your own\nimplementations inherit for shared behaviour, which still satisfies the Protocol."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes a class compatible with a `Protocol` in the eyes of a type checker?","options":["It must inherit from the Protocol class or be registered with it","It must have the Protocol's members with compatible types and signatures, regardless of inheritance","It must be decorated with `@runtime_checkable`","It must define `__protocol__` returning the Protocol name"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Protocols are structural: the checker compares members. Inheritance is allowed but never required, and `@runtime_checkable` only affects `isinstance`, not static compatibility."},{"q":"Why does a checker reject passing a `list[Dog]` to a parameter typed `list[Animal]`?","options":["Because `Dog` is not a subclass of `Animal` in the checker's eyes","Because lists cannot be generic over user-defined classes","Because the function could append a non-`Dog` `Animal`, corrupting the caller's list, so `list` is invariant","Because `list` is covariant and `Animal` is more specific than `Dog`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mutable containers are invariant precisely to prevent writes of the wrong subtype. A read-only `Sequence[Animal]` accepts `list[Dog]` because nothing can be inserted through it."},{"q":"What problem does `ParamSpec` solve for a retry decorator?","options":["It lets the decorator run the wrapped function in a thread","It validates argument values at runtime before the call","It preserves the wrapped function's parameter types so calls through the decorator stay type-checked","It makes the decorator usable on async functions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without `ParamSpec`, the decorator's return type is `Callable[..., Any]` and every call to a decorated function loses argument checking. `ParamSpec` carries the original signature through."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/typeddict-literal-enum","sources":["Lehtosalo, 2019 — PEP 589: TypedDict","Lee, Levkivskyi, Lehtosalo, 2019 — PEP 586: Literal Types","Warsaw, Bendersky, Furman, 2013 — PEP 435: Adding an Enum type to the Python standard library","Official docs: enum module"],"why":"- **Wire data arrives as dicts.** Provider responses, tool-call arguments and agent state typed as\n  `dict[str, Any]` let the checker accept `msg[\"contnt\"]` and `msg[\"role\"] = \"User\"`. Both surface as a\n  400 from the API or a silently skipped branch.\n- **Classes are often the wrong fix.** Converting every dict to a class costs allocations and conversion\n  both ways, while the dict *is* what the SDK consumes, what graph state stores and what gets logged.\n  What is missing is \"a dict with exactly these keys and value types\": `TypedDict`.\n- **Small vocabularies need names.** `role: str` admits `\"User\"`, `\"assistant \"` and `\"function\"`;\n  `finish_reason: str` admits anything. The checker cannot flag a typo, a missing `match` case or a\n  fourth invented role. `Literal` names the set with no runtime object, the right cost for wire-only\n  strings.\n- **Some vocabularies need behaviour.** Mapping each finish reason to an action, attaching a retry policy\n  per error class, or referring to the set from twenty modules needs somewhere to hang methods. Literal\n  values are plain strings, so the mapping lives in a separate dict that drifts. `Enum` members are\n  singleton objects with a name, value and methods, at the cost of a conversion at the JSON boundary.","how":"### TypedDict: a dict with a declared shape\n- **Nothing exists at runtime.** `Message(role=\"user\", content=\"hi\")` returns an ordinary `dict`, and\n  `isinstance(x, Message)` raises `TypeError`. The class exists only so the checker knows keys and value\n  types.\n- **Why:** the point is to type data you already hold as dicts without changing a single runtime object;\n  a wrapper class would defeat that.\n- **No validation.** A dict from `json.loads` (typed `Any`) is simply trusted, because the checker accepts\n  `Any` wherever a `TypedDict` is expected without complaint. Validate it with something, for example\n  Pydantic's `TypeAdapter`.\n\n| Feature | Spelling | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| All keys required | default | Fixed payloads |\n| All keys optional | `total=False` | Sparse patches |\n| Per-key optional or required | `NotRequired[T]`, `Required[T]` (3.11) | `tool_calls` present only on some turns |\n| Read an optional key | `d.get(\"k\")` yields an optional value | Checker forces a narrowing branch |\n| Must not be assigned | `ReadOnly[T]` (3.13) | Shared state slices |\n| Non-identifier keys (`\"content-type\"`) | functional form `TypedDict(\"Headers\", {...})` | HTTP headers |\n\n- **Structural assignability.** A TypedDict with a superset of keys is assignable where the subset is\n  expected, provided shared keys have the same value types and required-ness. A generic `Message`\n  function can therefore accept richer provider-specific dicts.\n\n### Literal: a finite set, checked statically\n- **Exact inhabitants.** `Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\"]` admits only those strings (ints, bools,\n  bytes and enum members are also allowed).\n- **Narrowing on comparison.** Inside `if role == \"user\":` the type of `role` is `Literal[\"user\"]`.\n- **Exhaustiveness is the payoff.** In a `match` over a Literal (or an Enum), a final\n  `case _: assert_never(x)` makes the checker report any unhandled member, so you learn at check time,\n  not in production, that a new role was added and a handler forgot it.\n- **Values vanish at runtime.** `role` is just a `str`, so nothing stops `\"robot\"` arriving from JSON\n  unless something validates it.\n- **The alias survives.** `typing.get_args(Role)` returns `(\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\")`; that is how Pydantic turns a Literal into a schema `enum`,\n  and how you iterate when you must, though the checker sees the tuple only as `tuple[Any, ...]`.\n- **Hence Literal suits** values that are only compared and serialized: tool names in a dispatcher,\n  next-node names returned by a router, an agent's mode.\n\n### Enum: a finite set of runtime objects\n- **Singleton members.** `class Reason(Enum): STOP = \"stop\"`; each member has `.name` (`\"STOP\"`) and\n  `.value` (`\"stop\"`).\n- **Lookup and iteration.** `Reason(\"stop\")` by value, `Reason[\"STOP\"]` by name, and `for r in Reason`\n  iterates members in definition order. Identity comparison (`r is Reason.STOP`) works because there is\n  exactly one object per member.\n- **Duplicate values become aliases**, not new members, so iteration skips them; a documented feature\n  that surprises people.\n- **`auto()`** assigns values when you do not care what they are.\n- **Methods live with the vocabulary.** Members are objects, so you attach methods and properties\n  (`reason.action`) instead of scattering `if` chains.\n\n### Plain Enum versus StrEnum\n| Behaviour | Plain `Enum` | `StrEnum` (3.11) / `IntEnum` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Reason.STOP == \"stop\"` | `False` | `True` |\n| `json.dumps` of a member | raises `TypeError` | serializes as its value |\n| Use at a JSON boundary | needs `.value` everywhere | transparent |\n\n`StrEnum` and `IntEnum` mix in the primitive type, which is what you want for any enum crossing JSON.\n\n### Choosing\n| Need | Pick |\n|---|---|\n| Dict-shaped data whose runtime form must stay a dict (SDK payloads, graph state, tool arguments) | `TypedDict` |\n| Validation, or a shape consumed in many places | a Pydantic model |\n| Small string set, only compared and serialized | `Literal` |\n| Iteration, identity, methods, or one value referenced from many modules | `Enum`; prefer `StrEnum` when it meets JSON |\n\n- **The model barely notices the choice.** Pydantic accepts all three and renders both Literal and Enum\n  as JSON Schema `enum` constraints: an Enum as a named entry under `$defs` referenced by `$ref`, a\n  multi-value Literal inline, and a single-value Literal as `const`.","example":"### Agent graph state and an exhaustive router\nThe state is a plain dict any graph framework can store and checkpoint; the router's `Literal` return is\nthe set of next nodes, and `assert_never` forces a branch for every finish reason. Passes\n`mypy --strict` on 3.11+.\n\n```python\nfrom enum import StrEnum\nfrom typing import Literal, NotRequired, TypedDict, assert_never\n\nclass ToolCall(TypedDict):\n    id: str\n    name: Literal[\"search\", \"calculator\"]       # the tool vocabulary, closed\n    arguments: str\n\nclass AgentState(TypedDict):                     # graph state: stays a plain dict\n    messages: list[dict[str, object]]\n    steps: int\n    pending: NotRequired[list[ToolCall]]\n\nclass Finish(StrEnum):\n    STOP = \"stop\"\n    LENGTH = \"length\"\n    TOOL_CALLS = \"tool_calls\"\n\nNext = Literal[\"run_tools\", \"respond\", \"give_up\"]\n\ndef route(state: AgentState, finish: Finish, max_steps: int = 8) -> Next:\n    if state[\"steps\"] >= max_steps:\n        return \"give_up\"\n    match finish:\n        case Finish.TOOL_CALLS: return \"run_tools\" if state.get(\"pending\") else \"respond\"\n        case Finish.STOP: return \"respond\"\n        case Finish.LENGTH: return \"respond\"\n        case _: assert_never(finish)\n\nstate: AgentState = {\"messages\": [], \"steps\": 2,\n                     \"pending\": [{\"id\": \"c1\", \"name\": \"search\", \"arguments\": '{\"q\": \"refund policy\"}'}]}\nprint(route(state, Finish(\"tool_calls\")))       # run_tools\nprint(route({\"messages\": [], \"steps\": 8}, Finish.STOP))   # give_up\n```\n\n- **Add a member and see:** a new `Finish.PAUSE` without a case makes the checker flag `assert_never`;\n  a `return \"run_tool\"` typo is flagged because it is not a `Next`.\n\n### Wire message, behavioural StrEnum, exhaustive match\nAn optional key, a `StrEnum` carrying behaviour, and an exhaustive `match` the checker polices. Requires\nPython 3.11+ for `NotRequired`, `StrEnum` and `assert_never`.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom enum import StrEnum\nfrom typing import Literal, NotRequired, TypedDict, assert_never\n\nRole = Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\", \"tool\"]\n\nclass ToolCall(TypedDict):\n    id: str\n    name: str\n    arguments: str                 # JSON text, exactly as the API sends it\n\nclass ChatMessage(TypedDict):\n    role: Role\n    content: str | None\n    tool_calls: NotRequired[list[ToolCall]]\n\nclass FinishReason(StrEnum):\n    STOP = \"stop\"\n    LENGTH = \"length\"\n    TOOL_CALLS = \"tool_calls\"\n    CONTENT_FILTER = \"content_filter\"\n\n    @property\n    def truncated(self) -> bool:\n        return self is FinishReason.LENGTH\n\ndef next_action(reason: FinishReason) -> Literal[\"return\", \"continue\", \"call_tools\", \"refuse\"]:\n    match reason:\n        case FinishReason.STOP: return \"return\"\n        case FinishReason.LENGTH: return \"continue\"\n        case FinishReason.TOOL_CALLS: return \"call_tools\"\n        case FinishReason.CONTENT_FILTER: return \"refuse\"\n        case _: assert_never(reason)   # add a member above without a case: checker error here\n\nmsg: ChatMessage = {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": None,\n                    \"tool_calls\": [{\"id\": \"c1\", \"name\": \"search\", \"arguments\": '{\"q\": \"kv cache\"}'}]}\nprint(type(msg))                                   # <class 'dict'>: no wrapper object exists\nreason = FinishReason(\"tool_calls\")                # lookup by wire value\nprint(reason.truncated, next_action(reason))       # False call_tools\nprint(json.dumps({\"finish\": reason}))              # {\"finish\": \"tool_calls\"}: StrEnum is a str\n# msg[\"role\"] = \"robot\"   -> checker error: \"robot\" is not a Role\n```\n\n- **Exhaustiveness in action:** remove the `CONTENT_FILTER` case and mypy or pyright reports that\n  `assert_never` received `Literal[FinishReason.CONTENT_FILTER]` (the member left over after\n  narrowing), not `Never`.\n\n### Validating a TypedDict at the boundary\n`json.loads` returns `Any`, so the checker would trust any shape. A `TypeAdapter` turns it into a\nchecked dict and still returns plain dicts.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom typing import Literal, NotRequired, TypedDict\nfrom pydantic import TypeAdapter, ValidationError\n\nclass ToolCall(TypedDict):\n    id: str\n    name: str\n    arguments: str\n\nclass ChatMessage(TypedDict):\n    role: Literal[\"system\", \"user\", \"assistant\", \"tool\"]\n    content: str | None\n    tool_calls: NotRequired[list[ToolCall]]\n\nmessages = TypeAdapter(list[ChatMessage])        # build once at import time, reuse per request\n\nraw = json.loads('[{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"hi\"}, {\"role\": \"robot\", \"content\": null}]')\ntry:\n    messages.validate_python(raw)\nexcept ValidationError as e:\n    print(e.error_count(), e.errors()[0][\"loc\"])  # 1 (1, 'role')\n\nok = messages.validate_python(raw[:1])\nprint(type(ok[0]), ok[0])                         # <class 'dict'> {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'hi'}\n```","patterns":["**Type graph state, keep it a dict.** Declare the agent's state as a `TypedDict` (messages, step count, pending tool calls, scratch fields) so checkpointers and tracers store it unchanged, and give each node a typed return. Monitor checkpoint size per run; state that grows without bound usually means tool results are stored whole instead of referenced.","**Close the tool-name set.** Derive a `Literal` (or `StrEnum`) of tool names from the registry and type dispatch against it, so a renamed tool fails CI everywhere it was routed by name. At runtime, answer an unknown name from the model with an error tool result, and count those per model version.","**One shared vocabulary package.** Put roles, finish reasons and error classes as `StrEnum`s in a common package that every service imports, and generate tool and output schemas from the same definitions. Monitor for raw string literals of those values in code review or a lint rule.","**`assert_never` in every handler over a closed set.** Each `match` on a role, finish reason or state ends with `case _: assert_never(x)`, so adding a member fails CI at every unhandled site. Track how many sites a new member breaks; that is your blast radius.","**Validate once, then trust the TypedDict.** Build `TypeAdapter(ChatMessage)` once at module import and call `validate_python` where provider JSON enters. Downstream code keeps plain dicts with no conversion. Log validation failures with the field `loc` and provider name.","**Unknown-value fallback at the edge.** When a provider may add a finish reason before you do, map unknown wire values to a logged `UNKNOWN` path at the parsing boundary instead of crashing mid-run. Alert on its count."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The loop branches on closed sets: role, finish reason, tool name, next\n  step. A typo or an unhandled new value becomes a silently skipped tool call or a stuck run;\n  `assert_never` turns that into a CI failure.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangGraph's state schema is commonly a `TypedDict`, with\n  `Annotated[list, add_messages]` as the reducer for the message list, and a conditional-edge function\n  returns the next node's name (a `Literal` return type documents the branches). Provider SDKs type\n  their message params as TypedDicts too.\n- **What you control.** Which shapes stay dicts (state, wire messages), which values are closed\n  (`Literal` or `StrEnum`), and where validation happens, since TypedDict checks nothing at runtime.\n- **What you can skip.** Enum flags, `IntFlag` and the functional Enum API. Know `StrEnum`, `NotRequired`\n  and `assert_never`.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Typing graph state.** A `TypedDict` state with an `Annotated` reducer for messages keeps graph state\n  a plain dict while the checker catches misspelled keys in nodes.\n- **Routing functions.** A conditional edge returning a `Literal` of node names documents every branch,\n  and `assert_never` fails CI when a new route is added but not handled.\n- **Finish-reason handling.** A `StrEnum` of finish reasons maps each value to an action such as\n  continue, run tools or stop, with exhaustive `match` checking.\n- **Wire messages for SDKs.** Provider message params are TypedDicts, so building them with typed dicts\n  catches a wrong role or missing field before the API returns a 400.","mini_project":"**Make an agent router exhaustive (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a small graph-style agent whose state, routes and finish reasons are all closed types.\n- **Steps:** define the state as a `TypedDict` with `NotRequired` optional keys; type the router's return\n  as a `Literal` of three node names; model finish reasons as a `StrEnum` and handle them in a `match`\n  ending in `assert_never`; dispatch on the router's result with a second `match` that also ends in\n  `assert_never`; then add a fourth node name to the `Literal` without a dispatch case.\n- **Done when:** the checker fails on the unhandled route and on a misspelled state key, passes after the\n  fix, and the state still serializes to JSON as a plain dict."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"What are you typing?\"] --> S{\"A dict shape or a single value?\"}\n    S -->|\"dict shape\"| D{\"Must it stay a plain dict at runtime?\"}\n    D -->|\"yes, SDK payload or graph state\"| T1[\"TypedDict, validated once with TypeAdapter\"]\n    D -->|\"no, needs validation in many places\"| PM[\"Pydantic model\"]\n    S -->|\"one of N values\"| B{\"Needs methods, iteration or many modules?\"}\n    B -->|\"no, only compared and serialized\"| L[\"Literal plus assert_never in match\"]\n    B -->|\"yes\"| J{\"Crosses JSON?\"}\n    J -->|\"yes\"| SE[\"StrEnum: equals the wire string, dumps as value\"]\n    J -->|\"no\"| E[\"Plain Enum\"]","caption":"The decision path from the Choosing table, as you meet it designing an agent: first ask whether you are\ntyping a dict's shape (a message, graph state) or one value's vocabulary (a tool name, a finish reason),\nthen how much runtime behaviour you need."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a TypedDict, and why does it have no runtime presence?","a":"- **Definition:** a static declaration of a dict's keys and value types; constructing one returns an\n  ordinary `dict`, and `isinstance` against it raises `TypeError`.\n- **Why:** the goal is to type data you already hold as dicts (SDK payloads, agent state) without\n  changing a single runtime object, so there is nothing to convert or allocate.\n- **Consequence:** it validates nothing, so `json.loads` output must be checked at the boundary."},{"q":"A branch handling `finish_reason == \"stop\"` never runs after a refactor to Enum. What happened?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the vocabulary became a plain `Enum`, and a plain member compares unequal to the raw\n  wire string, so `reason == \"stop\"` is always `False`.\n- **Fix:** switch to `StrEnum` (3.11), whose members are strings, or compare `.value`.\n- **Also check** logging: a plain Enum member in `json.dumps` raises `TypeError`."},{"q":"When would you pick Literal over Enum, and when does that flip?","a":"- **Literal** costs nothing at runtime and still gives narrowing and `assert_never` exhaustiveness, ideal\n  for roles and modes that are only compared and serialized.\n- **Flip to Enum** when you need methods or per-member data (a retry policy per error class), iteration\n  as a first-class operation, or one definition referenced from many modules."},{"q":"How do you make sure every handler is updated when a provider adds a new finish reason?","a":"- **Statically:** end every `match` over the set with `case _: assert_never(x)`, so adding the member to\n  the Enum or Literal fails CI at each unhandled site.\n- **At runtime:** the provider may ship the value before you add it, so map unknown wire values to a\n  logged fallback path at the parsing boundary and alert on its count, instead of crashing mid-run."},{"q":"You are designing the state for a multi-step agent graph. Why a TypedDict rather than a class, and what must you add?","a":"- **TypedDict keeps the runtime object a dict,** which graph frameworks, checkpointers and trace\n  exporters serialize as-is, while the checker still catches `state[\"mesages\"]` and wrong value types.\n- **Add validation where state is rebuilt from storage** (resuming a checkpoint) with a `TypeAdapter`,\n  because TypedDict validates nothing.\n- **Close the branching values:** `Literal` next-node names and a `StrEnum` finish reason, each `match`\n  ending in `assert_never`, so a new branch cannot be forgotten."},{"q":"Why does passing a TypedDict to a helper typed `dict[str, Any]` fail, and what do you use instead?","a":"- **Because** the helper could insert arbitrary keys or values, breaking the declared shape the caller\n  relies on, so the checker rejects it.\n- **Use `Mapping[str, object]`** for read-only helpers such as logging or redaction; it accepts any\n  TypedDict because it promises no writes."}],"tradeoffs":["**TypedDict: free at runtime vs no checks.** It keeps data SDK-ready, but gives no validation or methods; once the same shape is consumed in several modules, a Pydantic model's validation pays for itself.","**Literal: free exhaustiveness vs no behaviour.** Values are bare strings and iterating means `get_args` on the alias; once you want methods, per-member data or the set referenced from many modules, switch to an Enum.","**Enum: identity and methods vs boundary friction.** A plain Enum needs `.value` at every JSON boundary and compares unequal to raw strings; `StrEnum` removes that cost for string vocabularies.","**`NotRequired`: precision vs narrowing noise.** It models optional payload fields precisely, but every `.get()` returns `T | None` and forces narrowing; when the field is always present but nullable, a required `T | None` key is simpler.","**Closed tool-name Literal vs dynamic tools.** A `Literal` of tool names catches routing typos, but tools loaded at runtime (from an MCP server or a plugin directory) cannot appear in a static type. Use the Literal for built-in tools and a validated `str` plus registry lookup for dynamic ones."],"pitfalls":["**`isinstance(x, SomeTypedDict)` as validation.** It raises `TypeError` at runtime because TypedDict is not a real class. Validate with Pydantic or check keys explicitly.","**Comparing a plain `Enum` member to the wire string.** `reason == \"stop\"` is always `False`; the symptom is a branch that never executes. Use `StrEnum` or compare `.value`.","**Plain Enum into `json.dumps`.** Fails with \"Object of type ... is not JSON serializable\", typically deep inside a logging or request-serialization call.","**Assigning `json.loads(...)` straight to a TypedDict variable.** The result is `Any`, so the checker silently trusts an unverified shape and the `KeyError` comes later. Validate with `TypeAdapter(ChatMessage).validate_python(...)` at the boundary.","**The pre-3.11 idiom `class Reason(str, Enum)` in prompts or logs.** On 3.11+ `f\"{Reason.STOP}\"` renders `Reason.STOP`, not `stop`, so a prompt says \"finish reason Reason.STOP\". `StrEnum` formats as its value; or interpolate `.value` explicitly.","**Passing a TypedDict to a helper typed `dict[str, Any]`.** The checker rejects it, because the helper could insert arbitrary keys. Type read-only helpers as `Mapping[str, object]`: logging, redaction and metrics helpers then accept any TypedDict, agent state included.","**Two Enum members with the same value.** The second silently becomes an alias and disappears from iteration, which looks like a \"missing\" member in a dropdown or a prompt listing valid options.","**New member without an `assert_never` guard.** Nothing warns, and the new value falls through to a default branch at runtime."],"when":"- **`TypedDict`:** messages, tool-call records and state dicts flowing into and out of provider SDKs and\n  agent frameworks, where the dict is the runtime truth and validation already happened at the boundary.\n- **`Literal`:** roles, modes and finish reasons that are compared and serialized but never iterated;\n  pair every `match` over them with `assert_never`.\n- **`Enum` (usually `StrEnum`):** when a vocabulary needs behaviour, iteration or one definition shared\n  across modules, and when the same set appears in prompts, schemas and code.\n- **Measure:** count the `str` parameters that really mean \"one of N values\"; each is a latent typo.\n- **Interviews:** why TypedDict has no runtime, why Literal gives exhaustiveness, and what `StrEnum`\n  changes about equality and JSON.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Types the chat-message and tool-call dicts as TypedDicts and the role and finish reason fields as Literals, so a typo fails in CI instead of as an API 400.","architect":"Decides which vocabularies become shared Enums in a common package, so every service agrees on the same finish-reason and error-class names and schemas.","fde":"Maps a customer's status codes and category strings onto Enums with behaviour, turning their undocumented string conventions into one checked, iterable definition."},"check":{"q":"An agent's `finish_reason` field is a plain `str`. You want the checker to catch unhandled\nvalues when a provider adds a new one, you need to serialize it to JSON in logs, and one module\nneeds to map each value to a retry policy. Which of TypedDict, Literal or Enum do you pick and why?","a":"A `StrEnum`. Literal would give exhaustiveness checking, but its members are bare strings,\nso the retry-policy mapping would live in a separate dict keyed by those strings that can drift\nfrom the type (a checker will not tell you the dict lacks a new key). A plain\nEnum carries the mapping as a method or property but fails `json.dumps` and compares unequal to the\nwire string, costing a conversion at every boundary. `StrEnum` members are strings, so they\nserialize and compare transparently, you can still `match` over them with `assert_never` for\nexhaustiveness, and the policy lives on the member. TypedDict is irrelevant here: it types a\ndict's shape, not a single value's vocabulary."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `Message(role=\"user\", content=\"hi\")` return at runtime if `Message` is a TypedDict?","options":["An instance of `Message` with attribute access","A plain `dict`; the TypedDict class exists only for the type checker","A frozen mapping that rejects new keys","A `types.SimpleNamespace`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TypedDict adds no runtime type. The checker uses the declaration to verify keys and values, but the object you hold is an ordinary dict, which is why `isinstance` against it fails."},{"q":"Why does `match` with a final `case _: assert_never(x)` help when `x` is a Literal or Enum?","options":["It raises at runtime for any unexpected value, replacing validation","It makes the match statement faster by skipping unreachable cases","The checker reports an error if any member of the set is not handled by an earlier case","It converts the Literal into an Enum automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"After every member is matched, the checker narrows `x` to `Never`; if one is missing, `x` still has a type and `assert_never` is a static error. It documents and enforces exhaustiveness."},{"q":"Which statement about a plain `Enum` member such as `Reason.STOP = \"stop\"` is correct?","options":["`Reason.STOP == \"stop\"` is `True` because the value is a string","`json.dumps(Reason.STOP)` produces `\"stop\"`","`Reason(\"stop\")` returns the member, and two members with the same value are aliases of one","Members can be created at runtime by assignment, such as `Reason.NEW = \"new\"`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Lookup by value is supported and duplicate values create aliases. Equality with the raw string and direct JSON serialization only hold for `StrEnum`, which mixes in `str`."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pydantic","sources":["Official docs: Pydantic","Official docs: JSON Schema"],"why":"- **Type hints are inert.** A function typed `-> Ticket` will happily return a dict with a misspelled key.\n- **AI data is untrusted on arrival.** HTTP bodies, completions that should be JSON, tool arguments a\n  model invented, env vars, YAML configs, rows from a customer export. A model malforms them creatively:\n  a string for an int, an extra field, a value outside the set, valid JSON with a trailing sentence.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** Hand-written `if \"title\" not in data` checks grow to hundreds of lines that\n  drift from the types, with ad-hoc error messages. A `TypedDict` or `dataclass` documents the shape but\n  validates nothing. A separate JSON Schema file duplicates the class, and the two disagree within a month.\n- **One artifact, four jobs.** The annotated class is the static type, runtime validator, serializer and\n  JSON Schema source. The schema you send the model and the validator you run on its answer cannot\n  disagree, and the structured error list can be fed back for a repair attempt.\n- **The ecosystem builds on it.** FastAPI, the OpenAI and Anthropic SDK structured-output helpers,\n  `instructor`, LangChain and most agent frameworks rely on this property. (SDK support for passing a\n  Pydantic class directly varies by provider and version; the generated JSON Schema is the portable part.)","how":"### From annotations to a validator\n1. **Compile once.** At class creation, `BaseModel`'s metaclass reads `__annotations__` and each\n   `Field(...)` into a *core schema*, a tree of every field's type and constraints. This happens once per\n   class *because* building the validator costs far more than running it, and models are defined once\n   and used millions of times.\n2. **Run in Rust.** In Pydantic v2, `pydantic-core`, a Rust extension, executes the schema, which is why\n   validation is fast enough for every request.\n3. **Pick an entry point.**\n\n| Call | Input |\n|---|---|\n| `Model.model_validate(obj)` | Python object (dict, attributes) |\n| `model_validate_json(s)` | JSON text, parsed in Rust without an intermediate `json.loads` |\n| `Model(**kwargs)` | keyword arguments |\n\n4. **Fail with structure.** A `ValidationError` carries a list of errors, each with `loc` (path to the bad\n   field), `msg` and a machine-readable `type`. That structure, not one string, is what makes \"send the\n   errors back to the model and retry\" practical.\n\n### Lax versus strict, ignore versus forbid\n| Setting | Default behaviour | When to change |\n|---|---|---|\n| Coercion | *Lax*: `\"1200\"` becomes `1200` for `int`, `\"true\"` becomes `True`, because query strings, env vars and model output arrive as text | `ConfigDict(strict=True)` or `Field(strict=True)` when exact types matter, such as `1` vs `\"1\"` in an ID |\n| Unknown fields | *Ignored* | `ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")` for tool arguments, where a hallucinated field is a signal, not noise |\n\n- **V2 tightened some rules** relative to v1; for example an `int` is no longer accepted for a `str` field\n  by default.\n\n### The order things happen in\nFor one `model_validate(data)` call, knowing the pipeline tells you where custom logic belongs:\n\n1. **`@model_validator(mode=\"before\")`** sees the raw input (a dict, or whatever was passed) and can\n   reshape it, for example unwrapping `{\"ticket\": {...}}` that a model insisted on adding.\n2. **Each field, in declaration order:** lookup by name or alias, then `field_validator(mode=\"before\")`\n   on the raw value, then core type validation and coercion (`\"1200\"` to `1200`), then declared\n   constraints (`ge`, `min_length`), then `mode=\"after\"` validators on an already-typed value.\n   `mode=\"wrap\"` surrounds the core step and can short-circuit it.\n3. **Errors from every field are collected**, not stopped at the first, *because* a repair prompt that\n   lists all five problems converges in one retry instead of five.\n4. **`@model_validator(mode=\"after\")`** runs on the built instance if all fields passed. Cross-field\n   invariants such as `start < end` go here, since only now are all fields typed and present.\n\n### After construction\n- **Validation happens at construction only.** `t.affected_users = \"lots\"` is not checked unless the\n  model sets `validate_assignment=True`.\n- **`Model.model_construct(**data)` skips validation entirely:** the fast path for data you already\n  validated, and a hole for data you did not.\n- **`frozen=True`** makes instances immutable and hashable.\n\n### Validators, serialization, schema\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `field_validator` | Single-field normalisation and checks |\n| `model_validator` | Reshaping and cross-field cases above |\n| `Field(alias=\"userId\")` | Map a wire name onto a Python name (set `populate_by_name=True` to accept both) |\n| `@computed_field` | Expose derived values in dumps |\n| `model_dump()` / `model_dump_json()` | Dict / string output, controlled by `exclude`, `by_alias` and `mode=\"json\"` |\n| `model_json_schema()` | JSON Schema: `enum` for `Literal` and `Enum`, `minimum`/`maximum` from `ge`/`le`, `description` from `Field(description=...)`, which is text the model actually reads |\n| `TypeAdapter(list[Ticket])` | Validation and schema for non-models, including `TypedDict`s and plain containers |\n| `Field(discriminator=\"type\")` | Discriminated unions: route a payload to the right variant by a tag field, how you validate \"one of these N tool calls\" |\n\n### Against the alternatives\n| Option | Validation | Schema | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Stdlib `dataclasses` | None; lighter | No | Internal records you construct yourself |\n| `TypedDict` | Static only; data stays dicts (validate with a `TypeAdapter` if needed) | Via `TypeAdapter` | Wire shapes that must stay dicts |\n| `attrs` | Validators | No schema generation | Rich internal classes |\n| `msgspec` | Yes, faster | Yes, but a smaller feature set overall | Hot-path decoding |\n| Pydantic | Yes | Yes | One definition serving checker, validator, serializer and schema, which is why the AI ecosystem standardized on it |\n\nSettings management lives in the separate `pydantic-settings` package.\n\n### Sharp edges\n- **Validation has a cost:** do it at boundaries, not in hot loops over already-validated data.\n- **Lax coercion can mask bugs:** a float `1.0` silently becomes `1`.\n- **Optional fields render as `anyOf` with `null`.** Some providers' strict structured-output modes need\n  every property listed as required with `additionalProperties: false`. SDK helpers rewrite the schema\n  for you; a hand-built schema must do it itself.\n- **V1 and v2 APIs differ:** `validator` became `field_validator`, `.dict()` became `.model_dump()`,\n  `parse_obj` became `model_validate`, and the inner `Config` class became\n  `model_config = ConfigDict(...)`. Copying old snippets gives deprecation warnings or errors.","example":"### Agent tool dispatch: validation errors become tool results\nEach tool has an argument model; the schemas go in the request, and a failed validation becomes an\nerror `tool_result` (the Anthropic Messages shape, with `is_error`) so the model fixes the call on the\nnext turn instead of the handler running with bad input.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Any, Callable\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field, ValidationError\n\nclass GetOrder(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    order_id: str = Field(pattern=r\"^ORD-\\d{6}$\", description=\"Order id like ORD-123456\")\n\nclass Refund(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    order_id: str = Field(pattern=r\"^ORD-\\d{6}$\")\n    amount_cents: int = Field(gt=0, strict=True)       # money: no \"1250\" string coercion\n\nREGISTRY: dict[str, tuple[type[BaseModel], Callable[[Any], str]]] = {\n    \"get_order\": (GetOrder, lambda a: f\"{a.order_id}: shipped\"),\n    \"refund\": (Refund, lambda a: f\"refunded {a.amount_cents} on {a.order_id}\"),\n}\nTOOLS = [{\"name\": n, \"input_schema\": m.model_json_schema()} for n, (m, _) in REGISTRY.items()]\n\ndef tool_result(block: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Turn one tool_use block from the model into a tool_result block for the next turn.\"\"\"\n    model, handler = REGISTRY[block[\"name\"]]\n    try:\n        args = model.model_validate(block[\"input\"])\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        issues = \"; \".join(f\"{'.'.join(map(str, x['loc']))}: {x['msg']}\" for x in e.errors())\n        return {\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": block[\"id\"], \"is_error\": True,\n                \"content\": f\"Invalid arguments, fix and call again: {issues}\"}\n    return {\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": block[\"id\"], \"content\": handler(args)}\n\nbad = {\"id\": \"t1\", \"name\": \"refund\", \"input\": {\"order_id\": \"123456\", \"amount_cents\": \"1250\", \"reason\": \"late\"}}\nprint(tool_result(bad)[\"content\"])\n# Invalid arguments, fix and call again: order_id: String should match pattern '^ORD-\\d{6}$';\n#   amount_cents: Input should be a valid integer; reason: Extra inputs are not permitted\ngood = {\"id\": \"t2\", \"name\": \"refund\", \"input\": {\"order_id\": \"ORD-123456\", \"amount_cents\": 1250}}\nprint(tool_result(good)[\"content\"])     # refunded 1250 on ORD-123456\n```\n\n- **All three problems in one message:** errors are collected per field, so the model can fix the call\n  in one retry rather than three.\n- **A consequential tool stays strict:** `strict=True` refuses the string amount instead of coercing it.\n\n### Structured extraction with a repair message\nThe schema is generated from the model, and the validation errors become feedback for a repair attempt.\n`extra=\"forbid\"` makes an invented field fail loudly.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field, ValidationError, field_validator\n\nclass Ticket(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")          # hallucinated keys are errors\n    title: str = Field(min_length=3, max_length=120, description=\"Short summary of the issue\")\n    severity: Literal[\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]\n    affected_users: int = Field(ge=0)\n    tags: list[str] = Field(default_factory=list)\n\n    @field_validator(\"tags\")\n    @classmethod\n    def normalise(cls, v: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n        return sorted({t.strip().lower() for t in v if t.strip()})\n\nschema = Ticket.model_json_schema()      # send this as the tool/response schema\nprint(schema[\"properties\"][\"severity\"][\"enum\"])          # ['low', 'medium', 'high']\nprint(schema[\"properties\"][\"affected_users\"][\"minimum\"]) # 0\n\ndef parse_or_feedback(text: str) -> Ticket | str:\n    try:\n        return Ticket.model_validate_json(text)\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        issues = \"; \".join(f\"{'.'.join(map(str, err['loc']))}: {err['msg']}\" for err in e.errors())\n        return f\"Your JSON was rejected. Fix these fields and resend: {issues}\"\n\nraw = '{\"title\": \"Login broken\", \"severity\": \"critical\", \"affected_users\": \"1200\", \"tags\": [\"Auth\", \"auth \"]}'\nprint(parse_or_feedback(raw))\n# Your JSON was rejected. Fix these fields and resend: severity: Input should be 'low', 'medium' or 'high'\nt = parse_or_feedback(raw.replace(\"critical\", \"high\"))\nprint(t)        # title='Login broken' severity='high' affected_users=1200 tags=['auth']\nprint(type(t.affected_users))   # <class 'int'>: \"1200\" was coerced in lax mode\nprint(t.model_dump_json())      # {\"title\":\"Login broken\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"affected_users\":1200,\"tags\":[\"auth\"]}\n```\n\nWhat each field did in the first call:\n\n| Field | Outcome | Pipeline step |\n|---|---|---|\n| `affected_users` | passed | lax coercion of `\"1200\"` in the core step |\n| `tags` | passed | the after-validator received a real `list[str]` and collapsed `\"Auth\"` and `\"auth \"` into one tag |\n| `severity` | failed | `Literal` check; the error list would have named every failing field had there been more |\n\n- **Schema and validator agree:** because of `extra=\"forbid\"`, the schema also carries\n  `\"additionalProperties\": false`, so the model is told the same rule the validator enforces.\n- **The feedback string** is what an `instructor`-style retry loop sends back to the model.\n\n### Routing tool calls with a discriminated union\nOne adapter validates a list of mixed tool calls, picks the variant by `type`, and enforces a\ncross-field rule in an after-validator.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom typing import Annotated, Literal, Union\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field, TypeAdapter, ValidationError, model_validator\n\nclass SearchCall(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    type: Literal[\"search\"]\n    query: str = Field(min_length=1)\n\nclass ReportCall(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")\n    type: Literal[\"report\"]\n    start: date\n    end: date\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def check_range(self) -> \"ReportCall\":\n        if self.start >= self.end:                 # cross-field: only here are both typed\n            raise ValueError(\"start must be before end\")\n        return self\n\nToolCall = Annotated[Union[SearchCall, ReportCall], Field(discriminator=\"type\")]\ncalls = TypeAdapter(list[ToolCall])\n\nok = calls.validate_json('[{\"type\": \"search\", \"query\": \"kv cache\"},'\n                         ' {\"type\": \"report\", \"start\": \"2026-01-01\", \"end\": \"2026-02-01\"}]')\nprint([type(c).__name__ for c in ok])           # ['SearchCall', 'ReportCall']\n\ntry:\n    calls.validate_json('[{\"type\": \"report\", \"start\": \"2026-03-01\", \"end\": \"2026-02-01\"},'\n                        ' {\"type\": \"delete\", \"id\": 7}]')\nexcept ValidationError as e:\n    for err in e.errors():\n        print(err[\"loc\"], err[\"type\"])\n# (0, 'report') value_error\n# (1,) union_tag_invalid\n```","patterns":["**Bounded validate-and-repair loop.** On `ValidationError`, send the `loc: msg` list back to the model and retry at most once or twice, then fail the request. In an agent loop this is an error tool result that counts toward the step budget. Monitor first-pass and post-repair failure rates separately; a rising first-pass rate means the prompt or schema regressed.","**One argument model per tool, registered once.** Keep a registry from tool name to argument model and handler; generate the request's tool list from it, and validate every call through it. Tool schema and handler then cannot drift, and adding a tool is one entry. Track invalid-call rate per tool.","**Aggregate errors by `loc` and `type`.** Log `e.errors()` as structured fields, never only `str(e)`, and chart counts per field. One dominant field usually means an ambiguous `description` or an enum the model paraphrases.","**Strictness by source.** Use `extra=\"forbid\"` for model output and tool arguments, but leave the default `ignore` for third-party webhooks so a vendor's new field is not an outage. Set `Field(strict=True)` on IDs and money.","**Build adapters once.** Create `TypeAdapter(...)` instances and models at module import, not per request, because compiling the core schema is the expensive part. Watch per-request CPU after refactors that move adapters into functions.","**Snapshot the generated schema.** A test compares `Model.model_json_schema()` with a committed JSON file, so any change to what the model is shown appears in code review and can be paired with an eval run of the agent's tool-selection and argument accuracy."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every tool call and structured output is untrusted model JSON. Pydantic\n  turns it into a typed object or a precise error list, which is the difference between a tool that\n  quietly runs with a default and an agent that fixes its own call next turn.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangChain's `with_structured_output` and tool argument schemas,\n  `instructor`, Pydantic AI, the OpenAI Agents SDK's function tools, provider SDK structured-output\n  helpers and FastAPI request models all build on `BaseModel` and its JSON Schema.\n- **What you control.** Field descriptions (text the model reads), `extra=\"forbid\"` on tool arguments,\n  strictness for IDs and money, and the bounded repair loop around validation failures.\n- **What you can skip.** Custom core schemas, `wrap` validators for most tools, and v1 internals beyond\n  recognising old idioms in copied snippets.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool argument validation.** Each tool's arguments are a `BaseModel` with `extra=\"forbid\"`, so an\n  invented field or wrong type becomes a structured error instead of a silent default.\n- **Structured outputs.** Extraction and classification agents return a model whose JSON Schema is sent\n  to the provider and whose validator checks the answer.\n- **Self-repair loops.** Validation errors go back to the model as a tool error, with a small retry\n  budget, so it fixes its own call on the next turn.\n- **Agent service boundaries.** FastAPI request and response models and settings for keys and limits use\n  the same classes, so the API and the agent agree on shapes.","mini_project":"**Build a validated tool with a bounded repair loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a tool whose arguments are validated by Pydantic, with errors returned to the model for repair.\n- **Steps:** define a `BaseModel` for the arguments with field descriptions and `extra=\"forbid\"`; send\n  its JSON Schema as the tool definition; validate each call with `model_validate`; on failure return the\n  error list as the tool result; cap repairs at two; log every failure.\n- **Done when:** a test feeding three malformed calls (wrong type, extra field, missing field) shows each\n  repaired or stopped within the budget, and the logged errors name the exact field."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    IN[\"Raw input: dict or JSON text\"] --> MB[\"model_validator mode before: reshape raw input\"]\n    MB --> F[\"For each field in declaration order\"]\n    F --> FB[\"field_validator mode before on raw value\"]\n    FB --> CORE[\"Core type check and lax coercion, in Rust\"]\n    CORE --> CON[\"Constraints: ge, min_length\"]\n    CON --> FA[\"field_validator mode after on typed value\"]\n    FA --> COL{\"Any field errors collected?\"}\n    COL -->|\"yes\"| VE[\"ValidationError: list of loc, msg, type\"]\n    COL -->|\"no\"| MA[\"model_validator mode after: cross-field rules\"]\n    MA --> OBJ[\"Typed model instance\"]\n    VE -.->|\"loc and msg as repair prompt\"| IN","caption":"One `model_validate` call on a model's tool-call arguments, in the order described above. Field errors\nare collected rather than stopping at the first, the after model validator runs only if every field\npassed, and the dotted edge is the repair message sent back to the model."},"interview":[{"q":"What does Pydantic give you that type hints and dataclasses do not?","a":"- **Runtime validation:** type hints are inert and dataclasses check nothing, while a `BaseModel` rejects\n  or coerces bad input and reports every failure with a `loc`, `msg` and `type`.\n- **One definition for four jobs:** static type, validator, serializer and JSON Schema come from the\n  same class, so the schema you show a model and the validator you run on its answer cannot drift."},{"q":"A tool receives a default value even though the model clearly sent that argument. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect a misspelled or aliased key.** With the default `extra=\"ignore\"`, `severty` is silently\n  dropped and the field falls back to its default.\n- **Confirm** by logging the raw tool-call JSON next to `model_dump()`.\n- **Fix:** set `ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\")` on tool-argument models so the invented key becomes a\n  `ValidationError` that a repair loop can report back to the model."},{"q":"Your agent's tool calls fail validation on about one call in ten. How do you design the handling so runs still finish?","a":"- **Return the errors to the model** as an error tool result listing every `loc: msg`, not a stack\n  trace, so it can correct the call on the next step.\n- **Bound it:** a repair attempt counts against the step budget, and after one or two failed repairs the\n  run stops with a clear failure instead of looping.\n- **Fix the cause:** aggregate failures by tool, `loc` and `type`; a dominant field usually needs a\n  clearer `description`, a `Literal` instead of free text, or a flatter schema."},{"q":"Lax or strict mode: which would you choose, and when does it flip?","a":"- **Lax by default** suits text-shaped sources (query strings, env vars, model output), because `\"1200\"`\n  for an `int` is the common, harmless case.\n- **Strict** for identifiers, money and anything where `1` and `\"1\"` must differ, set per field with\n  `Field(strict=True)` or per model with `ConfigDict(strict=True)`, because lax coercion can hide\n  upstream type confusion, such as a float `1.0` becoming `1`."},{"q":"Validation shows up as a hot spot in a profile of your streaming endpoint. What do you look for?","a":"- **Validation per chunk:** validating inside a per-token loop is CPU-bound; accumulate and validate once\n  at the end.\n- **Adapters built per request:** compiling the core schema is the expensive part, so create\n  `TypeAdapter` instances and models at import time.\n- **Re-validating trusted data:** pass models inward, and use `model_construct` only for data you already\n  validated."},{"q":"Where would you enforce \"start must be before end\", and why there?","a":"- **In `@model_validator(mode=\"after\")`**, because only after every field has passed are both values typed\n  and present.\n- **Not in a field validator,** where the other field may be missing or still raw, and not in the caller,\n  where the rule would be skipped by any other entry point."}],"tradeoffs":["**One class for four jobs vs coupling.** Static type, validator, serializer and schema all follow Pydantic's interpretation, so a major-version change (v1 to v2) touches every boundary at once.","**Lax coercion vs hidden confusion.** Real-world text inputs just work, but type confusion upstream can hide; flip to strict mode for identifiers, money and anything where `\"1\"` and `1` must differ.","**Fast per call vs repeated calls.** Rust-backed validation still dominates in a tight loop over already-validated data; validate once at the boundary and pass models.","**Expressive schemas vs model confusion.** Deeply nested or recursive schemas can confuse models and some providers' strict modes; flatten or simplify when output quality drops.","**`extra=\"forbid\"`: early detection vs brittleness.** It catches hallucinated fields, but on *inputs you do not control* (a vendor adding a field) it turns a harmless addition into an outage; use it for model output, not third-party webhooks."],"pitfalls":["**Default `extra` (`ignore`) on tool arguments.** A misspelled key vanishes silently; the tool receives a default value and behaves as if the argument was never supplied.","**Hand-editing a JSON Schema.** After one side changes, validation rejects responses the schema allowed, or vice versa. Generate it from the model.","**Logging only `str(e)`.** You lose the structured `loc`/`type` list needed to retry with precise feedback or aggregate failure modes.","**Mixing v1 and v2 idioms** (`@validator`, `.dict()`, class `Config`) in a v2 project. Symptom: a wall of deprecation warnings, then breakage when the compatibility shims are removed.","**`tags: list[str] = []` as a default.** Pydantic copies defaults per instance so state is not shared, but readers assume it is; `default_factory` states the intent.","**Assuming mutations are checked.** With the default `validate_assignment=False`, `t.severity = \"critical\"` succeeds and the bad value surfaces later in serialization or downstream.","**Validating inside a per-token streaming loop.** Symptom: CPU-bound latency that disappears when you accumulate chunks and validate once at the end."],"when":"- **Use at every trust boundary:** HTTP requests and responses, LLM structured output, tool-call\n  arguments, configuration, and any file or message produced by another system.\n- **The flow:** define the model, generate the schema from it, validate input with it, and pass the\n  resulting object inward.\n- **Avoid for** small internal records you construct yourself in a hot path; a `dataclass` is lighter.\n- **Measure:** the error distribution from `ValidationError.errors()` over time. If the `type` field shows\n  the model failing the same constraint, that is a prompt or schema problem, not a code problem.\n- **Interviews:** explain lax versus strict, how the schema and validator stay in sync, and what you do\n  when the model's JSON fails. Day to day, Pydantic is the connective tissue between prompts, SDKs and\n  your code.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines response and tool-argument models, generates schemas from them, and builds the validate-then-retry loop around model output with structured error feedback.","architect":"Decides where validation happens (edge, service, agent core), which models are shared across services, and the strictness policy for inputs you control versus vendor inputs.","fde":"Models a customer's messy export or API payload as Pydantic classes on day one, so every malformed record is reported with a field path instead of a stack trace."},"check":{"q":"A model is asked to return JSON matching a Pydantic class. About 5% of responses fail\nvalidation. Walk through how you would diagnose and reduce that rate without just retrying blindly.","a":"First log `ValidationError.errors()` as structured data and aggregate by `loc` and `type`.\nIf one field dominates, it is usually a schema or prompt problem: an ambiguous `description`, an\nenum the model keeps paraphrasing, or a nested optional the provider's strict mode handles badly;\nfix the schema or the field description. Check that the schema sent to the model is generated from\nthe same class that validates, not a stale copy. Consider `extra=\"forbid\"` so hallucinated fields\nsurface. Then add a bounded repair loop that sends the exact error list back to the model, and\nmeasure the residual failure rate; if it is still material, simplify the schema or split the\nextraction into two smaller calls."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Pydantic compile a model's annotations into a core schema once at class creation?","options":["To register the model with FastAPI automatically","Building the validator is expensive while running it is cheap, and models are defined once but validated many times","Because annotations are only available at class creation time","To make the model immutable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compilation happens once per class and the compiled validator (executed in Rust) runs on every call. Paying the build cost per validation would make request-time validation far too slow."},{"q":"With default settings, what happens when an LLM returns `{\"title\": \"x\", \"severty\": \"high\"}` for a model whose field is `severity`?","options":["A `ValidationError` for the unknown field `severty`","`severty` is ignored and validation fails only because `severity` is missing","`severty` is matched to `severity` by fuzzy matching","The model instance is created with `severity=None`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`extra` defaults to `ignore`, so the misspelled key is dropped. The required `severity` is then missing. Set `extra=\"forbid\"` to make the hallucinated field itself an error."},{"q":"Which is the strongest reason to generate the JSON Schema from the Pydantic model rather than writing it by hand?","options":["Generated schemas are smaller","Hand-written schemas cannot express enums","The schema the model is given and the validator applied to its answer cannot drift apart","Providers reject schemas that were not produced by Pydantic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One definition serves both roles. A hand-maintained copy will eventually allow something the validator rejects or vice versa, producing failures that are hard to attribute."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/dataclasses","sources":["Smith, 2017 — PEP 557: Data Classes","Official docs: dataclasses module"],"why":"- **Systems fill up with mostly-data classes.** A `GenerationConfig` with a model name and temperature, a\n  `RetrievedChunk` with text and a score, an agent `Step` with a tool name, arguments and output.\n- **Hand-written, each costs thirty lines.** An `__init__` assigning every field, a `__repr__` for readable\n  logs, an `__eq__` for tests, and sometimes `__hash__` for cache keys.\n- **The boilerplate is where bugs live.** A field added to `__init__` but forgotten in `__eq__`, a repr\n  missing the one field you needed in the incident log, a `__hash__` that disagrees with `__eq__` and\n  silently breaks sets.\n- **The alternatives fail specifically.** A plain dict has no checkable field names, no repr beyond braces\n  and no methods. A tuple or `NamedTuple` is immutable and hashable but also indexable, so `chunk[1]`\n  compiles and position 1 changes meaning when you add a field. Hand-written dunders are correct until\n  the second person edits the class.\n- **One source of truth, ordinary class.** Methods are generated from the annotated field list, so they\n  cannot disagree with it. Generating code at class creation (not a dynamic `__getattr__` trick) keeps\n  fast attribute access, normal inheritance and nothing magic at call time.","how":"### What the decorator generates\n`@dataclass` reads the class's `__annotations__` in definition order, creates a field per annotated name,\nthen attaches methods:\n\n| Option | Generated behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| default | `__init__(self, a, b=default)` assigning each field; defaulted fields after non-defaulted ones (the function-parameter rule, for the same reason) |\n| default | `__repr__` printing `ClassName(a=..., b=...)`, which makes agent traces readable |\n| default | `__eq__` comparing field tuples, only when the other object is the same class |\n| `order=True` | `__lt__` and friends comparing field tuples in order |\n| `frozen=True` | `__setattr__` and `__delattr__` that raise `FrozenInstanceError` |\n| `slots=True` (3.10) | `__slots__`: no per-instance `__dict__`, less memory, faster attribute access, and assignment to undeclared attributes is rejected, catching typos like `cfg.temprature = 0.5` |\n| `kw_only=True` (3.10) | all fields keyword-only: add fields in any order, avoid positional mistakes on many same-typed fields |\n\n### When `__hash__` is generated\n| Settings | `__hash__` | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `frozen=True` with `eq=True` | hash of the field tuple | Fields cannot change, so the hash is stable |\n| `eq=True` alone | `__hash__ = None` (unhashable) | *Because* a mutable object whose hash changes would corrupt any set or dict holding it |\n| `unsafe_hash=True` | forced | You promise not to mutate |\n\n### Defaults and `field()`\n- **Mutable defaults are rejected.** `x: list[str] = []` raises `ValueError: mutable default ... use default_factory`,\n  because a class-level default is evaluated once and shared by every instance; the module checks\n  explicitly since the aliasing bug is otherwise silent.\n- **The check is a heuristic.** It rejects unhashable defaults (`list`, `dict`, `set`; before 3.11 exactly\n  those three). A mutable but hashable object, such as an instance of an ordinary class, passes and is\n  shared.\n- **`field(default_factory=list)`** calls the factory per instance.\n- **`field()` controls participation:** `repr=False` hides large payloads from logs, `compare=False`\n  excludes a timestamp from equality, `init=False` marks a derived field set in `__post_init__`.\n\n### Hooks and helpers\n| Helper | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `__post_init__` | Runs after the generated `__init__`; derived fields and cheap invariant checks (`if not 0 <= self.temperature <= 2: raise ValueError`) |\n| `InitVar[T]` | Init-only parameter passed to `__post_init__` but not stored |\n| `dataclasses.replace(obj, **changes)` | New instance with some fields changed; the idiom for \"modifying\" a frozen instance |\n| `asdict(obj)` | Recursive conversion to dicts and lists, deep-copying along the way: convenient for logging, expensive for large nested structures |\n| `fields(cls)` | Field metadata for generic code |\n| `match_args` (3.10) | Positional destructuring in `match` statements |\n\n### Inheritance\n1. **Fields are collected in reverse MRO order:** base-class fields first, in declaration order, then the\n   subclass's; a redeclared name keeps its original position.\n2. **The generated `__init__` is one flat parameter list in that order**, *which is why* a subclass cannot\n   add a required field after a base with a default: the signature would be\n   `(model, temperature=0.0, tenant)`, which Python rejects.\n3. **Fix:** `kw_only=True` on the subclass, or a `_: KW_ONLY` sentinel before the new fields, moves them\n   out of the positional sequence and the conflict disappears.\n\n### Frozen classes and derived fields\n- **The generated `__init__` bypasses the freeze** by setting fields through `object.__setattr__`.\n- **Your `__post_init__` does not get that privilege:** `self.n_tokens = ...` raises `FrozenInstanceError`.\n- **Fix:** a derived field (`n_tokens: int = field(init=False)`) is set with\n  `object.__setattr__(self, \"n_tokens\", ...)`, the same escape hatch the decorator uses.\n\n### Where it stops\n- **No type validation:** `GenConfig(temperature=\"hot\")` constructs happily.\n- **No JSON parsing, schemas or coercion.** Those are Pydantic's job, and Pydantic even offers\n  `pydantic.dataclasses.dataclass`, which adds validation to the stdlib form.\n- **The dividing line:** dataclasses for records *you* construct from already-trusted values; Pydantic for\n  data that arrives from outside.","example":"### An agent run trace: immutable steps, a budget, loop detection\nSteps are frozen records, the run owns a fresh list, a retry is a `replace` rather than a mutation, and\nthe hashable step fields make a repeated identical tool call visible. Passes `mypy --strict`.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field, replace\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True, kw_only=True)\nclass ToolStep:                                  # one immutable entry in the agent's trace\n    index: int\n    tool: str\n    args_json: str                               # canonical JSON text, so the step stays hashable\n    output: str = field(repr=False)              # can be huge: keep out of logs\n    input_tokens: int\n    output_tokens: int\n\n@dataclass(slots=True)\nclass Run:\n    task: str\n    max_steps: int = 8\n    steps: list[ToolStep] = field(default_factory=list)   # fresh list per run\n\n    def record(self, step: ToolStep) -> None:\n        if len(self.steps) >= self.max_steps:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"step budget exhausted\")\n        self.steps.append(step)\n\n    @property\n    def tokens(self) -> int:\n        return sum(s.input_tokens + s.output_tokens for s in self.steps)\n\nrun = Run(\"Find the refund policy\")\ns = ToolStep(index=0, tool=\"search\", args_json=json.dumps({\"q\": \"refund\"}, sort_keys=True),\n             output=\"...40 KB of HTML...\", input_tokens=1800, output_tokens=60)\nrun.record(s)\nrun.record(replace(s, index=1, input_tokens=2400))     # a retry: new record, original untouched\nprint(run.steps[0])            # ToolStep(index=0, tool='search', args_json='{\"q\": \"refund\"}', input_tokens=1800, output_tokens=60)\nprint(run.tokens)              # 4320\nseen = {(st.tool, st.args_json) for st in run.steps}\nprint(len(seen))               # 1: the same call twice, a loop signal\ntrace = asdict(run)            # deep copy, ready for json.dumps to a trace store\nprint(sorted(trace))           # ['max_steps', 'steps', 'task']\n```\n\n- **Why `args_json` and not `args: dict`:** a dict field would make the frozen step unhashable, so it\n  could not go in the `seen` set; canonical JSON text (`sort_keys=True`) keeps equal calls equal.\n- **The repr hides the 40 KB output**, so printing the step in a log line stays one line.\n\n### Frozen config as a cache key, mutable step record\nA frozen, slotted config works as a cache key; the step record gets a per-instance default list; `replace`\nproduces a varied config without touching the original.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field, replace, asdict\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)\nclass GenConfig:\n    model: str\n    temperature: float = 0.0\n    max_tokens: int = 256\n    stop: tuple[str, ...] = ()            # tuple, not list, so the instance stays hashable\n\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        if not 0.0 <= self.temperature <= 2.0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"temperature out of range: {self.temperature}\")\n\n@dataclass(slots=True)\nclass Step:\n    tool: str\n    args: dict[str, object]\n    started: float = field(default_factory=time.time)\n    output: str | None = field(default=None, repr=False)   # keep big payloads out of logs\n    notes: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)         # a fresh list per instance\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=1024)\ndef completion(prompt: str, cfg: GenConfig) -> str:        # cfg is hashable, so it can be a key\n    return f\"<{cfg.model}@{cfg.temperature}> {prompt[:24]}\"\n\nbase = GenConfig(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\")\nhot = replace(base, temperature=0.9)                       # new object; base unchanged\ncompletion(\"Summarise the KV cache\", base)\ncompletion(\"Summarise the KV cache\", GenConfig(\"gpt-4o-mini\"))\nprint(completion.cache_info().hits)                        # 1: equal configs are the same key\ntry:\n    base.temperature = 1.0\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(type(e).__name__)                                # FrozenInstanceError\n\ns1, s2 = Step(\"search\", {\"q\": \"a\"}), Step(\"search\", {\"q\": \"b\"})\ns1.notes.append(\"retried\"); print(s2.notes)                # []: default_factory did its job\nprint(s1)                                                  # Step(tool='search', args={'q': 'a'}, started=..., notes=['retried'])\nprint(asdict(s1)[\"notes\"] is s1.notes)                     # False: asdict deep-copies\n```\n\n- **Break it on purpose:** change `stop` to `list[str]` with a `default_factory` and `lru_cache` raises\n  `TypeError: unhashable type`, because a frozen dataclass's hash is built from its fields and a list\n  has none.\n\n### Derived field on a frozen class, plus a required subclass field\nThe `object.__setattr__` escape hatch sets `n_tokens`, and `KW_ONLY` lets a subclass add a required\n`tenant` after a defaulted base field. Passes `mypy --strict`.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import KW_ONLY, dataclass, field, fields\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Chunk:\n    text: str\n    score: float = 0.0\n    n_tokens: int = field(init=False)          # derived, not a constructor argument\n\n    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        # self.n_tokens = ... would raise FrozenInstanceError here\n        object.__setattr__(self, \"n_tokens\", len(self.text.split()))\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass TenantChunk(Chunk):\n    _: KW_ONLY\n    tenant: str                                # required, yet after a defaulted base field\n\nc = TenantChunk(\"kv cache stores keys and values\", 0.82, tenant=\"acme\")\nprint(c.n_tokens, c.tenant)                    # 6 acme\nprint([f.name for f in fields(c)])             # ['text', 'score', 'n_tokens', 'tenant']\nprint(c == TenantChunk(\"kv cache stores keys and values\", 0.82, tenant=\"acme\"))  # True\nprint(len({c, c}))                             # 1: frozen + eq gives a hash\n```","patterns":["**Immutable step records, append-only runs.** Record each model turn and tool call as a frozen dataclass appended to the run, never edited in place, so traces, replays and evals see exactly what happened. Key `(tool, canonical args)` into a counter per run to catch an agent repeating itself, and alert on runs that hit the step budget.","**Frozen configs as cache and dedup keys.** Model generation settings as `@dataclass(frozen=True)` with tuple-typed collections, and key `lru_cache` or a response cache on them. Monitor cache hit rate via `cache_info()`; a hit rate near zero often means a field like a timestamp should be `compare=False`.","**Slots for high-volume records.** Use `slots=True` on chunks, token events and trace spans created in large numbers. Verify the saving with `tracemalloc` snapshots before and after, because on 3.11+ the gain is smaller than older benchmarks suggest.","**`repr=False` on large or sensitive fields.** Raw documents, embeddings and prompt bodies stay out of logs and tracebacks. Watch log volume per request; a jump often means a new field leaked into a repr.","**Validate at the edge, dataclass inside.** Parse external input with Pydantic, then build internal dataclasses from the validated values. Keep only cheap invariant checks in `__post_init__`.","**`kw_only=True` on wide records.** Records with many same-typed fields (several `float` scores, several `str` IDs, input and output token counts) become keyword-only, so a reordered field never silently swaps two values."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A run is a sequence of records: each tool call, its arguments, output\n  and token cost. Frozen dataclasses make those records safe to share, hashable for loop detection and\n  caching, and readable in traces.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Your own step, run and config types; trace spans you export;\n  cache keys for LLM and tool calls. Frameworks mix styles (LangGraph state is often a `TypedDict` or\n  Pydantic model), so the line you draw is \"validated at the edge, dataclass inside\".\n- **What you control.** `frozen=True` for anything shared or used as a key, `default_factory` for\n  per-run lists, `repr=False` on huge tool outputs and prompts, `slots=True` on high-volume records.\n- **What you can skip.** `order=True`, `InitVar` and `match_args` details; you rarely need them in agent\n  code.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Step and run records.** A frozen `Step` holding tool name, arguments, output and token cost makes\n  each run a readable, shareable trace.\n- **Loop detection and caching.** Hashable frozen records of (tool, arguments) let the loop spot a\n  repeated call and serve cached tool results.\n- **Run budgets and generation configs.** Small dataclasses carry max steps, token limits, model and\n  temperature through the loop with one readable repr.\n- **Retrieved chunks.** A `RetrievedChunk` with text, score and source moves between retrieval, reranking\n  and prompt assembly with checkable field names.","mini_project":"**Trace an agent run with frozen records (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** record every step of a tool-calling loop as dataclasses and use them to stop loops.\n- **Steps:** define a frozen, slotted `Step` with `repr=False` on the tool output and a `Run` with a\n  `default_factory` list of steps and a budget; append a `Step` per tool call; keep a\n  `collections.Counter` of (tool, canonical argument JSON) keys and stop when any key reaches three;\n  export the run as JSON.\n- **Done when:** a scripted run that issues the same call over and over stops on its third occurrence,\n  the repr stays short with large outputs, and the exported trace lists every step with its cost."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"class body with annotated fields\"] --> D[\"@dataclass reads __annotations__ in order\"]\n    D --> MD{\"Mutable default such as a list?\"}\n    MD -->|\"yes\"| ERR[\"ValueError: use default_factory\"]\n    MD -->|\"no\"| G[\"Generate __init__, __repr__, __eq__\"]\n    G --> O[\"Options: order, slots, kw_only add methods or __slots__\"]\n    O --> H{\"frozen and eq?\"}\n    H -->|\"frozen=True, eq=True\"| HS[\"__hash__ from field tuple: usable as a key\"]\n    H -->|\"eq=True only\"| HN[\"__hash__ = None: unhashable\"]\n    H -->|\"unsafe_hash=True\"| HF[\"Hash forced: you promise not to mutate\"]\n    HS -.->|\"a list field inside\"| TE[\"TypeError: unhashable type at hash time\"]","caption":"What happens once, at class creation: the decorator reads the annotated fields and generates methods,\nand the `eq` and `frozen` settings decide whether instances can be hashed (and so used as LLM cache\nkeys or loop-detection entries for agent steps)."},"interview":[{"q":"What does `@dataclass` actually do, and why generate code instead of using a dynamic base class?","a":"- **It generates methods at class creation** (`__init__`, `__repr__`, `__eq__`, optionally ordering,\n  hashing and `__slots__`) from the annotated field list, so they cannot disagree with it.\n- **Why generation:** the result is an ordinary class with fast attribute access, normal inheritance and\n  nothing magic at call time, unlike a `__getattr__`-style trick."},{"q":"One agent step's notes are appearing on every other step. How do you find the cause?","a":"- **Suspect a shared default.** The dataclass check rejects `list`, `dict` and `set` defaults, but a\n  hashable object of an ordinary class (`field(default=RetryPolicy())`) or an unannotated base-class\n  attribute is silently shared.\n- **Confirm** with `a.notes is b.notes` on two fresh instances.\n- **Fix:** `field(default_factory=...)` so each instance gets its own object."},{"q":"How would you model an agent's run history so it supports tracing, replay and loop detection?","a":"- **Frozen step records** (tool, canonical argument JSON, output, token counts) appended to a run that\n  owns its list via `default_factory`, so nothing rewrites history after the fact.\n- **Hashability buys loop detection:** a set or counter of `(tool, args)` per run flags the same call\n  repeating, and the step budget is enforced in one `record` method.\n- **Keep big fields out of reprs** with `repr=False`, and validate model output with Pydantic before it\n  becomes a step, because the dataclass will store whatever it is given."},{"q":"When would you not use a dataclass?","a":"- **At a trust boundary:** it validates and coerces nothing, so `GenConfig(temperature=\"hot\")`\n  constructs happily; use Pydantic for external input or when you need a schema.\n- **When tuple unpacking is the interface:** a `NamedTuple` fits that better.\n- **When a library relies on `vars(obj)`** or stashes attributes on objects, `slots=True` breaks it."},{"q":"Your pipeline creates millions of chunk records and memory is high. What would you try, and how would you check it helped?","a":"- **Try `slots=True`:** it removes the per-instance `__dict__`, which can be a large share of a small\n  record's footprint, and rejects typo attributes as a bonus.\n- **Measure, do not assume:** compare `tracemalloc` snapshots before and after, because on 3.11+ instance\n  dicts are stored more compactly and the saving is smaller than older benchmarks suggest.\n- **Also check** whether `asdict` is deep-copying large trees on a hot path."},{"q":"A subclass adds a required `tenant` field and the module fails at import. Why, and what is the fix?","a":"- **Why:** fields are collected base first, and the generated `__init__` is one flat parameter list, so a\n  required field after a defaulted base field gives `non-default argument follows default argument`.\n- **Fix:** `kw_only=True` on the subclass, or a `_: KW_ONLY` sentinel before the new fields, which moves\n  them out of the positional sequence."}],"tradeoffs":["**Free dunders vs no validation.** Methods are correct and consistent, but once input comes from outside the process, a Pydantic model is the right tool despite its extra weight.","**`frozen=True`: safe sharing vs allocation.** Hashability and safe sharing across threads and cache keys, but every update allocates a new object via `replace`, which matters only in tight loops over large records.","**`slots=True`: memory and typo safety vs no `__dict__`.** Libraries that stash attributes on objects or rely on `vars(obj)` break.","**`order=True`: free sorting vs wrong ordering.** It compares fields positionally in declaration order, rarely the ordering you mean for a domain object.","**`asdict`: convenience vs deep copy.** It copies the whole tree; for large nested records a hand-written `to_dict` (or `vars(obj)`, on non-slotted classes) is cheaper."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting the mutable-default check completely.** `field(default=[])` is rejected like `= []`, but `field(default=RetryPolicy())` (a hashable object of an ordinary class) or an unannotated base-class attribute is silently shared. Symptom: one instance's changes appear in every other instance.","**Hand-written `__eq__` or `eq=True` on a mutable class used as dict keys.** Symptom: `TypeError: unhashable type` or, with `unsafe_hash`, items vanishing from sets after mutation.","**Required field after a defaulted one in a subclass.** Symptom: `TypeError: non-default argument follows default argument` at import, fixed by `kw_only=True`.","**Assigning a derived field in a frozen class's `__post_init__`.** `FrozenInstanceError` from inside the constructor looks like the object was frozen too early. Use `object.__setattr__` for that assignment.","**Expecting coercion.** `GenConfig(temperature=\"0.7\")` stores the string, and the error appears later as a `TypeError` in arithmetic. Validate in `__post_init__` or use Pydantic.","**A list inside a frozen dataclass.** The reference is frozen but the list is not, so hashing fails or, worse, the \"immutable\" config changes."],"when":"- **Use for trusted in-process records:** configs, intermediate results, agent steps, cache keys, test\n  fixtures. Day to day, the default container for anything with more than two fields.\n- **Prefer `frozen=True`** for anything shared across tasks or used as a key, and `slots=True` for anything\n  created in large numbers (retrieved chunks, token events).\n- **Switch away** to Pydantic when data crosses a trust boundary or needs a schema, and to `NamedTuple`\n  only when tuple unpacking is genuinely the interface.\n- **Measure** memory with `tracemalloc` when instance counts reach the millions. Slots remove the\n  per-instance `__dict__`, which can be a large share of a small record's footprint, though the saving is\n  smaller on 3.11+ where instance dicts are stored more compactly, so measure rather than assume.\n- **Interviews:** explain `default_factory`, when `__hash__` is generated and why, and the line between\n  dataclasses and Pydantic.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Models generation configs and agent steps as frozen, slotted dataclasses so they are hashable cache keys with readable reprs in traces.","architect":"Sets the rule that internal records are dataclasses and boundary types are Pydantic, so validation cost and schema generation stay where they belong.","fde":"Turns a customer's ad-hoc dicts into dataclasses during an integration, giving their pipeline checked field names and reprs that make the first incident debuggable."},"check":{"q":"You want to memoize LLM calls keyed on the prompt plus a configuration object holding the\nmodel, temperature and a list of stop sequences. The config is a mutable dataclass and `lru_cache`\nraises `TypeError: unhashable type`. Explain why and how you would fix it.","a":"A dataclass with `eq=True` (the default) and `frozen=False` sets `__hash__ = None`, because\na hashable object must not change its hash, and a mutable record can. Even with `frozen=True`, the\n`list` of stop sequences is unhashable, so the generated hash, which hashes the field tuple, fails.\nThe fix is `@dataclass(frozen=True)` with `stop: tuple[str, ...]` instead of a list, which gives a\nstable hash and lets `replace` produce variants. If the config really must be mutable, derive an\nexplicit immutable key (a tuple of the fields) and cache on that instead."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `@dataclass` reject `tags: list[str] = []` with a ValueError?","options":["Lists cannot be dataclass fields","The default would be evaluated once and shared by every instance, so the module demands `default_factory`","Empty lists are falsy and would break `__eq__`","Type hints for lists require `List[str]` from `typing`"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A class-level default is a single object. Sharing it across instances is the classic aliasing bug, so dataclasses raise at definition time and `field(default_factory=list)` creates one per instance."},{"q":"Under which setting does `@dataclass` generate a usable `__hash__`?","options":["`eq=True` alone","`order=True` alone","`frozen=True` together with `eq=True`","`slots=True` alone"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hashing requires the hash to be stable, which a mutable object cannot guarantee; `eq=True` without `frozen` therefore sets `__hash__ = None`. Frozen plus eq hashes the field tuple safely."},{"q":"What is the main thing a dataclass does not do that Pydantic does?","options":["Generate a readable `__repr__`","Support default values","Validate and coerce field values at construction","Support inheritance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Dataclasses generate methods from annotations but never check them; `Config(temperature=\"hot\")` succeeds. Pydantic validates, coerces and reports structured errors, which is why it owns boundaries."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/object-model-and-mutability","sources":["Official docs: The Python Language Reference, Data model","Official docs: copy module"],"why":"- **Four bugs, one fact.** `def ask(question, history=[])` passes its unit test, then every production\n  user sees everyone's messages. A tenant's `.copy()` of a prompt template still shares the inner `tools`\n  list. Normalising an embedding slice changes the original rows. An in-place tensor `add_` makes\n  autograd raise on the backward pass.\n- **They are not library bugs.** Python passes and stores *references to objects*, and mutating through\n  any reference is visible through all of them.\n- **The C-like intuition is wrong.** \"A variable holds a value and assignment copies it\" fails here, and\n  nothing in the syntax warns you: `a = b` is indistinguishable from a copy until someone mutates.\n- **Defensive copying is not the answer.** It costs memory and time AI workloads cannot spare (copying a\n  gigabyte of embeddings per request) and still fails, because shallow copies share their contents.\n- **The real defence is a correct model.** Know what an object is, which names point at it, which\n  operations create versus change objects, and prefer immutable values for shared data. That also makes\n  threads and async tasks tractable, because immutable data cannot race.","how":"### Objects, names and identity\n- **Every value is an object** with an identity (`id()`, its address in CPython), a type and a value.\n- **Namespaces map names to objects:** module globals, function locals, instance `__dict__`.\n- **Binding never copies.** `x = expr` binds `x` to the object `expr` produced; `y = x` binds a second name\n  to the *same* object, so `x is y` is `True`. Binding is O(1) regardless of what is bound, by design.\n- **`==` versus `is`.** `==` asks for equal values; `is` asks for the same object. Use `is` only for\n  singletons like `None`: CPython caches small integers and interns some strings, so `is` on numbers or\n  text depends on implementation details.\n\n### Functions and classes are objects too\n- **A function stores its defaults** in `f.__defaults__`, built when `def` executes.\n- **A class stores its body's assignments** in its own `__dict__`.\n- **Neither is re-created** per call or per instance, so anything mutable stored there is shared by\n  construction. Several \"quirks\" are this one rule.\n\n### Attribute lookup: where `obj.x` comes from\n1. **Reading `obj.x`:** data descriptors on the type (a `property`, a slot), then the instance's own\n   `__dict__`, then the class and its bases in MRO order (where plain functions become bound methods).\n2. **Writing `obj.x = v`:** unless a descriptor intercepts it, it always stores into the instance\n   `__dict__`.\n3. **That asymmetry is the class-attribute bug.** With `class Agent: history = []`,\n   `self.history.append(m)` *reads* `history`, finds the class's single list, and mutates it for every\n   agent. `self.history = [m]` would create a private instance attribute that shadows the class one.\n   Same-looking code, opposite sharing.\n\n### Operators are method calls on the type\n| Syntax | Calls |\n|---|---|\n| `a + b` | `type(a).__add__(a, b)` |\n| `a[i] = v` | `__setitem__` |\n| `a += b` | `__iadd__` if the type defines it, otherwise `a = a + b` |\n\nWhich one runs is a property of the type, not the syntax; the next section is that rule applied.\n\n### Mutable versus immutable\n| | Immutable | Mutable |\n|---|---|---|\n| Types | `int`, `float`, `str`, `bytes`, `tuple`, `frozenset`, `bool`, `None` | `list`, `dict`, `set`, `bytearray`, user-defined class instances, NumPy arrays, PyTorch tensors |\n| Operations | Always yield a new object | Change in place |\n| `+=` | `s += \"x\"` *rebinds* `s`; other names still see the old string | `lst += [1]` calls `__iadd__`, extending the existing list; every alias sees it |\n\n- **Same spelling, opposite effect**, so the distinction has to live in your head, not the syntax.\n- **Mutating methods return `None`** by convention (`list.sort`, `dict.update`, `random.shuffle`), precisely\n  so `x = lst.sort()` fails loudly rather than letting you believe you got a copy.\n\n### Function arguments\n- **Passed by assignment.** A parameter is a new local name bound to the caller's object.\n- **Mutating it** (`items.append`) is visible to the caller; **rebinding it** (`items = []`) is not.\n- **Defaults are evaluated once,** at `def` time, and stored on the function object. A mutable default is\n  therefore shared by all calls; the idiom is `None` with an in-body `if x is None: x = []`.\n\n### Containers hold references\n- **Multiplied lists alias.** `[[0] * 3] * 3` holds three references to one inner list; changing\n  `grid[0][0]` changes every row.\n- **Shallow copies** (`list(x)`, `x[:]`, `dict.copy()`, `copy.copy`) make a new outer container referencing\n  the *same* inner objects.\n- **`copy.deepcopy` recurses** at O(size) cost, and fails on objects that cannot be copied or pickled, such\n  as locks and sockets, and therefore on most SDK clients that hold them.\n- **Class attributes are shared by all instances.** `class Agent: history = []` is one list for every\n  agent; the per-instance version must be created in `__init__`.\n\n### Hashing and equality\n- **Keys must be hashable,** and equal objects must hash equally. Mutable built-ins are unhashable for that\n  reason.\n- **User classes** are hashable by identity, unless they define `__eq__` without `__hash__`, which makes\n  them unhashable; `@dataclass` applies the same rule.\n- **Mutating a key** after inserting it (through a custom `__hash__`) makes it unfindable.\n\n### Arrays and tensors\n- **NumPy basic slicing returns a view.** `a[:, :10]` and `a[::2]` share the buffer, so normalising the\n  slice normalises the original; `a.copy()` or fancy indexing returns an independent array.\n- **PyTorch marks in-place methods with a trailing underscore** (`t.add_()`), and autograd refuses in-place\n  changes to values it still needs for the backward pass.\n- **The trade is explicit:** in-place operations exist to avoid allocating gigabytes, so it is memory\n  versus aliasing risk.\n\n### Lifetime\n- **CPython frees an object when its reference count reaches zero;** cycles are collected later by the\n  garbage collector.\n- **A large tensor stays resident** while any name, container, closure, cached traceback or notebook\n  output history references it, which is why `del x` sometimes frees nothing.","example":"### Forked agent branches that share message dicts\nTwo branches of one run start from the same history. Branch A compacts long tool output in place for a\ncheaper model, and branch B silently loses the full text, because `list(history)` copied only the outer\nlist. The fix returns new dicts only where something changes.\n\n```python\ndef compact(messages: list[dict[str, str]], limit: int = 20) -> None:\n    for m in messages:                                  # BUG: edits the dicts in place\n        if m[\"role\"] == \"tool\" and len(m[\"content\"]) > limit:\n            m[\"content\"] = m[\"content\"][:limit] + \"...[truncated]\"\n\ndef fresh_history() -> list[dict[str, str]]:\n    return [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise ticket 4521\"},\n            {\"role\": \"tool\", \"content\": \"full ticket text \" * 10}]   # 170 characters\n\nhistory = fresh_history()\nbranch_a = list(history)                    # shallow: new list, SAME message dicts\nbranch_b = list(history)\ncompact(branch_a)                           # branch A compacts for a cheaper model\nprint(len(branch_b[1][\"content\"]))          # 34: branch B lost the full tool output too\nprint(history[1] is branch_b[1])            # True\n\ndef compacted(messages: list[dict[str, str]], limit: int = 20) -> list[dict[str, str]]:\n    return [{**m, \"content\": m[\"content\"][:limit] + \"...[truncated]\"}\n            if m[\"role\"] == \"tool\" and len(m[\"content\"]) > limit else m\n            for m in messages]               # new dicts only where something changes\n\nhistory = fresh_history()\nbranch_a = compacted(history)\nprint(len(history[1][\"content\"]), len(branch_a[1][\"content\"]))   # 170 34\n```\n\n| Branching approach | Cost per fork | Safe if a branch edits a message? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `branch = history` | nothing | no: one list, one set of dicts |\n| `list(history)` or `history[:]` | one list of references | no: dicts shared |\n| `copy.deepcopy(history)` | O(size), every fork | yes, but slow for long transcripts |\n| Treat messages as immutable, return new ones | only what changes | yes, and cheap |\n\n### Four aliasing bugs from real LLM code, with fixes\nA shared default leaks history between callers, a shallow-copied template leaks tools between tenants,\n`+=` differs for lists and strings, and nested-list multiplication aliases rows.\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\ndef ask(question: str, history: list[dict] = []) -> list[dict]:   # BUG: one list for every call\n    history.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question})\n    return history\n\nprint(len(ask(\"a\")), len(ask(\"b\")))              # 1 2  -> the second caller sees the first turn\n\ndef ask_ok(question: str, history: list[dict] | None = None) -> list[dict]:\n    history = [] if history is None else history  # fresh list per call unless one is passed\n    return [*history, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n\nBASE = {\"system\": \"You are a support bot.\", \"tools\": [\"search\"]}\n\ndef per_tenant(tenant: str) -> dict:\n    cfg = BASE.copy()                            # shallow: cfg[\"tools\"] IS BASE[\"tools\"]\n    cfg[\"tools\"].append(f\"{tenant}_crm\")         # leaks into every later tenant\n    return cfg\n\nper_tenant(\"acme\"); print(BASE[\"tools\"])        # ['search', 'acme_crm']\n\ndef per_tenant_ok(tenant: str) -> dict:\n    cfg = copy.deepcopy(BASE)                    # or rebuild; or store BASE as tuples\n    cfg[\"tools\"] = [*cfg[\"tools\"], f\"{tenant}_crm\"]\n    return cfg\n\na = [1, 2]; b = a; c = a[:]\nb += [3]                                        # list.__iadd__ mutates the shared list in place\nprint(a is b, a, c)                             # True [1, 2, 3] [1, 2]  (c is a shallow copy)\ns = \"x\"; t = s; s += \"y\"                        # str is immutable: += rebinds s only\nprint(t)                                        # x\ngrid = [[0] * 2] * 2; grid[0][0] = 9            # both rows are the same inner list\nprint(grid, [[0] * 2 for _ in range(2)])        # [[9, 0], [9, 0]] [[0, 0], [0, 0]]\n```\n\n### Class-attribute leak, read-only template, and the tuple `+=` puzzle\nThe class list is shared by every agent; a `MappingProxyType` template makes the tenant leak impossible\nrather than avoided; and `t[0] += [2]` both raises and mutates.\n\n```python\nfrom types import MappingProxyType\n\nclass Agent:\n    history: list[str] = []                     # BUG: one list on the class object\n\n    def remember(self, m: str) -> None:\n        self.history.append(m)                  # reads the class list, mutates it for everyone\n\nclass AgentOk:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.history: list[str] = []            # one list per instance\n\na, b = Agent(), Agent()\na.remember(\"hi from a\")\nprint(b.history)                                # ['hi from a']\nprint(AgentOk().history is AgentOk().history)   # False\n\nDEFAULTS = MappingProxyType({\"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\", \"headers\": MappingProxyType({\"X-App\": \"bot\"})})\ntry:\n    DEFAULTS[\"headers\"][\"X-Tenant\"] = \"acme\"\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(\"blocked:\", e)                        # blocked: 'mappingproxy' object does not support item assignment\nheaders = {**DEFAULTS[\"headers\"], \"X-Tenant\": \"acme\"}   # new small dict per request\nprint(headers, dict(DEFAULTS[\"headers\"]))       # {'X-App': 'bot', 'X-Tenant': 'acme'} {'X-App': 'bot'}\n\nt = ([1],)\ntry:\n    t[0] += [2]                                 # list.__iadd__ runs, then tuple item store fails\nexcept TypeError:\n    pass\nprint(t)                                        # ([1, 2],)\n```\n\n- **Why the proxy is nested:** freezing is shallow, so the inner `headers` dict needs its own\n  `MappingProxyType`, or it stays writable.","patterns":["**Append-only transcripts, new messages for edits.** Treat each message in an agent's history as immutable: compaction, redaction and truncation return new lists and new dicts, and graph nodes return state updates instead of mutating the state passed in. A test that forks a run, edits one branch and asserts the other is byte-identical catches regressions.","**Lint the two classic leaks.** Enable Ruff's flake8-bugbear rule `B006` (mutable argument defaults) and `RUF012` (mutable class attributes without `ClassVar`) in CI. Track the violation count to zero, then keep it blocking.","**Immutable shared templates, per-request construction.** Store system prompts, tool lists and default headers as tuples, frozen dataclasses or `MappingProxyType`, and build each request's mutable pieces with `{**base, ...}` or `[*base, ...]`. A cross-tenant test that runs two tenants back to back and asserts no overlap catches regressions.","**Factories, not `deepcopy`, for objects holding clients.** Construct per-request state from a function instead of deep-copying a prototype that may hold a lock, socket or SDK client.","**Explicit copies around array views.** In embedding pipelines, write `emb[idx].copy()` or out-of-place expressions before normalising, and document any deliberate in-place op. Add a test that checksums the source matrix before and after a retrieval call.","**Hunt retained memory by references.** When memory does not return after `del` (a GPU tensor during fine-tuning, or finished agent runs kept alive by a cache or callback), clear notebook output history and stored exceptions, and inspect holders with `gc.get_referrers(obj)`. Monitor allocated memory per step (for example `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()`) across a long run."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent's state is a pile of mutable lists and dicts: the transcript,\n  tool results, scratchpad, per-tenant tool lists. Forking a run, compacting history, running branches\n  in parallel or sharing a template across users all go wrong silently if two names point at one object.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Message lists you pass to SDKs, graph state handed to each node\n  (LangGraph nodes return updates rather than editing the state they receive), cached tool results,\n  module-level system prompts and tool registries, and in-place tensor ops when you fine-tune.\n- **What you control.** Build per-run and per-request state fresh, keep shared templates immutable,\n  return new messages instead of editing old ones, and lint mutable defaults and class attributes.\n- **What you can skip.** CPython's small-int cache and interning rules beyond \"never use `is` on values\";\n  descriptor internals beyond the lookup order below.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Forking agent runs.** Branching a run to try two plans needs its own message list and must never\n  edit the shared message dicts in place, or one branch's compaction or tool results leak into the other.\n- **Multi-tenant prompt templates.** A shared system prompt and tool list must stay immutable, or one\n  tenant's edit changes every other tenant's agent.\n- **History compaction.** Summarising old turns should build a new list, so traces and checkpoints keep\n  the original transcript.\n- **Graph nodes returning updates.** Nodes that return new values instead of editing received state keep\n  parallel branches and checkpoints consistent.","mini_project":"**Find and fix shared-state bugs in an agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** reproduce the mutable-default and shallow-copy bugs in a small agent, then fix them.\n- **Steps:** write an `ask` function with a `history=[]` default and a template dict holding a `tools`\n  list; run two simulated users and show their histories merge; give one tenant a `.copy()` of the\n  template and edit its tools; fix with `None` defaults, tuples or frozen records and `copy.deepcopy`\n  where needed.\n- **Done when:** a test with two users and two tenants fails before the fix, passes after it, and an `is`\n  check proves no list is shared between runs."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"name DEFAULTS\"] --> OD[\"dict object: outer defaults\"]\n    A[\"name cfg in request A\"] -->|\"DEFAULTS.copy()\"| CA[\"new outer dict A\"]\n    B[\"name cfg in request B\"] -->|\"DEFAULTS.copy()\"| CB[\"new outer dict B\"]\n    OD -->|\"key headers\"| H[\"ONE shared headers dict\"]\n    CA -->|\"key headers\"| H\n    CB -->|\"key headers\"| H\n    H -.->|\"A writes X-Tenant acme, B writes X-Tenant beta\"| L[\"Last write wins for both requests\"]\n    L --> F[\"Fix: build per request\"]\n    F --> N[\"new dict from DEFAULTS headers plus X-Tenant\"]","caption":"Names point at objects; a shallow copy of a plain-dict `DEFAULTS` makes a new outer dict whose entries\nstill point at the same inner objects. Follow both requests' edges to the one shared `headers` dict:\nthat is the tenant leak, and the bottom box is the fix used in the third example."},"interview":[{"q":"What does assignment do in Python, and why does that matter more in AI services than in scripts?","a":"- **Assignment binds a name to an object; it never copies.** `y = x` makes two names for one object, so a\n  mutation through either is visible through both.\n- **Why it matters in services:** templates, defaults and caches are shared across requests and tenants,\n  so one in-place mutation can leak one customer's context into another's prompt."},{"q":"Users report seeing other users' chat turns, but only in production. Where do you look first?","a":"- **Shared mutable state that outlives a request:** a mutable default argument (`history=[]`), a mutable\n  class attribute (`class Agent: history = []`), or a module-level template mutated in place.\n- **Why only in production:** a single test call never sees the second caller; concurrency and long-lived\n  processes do.\n- **Fix and guard:** `None` default with in-body construction, per-instance state in `__init__`, and\n  Ruff's `B006` and `RUF012` in CI."},{"q":"Your agent forks into three parallel branches from one history, and one branch's compaction shows up in the others. Explain and fix.","a":"- **The fork was shallow.** `list(history)` gives each branch its own list, but every element is the same\n  message dict, so editing `m[\"content\"]` in one branch edits it for all three and for the stored trace.\n- **Fix without deep copies:** treat messages as immutable values; compaction returns\n  `[{**m, \"content\": short} ...]` for the changed ones and reuses the rest, so forking costs one list.\n- **Guard it** with a fork-and-edit test, and freeze shared prefixes (system prompt, tool schemas) in\n  tuples or `MappingProxyType`."},{"q":"When would you choose `copy.deepcopy` over making the shared data immutable?","a":"- **Rarely.** `deepcopy` is O(size) per call and fails on objects holding locks, sockets or SDK clients.\n- **Immutable shared data plus small per-request construction** (`{**base, ...}`) costs only the part that\n  changes and makes the leak impossible rather than avoided.\n- **`deepcopy` fits** when you receive a nested mutable structure you do not own and must isolate it once."},{"q":"After `del big_tensor` your GPU memory does not drop. What is going on?","a":"- **CPython frees an object only when its reference count reaches zero.** Another holder (a list, a\n  closure, a cached traceback, a notebook `Out[]` entry) still references it.\n- **Find the holder** with `gc.get_referrers(obj)`, clear stored exceptions and output history, and\n  watch allocated memory per step to confirm the release."}],"tradeoffs":["**Immutable sharing vs allocation.** Tuples, frozen dataclasses and `MappingProxyType` are safe to share across tenants, threads and tasks and usable as cache keys, but every change allocates; for hot loops over large state, controlled in-place mutation wins.","**In-place arrays vs aliasing.** In-place array and tensor ops avoid copying gigabytes, at the cost of aliasing and autograd hazards; when memory is not the constraint, out-of-place ops are easier to reason about.","**`deepcopy` isolation vs cost.** It guarantees isolation but is O(size), can be slower than rebuilding the object, and fails on unpicklable members such as clients and locks; rebuild from a factory instead.","**Shallow freezing vs real protection.** A tuple of lists still has mutable lists inside, so protection must be applied at every level or the invariant is only cosmetic."],"pitfalls":["**A mutable default argument.** Symptom: state leaking between calls, and tests that pass in isolation but fail when run together.","**A mutable class attribute as per-instance state.** Every `Agent` shares one `history`; symptom: cross-conversation contamination.","**Shallow-copying a nested config.** Symptom: tenant A's tools or headers appear in tenant B's requests.","**Editing a message in place to compact or redact history.** Symptom: a forked branch, a parallel sub-agent or the stored trace shows the edited text, and a replay no longer matches what the model saw. Fix: return new message dicts.","**`x = lst.sort()` or `cfg = d.update(...)`.** Symptom: `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute`, because mutating methods return `None` by convention.","**Mutating a list or dict while iterating it.** Lists skip elements silently; dicts raise `RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration`.","**Normalising a NumPy slice or tensor view in place.** The source matrix changes; symptom: retrieval scores that drift between runs.","**GPU memory that does not return after `del`.** Another reference (a list, an exception traceback, a notebook `Out[]` entry) still holds the tensor.","**`t[0] += [x]` on a tuple holding a list.** The list's `__iadd__` mutates in place, then the store back into the tuple raises `TypeError`: an exception *and* a changed list, which looks impossible until you split `+=` into its two steps.","**`is` to compare strings or integers.** It works in tests because of interning and fails on values built at runtime."],"when":"- **Always on:** this is not a tool you choose; it is the model you need every time a value is shared.\n- **Immutability by default** for anything passed between layers, stored in a cache, or used by more than\n  one task: tuples for fixed sequences, frozen dataclasses for configs, `None` plus in-body construction\n  for defaults.\n- **Deliberate mutation** when memory or speed demands it, contained inside one function with an explicit\n  docstring note.\n- **Measure:** no mutable defaults in the codebase (linters flag them), no class-level mutable attributes,\n  copies at every tenant boundary.\n- **Interviews:** \"what does this print\" questions about `+=`, default arguments and nested list\n  multiplication are the standard probe of this model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds per-request message lists from scratch, keeps shared prompt templates immutable, and uses views and in-place tensor ops only where the memory saving is measured.","architect":"Sets the rule that cross-tenant and cross-request state is immutable or copied at the boundary, and designs caches around hashable, frozen keys.","fde":"Diagnoses \"customer B saw customer A's context\" incidents by tracing which shared object was mutated, usually a template or default argument in a customisation layer."},"check":{"q":"A multi-tenant service builds each request's config with `cfg = DEFAULTS.copy()` then\n`cfg[\"headers\"][\"X-Tenant\"] = tenant`. Under load, requests intermittently carry the wrong tenant\nheader. Explain the mechanism and give two fixes with their costs.","a":"`dict.copy()` is shallow: the new `cfg` is a different outer dict, but `cfg[\"headers\"]` is the\nvery same inner dict object that `DEFAULTS[\"headers\"]` references, so every request writes its\ntenant into one shared dict. With concurrency, request B's write lands between A's write and A's\nHTTP call, so A sends B's header. Fix one: `copy.deepcopy(DEFAULTS)` per request, which isolates\neverything at O(size) cost per call and breaks if DEFAULTS ever holds a client or lock. Fix two,\nusually better: make DEFAULTS immutable (tuples, `MappingProxyType`, a frozen dataclass) and build the\nper-request headers as a new dict `{**DEFAULTS[\"headers\"], \"X-Tenant\": tenant}`, which costs only the\nsmall structure that actually changes and makes the sharing impossible rather than merely avoided."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `def f(x, acc=[]): acc.append(x); return acc` return on the second call `f(2)` after `f(1)`?","options":["`[2]`, because the default is re-evaluated on every call","`[1, 2]`, because the default list was created once at definition time and is shared","A `TypeError`, because mutable defaults are rejected","`[1, 2]` only if both calls happen in the same thread"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Default values are evaluated once when `def` executes and stored on the function object. Every call that omits `acc` mutates that one list, which is why `None` plus in-body creation is the idiom."},{"q":"Why does `s += \"a\"` leave other names bound to the old string unchanged, while `lst += [1]` changes every alias of the list?","options":["Strings are copied on assignment and lists are not","The `+=` operator is only defined for mutable types","`str` is immutable so `+=` rebinds the name to a new object, whereas `list.__iadd__` extends the existing object in place","Lists are stored by reference and strings by value in the interpreter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Same spelling, different semantics: immutable types have no in-place operator, so a new object is created and only the assigned name moves. Mutable types implement in-place augmentation and all references observe it."},{"q":"After `row = emb[0]; row /= row.sum()` with a float32 NumPy embedding matrix `emb`, what happened to `emb`?","options":["Nothing; indexing returns an independent copy of the row","Its first row was modified, because basic indexing returns a view sharing the same buffer","It was converted to a float array but its values are unchanged","A `ValueError` was raised because views cannot be modified in place"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Basic slicing and indexing in NumPy return views, so an in-place operation on the view writes into the original buffer. `emb[0].copy()` or an out-of-place expression avoids the side effect."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/exceptions-and-custom-exception-hierarchies","sources":["Official docs: Python Language Reference and Library Reference, Built-in Exceptions","Official docs: Python Tutorial, Errors and Exceptions","Katriel, Selivanov, van Rossum, 2021 — PEP 654: Exception Groups and except*"],"why":"- **One LLM call, a dozen failure modes, different fixes.**\n\n| Failure | Correct response |\n|---|---|\n| Rate limit | Wait and retry |\n| Timeout | Retry, maybe with a smaller request |\n| Context-length error | Shrink the input; never a plain retry |\n| Output fails schema validation | Corrective re-prompt |\n| Authentication error or content-policy refusal | No retry; a human |\n| `KeyError` in your own parsing code | A bug fix, not a retry loop that quietly triples your bill |\n| A tool reports bad arguments or \"not found\" | Return it to the model as an error tool result, so it can fix the call or re-plan |\n\n- **`except Exception: retry()` is wrong.** If all of these arrive as \"an exception\", the handler can\n  only pick one policy. It retries the unretryable, including your own bugs, and hides them behind\n  success-after-retry statistics.\n- **Return codes, `None` and message matching are worse.** Codes and `None` get ignored by the first\n  caller that forgets to check, lose the stack trace, and spread `if result is None` everywhere.\n  `if \"rate limit\" in str(e)` couples logic to vendor wording and breaks on the next SDK release.\n- **Vendor classes everywhere couple you to one provider.** Switching or adding a fallback model means\n  touching every call site.\n- **Hierarchies fix this because `except` matches a class *and all subclasses*.** Inner nodes are policies\n  (`Transient`, `Permanent`); leaves carry data (`RateLimited.retry_after`, `ContextTooLong.limit`). Each\n  layer catches what it can act on, and translating vendor errors once, at the adapter, keeps the rest\n  vendor-neutral.","how":"### Raising, unwinding and catching\n1. **`raise X(...)`** creates an instance, attaches a traceback, and unwinds frame by frame until a `try`\n   with a matching `except` is found.\n2. **Matching uses `isinstance`,** so `except Transient` catches every subclass.\n3. **Handlers are tested in order,** which is why a subclass handler must come before its base or it is\n   unreachable.\n4. **`else`** runs when no exception occurred; **`finally`** always runs; a **bare `raise`** in a handler\n   re-raises the current exception with its original traceback intact.\n5. **Cost:** since Python 3.11 a `try` block costs nothing when nothing is raised; raising and unwinding\n   still cost, so exceptions are for failures, not routine control flow in hot loops.\n\n### The built-in tree and what `except Exception` means\n- **Everything derives from `BaseException`;** `Exception` is the branch for errors a program may recover\n  from.\n- **`KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, `GeneratorExit` and `asyncio.CancelledError` hang directly off\n  `BaseException`** *so that* `except Exception` does not swallow them: an \"any error\" handler must not\n  stop Ctrl-C, interpreter shutdown or task cancellation.\n- **Catching `BaseException` or writing a bare `except:`** breaks exactly those mechanisms.\n\n### Designing the hierarchy\n```\nLLMError(Exception)              one base per subsystem: \"anything from the LLM layer\"\n├── Transient                    safe to retry\n│   └── RateLimited              .retry_after seconds\n├── Permanent                    do not retry\n└── Recoverable                  optional: retry only after changing something\n    └── ContextTooLong           .tokens, .limit\n```\n\n- **Split by handling policy, not by source.**\n- **Agents add a model-visible branch.** A `ToolError(AgentError)` subtree (`ToolInputError`, `NotFound`)\n  means \"the model can act on this\": the loop turns it into an error tool result and continues. Every\n  other branch is handled by code and never enters the transcript.\n- **Leaves carry what the handler needs** as attributes: `retry_after` seconds, token counts and limits,\n  the list of validation errors. Give leaves a clear `__str__` for logs.\n- **Keep it shallow.** Three levels is usually enough, and every class must match a different decision\n  somewhere, otherwise it is noise.\n- **Also inherit a built-in when outsiders catch it.** `class ProviderTimeout(Transient, TimeoutError)` is\n  caught by your retry loop *and* any framework's `except TimeoutError`, because `except` checks the\n  whole MRO.\n- **Classes for `except` decisions, attributes for `if` decisions.** When handling depends on data (the\n  same `ProviderHTTPError` is retryable for 503, not for 400), a `retryable: bool` attribute can beat a\n  split. Prefer classes where you can, because an `except` clause cannot forget to check a flag.\n\n### Chaining: keep the cause\n| Form | Sets | Traceback says |\n|---|---|---|\n| `raise Domain(...)` inside `except` | `__context__` (automatic) | \"During handling of the above exception, another exception occurred\", which misreads as a second bug |\n| `raise Domain(...) from e` | `__cause__` | \"The above exception was the direct cause\": the honest story, vendor stack trace kept |\n| `raise Domain(...) from None` | suppresses context | Only the new error, for when the original is irrelevant noise |\n\n- **`e.add_note(\"request_id=...\")`** (3.11) attaches context without changing the type.\n\n### Translate at the boundary, decide at the top\n1. **Translate** vendor SDK or HTTP errors into your hierarchy in the one adapter that talks to the vendor.\n2. **Propagate** through layers that cannot act. Do not catch-log-reraise at every level, which prints the\n   same stack trace five times.\n3. **Decide** where the policy lives: the retry loop catches `Transient`, the request handler maps\n   `Permanent` to an HTTP status, the top level catches `LLMError` for the user-facing message.\n4. **Stay picklable** if exceptions cross process boundaries: pass the constructor arguments to\n   `super().__init__`.\n\n### Concurrent failures\n- **Several tasks can fail at once** (an `asyncio.TaskGroup` fanning out tool calls), so there is no\n  single exception to raise.\n- **`ExceptionGroup` (3.11) bundles them,** and `except* Transient` selects the matching members while\n  re-raising the rest, keeping the retry/no-retry split correct for parallel work.","example":"### Agent tool step: model-visible errors versus infrastructure errors\n`ToolError` subclasses become an error tool result the model reads; `Transient` is retried silently;\nwhen retries run out the run aborts. Passes `mypy --strict`.\n\n```python\nclass AgentError(Exception): ...\nclass ToolError(AgentError):                     # the model can act on it: show it, keep going\n    def __init__(self, tool: str, message: str):\n        super().__init__(tool, message)\n        self.tool, self.message = tool, message\nclass ToolInputError(ToolError): ...             # bad arguments: the model should fix the call\nclass NotFound(ToolError): ...                   # valid call, empty world: the model should re-plan\nclass Transient(AgentError): ...                 # infrastructure blip: retry, the model never sees it\nclass RunAborted(AgentError): ...                # stop the run, keep the trace\n\ndef lookup(order_id: str) -> str:\n    if not order_id.startswith(\"ORD-\"):\n        raise ToolInputError(\"lookup\", f\"order_id must look like ORD-123456, got {order_id!r}\")\n    if order_id == \"ORD-000000\":\n        raise NotFound(\"lookup\", \"no such order\")\n    if order_id == \"ORD-999999\":\n        raise Transient(\"orders API timed out\")\n    return f\"{order_id}: shipped\"\n\ndef run_tool(order_id: str, retries: int = 2) -> dict[str, object]:\n    for attempt in range(retries + 1):\n        try:\n            return {\"content\": lookup(order_id)}\n        except ToolError as e:                   # model-visible: becomes the tool result\n            return {\"content\": f\"{type(e).__name__}: {e.message}\", \"is_error\": True}\n        except Transient:\n            if attempt == retries:\n                raise RunAborted(\"lookup kept failing\") from None\n    raise AssertionError(\"unreachable\")          # loop always returns or raises\n\nprint(run_tool(\"12345\"))       # {'content': \"ToolInputError: order_id must look like ORD-123456, got '12345'\", 'is_error': True}\nprint(run_tool(\"ORD-000000\"))  # {'content': 'NotFound: no such order', 'is_error': True}\nprint(run_tool(\"ORD-123456\"))  # {'content': 'ORD-123456: shipped'}\n# run_tool(\"ORD-999999\") retries twice, then raises RunAborted; a KeyError in lookup escapes at once\n```\n\n| Failure in a tool step | Seen by the model? | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ToolInputError` | yes, as an error result | one more model turn to fix the call |\n| `NotFound` | yes | one more turn to re-plan |\n| `Transient`, then success | no | a retry, no extra tokens |\n| `Transient` three times | no; run ends with `RunAborted` | the run, with its trace kept |\n| Your own `KeyError` | no; escapes as a bug | zero retries, one clear trace |\n\n### A hierarchy, an adapter and a policy-aware retry loop\nHTTP failures are translated once at the adapter; the loop retries only what is marked retryable. The\nerror-body shape is illustrative; map your provider's codes the same way.\n\n```python\nimport time, httpx\n\nclass LLMError(Exception): ...\nclass Transient(LLMError): ...                      # safe to retry\nclass Permanent(LLMError): ...                      # do not retry\nclass Recoverable(LLMError): ...                    # retry only after changing the request\n\nclass RateLimited(Transient):\n    def __init__(self, retry_after: float):\n        super().__init__(retry_after)               # args == ctor args keeps it picklable\n        self.retry_after = retry_after\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return f\"rate limited, retry after {self.retry_after:.1f}s\"\n\nclass ContextTooLong(Recoverable):\n    def __init__(self, tokens: int, limit: int):\n        super().__init__(tokens, limit)\n        self.tokens, self.limit = tokens, limit\n\ndef call_provider(client: httpx.Client, prompt: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        r = client.post(\"/v1/complete\", json={\"prompt\": prompt}, timeout=30)\n        r.raise_for_status()\n    except httpx.TransportError as e:               # timeouts, refused or reset connections\n        raise Transient(f\"transport error: {e!r}\") from e   # keep the cause for logs\n    except httpx.HTTPStatusError as e:\n        status = e.response.status_code\n        if status == 429:\n            ra = e.response.headers.get(\"retry-after\", \"2\")      # may also be an HTTP date\n            raise RateLimited(float(ra) if ra.isdigit() else 2.0) from e\n        if status >= 500:                           # check before parsing: 5xx bodies are often HTML\n            raise Transient(f\"provider returned {status}\") from e\n        is_json = \"json\" in e.response.headers.get(\"content-type\", \"\")\n        body = e.response.json() if is_json else {}\n        if body.get(\"error\", {}).get(\"code\") == \"context_length_exceeded\":\n            raise ContextTooLong(body[\"error\"][\"tokens\"], body[\"error\"][\"limit\"]) from e\n        raise Permanent(f\"provider returned {status}: {body}\") from e   # 400/401/403: stop\n    return r.json()[\"text\"]\n\ndef answer(client: httpx.Client, prompt: str, attempts: int = 3) -> str:\n    last: LLMError | None = None\n    for n in range(attempts):\n        try:\n            return call_provider(client, prompt)\n        except RateLimited as e:                    # subclass handler before its base\n            last = e; time.sleep(min(e.retry_after, 30))   # cap a hostile or huge hint\n        except Transient as e:\n            last = e; time.sleep(2 ** n)\n        except ContextTooLong as e:                 # recoverable: shrink, then try again\n            last = e; prompt = prompt[: len(prompt) * e.limit * 9 // (10 * e.tokens)]\n    raise Permanent(f\"gave up after {attempts} attempts\") from last   # Permanent: propagates\n```\n\n### Worked trace: three failures through that code\n| Provider response | Adapter raises | What `answer` does | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 429 with `retry-after: 7` | `RateLimited(7.0)`, chained to the `HTTPStatusError` so the vendor trace survives | Matches `except RateLimited` first (listed before its base), sleeps seven seconds, loops | One wait, one retry |\n| 401 | `Permanent` | No handler names it, so it propagates immediately to the request handler | Zero retries spent |\n| 200 with a changed body | `r.json()[\"text\"]` raises `KeyError`, not an `LLMError` | Escapes on the first attempt as the bug it is | Zero retries, one clear trace |\n\n- **The handlers never inspect a status code or a message string;** the classification happened once, in\n  the adapter.\n\n### Parallel tool calls with `except*` (Python 3.11+)\nA `TaskGroup` fails with an `ExceptionGroup`; `except*` splits it by policy.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nclass LLMError(Exception): ...\nclass Transient(LLMError): ...\nclass Permanent(LLMError): ...\n\nasync def tool(name: str) -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n    if name == \"search\":\n        raise Transient(\"search timed out\")\n    if name == \"crm\":\n        raise Permanent(\"crm: 403 forbidden\")\n    return f\"{name}: ok\"\n\nasync def fan_out(names: list[str]) -> None:\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n        for n in names:\n            tg.create_task(tool(n))\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    try:\n        await fan_out([\"search\", \"crm\", \"calendar\"])\n    except* Transient as eg:                      # only the retryable members\n        print(\"retry:\", [str(e) for e in eg.exceptions])\n    except* Permanent as eg:                      # the rest, handled separately\n        print(\"stop:\", [str(e) for e in eg.exceptions])\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n# retry: ['search timed out']\n# stop: ['crm: 403 forbidden']\n```\n\n- **Why both failures appear:** both tasks fail in the same event-loop step. After the first failure a\n  `TaskGroup` cancels its remaining tasks, so a sibling that would have failed later is cancelled and\n  never shows up in the group; collect results with `gather(..., return_exceptions=True)` if you need\n  every outcome.","patterns":["**Model-visible errors as tool results, with a budget.** Catch only `ToolError` in the agent loop and return it as an error tool result with a short, actionable message (what was wrong, what a valid call looks like). Count those turns against the step budget, and monitor error-result rate per tool; a high rate on one tool means its schema or description is unclear.","**Retry by class, not by catch-all.** With tenacity, use `retry=retry_if_exception_type(Transient)` plus `stop_after_attempt(3)` and exponential waits, so `Permanent` and your own bugs fail on the first attempt. Monitor retries per exception class; a rise in one leaf points at one cause.","**One adapter per provider, unmapped means `Permanent`.** Every vendor exception is translated in a single module with `raise ... from e`, and anything unrecognised becomes `Permanent`, not a silent retry. Alert on a new or rising count of unmapped errors after SDK upgrades.","**Map the hierarchy to HTTP once, log once at the top.** In FastAPI, register `@app.exception_handler(Permanent)` and friends so `RateLimited` becomes 429 and `ContextTooLong` a 4xx with the limit in the body. Track the status mix per route.","**Notes on the way up.** Add identifiers on the way up with `e.add_note(...)`, and call `logger.exception` only in the top-level handler, so one failure is one trace with the full chain.","**Trip a breaker on `Transient` rate.** When `Transient` errors from one provider exceed a threshold in a window, route to a fallback model instead of retrying; this works because classification is already done. Monitor breaker open time per provider."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A tool-calling loop has a policy most services lack: some errors belong\n  to the model (\"order not found\", \"bad date format\") and go back as an error tool result so it can\n  re-plan, while others (rate limits, timeouts, your own bugs) must never reach the transcript.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider SDK error classes you translate in an adapter, tenacity\n  retry predicates, LangGraph and other frameworks' retry or error-handling options on nodes and tools,\n  `TaskGroup` fan-out of parallel tool calls, and the error flag on tool results in provider APIs.\n- **What you control.** The policy branches (`Transient`, `Permanent`, `Recoverable`, model-visible\n  `ToolError`), where vendor errors are translated, and the step and retry budgets that end a run.\n- **What you can skip.** Exception internals such as traceback objects and frame details; `except*`\n  beyond fan-out of tools.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Provider adapters.** Vendor SDK errors are translated once into `Transient` or `Permanent`, so the\n  agent core never imports a provider's exception classes.\n- **Model-visible tool errors.** A `ToolError` such as \"order not found\" goes back as an error tool\n  result so the model can fix its call or re-plan.\n- **Context-length recovery.** A context-length error triggers history compaction or a smaller request\n  instead of a plain retry.\n- **Parallel tool fan-out.** A `TaskGroup` running several tools raises an `ExceptionGroup`, and\n  `except*` splits model-visible errors from bugs.","mini_project":"**Give an agent loop an error policy (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a tool-calling loop where every failure is routed by class to retry, repair, recover or stop.\n- **Steps:** define `AgentError` with `Transient`, `Permanent`, `Recoverable` and `ToolError` branches;\n  write an adapter that maps fake provider errors onto them; retry only `Transient` with a capped\n  budget; on `ContextTooLong` (a `Recoverable`) trim the history before retrying; return `ToolError` to\n  the model; let a planted `KeyError` propagate; log each decision.\n- **Done when:** a scripted run shows a rate limit retried, a context-length error recovered by trimming,\n  a bad tool argument repaired by the model, an auth error stopping the run, and the planted bug failing\n  loudly with no retry."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    V[\"Vendor error: httpx exception or status code\"] --> AD[\"Adapter: translate once, raise from e\"]\n    AD -->|\"429\"| RL[\"RateLimited, a Transient, with retry_after\"]\n    AD -->|\"timeout or 5xx\"| TR[\"Transient\"]\n    AD -->|\"context_length_exceeded\"| CT[\"ContextTooLong, a Recoverable, with tokens and limit\"]\n    AD -->|\"400, 401, 403 or unmapped\"| PE[\"Permanent\"]\n    RL --> RLOOP[\"Retry loop: sleep retry_after capped, try again\"]\n    TR --> BACK[\"Retry loop: exponential backoff\"]\n    CT --> SHRINK[\"Shrink prompt to fit limit, then retry\"]\n    PE --> TOP[\"Top-level handler: log once, map to HTTP status\"]\n    BUG[\"Own bug such as KeyError\"] -.->|\"not an LLMError, no retry\"| TOP","caption":"The decision path for one failed provider call: the adapter classifies once with `raise ... from e`,\nand every layer above decides by class, never by status code or message text."},"interview":[{"q":"Why build a custom exception hierarchy instead of catching the SDK's exceptions directly?","a":"- **Policy, not source:** branches such as `Transient`, `Permanent` and `Recoverable` let each layer\n  catch exactly what it can act on, because `except` matches a class and all its subclasses.\n- **Vendor neutrality:** translating once in the adapter means adding a fallback provider touches one\n  module, not every call site, and handlers never depend on vendor message wording."},{"q":"After a refactor, Ctrl-C no longer stops your agent worker and shutdown hangs on cancelled tasks. What do you look for?","a":"- **A handler that is too broad:** a bare `except:` or `except BaseException:` around the agent loop or an\n  `await`. `KeyboardInterrupt` and `asyncio.CancelledError` hang off `BaseException`, not `Exception`,\n  so only those forms swallow them.\n- **Fix:** catch `Exception` (or your `LLMError` base) instead; if cleanup really must see every exit,\n  use `finally` or re-raise with a bare `raise` after the cleanup.\n- **Verify** by sending SIGINT in staging and checking that `TaskGroup` or `task.cancel()` completes\n  within your shutdown timeout."},{"q":"In an agent loop, which tool failures should the model see, and how do you enforce that?","a":"- **The model sees what it can act on:** invalid arguments, not-found results, permission denials it can\n  route around. These are `ToolError` subclasses returned as error tool results with a short fix hint.\n- **Code handles the rest:** rate limits and timeouts are retried by class without spending a model turn,\n  and your own bugs propagate to the top-level handler, never into the transcript.\n- **Enforce it structurally:** the loop catches only `ToolError`; both kinds count toward step and retry\n  budgets so a confused model cannot loop forever."},{"q":"When would you use an attribute such as `retryable` instead of a new subclass?","a":"- **When handling depends on data,** for example the same `ProviderHTTPError` is retryable for 503 but\n  not for 400, and splitting every status into a class would be sprawl.\n- **Prefer classes where you can,** because an `except` clause cannot forget to check a flag, while every\n  `if e.retryable` is a place to get it wrong."},{"q":"Three parallel tool calls fail at once inside a `TaskGroup`. How do you retry only the retryable ones?","a":"- **The group raises an `ExceptionGroup`** (3.11) bundling all failures, since there is no single\n  exception to raise.\n- **Use `except* Transient`** to select the retryable members and `except* Permanent` for the rest;\n  anything unmatched is re-raised, so the retry or no-retry split stays correct for parallel work."},{"q":"What is the difference between `raise Domain(...) from e` and `from None`, and when would you use each?","a":"- **`from e`** sets `__cause__`, so the traceback shows the vendor error as the direct cause: the default\n  for adapter translation, because on-call needs the vendor trace.\n- **`from None`** suppresses the context and shows only the new error, for when the original is\n  irrelevant noise, such as a `KeyError` from a lookup you are reporting more clearly."}],"tradeoffs":["**Precise catching vs class sprawl.** A policy-based tree lets callers catch exactly what they can handle, but every class must map to a distinct decision; a tree mirroring the vendor's twenty error codes is coupling in disguise.","**Vendor neutrality vs mapping upkeep.** Translating at the adapter costs a mapping maintained as providers add codes; unmapped errors should land in `Permanent`, not vanish.","**Structured data vs pickling.** Attributes like `retry_after` and `limit` make handlers precise, but non-trivial constructors must stay pickle-compatible if the exception ever crosses a process pool.","**Faithful parallel failures vs readability.** `except*` semantics (partial matching, re-raising the remainder) are harder to read than a sequential loop; use them only where fan-out genuinely happens."],"pitfalls":["**`except Exception:` with a retry around the provider call.** Symptom: a `KeyError` in your own parser appears in logs five times, each with a paid request behind it.","**A bare `except:` or `except BaseException:`.** Ctrl-C is ignored and async tasks refuse to cancel, because `CancelledError` and `KeyboardInterrupt` were swallowed.","**`except Transient` before `except RateLimited`.** The leaf handler is unreachable and the `retry_after` hint is ignored, so you hammer a rate-limited endpoint with exponential backoff instead.","**A domain error raised without `from e`.** The traceback says \"during handling of the above exception, another exception occurred\", and on-call chases a phantom second bug.","**`assert` to reject bad model output or tool arguments.** Asserts are removed under `python -O`, so validation works in tests and silently vanishes in an optimised deployment. Raise a real exception.","**Feeding raw stack traces to the model.** Catching everything in the tool step and returning `str(e)` or a traceback as the tool result leaks internals, wastes tokens and invites the model to retry a bug. Fix: only `ToolError` messages reach the model; the rest propagate.","**Matching on `str(e)` from an SDK.** It works until a minor release rewords the message and every rate limit becomes a permanent failure overnight.","**`logger.exception` at every layer, then re-raise.** One failure produces a dozen stack traces, which trains people to ignore logs.","**A custom `__init__` with keyword-only arguments that passes a formatted string to `super()`.** Symptom: `TypeError` when a worker process tries to unpickle the exception."],"when":"- **Introduce a hierarchy** as soon as a subsystem has more than one failure policy, which for anything\n  calling a model or external API is immediately.\n- **Keep it small:** one base plus policy branches plus data-carrying leaves; add a class only when a\n  handler would treat it differently.\n- **Flow:** translate at the adapter, propagate through the middle, decide at the top.\n- **Measure:** retries per error type, and that no handler catches `Exception` around a paid call.\n- **Day to day,** this is the shape of every provider client wrapper.\n- **Interviews:** \"how do you decide what to retry\" has the answer \"by exception class, with the mapping\n  done once where the vendor error enters the system\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the adapter that maps SDK and HTTP errors into `Transient`/`Permanent` leaves and the retry loop that catches only `Transient`, with backoff hints read from the exception.","architect":"Owns the shared hierarchy across services so retry, fallback and circuit-breaker policies key off the same classes, and decides which failures reach the user as which status.","fde":"Extends the mapping for a customer's gateway or proxy that rewrites provider errors, so a 429 hidden inside a 502 is still classified as transient."},"check":{"q":"A colleague wraps every provider call in `except Exception as e: log(e); retry()` with three\nattempts and reports that reliability improved. What is wrong with that picture, and how would you\nrestructure it?","a":"The improvement is partly fake: transient failures were already retryable, while the policy\nalso retries permanent ones (bad API key, content refusal, context overflow) and your own bugs,\ntripling cost and latency on exactly the calls that cannot succeed, and hiding `KeyError`-style bugs\nbehind eventual success or a generic failure. The log line at that layer also duplicates whatever the\ntop level logs. Restructure with a small hierarchy under `LLMError`: `Transient` for timeouts, 429s\nand 5xx with a `retry_after` attribute, `Permanent` for 4xx auth and policy errors, and a recoverable\n`ContextTooLong` carrying the limit. Translate vendor exceptions into it once in the adapter with\n`raise ... from e`, retry only `Transient`, shrink on `ContextTooLong`, and let everything else\npropagate to a single top-level handler that logs once with the full chain and maps to a status."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `except Exception:` not catch `KeyboardInterrupt` or `asyncio.CancelledError`?","options":["Those are warnings, not exceptions, and are handled by the `warnings` module","They derive from `BaseException` directly, so a handler for recoverable errors cannot accidentally block shutdown or cancellation","They are raised in a different thread than the handler","They are only catchable with `except*` because they arrive as exception groups"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The split between `BaseException` and `Exception` is deliberate: \"any error I might recover from\" must not include interrupts and cancellations. Catching `BaseException` or using a bare `except` does swallow them and breaks Ctrl-C and task cancellation."},{"q":"What does `raise ContextTooLong(...) from e` change compared with a plain `raise ContextTooLong(...)` inside an `except` block?","options":["It discards the original exception to keep the traceback short","It converts `e` into an instance of `ContextTooLong`","It sets `__cause__` so the traceback shows the original as the direct cause instead of an unrelated error that occurred during handling","It makes the new exception retryable"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Both forms keep the original reachable, but implicit chaining prints \"during handling ... another exception occurred\", which reads as two bugs. Explicit `from e` records intent and keeps the vendor trace for debugging."},{"q":"Which principle should organise a custom exception tree for an LLM client?","options":["One class per vendor error code so nothing is lost","One class per module that can raise, mirroring the package layout","Branches by handling policy (retry, recover, stop) with leaves that carry the data handlers need","A flat list of classes all inheriting directly from `Exception`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Handlers catch by class, so the tree should let a retry loop say `except Transient` and a request handler say `except Permanent`. Mirroring vendor codes or modules forces every caller to know details that belong in the adapter."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/async-await","sources":["Selivanov, 2015 — PEP 492: Coroutines with async and await syntax","Official docs: asyncio"],"why":"- **AI workloads are mostly waiting.** An LLM request takes on the order of seconds (illustrative; it\n  varies with model and output length). One retrieval-augmented agent turn may issue an embedding call,\n  several vector-store queries, a few tool calls and two or three model calls; a batch evaluation issues\n  thousands of independent calls. Nearly all that time the CPU is idle on a socket.\n- **Sequential code serializes the waits.** Twenty independent one-second calls take twenty seconds.\n- **Threads work but scale badly for this shape.** `concurrent.futures` makes a thread per in-flight call\n  easy, but each OS thread carries its own stack and kernel scheduling state, shared state needs locks,\n  and the small CPU portions (JSON parsing, prompt assembly) contend on the GIL. A streaming response,\n  with tokens arriving over seconds, pins a thread for its whole duration.\n- **One thread, explicit pause points.** A coroutine awaiting a network response yields; the loop runs\n  another; when the socket becomes readable the first resumes. Ten thousand waiting coroutines are ten\n  thousand small Python objects, not ten thousand threads.\n- **Cooperative *because* that is what makes it cheap:** no preemption, no kernel involvement, and no lock\n  needed to protect state between two `await`s. The price is discipline: anything that blocks without\n  awaiting freezes every other coroutine.","how":"### Coroutines and awaitables\n- **Calling does not run.** Calling an `async def` function returns a coroutine object, a suspended\n  computation.\n- **`await coro` runs it** until it either finishes (the `await` expression evaluates to the return value)\n  or hits an `await` on something not yet ready, at which point control returns to the event loop.\n- **Built on generators.** `await x` is roughly `yield from x.__await__()`, and the coroutine's frame is\n  kept alive between resumptions.\n- **What you can await:** coroutines, `Task`s, `Future`s and any object implementing `__await__`.\n\n### The event loop\n1. **`asyncio.run(main())`** creates a loop and runs it to completion.\n2. **The loop keeps a queue of ready callbacks** and asks the operating system's I/O multiplexer (epoll,\n   kqueue, IOCP) which sockets are ready.\n3. **A network read registers interest in a socket** and returns a `Future`.\n4. **When the OS reports readiness,** the loop resolves the Future and reschedules the coroutine awaiting it.\n5. **One callback runs at a time.** That is why there is no data race between two `await` points, and also\n   why a long computation inside a coroutine blocks every other one.\n\n### A trace: two calls on one thread\nTasks A and B each send a request and await a reply that takes 1 s.\n\n| Time | Loop does | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| t=0 | Runs A until it awaits the socket read; A's frame is parked with a pending Future | Control returns to the loop |\n| t=0 | Runs B the same way | Both requests are now in flight |\n| 0 to 1 s | Nothing is ready, so it sleeps inside `epoll`/`kqueue` | No CPU used |\n| t≈1 s | Both sockets readable: resolves both Futures, resumes A then B, each from the line after its `await` | Total ≈1 s, not 2 s |\n\n- **At no moment did two pieces of Python run at once.** That is the safety property (no torn updates\n  between awaits) and the hazard: had A computed for 1 s instead of awaiting, B would not even have\n  *sent* its request until A finished.\n\n### Function colouring\n- **`await` is legal only inside `async def`,** so a function that needs to await must itself become\n  async, and so must its callers, up to `asyncio.run` or a framework that owns the loop.\n- **Why accept it:** you can see every place your code can be interleaved, *because* each one is spelled\n  `await`.\n- **Consequences:** an async SDK cannot be called from plain sync code without starting a loop, a sync\n  helper cannot await, and libraries often ship both a sync and an async client (`OpenAI` and\n  `AsyncOpenAI`, `httpx.Client` and `httpx.AsyncClient`) rather than one that works in both worlds.\n\n### Concurrency: tasks, gather, groups\n| Construct | Behaviour | Use when |\n|---|---|---|\n| `await a(); await b()` | Sequential: `b` starts after `a` finishes | Steps depend on each other |\n| `asyncio.create_task(coro)` | Wraps it in a `Task` and schedules it immediately; await the task later | Overlap work you will collect later |\n| `asyncio.gather(*coros)` | Runs a list concurrently and returns results in order; `return_exceptions=True` makes failures appear as values instead of raising the first | Partial results are valuable |\n| `asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11) | Structured: tasks started inside `async with` are all awaited at the block's end; if one fails the others are cancelled and failures are raised together as an `ExceptionGroup` | Fan-out where a partial result is useless |\n\n### Limits and timeouts\n- **Unbounded fan-out against a provider turns into a rate-limit storm.**\n- **`asyncio.Semaphore(n)`** with `async with sem:` around the call caps in-flight requests at `n`, the\n  knob that matches your provider's concurrency allowance.\n- **It bounds concurrency, not rate.** Five slots with half-second calls still allow about ten requests per\n  second, so a requests- or tokens-per-minute quota needs a token-bucket limiter alongside it.\n- **`async with asyncio.timeout(seconds):`** (3.11; earlier, `asyncio.wait_for`) cancels the awaited work\n  when the budget expires and raises `TimeoutError`, which is how you stop a hung stream from holding a\n  slot forever.\n\n### Async iteration and context managers\n- **`async for chunk in stream:`** consumes an async generator or iterator, awaiting each item: the natural\n  shape for token streaming, where each chunk arrives from the socket at its own time.\n- **`async with client:`** runs `__aenter__`/`__aexit__`, which themselves await; this is how an\n  `httpx.AsyncClient` or an SDK client opens and closes its connection pool.\n\n### What blocks the loop\n- **Calls that never await:** `time.sleep`, `requests.get`, a synchronous database driver, a synchronous\n  SDK client, heavy `json.loads` on a huge payload, a CPU-bound embedding computation. The loop cannot\n  switch away from any of them.\n- **The symptom is global:** every request's latency rises together, and a \"fast\" endpoint becomes slow\n  because a different endpoint blocked.\n- **Fixes:** use async libraries, move blocking I/O to a thread with `await asyncio.to_thread(fn, ...)`,\n  and move CPU work to a process pool.\n- **Scope:** the GIL, threads versus processes and cancellation semantics each have their own lesson in\n  this module; this one is about the syntax and the cooperative model they build on.","example":"### Running one turn's tool calls in parallel, with a per-tool timeout\nThe model asked for three tools in one turn. They run concurrently under a cap, each with its own\nbudget; a hung tool becomes an error result instead of stalling the run, and `gather` keeps results in\ncall order so each pairs with its `tool_use_id`. Passes `mypy --strict`.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nasync def search(q: str) -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.8); return f\"3 docs for {q!r}\"\nasync def weather(city: str) -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.5); return f\"{city}: 14C\"\nasync def crm(customer: str) -> str:\n    await asyncio.sleep(5.0); return \"never returns in time\"     # a hung dependency\n\nTOOLS = {\"search\": search, \"weather\": weather, \"crm\": crm}\n\nasync def run_one(call: dict[str, str], sem: asyncio.Semaphore, budget_s: float) -> dict[str, object]:\n    async with sem:\n        try:\n            async with asyncio.timeout(budget_s):\n                out = await TOOLS[call[\"name\"]](call[\"arg\"])\n            return {\"tool_use_id\": call[\"id\"], \"content\": out}\n        except TimeoutError:\n            return {\"tool_use_id\": call[\"id\"], \"content\": f\"{call['name']} timed out\", \"is_error\": True}\n\nasync def run_tool_calls(calls: list[dict[str, str]], max_parallel: int = 4) -> list[dict[str, object]]:\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(max_parallel)\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(run_one(c, sem, budget_s=2.0) for c in calls))  # keeps call order\n\ncalls = [{\"id\": \"t1\", \"name\": \"search\", \"arg\": \"refund policy\"},\n         {\"id\": \"t2\", \"name\": \"weather\", \"arg\": \"Oslo\"},\n         {\"id\": \"t3\", \"name\": \"crm\", \"arg\": \"acme\"}]\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\nfor r in asyncio.run(run_tool_calls(calls)):\n    print(r)\nprint(f\"{time.perf_counter() - t0:.1f}s\")   # about 2.0s: bounded by the timeout, not 0.8 + 0.5 + 5.0\n```\n\n| Execution of this turn | Step latency (simulated) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sequential, no timeout | 0.8 + 0.5 + 5.0 = 6.3 s | the hung tool dominates |\n| Sequential, 2 s timeout each | 0.8 + 0.5 + 2.0 = 3.3 s | still the sum |\n| Parallel, 2 s timeout | about 2.0 s | the slowest bounded call |\n| Parallel, no timeout | 5.0 s | parallelism alone does not fix a hung tool |\n\n- **Only parallelise independent calls.** If one tool's output feeds another's input, or two tools write\n  the same record, run them in order.\n\n### Capped fan-out, per-call timeout and a token stream\nTwenty simulated model calls with a concurrency cap of five, a per-call timeout, references kept to every\ntask, and an async generator standing in for a token stream. `asyncio.sleep` plays the role of network\nlatency.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, random, time\n\nMAX_INFLIGHT = 5\nin_flight = peak = 0\n\nasync def llm_call(prompt: str, sem: asyncio.Semaphore) -> str:\n    global in_flight, peak\n    async with sem:                                    # at most MAX_INFLIGHT past this line\n        in_flight += 1; peak = max(peak, in_flight)    # safe: no await between these two lines\n        try:\n            async with asyncio.timeout(2.0):           # per-call budget; raises TimeoutError\n                await asyncio.sleep(random.uniform(0.2, 0.6))   # stands in for the network wait\n            return f\"answer to {prompt!r}\"\n        finally:\n            in_flight -= 1\n\nasync def fan_out(prompts: list[str]) -> list[str | BaseException]:\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_INFLIGHT)\n    tasks = [asyncio.create_task(llm_call(p, sem)) for p in prompts]   # keep references\n    return await asyncio.gather(*tasks, return_exceptions=True)\n\nasync def stream_tokens(text: str):\n    for tok in text.split():\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.02)                      # a real client awaits each chunk here\n        yield tok\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    results = await fan_out([f\"q{i}\" for i in range(20)])\n    print(f\"{len(results)} results in {time.perf_counter() - t0:.2f}s, peak in-flight={peak}\")\n    # roughly: 20 results in ~1.7-1.9s, peak in-flight=5  (sequential ~8s)\n    async for tok in stream_tokens(\"tokens arrive as the model produces them\"):\n        print(tok, end=\" \", flush=True)\n    print()\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **Break it on purpose:** replace `await asyncio.sleep(...)` with `time.sleep(...)` and the run takes the\n  full sequential time with `peak in-flight=1`: the blocking call never yields, so no other coroutine can\n  start.\n\n### Worked numbers: the same 20 calls under different settings\nEach call waits 0.2 to 0.6 s (mean 0.4 s), measured with the fan-out above. Times are random, so\nexpect run-to-run spread of a few tenths of a second.\n\n| Setting | Wall time (approx.) | Peak in flight | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Semaphore of 1 (sequential) | ~7.5-8 s | 1 | 20 × 0.4 s of waits, one after another |\n| Semaphore of 5 | ~1.7-2.2 s | 5 | Each slot serves about 4 calls: 20 × 0.4 / 5 = 1.6 s, plus waiting for the last slow call |\n| No cap (all 20 at once) | ~0.6 s | 20 | Bounded by the slowest call, and by the provider's patience |\n| Cap of 5 but `time.sleep` inside | ~7.5-8 s | 1 | The loop never regains control, so it degrades to sequential |\n\n- **The last row is the production incident:** the code looks concurrent and the profile shows idle CPU.\n\n### Check-then-act across an `await`: an overspent budget\nNo two coroutines run at once, yet state read before an `await` can be stale after it. Five tasks each\ncheck a budget of 10 units before spending 4.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nbudget = 10            # quota left, in arbitrary token units\nCOST = 4\n\nasync def model_call() -> None:\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.1)          # stands in for the provider round trip\n\nasync def spend_racy() -> None:\n    global budget\n    if budget >= COST:                # every task passes the check...\n        await model_call()            # ...because all of them suspend here first\n        budget -= COST\n\nasync def spend_safe() -> None:\n    global budget\n    if budget < COST:                 # check and reserve with no await in between\n        return\n    budget -= COST\n    try:\n        await model_call()\n    except Exception:\n        budget += COST                # refund if the call failed\n        raise\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    global budget\n    await asyncio.gather(*(spend_racy() for _ in range(5)))\n    print(\"racy:\", budget)            # racy: -10  (20 units spent from a budget of 10)\n    budget = 10\n    await asyncio.gather(*(spend_safe() for _ in range(5)))\n    print(\"safe:\", budget)            # safe: 2    (only two calls admitted)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **Overspend by roughly the concurrency factor:** 5 tasks spent 20 units of 10.\n- **Reserve before awaiting,** or hold an `asyncio.Lock` across the check-and-act when the check itself\n  must await (for example reading the budget from Redis).","patterns":["**Parallel tool execution with per-tool budgets.** When a model turn contains several independent tool calls, run them with `gather` (or a `TaskGroup` when one failure should cancel the rest) under a semaphore, with an `asyncio.timeout` per tool and timeouts returned as error tool results. Monitor per-tool latency p95 and timeout rate, and the step latency per agent turn.","**One shared async client per process.** Create a single `httpx.AsyncClient` or `AsyncOpenAI` at startup (for example in a FastAPI lifespan), reuse it for every request and close it with `async with` or `aclose()` on shutdown, so connections are pooled. Monitor connection-pool waits and open sockets.","**Per-provider semaphore plus timeout.** Wrap every call in `async with sem:` sized to the provider's concurrency allowance and an `asyncio.timeout(...)` budget, with a token-bucket limiter on top for per-minute quotas. Monitor in-flight count, 429 rate and timeout rate per provider.","**Find blocking calls with debug mode.** Run staging with `asyncio.run(main(), debug=True)` or `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1`; the loop then logs callbacks slower than `loop.slow_callback_duration` (default 0.1 s). In production, export event-loop lag from a heartbeat task that sleeps a fixed interval and records how late it woke.","**Offload, do not block.** Wrap unavoidable sync SDKs in `await asyncio.to_thread(...)` and send CPU work (tokenizing a corpus, local embeddings) to a `ProcessPoolExecutor` via `loop.run_in_executor`. Monitor executor queue depth; `to_thread` shares the loop's default thread pool.","**Keep references to background tasks.** Add each `create_task` result (a background memory write, a trace upload) to a module-level `set` and call `task.add_done_callback(tasks.discard)`, so the loop's weak reference is not the only one. Log exceptions from the done callback so failures are not silent."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** When a model returns three tool calls in one turn, running them\n  concurrently makes the step cost the slowest call, not the sum. Serving many chats, streaming tokens\n  to a UI and fanning out an eval suite are the same shape.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `AsyncOpenAI` and the async Anthropic client, `httpx.AsyncClient`,\n  FastAPI endpoints, async tools in LangChain, LangGraph and the OpenAI Agents SDK (which mostly offer\n  both sync and async entry points), MCP clients, and eval harnesses that call the model thousands of\n  times.\n- **What you control.** Which tool calls run in parallel, the per-provider concurrency cap, the per-tool\n  timeout, and keeping every call in the loop on an async client so one sync call cannot stall them all.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing your own event loop or awaitables; the generator machinery under\n  `await` beyond \"the frame is parked\".","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Parallel tool calls.** When the model returns several tool calls in one turn, running them\n  concurrently makes the step cost the slowest call rather than the sum.\n- **Serving many agent sessions.** One event loop in a FastAPI service handles many chats that are\n  mostly waiting on model and tool responses.\n- **Streaming to a UI.** Tokens are forwarded as they arrive while other sessions keep running on the\n  same loop.\n- **Eval fan-out.** An eval harness runs thousands of cases under a semaphore so it finishes fast without\n  triggering 429 responses.","mini_project":"**Run parallel tool calls with limits and timeouts (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an async agent step that executes a turn's tool calls concurrently and safely.\n- **Steps:** write three fake async tools that sleep for different times, one of which sometimes hangs;\n  run them sequentially, then concurrently with `asyncio.gather` under a semaphore of 3; wrap each in a\n  per-tool timeout and return a timeout as an error tool result; time each variant, then rerun with a\n  semaphore of 2 and explain why the third call now waits for a free slot.\n- **Done when:** the concurrent step takes about the slowest bounded call instead of the sum, the hung\n  tool is cut off at its timeout without stalling the others, and a table shows wall time per variant."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant L as Event loop\n    participant A as Task A\n    participant B as Task B\n    participant OS as epoll or kqueue\n    L->>A: run until first await\n    A->>OS: send request, register socket, park on Future\n    L->>B: run until first await\n    B->>OS: send request, register socket, park on Future\n    Note over L: nothing ready, loop sleeps, no CPU used\n    OS-->>L: both sockets readable at about 1 s\n    L->>A: resolve Future, resume after await\n    L->>B: resolve Future, resume after await\n    Note over A,B: a blocking call in A delays B until A finishes","caption":"The two-call trace above as messages (read A and B as two tool calls from one agent turn): both tasks\npark at `await`, the loop sleeps in the OS multiplexer, and both resume when their sockets are readable,\nfor about 1 s total instead of 2 s."},"interview":[{"q":"What actually happens when a coroutine executes `await`?","a":"- **If the awaitable is ready,** execution simply continues with its result.\n- **If not,** the coroutine suspends with its frame kept alive (the generator machinery under `await`),\n  control returns to the event loop, and the loop runs other ready callbacks.\n- **Resumption:** when the OS multiplexer reports the socket ready, the loop resolves the `Future` and\n  resumes the coroutine on the line after its `await`."},{"q":"Why can asyncio handle thousands of concurrent LLM calls when a thread-per-call design struggles?","a":"- **Cost per wait:** a waiting coroutine is a small Python object, while each OS thread carries its own\n  stack and kernel scheduling state.\n- **No locks between awaits:** only one callback runs at a time, so simple shared state needs no locking.\n- **Streaming fits:** a stream that delivers tokens over seconds parks a coroutine, not a whole thread."},{"q":"An agent's token budget is sometimes overspent by about 5x when it fans out tool calls. What is the bug?","a":"- **Check-then-act across an `await`:** every task reads the budget, suspends on the model call, and only\n  then subtracts, so all of them pass the check first.\n- **Overspend matches the concurrency factor,** which is the tell.\n- **Fix:** reserve (subtract) before awaiting and refund on failure, or hold an `asyncio.Lock` across the\n  whole check-and-act when the check itself awaits."},{"q":"The model returns four tool calls in one turn and one of them sometimes hangs. How do you execute the turn?","a":"- **Run independent calls concurrently** with `gather` under a semaphore, so the turn costs the slowest\n  call rather than the sum; keep results in call order so each pairs with its tool-call id.\n- **Bound each call** with `asyncio.timeout(...)` and turn a timeout into an error tool result, so the\n  model can retry or route around it and the run does not stall.\n- **Decide all-or-nothing deliberately:** `TaskGroup` cancels siblings on the first failure, which suits\n  calls whose results are useless alone; otherwise collect partial results."},{"q":"When would you not use async, or prefer a thread pool?","a":"- **CPU-bound work** such as tokenizing a large corpus or computing embeddings locally: it never yields,\n  so it belongs in a process pool.\n- **A stack whose key dependencies are synchronous:** one sync driver in the request path removes most of\n  the benefit, and a thread pool is simpler and more honest.\n- **Glue around one blocking call** can stay async if that call goes through `asyncio.to_thread`."},{"q":"You must call a provider with a limit of 20 concurrent requests and a per-minute token quota. How do you design the client?","a":"- **`asyncio.Semaphore(20)`** around each call bounds in-flight requests.\n- **A token-bucket limiter on top,** because a semaphore bounds concurrency, not rate: short calls can still\n  exceed a per-minute quota.\n- **`asyncio.timeout(...)` per call** so a hung stream releases its slot, plus retry with backoff only on\n  transient errors; monitor in-flight count, 429 rate and event-loop lag."}],"tradeoffs":["**Thousands of cheap waits vs one blocking call freezes all.** One thread handles thousands of concurrent waits cheaply, but a single blocking call freezes all of them; the model flips to threads or processes when you cannot avoid blocking libraries.","**No preemption vs interleaving at `await`.** No data races between `await`s and no locks for simple state, yet any state touched across an `await` can be interleaved and needs the same care as threaded code.","**Fan-out speed vs rate limits.** `gather` collapses wall-clock time, but without a semaphore it collapses into provider rate limits and retries that cost more than the sequential version.","**End-to-end async vs one sync dependency.** Async SDKs and drivers are required end to end; a stack with one synchronous dependency in the request path gains little and complicates debugging, so sometimes a thread pool is the honest choice.","**Structured concurrency vs partial results.** `TaskGroup` gives clean cancellation and error aggregation, but forces all-or-nothing semantics; when partial results are valuable, `gather` with `return_exceptions=True` fits better."],"pitfalls":["**Calling a coroutine without `await`.** `client.chat(...)` instead of `await client.chat(...)`: nothing runs, and the only hint is a `RuntimeWarning: coroutine ... was never awaited`, emitted when the unused coroutine object is garbage-collected (often right away, sometimes much later).","**A synchronous SDK client or `requests` inside an async endpoint.** Symptom: every endpoint's p99 latency rising together under modest load. Fix: an async client or `to_thread`.","**Discarding the result of `create_task`.** The loop holds only a weak reference, so the task can be garbage-collected mid-execution and silently disappear.","**`gather` over thousands of provider calls with no semaphore.** Symptom: a burst of 429 responses followed by a retry storm that can end up slower than running sequentially.","**Read, await, then write based on what you read.** `if budget > cost: await call(); budget -= cost`: every task passes the check before any of them subtracts, so a token budget or quota is overspent by roughly the concurrency factor. Hold an `asyncio.Lock` across the check-and-act, or reserve before awaiting.","**Parallelising dependent or conflicting tool calls.** Two calls that update the same record, or where one needs the other's output, run concurrently and produce a lost update or a call with stale input. Fix: parallelise only calls the dispatcher knows are independent and read-only; run the rest in order.","**Calling `asyncio.run()` from inside a running loop** (notebooks, some frameworks). It raises `RuntimeError`; `await` directly or use the framework's loop.","**Catching `BaseException` or bare `except:` around awaits.** It swallows `CancelledError` and produces tasks that cannot be cancelled and shutdowns that hang."],"when":"- **Use when** the work is I/O-bound and concurrent: fan-out model calls, parallel tool execution,\n  streaming tokens to a client, serving many simultaneous chats from one process.\n- **Avoid when** the work is CPU-bound (tokenization of large corpora, local embedding computation) or\n  your critical dependencies are synchronous; use threads or processes there, or wrap the blocking parts\n  in `to_thread`.\n- **Measure** event-loop lag (how late scheduled callbacks run) and in-flight counts; a rising lag with low\n  CPU points at a blocking call.\n- **Day to day,** this is the shape of every FastAPI endpoint, agent loop and evaluation harness you will\n  write.\n- **Interviews:** be ready to explain what `await` actually does, why one `time.sleep` breaks everything,\n  and how you bound concurrency against a rate limit.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes agent loops and eval harnesses with `create_task`, `gather`, semaphores and timeouts, and keeps every call in the request path on an async client.","architect":"Decides which services run on the event loop versus thread or process pools, and sets concurrency caps and rate limiters per provider so fan-out stays inside negotiated quotas.","fde":"Finds the one synchronous call (a legacy auth client, a sync database driver) stalling a customer's async service and moves it to `to_thread` or an async driver."},"check":{"q":"A FastAPI service with async endpoints handles 50 requests per second comfortably, but after\na developer adds a feature that calls a legacy sync HTTP client, latency on *every* endpoint rises\nunder load, with the CPU mostly idle. Explain the mechanism and how you would fix and verify it.","a":"The sync client blocks the event-loop thread for the whole request round trip. During that\ntime no other coroutine can run, so every in-flight request, including unrelated endpoints, waits.\nCPU stays idle because the thread is blocked on a socket, not computing. The fix is to make the\ncall non-blocking: switch to an async client such as `httpx.AsyncClient`, or wrap the legacy call\nin `await asyncio.to_thread(...)` so it runs in a worker thread while the loop continues. Verify by\nmeasuring event-loop lag or by load-testing one endpoint while the slow one is exercised; after the\nfix the unrelated endpoint's latency should no longer move."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when a coroutine hits `await` on a network response that is not yet ready?","options":["The thread blocks until the response arrives","The coroutine suspends and the event loop runs other ready coroutines until the socket is readable","A new thread is spawned to wait for the response","The response is polled in a busy loop until it arrives"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`await` yields control to the loop, which uses the OS I/O multiplexer to learn when the socket is ready and then resumes the coroutine. No thread blocks and no busy-waiting occurs."},{"q":"Why does a single `time.sleep(1)` inside one coroutine delay every other request in the process?","options":["`time.sleep` acquires the GIL exclusively","asyncio runs each coroutine on its own thread and `time.sleep` joins them","The loop is cooperative and single-threaded; a call that never awaits never yields, so nothing else can run","`time.sleep` cancels pending tasks"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Coroutines only switch at `await`. A blocking call holds the one thread the loop uses, so all scheduled work waits. `await asyncio.sleep(1)` yields instead and the problem disappears."},{"q":"What is the purpose of wrapping each provider call in `async with asyncio.Semaphore(5)`?","options":["To make the calls run in parallel on five CPU cores","To retry each call up to five times","To cap the number of in-flight calls at five so fan-out cannot flood the provider all at once","To guarantee results are returned in order"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A semaphore bounds concurrency: the sixth call waits until one of the five completes. `gather` alone would start all calls at once and trigger 429 responses. Note it limits calls in flight, not requests per minute; a requests- or tokens-per-minute quota needs a rate limiter on top."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/asyncio","sources":["Official docs: asyncio","PEP 3156 — Asynchronous IO Support Rebooted: the \"asyncio\" Module","PEP 492 — Coroutines with async and await syntax"],"why":"- **Agents mostly wait.** An agent backend calling a model with a 3-second median response holds a\n  synchronous thread for those 3 seconds doing nothing but waiting on a socket, and a 10-step run does\n  that ten times plus every tool call.\n- **Threads are an expensive way to wait.** 1,000 concurrent agent sessions need 1,000 threads, each with\n  a kernel object, scheduler time and a reserved stack (512 KB on macOS to 8 MB on typical Linux, mostly\n  virtual address space rather than resident memory). Under the GIL they cannot even run Python in parallel.\n- **A bigger pool moves the ceiling, not the cost shape.** A waiting agent step needs almost no state: a\n  socket to watch and the point in the code to resume from.\n- **The OS already watches many sockets cheaply** (`epoll` on Linux, `kqueue` on macOS, IOCP on Windows).\n  Python lacked a way to suspend a function mid-body, keep its locals and resume it later. Generators had\n  that since `yield`; PEP 492 gave it syntax (`async def` / `await`); asyncio is the scheduler joining\n  suspended functions to OS readiness events.\n- **Coroutines beat callbacks for readability.** Callback frameworks solved the same problem, but an agent\n  loop written as callbacks reads as a graph; coroutines read top to bottom and behave the same underneath.","how":"### The three objects\n| Object | What it is | Role in an agent |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Coroutine** | What calling an `async def` function returns: a suspended frame that has not started | `run_tool(call)` before anything drives it |\n| **Future** | One-shot result holder with callbacks: \"when this is set, call these\" | Socket readiness, timers and thread results (a sync tool in `to_thread`) all surface as futures |\n| **Task** | A coroutine wrapped in a future, plus the driver | On each `await` of a not-ready future, registers a callback and returns control to the loop; when the future resolves, the task is rescheduled and resumes exactly where it stopped |\n\n### One iteration of the loop\nThe event loop is a single-threaded `while True`:\n1. **Find the next deadline.** Compute how long until the earliest timer (`asyncio.sleep`, tool timeouts).\n2. **Wait on the selector.** Ask the OS for readable or writable sockets, waiting at most that long. This\n   is the only place the thread blocks, and it blocks on behalf of *all* tasks (every agent session) at once.\n3. **Queue handlers.** For each ready socket or expired timer, queue its handler. The handler reads the\n   bytes and resolves the future; resolving schedules the waiting task's step callback with `call_soon`,\n   so it lands on the ready queue too.\n4. **Drain the ready queue.** Run the callbacks that were ready when this iteration began, in FIFO order,\n   each task until its next `await`. New work waits for the next iteration, *because* a task that keeps\n   rescheduling itself could otherwise starve the selector and every timer.\n\n**Why \"cooperative\":** a task runs until it voluntarily yields at an `await` on a not-yet-ready future.\n- Awaiting an already-complete future, or a plain coroutine that never touches I/O, does not yield at all.\n- `await asyncio.sleep(0)` is the explicit \"let others run\" hint, useful inside a long parsing step.\n\n### Design choices and their reasons\n- **Single thread**, *because* it removes data races between awaits: between two `await`s, no other task\n  can touch your agent state. It does not remove races *across* an await (read memory, await the model,\n  write memory); that is what `asyncio.Lock` is for.\n- **The cost of one thread.** A blocking call (`time.sleep`, `requests.get` inside a tool, a CPU-heavy\n  loop) freezes every task, because there is no other thread to keep the loop turning.\n- **Explicit `await` points**, *because* they make interleaving visible in the source. A sync-looking line\n  can never secretly switch tasks, which is why most agent code is reasonable without locks.\n- **`create_task` schedules rather than runs**, *because* the creator should continue; the new task starts\n  on a later loop iteration. `asyncio.gather(*coros)` wraps each coroutine in a task and returns results in\n  order; `asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11+) does the same with structured cancellation.\n- **Blocking work goes off the thread** with `asyncio.to_thread(fn, *args)` (a `ThreadPoolExecutor` under\n  the hood) or `loop.run_in_executor(process_pool, fn)`; the result comes back as an awaitable future. The\n  loop stays responsive, but pure-Python work is still serialized by the GIL.\n\n### Coordinating parallel tool calls and sub-agents\nThe primitives differ in what they give back and when, which is the real choice:\n\n| Primitive | Returns | Use it in an agent when |\n|---|---|---|\n| `gather` | All results, input order, once every child is done | The model asked for three tools and needs all three results in the next message |\n| `as_completed` | Results in finish order | Stream partial findings to the UI as each sub-agent finishes |\n| `asyncio.wait(..., return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)` | `(done, pending)` sets; pending tasks keep running | Race two search backends, take the first good answer, cancel the rest |\n| `TaskGroup` | Like `gather`, with structured cleanup | One failed tool should cancel its siblings |\n| `asyncio.Queue(maxsize=N)` + fixed workers | Whatever workers produce | Batch evals or ingestion that must bound concurrency and memory |\n\n- **Queues give backpressure**, *because* producers block on `put` when consumers fall behind.\n- **`gather` does not**: over 10,000 coroutines it creates 10,000 tasks up front.\n\n### Measuring loop health\n- **Loop lag is the health number:** schedule a callback for time *t* and record when it actually runs.\n- **Measure it directly:** a task doing `await asyncio.sleep(0.5)` in a loop and logging\n  `actual_elapsed − 0.5`.\n- **Read it:** lag near zero means tasks are yielding. Lag that tracks your slowest synchronous tool means\n  something is blocking, and every agent session on that loop pays it.\n\n### Entry and exit\n- **`asyncio.run(main())`** creates a fresh loop, runs `main` as a task, then cancels still-pending tasks,\n  finalizes async generators and closes the loop. Use it in eval scripts and CLIs.\n- **Not inside a running loop.** It cannot be called there; Jupyter already runs one, so use `await main()`.\n- **Debug mode** (`PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` or `asyncio.run(..., debug=True)`) adds the creation traceback to\n  \"coroutine was never awaited\" warnings and logs callbacks that hog the loop longer than\n  `loop.slow_callback_duration` (100 ms by default). It is the first tool for finding a blocking tool.","example":"### Parallel tool calls in an agent step\nThe model returned three tool calls in one turn. Run them concurrently under a `TaskGroup`, give each a\ntimeout, push the one sync tool to a thread, and send every result back (errors included) in one message.\nThe model call is a stub so the example runs offline; swap in your provider's async client.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, inspect, json, time\n\nasync def call_model(messages):                     # stub: a real agent awaits the provider here\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.3)\n    if messages[-1][\"role\"] == \"tool\":\n        return {\"content\": \"Done.\", \"tool_calls\": []}\n    return {\"content\": None, \"tool_calls\": [\n        {\"id\": \"a\", \"name\": \"search\", \"args\": {\"q\": \"refund policy\"}},\n        {\"id\": \"b\", \"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"args\": {\"order\": 42}},\n        {\"id\": \"c\", \"name\": \"legacy_crm\", \"args\": {\"user\": 7}}]}\n\nasync def search(q):        await asyncio.sleep(1.0); return f\"3 docs for {q}\"\nasync def lookup_order(order): await asyncio.sleep(0.8); return {\"order\": order, \"status\": \"shipped\"}\ndef legacy_crm(user):       time.sleep(0.5); return {\"user\": user, \"tier\": \"gold\"}   # sync, blocking\n\nTOOLS = {\"search\": search, \"lookup_order\": lookup_order, \"legacy_crm\": legacy_crm}\n\nasync def run_tool(call, timeout=5.0):\n    fn = TOOLS[call[\"name\"]]\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(timeout):          # 3.11+: no tool may hang the step\n            if inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn):\n                out = await fn(**call[\"args\"])\n            else:\n                out = await asyncio.to_thread(fn, **call[\"args\"])   # keep the loop free\n        return {\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call[\"id\"], \"content\": json.dumps(out)}\n    except Exception as e:                            # report failures to the model, do not crash the run\n        return {\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call[\"id\"], \"content\": f\"error: {e!r}\"}\n\nasync def agent(task, max_steps=5):\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}]\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        reply = await call_model(messages)\n        if not reply[\"tool_calls\"]:\n            return reply[\"content\"]\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n            tasks = [tg.create_task(run_tool(c)) for c in reply[\"tool_calls\"]]\n        messages += [t.result() for t in tasks]\n        print(f\"{len(tasks)} tools in {time.perf_counter() - t0:.2f}s\")   # ~1.0s, not 2.3s\n    return \"step limit reached\"\n\nprint(asyncio.run(agent(\"Where is my order and can I get a refund?\")))\n```\n\n**The timeline:**\n1. `TaskGroup` wraps the three tool coroutines in tasks.\n2. On the next loop iteration each runs until its first not-ready await and yields, so all three are in\n   flight within a few milliseconds; the sync CRM call runs in a worker thread.\n3. The loop then blocks once in the selector for all of them, and resumes each task as its socket,\n   timer or thread future becomes ready.\n4. The step costs about the slowest tool (1.0 s) instead of the sum (2.3 s).\n\n### Fan out twenty chat completions\nTwenty calls share one HTTP connection pool; a blocking tokenizer step moves to a thread so it cannot\nstall the loop. Point `API` at any OpenAI-compatible server (a local vLLM or Ollama instance works). This\nis the shape of an eval harness running twenty agent prompts.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nimport httpx\n\nAPI = \"http://localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions\"\nHEADERS = {\"Authorization\": \"Bearer local\"}\n\nasync def ask(client: httpx.AsyncClient, question: str) -> str:\n    body = {\"model\": \"small-chat\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]}\n    r = await client.post(API, headers=HEADERS, json=body, timeout=60)   # the await is where we yield\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    return r.json()[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"]\n\ndef count_tokens(text: str) -> int:\n    time.sleep(0.05)                      # pretend this is a slow, blocking tokenizer\n    return len(text.split())\n\nasync def main(questions: list[str]) -> None:\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:          # one connection pool for every call\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        answers = await asyncio.gather(*(ask(client, q) for q in questions))\n        # blocking work goes to a thread so the loop keeps serving other tasks meanwhile\n        counts = await asyncio.gather(*(asyncio.to_thread(count_tokens, a) for a in answers))\n        print(f\"{len(questions)} calls in {time.perf_counter() - t0:.2f}s, {sum(counts)} tokens\")\n\nasyncio.run(main([f\"Summarise item {i} in one line.\" for i in range(20)]))\n```\n\n### Worked numbers: what the fan-out buys and what caps it\n| Scenario | Wall time | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sequential, each call 1.5 s | about 20 × 1.5 = 30 s | One call at a time |\n| `gather` over 20 calls | roughly 1.5 s plus scheduling overhead | About the slowest single call |\n| Server allows four parallel requests | the other sixteen queue | Server-side cap |\n| Client pool full | request 101 waits for a free connection | `httpx` allows 100 connections by default |\n| 20 × `time.sleep(0.05)` inline in the coroutine | one full second, back to back | No other task (say another user's agent stream) gets a single step |\n| Same 20 via `to_thread` | roughly 50 ms | Default executor has `min(32, cpu_count + 4)` threads, so all twenty run at once on an 8-core machine, and `time.sleep` releases the GIL |\n| 20 pure-Python CPU loops via `to_thread` | no overlap | Loop stays responsive but the GIL serialises them; use a process pool |\n\n### A loop-lag probe you can run\nA blocking tool is invisible in CPU graphs but obvious in lag. This prints lag every 0.5 s.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nasync def lag_probe(interval=0.5):\n    while True:\n        t = time.perf_counter()\n        await asyncio.sleep(interval)\n        lag = time.perf_counter() - t - interval\n        print(f\"loop lag {lag * 1000:.0f} ms\")\n\nasync def main():\n    probe = asyncio.create_task(lag_probe())\n    await asyncio.sleep(1.2)\n    time.sleep(1.0)          # blocking call on the loop thread\n    await asyncio.sleep(1.2)\n    probe.cancel()\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n# Prints lag of a few ms, then one reading of about 700 ms (the probe was due mid-block), then a few ms again.\n```","patterns":["**Run the model's parallel tool calls concurrently.** When one turn returns several tool calls, run them under a `TaskGroup` (or `gather(..., return_exceptions=True)`) and convert each failure into a tool result message, so one bad tool does not lose the step. Monitor tool latency per name and step latency.","**Bound every fan-out.** When an agent or eval harness calls a provider for many items, wrap each call in `async with sem:` where `sem = asyncio.Semaphore(32)` (size it to the provider's rate limit), or use a `Queue` with N workers. Monitor 429 rate, in-flight count and queue depth.","**One shared client per process.** Create a single `httpx.AsyncClient` (and one provider SDK client) at startup (FastAPI lifespan), size it with `httpx.Limits(max_connections=...)`, and close it on shutdown. Monitor pool-wait timeouts and connection errors.","**Timeouts on every external await.** Wrap model, tool and MCP calls in `asyncio.timeout(seconds)` (3.11+) or `asyncio.wait_for`, so one hung socket cannot hold an agent run forever. Add an overall run budget too. Monitor timeout counts per dependency.","**Export loop lag and debug in staging.** Run the lag probe as a background task and publish lag as a gauge; alert when p99 lag approaches your step latency budget. In staging set `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` and a lower `loop.slow_callback_duration` (for example 0.05 s); keep it off in production for overhead.","**Keep references to background tasks.** For fire-and-forget work such as writing a trace or memory update, follow the docs pattern: add each task to a set and call `task.add_done_callback(tasks.discard)`, or run them in a `TaskGroup`. Monitor the set's size for leaks."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Each agent step is \"call the model, then call one or more tools, then\n  call the model again\". Every one of those is network I/O, so the loop decides whether a worker serves\n  ten agent sessions or a thousand, and whether three tool calls the model asked for run in parallel.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The async clients of the OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs, `httpx`,\n  FastAPI routes, the MCP SDK, and the async entry points of agent frameworks (LangGraph's `ainvoke` and\n  `astream`, for example) all sit on an asyncio loop.\n- **What you control.** Fan-out of parallel tool calls (`TaskGroup`, `gather`), a concurrency cap per\n  provider (`Semaphore`), a timeout on every tool and model await, and keeping sync tools off the loop\n  with `asyncio.to_thread`.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing your own event loop, transports and protocols, or custom selectors.\n  Know what happens at an `await`, why one blocking tool freezes every session, and how to measure lag.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Parallel tool calls in one agent step.** When the model asks for three tools at once, a `TaskGroup`\n  runs them together so the step takes as long as the slowest tool, not the sum.\n- **Many agent sessions per worker.** A single async FastAPI worker holds hundreds of concurrent chat\n  sessions, because each one spends most of its time awaiting the model.\n- **Streaming agent responses.** Tokens and tool events flow to the browser over SSE while the same loop\n  keeps serving other users.\n- **Bulk evals and embedding jobs.** A `Semaphore`-capped fan-out runs thousands of eval prompts or\n  embedding batches against a provider without tripping its rate limits.","mini_project":"**Build an async agent step runner and find the blocking tool (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show that parallel tool calls on one event loop cut step latency, and that one sync tool\n  freezes every session.\n- **Steps:** write three fake async tools that `await asyncio.sleep` for 1, 2 and 3 seconds; run them\n  sequentially, then in a `TaskGroup`, and time both; add a fourth tool that calls `time.sleep(2)`, run\n  ten simulated sessions at once and log event-loop lag; then wrap that tool in `asyncio.to_thread` and\n  rerun.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of step latency (sequential versus parallel) and loop lag (blocking\n  versus `to_thread`), plus a short note on which change fixed which number."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Earliest timer deadline\"] --> S[\"Selector waits on all sockets\"]\n    S -->|\"model or tool socket ready\"| F[\"Handler resolves the future\"]\n    F -->|\"call_soon task step\"| R[\"Ready queue (FIFO)\"]\n    R --> X[\"Run agent task until next await\"]\n    X -->|\"awaits model or tool\"| W[\"Register callback, yield\"]\n    W --> T\n    X -->|\"blocking tool (sync SDK)\"| B[\"Loop frozen: all sessions wait\"]\n    B --> X\n    X -->|\"run finishes\"| D[\"Task done, result set\"]","caption":"One loop iteration: the thread blocks only in the selector, then runs each ready agent task until its next\n`await`. A blocking tool call inside a task step stops every session on the loop."},"interview":[{"q":"What actually happens when a coroutine hits an await on a model or tool call?","a":"- **The task registers and yields.** The awaited future is not ready, so the task adds a callback to it\n  and returns control to the event loop; its frame and locals (the agent's messages) stay suspended.\n- **The loop waits for all sockets at once.** The selector (`epoll`, `kqueue`, IOCP) blocks on behalf of\n  every task, and when the socket is readable the handler resolves the future.\n- **Resolution reschedules the task.** `call_soon` puts its step on the ready queue, and it resumes\n  exactly after the `await` on a later iteration."},{"q":"The model returns four tool calls in one turn. How do you execute them, and what about failures?","a":"- **Run them concurrently** under a `TaskGroup` or `gather`, because they are independent network waits:\n  the step costs about the slowest tool instead of the sum.\n- **Give each a timeout** with `asyncio.timeout`, and push sync tools through `asyncio.to_thread`.\n- **Turn failures into tool results** (an error string with the tool call id) rather than letting one\n  exception cancel the siblings and kill the run, so the model can retry or route around it."},{"q":"Is asyncio agent code free of race conditions because it is single-threaded?","a":"- **Free between awaits, not across them.** No other task can run between two `await`s, so a plain\n  read-modify-write with no await in the middle is safe.\n- **A read, await, write sequence is not safe**, because another task (a parallel tool or sub-agent) can\n  change shared memory while the first is suspended. Guard that section with `asyncio.Lock` or restructure\n  so the write does not depend on a stale read."},{"q":"You must embed 50,000 chunks for an agent's RAG index through a provider capped at 32 concurrent requests. How do you write it?","a":"- **Do not `gather` all 50,000 coroutines**, because that creates 50,000 tasks up front, floods the\n  provider with 429s and holds every result and socket in memory.\n- **Bound concurrency:** an `asyncio.Semaphore(32)` around each call, or an `asyncio.Queue(maxsize=N)`\n  with 32 workers, which also gives backpressure to the producer.\n- **Add per-call timeouts and retries**, and monitor in-flight count, 429 rate and queue depth."},{"q":"When would you not use asyncio, or move work off the loop?","a":"- **CPU-bound Python** (re-ranking loops, parsing large files) gains nothing, because the loop thread\n  is the bottleneck; use a process pool via `loop.run_in_executor`.\n- **Blocking tool libraries with no async version** go through `asyncio.to_thread`, accepting that the\n  default executor has `min(32, cpu_count + 4)` threads and pure-Python work stays GIL-serialized.\n- **Mostly-sync codebases** may be simpler with a thread pool than with function colour everywhere."},{"q":"You raise the fan-out semaphore from 32 to 200, but throughput barely moves and some calls fail with PoolTimeout. Why?","a":"- **The client pool is the real cap.** A default `httpx.AsyncClient` allows 100 connections, so above\n  100 in-flight calls the extra tasks wait for a free connection instead of sending.\n- **Pool waits count against the timeout.** httpx's default timeout (5 seconds) also covers acquiring a\n  connection from the pool, so tasks queued behind 3-second LLM calls can raise `httpx.PoolTimeout`.\n- **Match the numbers:** size `httpx.Limits(max_connections=...)` to the semaphore and keep both under the\n  provider's concurrency limit, because the provider's cap (seen as 429s) is the one you cannot change."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheap waits vs CPU work.** Thousands of concurrent agent waits cost kilobytes each instead of a thread apiece, but once a step is CPU-bound Python (local re-ranking, big JSON parsing) the single loop thread is the bottleneck and threads or processes win.","**Auditable interleaving vs function colour.** Explicit await points make interleaving visible, but \"colour\" is contagious: an async agent loop cannot call a blocking tool library without `to_thread`, so library choice (httpx vs requests, async vs sync database driver) is dictated by the loop.","**No preemption vs blast radius.** Cooperative scheduling has no preemption overhead and needs no locks for most state, but one misbehaving tool (a tight loop, a sync HTTP call) degrades every agent session on the loop, where a thread pool would only lose one worker.","**Concurrency vs debuggability.** A traceback shows the await chain inside one task but not which task spawned it, and profilers see interleaved task steps under the loop's frames. Named tasks, debug mode and per-run trace IDs (LangSmith or OpenTelemetry spans) close most of the gap."],"pitfalls":["**Blocking inside a coroutine** (`time.sleep`, the sync OpenAI client, `requests` in a tool, `pandas.read_csv` on a large file). Symptom: p99 step latency climbs under load while CPU sits idle, and debug mode logs \"Executing <Task ...> took 2.3 seconds\". Fix: async client or `to_thread`.","**Forgetting to `await` a coroutine.** Nothing runs. Symptom: `RuntimeWarning: coroutine 'x' was never awaited` and a tool or memory write that silently does nothing.","**Fire-and-forget `asyncio.create_task(...)` without a reference.** The loop holds tasks weakly, so a task can be garbage-collected mid-flight. Symptom: trace uploads or memory writes that sometimes vanish. Fix: store tasks in a set and discard on completion, or use a `TaskGroup`.","**`asyncio.run` inside a running loop** (a FastAPI handler, a notebook, a framework callback). Symptom: `RuntimeError: asyncio.run() cannot be called from a running event loop`. Fix: `await` directly.","**Treating `gather` as a limiter.** Fanning out 5,000 embedding calls or eval cases at once floods the provider and your own sockets. Symptom: a wave of 429s and connection errors. Fix: bound it with a `Semaphore`."],"when":"- **Use when** time is dominated by waiting on many independent network operations: LLM and embedding\n  calls, tool and MCP servers, vector stores, streaming to many clients. That is most of an agent service,\n  which is why FastAPI, httpx, the OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs and the MCP SDK all expose async APIs.\n- **Avoid when** the goal is speeding up CPU-bound code; asyncio will not help and makes that code harder\n  to run. A one-off sync script calling one model is fine without it.\n- **Measure:** event-loop lag (time between scheduling a callback and it running), the number of pending\n  tasks, and per-step latency of agent runs; together they tell you whether the loop is healthy.\n- **Interviews:** explain what happens at an `await`, why a blocking tool hurts every session, and when you\n  would move work to a thread or process instead.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async handlers and clients, fans out model and tool calls with `gather` or `TaskGroup`, and keeps blocking work off the loop with `to_thread`.","architect":"Decides which services are async-native and where the sync/async boundaries sit, so one blocking dependency cannot stall a whole worker process.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"slow under load but CPU is idle\" by finding the blocking call on their loop, often a sync SDK inside an async route."},"check":{"q":"A FastAPI service declares its chat route `async def`, and inside it calls the provider with a\nsynchronous SDK client. At ten concurrent users p99 latency is about thirty seconds while CPU sits at\nfive percent. Explain the mechanism and give two fixes with their trade-offs.","a":"The route runs on the event loop thread, and the sync client blocks that thread for the whole\nprovider round-trip, so every other request on the worker waits in line: with ten users and a\nthree-second call, the last one waits around thirty seconds, which matches the symptom, and CPU is idle\nbecause the thread is parked on a socket. Fix one: switch to the SDK's async client so the call becomes\nan `await` and the loop keeps serving others; this is the right default but requires async-compatible\ncode throughout the call chain. Fix two: keep the sync client but call it via\n`await asyncio.to_thread(...)`, or declare the route with plain `def` so the framework runs it in its\nthread pool; this is a smaller change, but the pool is bounded (AnyIO, which FastAPI uses, defaults to\n40 threads), so concurrency caps at its size and each call still costs a thread. To confirm the\ndiagnosis before fixing, turn on asyncio debug mode or a loop-lag probe: lag of about three seconds per\nrequest is the fingerprint."},"quiz":[{"q":"A coroutine on the event loop calls `time.sleep(2)`. What happens to the other tasks?","options":["They continue, because the loop runs each task in its own thread","They all stall for two seconds, because the one loop thread is blocked and cannot resume anyone","Only tasks waiting on the same socket stall","The loop detects the block and migrates the call to a thread automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"asyncio is single-threaded and cooperative. A blocking call never returns control to the loop, so no ready callback or timer can run until it finishes. Only `asyncio.sleep` or `to_thread` keep the loop free while waiting."},{"q":"Why should you keep a reference to a task created with `asyncio.create_task`?","options":["Tasks cannot start until they are awaited by someone","The result is discarded unless a reference exists","The loop keeps only weak references, so an unreferenced task can be garbage-collected before it finishes","`create_task` returns a coroutine, not a task, until it is referenced"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The task starts on a later loop iteration regardless, but the event loop does not own it. Without a strong reference elsewhere, garbage collection may destroy it mid-execution, which is why the docs recommend storing tasks in a collection or using a TaskGroup."},{"q":"Which workload gains the most from moving to asyncio?","options":["Re-ranking 10,000 candidates with a pure-Python scoring loop","Calling an embeddings API for 2,000 chunks with a concurrency cap of 32","Parsing a 5 GB JSONL file into pandas","Training a small model with PyTorch on a GPU"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The embeddings fan-out is I/O-bound: each call waits on the network, so one thread can keep 32 requests in flight. The other options are CPU- or GPU-bound and would not benefit; they would block the loop instead."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/async-cancellation-and-exception-propagation","sources":["Official docs: asyncio (Coroutines and Tasks, Task Groups, Timeouts)","PEP 654 — Exception Groups and except*"],"why":"- **Concurrent work must stop cleanly.** An agent turn runs three tool calls and a retrieval query; then\n  the user closes the tab, the 30-second deadline passes, or one tool returns a fatal error that makes the\n  others pointless.\n- **Stopping badly is expensive.** An LLM stream keeps generating (and, depending on the provider, keeps\n  billing), a connection never returns to the pool, a checkpoint store gets a half-written row.\n- **Killing from outside is unsafe.** Interrupting a thread or coroutine at an arbitrary instruction\n  leaves locks held and sockets half-written, which is why Python offers no safe way to kill a thread.\n- **A `stop` flag only works if every piece of code checks it**, and library code you call (provider SDKs,\n  framework internals) never will.\n- **Ignored background errors are worse.** A task that raised but was never awaited loses its exception\n  until garbage collection logs \"Task exception was never retrieved\", long after the agent returned a\n  wrong answer.\n\n**asyncio's answer:** cooperative cancellation delivered as an exception. A coroutine can only be\nsuspended at an `await`, so that is the only place it can be interrupted; cancellation arrives at a known\npoint and travels up the stack through ordinary `try/finally` and `async with` cleanup. Structured\nconcurrency (`TaskGroup`) then supplies rules for siblings when one child fails.","how":"### Cancellation is a request, delivered at an await\n`task.cancel()` does three things:\n1. **Cancel what it waits on.** If the task is suspended on a future, it cancels *that* future. If it\n   was awaiting another task (`await other_task`), the inner task is cancelled too, so the request\n   travels **down** the await chain to whatever is actually waiting on I/O (the model stream, the tool's\n   HTTP call).\n2. **Wake the task.** Cancelling the future wakes the task on the next loop iteration, exactly as a\n   result would.\n3. **Throw.** On resume, `CancelledError` is thrown into the coroutine at the suspended `await`, and\n   travels **up** the call stack.\n\nWhat follows from that:\n- **Running or ready tasks only get a flag;** the throw happens at the next suspension point.\n- **Code between awaits runs to completion;** a CPU loop with no await cannot be cancelled at all.\n- **Unwinding is ordinary:** `finally` blocks run, `async with` exits run (the `httpx` client closes the\n  stream, the lock is released).\n- **`BaseException` since Python 3.8.** `CancelledError` subclasses `BaseException`, not `Exception`,\n  *because* the common `except Exception:` retry or logging block used to catch it by accident and keep\n  the task alive.\n\n**The contract:** catch `CancelledError` to clean up or log, but always re-raise it. A task that swallows\nit reports as completed, and whoever cancelled it (a timeout, a TaskGroup, server shutdown) believes the\ncancellation failed or never happened.\n\n### Timeouts are cancellation plus translation\n1. `asyncio.timeout(delay)` (3.11+) schedules a cancel of the current task at the deadline.\n2. When that `CancelledError` reaches the `async with` exit, the context manager recognises it as its\n   own and converts it into `TimeoutError`.\n\n- **`asyncio.wait_for`** is built on `asyncio.timeout` since 3.12. Before that it ran the awaitable as a\n  separate inner task and cancelled that task, with the same cancel-then-translate shape.\n- **Hence the bug:** a swallowed cancellation inside a timeout block makes the deadline silently not fire,\n  so an agent with a 30-second turn budget can run for minutes.\n- **Telling cancellations apart.** Python 3.11 added `Task.cancelling()` and `Task.uncancel()` so these\n  constructs can distinguish their own cancellation from one requested from outside.\n\n### Where a task's exception goes\n- **Stored, not raised.** An exception inside a task is stored on the task.\n- **It surfaces only** when someone awaits the task (re-raised in the awaiter with its original\n  traceback) or calls `task.result()` / `task.exception()`.\n- **Otherwise it is lost for a while.** The loop logs \"Task exception was never retrieved\" when the task\n  object is garbage-collected, which may be minutes later or never.\n- **So every task needs an owner:** something that awaits it, a TaskGroup, or a done-callback that logs.\n  A background memory write or trace upload without an owner fails invisibly.\n- **`asyncio.wait` follows the same rule:** it never raises children's exceptions; it hands you `done`\n  tasks whose results you must still check.\n\n### Propagation: gather versus TaskGroup\n| Construct | When a child (tool) fails | Other children | Error you see |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `asyncio.gather(*aws)`, default `return_exceptions=False` | First exception propagates to the caller | Keep running, orphaned | The first exception |\n| `gather(..., return_exceptions=True)` | Exceptions returned as list items | Keep running | None raised; inspect every result |\n| Cancelling the gather itself | — | All cancelled | `CancelledError` |\n| `asyncio.TaskGroup` (3.11+) | Group cancels remaining children (and the `async with` body if still running), waits for them to unwind | Cancelled, cleanup runs | `ExceptionGroup` of every child failure |\n\n- **No child outlives the block** in a TaskGroup; that is structured concurrency, and it means no tool\n  writes to a CRM after the turn already returned.\n- **Cancelled children are not reported as failures**, *because* the group itself caused them.\n- **Handle the group with `except*`**, which matches by type inside the group, *because* concurrent code\n  can genuinely fail in several ways at once and a single exception would hide the others.\n\n### Shielding\n- **What it does.** `asyncio.shield(aw)` protects an inner awaitable from a cancellation aimed at the\n  outer task.\n- **Why it is needed.** Because of step 1 above, cancelling the outer task would otherwise cancel the\n  inner one it awaits. `shield` inserts a separate future between them, so the outer `await` still raises\n  `CancelledError` while the inner work continues.\n- **Keep a reference to the inner task**, or it can be garbage-collected once nobody awaits it.\n- **Use it narrowly**, for work that must complete even when the requester is gone, such as committing an\n  audit record or a usage row for billing.","example":"### Fail-fast parallel tool calls with a turn deadline\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def call(name: str, delay: float, fail: bool = False) -> str:\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(delay)            # stands in for an HTTP call\n        if fail:\n            raise ValueError(f\"{name}: malformed tool output\")\n        return f\"{name}: ok\"\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print(f\"{name}: cancelled, closing stream\")\n        raise                                 # re-raise, always\n    finally:\n        print(f\"{name}: connection released\")\n\nasync def agent_turn() -> None:\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(2.0):      # overall turn deadline\n            async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:\n                tg.create_task(call(\"retriever\", 0.5))\n                tg.create_task(call(\"calculator\", 0.2, fail=True))\n                tg.create_task(call(\"llm\", 5.0))\n    except* ValueError as eg:\n        for exc in eg.exceptions:\n            print(\"tool failed:\", exc)\n    except* TimeoutError:\n        print(\"turn deadline exceeded\")\n\nasyncio.run(agent_turn())\n```\n\n**Timeline as written:**\n| Time | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 0 s | Three children start and suspend on their sleeps |\n| 0.2 s | The calculator raises `ValueError` and releases its connection |\n| just after | The TaskGroup cancels the retriever and the LLM call; each prints \"cancelled\" and \"connection released\" as `CancelledError` unwinds it |\n| then | The group raises an `ExceptionGroup`, which `except* ValueError` handles |\n\n**With `fail=False`:** the 2-second timeout fires instead. It cancels `agent_turn`, the group passes that\ncancellation to its children, the LLM call unwinds, and the caller sees `TimeoutError`.\n\n**If you delete the `raise` in the child:** this particular run would not break, because the timeout\nbelongs to `agent_turn`, not the child. But the cancelled child would then report success, and the same\nswallow in the task that owns a timeout makes the block exit normally, so no `TimeoutError` is ever raised.\n\n### Worked scenario: the Stop button that did not stop\nA support agent streams its answer while two tools run. The user clicks Stop 2 seconds in. Figures are\nillustrative.\n\n| Implementation | What keeps running after Stop | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `gather` and a tool that wraps its call in a bare `except:` returning `None` | The model stream finishes its remaining tokens; the swallowing tool completes its CRM write | Tokens billed for an answer nobody reads, and a side effect the user cancelled |\n| `TaskGroup`, every tool re-raises, stream iterated inside `async with` | Nothing; each child unwinds through `finally` within one loop iteration of its next await | Connections back in the pool, no late writes |\n| Same, plus the usage row written under `asyncio.shield` | Only the short usage write | Billing and audit stay correct |\n\nThe test below is how you prove the second row before shipping.\n\n### Test that cancellation actually cleans up\nA test that cancels mid-call and checks resources are back to idle catches swallowed cancellations\nbefore production does. Runs on Python 3.8+.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nin_flight = 0\n\nasync def stream_llm(swallow: bool = False):\n    global in_flight\n    in_flight += 1\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(10)          # stands in for a long LLM stream\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        if not swallow:\n            raise                        # correct: re-raise\n        return \"partial\"                 # bug: swallows the cancellation\n    finally:\n        in_flight -= 1                   # runs on cancellation too\n\nasync def cancel_mid_call(swallow: bool) -> None:\n    t = asyncio.create_task(stream_llm(swallow))\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.1)             # let it start and suspend\n    assert in_flight == 1\n    t.cancel()\n    try:\n        await t\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        pass\n    print(f\"swallow={swallow}: cancelled={t.cancelled()}, in_flight={in_flight}\")\n\nasyncio.run(cancel_mid_call(swallow=False))   # cancelled=True, in_flight=0\nasyncio.run(cancel_mid_call(swallow=True))    # cancelled=False, in_flight=0\n```\nIn both runs the `finally` releases the resource, but only the correct version reports\n`cancelled=True`; the swallowing one looks like a normal return to whoever cancelled it. Assert on\n`t.cancelled()` in tests of every tool wrapper.","patterns":["**Nested deadline budget per agent run.** Give each run and each turn an outer `asyncio.timeout(...)` and each tool or model call a shorter inner one, so inner deadlines fire first with a specific error the agent can report to the model. Monitor timeout counts per layer; outer timeouts firing means an inner one is missing or too long.","**Owner for every task.** Put parallel tool calls in a `TaskGroup`; for true background work (trace upload, memory consolidation), add a done-callback that logs `t.exception()` when `not t.cancelled()`. Alert on \"Task exception was never retrieved\" in logs.","**Close the upstream stream on disconnect.** In a streaming agent endpoint, iterate the provider stream inside `async with` so cancellation closes it, and check `await request.is_disconnected()` (Starlette) in the loop. Monitor upstream tokens generated after client disconnect.","**Bound cleanup awaits.** Inside `finally`, wrap slow cleanup (checkpoint flush, sandbox teardown) in its own `asyncio.timeout(...)` so a hung close cannot stall shutdown. Monitor shutdown duration against the process manager's kill timeout.","**Shield only short, critical writes.** Create the audit or billing write as its own task, keep it in a set, and `await asyncio.shield(task)`. Monitor the number of shielded tasks in flight during incidents."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent turn is a tree of concurrent awaits: a model stream, parallel\n  tool calls, a retrieval query, maybe sub-agents. Cancellation is how that tree stops when the user\n  leaves, a step budget runs out or one tool fails, without leaking tokens or half-done side effects.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Client disconnects on streaming FastAPI routes, per-tool and\n  per-turn `asyncio.timeout`, `TaskGroup` around parallel tool calls, and the async run and stream methods\n  of agent frameworks, which cancel your tool coroutines when the run is cancelled.\n- **What you control.** Re-raising `CancelledError` in every tool, releasing resources in `finally` or\n  `async with`, choosing fail-fast (`TaskGroup`) or best-effort (`gather(return_exceptions=True)`) per\n  step, and shielding only short critical writes such as an audit record.\n- **What you can skip.** The internals of `Task.uncancel()` bookkeeping and the pre-3.12 `wait_for`\n  implementation. Know the contract, the TaskGroup versus gather table and how to test it.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **User presses Stop mid-turn.** Cancelling the turn's task closes the model stream and aborts the\n  in-flight tool calls instead of letting them run on and bill.\n- **Per-turn and per-tool deadlines.** `asyncio.timeout` around each tool and the whole turn keeps a slow\n  search API from holding a session past its latency budget.\n- **Fail-fast parallel tools.** A `TaskGroup` cancels the sibling tools as soon as one hits a fatal error,\n  so the agent can re-plan without waiting on pointless work.\n- **Client disconnects on streaming routes.** When the browser drops an SSE connection, cancellation\n  unwinds the agent loop and releases its connections and sandboxes.\n- **Audit writes that must land.** A short shielded write records what the agent did, even when the turn\n  around it is cancelled.","mini_project":"**Make an agent turn stop cleanly (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove that cancelling a turn stops every tool, runs every cleanup and keeps deadlines\n  working.\n- **Steps:** build a fake turn that streams \"tokens\" and runs three tools in a `TaskGroup`, each logging\n  in `finally`; cancel the turn after 1 second and check all cleanups ran; give one tool its own\n  `asyncio.timeout` and make it swallow `CancelledError` inside that block, then show its `TimeoutError`\n  never fires; fix it by re-raising; finally compare a failing tool under `TaskGroup` with\n  `gather(return_exceptions=True)`.\n- **Done when:** a pytest file asserts cleanup on cancel, timeout firing, and the fail-fast versus\n  best-effort behaviour, and the swallowing tool's test fails until you fix it."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant TO as asyncio.timeout block\n    participant T as Task (agent turn)\n    participant I as Inner await (model stream)\n    participant L as Event loop\n    TO->>T: deadline reached, task.cancel()\n    T->>I: cancel the future it is waiting on\n    L->>T: wake task on next iteration\n    I-->>T: CancelledError thrown at the await\n    Note over T: finally and async with exits run, stream closed\n    T-->>TO: CancelledError re-raised up the stack\n    Note over TO: recognises its own cancel, converts it\n    TO-->>T: TimeoutError raised to the caller\n    Note over T,TO: if the task swallowed CancelledError, no TimeoutError appears","caption":"A turn deadline firing: the cancel request travels down to the model stream, `CancelledError` travels back\nup through `finally`, and the timeout block converts it to `TimeoutError` only if nobody swallowed it."},"interview":[{"q":"How does task.cancel() actually stop a coroutine in asyncio?","a":"- **It is a request, not a kill.** `task.cancel()` cancels the future the task is suspended on (and\n  any task it awaits), then wakes it so `CancelledError` is thrown at that `await`.\n- **It unwinds through ordinary cleanup.** `finally` blocks and `async with` exits run on the way up,\n  so connections and locks are released.\n- **Code between awaits runs to completion**, so a CPU loop with no await cannot be cancelled."},{"q":"An agent's 30-second turn timeout sometimes never fires. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect a swallowed cancellation.** `asyncio.timeout` works by cancelling the task and converting\n  the `CancelledError` at block exit; a bare `except:` or `except BaseException:` without re-raise in a\n  tool or retry wrapper leaves nothing to convert.\n- **Grep for broad handlers** and retry wrappers in the call path, and add a test that cancels mid-call\n  and asserts `t.cancelled()` is true.\n- **Also check for blocking calls** such as `requests.get` in a tool, which keep the task cancelling until\n  they return on their own."},{"q":"When would you choose gather with return_exceptions=True over a TaskGroup for tool calls?","a":"- **Independent, best-effort children:** a batch of embedding calls or optional enrichment tools where\n  one failure should not cancel the rest, and the model can reason over partial results.\n- **The cost is manual inspection.** Exceptions come back as list items, siblings are never cancelled,\n  and you must check every result yourself.\n- **Choose TaskGroup** when every result matters or a failure makes the others pointless, because it\n  cancels siblings and reports an `ExceptionGroup`."},{"q":"A user presses Stop on a streaming agent. What must happen, and how do you verify it?","a":"- **The route's task is cancelled** when the client disconnects, and the cancellation must reach the\n  provider stream and every running tool, so iterate the stream inside `async with` and run tools in a\n  `TaskGroup`.\n- **Every tool re-raises `CancelledError`** and releases its connection in `finally`; only a short usage\n  or audit write runs under `asyncio.shield`.\n- **Verify with metrics and tests:** upstream tokens generated after disconnect should be near zero, and a\n  test that cancels mid-call should see `t.cancelled()` true and an idle pool."},{"q":"What does asyncio.shield protect, and what are the risks in production?","a":"- **It protects inner work from outer cancellation.** The outer `await` still raises `CancelledError`,\n  but the inner awaitable keeps running, which suits committing an audit or billing record.\n- **Keep a reference** to the inner task, or it can be garbage-collected once nobody awaits it.\n- **Shielded work outlives the request**, so during an incident it can pile up; keep it short and\n  bounded and monitor how many shielded tasks are in flight."},{"q":"Why did Python 3.8 make CancelledError a BaseException, and what changed for retry code?","a":"- **Accidental swallowing.** Retry and logging blocks written as `except Exception:` used to catch\n  cancellation and keep the task alive, breaking timeouts and shutdown.\n- **Now generic handlers let it pass**, but anything catching `BaseException` or using a bare `except:`\n  must still re-raise it, and code on Python 3.7 and earlier keeps the old risk."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fail-fast vs partial results.** TaskGroup stops wasted work and guarantees no orphans, but one flaky optional tool cancels the whole fan-out. When partial results are acceptable (the model can work with two of three tool results), catch errors inside each child and return them as tool messages.","**Keep everything vs manual inspection.** `gather(return_exceptions=True)` keeps every result and never cancels siblings, at the cost of manual inspection. It is the better choice when children are independent and best-effort, such as an eval batch.","**Safe cleanup vs reach.** Cooperative cancellation keeps cleanup safe and predictable, but cannot interrupt CPU-bound or blocking code. Such work must run in a thread or process, where cancellation only abandons the wait.","**Guaranteed completion vs pile-up.** `shield` makes critical work finish, but that work outlives the request and can pile up during an incident. It is only safe when the shielded work is short and bounded."],"pitfalls":["**Swallowing cancellation in a tool wrapper.** A bare `except:` or `except BaseException:` without re-raise (or `except Exception` on Python 3.7 and earlier). Symptom: timeouts that never fire and shutdowns that hang, when the swallow runs in the task that owns the deadline (an inline `await`, not a separate child task). Fix: re-raise.","**Fire-and-forget `asyncio.create_task(...)`** without keeping a reference or awaiting it. Symptom: \"Task exception was never retrieved\" in logs and occasionally tasks garbage-collected mid-flight. Fix: keep tasks in a set with a done-callback that discards and logs them, or use a `TaskGroup`.","**Assuming a `gather` failure stops the other tool calls.** Symptom: token spend and tool side effects continue after the agent already returned an error. Fix: `TaskGroup`.","**Unbounded awaits in `finally` during cancellation.** A slow cleanup call can itself hang, so shutdown stalls until the process manager kills it. Fix: a timeout around cleanup.","**Expecting cancel to stop a blocking `requests.get` or a tight loop.** Symptom: a task that stays \"cancelling\" until the blocking call returns on its own. Fix: use an async client, or run the call in `asyncio.to_thread` with its own timeout (for example `requests.get(url, timeout=10)`), because cancelling there only abandons the wait while the thread keeps running."],"when":"- **You meet it whenever** an agent starts more than one task or has a deadline, which is almost always:\n  per-request timeouts on model calls, client disconnects on streaming endpoints, parallel tool calls,\n  step budgets, graceful shutdown.\n- **Defaults:** `TaskGroup` for fan-out where all results matter, `asyncio.timeout` for deadlines,\n  `gather(return_exceptions=True)` for best-effort batches.\n- **Resources:** every tool coroutine that holds a resource releases it in `finally` or `async with`.\n- **Test it:** cancel mid-call and assert the connection pool and locks are back to idle.\n- **Interviews:** explain why `CancelledError` is a `BaseException` and how TaskGroup differs from gather\n  when one child fails.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes children that re-raise `CancelledError`, wraps fan-out in `TaskGroup`, and tests that a client disconnect actually closes the upstream LLM stream.","architect":"Sets the deadline budget across layers so inner timeouts are shorter than outer ones, and decides which side effects are shielded versus abandoned on cancel.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's hanging worker or runaway token bill by finding the swallowed cancellation or orphaned gather in their integration code."},"check":{"q":"An agent runs five tool calls with `asyncio.gather`. One raises; the endpoint returns 500, yet\nlogs show the other four tools finishing later and one writing to a CRM. Explain and fix.","a":"`gather` without `return_exceptions` propagates the first exception to the caller but does not\ncancel the remaining children, so they keep running as orphans and complete their side effects after\nthe response was sent. Switching to `asyncio.TaskGroup` cancels siblings when one fails and waits for\nthem to unwind before raising an `ExceptionGroup`, so nothing outlives the request. Each tool must\nre-raise `CancelledError` and release resources in `finally`. Because cancellation only lands at an\nawait, a CRM write already sent cannot be undone, so consequential tools also need idempotency keys\nor a compensating action."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does `CancelledError` inherit from `BaseException` rather than `Exception` in modern Python?","options":["So that it cannot be caught by any handler at all","So generic `except Exception` blocks for retries and logging do not swallow cancellation by accident","Because it is raised by the interpreter rather than by user code","So that it propagates across threads as well as tasks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Broad `except Exception` handlers are common in retry and logging code; when cancellation was an `Exception` they absorbed it and kept the task alive. It can still be caught explicitly, and must then be re-raised."},{"q":"A child inside `asyncio.TaskGroup` raises `ValueError` while two siblings are awaiting network calls. What happens?","options":["The ValueError propagates immediately and the siblings continue in the background","The siblings are killed instantly without running their finally blocks","The siblings are cancelled, the group waits for them to unwind, then raises an ExceptionGroup","The group retries the failed child once before failing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TaskGroup guarantees that no child outlives the block: it cancels the others, waits for their cleanup to run, and reports all failures together. The first option describes `gather`, not TaskGroup."},{"q":"A coroutine running `async with asyncio.timeout(5):` catches `CancelledError` and returns `None`. What is the effect?","options":["The timeout raises TimeoutError as normal after cleanup","The event loop logs a warning and re-raises automatically","Nothing changes, because timeouts do not use cancellation","The block exits normally, so the expected TimeoutError is never raised and the deadline is silently lost"],"answer":3,"explanation":"`asyncio.timeout` works by cancelling the task and converting the arriving `CancelledError` into `TimeoutError` at block exit; swallowing the exception means there is nothing to convert."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/race-conditions-and-synchronization-primitives","sources":["Official docs: asyncio (Synchronization Primitives)"],"why":"- **\"Async cannot race\" is half true.** Between two awaits a coroutine runs uninterrupted, so\n  `self.count += 1` on its own is safe in asyncio, unlike in threaded code. But real agent logic spans\n  awaits.\n- **Check-then-act across an await races.** \"If the auth token is expired, fetch a new one and store it\"\n  reads, awaits a network call, then writes. Meanwhile a hundred other requests see the same expired\n  token and each starts a refresh: a thundering herd on the auth server, and if refreshes invalidate\n  earlier tokens, some requests hold revoked ones. \"If budget remains, call the model, then deduct\" is the\n  same bug for agents.\n- **Unbounded fan-out exhausts resources.** `gather` over 5,000 prompts starts 5,000 HTTP requests at\n  once. The provider answers most with 429s, the connection pool queues the rest, memory fills with\n  pending responses, and timeouts fire on requests that never left the process. You need a gate that\n  lets N through.\n- **A boolean `refreshing` flag fails**, because it is itself check-then-act and needs a way to wait for\n  the refresh to finish, which is a lock with extra steps.\n- **A `threading.Lock` fails**, because when contended it blocks the whole event loop, stalling every\n  coroutine instead of just the waiter. Waiting must itself be an `await` that yields to the loop.","how":"### Where races come from\nAn asyncio race needs all three:\n1. **Shared mutable state** (an agent's budget, memory, cache).\n2. **A read and a dependent write.**\n3. **An `await` between them**, because every `await` is a point where the loop may run any other ready\n   coroutine, such as a sibling tool call.\n\n**Audit question per coroutine:** \"what shared state did I read before this await, and do I still rely\non it after?\"\n\nThe interleaving for the token refresh, with two requests A and B:\n\n```\nA: token expired? yes          B: (not yet run)\nA: await refresh()  -> yields\n                               B: token expired? yes   (A has not written yet)\n                               B: await refresh()  -> yields\nA: store token_1\n                               B: store token_2        (token_1 now revoked)\n```\n\n- **Not a torn write but a stale decision.** Nothing is corrupted, which is what threads give you.\n- **So it hides from tests.** asyncio races are logic bugs that pass unit tests (which run one coroutine at\n  a time) and appear only under concurrent load, or only when the model happens to request tools in\n  parallel.\n\n### asyncio.Lock\n- **Ownership.** At most one coroutine owns it. `async with lock:` awaits `acquire()`; if held, the\n  coroutine parks in a FIFO queue of waiters and the loop runs other work. Release wakes the next waiter.\n- **Waiting is nearly free**, *because* no thread is blocked; it costs a queued future.\n- **Uncontended is nearly free:** `acquire()` returns without suspending.\n- **Double-checked locking** for shared caches: check without the lock on the fast path, take the lock on a\n  miss, then check again inside, *because* another coroutine may have refreshed the value while you waited.\n- **Not reentrant.** Acquiring it again from the coroutine that holds it deadlocks.\n- **Cancellation-safe with `async with`.** A coroutine cancelled while holding the lock still releases it\n  in the exit; one cancelled while *waiting* is removed from the queue.\n\n### Single-flight: share the in-flight work instead of locking around it\nFor \"compute once, many waiters\", store the in-flight task itself:\n1. The first caller on a miss creates `self._refresh = asyncio.create_task(fetch())`.\n2. Every caller, first or later, does `await self._refresh`.\n3. All waiters get the same result, or the same exception, from one network call.\n\nTwo cares:\n- **Failure:** clear the stored task when it raises, or every later caller re-reads the cached error.\n- **Cancellation:** one caller being cancelled should not cancel the shared task; that is what\n  `asyncio.shield` around the await is for.\n\n### asyncio.Semaphore\n- **Mechanism.** A counter initialised to N. `acquire()` decrements it, or waits if it is zero;\n  `release()` increments it and wakes a waiter.\n- **Effect.** `async with sem:` around an LLM call keeps at most N calls in flight, however many agent\n  sessions and tool tasks exist.\n- **`BoundedSemaphore`** also raises if you release more times than you acquired, catching bookkeeping bugs.\n- **It caps *concurrency*, not rate.** Throughput is concurrency divided by latency (Little's law), so if\n  latency rises, the same semaphore yields fewer requests per second.\n- **Sizing:** to sustain λ requests per second at latency W seconds, you need N ≈ λ × W slots.\n\n```\nN ≈ λ × W        e.g. 20 requests/s × 1.5 s latency ≈ 30 slots\n```\n\n- **Too few slots** and you cannot reach the target; **many more** and you only queue inside the provider\n  instead of inside your process.\n- **Per-minute quotas need a rate limiter.** A limit in requests or tokens per minute needs something like a\n  token bucket, often combined with a semaphore.\n\n### The other primitives\n| Primitive | What it does | Typical agent use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `asyncio.Event` | Many coroutines wait until one sets a flag | \"model warmed up\", \"shutdown started\", \"user pressed Stop\" |\n| `asyncio.Condition` | A lock plus wait/notify | More complex predicates |\n| `asyncio.Queue(maxsize=N)` | Bounded producer/consumer queue | Often the best primitive: concurrency limits and backpressure without explicit locking, for eval runs and ingestion |\n\n### What they do not cover\n- **One event loop only.** They coordinate coroutines on one loop; they are not thread-safe and do nothing\n  across processes or replicas.\n- **Limits multiply per process.** Two Uvicorn workers each holding their own `Semaphore(8)` allow 16\n  concurrent calls.\n- **Cross-replica limits** need a shared store such as Redis or a gateway. So does agent state that must\n  survive across workers, such as a per-user budget.","example":"### Agent budget shared by parallel tool calls\nFour parallel tool calls each check the run's remaining budget, await an LLM sub-call, then deduct. Without\nthe lock all four pass the check before any deduction lands. Costs are illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nclass RunBudget:\n    def __init__(self, dollars: float) -> None:\n        self.left = dollars\n        self._lock = asyncio.Lock()\n\n    async def spend_racy(self, cost: float) -> bool:\n        if self.left < cost:                 # read\n            return False\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.1)             # model sub-call: other tools run here\n        self.left -= cost                    # write based on a stale read\n        return True\n\n    async def spend_safe(self, cost: float) -> bool:\n        async with self._lock:               # reserve before awaiting\n            if self.left < cost:\n                return False\n            self.left -= cost\n        try:\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.1)         # model sub-call outside the lock\n            return True\n        except BaseException:\n            async with self._lock:\n                self.left += cost            # refund if the call failed or was cancelled\n            raise\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    for name in (\"spend_racy\", \"spend_safe\"):\n        b = RunBudget(1.00)\n        ok = await asyncio.gather(*(getattr(b, name)(0.40) for _ in range(4)))\n        print(f\"{name}: approved={sum(ok)} left=${b.left:.2f}\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n# spend_racy: approved=4 left=$-0.60    spend_safe: approved=2 left=$0.20\n```\n\n- **Reserve, then await.** The safe version decides and deducts with no `await` inside the lock, so the\n  lock is held for microseconds rather than the whole model call.\n- **Refund on failure** keeps the budget honest when a tool errors or the turn is cancelled.\n\n### Token cache plus concurrency cap\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\n\nclass TokenCache:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._token: str | None = None\n        self._expires = 0.0\n        self._lock = asyncio.Lock()\n        self.refreshes = 0\n\n    def _valid(self) -> bool:\n        return self._token is not None and time.monotonic() < self._expires\n\n    async def get(self) -> str:\n        if self._valid():\n            return self._token            # fast path, no lock\n        async with self._lock:\n            if not self._valid():         # re-check: someone may have refreshed\n                self.refreshes += 1\n                await asyncio.sleep(0.1)  # stands in for the auth call\n                self._token, self._expires = \"tok\", time.monotonic() + 300\n            return self._token\n\nasync def call_llm(cache: TokenCache, sem: asyncio.Semaphore, i: int) -> int:\n    async with sem:                       # at most 8 requests in flight\n        await cache.get()\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.05)         # stands in for the model call\n        return i\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    cache, sem = TokenCache(), asyncio.Semaphore(8)\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(call_llm(cache, sem, i) for i in range(100)))\n    print(len(results), \"calls,\", cache.refreshes, \"token refresh\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n| Variant | Refreshes |\n|---|---|\n| As written | 1 (prints `100 calls, 1 token refresh`) |\n| Lock removed, `Semaphore(8)` | 8: the first eight through the semaphore all see no token and all await the auth call |\n| Lock removed, semaphore raised to 100 | 100 |\n\nThe bug scales with your concurrency setting, which is why it often appears only under load.\n\n### Single-flight loader\nFifty agent sessions, one index load. The shield keeps one caller's cancellation from killing the shared\nwork, and the callback clears a failed task so the next caller retries.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nclass SingleFlight:\n    def __init__(self, fetch):\n        self._fetch = fetch\n        self._task: asyncio.Task | None = None\n\n    async def get(self):\n        if self._task is None:                        # first caller on a miss starts the work\n            self._task = asyncio.create_task(self._fetch())\n            self._task.add_done_callback(self._clear_on_error)\n        return await asyncio.shield(self._task)       # a cancelled caller must not cancel the shared fetch\n\n    def _clear_on_error(self, t: asyncio.Task) -> None:\n        if t.cancelled() or t.exception() is not None:\n            self._task = None                         # next caller retries instead of re-reading the error\n\ncalls = 0\nasync def load_index():\n    global calls\n    calls += 1\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.2)                          # stands in for loading a model or index\n    return \"index\"\n\nasync def main():\n    sf = SingleFlight(load_index)\n    results = await asyncio.gather(*(sf.get() for _ in range(50)))\n    print(len(results), \"callers,\", calls, \"load\")    # 50 callers, 1 load\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n### Worked numbers: sizing with Little's law\n| Setting | Latency | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Target 20 requests/s | 1.5 s | Needs about 30 slots |\n| `Semaphore(10)` | 1 s | About 10 requests/s |\n| Same `Semaphore(10)` | latency improves to 0.25 s | About 40 requests/s: the rate quadruples with no config change |\n| `Semaphore(8)` × 2 Uvicorn workers | any | 16 concurrent calls, not 8 |","patterns":["**Reserve agent budgets before the call.** Deduct the estimated cost of a model or tool call under a lock (or an atomic decrement in Redis for cross-worker budgets) before awaiting it, then reconcile with actual usage. Monitor runs that exceed budget; any at all means a read-await-write slipped through.","**Size per-worker slots from the fleet target.** Compute N ≈ λ × W for the whole fleet, then divide by worker count × replicas. Monitor in-flight requests and the time spent waiting to acquire the semaphore.","**Shared rate limiter for quotas.** Enforce requests- and tokens-per-minute with a token bucket in a shared store such as Redis or at a gateway, and keep the local semaphore only to bound memory and connections. Monitor 429 rate; honour `Retry-After` with jittered backoff.","**Timeout inside the gate.** Write `async with sem:` then `async with asyncio.timeout(...)`, so one hung upstream call cannot hold a slot forever. Monitor timeouts per dependency and slot wait time.","**Single-flight for expensive shared loads.** Use the pattern above for tokens, clients, and lazy model or index loading. Monitor load counts; more than one per expiry window means the guard is missing.","**Create primitives inside the running loop.** Build locks and semaphores in app startup (FastAPI lifespan) or per test, not at import time, so each belongs to the loop that uses it. Watch for \"bound to a different event loop\" errors in CI."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Once an agent runs tools in parallel, spawns sub-agents or serves many\n  sessions per process, they share mutable state: a per-run token or dollar budget, a scratchpad or memory\n  store, an auth token, a lazily loaded index. Each check-then-act across an `await` is a race.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Budget and step counters in your agent loop, provider client and\n  token caches, lazy loading of embedding models or vector indexes, and the concurrency cap on every model,\n  embedding and tool provider you fan out to.\n- **What you control.** Which state is shared (prefer per-run state over globals), a `Lock` or\n  single-flight task around read-await-write, a `Semaphore` or bounded `Queue` per provider, and a shared\n  rate limiter once you run more than one worker.\n- **What you can skip.** `asyncio.Condition` details and building your own primitives. Know the three\n  ingredients of a race, double-checked locking, single-flight and Little's law for sizing.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Per-run token and dollar budgets.** Parallel tool calls and sub-agents deduct from one budget, so the\n  check and the deduction must sit under one `Lock` or reserve-then-settle step.\n- **Shared auth token refresh.** Single-flight refresh stops a hundred concurrent agent sessions from each\n  hitting the auth server when a token expires.\n- **Lazy loading of indexes and models.** Double-checked locking loads a vector index or tokenizer once,\n  even when the first ten requests arrive together.\n- **Provider concurrency caps.** A `Semaphore` per model, embedding and tool provider keeps fan-out inside\n  what each provider and connection pool can take.","mini_project":"**Break and fix a shared agent budget (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** reproduce a budget overrun from parallel tool calls, then fix it and cap concurrency.\n- **Steps:** give a fake agent a $1.00 budget and ten parallel \"model calls\" that each check the budget,\n  `await asyncio.sleep(0.1)`, then deduct $0.20; record the total spent; fix it with an `asyncio.Lock`\n  around reserve-then-deduct; then add a `Semaphore(3)` and log the peak number of calls in flight.\n- **Done when:** the unlocked run overspends, the locked run never exceeds $1.00, peak in-flight calls\n  never exceed 3, and a short note explains which `await` opened the race."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Agent step coroutine\"] --> S[\"async with Semaphore(N)\"]\n    S --> V{\"Token valid? (no lock)\"}\n    V -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Call the LLM\"]\n    V -->|\"no\"| L[\"async with Lock: wait in FIFO\"]\n    L --> V2{\"Still invalid? (re-check)\"}\n    V2 -->|\"no, another waiter refreshed\"| C\n    V2 -->|\"yes\"| F[\"await refresh(), store token\"]\n    F --> C\n    C --> X[\"Release slot, wake next waiter\"]","caption":"The token-cache request path: the semaphore caps calls in flight, and the lock plus a second validity\ncheck makes sure only the first waiter refreshes while the rest reuse its token."},"interview":[{"q":"How can single-threaded asyncio code have a race condition at all?","a":"- **Interleaving happens at every await.** The loop can run any ready coroutine there, so shared state\n  read before an `await` may be stale after it.\n- **The race needs three things:** shared mutable state, a read and a dependent write, and an `await`\n  between them. Code with no await in between is atomic with respect to other coroutines.\n- **It is a stale decision, not a torn write**, which is why it passes unit tests and appears only\n  under concurrent load."},{"q":"Your agent overspent its per-run budget only on turns where the model called tools in parallel. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Read-await-write on a shared budget.** Each parallel tool checked the remaining budget, awaited its\n  model sub-call, then deducted, so all of them passed the check before any deduction landed.\n- **Reserve before awaiting:** decide and deduct inside an `asyncio.Lock` with no await in the critical\n  section, then refund on failure or cancellation.\n- **Across workers** the same reservation must be an atomic operation in a shared store such as Redis,\n  because an in-process lock is invisible to other processes."},{"q":"Fifty concurrent agent sessions all trigger a reload of the same vector index. How would you fix it?","a":"- **Single-flight the load.** The first caller on a miss stores `asyncio.create_task(load())`, and\n  every caller awaits that same task, so one load serves all fifty.\n- **Shield the shared await** so one caller's cancellation does not cancel the load for the others.\n- **Clear the stored task on failure**, or every later caller re-reads the cached exception; monitor load\n  counts per expiry window."},{"q":"Why not just use threading.Lock in async code?","a":"- **A contended `threading.Lock` blocks the thread**, and in asyncio that thread is the event loop, so\n  every coroutine stalls instead of just the waiter.\n- **`asyncio.Lock` waits with an await**, parking the coroutine in a FIFO queue of futures while the loop\n  keeps running other work.\n- **The reverse also holds:** asyncio primitives are not thread-safe, so code shared with real threads\n  needs thread-safe hand-off such as `loop.call_soon_threadsafe`."},{"q":"When would you use a bounded asyncio.Queue with workers instead of a semaphore around each task?","a":"- **When work arrives continuously** (an ingest pipeline, a crawler, an eval run streaming cases): producers\n  block on `put` once `Queue(maxsize=N)` is full, so memory stays bounded. A semaphore over `gather` still\n  creates every task up front and only gates the call itself.\n- **When you want a fixed, visible concurrency level:** N worker tasks means N calls in flight, and each\n  worker owns its retries and error handling.\n- **Not for a one-off batch of known size**, because the queue costs more structure (worker loops,\n  shutdown via sentinels or cancellation, collecting results); a semaphore around each call is simpler."},{"q":"How many semaphore slots do you need to sustain 20 requests per second at 1.5 seconds latency?","a":"- **Little's law:** N ≈ λ × W, so 20 × 1.5 ≈ 30 slots.\n- **Fewer slots** cannot reach the target; **many more** only move the queue into the provider.\n- **Divide across the fleet:** with several workers and replicas, each process gets its share, and you\n  monitor in-flight count and slot wait time to validate the sizing."}],"tradeoffs":["**Correctness vs serialisation.** A lock makes a critical section correct but serialises it; with a slow await inside (a model call), throughput collapses to one call at a time. Keep only the minimum inside the lock, as the reserve-then-await budget does.","**Protection vs unused capacity.** A tight semaphore protects the provider and your memory but leaves capacity unused; when quota is generous and latency high, a larger limit gives more throughput.","**Simple vs fleet-wide.** In-process primitives are fast and simple but invisible to other workers and replicas; once you scale horizontally, limits and budgets move to a shared store or an API gateway.","**Queue vs semaphore.** A bounded `asyncio.Queue` with fixed workers is easier to reason about than many tasks behind a semaphore, at the cost of more structure. It wins when work arrives continuously rather than in batches."],"pitfalls":["**Assuming single-threaded means race-free.** Symptom: duplicate refreshes, duplicate writes to agent memory or double-spent budgets that only show up under concurrent load or parallel tool calls.","**Creating a primitive at import time and using it from different loops**, as tests often do. Since 3.10 a primitive binds to the loop on which it first has to wait and raises `RuntimeError` (\"bound to a different event loop\") when it must wait on another. The failure appears only under contention and looks flaky. Fix: create primitives in app startup or per test.","**Using a semaphore to enforce a requests-per-minute quota.** Symptom: 429s return when provider latency drops, because the same concurrency then produces more requests per minute.","**Re-acquiring the same `asyncio.Lock` in a nested call.** The coroutine waits on itself forever and the agent step hangs with no error. Fix: take the lock once at the outer level and call an unlocked inner helper.","**Holding a lock or semaphore across a call with no timeout.** One hung upstream request starves every waiter behind it. Fix: `async with asyncio.timeout(...)` inside the gate."],"when":"- **Lock when** a coroutine reads shared state, awaits, then writes based on what it read: token and\n  client caches, lazy model or index loading, per-conversation agent state, budget counters.\n- **Semaphore (or bounded queue) when** you fan out to a dependency with finite capacity: LLM providers,\n  embedding APIs, vector stores, scrapers, sandboxed code runners.\n- **Avoid** locks around code with no await inside; it is already atomic with respect to other coroutines.\n- **Measure:** in-flight requests, 429 rate and queue wait time. If wait time dominates latency, the limit\n  is too tight or the dependency is saturated.\n- **Interviews:** be ready to explain why asyncio can race at all.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wraps provider calls in a semaphore sized from the quota, and uses double-checked locking for shared clients and token caches.","architect":"Decides where concurrency limits live, per process, per replica or in a shared gateway, so the total stays inside provider quotas as the fleet scales.","fde":"Tunes a customer's batch-ingestion concurrency against their own API quota and explains why doubling workers doubled their 429s instead of their throughput."},"check":{"q":"A team wraps OpenAI calls in `asyncio.Semaphore(50)` per worker, runs 4 Uvicorn workers, and still\ngets 429s on a 3,000 requests-per-minute quota. Explain and propose a fix.","a":"The semaphore is per process, so the fleet allows 200 concurrent calls, and it limits concurrency\nrather than rate. By Little's law, 200 in flight at 2 seconds average latency is about 6,000 requests per\nminute (illustrative numbers), double the quota, and if latency drops the rate rises further. The fix is\na rate limiter based on requests and tokens per minute, shared across workers through Redis or enforced\nby a gateway, with the semaphore kept only to bound memory and connections. Retries on 429 should honour\n`Retry-After` with jittered backoff so they do not amplify the burst."},"quiz":[{"q":"In single-threaded asyncio code, when can two coroutines interfere with each other's view of shared state?","options":["Never, because only one coroutine runs at a time","Only when the GIL is released by a C extension","When one reads the state, awaits something, and then acts on what it read","Only when the shared state is a list or dict rather than an int"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The event loop switches coroutines only at awaits, so a read and a dependent write separated by an await can interleave with another coroutine doing the same. Code with no await in between is atomic."},{"q":"Why does the token cache re-check validity after acquiring the lock?","options":["Because asyncio.Lock does not guarantee mutual exclusion","Because another coroutine may have refreshed the token while this one waited for the lock","Because the clock may have moved backwards during the await","Because the fast-path check is not allowed to read private attributes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Several coroutines can miss on the fast path at once; they queue on the lock. The first refreshes, and without the second check every waiter behind it would refresh again on entry."},{"q":"A semaphore of 10 wraps an API with 1-second latency. Latency improves to 0.25 seconds. What happens to request rate?","options":["It stays at 10 requests per second because the semaphore is unchanged","It rises to about 40 requests per second because the semaphore bounds concurrency, not rate","It falls, because shorter requests release the semaphore less often","It becomes unbounded because the semaphore stops blocking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Throughput equals concurrency divided by latency. Ten slots each finishing in 0.25 seconds allow about 40 requests per second, which is why semaphores cannot enforce per-minute quotas."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/asyncio-vs-threads-vs-multiprocessing","sources":["Official docs: asyncio, threading, multiprocessing, concurrent.futures","PEP 703 — Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional in CPython"],"why":"- **Agent backends do two kinds of work.** Most wall-clock time is waiting (model provider, embedding API,\n  vector database, S3 download, web search tool). Some is pure CPU (parsing PDFs, chunking, tokenising,\n  JSON-schema validation of large tool outputs, local re-ranking). Each of Python's three models fits only\n  some of it.\n- **\"Everything async\" breaks on the first sync library** (a vendor SDK, a PDF parser, a database driver\n  without async support inside a tool): it blocks the event loop and every concurrent agent session stalls\n  behind it.\n- **\"Everything threads\" does not speed up pure-Python CPU work**, because in standard CPython the GIL\n  lets only one thread run bytecode at a time, and thousands of threads for thousands of in-flight\n  sessions are heavy.\n- **\"Everything multiprocessing\" costs memory and pickling.** Each process has its own memory, model copies\n  and connection pools, and every argument and result is pickled across a pipe, which can cost more than\n  the work itself.\n- **Getting it wrong is quietly expensive.** Usually not a crash, but an agent service whose throughput\n  does not move however many workers you add.","how":"### The three models at a glance\n| | asyncio | Threads | Processes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Scheduling | Cooperative, only at `await` | Preemptive, by the kernel | Separate interpreters, each with its own GIL |\n| Parallel Python CPU | None | No (one thread runs bytecode at a time) | Yes, across cores |\n| Practical count | Tens of thousands of waits on one thread | Hundreds to low thousands | About the core count |\n| Shared state | Shared; races only across awaits | Shared; needs `threading.Lock` | No shared heap; data is pickled |\n| Main cost | Every layer must be async | GIL for pure Python | Pickling, startup, memory per worker |\n| Agent fit | The loop, model calls, async tools | Sync SDK tools, NumPy-heavy tools | Parsing, chunking, CPU-heavy tools |\n\n### asyncio: one thread, many waits\n- **Mechanism.** An event loop multiplexes sockets and resumes coroutines when their I/O is ready.\n- **Cheap.** A suspended coroutine is a small heap object, so tens of thousands of concurrent LLM streams\n  are feasible on one thread.\n- **Easier races.** Switching happens only at `await`.\n- **The cost is \"function colouring\":** every layer must be async, and any blocking call freezes the\n  loop. It gives no CPU parallelism at all.\n\n### Threads: preemptive, shared memory, GIL-limited\n- **Mechanism.** OS threads are kernel-scheduled and share the process's memory.\n- **When the GIL is released.** A thread holds the GIL while running bytecode and releases it on blocking\n  I/O and inside C extensions that choose to (NumPy on large arrays, many PyTorch ops, hashlib,\n  compression).\n- **So threads give real concurrency** for I/O and GIL-releasing native code, but pure-Python CPU work runs\n  one thread at a time.\n- **Bounded count.** Each thread reserves its own stack and the OS schedules it: hundreds to low\n  thousands, not tens of thousands.\n- **Shared state needs `threading.Lock`**, because switching can happen between almost any two bytecodes.\n\n### Processes: true parallelism, no shared heap\n- **Mechanism.** `multiprocessing` and `ProcessPoolExecutor` start separate interpreters, each with its own\n  GIL, so CPU-bound Python scales across cores.\n- **Costs.** Arguments and results are pickled (lambdas and open clients cannot be sent), each worker pays\n  startup and import cost, and memory is multiplied by the number of workers.\n\n**Start methods:**\n| Method | Behaviour | Caveat |\n|---|---|---|\n| `fork` | Copies the parent quickly | Unsafe if the parent has threads or CUDA state |\n| `spawn` | Default on macOS and Windows; starts clean | Re-imports your modules |\n| `forkserver` | Linux default since 3.14 | Same import requirements as spawn |\n\n- **Guard entry points.** Under spawn and forkserver, worker entry points must be importable and guarded\n  by `if __name__ == \"__main__\":`.\n- **Heavy per-worker state** (a tokenizer, a parser model) belongs in the pool's `initializer=` function,\n  which runs once per worker, *because* passing it with every task would pickle it every time.\n- **The payoff test is a ratio:** per-task compute time against the time to pickle arguments and result\n  (`len(pickle.dumps(arg))` and a quick timing tell you).\n- **Batch small items** into chunks (`chunksize=` on `map`) until compute dominates.\n\n### A decision procedure (per tool or pipeline stage)\n1. **Measure first:** wall time, process CPU time and per-core utilisation for a representative run.\n2. **CPU well below one core while latency is high:** you are waiting. Use asyncio if the libraries are\n   async, threads if they are blocking. Either way, bound concurrency to what the dependency allows.\n3. **One core pinned and adding threads changes nothing:** pure-Python CPU work under the GIL. Use\n   processes, or move the hot loop into native code (NumPy, Polars, a compiled library).\n4. **Several cores busy with threads:** the hot path is in GIL-releasing native code, and threads are the\n   cheapest parallelism you have, sharing memory and model weights.\n5. **The work mixes both:** keep one async core and push each kind of work to the executor that matches it.\n\n### Bridges between them\n- **`await asyncio.to_thread(fn, *args)`** (3.9+) runs a blocking call in the default thread pool without\n  blocking the loop.\n- **`loop.run_in_executor(process_pool, fn, *args)`** sends CPU work to processes and returns an awaitable.\n- **The usual shape:** async at the edges for I/O fan-out, executors for everything else.\n- **Free-threaded CPython (PEP 703)** removes the GIL in an optional build, which may change the\n  thread-versus-process answer for CPU work. Check its status and your dependencies' support for your\n  Python version before relying on it.","example":"### Routing agent tools to the right executor\nOne dispatcher decides per tool: async tools are awaited, blocking tools go to a bounded thread pool, and\nCPU-heavy tools go to a process pool. Tool bodies are stand-ins.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor, ThreadPoolExecutor\n\nasync def web_search(q):            # async client: await directly\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.4); return f\"results for {q}\"\n\ndef crm_lookup(user_id):            # sync vendor SDK: blocks, releases GIL on I/O\n    time.sleep(0.3); return {\"id\": user_id, \"tier\": \"gold\"}\n\ndef parse_attachment(text):         # pure-Python CPU: holds the GIL\n    return sum(ord(c) % 7 for c in text * 2000)\n\nTOOLS = {\"web_search\": (\"async\", web_search),\n         \"crm_lookup\": (\"thread\", crm_lookup),\n         \"parse_attachment\": (\"process\", parse_attachment)}\n\nasync def run_tool(name, arg, procs):\n    kind, fn = TOOLS[name]\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    if kind == \"async\":\n        return await fn(arg)\n    if kind == \"thread\":\n        return await asyncio.to_thread(fn, arg)           # default executor, bounded below\n    return await loop.run_in_executor(procs, fn, arg)     # fn and arg must be picklable\n\nasync def main():\n    asyncio.get_running_loop().set_default_executor(ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=16))\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as procs:\n        t = time.perf_counter()\n        calls = [(\"web_search\", \"refund policy\"), (\"crm_lookup\", 7),\n                 (\"parse_attachment\", \"invoice line \" * 500)]\n        out = await asyncio.gather(*(run_tool(n, a, procs) for n, a in calls))\n        print(f\"{len(out)} tools in {time.perf_counter() - t:.2f}s; loop never blocked\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    asyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one agent step with three tools\nIllustrative numbers for a step where the model requested a web search (0.4 s network), a CRM lookup\nthrough a sync SDK (0.3 s network) and parsing an attachment (0.8 s pure-Python CPU), on a worker serving\n50 other sessions.\n\n| Design | Step time | Effect on other sessions |\n|---|---|---|\n| All three called directly in the coroutine | 0.4 + 0.3 + 0.8 = 1.5 s, sequential | Loop frozen for 1.1 s (CRM plus parse) |\n| Search awaited, CRM and parse via `to_thread` | about 0.8 s | Loop free, but the parse still holds the GIL, so other sessions' Python work is slowed while it runs |\n| Search awaited, CRM via `to_thread`, parse in a process pool | about 0.8 s | Loop free and the parse runs on another core |\n\n### Worked scenario: ingesting 2,000 PDFs for an agent's RAG index\nIllustrative numbers. Each PDF: download from S3 (about 0.3 s, network), extract text with a pure-Python\nparser (about 0.5 s CPU), embed chunks via an API (about 0.4 s, network). 8-core machine.\n\n| Design | Time | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sequential | 2,000 × 1.2 s = 2,400 s, about 40 minutes | Nothing overlaps |\n| All asyncio, parser called directly | at least ~17 minutes | Parsing alone is 2,000 × 0.5 s = 1,000 s on one core; during each parse no downloads or embeddings progress |\n| Threads for everything | the same ~1,000 s floor, plus contention | I/O overlaps, but parses serialise on the GIL |\n| Async I/O + process pool of 8 for parsing | near the parse floor, a couple of minutes | Parse time ≈ 1,000 s ÷ 8 ≈ 125 s if it scales cleanly, with I/O bounded by a semaphore and overlapped |\n\nThe last row holds only if pickling the PDF bytes in and the extracted text back is cheap relative to\n0.5 s of work.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def handle(pdf_key, pool, sem):                   # pool: ProcessPoolExecutor(8)\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    async with sem:\n        raw = await s3_get(pdf_key)                     # async I/O\n    text = await loop.run_in_executor(pool, parse, raw) # CPU in a process\n    async with sem:\n        return await embed(chunk(text))                 # async I/O\n```\n\n### Measure it yourself: threads, processes and chunksize\nPure Python, standard library only.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, ProcessPoolExecutor\n\ndef score(doc: str) -> int:                  # pure-Python CPU work: holds the GIL\n    return sum(ord(c) % 7 for c in doc)\n\ndef timed(executor, docs, chunksize=1) -> float:\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    with executor as ex:                     # map's chunksize is ignored by thread pools\n        list(ex.map(score, docs, chunksize=chunksize))\n    return time.perf_counter() - t\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":                   # required under spawn and forkserver\n    big = [\"chunk this text \" * 15_000] * 64  # few large tasks: compute dominates\n    tiny = [\"short doc\"] * 50_000             # many tiny tasks: overhead dominates\n    print(f\"big,  1 thread      {timed(ThreadPoolExecutor(1), big):.2f}s\")\n    print(f\"big,  8 threads     {timed(ThreadPoolExecutor(8), big):.2f}s\")\n    print(f\"big,  8 processes   {timed(ProcessPoolExecutor(8), big):.2f}s\")\n    print(f\"tiny, 8 proc, cs=1  {timed(ProcessPoolExecutor(8), tiny):.2f}s\")\n    print(f\"tiny, 8 proc, cs=1k {timed(ProcessPoolExecutor(8), tiny, 1000):.2f}s\")\n```\n\nWhat to expect (one laptop run, illustrative, not a benchmark):\n- **8 threads ≈ 1 thread** (about 0.56 s each): the GIL serialises pure-Python work.\n- **8 processes about 3× faster** (0.16-0.20 s), less than 8× because pool startup and pickling are\n  included in the timing.\n- **Tiny tasks: about 4.4 s with `chunksize=1`, under 0.1 s with `chunksize=1000`.** Per-task overhead in\n  the parent dominated until batching made compute the larger share.","patterns":["**Classify every tool by executor.** Keep a registry that marks each agent tool async, thread or process, and route through one dispatcher as above, so a new sync tool cannot land on the loop by accident. Monitor loop lag and per-tool latency.","**Bound the default thread pool.** At startup, call `loop.set_default_executor(ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=N))` so `to_thread` calls to a slow sync SDK cannot grow without limit. Monitor executor queue length and the latency of offloaded calls.","**Initialise workers once, recycle them.** Create `ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=..., initializer=load_parser)` and, on 3.11+, set `max_tasks_per_child=` to restart workers that leak memory. Monitor per-worker RSS.","**Choose the start method explicitly.** Pass `mp_context=multiprocessing.get_context(\"spawn\")` (or `\"forkserver\"`) when the parent has threads or CUDA, instead of relying on the platform default. Watch for intermittent worker hangs, the fork-with-threads fingerprint.","**Batch CPU tasks and split them out.** Send hundreds of small documents per task (`chunksize=` or explicit batches), tuning the size empirically against throughput, and run RAG parsing and chunking as queue-fed workers scaled separately from the I/O-bound agent API tier. Monitor tasks/s, the parent process's CPU, queue depth and age of the oldest job.","**Size server workers against memory.** Each Uvicorn/Gunicorn worker process duplicates models and pools, so set the worker count from the memory budget, not only from cores. Monitor RSS per worker."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent loop is mostly network waits (model, search, MCP servers), but\n  its tools are a mix: a sync database driver, a vendor SDK with no async client, a PDF parser or code\n  linter that burns CPU. Each tool must run where it cannot freeze the loop that serves every session.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** FastAPI and Uvicorn workers serving the agent, `asyncio.to_thread`\n  around sync tools (frameworks such as LangChain also run sync tools in a thread pool when invoked\n  asynchronously), and process pools or separate workers for RAG ingestion and document parsing.\n- **What you control.** Which executor each tool uses, the size of the default thread pool, process-pool\n  `initializer=`, `chunksize=` and start method, and how many server workers fit your memory budget.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing your own process orchestration or shared-memory schemes. Learn the\n  decision procedure and the bridges; reach for a job queue when CPU work outgrows one machine.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Async agent core with sync tools.** Model and MCP calls stay on the loop, while a vendor SDK with no\n  async client runs through `asyncio.to_thread`.\n- **CPU-heavy tools in a chat agent.** PDF parsing or code linting moves to a process pool so one upload\n  does not stall every other session.\n- **RAG ingestion pipelines.** Downloads and embedding calls run async; chunking and parsing fan out over\n  processes, sized to the cores you have.\n- **Sizing an agent service.** Choosing server worker count and pool sizes against a memory budget,\n  because each process carries its own clients and model copies.","mini_project":"**Route three tools to the right executor (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure what each concurrency model does for an I/O tool, a blocking SDK tool and a CPU\n  tool inside one async agent.\n- **Steps:** write an async HTTP-style tool (`asyncio.sleep`), a blocking tool (`time.sleep`) and a\n  pure-Python CPU tool (a tight loop over a few million items); run 8 concurrent sessions calling each\n  tool directly on the loop, via `asyncio.to_thread`, and via a `ProcessPoolExecutor`; record wall time\n  and event-loop lag.\n- **Done when:** a table shows the best executor per tool, with numbers, and you can explain why threads\n  did not help the CPU tool on standard CPython."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Measure wall time, CPU, cores\"] --> Q1{\"CPU low while latency high?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"yes, waiting on I/O\"| Q2{\"Library async?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| A[\"asyncio plus a Semaphore\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no, blocking SDK\"| T[\"Threads via asyncio.to_thread\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no, CPU busy\"| Q3{\"Native code releases GIL?\"}\n    Q3 -->|\"yes, several cores busy\"| T2[\"Threads, shared weights\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"no, one core pinned\"| P[\"Process pool, batched\"]\n    A --> MIX[\"Async core plus executor bridges\"]\n    T --> MIX\n    P --> MIX","caption":"The decision procedure, applied to each tool or pipeline stage: measure first, then let what the work\nwaits on pick the model. Mixed agents keep one async core and bridge out."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the core difference between asyncio, threads and processes in Python?","a":"- **asyncio** is cooperative scheduling on one thread, switching only at `await`, so tens of thousands of\n  network waits are cheap but there is no CPU parallelism.\n- **Threads** are preemptive and share memory, giving real concurrency for blocking I/O and GIL-releasing\n  C code, but pure-Python bytecode runs one thread at a time in standard CPython.\n- **Processes** each have their own interpreter and GIL, so CPU-bound Python scales across cores at the\n  cost of pickling, startup and memory per worker."},{"q":"Your agent has a web-search tool, a sync CRM SDK tool and a PDF-parsing tool. How do you run them?","a":"- **Search is awaited directly** on the loop with an async client, inside a semaphore and a timeout.\n- **The CRM tool goes through `asyncio.to_thread`** with a bounded default executor, because it blocks on\n  I/O and releases the GIL while waiting.\n- **The parser goes to a process pool** (or a separate worker service), because pure-Python CPU work would\n  otherwise hold the GIL and slow every session; check that its inputs and outputs pickle cheaply."},{"q":"An async agent API calls a vendor SDK that has no async client. How do you integrate it?","a":"- **Offload each call** with `await asyncio.to_thread(sdk_call, ...)`, so the event loop keeps serving\n  other sessions while the call blocks in a worker thread.\n- **Bound the pool:** set `loop.set_default_executor(ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=N))` at startup and\n  cap in-flight calls, because each offloaded call holds a thread.\n- **Monitor** executor queue length and offloaded-call latency, and switch to an async client when one\n  exists."},{"q":"Would free-threaded CPython (PEP 703) change how you design CPU-bound work?","a":"- **Potentially, for CPU work.** Without the GIL, threads could run pure-Python code in parallel while\n  sharing memory and model weights, avoiding pickling and per-worker copies.\n- **Not yet by default:** it is an optional build, so check its status for your Python version and\n  whether every C extension you depend on supports it.\n- **Shared state then needs real locking**, because code that was accidentally safe under the GIL can\n  race; measure before replacing a working process pool."},{"q":"Why would you choose spawn or forkserver over fork for a worker pool?","a":"- **`fork` copies the parent's state**, including locks held by other threads and CUDA state, so\n  children can deadlock intermittently or hit \"Cannot re-initialize CUDA in forked subprocess\".\n- **`spawn` and `forkserver` start clean** but re-import your modules, so entry points need the\n  `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` guard and models must load in the initializer, not at import.\n- **Pass the context explicitly** with `mp_context=` rather than relying on platform defaults."},{"q":"How do you size Uvicorn or Gunicorn workers for an agent service that loads a local model?","a":"- **Memory first:** each worker process duplicates the model and connection pools, so the count comes\n  from the memory budget divided by per-worker RSS, not only from cores.\n- **Keep the I/O tier async** so each worker handles many concurrent agent sessions, and move CPU-heavy\n  parsing to a separately scaled queue-fed worker service.\n- **Monitor** RSS per worker, queue depth and oldest-job age."}],"tradeoffs":["**Concurrency per byte vs async everywhere.** asyncio gives the most concurrency per byte of memory but demands async libraries throughout; one synchronous tool dependency in the hot path makes threads the pragmatic choice for that tool.","**Easy parallel I/O vs no CPU gain.** Threads are the easiest way to parallelise blocking SDK calls, but shared state needs locks and pure-Python CPU work gains nothing. That flips when the CPU work is in GIL-releasing native code.","**Real CPU parallelism vs overhead.** Processes pay pickling, startup and memory per worker; they stop paying off when each task is so small that serialisation dominates the compute.","**Free scaling vs memory.** Separate server workers (Uvicorn/Gunicorn processes) scale an agent tier across cores without code changes, but multiply model memory and connection pools per worker, which matters with local models and embedding models loaded in-process."],"pitfalls":["**A synchronous SDK or `requests` inside an `async def` tool.** Symptom: p99 latency rises for all sessions on a worker while CPU stays low. Fix: async client or `to_thread`.","**Threads to speed up pure-Python parsing or tokenisation.** Symptom: CPU sits at about one core and wall time barely changes. Fix: processes or native code.","**Large objects per task to a process pool** (documents, NumPy arrays, model weights). Symptom: profiling shows most time in pickling, and worker memory spikes. Fix: `initializer=` or shared storage.","**`fork` after starting threads or initialising CUDA.** Symptom: workers that deadlock intermittently on a lock some parent thread held at fork time, or PyTorch's \"Cannot re-initialize CUDA in forked subprocess\" error. Fix: spawn or forkserver.","**Loading a local model at import time in a module that `spawn` re-imports.** Each worker loads its own copy and the machine runs out of memory. Fix: load in the initializer or behind the main guard."],"when":"- **Measure first.** CPU low while latency is high means I/O-bound; one core pinned means CPU-bound in Python.\n- **Agent API calling LLMs, tools and vector stores:** asyncio with async clients such as `httpx` or the\n  official async SDKs; wrap unavoidable blocking tools in `asyncio.to_thread`.\n- **Heavy parsing, chunking or local inference preprocessing:** a process pool, or a separate worker service.\n- **GPU inference:** the work runs in native code, and a dedicated serving engine usually beats rolling your\n  own concurrency.\n- **Interviews:** justify the choice from the bottleneck, not from preference.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps the event loop free of blocking calls, uses `to_thread` and process pools as bridges, and profiles before adding workers.","architect":"Splits I/O-bound request handling from CPU-bound batch work into separately scaled services, and decides worker counts against memory budgets.","fde":"Finds the synchronous call blocking a customer's async service, or the thread pool that cannot speed up their parser, and fixes it in their codebase."},"check":{"q":"A team's chunking step (pure-Python regex and string splitting, about 2 ms per document) is too slow.\nThey wrap it in a 16-thread pool: no change, and `top` shows the process at about 100% CPU. They switch to a\n`ProcessPoolExecutor(16)` submitting one document per task, and it gets slower. Explain both results and\nwhat you would do.","a":"The threads changed nothing because the work is pure-Python bytecode: under the GIL only one thread\nruns at a time, and \"100% CPU\" means one core, the fingerprint of GIL-bound work. The process pool removed\nthe GIL limit but introduced a per-task cost paid serially in the parent: it pickles every document,\npushes it through a pipe, tracks a future per task, and unpickles every list of chunks that comes back.\nSixteen workers each finishing a task every 2 ms demand thousands of tasks per second from that one\nparent thread, so the parent becomes the bottleneck, and when documents are large the per-task\nserialisation alone can exceed the 2 ms of work it is shipping. The fix keeps\nprocesses but changes the grain: send batches of a few hundred documents per task (`map` with\n`chunksize`, or explicit batches) so compute dominates, initialise any heavy objects once per worker,\nand size the pool to physical cores. Then measure throughput against batch size, because the optimum is\nempirical. If the hot loop can be expressed as vectorised Polars string operations, that may beat both."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which workload is the best fit for a `ProcessPoolExecutor` rather than threads or asyncio?","options":["Calling an LLM API 5,000 times with async-capable clients","Running large NumPy matrix multiplications that release the GIL","Parsing thousands of documents with a pure-Python parser","Streaming tokens from a model provider to many websocket clients"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Pure-Python CPU work is serialised by the GIL in threads and blocks the loop in asyncio, so only separate processes run it in parallel. NumPy releasing the GIL makes threads viable for the second option."},{"q":"What does `await asyncio.to_thread(fn)` achieve inside a coroutine?","options":["It makes `fn` run in parallel with other Python bytecode regardless of the GIL","It runs the blocking `fn` in a worker thread so the event loop keeps serving other coroutines","It converts `fn` into a coroutine that yields at each line","It runs `fn` in a separate process to avoid the GIL"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The event loop stays responsive because the blocking call waits in another thread. It does not bypass the GIL, so it helps for blocking I/O but not for pure-Python CPU work."},{"q":"Why can a process pool make a job slower than running it sequentially?","options":["Processes share one GIL, so they contend more than threads","The operating system limits processes to one core each by default","Process pools cannot run more than one task per worker","Each task's arguments and results are pickled and copied, which can cost more than tiny tasks"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Processes have separate memory, so data crosses a pipe as pickled bytes. When per-task compute is small or the payload is large, serialisation and startup overhead outweigh the parallel speed-up."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/gil","sources":["PEP 703 — Making the Global Interpreter Lock Optional in CPython","PEP 684 — A Per-Interpreter GIL","Official docs: Python/C API (Thread State and the Global Interpreter Lock)"],"why":"- **Reference counts change constantly.** Every CPython object's count is updated on nearly every\n  assignment, call and attribute access. Two threads updating one count without coordination corrupt it:\n  the object is freed while in use (a crash) or never freed (a leak).\n- **Interpreter internals are shared too:** dicts backing modules and classes, the allocator's free lists,\n  the import machinery.\n- **The alternatives are expensive.** Fine-grained locks on every object, or atomic instructions on every\n  refcount change, slow single-threaded code, which was and is the common case. They also force every C\n  extension author to reason about concurrency.\n- **One lock bought simplicity and speed.** The interpreter and its huge C ecosystem (NumPy, database\n  drivers, compression) stayed easy to write correctly and single-threaded Python stayed fast. The cost\n  came with multi-core machines: a CPU-bound Python program using threads still uses roughly one core.\n- **Agent workloads straddle the line.** Calling LLM APIs and tools is I/O-bound, fine with threads or\n  asyncio. Tokenising a corpus, parsing documents or a pure-Python scoring loop is CPU-bound, and threads\n  give almost nothing. Knowing the side decides threads, processes or native code.","how":"### Background: holding and handing over\nA thread must hold the GIL to run bytecode or touch Python objects. It gives the GIL up in two ways:\n\n| Handover | When | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Voluntary** | Around blocking operations: socket reads, file I/O, `time.sleep`, waiting on a lock. I/O code releases before the system call and reacquires after | No reason to block other threads while the kernel waits |\n| **On request** | Another thread has waited longer than the switch interval (`sys.getswitchinterval()`, 5 ms by default); it sets a flag and the running thread drops the GIL at its next eval-loop check point | Keeps threads fair |\n\n- **The forced handover is also why CPU-bound threads add switching overhead rather than speed.**\n- **Model and tool calls are the voluntary case:** a thread waiting on a provider socket holds no GIL, so\n  sync SDK calls in a thread pool overlap fine.\n\n### The hidden cost: CPU threads slow down the agent's event loop\n1. An I/O thread's socket becomes ready (the next model tokens arrived) and the kernel wakes it.\n2. It must still reacquire the GIL before running any Python.\n3. If a CPU-bound thread holds the GIL, the I/O thread waits until that thread reaches a check point and\n   yields, roughly bounded by the switch interval each time it needs the lock back.\n\n- **The event loop pays this on every task step**, because it needs the GIL for each one.\n- **So `asyncio.to_thread` for pure-Python CPU work** keeps the loop *unblocked* but not *unaffected*: each\n  step can be delayed by milliseconds and streaming agent responses get visibly jittery. The work belongs\n  in a process.\n\n### Native code can opt out\n- **How.** C, C++ and Rust extensions can release the GIL while working on their own memory, as long as\n  they touch no Python objects. In C this is the `Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS` / `Py_END_ALLOW_THREADS` pair\n  around the native work; the extension must reacquire the GIL before building the Python result.\n- **Who does it.** NumPy for many operations on large arrays, PyTorch for most tensor ops, `hashlib` for\n  large inputs, compression libraries for large buffers.\n- **So \"Python is single-core\" is wrong here:** a thread pool running NumPy-heavy or PyTorch CPU work (a\n  local embedding model, a cosine-similarity pass over retrieved vectors) can use several cores.\n- **It is per function.** Whether a call releases the GIL is a property of that library and function, so\n  check or measure rather than assume.\n\n### What the GIL does not give you\n- **It protects the interpreter, not your invariants.** `counter += 1` compiles to several bytecodes\n  (load, add, store), and a switch can occur between them, so two threads can lose an update to a shared\n  token counter.\n- **Check-then-act on a dict across threads needs a `threading.Lock`.**\n- **Reproducibility varies** with Python version and timing, which makes the race more dangerous, not less.\n- **Some single operations are atomic by accident.** One `list.append` or one `d[key] = value` cannot be\n  torn. Code relying on this \"works\" by accident of implementation.\n- **Hence the documented tools** are `queue.Queue` and explicit locks, and the free-threaded build had to add\n  per-object locking inside built-in containers to keep such single operations safe.\n\n### Ways around it, and its future\n| Route | What it gives | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Processes (`multiprocessing`, `ProcessPoolExecutor`) | Own interpreter and GIL each | Always available |\n| PEP 684 per-interpreter GIL (Python 3.12) | Parallelism within one process, isolated state | C API only in 3.12; Python-level `concurrent.interpreters` in 3.14 (PEP 734) |\n| PEP 703 free-threaded build | No GIL; biased reference counting and other techniques | Experimental in 3.13; officially supported but still a separate build in 3.14 |\n\n- **Free-threaded caveats:** extensions must be built and marked compatible, and there is some\n  single-threaded overhead.\n- **Status changes by release**, so check the docs for your version and dependencies before planning\n  around it.","example":"### Worked scenario: a streaming agent worker with a CPU-heavy tool\nOne Uvicorn worker streams answers for 40 sessions. A new tool post-processes retrieved documents with a\npure-Python cleaning loop taking about 400 ms per call. Figures are illustrative.\n\n| Placement of the cleaning tool | Event loop | What users see |\n|---|---|---|\n| Called directly in the coroutine | Blocked about 400 ms per call | Every stream on the worker freezes during each call |\n| `asyncio.to_thread` | Unblocked, but each task step may wait up to about 5 ms for the GIL | Streams keep moving but tokens arrive in uneven bursts |\n| `ProcessPoolExecutor` via `run_in_executor` | Unblocked and not contending for this process's GIL | Smooth streams; the tool pays pickling for its input and output |\n| Rewritten with vectorised native code that releases the GIL | Unblocked | Smooth, and the tool may also get faster |\n\nThe diagnostic below tells you which row you are in before you pick a fix.\n\n### Threads vs processes vs GIL-releasing C code\n```python\nimport hashlib, time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor, ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef py_work(n: int) -> int:            # pure Python: holds the GIL throughout\n    total = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        total += i * i % 7\n    return total\n\ndef c_work(data: bytes) -> str:        # hashlib releases the GIL on large inputs\n    return hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()\n\ndef timed(label, run):\n    t = time.perf_counter()\n    run()\n    print(f\"{label:<20}{time.perf_counter() - t:6.2f}s\")\n\ndef pool(cls, fn, args):\n    with cls(max_workers=4) as ex:\n        list(ex.map(fn, args))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    nums, blobs = [5_000_000] * 4, [b\"x\" * 200_000_000] * 4\n    timed(\"python, sequential\", lambda: [py_work(n) for n in nums])\n    timed(\"python, 4 threads\", lambda: pool(ThreadPoolExecutor, py_work, nums))\n    timed(\"python, 4 procs\", lambda: pool(ProcessPoolExecutor, py_work, nums))\n    timed(\"sha256, sequential\", lambda: [c_work(b) for b in blobs])\n    timed(\"sha256, 4 threads\", lambda: pool(ThreadPoolExecutor, c_work, blobs))\n```\n\nOn standard (GIL) CPython on a multi-core laptop:\n| Run | Relative to sequential | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pure Python, 4 threads | about the same | GIL serialises bytecode |\n| Pure Python, 4 processes | roughly a third (less than 4× faster) | Process startup is included |\n| sha256, 4 threads | about 4× faster | `hashlib` releases the GIL |\n\nAbsolute times depend on the machine; the ratios are the lesson.\n\n### A two-number GIL diagnostic\nAsk the interpreter whether a GIL is active, then measure process CPU time over wall time with N threads:\nabout 1.0 means the GIL is serialising you, close to N means the work releases it. Run it on each\nCPU-heavy tool before deciding where it runs.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, sys, sysconfig, threading, time\n\ndef gil_status() -> str:\n    build = \"free-threaded\" if sysconfig.get_config_var(\"Py_GIL_DISABLED\") else \"standard\"\n    enabled = sys._is_gil_enabled() if hasattr(sys, \"_is_gil_enabled\") else True   # 3.13+\n    return f\"{build} build, GIL enabled: {enabled}\"\n\ndef cpu_wall_ratio(fn, n_threads: int = 4) -> float:\n    threads = [threading.Thread(target=fn) for _ in range(n_threads)]\n    wall, cpu = time.perf_counter(), time.process_time()\n    for t in threads: t.start()\n    for t in threads: t.join()\n    return (time.process_time() - cpu) / (time.perf_counter() - wall)\n\ndef py_loop():  sum(i * i % 7 for i in range(3_000_000))       # holds the GIL\nBLOB = b\"x\" * 200_000_000                                      # allocate outside the threads\ndef hashing():  hashlib.sha256(BLOB).hexdigest()                # releases it\n\nprint(gil_status())\nprint(f\"pure Python, 4 threads: CPU/wall = {cpu_wall_ratio(py_loop):.1f}\")   # about 1.0\nprint(f\"sha256,      4 threads: CPU/wall = {cpu_wall_ratio(hashing):.1f}\")   # close to 4\n```\nNote `BLOB` is built once outside the threads: building a 200 MB bytes object inside each thread is\nPython-level work that holds the GIL, and in testing it pulled the hashing ratio well below 4.","patterns":["**Keep CPU-heavy tools away from the agent's event loop process.** Run pure-Python preprocessing (chunking, cleaning, scoring retrieved documents) in a process pool or a separate worker service, not in `to_thread` beside a streaming loop. Monitor loop lag and inter-token jitter on streams.","**Profile CPU/wall before adding threads.** For any slow batch stage (RAG ingestion, eval scoring), record process CPU time against wall time with the planned thread count. Ratio near 1.0: move to processes or native code; near N: threads are fine. Track the ratio per stage in benchmarks.","**Cap native thread pools when you also use Python threads.** NumPy and PyTorch run their own thread pools; set `OMP_NUM_THREADS` or `torch.set_num_threads(...)` so Python threads × native threads does not exceed the cores, which matters when a local embedding model shares a box with the agent API. Monitor CPU saturation and run-queue length.","**Verify the GIL state on free-threaded deploys.** Log `sys._is_gil_enabled()` at startup and fail the health check if it is `True` where you expected `False`, because an incompatible extension re-enables the GIL at runtime with only a warning.","**Lock shared state anyway.** Guard usage counters, caches and check-then-act sequences with `threading.Lock` or hand work through `queue.Queue`, so the code stays correct under any interpreter. Look for lost updates in load tests, not unit tests."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent workers mix network waits (model, tools) with CPU work (chunking\n  retrieved documents, validating big JSON tool outputs, local scoring). The GIL decides whether that CPU\n  work can share the process with the event loop or must move out, and it explains slow, jittery streams.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `asyncio.to_thread` around CPU-heavy tools, thread pools in eval and\n  ingestion scripts, local embedding or reranking models in PyTorch, and Uvicorn worker counts.\n- **What you control.** Which executor runs each CPU-heavy step, native thread counts (`OMP_NUM_THREADS`,\n  `torch.set_num_threads(...)`), locks around shared agent state, and whether you deploy a free-threaded\n  build.\n- **What you can skip.** Eval-loop internals and the C API beyond knowing that native code can release the\n  GIL. Learn the CPU/wall diagnostic and the threads-versus-processes rule.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Diagnosing jittery token streams.** A CPU-heavy step on the same process holds the GIL and delays\n  every stream; the CPU-versus-wall diagnostic finds it.\n- **Choosing threads or processes for agent tools.** Threads suit model and tool I/O and GIL-releasing\n  native code; pure-Python parsing or scoring needs processes.\n- **Local embedding or reranking models.** PyTorch releases the GIL inside its kernels, so the real knob is\n  native thread counts per worker, not Python threads.\n- **Shared agent state across threads.** The GIL does not make check-then-act safe, so budgets and caches\n  touched by worker threads still need locks.","mini_project":"**Measure the GIL on an agent worker (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show which agent workloads threads speed up and which they do not.\n- **Steps:** write a pure-Python chunker over a large text, a `hashlib`-based hashing step (which\n  releases the GIL for large inputs) and a fake model call using `time.sleep`; run each with 1 and 4\n  threads, then the chunker with 4 processes; record wall time and CPU time for each run.\n- **Done when:** a table shows the speed-up per workload, the chunker gains little from threads but scales\n  with processes, and you can state which executor each step of your agent should use."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant A as Thread A (pure-Python parse)\n    participant G as GIL\n    participant B as Thread B (agent event loop)\n    participant K as Kernel\n    B->>G: release before socket read system call\n    A->>G: acquire, run bytecode\n    K-->>B: model tokens arrived, thread woken\n    B->>G: request GIL, must wait\n    Note over B,G: waits up to the switch interval (5 ms default)\n    B->>G: set drop request flag\n    A->>G: drop at next eval-loop check point\n    G-->>B: acquired, run Python for this task step\n    Note over A,B: native code (NumPy, hashlib) can release the GIL and run in parallel","caption":"Handover between a CPU-bound thread and the agent's event-loop thread: the loop is woken by the kernel\nbut still waits for the GIL, and only a drop request after the switch interval gets it back."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the GIL, and why does CPython have one?","a":"- **A single interpreter mutex:** a thread must hold it to run bytecode or touch Python objects, so only\n  one thread executes Python at a time per interpreter.\n- **It protects reference counts and shared internals**, which change on nearly every operation; without\n  coordination objects get freed while in use or leak.\n- **One lock was the cheap option**, because fine-grained locks or atomic refcounts slow the common\n  single-threaded case and burden every C extension author."},{"q":"Agent streams get jittery after a teammate moved a document-cleaning tool into asyncio.to_thread. Why?","a":"- **The loop is unblocked but not unaffected.** The cleaning thread holds the GIL running pure Python,\n  and the event loop needs the GIL for every task step.\n- **Each loop step can wait** up to about the switch interval (5 ms by default) for a handover, so token\n  delivery becomes uneven across every session on the worker.\n- **Fix:** move the CPU work to a process pool or a separate worker service, and monitor loop lag and\n  inter-token jitter."},{"q":"Your agent service runs many tool calls in a thread pool. Does the GIL limit its throughput?","a":"- **Not for I/O-bound tools.** Threads waiting on model, search or database sockets release the GIL, so\n  they overlap; the limits are pool size and provider quotas.\n- **Yes for pure-Python CPU tools**, which serialise on one core however many threads you add; measure\n  CPU/wall per tool and move those to processes.\n- **Shared state still needs locks** (usage counters, caches), because the GIL does not protect\n  multi-bytecode updates."},{"q":"Does the GIL make my shared dict or counter thread-safe?","a":"- **No, it protects the interpreter, not your invariants.** `counter += 1` is several bytecodes (load,\n  add, store), and a switch between them loses updates.\n- **Check-then-act needs a `threading.Lock`**, or hand work through `queue.Queue`.\n- **Single operations like `list.append` happen to be atomic**, but relying on that is an implementation\n  accident; the free-threaded build had to add per-object locks to preserve it."},{"q":"When are Python threads a good choice for CPU-heavy AI work despite the GIL?","a":"- **When the hot path is GIL-releasing native code:** NumPy on large arrays, most PyTorch tensor ops,\n  `hashlib` and compression on large buffers.\n- **Threads then share memory and weights**, avoiding process pickling and duplicated models.\n- **Verify per function**, because some C libraries hold the GIL for the whole call; and cap native pools\n  with `OMP_NUM_THREADS` or `torch.set_num_threads(...)` to avoid oversubscribing cores."},{"q":"How do you prove, with numbers, that the GIL is your bottleneck?","a":"- **Measure process CPU time over wall time with N threads.** A ratio near 1.0 means the GIL serialises\n  you; close to N means the work releases it.\n- **Confirm with a sampling profiler** such as py-spy to see which thread holds the GIL.\n- **On free-threaded builds**, also log `sys._is_gil_enabled()` (3.13+), since an incompatible\n  extension can silently re-enable it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Simple, fast single thread vs one core.** The GIL keeps single-threaded code and C extensions simple and fast, but caps pure-Python CPU work at one core per process. It flips when your hot loop lives in GIL-releasing native code.","**Full parallelism vs process overhead.** Processes sidestep the GIL completely, at the cost of separate memory, pickled arguments and slower startup. They win when each task carries far more compute than data.","**Native speed vs maintenance.** Moving a hot loop into NumPy, a compiled extension or a Rust library removes the GIL limit and interpreter overhead, but adds a build and maintenance burden worth paying only for real hot spots.","**Free threading vs ecosystem risk.** A free-threaded build allows true thread parallelism for Python code, but trades some single-thread speed and depends on every compiled dependency supporting it, so it suits measured, controlled deployments."],"pitfalls":["**Threads to speed up a pure-Python tokenising or parsing loop.** Symptom: CPU pinned near 100% of one core and no wall-clock improvement. Fix: a process pool, or move the hot loop into native code.","**Assuming the GIL makes shared counters or dicts thread-safe.** Symptom: occasional lost updates or inconsistent state that disappears when you add logging. Fix: a `threading.Lock` or `queue.Queue`.","**Blaming the GIL for a slow I/O-bound agent.** If CPU is low and latency high, the problem is waiting or blocking calls; the GIL is not the bottleneck. Fix: find the blocking call or the slow dependency.","**Assuming a library releases the GIL because it is written in C.** Some hold it for the whole call, and profiling shows threads queuing instead of running in parallel. Fix: measure CPU/wall per call first.","**A free-threaded build plus an extension not marked compatible.** CPython re-enables the GIL at runtime (with a warning), threads stop scaling, and `sys._is_gil_enabled()` (3.13+) returns `True` where you expected `False`. Fix: use free-threaded wheels for every compiled dependency, and check the flag at startup."],"when":"You do not use the GIL; you design around it.\n- **First ask:** is the slow agent step waiting (I/O) or computing (CPU)?\n- **I/O:** threads or asyncio are fine and the GIL is irrelevant.\n- **CPU-bound pure Python:** processes, vectorise with NumPy or Polars, or move the hot loop to native code.\n- **CPU work inside NumPy or PyTorch:** threads can work; measure with a quick timing like the examples.\n- **Measure:** the ratio of process CPU time to wall time with N threads (about 1.0 means the GIL is\n  serialising you, close to N means the work releases it). A sampling profiler such as py-spy shows which\n  thread holds the GIL when that ratio surprises you.\n- **Track** the free-threaded build's status if you run CPU-heavy Python at scale.\n- **Interviews:** explain why the GIL exists (refcounting), when it bites (CPU-bound bytecode), and why it\n  does not make code thread-safe.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Profiles before parallelising, uses processes or vectorised libraries for CPU-bound preprocessing, and still guards shared state across threads with locks.","architect":"Plans process-level scaling for CPU-heavy stages and pushes heavy compute into native libraries or separate services instead of fighting the GIL in one process.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their sixteen-thread ingestion script uses one core, and fixes it with a process pool or a vectorised rewrite on their hardware."},"check":{"q":"A colleague's embedding-prep script runs a pure-Python text-cleaning function over 1M documents with a\n32-thread pool on a 32-core machine and sees no speed-up. They propose 64 threads. What do you say?","a":"The cleaning function is pure-Python CPU work, so in standard CPython the GIL lets only one thread\nexecute bytecode at a time; more threads only add switching overhead, which is why 32 threads barely beat\none. The fix is a `ProcessPoolExecutor` with roughly one worker per core, sending documents in chunks so\npickling is amortised, or moving the hot loop into native code: Polars string expressions or a compiled\nlibrary. Fewer, larger `re` calls also cut per-document interpreter overhead, but the `re` module holds the\nGIL, so regex work still does not parallelise across threads. I would time a small sample sequentially and with processes to confirm the gain before running\nthe full million. Threads would be right only if the work were I/O or GIL-releasing native code."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why did CPython adopt a single global lock rather than fine-grained locks?","options":["Because operating systems in the 1990s did not support threads","Because reference counting and C extensions needed protection, and one lock kept single-threaded code fast and simple","Because a global lock makes all user code thread-safe automatically","Because fine-grained locks are impossible to implement in C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every object's refcount changes constantly; protecting each one individually would slow the common single-threaded case and complicate every extension. The GIL protects interpreter state, not user invariants."},{"q":"A thread pool running large NumPy matrix multiplications uses several cores. Why is that possible under the GIL?","options":["NumPy spawns hidden subprocesses for each call","The GIL is only enforced for I/O operations","NumPy releases the GIL inside its native code while it works on its own buffers","Threads automatically bypass the GIL after the switch interval"],"answer":2,"explanation":"C extensions may drop the GIL while not touching Python objects, so NumPy's compiled kernels run in parallel across threads. The switch interval only governs handover between threads running bytecode."},{"q":"Two threads each run `counter += 1` a million times on a shared int. What can happen in standard CPython?","options":["The result is always exactly two million because of the GIL","The program raises a RuntimeError for concurrent modification","The result is always one million because the threads serialise","Updates can be lost, because the increment spans several bytecodes and a switch can occur between them"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The GIL makes each bytecode safe, not each statement. Load, add and store are separate steps, so an interleaving can overwrite another thread's increment; whether it shows up depends on version and timing."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/generators","sources":["PEP 255 — Simple Generators","PEP 380 — Syntax for Delegating to a Subgenerator","PEP 525 — Asynchronous Generators","Official docs: Python language reference (yield expressions)"],"why":"- **Agent data is too big or too slow to materialise:** a corpus to chunk and embed, an eval set of 50,000\n  prompts, a vector store export, server-sent events from a model, a long agent run's event stream.\n- **Lists fail three ways.** Read every line, parse every document, chunk everything, then embed: memory\n  grows with the dataset, the first embedding call waits for the whole corpus, and a crash at item\n  900,000 loses all work in flight. For an agent, a list means the user sees nothing until the run ends.\n- **Hand-written iterator classes are inside-out.** `__iter__`, `__next__` and explicit state fields\n  (\"which file, which line, which chunk offset\") turn a simple nested loop into an error-prone state\n  machine.\n- **Generators keep the natural loop.** The frame is suspended at `yield` and resumed on the next request,\n  so locals, the open file and the loop position survive between items.\n- **The consumer pulls**, so streaming, early results and bounded memory come for free.","how":"### The protocol\n1. **Calling creates, does not run.** Calling a function that contains `yield` runs none of its body; it\n   returns a generator object, which is an iterator.\n2. **`next()` resumes** execution until the next `yield`, which hands a value out and freezes the frame.\n3. **Return ends it.** The generator raises `StopIteration` (carrying the return value), and `for` loops\n   treat that as the end.\n4. **Single-pass.** Once exhausted, iterating again yields nothing, with no error.\n\n**Mechanically:** the generator object owns a frame (locals, evaluation stack, position of the last\nexecuted instruction). An ordinary call discards its frame on return; a generator keeps it alive between\n`next()` calls, which is the whole trick.\n\n| `inspect.getgeneratorstate(g)` | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `GEN_CREATED` | Before the first `next()` |\n| `GEN_RUNNING` | Currently executing |\n| `GEN_SUSPENDED` | Paused at a `yield` |\n| `GEN_CLOSED` | Finished |\n\n- **No re-entry.** A running generator cannot be resumed, so `next()` on one already executing (say from a\n  second thread) raises `ValueError: generator already executing`.\n\n### Pipelines compose lazily\n- **Building does no work.** `chunks(read_jsonl(path))` builds a pipeline; nothing happens until the\n  consumer asks for the first item. Then one line is read, parsed, chunked and passed down.\n- **Memory is bounded by what each stage holds**, not by the dataset, *because* items flow one at a time.\n- **Pull-based backpressure.** The consumer's `next()` propagates up the chain, so a slow consumer (an\n  embedding API at its rate limit) automatically slows reading and nothing piles up between stages. That\n  is backpressure without any queue.\n- **Standard parts.** Generator expressions, `(f(x) for x in xs)`, are the inline form; `itertools`\n  (`islice`, `chain`, and `batched` from 3.12) supplies the standard pipeline stages.\n\n### Two-way communication and delegation\n`yield` is an expression, so the caller can talk back:\n\n| Call | Effect at the paused `yield` |\n|---|---|\n| `gen.send(value)` | Resumes; the `yield` evaluates to `value` |\n| `gen.throw(exc)` | Raises the exception there |\n| `gen.close()` | Raises `GeneratorExit` there, so `finally` blocks run |\n| `yield from sub` (PEP 380) | Delegates to a sub-generator, forwarding sends and throws, evaluating to its return value |\n\n- **`yield from` exists** *because* refactoring a generator into helpers would otherwise need hand-written\n  forwarding. Async generators have no `yield from`; re-yield with `async for x in sub: yield x`.\n- **Ancestor of coroutines.** This send-and-suspend machinery is where `async`/`await` came from:\n  generator-based coroutines.\n\n### Cleanup and async generators\n- **When resources are released.** A generator holding a resource (an open file inside `with`) releases it\n  when it finishes, when `close()` is called, or when it is garbage-collected, since `close()` runs the\n  `finally`.\n- **Early exit leaks until then.** If a consumer breaks out early and keeps a reference, the resource stays\n  open.\n- **Async generators** (PEP 525): `async def` with `yield`, consumed with `async for`. Their `finally` may\n  need to await, so handle early exit with `contextlib.aclosing()` (3.10+) to close deterministically rather\n  than leaving cleanup to the event loop's finaliser. This is the Stop-button path of every streaming agent.","example":"### A streaming agent loop as an async generator\nThe loop yields typed events (tokens, tool start and end, final answer) that a UI or SSE endpoint renders\nas they happen. The model and tool are stubs so it runs offline.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import aclosing\n\nasync def model_stream(messages):              # stub for a provider's streaming call\n    if messages[-1][\"role\"] == \"tool\":\n        for tok in [\"Your order\", \" shipped\", \" today.\"]:\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.01); yield {\"type\": \"token\", \"text\": tok}\n    else:\n        yield {\"type\": \"tool_call\", \"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"args\": {\"id\": 42}}\n\nasync def lookup_order(id):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.05); return f\"order {id}: shipped\"\n\nasync def run_agent(question, max_steps=4):\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    for step in range(max_steps):\n        call, text = None, []\n        async with aclosing(model_stream(messages)) as stream:   # closed even if our consumer stops\n            async for ev in stream:\n                if ev[\"type\"] == \"token\":\n                    text.append(ev[\"text\"]); yield ev            # stream tokens straight through\n                else:\n                    call = ev\n        if call is None:\n            yield {\"type\": \"final\", \"text\": \"\".join(text), \"steps\": step + 1}\n            return\n        yield {\"type\": \"tool_start\", \"name\": call[\"name\"]}\n        result = await lookup_order(**call[\"args\"])\n        yield {\"type\": \"tool_end\", \"name\": call[\"name\"], \"result\": result}\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"content\": result})\n\nasync def main():\n    async with aclosing(run_agent(\"Where is order 42?\")) as events:\n        async for ev in events:\n            print(ev)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **One generator, many consumers.** The same `run_agent` feeds a CLI, an SSE endpoint\n  (`StreamingResponse` over formatted events) or an eval harness that collects only `final` events.\n- **Stopping is closing.** If the client disconnects, closing `run_agent` runs `aclosing` on the inner\n  model stream, so the upstream connection is released at a known point.\n\n### A lazy embedding pipeline for the agent's index\n```python\nimport itertools, json\nfrom collections.abc import Iterator\n\ndef read_jsonl(path: str) -> Iterator[dict]:\n    with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:      # stays open while iterated\n        for line_no, line in enumerate(f, 1):\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            try:\n                yield json.loads(line)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n                print(f\"skipping malformed line {line_no}\")\n\ndef chunks(docs: Iterator[dict], size: int = 800) -> Iterator[tuple[str, str]]:\n    for doc in docs:\n        text = doc[\"text\"]\n        for start in range(0, len(text), size):\n            yield doc[\"id\"], text[start:start + size]\n\ndef embed_corpus(path: str, batch_size: int = 64) -> int:\n    sent = 0\n    for batch in itertools.batched(chunks(read_jsonl(path)), batch_size):  # 3.12+\n        # vectors = embed_api([text for _, text in batch])  -> upsert by id\n        sent += len(batch)\n    return sent\n```\n\n**Worked sizes (illustrative), 4 GB JSONL corpus:**\n| | List-based | This pipeline |\n|---|---|---|\n| Held in memory | Parsed corpus plus every chunk, before the first API call | One line, one parsed document, one batch of 64 × 800 characters (about 50 KB of text) |\n| First batch ready | After the whole corpus is parsed | Within milliseconds of starting |\n| Malformed line | Often kills the job | Skipped and logged |\n\n**See the laziness:** put a `print` in each stage and run it on a two-document file with 2,000-character\ntexts.\n1. Output interleaves: read doc 1, chunk 1a, chunk 1b, chunk 1c, read doc 2, chunk 2a, and so on.\n2. The batch closes once 64 chunks (or the end of input) arrive.\n3. A list pipeline would print every \"read\" before the first \"chunk\".\n4. The mechanism: `batched` pulls a chunk, `chunks` needs a document, `read_jsonl` resumes inside its `for`\n   over the file, reads one line, and suspends again at `yield`, with the file still open and the line\n   counter intact.\n\n### Stop a token stream early, cleanly\nAn async generator stands in for a provider's SSE stream. With `aclosing`, breaking out runs the\ngenerator's async `finally` before the next line, so the connection is released at a known point.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import aclosing\n\nasync def token_stream(prompt: str):\n    print(\"open connection\")\n    try:\n        for tok in [\"The\", \" answer\", \" is\", \" 42\", \".\"]:\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.01)          # stands in for an SSE chunk arriving\n            yield tok\n    finally:\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)                 # async cleanup, e.g. await response.aclose()\n        print(\"connection closed\")\n\nasync def first_n(n: int) -> str:\n    out = []\n    async with aclosing(token_stream(\"q\")) as stream:\n        async for tok in stream:\n            out.append(tok)\n            if len(out) == n:\n                break                          # aclosing awaits aclose() as the block exits\n    print(\"after block\")\n    return \"\".join(out)\n\nprint(asyncio.run(first_n(2)))\n# open connection / connection closed / after block / The answer\n```\nWithout `aclosing`, \"connection closed\" would print only when the event loop finalises the generator,\nwhich under load means connections held open well past the request.","patterns":["**Yield typed agent events, not raw text.** Have the agent loop yield small dicts or Pydantic models (`token`, `tool_start`, `tool_end`, `final`, `error`) with a step number, and format them as SSE only at the HTTP edge. Monitor time to first event and events per run.","**Stream responses with an async generator.** Pass an async generator to FastAPI's `StreamingResponse`, open the upstream stream inside it with `aclosing`, and release it in `finally`. Monitor open upstream connections and stream duration after client disconnects.","**Batch at the boundary to the API.** Feed generator stages into `itertools.batched(it, n)` (3.12+), or on older Pythons `while batch := tuple(islice(it, n)): ...`, sized to the provider's max inputs per call. Monitor peak RSS and items per second.","**Checkpoint the position, not the data.** Yield the line number or record id with each item and persist the last fully processed one every N batches, so a crash at item 900,000 resumes instead of restarting. Monitor checkpoint age.","**Count and quarantine bad records.** Replace the `print` in `read_jsonl` with a counter and a write to a dead-letter file. Alert on malformed-record rate, because a spike usually means an upstream format change.","**Paginate tool APIs as a generator.** Wrap cursor pagination in a generator that yields items and fetches the next page only when the consumer needs it, so `islice(items(), 100)` stops after the pages it used. Monitor pages fetched per job."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Users expect to watch an agent think: tokens, \"calling search...\", tool\n  results, the final answer. An async generator that yields those events is the natural shape of a\n  streaming agent loop, and plain generators keep RAG ingestion and eval runs at flat memory.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider SDK streaming (`async for` over chunks), FastAPI's\n  `StreamingResponse` for SSE, agent-framework streaming APIs (LangGraph's `stream` and `astream` yield\n  step updates), and ingestion pipelines feeding an embedding API in batches.\n- **What you control.** The event schema your agent loop yields, closing upstream streams with\n  `contextlib.aclosing()` when the user stops, batch size at the API boundary, and checkpoint positions so\n  long ingestion or eval jobs resume.\n- **What you can skip.** Hand-written iterator classes and generator-based coroutines (pre-`async`). Know\n  laziness, single-pass behaviour, `yield from` and async-generator cleanup.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Streaming agent events to a UI.** An async generator yields tokens, tool-call starts and tool results,\n  and FastAPI's `StreamingResponse` sends them as SSE.\n- **RAG ingestion at flat memory.** A chain of generators reads, chunks and batches a large corpus for the\n  embedding API without loading it all.\n- **Large eval runs.** Prompts stream from a JSONL file through the agent, with results written as they\n  arrive and a checkpoint so a crash resumes.\n- **Stopping a stream early.** When the user stops a run, `contextlib.aclosing()` closes the upstream model\n  stream at a known point.","mini_project":"**Stream an agent loop as typed events (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn a two-tool agent loop into an async generator of events and stream it to a browser.\n- **Steps:** define an event schema (token, tool_call, tool_result, final); write the loop as an async\n  generator that yields those events from a fake model and two fake tools; expose it through a FastAPI\n  `StreamingResponse` as SSE; wrap the upstream stream in `aclosing()` and log when it closes.\n- **Done when:** `curl` shows events arriving one at a time, closing the client mid-run logs the upstream\n  close, and memory stays flat over 1,000 simulated runs."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Consumer (embed loop)\n    participant B as itertools.batched\n    participant K as chunks()\n    participant R as read_jsonl()\n    participant F as Open file\n    C->>B: next() for a batch\n    B->>K: next() for a chunk\n    K->>R: next() for a document\n    R->>F: read one line\n    F-->>R: line text\n    R-->>K: yield parsed doc (file still open)\n    K-->>B: yield (id, chunk) and suspend\n    Note over B,K: repeats until 64 chunks or end of input\n    B-->>C: tuple of 64 chunks\n    Note over C: slow embedding API means slow pulls, backpressure with no queue","caption":"One pull through the RAG embedding pipeline below: each `next()` travels up the chain, one line is read,\nand each stage suspends at `yield` again with its state intact. Nothing moves until the consumer asks."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a generator in Python, and why does it save memory?","a":"- **A function that pauses at `yield`.** Calling it returns a generator object without running the\n  body; each `next()` resumes the saved frame until the next `yield`.\n- **It saves memory because items flow one at a time**, so a pipeline holds the current item or batch\n  instead of the whole dataset.\n- **The kept frame is the trick:** locals, the open file and the loop position survive between items."},{"q":"How would you design the streaming interface of an agent that calls tools mid-answer?","a":"- **Make the agent loop an async generator** that yields typed events: tokens, tool start, tool end with\n  a short result, final answer, error. The HTTP layer only formats them as SSE.\n- **Close inner streams with `aclosing`**, so a client disconnect closes the run generator, which closes\n  the model stream and releases the connection.\n- **Yield an `error` event** for failures after the first byte, because the status code is already sent,\n  and monitor time to first event."},{"q":"A streaming endpoint exhausts its HTTP connection pool when clients disconnect early. What is wrong?","a":"- **The async generator wrapping the upstream stream is not closed deterministically.** Breaking out\n  without `contextlib.aclosing()` leaves its async `finally` to the event loop's finaliser.\n- **Fix:** iterate inside `async with aclosing(stream) as s:` and release the upstream response in the\n  generator's `finally`.\n- **Monitor** open upstream connections and stream duration after disconnect, and watch for \"unclosed\"\n  warnings under load."},{"q":"When would you not use a generator pipeline?","a":"- **When you need the data more than once**, random access or `len()`, because a generator is\n  single-pass and a second iteration silently yields nothing.\n- **For numeric hot loops over millions of small values**, where vectorised NumPy or Polars beats yielding\n  Python objects.\n- **For complex multi-producer flows**, where a bounded `asyncio.Queue` between tasks can be clearer."},{"q":"How would you make a 10-hour embedding job for a RAG index resumable?","a":"- **Yield the position with each item**, such as the line number or record id, alongside the data.\n- **Persist the last fully processed position every N batches**, so a crash at item 900,000 resumes\n  there instead of restarting from zero.\n- **Make upserts idempotent by id**, so replaying a partly written batch is harmless, and alert on\n  checkpoint age."},{"q":"What do send(), throw() and yield from add beyond plain iteration?","a":"- **`send(value)`** resumes the generator with the `yield` evaluating to that value; **`throw(exc)`**\n  raises an exception at the paused `yield`; **`close()`** raises `GeneratorExit` so `finally` runs.\n- **`yield from sub`** (PEP 380) delegates to a sub-generator, forwarding sends and throws and evaluating\n  to its return value.\n- **This machinery is the ancestor of `async`/`await`**, through generator-based coroutines."}],"tradeoffs":["**Flat memory vs single pass.** Lazy pipelines keep memory flat and deliver first results early, but each item is produced once. For random access, `len()` or several passes, materialise to a list or reopen the source.","**Plain-loop readability vs late errors.** Streaming code reads like a plain loop, but errors surface at consumption time, not at the call, so a traceback may point at the consumer far from the faulty stage. In a streaming agent, an exception after the first event cannot change the HTTP status, so yield an `error` event instead.","**Negligible overhead vs numeric hot loops.** Per-item overhead is negligible for I/O-bound work, but over millions of small values vectorised NumPy or Polars operations are far faster than yielding Python objects.","**Natural streams vs cleanup.** Async generators expose network streams naturally, at the cost of trickier cleanup when consumers stop early. For complex flows (several producers, sub-agents streaming at once), a bounded `asyncio.Queue` between tasks can be clearer."],"pitfalls":["**Iterating the same generator twice**, for example once to count and once to process. The second pass silently yields nothing and an eval reports zero results. Fix: materialise to a list if it fits, or rebuild the generator for each pass.","**Expecting side effects from a call.** Nothing runs until iteration, so a \"write records\" generator that is never consumed writes nothing, and an agent generator nobody iterates never calls its tools.","**Breaking out of an async generator wrapping an HTTP stream without `aclosing`.** Symptom: connection-pool exhaustion or \"unclosed\" warnings under load. Fix: `async with aclosing(gen) as s:`.","**`try/except` around the generator call.** Exceptions are raised later during iteration, outside the handler, so the guard never triggers. Fix: wrap the consuming loop, or handle errors inside the stage.","**Leaving a debugging `list(gen)` in.** Memory returns to dataset-sized and the job slows on large inputs; on an agent stream it also hides every event until the run ends.","**Bare `next(it)` inside a generator on an iterator that may be exhausted.** Since PEP 479 the stray `StopIteration` becomes `RuntimeError: generator raised StopIteration` instead of silently ending the outer generator. Use `next(it, None)` or catch it explicitly.","**Sharing one generator between threads or DataLoader-style workers.** Symptom: `ValueError: generator already executing` or duplicated and missing items. Give each worker its own source or put a `queue.Queue` between them."],"when":"- **Use when** sequences are large, slow to arrive or potentially unbounded: streaming model output and\n  agent step events to a client, reading JSONL and Parquet exports row by row, chunking documents for\n  embedding, batching requests, paginating an API. FastAPI's `StreamingResponse` and most SDK streaming\n  interfaces accept or return (async) iterators.\n- **Prefer a list when** the data is small and you need it more than once, such as the message history you\n  send to the model each step.\n- **Measure:** peak memory before and after converting a list pipeline, and time to first event for streams.\n- **Interviews:** explain laziness, single-pass behaviour, `yield from`, and how async generators relate to\n  token streaming.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes ingestion and eval pipelines as generator stages with batching, and wraps streaming responses in async generators closed with `aclosing`.","architect":"Designs data and streaming paths so memory stays bounded by batch size, not dataset size, and so backpressure flows from the consumer to the source.","fde":"Rewrites a customer's notebook that loads a whole export into memory into a streaming script that runs on their modest VM without crashing."},"check":{"q":"An eval script does `cases = (load(x) for x in paths)`, logs `sum(1 for _ in cases)` cases, then runs\nthe model over `cases`. It reports 500 cases loaded but zero scored. Explain and fix it.","a":"`cases` is a generator expression, a single-pass iterator. Counting consumes it completely, so when\nthe scoring loop iterates it the generator is already exhausted and yields nothing, silently. The fix depends\non size: if the cases fit in memory, materialise once with `cases = [load(x) for x in paths]` and use\n`len(cases)`; if not, count while scoring in the same pass, or rebuild the generator from `paths` for each\npass. A good answer also notes that generators raise no error on reuse, so a test asserting the scored\ncount equals the loaded count would have caught this."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when you call a function whose body contains `yield`?","options":["The body runs until the first `yield` and returns that value","None of the body runs; a generator object is returned that runs the body on iteration","The whole body runs eagerly and the yielded values are collected into a list","It raises TypeError unless called inside a for loop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The presence of `yield` makes the function a generator function. Calling it only creates the generator; execution starts on the first `next()` and pauses at each `yield`."},{"q":"Why does a generator pipeline over a large JSONL file keep memory roughly constant?","options":["Because Python compresses generator state automatically","Because generators store their outputs on disk instead of in memory","Because the file is memory-mapped by the generator","Because each stage holds only the current item or batch, pulled on demand by the consumer"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Items flow one at a time from the source through each stage, so peak memory depends on batch size and per-item size, not on how many items the file contains."},{"q":"What is the safest way to stop consuming an async generator that wraps an HTTP stream early?","options":["Delete the variable and rely on reference counting to close it immediately","Iterate it inside `contextlib.aclosing()` so its cleanup is awaited when the block exits","Raise StopIteration inside the async for loop body","Call `next()` on it until it is exhausted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Async generator cleanup may need to await, which garbage collection cannot do synchronously. `aclosing` awaits `aclose()` deterministically, releasing the connection at a known point."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/decorators","sources":["PEP 318 — Decorators for Functions and Methods","PEP 3129 — Class Decorators","Official docs: functools (wraps, cache, lru_cache)"],"why":"- **Every model and tool call needs the same wrapping:** retry on 429 and timeouts with backoff, record\n  latency and token counts, open a trace span, check permissions, cache identical calls.\n- **Inline copies drift.** Every function starts with the same fifteen lines of try/except/sleep/log, the\n  copies diverge, and one forgotten retry is an outage.\n- **Helper calls at top and bottom still repeat** (`start = timer(); ...; log(start)`), and miss early\n  returns and exceptions unless each is wrapped in `try/finally`.\n- **Base classes and manual wrapping are worse.** Inheritance forces unrelated functions into class\n  hierarchies; `fetch = with_retry(fetch)` works but sits apart from the definition, easy to forget.\n- **Functions are first-class objects**, so a function can take another and return a new one that adds\n  behaviour. `@` (PEP 318) puts that on the definition, where readers see it. Frameworks use it for\n  registration: `@app.get(\"/chat\")` in FastAPI, `@mcp.tool()` in the MCP SDK and agent-framework tool\n  decorators record your function and read its signature and docstring to build a schema.","how":"### Desugaring\n```\n@timed\ndef embed(texts): ...\n```\n- **Is exactly `embed = timed(embed)`,** executed once, at definition time (usually import time).\n- **Typical shape:** `timed` defines an inner `wrapper(*args, **kwargs)` that does work before and after\n  calling the original, and returns `wrapper`.\n- **The wrapper is a closure:** it keeps a reference to the original function in its enclosing scope,\n  which is how it can call it later.\n\n### Preserve identity with functools.wraps\n- **The problem.** The wrapper has its own `__name__`, `__doc__` and signature, so every decorated function\n  looks like `wrapper(*args, **kwargs)` to logs, debuggers and, critically, frameworks that build tool or\n  API schemas from signature and docstring.\n- **The fix.** `@functools.wraps(fn)` copies the name, docstring, module and annotations, and sets\n  `__wrapped__` so `inspect.signature` reports the original signature.\n\n### Decorators with arguments are factories\n1. `@retry(attempts=3)` calls `retry(attempts=3)` first.\n2. That returns the real decorator, which receives the function.\n3. The decorator returns the wrapper.\n\nThree levels of nesting: factory, decorator, wrapper. Class decorators (PEP 3129) work the same way on\nclasses; `@dataclass` is one.\n\n### Async functions need async wrappers\n- **Calling an `async def` returns a coroutine without running it.**\n- **So a plain sync wrapper** measures how long it took to *create* the coroutine (microseconds), and its\n  `try/except` never sees the real errors, which happen later at `await`.\n- **Fix:** a decorator meant for both checks `inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn)` and returns an `async def`\n  wrapper that awaits the call.\n\n### Typing: runtime metadata is not static types\n- **`wraps` fixes runtime only.** A type checker sees the wrapper's declared type. Annotate a decorator as\n  `Callable[..., Any] -> Callable[..., Any]` and every decorated function loses its parameter types in mypy\n  and the IDE.\n- **`ParamSpec` (PEP 612, 3.10+) carries them through:**\n  `def deco(fn: Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]) -> Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]`, with the wrapper taking\n  `*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs`.\n- **Signature-changing decorators** (injecting a client argument, say) need `Concatenate` to say so.\n\n### Class-based decorators and methods\n- **Any callable works**, including a class whose `__call__` wraps the function; handy when the decorator\n  holds state such as counters or a cache.\n- **The catch is method binding.** Plain functions are descriptors, so `obj.method` binds `self`\n  automatically. An instance of your class is not, so decorating a method with it silently drops `self`\n  unless the class implements `__get__`.\n- **Function-based wrappers avoid this**, because the wrapper is itself a function.\n\n### Stacking order\n```\n@traced\n@retry(attempts=3)\ndef call(): ...\n```\n- **Equals `call = traced(retry(attempts=3)(call))`.** Decorators apply bottom-up, so at call time the\n  outermost one, `traced`, runs first.\n- **Here one span covers all three attempts;** swap them and each attempt gets its own span. Order is a\n  design decision, not style.\n- **Registration decorators go on top.** `@mcp.tool()` or `@app.get(...)` registers whatever object it\n  receives. Put it below `@retry` and the framework registers the un-retried function, while the retrying\n  wrapper is bound to a module name nobody calls.","example":"### A guarded agent tool: approval, timeout and errors as results\nOne decorator factory gives every tool the same contract: side-effecting tools need an approval flag, each\ncall has a timeout, and failures come back as a string the model can read instead of crashing the loop.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, functools, inspect\n\nclass NeedsApproval(Exception): ...\n\ndef agent_tool(timeout: float = 10.0, side_effect: bool = False):\n    def decorator(fn):\n        if not inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn):\n            raise TypeError(\"agent_tool expects async def tools\")\n\n        @functools.wraps(fn)                     # keep name, docstring and signature for the schema\n        async def wrapper(*args, approved: bool = False, **kwargs):\n            if side_effect and not approved:\n                raise NeedsApproval(f\"{fn.__name__} needs human approval\")   # loop pauses for a human\n            try:\n                async with asyncio.timeout(timeout):\n                    return await fn(*args, **kwargs)\n            except TimeoutError:\n                return f\"error: {fn.__name__} timed out after {timeout}s\"\n            except Exception as e:                # CancelledError is BaseException, so it still propagates\n                return f\"error: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\"\n        wrapper.side_effect = side_effect          # metadata the agent loop can read\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@agent_tool(timeout=2.0)\nasync def get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Current weather for a city.\"\"\"\n    if city == \"Atlantis\":\n        raise KeyError(\"unknown city\")\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.1); return f\"{city}: 18C, cloudy\"\n\n@agent_tool(side_effect=True)\nasync def send_email(to: str, body: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Send an email to a customer.\"\"\"\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.1); return f\"sent to {to}\"\n\nasync def main():\n    print(get_weather.__name__, inspect.signature(get_weather))   # get_weather (city: str) -> str\n    print(await get_weather(\"Paris\"))                             # Paris: 18C, cloudy\n    print(await get_weather(\"Atlantis\"))                          # error: KeyError: 'unknown city'\n    try:\n        await send_email(\"a@b.c\", \"hi\")\n    except NeedsApproval as e:\n        print(\"paused:\", e)\n    print(await send_email(\"a@b.c\", \"hi\", approved=True))         # sent to a@b.c\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **`inspect.signature` follows `__wrapped__`**, so the schema still shows `(city: str)`; the extra\n  `approved` keyword lives only on the wrapper and is never exposed to the model.\n- **Errors as results** let the model retry with different arguments or explain the failure, which is\n  usually better than aborting the whole run.\n\n### An async retry decorator with backoff and jitter\n```python\nimport asyncio, functools, inspect, random, time\nfrom collections.abc import Awaitable, Callable\nfrom typing import ParamSpec, TypeVar\n\nP, R = ParamSpec(\"P\"), TypeVar(\"R\")\n\nclass Transient(Exception): ...\n\ndef retry(attempts: int = 3, base_delay: float = 0.5):\n    def decorator(fn: Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]) -> Callable[P, Awaitable[R]]:\n        if not inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn):\n            raise TypeError(\"retry() here supports async functions only\")\n\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        async def wrapper(*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs) -> R:\n            for attempt in range(1, attempts + 1):\n                start = time.perf_counter()\n                try:\n                    return await fn(*args, **kwargs)\n                except Transient as exc:\n                    ms = (time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000\n                    print(f\"{fn.__name__} attempt {attempt} failed after {ms:.0f} ms: {exc}\")\n                    if attempt == attempts:\n                        raise\n                    delay = base_delay * 2 ** (attempt - 1)\n                    await asyncio.sleep(delay * random.uniform(0.5, 1.5))  # jitter\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@retry(attempts=4, base_delay=0.1)\nasync def complete(prompt: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Call the model provider.\"\"\"\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.05)\n    if random.random() < 0.5:\n        raise Transient(\"429 rate limited\")\n    return f\"answer to {prompt!r}\"\n\nprint(complete.__name__, complete.__doc__)     # complete Call the model provider.\nprint(asyncio.run(complete(\"hi\")))\n```\n\n**The numbers** (4 attempts, 0.1 s base delay, 50% failure rate):\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Waits between failures | about 0.1, 0.2 and 0.4 s, with ±50% jitter |\n| A call that never succeeds | gives up after roughly 0.7 s of backoff plus four call durations (0.2 s here) |\n| Probability all four attempts fail | 0.5⁴ = 1/16, so about 6% of calls still raise |\n\n- **Retries change the error rate; they do not remove it.** In a 10-step agent run where every step makes\n  one such call, a 6% per-call failure rate compounds, which is why tool errors should come back as results.\n- **What ran when:** `retry(...)` executed once at import and returned `decorator`; `decorator(complete)`\n  also ran once at import and rebound the name `complete` to `wrapper`; only `wrapper`'s body runs per call.\n- **Why jitter:** many clients failing on the same 429 would otherwise retry in lockstep and hit the\n  provider together again.\n- **In production** you would usually use `tenacity` rather than hand-roll this.\n\n### A tool registry that survives decoration\nRegistration plus an async-aware timer, stacked in the right order. Remove the `@functools.wraps(fn)`\nabove `async_wrapper` and the registry records `async_wrapper` with no docstring and params\n`['args', 'kwargs']`: exactly the schema an LLM would then guess against.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, functools, inspect, time\n\nTOOLS: dict[str, dict] = {}\n\ndef tool(fn):                                    # registration: records whatever it receives\n    TOOLS[fn.__name__] = {\"doc\": inspect.getdoc(fn),\n                          \"params\": list(inspect.signature(fn).parameters)}  # follows __wrapped__\n    return fn\n\ndef timed(fn):\n    def report(start: float) -> None:\n        print(f\"{fn.__name__} took {(time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000:.0f} ms\")\n\n    if inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn):          # async in, async out\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        async def async_wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n            start = time.perf_counter()\n            try:\n                return await fn(*args, **kwargs)  # time the awaited work, not coroutine creation\n            finally:\n                report(start)\n        return async_wrapper\n\n    @functools.wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        try:\n            return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n        finally:\n            report(start)\n    return wrapper\n\n@tool                                            # on top, so it registers the timed wrapper\n@timed\nasync def search_docs(query: str, k: int = 5) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Search the knowledge base.\"\"\"\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.12)                    # stands in for a vector-store query\n    return [query] * k\n\nprint(TOOLS)       # {'search_docs': {'doc': 'Search the knowledge base.', 'params': ['query', 'k']}}\nprint(inspect.iscoroutinefunction(search_docs))  # True: frameworks will await it\nasyncio.run(search_docs(\"refund policy\"))        # search_docs took 120 ms (about)\n```","patterns":["**One house tool decorator.** Give every agent tool the same wrapper (timeout, errors returned as results, tracing span, approval for side effects) and put the framework's registration decorator above it. Monitor tool error rate and timeout rate per tool name.","**Retries via tenacity, scoped to transient errors.** Use `@retry(stop=stop_after_attempt(4), wait=wait_random_exponential(multiplier=0.5, max=10), retry=retry_if_exception_type(...))` listing only rate-limit, timeout and 5xx errors, with `before_sleep=before_sleep_log(logger, logging.WARNING)`. Monitor retries per call and final failure rate.","**Retry only idempotent tools.** Mark side-effecting tools (email, CRM writes) as non-retryable, or pass an idempotency key the downstream API honours, so a timeout after the write cannot duplicate it. Alert on duplicate-key rejections.","**Order deadlines deliberately.** For per-attempt timeouts put `@retry` above the timeout decorator; wrap the whole call in an outer deadline as well. Check traces: each attempt's span should end at its own timeout.","**Check library decorators handle async.** Before adopting a tracing or metrics decorator, decorate an `async def` and assert the recorded duration includes the awaited work, because a sync wrapper inside a library has the same microsecond-latency bug.","**Test the generated schema and caches.** Add a unit test that every registered tool has a docstring and no `args`/`kwargs` parameters, which catches a missing `wraps` or a misplaced registration decorator. Use `functools.lru_cache(maxsize=...)` only for pure functions on hashable arguments (prompt templates, tokenizer loading) and export `fn.cache_info()` hits and misses; use a TTL cache elsewhere."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The model only sees your tools through a schema built from name,\n  signature and docstring, and every tool call needs the same wrapping: retries, timeouts, tracing,\n  permission checks, error-to-result conversion. Decorators attach all of that at the definition.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `@mcp.tool()` in the MCP SDK, `@tool` in LangChain, LangSmith's\n  `@traceable` for tracing, tenacity's `@retry`, FastAPI's `@app.post(...)`, and `functools.cache` for\n  loading tokenizers or prompt templates once.\n- **What you control.** Whether wrappers keep metadata (`wraps`), whether they are async-aware, the\n  stacking order (registration on top, retry versus timeout and tracing), and which tools are retryable\n  or need approval.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing class-based decorators with `__get__` and elaborate generic typing unless\n  you maintain a library. Know the desugaring, the factory pattern and the three edges.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Registering agent tools.** `@mcp.tool()` and framework `@tool` decorators read the name, signature and\n  docstring to build the schema the model sees.\n- **Uniform tool wrapping.** One decorator adds retry, timeout and error-to-result conversion to every\n  tool, so a failing tool returns a message the model can act on.\n- **Tracing agent steps.** A tracing decorator records latency, inputs and token counts per model and tool\n  call without touching the tool body.\n- **Approval gates.** A decorator marks risky tools (send email, run code) as needing human approval before\n  the call goes through.","mini_project":"**Build a tool registry with an async-aware wrapper (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** register tools with a decorator, generate their schemas, and wrap every call with timing and\n  retry that works for async tools.\n- **Steps:** write a `@tool` decorator that stores each function in a registry and builds a JSON schema\n  from `inspect.signature` and the docstring; write a `@traced` decorator using `functools.wraps` that\n  awaits async tools and records duration; stack them on three tools; then remove `wraps` and watch the\n  schemas break.\n- **Done when:** the generated schemas show real tool names and docstrings, timings reflect real awaited\n  durations, and you can explain why the stacking order you chose is correct."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    subgraph IMPORT[\"Definition time, once at import\"]\n        D[\"def search_docs(query, k)\"] --> T[\"timed(fn) returns async_wrapper\"]\n        T -->|\"wraps copies name, doc, __wrapped__\"| R[\"tool(async_wrapper) records schema\"]\n        R --> N[\"search_docs bound to async_wrapper\"]\n    end\n    subgraph CALL[\"Call time, every tool call\"]\n        C[\"await search_docs(q)\"] --> W[\"async_wrapper: start timer\"]\n        W --> O[\"await original search_docs\"]\n        O -->|\"return or raise\"| F[\"finally: report elapsed ms\"]\n    end\n    N --> C","caption":"Two phases for the stacked `@tool` over `@timed` example below: decorators run bottom-up once at import,\nthen every tool call from the agent enters the outermost wrapper first."},"interview":[{"q":"What does the @ decorator syntax actually do, and when does it run?","a":"- **It is a rebinding:** `@timed` above `def embed` is exactly `embed = timed(embed)`, executed once at\n  definition time, usually import.\n- **The wrapper is a closure** that keeps a reference to the original function and runs per call, while\n  the decorator body itself never runs again.\n- **So expensive work in the decorator body** slows startup or freezes a value that should be read per\n  call."},{"q":"How would you give every tool in an agent the same timeout, error handling and approval rules?","a":"- **One decorator factory** such as `agent_tool(timeout=..., side_effect=...)` that wraps each async tool,\n  enforces `asyncio.timeout`, returns failures as model-readable strings and refuses side-effecting calls\n  without approval.\n- **Keep `functools.wraps`** so the schema the model sees is the original name, docstring and parameters,\n  and put the framework's registration decorator on top.\n- **Let `CancelledError` through** (it is a `BaseException`), and test that each registered tool has a\n  docstring and no `args`/`kwargs` parameters."},{"q":"Write a decorator that takes arguments, such as retry(attempts=3). Why three levels?","a":"- **Factory, decorator, wrapper.** `retry(attempts=3)` is called first and returns the real decorator,\n  which receives the function and returns the wrapper.\n- **The arguments live in the factory's closure**, so every decorated function gets its own settings.\n- **For async targets** the wrapper must be `async def` and await the call, and `ParamSpec` (PEP 612,\n  3.10+) keeps parameter types visible to mypy and the IDE."},{"q":"Traces show one span covering three retry attempts of a tool call, but you wanted a span per attempt. What happened?","a":"- **Stacking order.** Decorators apply bottom-up, so `@traced` above `@retry(...)` makes the span wrap\n  all attempts.\n- **Swap them** so `@retry` is outermost and each attempt gets its own span; the same reasoning puts\n  `@retry` above a per-attempt timeout decorator.\n- **Keep registration decorators on top**, or the framework registers the unwrapped function."},{"q":"When would you avoid a decorator, or a functools cache, in an AI service?","a":"- **When behaviour depends on per-call context** the reader must see; an explicit wrapper or context\n  manager is clearer than hidden control flow.\n- **`lru_cache` on methods or per-user data** keeps `self` alive in every key and never expires, so\n  memory grows steadily; use a TTL cache instead.\n- **Generic retries on non-idempotent tools** (email, CRM writes) duplicate side effects after a timeout."},{"q":"With 4 attempts and a 50% transient failure rate, how many calls still fail, and how long do they take?","a":"- **All four fail with probability 0.5⁴ = 1/16**, so about 6% of calls still raise: retries change the\n  error rate, they do not remove it.\n- **Backoff of 0.1, 0.2 and 0.4 s** adds roughly 0.7 s before giving up, plus the four call durations,\n  and jitter of ±50% stops clients retrying in lockstep.\n- **Monitor** retries per call and the final failure rate, not just the success count."}],"tradeoffs":["**Less duplication vs hidden control flow.** Decorators remove duplicated cross-cutting code and make it visible at the definition, but hide control flow. When behaviour depends on per-call context (which user, which run), an explicit wrapper or context manager is clearer.","**Concise registration vs import-time effects.** Registration decorators make tool and route definitions concise but run at import time, so import order and module side effects start to matter, which bites in large packages and test setups.","**Instant memoisation vs lifetime.** `functools.lru_cache` and `cache` key on arguments and live for the process; they are wrong for unhashable inputs, per-user data or anything that must expire.","**One decorator for both vs simplicity.** Generic sync-and-async decorators are convenient, at the cost of branching and harder typing; separate decorators are simpler when a codebase is all-async."],"pitfalls":["**Omitting `functools.wraps`.** Symptom: every tool in an agent's schema named `wrapper` with no description, or FastAPI dependencies resolving the wrong parameters.","**A sync wrapper on an `async def`.** Latency metrics show microseconds and retries never fire, because the error only appears when the coroutine is awaited elsewhere. Fix: branch on `inspect.iscoroutinefunction(fn)` and return an `async def` wrapper that awaits the call.","**`lru_cache` or `cache` on a method.** The cache holds a reference to `self` in every key, so instances stay alive while cached (forever under `cache`). Symptom: steadily growing memory. Fix: cache a module-level function keyed on plain values, or keep a bounded cache on the instance.","**Retry and timeout stacked against your intent.** Timeout outside retry is one deadline for all attempts; retry outside timeout gives each attempt its own. Symptom: traces show either one timeout killing all retries or retries continuing far past the deadline. Fix: choose the order on purpose, and add an outer deadline when retries are per-attempt.","**Expensive work in the decorator body instead of the wrapper.** It runs once at import, which slows startup or freezes a value that should be read per call.","**A framework's registration decorator below your wrappers.** The framework registers the bare function. Symptom: tools or routes that work but never retry, trace or check auth.","**Generic retry on non-idempotent calls** (a tool that sends an email or writes a CRM row). A timeout after the side effect happened shows up as duplicate emails or rows."],"when":"- **Use when** the same behaviour wraps many functions and does not depend on their internals: tool\n  registration, retries, timeouts, tracing spans, metrics, auth and approval checks, caching.\n- **Prefer established libraries:** tenacity for retries, OpenTelemetry or your tracing SDK's decorator,\n  `functools.cache`, over hand-rolled versions.\n- **Avoid** decorators for logic that varies per call or that the reader must see to understand the function.\n- **Review checklist:** every wrapper uses `wraps`, handles async correctly, and is stacked in the intended order.\n- **Interviews:** write a parameterised decorator and explain the desugaring.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async-aware decorators with `functools.wraps` for retries and tracing, and uses framework decorators to register tools and routes.","architect":"Standardises the cross-cutting layer, one retry policy and one tracing decorator, so every model and tool call is observable and resilient by default.","fde":"Adds tracing or retry decorators to a customer's existing functions without rewriting them, quickly instrumenting a pilot to find where latency goes."},"check":{"q":"A team adds `@timed` (sync wrapper, no `wraps`) to their async tool functions and registers them with an\nagent framework. Latency dashboards show 0.01 ms per tool and the model starts calling tools with wrong\narguments. Explain both symptoms.","a":"The sync wrapper calls the async function, which only creates a coroutine and returns immediately, so\nthe timer measures coroutine creation, not the awaited work; the real latency happens later, if at all, when\nthe framework awaits the returned coroutine. Frameworks that decide how to call a tool with\n`inspect.iscoroutinefunction` now see a sync function, and may never await the result. Without `functools.wraps`, the wrapper exposes its own name,\nmissing docstring and `(*args, **kwargs)` signature, so the framework generates tool schemas with no\nparameter names or descriptions, and the model guesses the arguments. The fix is an `async def` wrapper\nthat awaits the call, decorated with `functools.wraps(fn)` so name, docstring and signature survive."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is `@a` stacked above `@b` above `def f` equivalent to?","options":["`f = b(a(f))`, so `b` runs first on each call","`f = a(b(f))`, so `a`'s wrapper runs first on each call","`f = a(f); f = b(f)` with `b` outermost","It depends on which decorator was imported first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Decorators apply bottom-up at definition time, so the top decorator wraps everything below it and its wrapper is entered first at call time. That determines, for example, whether a span covers all retries."},{"q":"Why does an agent framework that reads function signatures need decorated tools to use `functools.wraps`?","options":["Because `wraps` makes the function run faster","Because frameworks reject any function that has been decorated","Because otherwise the framework sees the wrapper's generic name, docstring and `*args, **kwargs` signature","Because `wraps` converts the function into a coroutine automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tool schemas are built from name, docstring and parameters. `wraps` copies metadata and sets `__wrapped__`, so `inspect.signature` reports the original parameters instead of the wrapper's."},{"q":"A sync timing decorator wraps an `async def` model call. What does it actually measure?","options":["The full network round-trip of the model call","Nothing, because Python refuses to decorate coroutines","The time until the first token arrives","Only the time to create the coroutine object, since the call runs later when it is awaited"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Calling an async function returns a coroutine without executing its body. Timing, retries and exception handling must happen in an async wrapper that awaits the call."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/context-managers-resource-cleanup","sources":["PEP 343 — The \"with\" Statement","PEP 492 — Coroutines with async and await syntax (async with)","Official docs: contextlib"],"why":"- **Resources leak on the unhappy path.** A streaming LLM call opens a connection; the client\n  disconnects, a parser raises on malformed JSON, or a timeout cancels the task, and the line that closes\n  the stream never runs. A sandbox container started for a code tool stays up after the run crashes.\n- **Small leaks compound.** An agent backend does this thousands of times an hour: the connection pool\n  fills, new requests wait for a free connection, and latency climbs until the service looks down. GPU\n  code has the same shape: forget to restore a mode or release a buffer and memory creeps up.\n- **The naive fixes fall short.**\n  - Calling `close()` at the end of the function misses every early return and exception.\n  - `try/finally` is correct, but must be repeated at every call site, nested for each additional\n    resource, and written correctly every time.\n  - Relying on `__del__` or garbage collection gives no timing guarantee: reference cycles delay it, other\n    interpreters do not refcount, and async cleanup cannot run from a finaliser at all.\n- **PEP 343 packaged `try/finally` into the resource itself.** The object that knows how to acquire also\n  knows how to release, and the `with` statement guarantees the release runs. Callers write one line and\n  cannot get the cleanup wrong.\n- **What it covers:** HTTP connection pools, streams, DB sessions, locks, temp files and GPU memory\n  modes, which all leak quietly when cleanup depends on every code path remembering it.","how":"### The protocol\n`with expr as x:` runs these steps:\n1. **Evaluate** `expr` to a manager.\n2. **Enter:** call `manager.__enter__()` and bind its return value to `x`.\n3. **Run the block.**\n4. **Always exit:** call `manager.__exit__(exc_type, exc, tb)`. On a normal exit all three are `None`; on\n   an exception they describe it.\n\n- **Suppression.** If `__exit__` returns a truthy value the exception is **suppressed**, *because* some\n  managers (`contextlib.suppress`) exist precisely to do that; everyone else must return `None` or `False`.\n- **Reverse unwinding.** Multiple managers in one statement exit in reverse order of entry, so\n  dependencies unwind correctly.\n\nWritten out, `with mgr as x: body` is approximately what PEP 343 specifies:\n\n```\nenter, exit = type(mgr).__enter__, type(mgr).__exit__   # looked up on the type, once\nx = enter(mgr)                    # if this raises, exit is never called\nok = True\ntry:\n    body\nexcept BaseException as e:\n    ok = False\n    if not exit(mgr, type(e), e, e.__traceback__):\n        raise                     # falsy return: propagate the original\nfinally:\n    if ok:\n        exit(mgr, None, None, None)   # normal exit, including return/break\n```\n\nThree consequences follow from that expansion:\n| Consequence | Why | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| `__exit__` runs only if `__enter__` succeeded | The protocol cannot know how far a failed setup got | A manager that acquires two things in `__enter__` must clean up the first itself if the second fails |\n| Interrupts trigger cleanup too | `except BaseException` catches `KeyboardInterrupt` and `CancelledError` | Rely on `with` for cleanup in cancellable agent code |\n| A raising `__exit__` replaces the original error | The original survives only as `__context__` in the traceback | A cleanup that can fail should log and swallow its own error rather than mask the real one |\n\n### async with\n- **Awaited entry and exit.** `async with` calls `await __aenter__()` and `await __aexit__(...)`,\n  *because* closing a network resource is itself I/O: flushing a stream, returning a connection, sending a\n  close frame, stopping a sandbox.\n- **Common examples:** `httpx.AsyncClient`, async DB sessions, MCP client sessions and `asyncio.Lock` are\n  all async context managers.\n- **Cancellation passes through.** It arrives as `CancelledError` and passes through `__aexit__` like any\n  exception, which is why `async with` is the reliable way to release resources when a user stops a run.\n- **Keep exit code short and bounded**, because it can itself be interrupted during shutdown.\n\n### contextlib: managers without classes\n- **`@contextmanager` turns a generator into a manager.** Code before `yield` is setup, the yielded value\n  is bound by `as`, code after is teardown.\n- **Teardown belongs in `finally`.** The exception from the block is thrown in at the `yield`, so teardown\n  must sit in `finally` or it is skipped on error.\n- **`@asynccontextmanager`** is the async version, and it is what FastAPI's `lifespan` expects.\n- **`ExitStack` and `AsyncExitStack`** manage a number of resources known only at runtime (the MCP servers\n  and tools this particular run needs) and unwind whatever was entered if a later setup step fails, which\n  is exactly the partial-setup problem from the expansion above.\n- **Smaller helpers:** `closing()` for objects with only `close()`, `suppress()`, `nullcontext()` for\n  optional managers, and `aclosing()` for async generators.\n- **Rules for generator managers.** A `@contextmanager` object is single-use (it wraps one generator, so\n  entering it twice raises), and its generator must yield exactly once.\n- **It doubles as a decorator:** `@timed_span()` above a function wraps every call in the block.\n\n### When there is no block to hang it on\n- **Some lifetimes do not fit a lexical block:** an object handed to a caller, a cache entry, a worker\n  that lives until shutdown, a long-lived agent session that spans many HTTP requests.\n- **Then the owner of that lifetime closes it explicitly:** the app's lifespan, the pool, the session\n  manager's idle timeout, or the cache's eviction hook.\n- **`weakref.finalize(obj, cleanup)` is a better last resort than `__del__`**, since it runs at most once\n  and also at interpreter exit. It still runs on garbage-collection timing, so treat it as leak insurance,\n  not as the cleanup plan.\n\n### Context managers that are not about I/O\n- **Mode switches:** `torch.no_grad()` and `torch.inference_mode()` switch autograd off for a block and\n  restore it after.\n- **Temporary state:** `decimal.localcontext()` and temporary environment patches in tests follow the same\n  pattern: change state, guarantee restoration.\n- **The principle generalises** to anything with a \"before\" and a mandatory \"after\", including a trace span\n  around an agent step.","example":"### Per-run resources for an agent with AsyncExitStack\nA run opens a sandbox and only the tool servers its task needs, decided at runtime. Whether the run\nfinishes, a tool fails or the user stops it, everything closes in reverse order. Resources are stubs.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import AsyncExitStack, asynccontextmanager\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def resource(kind: str, name: str):\n    print(f\"open  {kind}:{name}\")\n    try:\n        yield f\"{kind}:{name}\"\n    finally:\n        await asyncio.sleep(0)                       # async teardown: stop container, close session\n        print(f\"close {kind}:{name}\")\n\nasync def agent_run(task: str, servers: list[str], fail: bool = False) -> str:\n    async with AsyncExitStack() as stack:\n        await stack.enter_async_context(resource(\"span\", task))         # trace span for the run\n        sandbox = await stack.enter_async_context(resource(\"sandbox\", \"py\"))\n        sessions = [await stack.enter_async_context(resource(\"mcp\", s)) for s in servers]\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.05)                    # model and tool steps would run here\n        if fail:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"tool returned malformed output\")\n        return f\"done with {sandbox} and {len(sessions)} servers\"\n\nasync def main():\n    print(await agent_run(\"triage\", [\"github\", \"jira\"]))\n    try:\n        await agent_run(\"triage\", [\"github\"], fail=True)\n    except RuntimeError as e:\n        print(\"run failed:\", e)\n    t = asyncio.create_task(agent_run(\"long\", [\"github\", \"jira\"]))\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01); t.cancel()            # user pressed Stop\n    try:\n        await t\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print(\"run cancelled\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **Every path closes everything:** success, the raised tool error and the cancellation all print the same\n  `close` lines, newest first, so the MCP sessions close before the sandbox and the span ends last.\n- **The list of servers is data**, which is why `AsyncExitStack` beats nested `async with` here.\n\n### App lifespan with AsyncExitStack\nTwo process-scoped clients opened in a lifespan; a crash mid-request still closes both, in reverse order.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import AsyncExitStack, asynccontextmanager\n\nclass Client:                              # stands in for httpx.AsyncClient, a DB pool\n    def __init__(self, name: str): self.name = name\n    async def aclose(self): print(f\"{self.name}: closed\")\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def open_client(name: str):\n    client = Client(name)\n    print(f\"{name}: opened\")\n    try:\n        yield client\n    finally:\n        await client.aclose()              # runs on success, error or cancellation\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app=None):              # FastAPI calls lifespan(app) with this shape\n    async with AsyncExitStack() as stack:\n        llm = await stack.enter_async_context(open_client(\"llm-http\"))\n        db = await stack.enter_async_context(open_client(\"vector-db\"))\n        yield {\"llm\": llm, \"db\": db}\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    try:\n        async with lifespan() as deps:\n            print(\"serving with\", sorted(deps))\n            raise RuntimeError(\"crash mid-request\")\n    except RuntimeError as exc:\n        print(\"propagated:\", exc)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n**Output, in order:**\n1. `llm-http: opened`, `vector-db: opened`, `serving with ['db', 'llm']`.\n2. `vector-db: closed` then `llm-http: closed`, in reverse order.\n3. `propagated: crash mid-request`.\n\n- **The error is not suppressed**, both clients are closed, and the dependency opened last is closed first.\n- **The clients are created once per process**, not once per request or agent run, which is the point of\n  putting them in a lifespan.\n\n### Guarding partial setup, plus a timing span that is also a decorator\nA class that acquires two resources in `__enter__` uses an `ExitStack` so a failure on the second closes\nthe first, then hands the stack over with `pop_all()`. Runs on Python 3.9+.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom contextlib import ExitStack, contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef resource(name: str, fail: bool = False):\n    if fail:\n        raise ConnectionError(f\"{name}: connect failed\")\n    print(f\"{name}: opened\")\n    try:\n        yield name\n    finally:\n        print(f\"{name}: closed\")              # runs on success and on error\n\nclass Pipeline:                               # acquires two resources in __enter__\n    def __init__(self, fail_second: bool = False):\n        self.fail_second = fail_second\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        with ExitStack() as stack:\n            self.llm = stack.enter_context(resource(\"llm\"))\n            self.db = stack.enter_context(resource(\"db\", fail=self.fail_second))\n            self._stack = stack.pop_all()     # success: keep both open past this block\n        return self                           # failure: the with above closes llm\n\n    def __exit__(self, *exc):\n        return self._stack.__exit__(*exc)     # unwind in reverse order\n\n@contextmanager\ndef timed_span(name: str):\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n    try:\n        yield\n    finally:\n        print(f\"{name}: {(time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000:.0f} ms\")\n\n@timed_span(\"embed\")                          # also works as a decorator, fresh generator per call\ndef embed(texts: list[str]) -> int:\n    time.sleep(0.05)\n    return len(texts)\n\nwith Pipeline():\n    print(embed([\"a\", \"b\"]))\ntry:\n    with Pipeline(fail_second=True):\n        pass\nexcept ConnectionError as exc:\n    print(\"setup failed:\", exc)\n# llm: opened / db: opened / embed: 50 ms (about) / 2 / db: closed / llm: closed\n# llm: opened / llm: closed / setup failed: db: connect failed\n```\n\nWithout the `ExitStack` in `__enter__`, the failing run would leak `llm`, because `__exit__` never runs\nwhen `__enter__` raises.\n\n### Worked incident: a stream leaked on the error path\nIllustrative numbers for one agent worker using a shared `httpx.AsyncClient` (100 connections by default).\n\n| Step | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Code opens the provider stream, parses chunks, closes it at the end | Happy path releases the connection |\n| About 1% of responses contain malformed JSON (a bad tool-call payload) and the parser raises | The close line is skipped, the connection stays checked out |\n| At 10,000 streams an hour, about 100 leak per hour | The pool's 100 connections are gone in roughly an hour |\n| New requests wait for a free connection, then hit pool timeouts | Latency climbs while CPU is idle; restarts \"fix\" it for an hour |\n| Fix: iterate inside `async with client.stream(...)` | The connection returns on success, parse error and cancellation |","patterns":["**One exit stack per agent run.** Open run-scoped resources (sandbox, MCP sessions, browser, temp directory, trace span) through an `AsyncExitStack` owned by the run, so success, failure and Stop share one cleanup path. Monitor sandboxes and sessions still open after their run ended; the number should be zero.","**Process-scoped clients in a lifespan.** Open shared clients and pools in an `@asynccontextmanager` passed to FastAPI as `lifespan=`, with an `AsyncExitStack` when there are several. Monitor open connections and pool wait time per worker.","**Keep streams open exactly as long as they are consumed.** Put `async with client.stream(...)` inside the async generator you hand to `StreamingResponse`, so the stream closes when iteration ends or the client disconnects. Monitor open upstream connections after client disconnects.","**Make leaks loud in tests.** `ResourceWarning` is ignored by default, so run the suite with `-W error::ResourceWarning` (pytest accepts the same flag), or `python -X dev` to at least show them, and treat \"unclosed\" warnings as failures. Add error-injection tests that assert the pool is idle afterwards.","**Bound cleanup that does I/O.** Wrap awaited cleanup in `__aexit__` or `finally` with `asyncio.timeout(...)`, and log rather than raise if it fails, so a hung close (a sandbox that will not stop) neither stalls shutdown nor masks the real error. Monitor shutdown duration.","**Transactions as managers for memory writes.** Use the driver's transaction manager (for example `with Session(engine) as session, session.begin():` in SQLAlchemy) so commit happens on success and rollback on any exception. Monitor rollback rate and long-open transactions."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent run acquires a lot: a model stream, MCP server sessions, a code\n  sandbox or browser, a temp working directory, a trace span, a DB transaction for memory writes. Runs fail\n  and get cancelled often (bad tool output, user Stop, step limit), and every one of those paths must\n  release everything.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `httpx.AsyncClient` and provider stream helpers, the MCP Python SDK\n  client (its transports and sessions are async context managers), FastAPI `lifespan`, SQLAlchemy\n  sessions, tracing spans, `asyncio.timeout` and `asyncio.Lock`.\n- **What you control.** Which resources are process-scoped (clients, pools in a lifespan) versus run-scoped\n  (sandbox, MCP session, span), an `AsyncExitStack` for the tools a run turns out to need, and bounded,\n  non-masking cleanup.\n- **What you can skip.** The exact PEP 343 expansion beyond its three consequences, and writing class-based\n  managers when `@asynccontextmanager` will do.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **MCP sessions per agent run.** The MCP client's transports and sessions are async context managers, so\n  an `AsyncExitStack` closes every server a run opened.\n- **Code sandboxes and browsers.** A run-scoped context manager stops the container or browser even when\n  the run crashes or the user presses Stop.\n- **Process-scoped clients.** FastAPI `lifespan` opens model and HTTP clients once and closes them on\n  shutdown, instead of per request.\n- **Tracing and memory writes.** Spans and DB transactions close or roll back on every exit path, so traces\n  and agent memory never end up half-written.","mini_project":"**Leak-proof agent run with an exit stack (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** acquire several run-scoped resources and show they are all released on success, error and\n  cancellation.\n- **Steps:** write three `@asynccontextmanager` fakes (an MCP session, a sandbox, a trace span) that log\n  open and close; in an agent run, enter only the ones the plan needs via `AsyncExitStack`; run it three\n  ways: normal finish, a tool that raises, and a task cancelled mid-run; count open resources after each.\n- **Done when:** every scenario ends with zero open resources, the logs show reverse-order cleanup, and a\n  test fails if any close is skipped."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"Evaluate expr to a manager\"] --> EN[\"Call __enter__\"]\n    EN -->|\"raises\"| P0[\"Error propagates, no __exit__\"]\n    EN -->|\"returns value\"| B[\"Bind to x, run the block\"]\n    B -->|\"normal end, return or break\"| X1[\"__exit__(None, None, None)\"]\n    B -->|\"raises, incl. CancelledError\"| X2[\"__exit__(type, exc, tb)\"]\n    X2 --> Q{\"Truthy return value?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Exception suppressed\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| P1[\"Original exception re-raised\"]\n    X1 --> D[\"Continue after with\"]","caption":"The life of one `with` statement: `__exit__` runs on every path once `__enter__` has succeeded, and its\nreturn value decides whether an exception propagates."},"interview":[{"q":"What are the arguments and return value of __exit__, and why do they matter?","a":"- **Arguments:** `exc_type`, `exc` and `tb` describe the exception that left the block, or are all\n  `None` on a normal exit, so the manager can clean up differently (for example roll back instead of\n  commit).\n- **Return value:** truthy suppresses the exception, which is how `contextlib.suppress` works; anything\n  else must return `None` or `False`, or errors silently vanish."},{"q":"An agent run uses a code sandbox and a varying set of MCP servers. How do you guarantee cleanup?","a":"- **One `AsyncExitStack` per run.** Enter the span, the sandbox and each MCP session through it as the run\n  discovers what it needs, so the set can be decided at runtime.\n- **Every exit path unwinds it:** success, a tool error and cancellation from a user Stop all run the\n  stack's exits in reverse order, newest resource first.\n- **Bound and isolate cleanup:** wrap slow teardown in `asyncio.timeout`, log rather than raise, and\n  monitor resources still open after their run ended."},{"q":"Write a @contextmanager that always releases its resource. What is the common bug?","a":"- **Setup before `yield`, teardown in `finally`.** The block's exception is thrown in at the `yield`, so\n  teardown placed after it without `finally` is skipped on error.\n- **Yield exactly once**, and remember the object is single-use; for async resources use\n  `@asynccontextmanager` and await the close.\n- **Test the error path:** raise inside the block and assert the resource was released."},{"q":"An agent service's open connections climb slowly after every upstream outage. How do you find the leak?","a":"- **Suspect a setup-failure or error path.** A second resource acquired in `__enter__` without a guard\n  leaks the first when it fails, because `__exit__` never runs.\n- **Reproduce with error injection** while watching open connections, pool wait time and file\n  descriptors; enable `ResourceWarning` to see \"unclosed\" objects.\n- **Fix with `ExitStack` in `__enter__`** or by moving each resource into its own `with`."},{"q":"When should a resource not be scoped to a with block?","a":"- **When it must outlive the function**, such as an `httpx.AsyncClient` pool shared by all requests;\n  per-request `async with` gives correct cleanup but repeated TLS handshakes.\n- **Move it to the owner of its lifetime:** the app lifespan, the pool or the cache's eviction hook.\n- **Use `weakref.finalize(obj, cleanup)` only as leak insurance**, since it runs on garbage-collection\n  timing."},{"q":"Why is async with the reliable way to release resources in cancellable asyncio code?","a":"- **Cancellation is an exception.** `CancelledError` passes through `await __aexit__(...)` like any other\n  error, so the close runs when a timeout or disconnect cancels the task.\n- **Closing is I/O**, such as returning a connection or sending a close frame, so it must be awaited\n  rather than run from a finaliser.\n- **Keep exit short and bounded**, because shutdown can interrupt it too."}],"tradeoffs":["**Visible cleanup point vs tied lifetime.** `with` guarantees cleanup at a lexically visible point, but ties resource lifetime to a block. When a resource must outlive the function (a pool shared by all requests, an agent session spanning many turns), move it to an app-level lifespan or a session manager.","**Short vs easy to get wrong.** `@contextmanager` is far shorter than a class, but forgets cleanup on error if you omit `try/finally`. A class with `__exit__` is clearer when the manager carries state or is reused often.","**Dynamic sets vs readable control flow.** `ExitStack` handles dynamic resource sets, at the cost of less obvious control flow. For two or three fixed resources a single multi-item `with` statement is easier to read.","**Graceful degradation vs hidden bugs.** Suppressing exceptions in `__exit__` can turn a crash into graceful degradation, but hides real bugs unless the manager suppresses only specific, expected exception types."],"pitfalls":["**Creating an `httpx.AsyncClient` inside each request with `async with`.** Cleanup is correct but there is no connection reuse. Symptom: high latency from repeated TLS handshakes. Fix: one client per process, opened in the lifespan.","**Returning a lazy stream or generator from inside a `with` block.** The resource closes on return. Symptom: the consumer later fails with \"stream closed\" or reads nothing.","**Writing `@contextmanager` teardown after `yield` without `finally`.** It runs in tests on the happy path and silently does not run when the block raises. Fix: wrap the `yield` in `try/finally`.","**An `__exit__` that returns `True` by accident** (e.g. returning the result of a cleanup call). Symptom: exceptions vanish and callers see `None` results with no traceback. Fix: return `None` explicitly unless suppression is the manager's purpose.","**Relying on `__del__` to close connections.** Symptom: \"unclosed client session\" warnings and pool exhaustion that varies with garbage-collection timing.","**Acquiring a second resource in `__enter__` after the first without guarding it.** When the second fails, `__exit__` never runs, and the first leaks only on that rare setup-failure path. Symptom: slowly climbing open connections after upstream outages. Fix: an `ExitStack` in `__enter__`, as above.","**A cleanup step in `__exit__` or `finally` that can raise** (closing an already-broken stream). Symptom: the traceback reports the cleanup error and the real cause is buried under \"During handling of the above exception\". Fix: log and swallow the cleanup's own error so the original propagates."],"when":"- **Use for anything with an acquire-and-release pair:** clients and connection pools, streams, MCP\n  sessions, sandboxes, DB sessions and transactions, locks and semaphores, temp files and directories,\n  tracing spans, and temporary mode changes such as `torch.inference_mode()`.\n- **Prefer the library's own manager** over manual `close()` calls.\n- **Match scope to lifetime:** give long-lived resources a lifespan-scoped manager, per-run resources a\n  run-scoped exit stack, and short-lived ones a block-scoped manager.\n- **Measure:** watch open connections, pool wait time and file descriptors under load and after error\n  injection; if they climb after errors, a path is skipping cleanup.\n- **Interviews:** expect to explain `__exit__`'s arguments and return value and write a `@contextmanager`\n  with a correct `finally`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wraps every client, stream, session and lock in `with`/`async with`, and writes `@asynccontextmanager` helpers with cleanup in `finally`.","architect":"Defines resource lifetimes, process-scoped pools in a lifespan versus per-request sessions, so connection counts and shutdown behaviour are predictable.","fde":"Traces a customer's \"connection pool exhausted\" incident to a stream opened without a context manager on an error path, and fixes it under their load."},"check":{"q":"A helper does `with httpx.stream(\"POST\", url, json=body) as r: return r.iter_lines()`, and callers loop over\nthe result. It fails in production with errors about a closed stream. Why, and how should it be written?","a":"`return` leaves the `with` block, so `__exit__` runs immediately and closes the response before\nanyone iterates; `iter_lines()` is lazy, so the caller iterates a closed stream. The context manager must\nstay open for as long as the data is consumed. Either make the helper a generator that `yield`s lines\ninside the `with` block, so the stream closes when iteration ends, with consumers using `closing()` or\n`aclosing()` for early exit, or have the helper itself be a context manager that yields the response so\nthe caller controls its lifetime with `with`."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens if an `__exit__` method returns `True` while the block raised an exception?","options":["The exception is re-raised after cleanup completes","The exception is suppressed and execution continues after the `with` statement","Python raises a TypeError because `__exit__` must return None","The block is retried once before the exception propagates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A truthy return from `__exit__` tells the interpreter the exception was handled. That is how `contextlib.suppress` works, and why accidental truthy returns hide bugs."},{"q":"Why does an async HTTP client use `async with` rather than plain `with`?","options":["Because plain `with` cannot be used inside an `async def` function","Because `async with` runs the block in a separate thread","Because opening and closing it involve I/O that must be awaited without blocking the event loop","Because `async with` suppresses CancelledError during cleanup"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Releasing connections and closing streams involve network I/O, so `__aenter__`/`__aexit__` are coroutines. Ordinary `with` remains usable inside async code for purely synchronous resources."},{"q":"A `@contextmanager` function has setup, `yield conn`, then `conn.close()` with no try/finally. When does the connection leak?","options":["Never, because the decorator always runs code after the yield","Only when the generator is garbage-collected before the block starts","Only when the block returns early with `return`","When the block raises, because the exception is thrown in at the yield and skips the close"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The exception from the block is raised inside the generator at the `yield`, so code after it runs only if it is in `finally`. An early `return` exits normally and does run it."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/dependency-injection-and-interface-boundaries","sources":["Fowler, 2004 — Inversion of Control Containers and the Dependency Injection pattern","Cockburn, 2005 — Hexagonal Architecture (Ports and Adapters)","Official docs: typing.Protocol"],"why":"- **Agents are glue around external services.** Models, tools, vector stores and memory are slow,\n  expensive, non-deterministic and owned by someone else. The first version is always a module-level\n  `client = OpenAI()` plus a `client.chat.completions.create(...)` call inside whichever function needs\n  an answer.\n- **Testing breaks.** Every unit test of that function needs an API key and a network, costs money,\n  takes seconds and returns a different string each run, so the test either hits production or gets\n  skipped. For an agent that loops ten times, each run multiplies that cost and flakiness.\n- **Swapping and configuration break.** Evaluating Anthropic, a self-hosted vLLM endpoint or a cheaper\n  model means vendor shapes scattered through forty files. Reading `OPENAI_API_KEY` at import time makes\n  a script that only wanted a helper either crash or silently talk to the wrong environment.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** `unittest.mock.patch(\"app.summarize.client\")` breaks when a refactor moves the\n  module, because it couples to an internal path, not a contract. `if os.getenv(\"FAKE_LLM\")` puts test\n  branches in production. A global registry (`get_llm_client()`) hides the dependency one hop away:\n  nothing in the function signature says it needs a model.\n- **DI flips the direction.** The code that needs a completer is given one; the boundary names the need\n  in types your code owns; one composition root wires it. The cost is explicitness; the payoff is a\n  visible, testable dependency graph changeable in one place.","how":"### Move 1: name the dependency as an interface you own\n- **Declare a narrow port.** A `Protocol` with exactly the methods your agent calls:\n  `Completer.complete(system, user, *, max_tokens) -> Completion`, or a `Tool` with a name, a JSON\n  schema and `run(args)`.\n- **`typing.Protocol`, not an ABC,** *because* protocols are structural: the vendor adapter and the test fake\n  satisfy it by having the right method, without inheriting from your package, and mypy checks the\n  match statically.\n- **It lives next to the domain code,** so the dependency arrow points from the adapter toward your\n  code, never the other way round.\n\n### Move 2: translate at the edge\n- **The adapter returns your own `Completion` dataclass** (text, tool calls, token counts, finish\n  reason). If `openai.types.chat.ChatCompletion` objects flow through your services, the vendor is still a\n  dependency of everything that touches them, and a provider switch becomes a rewrite.\n- **The boundary is only as strong as the types that cross it.** Tool-call formats differ by provider,\n  so normalize them into one `ToolCall(name, args)` type in the adapter.\n- **One file per vendor imports the SDK.** It handles the SDK's exception types and maps status codes\n  into your error hierarchy: `openai.RateLimitError` becomes your `ProviderRateLimited`, so the upstream\n  retry policy is written once and works for every vendor.\n- **This is dependency inversion in practice:** high-level policy (the agent loop) and the low-level SDK\n  both depend on an abstraction the high-level side owns.\n\n### Move 3: inject at construction, wire at the root\n1. **Constructor injection** (`Summarizer(completer)`, `Agent(completer, tools, memory)`) is the Python\n   default *because* the dependency is in the signature: readers, type checkers and test authors all\n   see it.\n2. **The composition root** (`main()`, an app factory, a FastAPI lifespan) is the single place that reads\n   settings, instantiates real clients and connects them.\n3. **Tests and evals build the same graph with fakes** or with recorded responses.\n4. **Nothing else reads environment variables or creates network clients,** which removes import-time\n   side effects.\n\n### Not every dependency needs a class\n- **Inject a callable for a single operation:**\n  `def __init__(self, now: Callable[[], datetime] = datetime.now)` or\n  `embed: Callable[[list[str]], list[list[float]]]`. Still DI, *because* the caller decides what runs;\n  it is the smallest possible interface. Many agent tools are exactly this: a typed function.\n- **Promote to a Protocol** when a second method appears or the call needs named, typed options.\n- **Default-argument trap.** A default is evaluated once, when the `def` runs at import time, so\n  `completer: Completer = OpenAICompleter(OpenAI())` quietly reintroduces an import-time client. Defaults\n  are safe only for cheap, side-effect-free values such as `datetime.now` itself.\n\n### Sync or async is part of the contract\n| Mismatch | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Async service calls a sync adapter | event loop blocks on every network call |\n| Sync service handed an async adapter | gets an un-awaited coroutine; mypy flags it, the runtime only fails later (an `AttributeError` reading `.text` off the coroutine, plus a \"never awaited\" warning) |\n\n- `def complete(...)` returns a `Completion`; `async def complete(...)` returns a coroutine. They are\n  different interfaces, so decide at the port.\n- **Need both?** Define two ports (`Completer`, `AsyncCompleter`), not one that tries to be both. Agents\n  that run tools in parallel almost always want the async port.\n\n### Why Python rarely needs a container\n- **Java-style containers** reflect on constructors to auto-wire graphs.\n- **Python already has the tools:** first-class functions, closures, `functools.partial` and dataclasses\n  cover almost every wiring need in a dozen lines.\n- **FastAPI's `Depends`** is a request-scoped injector that handles per-request lifetime for you.\n- **Reach for a container** (`dependency-injector`, `punq`, `lagom`) only with dozens of services of mixed\n  lifetimes (singleton HTTP clients and loaded models vs per-request sessions) and a hand-written root\n  that has become the hardest file to read.\n\n### Where to draw boundaries in an agent\n- **At I/O and non-determinism:** LLM and embedding providers, vector stores, memory stores, tool\n  backends (HTTP, databases, filesystem, sandboxes), the clock, randomness.\n- **Not around pure logic:** a `PromptBuilder` that renders a dataclass into a string, or a function that\n  trims a transcript to a token budget, needs no interface, just a direct test.\n- **Working rule:** if you would replace it in a test, an eval run or at deployment time, it is a port;\n  otherwise it is just code.\n- **Keep ports narrow, widen deliberately** (add `stream()` when a use case streams). A port mirroring the\n  SDK's fifteen methods is the SDK with extra steps, and nobody can write a fake for it.\n\n### Lifetimes\n- **Shared, created once at the root:** an `httpx.AsyncClient` with its connection pool, a loaded\n  sentence-transformer, the model client.\n- **Per-request or per-run, created by the handler:** a bound logger with `request_id`, a DB session,\n  the agent's scratchpad memory for one conversation.\n- **Constructor injection makes sharing cheap,** because the same instance is simply passed to several\n  consumers.\n\n### Test doubles at the boundary\n- **Hand-written fake:** returns scripted answers (including scripted tool calls) and records calls, so\n  agent-logic tests are fast and deterministic.\n- **Contract test for the real adapter:** against the real API or a recorded response, so fake and\n  reality cannot drift unnoticed.\n- **Keep fakes dumb:** a fake that re-implements vendor logic will pass tests the real thing fails.","example":"### Port, adapter, fake and composition root\n`Summarizer` and the `Completer` protocol import nothing from any SDK, the vendor import is deferred into\nthe root, and the test needs no network.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Protocol\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Completion:\n    text: str\n    input_tokens: int\n    output_tokens: int\n\nclass Completer(Protocol):                       # the port: your vocabulary, your types\n    def complete(self, system: str, user: str, *, max_tokens: int) -> Completion: ...\n\nclass OpenAICompleter:                           # the adapter: the only file that imports the SDK\n    def __init__(self, client, model: str) -> None:\n        self._client, self._model = client, model\n    def complete(self, system, user, *, max_tokens):\n        r = self._client.chat.completions.create(\n            model=self._model, max_tokens=max_tokens,\n            messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": system}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user}])\n        return Completion(r.choices[0].message.content or \"\",\n                          r.usage.prompt_tokens, r.usage.completion_tokens)\n\nclass FakeCompleter:                             # test double: scripted answers, records calls\n    def __init__(self, answers: list[str]) -> None:\n        self.answers, self.calls = list(answers), []\n    def complete(self, system, user, *, max_tokens):\n        self.calls.append((system, user, max_tokens))\n        return Completion(self.answers.pop(0), 10, 5)\n\nclass Summarizer:                                # domain service: knows nothing about vendors\n    def __init__(self, completer: Completer, max_tokens: int = 200) -> None:\n        self._c, self._max = completer, max_tokens\n    def summarize(self, doc: str) -> str:\n        return self._c.complete(\"Summarize in two sentences.\", doc, max_tokens=self._max).text\n\ndef build_summarizer() -> Summarizer:            # composition root: the one place real things are chosen\n    from openai import OpenAI\n    return Summarizer(OpenAICompleter(OpenAI(), model=\"gpt-4o-mini\"))\n\ndef test_summarizer_passes_document_through():\n    fake = FakeCompleter([\"Short summary.\"])\n    assert Summarizer(fake).summarize(\"long text\") == \"Short summary.\"\n    assert fake.calls[0][1] == \"long text\" and fake.calls[0][2] == 200\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: testing a tool-calling loop with a scripted model\nThe loop depends on two ports: a model that returns either a tool call or a final answer, and a dict of\ntools. The test scripts a two-step run and checks routing, arguments and the step budget, with no\nnetwork. Standard library only.\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Callable, Protocol\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass ToolCall:\n    name: str\n    args: dict\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Step:                                      # your type: adapters translate vendor tool calls into it\n    text: str = \"\"\n    tool_call: ToolCall | None = None\n\nclass Model(Protocol):\n    def next_step(self, transcript: list[dict]) -> Step: ...\n\nclass Agent:\n    def __init__(self, model: Model, tools: dict[str, Callable[..., str]], max_steps: int = 8):\n        self._model, self._tools, self._max = model, tools, max_steps\n    def run(self, task: str) -> str:\n        transcript = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}]\n        for _ in range(self._max):\n            step = self._model.next_step(transcript)\n            if step.tool_call is None:\n                return step.text\n            result = self._tools[step.tool_call.name](**step.tool_call.args)\n            transcript.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"name\": step.tool_call.name, \"content\": result})\n        raise RuntimeError(\"step budget exhausted\")\n\n@dataclass\nclass ScriptedModel:                             # dumb fake: replays steps, records what it saw\n    steps: list[Step]\n    seen: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n    def next_step(self, transcript):\n        self.seen.append(len(transcript))\n        return self.steps.pop(0)\n\ncalls = []\ndef lookup_order(order_id: str) -> str:\n    calls.append(order_id)\n    return \"shipped\"\n\nmodel = ScriptedModel([Step(tool_call=ToolCall(\"lookup_order\", {\"order_id\": \"A17\"})),\n                       Step(text=\"Your order A17 has shipped.\")])\nassert Agent(model, {\"lookup_order\": lookup_order}).run(\"Where is A17?\") == \"Your order A17 has shipped.\"\nassert calls == [\"A17\"] and model.seen == [1, 2]   # the tool result reached the second model call\n```\n\n### Injecting the clock and translating errors at the edge\nA rate-limited retry wrapper that depends only on your exception and an injected `sleep`, so the test\nruns instantly and works for every vendor adapter. Runnable with the standard library alone.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Callable\n\nclass ProviderRateLimited(Exception):            # your error type, raised by every adapter\n    pass\n\nclass RetryingCompleter:\n    def __init__(self, inner, sleep: Callable[[float], None], attempts: int = 3) -> None:\n        self._inner, self._sleep, self._attempts = inner, sleep, attempts\n    def complete(self, system, user, *, max_tokens):\n        for i in range(self._attempts):\n            try:\n                return self._inner.complete(system, user, max_tokens=max_tokens)\n            except ProviderRateLimited:\n                if i == self._attempts - 1:\n                    raise\n                self._sleep(2 ** i)               # 1s, 2s ... in production; recorded in tests\n\nclass FlakyFake:\n    def __init__(self, failures: int) -> None:\n        self.failures = failures\n    def complete(self, system, user, *, max_tokens):\n        if self.failures:\n            self.failures -= 1\n            raise ProviderRateLimited()\n        return \"ok\"\n\nslept: list[float] = []\nc = RetryingCompleter(FlakyFake(failures=2), sleep=slept.append)\nassert c.complete(\"s\", \"u\", max_tokens=10) == \"ok\" and slept == [1, 2]\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: adding a provider to a live agent, before and after\nA team must route one agent's planning step to a self-hosted vLLM endpoint while tool calls stay on a\nhosted model.\n\n| Measure | Module-level client | Port + adapters |\n|---|---|---|\n| Files touched | every file that calls `client.chat.completions.create` | one new adapter + the root |\n| Unit tests needing a key | all of them | none (fakes) |\n| Retry logic | per call site, per vendor exception | once, against `ProviderRateLimited` |\n| Tool-call parsing | per call site, per vendor format | once per adapter, into `ToolCall` |\n| Proof the adapter works | manual | one contract test per adapter |","patterns":["**One composition root per entry point.** Build the agent graph in `main()`, an app factory or a FastAPI lifespan, and store shared clients there; handlers receive them via `Depends`. Monitor startup time and that no module creates clients at import (a CLI `--help` should not need credentials).","**Provider and model selected by config, at the root only.** Read a setting such as the provider name once and construct the matching adapter; the agent loop never branches on it. Watch per-provider error rates, latency and tokens per run, tagged by adapter.","**Error translation in every adapter.** Map each SDK's rate-limit, timeout and auth exceptions to your own hierarchy so retries, fallbacks and alerts are written once. Monitor counts per translated error type.","**Scripted-model tests for agent control flow.** Replay a fixed sequence of tool calls and answers to test routing, step budgets, error handling and stop conditions in CI on every commit; keep real-model evals for answer quality. Track how long the unit suite takes; it should stay seconds, not minutes.","**Contract tests per adapter.** Run a small suite against the real API (nightly or behind a marker) or replay recorded responses, and assert the adapter returns a valid `Completion`. Alert when it fails, since that is the drift your fakes cannot see.","**Shared resources injected, never re-created.** One `httpx.AsyncClient` and one loaded embedding model per process, passed to every consumer, including tools. Monitor open connections and process memory."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent is a loop around the slowest, most expensive and least\n  deterministic dependency you have: the model. Without injection, every test of planning, tool routing\n  or stop conditions needs an API key, costs money and returns different text each run.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangChain's chat-model interface and LangGraph nodes are already\n  ports you call through; FastAPI `Depends` and a lifespan wire agent services; an OpenTelemetry\n  tracer or a LangSmith client can be injected like any other collaborator. Your own `Completer`, `Tool` and\n  `MemoryStore` protocols sit on top so business logic does not import any of them.\n- **What you control.** Which ports exist (model, tools, retriever, memory, clock), what types cross\n  them (your `Completion` and tool-call dataclasses, not vendor objects), and the one composition root\n  that picks real implementations per environment.\n- **What you can skip.** DI containers and their decorators. A hand-written root plus constructor\n  injection covers almost every agent service.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Deterministic agent-loop tests.** A scripted fake `Completer` replays tool calls in order, so planning,\n  routing and stop conditions are tested in CI with no API key and no flaky text.\n- **Swapping model providers.** One `Completer` port with an adapter per vendor lets a team move a step to a\n  cheaper or self-hosted model by changing the composition root, not the agent logic.\n- **Per-environment tool wiring.** The same agent runs with sandboxed or read-only tools in staging and real\n  ticketing or payment tools in production, chosen in one place.\n- **Replay and eval harnesses.** Injecting a recorded-response model and a fixed clock lets the eval suite\n  re-run yesterday's production traces against a new prompt.","mini_project":"**Make a tool-calling agent testable without a model (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove the agent loop's logic is correct using only injected fakes.\n- **Steps:** define `Completer`, `Tool` and `Clock` protocols and your own `Completion` and tool-call\n  dataclasses; write a small loop that calls the model, runs requested tools and stops on a final answer or\n  a step cap; build a composition root with a real provider adapter and a scripted fake that returns a\n  fixed list of tool calls; write pytest cases for \"calls the right tool\", \"stops at the step cap\" and\n  \"surfaces a tool error back to the model\".\n- **Done when:** the tests pass offline in under a second, the loop module imports no vendor SDK, and\n  switching to the real adapter needs a change only in the composition root."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Settings and env vars\"] --> R[\"Composition root\"]\n    R -->|\"builds\"| A1[\"OpenAICompleter adapter\"]\n    R -->|\"builds\"| A2[\"vLLM or Anthropic adapter\"]\n    R -->|\"builds\"| T[\"Tool adapters\"]\n    R -->|\"injects via constructor\"| D[\"Agent loop\"]\n    D -->|\"calls complete, gets Completion\"| P[\"Completer Protocol\"]\n    D -->|\"calls run\"| TP[\"Tool Protocol\"]\n    A1 -->|\"implements\"| P\n    A2 -->|\"implements\"| P\n    T -->|\"implements\"| TP\n    F[\"Scripted fakes in tests\"] -->|\"implements\"| P\n    A1 -.->|\"only file importing the SDK\"| V[\"Vendor SDK and network\"]","caption":"The composition root (`main()`, an app factory or a FastAPI lifespan) is the only place that reads settings\nand builds real clients; every adapter's arrow points toward the ports your agent owns, and tests plug\nfakes into the same ports."},"interview":[{"q":"What is dependency injection, and why does it matter more in agents than in typical CRUD code?","a":"- **Definition:** a component receives its collaborators from outside (usually through its constructor)\n  instead of creating or importing them, so the caller decides what actually runs.\n- **Why it matters here:** an agent loops over LLM providers, tools, vector stores and memory that are\n  slow, paid, non-deterministic and third-party, so unit tests need a fake and deployments need to swap\n  providers.\n- **Result:** agent logic runs in a test with no network and no API key, and a provider switch is one\n  new adapter plus a change in the composition root."},{"q":"How would you unit-test an agent's tool-routing and stop logic without calling a model?","a":"- **Inject the model as a port** that returns your own `Step` type: either a tool call or a final answer.\n- **Script a fake** that replays a fixed sequence (call `lookup_order`, then answer) and records the\n  transcript length it saw, so you can assert the tool result was fed back.\n- **Cover the edges:** unknown tool names, a tool raising, and the step budget being exhausted; leave\n  answer quality to real-model evals."},{"q":"Your test suite fails at import with a missing `OPENAI_API_KEY`. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Find the import-time side effect:** look for a module-level `client = OpenAI()`, a settings object read\n  at import, or a default argument such as `completer: Completer = OpenAICompleter(OpenAI())`.\n- **Move construction to the root:** build clients in `main()`, an app factory or a FastAPI lifespan and pass\n  them into constructors.\n- **Verify:** importing every module, or running a CLI `--help`, should succeed with no credentials set."},{"q":"Why use `typing.Protocol` for a port instead of an ABC, and when would you widen the port?","a":"- **Structural typing:** an adapter or a fake satisfies a Protocol by having the right methods, checked\n  statically by mypy, without inheriting from your package.\n- **Narrow by default:** expose only the methods the use case calls, so a fake is a few lines.\n- **Widen on purpose:** add `stream()` or tool-call support when a use case needs it, accepting that the\n  port is no longer lowest-common-denominator."},{"q":"When would you not introduce an interface boundary?","a":"- **Pure logic:** a prompt renderer or a token-budget calculator has no I/O, so test it directly.\n- **Small scripts:** a 200-line script with one call site gains nothing from a port and an adapter.\n- **Single-method needs:** inject a callable such as `now: Callable[[], datetime]` rather than a full\n  Protocol class."},{"q":"How do you make sure a fake does not drift from the real provider adapter in a production agent?","a":"- **Keep fakes dumb:** scripted answers and recorded calls, never logic that parses the prompt.\n- **Add a contract test per adapter:** run it against the real API nightly or behind a marker, or replay\n  recorded responses, and assert it returns a valid `Completion` with correctly parsed tool calls.\n- **Alert on failure,** because a failing contract test is exactly the drift green unit tests cannot see."}],"tradeoffs":["**Testability and swappability vs an extra file and hop per boundary.** Flips for a 200-line script with one call site, where calling the SDK directly is right.","**Vendor independence vs provider features.** A lowest-common-denominator port hides tool-call formats, prompt caching controls and logprobs; when you deliberately depend on those, widen the port on purpose rather than pretending neutrality.","**Your ports vs a framework's.** LangChain or LangGraph interfaces already abstract providers; wrapping them again buys isolation from framework upgrades at the cost of one more layer. Worth it when the framework is likely to change under you; skip it for a prototype.","**Fast, deterministic fakes vs drift.** A fake can drift from the real adapter, so keep a thin integration or contract test against the real API or a recorded response.","**Explicit constructors vs long signatures.** A class taking eight collaborators does too much; split it or group related settings, rather than blaming injection.","**Container vs hand-written root.** A container automates wiring for a large graph with mixed lifetimes but hides it behind decorators and configuration; below a few dozen services the hand-written root is clearer."],"pitfalls":["**Clients created at import time** (`client = OpenAI()` at the top of a file). Symptom: tests fail on import with a missing API key, or a CLI `--help` takes seconds and needs production credentials. Fix: build clients in the root.","**Vendor objects in agent code.** Symptom: `response.choices[0].message.content` in business logic and a provider switch touching dozens of files. Fix: translate to your dataclass in the adapter.","**Patch-heavy tests** (`@patch(\"app.services.summarize.openai.OpenAI\")` stacked three deep). Symptom: thirty tests break when a module is renamed, each re-implementing a slice of the vendor response. Fix: inject a fake.","**A port mirroring the SDK surface with one implementation.** Symptom: nobody can write a fake, so tests mock the port's internals. Fix: narrow it to what the use case calls.","**Clever fakes** that parse the prompt and generate plausible answers. Symptom: green tests for behaviour the real provider does not exhibit. Fix: scripted answers only.","**A sync port in an async service** (or an adapter wrapping an async SDK in `asyncio.run`). Symptom: request latency that grows with concurrency because each LLM call blocks the event loop, or `RuntimeError: asyncio.run() cannot be called from a running event loop`. Fix: an `AsyncCompleter` port.","**Threading config values** (`api_key`, `model`, `temperature`) through five layers. Symptom: signatures change whenever a setting is added. Fix: inject the configured adapter instead."],"when":"- **Use when:** agent code meets something slow, paid, non-deterministic or third-party: model providers,\n  embedding services, vector stores, memory stores, tool backends, databases, HTTP, time and randomness.\n- **Avoid when:** the logic is pure; test it directly.\n- **Measure:** can the agent loop run in a test with no network and no credentials, and how many files\n  would a provider switch touch?\n- **Day to day:** the adapter you write when adding a provider or tool, the fake you hand to a test, the\n  one root you edit when configuration changes.\n- **Interviews:** explain why Protocols beat ABCs here, why the boundary's types must be yours, and why a\n  composition root exists at all.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `Completer` and `VectorStore` protocols, one adapter per provider, dumb fakes for tests, and keeps the SDK import inside the adapter and the composition root.","architect":"Owns where boundaries sit and what crosses them: which provider features are exposed through the port, how adapters report errors, and which objects are shared versus per-request.","fde":"Swaps a customer's mandated provider, on-prem endpoint or internal vector database in by writing one adapter against the existing port, without touching the delivered business logic."},"check":{"q":"A service class imports both `openai` and `anthropic` and switches between them on a config\nstring inside its methods. The team says this is already \"provider-agnostic\". What is wrong, and how\nwould you restructure it?","a":"The service still depends on both SDKs: any test imports both, any new provider edits the\nservice, and vendor response objects are handled inline in domain code. Provider choice belongs in the\ncomposition root, not in the service. Define a `Completer` protocol in your own types, write one\nadapter per vendor that imports its SDK and translates responses into your `Completion` dataclass,\nand have the service take a `Completer` in its constructor. The root reads the config string once and\nconstructs the matching adapter. Tests pass a fake; adding a vendor adds one file; the service never\nchanges. A strong answer also mentions keeping the port narrow and a contract test per adapter so the\nfakes cannot drift from reality."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is `typing.Protocol` usually preferred over an abstract base class for a port such as `Completer`?","options":["Protocols are checked at runtime, so a bad adapter fails at construction time","Protocols are structural, so vendor adapters and test fakes satisfy them without inheriting from your package","Abstract base classes cannot have more than one concrete implementation","Protocols are faster because they skip method resolution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A class satisfies a Protocol by having the right methods, checked statically by mypy, so adapters stay decoupled from your code. Protocols are not enforced at runtime (`@runtime_checkable` only checks that method names exist, not signatures), and ABCs support many implementations; the difference is nominal versus structural typing."},{"q":"Which of these should NOT get its own interface boundary in a typical RAG service?","options":["The vector store client used for retrieval","The system clock used by a rate limiter","A pure function that renders a dataclass into a prompt string","The embedding model that turns chunks into vectors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Boundaries belong at I/O and non-determinism, where you want to substitute a fake in tests or a different implementation at deployment. Pure, deterministic logic is tested directly; wrapping it in a Protocol adds indirection with nothing to swap."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of a composition root?","options":["To cache expensive objects so they are constructed only once per process","To validate configuration before the application starts","To be the single place that chooses concrete implementations and wires them into the object graph","To provide a global registry that any module can query for a dependency"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The root is where real adapters are instantiated and handed to the services that need them, so nothing else reads settings or builds clients. A queryable global registry is the service-locator anti-pattern, which hides dependencies instead of making them explicit."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/package-structure-and-import-design","sources":["Official docs: Python import system (Python Language Reference)","PEP 420 — Implicit Namespace Packages","PEP 562 — Module __getattr__ and __dir__","Official docs: Python Packaging User Guide (src layout vs flat layout)"],"why":"- **Notebooks grow into tangles.** One agent notebook becomes a `utils.py`, then forty modules where\n  everything imports everything. Three failures follow, one per bullet below.\n- **Circular imports.** `agent.py` imports `tools.py`, which imports `agent.py` for a type, until Python\n  raises `ImportError: cannot import name 'Agent' from partially initialized module`.\n- **Slow, fragile startup.** Importing the package pulls in `torch`, `transformers` and three vendor SDKs,\n  so a CLI that only lists prompts takes seconds. A module-level `client = OpenAI()` fails at import when\n  `OPENAI_API_KEY` is missing, which also breaks test collection.\n- **Tests pass for the wrong reason.** With a flat layout, `pytest` imports the package from the working\n  directory, not from what `pip install` would ship, so a missing wheel file or wrong package-data\n  declaration stays invisible until production.\n- **Naive fixes hide the cause.** `sys.path.insert(0, \"..\")` or `PYTHONPATH=.` makes imports depend on\n  where the process started, so they differ in CI, Docker and a teammate's laptop. `try/except ImportError`\n  hides a cycle until another import order triggers it. \"Just be careful\" does not scale because nothing\n  enforces it: design the structure, give it a direction and check it with a tool, the same way you would\n  design service boundaries.","how":"### What an import actually does\n1. `import pkg.mod` looks in `sys.modules`.\n2. If absent, it walks `sys.path` for `pkg` and creates the module object.\n3. It **inserts the module into `sys.modules` before executing its body**.\n4. It runs the module top to bottom.\n\nTwo consequences drive every rule below:\n- **The body runs once per process, at first import.** Anything at top level (network clients, file\n  reads, model loading, `logging.basicConfig`) is an import-time side effect.\n- **A cycle does not loop forever.** The half-built module is already registered, so the second module\n  gets the partially initialized first one, and `from a import X` fails if `X` is not defined yet.\n\n### `import a` vs `from a import X`\n| Form | Binds | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `import a` | the module object | a cycle survives if `a.X` is only looked up later, at call time |\n| `from a import X` | a new name `X` in the importer, bound to whatever `a.X` is *now* | fails mid-cycle |\n\n- **Patch where the name is used.** `mock.patch(\"a.X\")` does not affect a module `b` that already did\n  `from a import X`, because `b` holds its own reference; patch `b.X`.\n\n### Where `sys.path[0]` comes from\n- **`python path/to/script.py`** puts the script's own directory first on `sys.path`.\n- **`python -m pkg.mod`** puts the current directory there and imports `pkg.mod` as part of its package.\n- **So `python src/myapp/entrypoints/cli.py` breaks things:** `entrypoints/` tops the search path, siblings\n  become top-level modules, relative imports fail with \"attempted relative import with no known parent\n  package\", and a sibling named like a library shadows it.\n- **Fix:** run `python -m myapp.entrypoints.cli`, or declare a console script under `[project.scripts]` in\n  `pyproject.toml`, *because* both resolve imports through the installed package rather than wherever the\n  file sits.\n\n### src layout\n- **Shape:** code in `src/myapp/`, `tests/` beside `src/`, installed editable (`uv sync`, or\n  `pip install -e .`).\n- **Why it helps:** the project root has no importable `myapp` directory, so tests can only import through\n  the installation. Undeclared dependencies and modules not really in the package show up instead of being\n  silently picked up from the working directory.\n- **Caveat:** an editable install still points back at `src/`. Files on disk but missing from the wheel\n  (prompt templates, package data) are caught only when CI builds the wheel and tests a non-editable\n  install of it.\n- **Flat layout** (`myapp/` at the root) is fine for scripts and small tools, but it lets the current\n  directory shadow the installed package.\n\n### Layering and direction\nAllow imports only downward:\n\n| Layer | Contains | May import |\n|---|---|---|\n| `domain/` | pure types and logic (Pydantic models, prompt templates, scoring) | nothing from the app |\n| `services/` | orchestration (RAG pipeline, agent loop) | domain + *interfaces* (Protocols) for LLMs, vector stores, tools |\n| `adapters/` | concrete clients (OpenAI, Anthropic, pgvector, Redis) | implement those Protocols |\n| `entrypoints/` | FastAPI app, CLI, workers | the composition root that wires adapters into services |\n\n- **Why this direction:** the parts that change most (vendor SDKs) should be the parts nothing else\n  depends on.\n- **Enforce it:** `import-linter` turns this into a CI check with \"layers\" and \"forbidden\" contracts, so a\n  stray `from myapp.adapters.openai import ...` inside `domain/` fails the build.\n\n### Breaking cycles properly\n- **A cycle is a design signal:** two modules share a concept that belongs in a third. Move the shared\n  types down into `domain/`.\n- **Type-only import?** Guard it with `if TYPE_CHECKING:` and use string or postponed annotations,\n  because type checkers read it but the runtime never executes it.\n- **Function-local imports** also break cycles; use them deliberately, not as a habit.\n\n### Public surface and lazy imports\n- **`__init__.py` defines what callers see.** Re-export a small API (`from .client import Client`, plus\n  `__all__`) to reorganize internals freely. Re-exporting everything makes `import myapp` load everything.\n- **Defer heavy optional dependencies:** import inside the function that needs them, or use a module-level\n  `__getattr__` (PEP 562) to load on first attribute access.\n- **Measure:** `python -X importtime -c \"import myapp\"` prints per-module import cost, so you see what to\n  defer.\n\n### Regular vs namespace packages\n- **Regular package:** a directory with `__init__.py`.\n- **Namespace package (PEP 420):** a directory *without* one is still importable. It exists so several\n  separately installed distributions can contribute subpackages to one name (`acme.llm` and `acme.rag`\n  shipped independently). Use it on purpose only.\n- **Accidental namespace package** (a forgotten `__init__.py`) usually imports in development, but\n  setuptools' `find_packages()` skips it, so the subpackage can silently be left out of the wheel, and some\n  tools resolve its module names differently.\n- **Rule:** in a single application package, give every package directory an `__init__.py`.\n\n### Absolute vs relative imports\n- **Absolute** (`from myapp.domain import Query`): explicit, survives copying a module elsewhere.\n- **Explicit relative** (`from .models import Query`): shorter, survives renaming the top package.\n- **Either is fine:** pick one and let `ruff` enforce it. Implicit relative imports do not exist in\n  Python 3.","example":"### Layered layout with a type-only import and a lazy adapter\nThe `TYPE_CHECKING` import removes a runtime cycle between the agent and its tool registry; the embedder\ndefers `torch` until the first call, so importing the package (or collecting tests) stays fast and\nside-effect free.\n\n```python\n# src/myapp/\n#   domain/models.py        <- pure Pydantic models, no app imports\n#   services/agent.py       <- depends on domain + Protocols\n#   adapters/embeddings.py  <- imports torch / sentence-transformers lazily\n#   entrypoints/cli.py      <- composition root\n\n# services/agent.py\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING, Protocol\nfrom myapp.domain.models import Message\n\nif TYPE_CHECKING:                      # seen by mypy/pyright, never executed at runtime\n    from myapp.services.tools import ToolRegistry\n\nclass LLM(Protocol):\n    def complete(self, messages: list[Message]) -> Message: ...\n\nclass Agent:\n    def __init__(self, llm: LLM, tools: ToolRegistry) -> None:\n        self.llm, self.tools = llm, tools\n\n# adapters/embeddings.py\nclass LocalEmbedder:\n    def __init__(self, model_name: str) -> None:\n        self.model_name, self._model = model_name, None   # nothing heavy at import\n\n    def embed(self, texts: list[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        if self._model is None:\n            from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer  # pays torch import on first use\n            self._model = SentenceTransformer(self.model_name)\n        return self._model.encode(texts).tolist()\n\n# Measure: python -X importtime -c \"import myapp.entrypoints.cli\" 2> imports.log\n```\n\n### Reproduce a partial-init cycle and a wrong-place patch\nStandard library only. It writes four tiny modules to a temp directory and shows both failure modes.\n\n```python\nimport sys, tempfile, textwrap, pathlib\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nroot = pathlib.Path(tempfile.mkdtemp())\ndef write(name, src):\n    (root / name).write_text(textwrap.dedent(src))\n\nwrite(\"a.py\", \"\"\"\n    from b import helper          # runs b before X exists\n    X = 1\n\"\"\")\nwrite(\"b.py\", \"\"\"\n    from a import X               # a is in sys.modules but half-built\n    def helper(): return X\n\"\"\")\nwrite(\"llm.py\", \"def call(): return 'real network call'\\n\")\nwrite(\"rag.py\", \"\"\"\n    from llm import call          # copies the reference into rag's namespace\n    def answer(): return call()\n\"\"\")\nsys.path.insert(0, str(root))     # demo only: never do this in project code\n\ntry:\n    import a\nexcept ImportError as e:\n    print(\"cycle:\", e)            # cannot import name 'X' from partially initialized module 'a'\n\nimport rag\nwith mock.patch(\"llm.call\", return_value=\"fake\"):\n    print(rag.answer())           # real network call: patched where defined\nwith mock.patch(\"rag.call\", return_value=\"fake\"):\n    print(rag.answer())           # fake: patched where it is looked up\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: an agent worker whose cold start hid in imports\nAn autoscaled queue worker runs a tool-calling agent. New pods took too long to pick up jobs during a\ntraffic spike. The team captured `python -X importtime` and traced the cost (figures are illustrative,\nfrom one team's profile, not a benchmark).\n\n| Finding | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Most startup spent importing `torch` | `myapp/__init__.py` re-exported the local embedder used by one tool | minimal `__init__.py`, lazy import inside the embedder |\n| Every vendor SDK imported at boot | tool registry module imported all adapters at top level | registry maps tool names to adapter factories, built in the composition root |\n| Tests failed without `OPENAI_API_KEY` | module-level `client = OpenAI()` in the planner | client built in the root and injected into the agent |\n| Prompt templates missing in prod | flat layout, templates never declared as package data | src layout plus a CI job that tests the built wheel |\n\nAfter the change, `import myapp.entrypoints.worker` loads only the agent loop and the adapters that\nworker's tools need, and unit tests run the agent with a scripted fake model.\n\n### Layer contract as a CI check\nAn `import-linter` contract in `pyproject.toml`; `lint-imports` fails the build when a lower layer imports a\nhigher one. Layers are listed from highest to lowest; forbidding third-party packages such as `openai`\nalso needs `include_external_packages = true`, because import-linter ignores external imports by default.\n\n```toml\n[tool.importlinter]\nroot_package = \"myapp\"\ninclude_external_packages = true   # required for the forbidden contract on openai, anthropic, torch\n\n[[tool.importlinter.contracts]]\nname = \"Layered architecture\"\ntype = \"layers\"\nlayers = [\n    \"myapp.entrypoints\",\n    \"myapp.adapters\",\n    \"myapp.services\",\n    \"myapp.domain\",\n]\n\n[[tool.importlinter.contracts]]\nname = \"Domain and services never import vendor SDKs\"\ntype = \"forbidden\"\nsource_modules = [\"myapp.domain\", \"myapp.services\"]\nforbidden_modules = [\"openai\", \"anthropic\", \"torch\"]\n```","patterns":["**Tools as adapters behind a registry.** Keep each tool's backend client in `adapters/`, expose it to the agent through a Protocol or a typed function, and register tools in the composition root. The agent loop then imports no SDK, and adding a tool touches one adapter file and the root. Monitor which tools each worker actually loads at boot.","**Prompts and tool schemas as package data.** Store prompt templates and JSON tool schemas inside `src/myapp/` and load them with `importlib.resources`, so they ship in the wheel and version with the code. Check for them in the wheel-install CI job.","**Test the built wheel, not the checkout.** In CI, build the wheel (`uv build` or `python -m build`), install it into a fresh environment without `-e`, and run the test suite against it. Catches missing package data and undeclared dependencies; monitor CI for `ModuleNotFoundError` on this job only.","**Import contracts in CI.** Run `lint-imports` with layers and forbidden contracts like the one above on every pull request, so direction is enforced by a tool, not by review. Track contract violations as build failures.","**Import-time budget for cold-start paths.** For CLIs, serverless handlers and autoscaled workers, capture `python -X importtime -c \"import myapp.entrypoints.cli\"` in CI and review the slowest modules; defer them with function-local imports or PEP 562 `__getattr__`. Monitor cold-start latency.","**Console scripts, never file paths.** Declare entrypoints under `[project.scripts]` and run workers with `python -m` in Dockerfiles and process managers, so `sys.path` is the same everywhere.","**Eager imports at server boot.** For long-running API servers, import adapters during startup (the lifespan or app factory) so a missing optional dependency fails the deploy health check instead of the first user request."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent code has natural cycles: the agent needs its tools, tools want the\n  agent's types, and both want the LLM client. Without a direction, you get partial-init `ImportError`s\n  and an agent package that imports `torch` and three vendor SDKs just to list its tools.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangChain ships provider integrations as separate packages\n  (`langchain-openai` and similar) so you import only what you use; your repo should follow the same\n  idea, with LangGraph graphs and agent loops in `services/`, tool and model clients in `adapters/`, and\n  the FastAPI app or queue worker in `entrypoints/`.\n- **What you control.** The layer contract, where tool registries and prompt templates live, which\n  imports are lazy on cold-start paths (serverless tools, autoscaled agent workers), and whether CI tests\n  the built wheel so prompt files actually ship.\n- **What you can skip.** Namespace packages and multi-distribution monorepos, until several teams ship\n  agent components independently.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agent services that grow tools fast.** A clear `services/`, `adapters/`, `entrypoints/` direction keeps\n  new tools from importing the agent loop and creating cycles.\n- **Cold-start-sensitive tool workers.** Lazy imports of heavy libraries keep a serverless tool or an\n  autoscaled agent worker from loading model libraries just to list its tools.\n- **Shipping prompts and tool schemas as package data.** Prompt templates and JSON schemas inside the\n  package, tested from the built wheel, stop \"works locally, missing in the image\" failures.\n- **Shared agent components across teams.** A stable public import surface lets several agents reuse the\n  same tool library without depending on its internals.","mini_project":"**Restructure a flat agent script into a layered package (1-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn a single-file agent with two tools into a package with a one-way import direction.\n- **Steps:** move the loop into `services/`, the model client and tools into `adapters/`, and a CLI or\n  FastAPI app into `entrypoints/`; put prompt templates in package data; make any heavy import lazy; add\n  an import-direction check (a linter rule or a small test that inspects imports); build the wheel and run\n  one test against the installed wheel, not the source tree.\n- **Done when:** there are no import cycles, importing the tool registry does not load the heavy library,\n  and the wheel test finds the prompt files."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"import pkg.mod\"] --> C{\"Already in sys.modules?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| U[\"Return cached module, body not re-run\"]\n    C -->|\"no\"| F[\"Search sys.path for pkg\"]\n    F -->|\"not found\"| E[\"ModuleNotFoundError\"]\n    F -->|\"found\"| R[\"Create module object, insert into sys.modules\"]\n    R --> B[\"Execute module body top to bottom\"]\n    B -->|\"body imports a module that imports pkg.mod back\"| P[\"Cycle gets the partially initialized module\"]\n    P -->|\"from pkg.mod import X before X is defined\"| X[\"ImportError: partially initialized module\"]\n    B -->|\"body finishes\"| D[\"Module fully initialized, names bound\"]","caption":"Follow one `import` statement: the module is registered in `sys.modules` before its body runs, which is\nwhy a cycle hands the second importer a half-built module instead of looping forever."},"interview":[{"q":"What does a src layout buy you over a flat layout, and what does it not catch on its own?","a":"- **What it buys:** the project root has no importable `myapp` directory, so tests import through the\n  installation and undeclared dependencies or stray modules surface instead of being picked up from the\n  working directory.\n- **What it misses:** an editable install still points back at `src/`, so files missing from the wheel\n  (prompt templates, package data) only show up when CI builds the wheel and tests a non-editable install."},{"q":"A teammate hits `ImportError: cannot import name 'Agent' from partially initialized module`. Walk me through the fix.","a":"- **Explain the cause:** Python inserts a module into `sys.modules` before running its body, so the cyclic\n  import gets a half-built module and `from a import X` fails if `X` is not defined yet.\n- **Find the shared concept:** if both modules need the same types, move them down into `domain/`.\n- **Type-only edge:** guard it with `if TYPE_CHECKING:` plus postponed annotations; avoid\n  `try/except ImportError`, which hides the cycle until another import order triggers it."},{"q":"How would you enforce a layered dependency direction across a team of twenty engineers?","a":"- **Write the rule down as a contract:** `import-linter` with a \"layers\" contract (entrypoints, adapters,\n  services, domain) and a \"forbidden\" contract keeping `openai`, `anthropic` and `torch` out of the inner\n  layers.\n- **Run `lint-imports` in CI** on every pull request, so a stray adapter import in `domain/` fails the build\n  rather than relying on reviewers."},{"q":"Your test patches `myapp.adapters.llm.OpenAI` but still makes real network calls. Why?","a":"- **Names are looked up where they are used:** `services/rag.py` did `from myapp.adapters.llm import\n  OpenAI`, so it holds its own reference and the patch on the defining module does not touch it.\n- **Fix:** patch `myapp.services.rag.OpenAI`, or better, inject the client so no patching is needed.\n- **Prevent recurrence** by building clients in the composition root rather than at module top level."},{"q":"New agent worker pods take far too long to pick up their first job after scale-out. How do you check whether imports are the cause?","a":"- **Measure first:** run `python -X importtime -c \"import myapp.entrypoints.worker\"` in the worker image\n  and sort by cumulative time; heavy modules such as `torch` or every vendor SDK show up at the top.\n- **Trace why they load:** usually a re-exporting `__init__.py` or a tool registry that imports every\n  adapter at top level, although this worker serves only two tools.\n- **Fix and verify:** minimal `__init__.py`, lazy imports inside heavy adapters, tools registered per\n  entrypoint in the composition root; re-measure and watch cold-start latency."}],"tradeoffs":["**Swappable adapters vs ceremony.** Strict layering makes services testable with fakes, but adds indirection that feels like ceremony in a 500-line prototype; adopt it once a second adapter or a test suite appears.","**Fast startup vs late failure.** Lazy imports cut startup time and make optional extras optional, but move `ImportError` from startup to first use; for a long-running server, eager import at boot can be safer because it fails fast.","**Tidy API vs import cost.** A rich `__init__.py` gives users a tidy API but couples import cost to the whole package; prefer minimal `__init__` files once the package contains heavy or optional dependencies.","**Many packages vs one layered package.** Splitting into many small packages (a monorepo with workspaces) isolates dependencies cleanly, but costs versioning and release overhead that a single package with clear layers usually does not need."],"pitfalls":["**One tools module importing every tool backend.** Symptom: an agent worker that serves two tools pays the import cost and credential checks of all twelve, and one missing optional SDK breaks every agent. Fix: register tools per entrypoint in the composition root, with lazy imports in each adapter.","**Clients or models created at module top level.** Symptom: tests fail at collection with a missing API key, or a CLI `--help` takes several seconds. Fix: build them in the composition root.","**Moving an import into a function without fixing the shared concept.** Symptom: the same cycle reappears in a new form a few weeks later. Fix: move the shared types into `domain/`.","**A module named after a dependency** (`openai.py`, `logging.py`, `datasets.py`) on `sys.path`. Symptom: `AttributeError: module 'openai' has no attribute 'OpenAI'` because your file shadowed the library. Fix: rename it.","**Patching where a name is defined, not where it is looked up** (`patch(\"myapp.adapters.llm.OpenAI\")` when `services/rag.py` did `from myapp.adapters.llm import OpenAI`). Symptom: the test still makes real network calls although the patch \"is applied\". Fix: patch `myapp.services.rag.OpenAI`, or inject.","**Running modules as files** (`python src/myapp/cli.py`) instead of with `-m` or a console script. Symptom: \"attempted relative import with no known parent package\", or imports that work from one directory and fail from another.","**Flat layout and no wheel build in CI.** Symptom: `ModuleNotFoundError` or missing prompt templates only after deployment, because package data was never declared."],"when":"- **Use when:** agent code leaves the notebook and gets a `pyproject.toml`; use a src layout for anything\n  installed, deployed or shared, such as an agent service, a tool server or a shared tools library.\n- **Avoid when:** it is a one-off script; keep a flat layout.\n- **Add explicit layers when:** you have more than one implementation of an external dependency, or tests\n  start patching module globals to avoid network calls.\n- **Measure:** import time when startup matters (CLIs, serverless cold starts, worker autoscaling).\n- **Code review / interviews:** ask \"which way does this import point?\"; explain why a cycle signals a\n  misplaced concept and how `sys.modules` makes a cycle fail the way it does.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Keeps vendor SDK imports in adapters, guards type-only imports, and runs `-X importtime` when a worker's cold start creeps up.","architect":"Defines the layer contract and enforces it in CI, so the dependency direction survives team growth and vendor swaps.","fde":"Restructures a customer's notebook-grown repo into an installable package that their platform team can deploy, test and own after handover."},"check":{"q":"Your FastAPI service imports `myapp`, and `myapp/__init__.py` re-exports everything. Cold starts on\nyour serverless platform take several seconds and unit tests fail without an `OPENAI_API_KEY`. What do you\nchange and why?","a":"Both symptoms come from import-time work. Re-exporting everything means `import myapp` executes every\nmodule, including ones that import `torch` and ones that construct SDK clients at top level. I would shrink\n`__init__.py` to a small public API, move client construction into the composition root (the FastAPI lifespan\nor a factory), and inject clients into services. Heavy libraries move behind function-local or PEP 562 lazy\nimports. Then `-X importtime` confirms the cold-start gain, and tests run with fakes because nothing touches\nthe network or environment at import time."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a circular import often fail with \"cannot import name X from partially initialized module\"?","options":["Python detects the cycle and refuses to load either module","The first module is registered in sys.modules before its body finishes, so the second gets it half-built","Relative imports are not allowed inside packages that import each other","The bytecode cache is invalidated when two modules reference each other"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Python inserts a module into sys.modules before executing it. The cyclic import returns that partially executed module, so names defined later in its body do not exist yet; Python does not refuse cycles outright."},{"q":"What is the main reason to prefer a src layout for a deployable package?","options":["It makes imports run faster at startup","It is required by pyproject.toml build backends","Tests import the installed package instead of the working-directory copy, exposing packaging mistakes","It prevents circular imports between subpackages"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With src/, the repository root has no importable package directory, so tests use what installation provides (pair it with a test run against the built wheel to catch missing package data). It does not affect import speed or cycles, and build backends support flat layouts too."},{"q":"A type annotation is the only reason module A imports module B, and this creates a cycle. Best fix?","options":["Merge A and B into one module","Replace the import with importlib.import_module at call time","Import B under `if TYPE_CHECKING:` and use postponed or string annotations","Catch the ImportError and set the name to None"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TYPE_CHECKING is False at runtime, so the import exists only for type checkers and the runtime cycle disappears. Merging modules or dynamic imports work but are heavier changes than the cause needs."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/tenacity","sources":["Official docs: tenacity","Brooker, 2015 — Exponential Backoff And Jitter (AWS Architecture Blog)"],"why":"- **Remote calls fail transiently, constantly.** A production AI service makes thousands of chat\n  completions, embeddings, vector-store queries and tool calls. Rate limits, overloaded upstreams and\n  dropped connections go away on their own; without retries each is a user-facing error or a dead agent\n  run.\n- **The naive loop retries everything.** `for i in range(3): try: ... except Exception: time.sleep(1)`\n  also retries a 400 for a malformed request or a 401 for a bad key, wasting time and quota on errors that\n  never succeed.\n- **Fixed waits cause a thundering herd.** When a provider rate-limits a fleet, every worker comes back at\n  the same instant and gets limited again.\n- **No total deadline, no visibility.** Three attempts with long timeouts can outlive the caller's own\n  timeout, and no log says a call needed four attempts, so a degrading dependency looks healthy until it\n  falls over.\n- **tenacity fills the gap:** the correct policy, declared once, identical for sync and async code.","how":"A policy is four composable pieces passed to `@retry` (or to the `Retrying` / `AsyncRetrying` objects).\n\n| Piece | Question | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| `stop` | when to give up | **never stop**, so a bare `@retry` loops forever |\n| `wait` | how long between attempts | no wait at all |\n| `retry` | which outcomes count as failures | any exception |\n| hooks | what to log, what to raise at the end | `RetryError` on final failure |\n\n### stop: when to give up\n- **Building blocks:** `stop_after_attempt(5)`, `stop_after_delay(30)` (seconds since the first attempt),\n  combined with `|` (\"whichever comes first\").\n- **Use a time-based stop** *because* the caller has its own deadline; attempt counts alone do not bound\n  latency.\n- **`stop_after_delay` is a soft budget.** Stop conditions are evaluated only after an attempt fails, so an\n  attempt starting at 29 s still runs to completion (or its own timeout). True worst case ≈ the delay plus\n  one wait plus one attempt timeout.\n- **Recent releases add `stop_before_delay`,** which also refuses an attempt that would begin past the\n  budget. Either way, keep the per-attempt timeout small relative to the budget.\n\n### wait: how long between attempts\n- **Options:** `wait_fixed`, `wait_random`, `wait_exponential(multiplier=, min=, max=)`, and jittered\n  variants such as `wait_random_exponential` (random wait up to an exponentially growing cap) and\n  `wait_exponential_jitter`.\n- **Waits add together:** `wait_fixed(1) + wait_random(0, 2)`.\n- **Why both:** exponential growth backs off from an overloaded server; jitter spreads clients so retries\n  do not arrive in synchronized waves.\n\nThe arithmetic, once:\n\n```\nwait_exponential(multiplier=m, max=c):  wait after n-th failure = min(m · 2^(n−1), c)\n```\n\n| After attempt | `wait_exponential`, m=1 | `wait_random_exponential` (\"full jitter\") |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 s | uniform in [0, 1] s |\n| 2 | 2 s | [0, 2] s |\n| 3 | 4 s | [0, 4] s |\n| 4 | 8 s | [0, 8] s |\n| 5 | 16 s | — |\n| Total before 5th attempt | exactly 15 s | about 7.5 s in expectation |\n\n- **Effect:** two hundred workers rate-limited in the same second return spread over seconds, not as one\n  spike (the scheme from Brooker's analysis).\n- **Cost:** one unlucky client can draw near-zero waits and retry almost immediately; the cap and the\n  attempt limit bound the harm.\n\n### retry: which outcomes count as failures\n- **Predicates:** `retry_if_exception_type((RateLimitError, APITimeoutError))`,\n  `retry_if_exception(predicate)`, or `retry_if_result(lambda r: r is None)` for \"successful\" responses\n  that are actually bad; combine with `|` and `&`.\n- **Encode the classification here:** retry 429, 5xx and timeouts; never retry 400, 401, 403 or validation\n  errors.\n- **The default retries on any exception,** which is exactly the classification bug above.\n\n### hooks and the final failure\n- **Log every retry:** `before_sleep=before_sleep_log(logger, logging.WARNING)` logs the function name, the\n  upcoming wait and the exception (or bad result) that triggered it. For the attempt number, write a small\n  hook that reads `retry_state.attempt_number`.\n- **Final failure:** when the stop fires, tenacity raises `RetryError` wrapping the last attempt. With\n  `reraise=True` it re-raises the original exception, which callers and error handlers usually expect.\n- **`RetryCallState`:** every callback receives it (attempt number, elapsed time, `outcome`). Any callable\n  taking the state and returning seconds is a valid `wait`, so a custom wait can honor `Retry-After`:\n\n```python\nfallback = wait_random_exponential(multiplier=1, max=20)\ndef wait_retry_after(state):\n    exc = state.outcome.exception()\n    hdr = getattr(getattr(exc, \"response\", None), \"headers\", {}).get(\"retry-after\")\n    return min(float(hdr), 60) if hdr and hdr.isdigit() else fallback(state)\n```\n\n- **Why it is safe:** the server knows its own rate window, so its hint beats any guess. The `min` cap stops\n  a hostile or buggy header parking a worker for an hour; the `isdigit` check skips the HTTP-date form.\n- **Per-call control:** decorated functions expose `.retry_with(...)` to override the policy per call site,\n  and `.statistics` (attempt number, idle time) after a call. Older releases documented\n  `.retry.statistics`; in tenacity 9 that dict stays empty because each call runs on a copy.\n\n### Async and code blocks\n- **`async def` works directly:** tenacity detects the coroutine and sleeps with the event loop rather than\n  blocking it.\n- **Retry a block, not a function:** `for attempt in Retrying(...): with attempt: ...`.\n\n### Retries inside an agent loop\nAn agent has three places a failure can be handled, and each suits a different error class:\n\n| Error class | Handle it | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| Transient transport error (429, 5xx, timeout) | tenacity around the model or tool adapter | the same request will likely succeed shortly |\n| Permanent tool error (404 order, bad arguments) | return it to the model as the tool result | the model can fix its arguments or choose another tool |\n| Bad model output (invalid JSON, unknown tool) | re-prompt with the validation error | the identical prompt tends to reproduce the same output |\n| Sustained outage | circuit breaker, then a clear failure | retries only multiply load |\n\n- **Budget per step, not per call.** The run has a deadline; give each step a slice of it and set\n  `stop_after_delay` from that slice.\n- **Write tools need idempotency keys** before any retry, because a timeout does not prove the write failed.\n\n### Alternatives\n| Tool | Scope |\n|---|---|\n| `backoff` | similar decorator library |\n| `stamina` | thin, opinionated wrapper over tenacity with safer defaults |\n| `urllib3.Retry`, httpx transports | HTTP layer only |\n| Vendor SDKs (OpenAI, Anthropic) | retry some errors internally via a `max_retries` setting; account for it before adding your own layer |","example":"### Chat-completion policy with one retry layer\nRetry only transient errors, jittered exponential backoff, a 30-second retry budget, a log line per retry,\nand the original exception re-raised. The SDK's own retries are switched off so attempts do not multiply.\n\n```python\nimport logging\nfrom openai import OpenAI, RateLimitError, APITimeoutError, APIConnectionError, InternalServerError\nfrom tenacity import (retry, stop_after_attempt, stop_after_delay, wait_random_exponential,\n                      retry_if_exception_type, before_sleep_log)\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"llm\")\nclient = OpenAI(max_retries=0, timeout=20.0)   # one retry layer only: ours\n\nTRANSIENT = (RateLimitError, APITimeoutError, APIConnectionError, InternalServerError)\n\n@retry(\n    retry=retry_if_exception_type(TRANSIENT),            # 400/401/422 fail immediately\n    wait=wait_random_exponential(multiplier=1, max=20),  # jittered, capped backoff\n    stop=stop_after_attempt(5) | stop_after_delay(30),   # whichever comes first\n    before_sleep=before_sleep_log(log, logging.WARNING),\n    reraise=True,                                        # caller sees RateLimitError, not RetryError\n)\ndef summarize(text: str) -> str:\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Summarize:\\n{text}\"}],\n    )\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\n# Per-call override, e.g. a batch job that can wait longer:\n# summarize.retry_with(stop=stop_after_attempt(10))(doc)\n```\n\nWorst-case latency of this policy, because the budget is checked between attempts:\n\n| Component | Bound |\n|---|---|\n| An attempt fails just before the budget, so the stop check passes | just under 30 s elapsed |\n| One more wait | at most 8 s (five attempts allow only four waits, windows 1, 2, 4 and 8 s) |\n| One more attempt, running to its timeout | 20 s |\n| Total | just under a minute |\n\nNeed a strict ceiling? Shrink the per-attempt timeout or enforce an outer deadline.\n\n### Retry on a bad result, test-friendly\nNeeds only `pip install tenacity`. A \"successful\" `None` counts as a failure, and `wait_none()` keeps the\ntest instant; production would use the jittered wait above.\n\n```python\nfrom tenacity import retry, stop_after_attempt, wait_none, retry_if_exception_type, retry_if_result\n\ncalls = {\"n\": 0}\n\n@retry(\n    retry=retry_if_exception_type(ConnectionError) | retry_if_result(lambda r: r is None),\n    stop=stop_after_attempt(4),\n    wait=wait_none(),\n    reraise=True,\n)\ndef lookup():\n    calls[\"n\"] += 1\n    if calls[\"n\"] == 1:\n        raise ConnectionError(\"dropped\")      # transient: retried\n    if calls[\"n\"] == 2:\n        return None                           # bad result: retried\n    return {\"ok\": True}\n\nassert lookup() == {\"ok\": True} and calls[\"n\"] == 3\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: transient errors retried, permanent errors handed to the model\nThe tool adapter retries only transport errors; a permanent error becomes a tool result the model can act\non, so the loop never burns attempts on a 404. Needs only `pip install tenacity`.\n\n```python\nfrom tenacity import retry, stop_after_attempt, stop_after_delay, wait_random_exponential\nfrom tenacity import retry_if_exception_type\n\nclass Transient(Exception): ...          # adapters map 429, 5xx, timeouts to this\nclass ToolError(Exception): ...          # adapters map 4xx to this: the model should see it\n\nattempts = {\"n\": 0}\n\n@retry(retry=retry_if_exception_type(Transient),\n       wait=wait_random_exponential(multiplier=0.01, max=0.05),   # tiny for the demo\n       stop=stop_after_attempt(4) | stop_after_delay(10), reraise=True)\ndef get_order(order_id: str) -> str:\n    attempts[\"n\"] += 1\n    if attempts[\"n\"] < 3:\n        raise Transient(\"503 from orders API\")\n    if order_id == \"Z99\":\n        raise ToolError(\"order Z99 not found\")\n    return f\"order {order_id}: shipped\"\n\ndef run_tool(name: str, args: dict) -> dict:\n    try:\n        return {\"role\": \"tool\", \"name\": name, \"content\": get_order(**args)}\n    except ToolError as e:                # permanent: hand back to the model, no retry\n        return {\"role\": \"tool\", \"name\": name, \"content\": f\"error: {e}\", \"is_error\": True}\n    # Transient that exhausted retries propagates: the step fails, the run can checkpoint and stop\n\nprint(run_tool(\"get_order\", {\"order_id\": \"A17\"}), attempts)   # succeeded on attempt 3\nprint(run_tool(\"get_order\", {\"order_id\": \"Z99\"}))             # 1 attempt, error shown to the model\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: stacked retry layers\nEach layer makes up to 3 attempts: the agent loop re-runs a failed tool up to 3 times, the tool function has\n`@retry(stop=stop_after_attempt(3))`, and the SDK makes up to 3 attempts internally.\n\n| Layers retrying | Upstream calls per user action, worst case |\n|---|---|\n| SDK only | 3 |\n| SDK + function | 3 × 3 = 9 |\n| SDK + function + agent loop | 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 |\n\nUnder a rate limit, those 27 calls make the limit worse. Fix: `max_retries=0` on the SDK, one tenacity\npolicy, and the agent loop treats the re-raised error as final.","patterns":["**Classify failures at the tool adapter.** Map transport errors to one transient type that tenacity retries, and permanent errors to a tool-error result the model sees, so the agent can correct its arguments. Monitor tool error rate by tool and class; a spike in permanent errors usually means a prompt or schema regression, not an outage.","**Retry the step, not the run.** Checkpoint agent state after each completed step (LangGraph checkpointers do this) so an exhausted retry fails one step and the run resumes from there instead of repeating paid model calls and side effects. Monitor resumed runs and repeated tool calls per run.","**Exactly one retry layer per call path.** Disable SDK retries (`OpenAI(max_retries=0)`) or HTTP-transport retries when you add tenacity, and document which layer owns the policy. Monitor upstream calls per user action.","**Retry budget below the caller's deadline.** Set `stop_after_delay` plus one attempt timeout plus one max wait under the gateway or proxy timeout; for user-facing paths tighten it, for batch jobs use `.retry_with(...)` to allow more. Monitor p99 latency and timeouts at the proxy.","**Honor `Retry-After`, capped.** Use a custom wait like `wait_retry_after` above with a jittered fallback, so you obey the provider's window without trusting it blindly. Monitor 429 rate per provider.","**Log and count every retry.** `before_sleep=before_sleep_log(log, logging.WARNING)` (or a hook that increments a metric tagged by dependency). Alert on a rising retry rate, the early warning that a provider is degrading.","**Idempotency keys before retries on side effects.** For \"create ticket\" or \"send email\" tools, send a client-generated ID the server deduplicates on, and restrict `retry=` to errors that guarantee the request was not processed. Monitor duplicate-record counts.","**Circuit breaker after a few attempts.** Under a sustained outage, fail fast instead of multiplying load; retries handle blips, breakers handle outages. Monitor breaker open time."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A 20-step agent run makes dozens of model and tool calls. If 1 in\n  100 calls fails transiently, a 40-call run fails about a third of the time (1 − 0.99^40 ≈ 0.33) unless\n  something retries. Retries in the\n  wrong place multiply load or create duplicate side effects, such as two tickets from one tool call.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Vendor SDKs retry internally via `max_retries`; LangChain runnables\n  offer a retry wrapper and LangGraph lets you attach a retry policy to a node; your own tool adapters use\n  tenacity. Pick one owner per call path, and trace retries in LangSmith or your logs.\n- **What you control.** Which errors are transient (retry) versus permanent (fail or hand back to the\n  model), the time budget per step under the run's deadline, jitter, `Retry-After` handling, and\n  idempotency keys on write tools.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing custom backoff math. `wait_random_exponential` plus a stop budget covers\n  almost every agent call.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Flaky tool APIs inside agent runs.** Retrying transient search, CRM or database errors keeps a long\n  run from failing on one bad call, while permanent errors go back to the model as a tool result.\n- **Rate-limited model calls under load.** Jittered exponential backoff that honours `Retry-After` spreads\n  retries from many concurrent agent sessions instead of hammering the provider in sync.\n- **Write tools with side effects.** Retrying a \"create ticket\" or \"send email\" tool only with an\n  idempotency key prevents duplicates when the first attempt actually succeeded.\n- **Step budgets under a run deadline.** A stop on total delay keeps one retried step from consuming the\n  whole run's latency budget.","mini_project":"**Add a retry policy to an agent's tool layer (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make a two-tool agent survive injected transient failures without duplicate side effects.\n- **Steps:** wrap a fake search tool that fails randomly with a timeout or a 429, and a fake \"create\n  ticket\" tool; add tenacity with `wait_random_exponential`, a stop on attempts and total delay,\n  `reraise=True` and retry only on transient types; give the write tool an idempotency key; log each retry\n  with the run and step; run 100 simulated agent runs with and without the policy.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of run success rate and total retries for both settings, no run creates\n  a duplicate ticket, and permanent errors reach the model instead of being retried."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Call wrapped function: attempt n\"] --> O{\"retry predicate: is this outcome a failure?\"}\n    O -->|\"no: success or non-retryable error\"| R[\"Return result or raise immediately\"]\n    O -->|\"yes: 429, 5xx, timeout, bad result\"| S{\"stop: attempts or elapsed budget used up?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes, reraise=True\"| E1[\"Re-raise original exception\"]\n    S -->|\"yes, default\"| E2[\"Raise RetryError wrapping last attempt\"]\n    S -->|\"no\"| W[\"wait: jittered exponential, or Retry-After\"]\n    W --> H[\"before_sleep hook logs wait and error\"]\n    H --> SL[\"Sleep, or await asyncio sleep in async code\"]\n    SL -->|\"n plus 1\"| A","caption":"Each attempt's outcome goes through the `retry` predicate first, then the `stop` check, and only if it does\nnot stop does tenacity sleep the `wait`; the stop is evaluated after a failure, which is why a time budget\nis soft."},"interview":[{"q":"Which errors from an LLM API should you retry, and which should you never retry?","a":"- **Retry transient ones:** 429 rate limits, 5xx server errors, timeouts and dropped connections, because\n  they usually go away on their own.\n- **Never retry permanent ones:** 400 malformed requests, 401 or 403 auth failures and validation errors,\n  because every attempt fails the same way and burns time and quota.\n- **Encode it in `retry=`** with `retry_if_exception_type(...)`, since the default retries on any exception."},{"q":"Why exponential backoff with jitter rather than a fixed wait?","a":"- **Exponential growth** backs off from an overloaded upstream: with `wait_exponential(multiplier=1)` the\n  waits are 1, 2, 4, 8 s.\n- **Jitter desynchronizes a fleet:** two hundred workers rate-limited in the same second come back spread\n  over seconds instead of as one spike that triggers the limit again.\n- **Cost:** worst-case latency is less predictable, so cap the wait with `max=` and bound attempts."},{"q":"Your service's p99 jumped to a minute during a provider incident although `stop_after_delay(30)` is set. Explain.","a":"- **The stop is checked only after a failed attempt,** so an attempt starting at 29 s still runs to its own\n  timeout.\n- **Worst case is the budget plus one wait plus one attempt timeout:** with the example policy, just under\n  30 s plus up to 8 s plus 20 s, so just under a minute.\n- **Fix:** shrink the per-attempt timeout, use `stop_before_delay` where your tenacity version has it, or\n  enforce an outer deadline."},{"q":"When is retrying the wrong tool?","a":"- **Non-idempotent side effects** without an idempotency key: a timeout that actually succeeded upstream\n  becomes a duplicate ticket or email.\n- **Sustained outages:** per-call retries multiply load, so a circuit breaker that fails fast is better.\n- **Deterministic bad results:** retrying invalid JSON with the identical prompt at temperature 0 tends to\n  fail identically; feed the validation error back instead."},{"q":"In an agent, when should a tool failure be retried and when should it go back to the model?","a":"- **Retry transient transport errors** (429, 5xx, timeouts) in the adapter with tenacity, because the\n  same request will likely succeed and the model learns nothing from them.\n- **Return permanent errors as the tool result** (not found, invalid arguments), so the model can fix its\n  call or pick another tool instead of the loop wasting attempts.\n- **Never blindly retry write tools:** require an idempotency key first, since a timeout may hide a\n  write that succeeded."},{"q":"How many upstream calls can one user action cause if the SDK, your function and the agent loop each retry 3 times?","a":"- **Multiply the layers:** 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 calls in the worst case, which makes a rate limit worse.\n- **Fix:** keep exactly one retry layer, for example `OpenAI(max_retries=0)` plus one tenacity policy, and\n  have the agent loop treat the re-raised error as final.\n- **Monitor** upstream calls per user action and retry rate per dependency."}],"tradeoffs":["**Errors vs latency.** Retries convert transient failures into latency, right for background jobs but able to blow a user-facing p99; tighten `stop_after_delay` when a person is waiting.","**Upstream protection vs predictability.** Jittered exponential backoff protects a struggling upstream but makes worst-case latency less predictable than a fixed wait; predictability wins only with a single client, not a fleet.","**One policy place vs duplication.** Retrying in your code centralizes policy and logging but duplicates what SDKs and HTTP transports already do; it pays off when you disable the inner layers or retry at a higher level (a whole tool call).","**Blips vs outages.** Per-call retries handle blips, but under a sustained outage they multiply load; beyond a few attempts a circuit breaker that fails fast is the better tool."],"pitfalls":["**Retrying a whole agent run on any failure.** Symptom: one flaky tool at step 9 replays eight paid model calls and repeats earlier side effects. Fix: retry at the call, checkpoint each step, and resume the run from the last completed step.","**Bare `@retry` with no arguments.** Symptom: a request hangs forever, spinning with no wait, against an endpoint returning a permanent error. Fix: always set stop, wait and retry.","**Retrying non-idempotent operations** (\"send email\", \"create ticket\") without an idempotency key. Symptom: duplicate side effects after a timeout that actually succeeded upstream. Fix: an idempotency key, and retry only errors that prove the request was not processed.","**Stacked retries** in the SDK, your function and the agent loop. Symptom: with 3 layers of 3 attempts, up to 27 upstream calls per user action and rate limits that get worse under load. Fix: one retry layer.","**Forgetting `reraise=True`.** Symptom: handlers catching `RateLimitError` never fire because the exception arriving is a `tenacity.RetryError`. Fix: set `reraise=True`.","**Retrying a bad LLM *result*** (invalid JSON, failed schema) with the identical prompt at temperature 0. Symptom: every attempt fails the same way, because the same input tends to reproduce the same output. Fix: feed the validation error back into the next attempt, as Instructor does, or change something.","**Retrying on `Exception`, and thereby on your own bugs.** Symptom: a `KeyError` in response parsing costs five paid API calls before surfacing. Fix: an explicit `retry_if_exception_type(...)` list."],"when":"- **Use when:** agent code calls something remote that fails transiently and the operation is safe to repeat:\n  LLM and embedding calls, vector-store queries, idempotent tool APIs, downloads.\n- **Prefer SDK retries when:** they already cover your error classes and you only need to tune a count.\n- **Prefer tenacity when:** you need custom classification (retry on a bad *result*, such as invalid JSON),\n  a time budget, logging, or retries around a multi-step block.\n- **Avoid when:** papering over permanent errors or sustained outages.\n- **Measure:** attempts per call and retry rate per dependency; a rising retry rate is an early warning\n  that a provider is degrading.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the stop/wait/retry policy per dependency, disables duplicate SDK retries, and adds `before_sleep` logging so retries show up in traces.","architect":"Owns the retry budget across layers, which operations are idempotent, and where circuit breakers take over from retries.","fde":"Tunes backoff to the customer's actual provider quota and gateway timeouts so retries fit inside their proxy's deadline instead of being cut off mid-attempt."},"check":{"q":"An agent's tool calls a customer's ticketing API through a function decorated with\n`@retry(stop=stop_after_attempt(3))`. Customers report duplicate tickets. What is happening and how do you fix it?","a":"Creating a ticket is not idempotent. When a request times out after the server has already created\nthe ticket, tenacity retries on the exception and creates a second one; the policy also retries on any\nexception with no wait. Fixes, in order: send an idempotency key (or a client-generated ticket ID) so the\nserver deduplicates; restrict `retry=` to errors that guarantee the request was not processed, such as\nconnection refused or explicit 429/503; add jittered backoff and a time budget; and check whether the HTTP\nclient or SDK also retries, so attempts are not multiplied across layers."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `@retry` with no arguments do when the wrapped function keeps raising?","options":["Retries three times with exponential backoff, then re-raises","Retries indefinitely with no wait between attempts","Retries once, then raises RetryError","Retries only on network-related exceptions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"tenacity's defaults are stop-never, no wait, and retry on any exception. That is why every real policy sets stop, wait and retry explicitly; the \"three attempts with backoff\" behaviour must be configured."},{"q":"Why add jitter to exponential backoff when many workers call the same LLM API?","options":["Jitter reduces the total number of attempts each worker makes","Jitter is required for tenacity to work with asyncio","It desynchronizes clients so retries do not arrive together and trigger the rate limit again","It makes the latency of each retry predictable for SLOs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without jitter, workers limited at the same moment back off by identical amounts and return in a wave. Randomizing the wait spreads the load; it makes individual latency less predictable, not more."},{"q":"Your error handler catches `openai.RateLimitError`, but after retries are exhausted it never fires. Likely cause?","options":["The SDK swallows rate-limit errors after retries","tenacity converts all exceptions to TimeoutError","Without reraise=True, tenacity raises RetryError wrapping the last attempt instead of the original exception","before_sleep_log suppresses the final exception"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When the stop condition fires, tenacity raises RetryError by default. Setting reraise=True re-raises the last underlying exception, so existing except clauses for the SDK's error types keep working."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/logging-structlog","sources":["Official docs: Python logging (Logging HOWTO and Logging Cookbook)","Official docs: structlog"],"why":"- **Concurrency makes prose logs useless.** `print()` and f-string log lines work until there are many\n  concurrent requests. An LLM service interleaves hundreds of in-flight calls in one\n  process; `\"call failed after 3.2s\"` cannot tell you which user, conversation, model or prompt version it\n  belongs to.\n- **Prose cannot be aggregated.** \"p95 latency by model\" or \"token spend by tenant\" needs fields; regexing\n  them back out of free text is fragile and slow.\n- **Stdlib solves routing, not structure.** It decides who emits, at what level, to where. You can pass\n  `extra={...}`, but every formatter must know which keys to print.\n- **No natural request context.** There is no built-in way to attach a request ID once and have every\n  downstream line carry it, especially across `await` points.\n- **structlog fills the gap.** Context is bound once, every event is a dict, and rendering (JSON for\n  machines, coloured console for humans) is configuration, not baked into every call site.","how":"### stdlib logging: the routing layer\n| Piece | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **Loggers** | named and hierarchical (`myapp.rag.retriever` is a child of `myapp`); use `logging.getLogger(__name__)` so names mirror modules |\n| **Levels** | `DEBUG < INFO < WARNING < ERROR < CRITICAL`, checked on the logger and again on each handler |\n| **Handlers** | send records somewhere (stderr, file, syslog) |\n| **Formatters** | turn a record into text |\n\n- **Propagation:** a record goes to its logger's handlers, then up to ancestors, ending at the root.\n- **So libraries never configure logging.** No `basicConfig`, no handlers: configuration belongs to the\n  application. A library adds only a `NullHandler`, staying silent unless the app opts in.\n- **Use `log.info(\"retrieved %d chunks\", n)`, not an f-string,** *because* formatting is deferred and\n  skipped entirely when the level is disabled.\n- **`log.exception(...)` inside `except`** attaches the traceback.\n\n### structlog: events and processors\n1. A call such as `log.info(\"llm_call\", model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", tokens_in=812)` creates an **event dict**.\n2. The dict passes through a **processor chain**: an ordered list of functions\n   `(logger, method_name, event_dict) -> event_dict`.\n3. A final **renderer** turns it into output.\n\nTypical chain, in order:\n\n| Processor | Job |\n|---|---|\n| `merge_contextvars` | pull in request-scoped context |\n| `add_log_level` | add `level` |\n| `TimeStamper(fmt=\"iso\")` | add a timestamp |\n| `format_exc_info` or `dict_tracebacks` | structured exceptions |\n| custom redaction step | scrub sensitive keys |\n| `JSONRenderer()` / `ConsoleRenderer()` | production / local rendering |\n\n- **Policy lives in one place** (timestamps, redaction, sampling), not at every call site.\n- **A processor is just a function,** so redaction and sampling look like this:\n\n```python\nSENSITIVE = {\"api_key\", \"authorization\", \"prompt\", \"completion\"}\ndef redact(logger, method_name, event_dict):\n    for key in SENSITIVE & event_dict.keys():\n        event_dict[key] = \"[redacted]\"\n    return event_dict\n\ndef sample_debug(logger, method_name, event_dict):\n    if method_name == \"debug\" and random.random() > 0.01:\n        raise structlog.DropEvent          # silently discards this event\n    return event_dict\n```\n\n- **Order matters** *because* each processor sees only what earlier ones produced. Redaction must run after\n  `merge_contextvars` (or a key bound in context escapes it) and before the renderer (after which there is\n  only a string).\n- **Keep processors cheap:** every processor runs on every emitted event.\n\n### Context binding\n- **Local binding:** `log = log.bind(conversation_id=cid)` returns a new logger carrying that field. Bound\n  loggers are immutable, so binding in one function cannot leak into another.\n- **Request-wide:** `structlog.contextvars` (`bind_contextvars`, `clear_contextvars`) stores fields in a\n  `contextvars.ContextVar`.\n- **Why it works across `await`:** context variables are copied into each asyncio task, so a request ID bound\n  in FastAPI middleware appears on every line for that request without passing it explicitly.\n- **Clear at request start** so a reused worker does not carry stale values.\n- **Limits of the copy.** It is one-way and happens at task creation:\n\n| Situation | Sees the request context? |\n|---|---|\n| Child asyncio task | yes, a copy; values bound in the child are invisible to the parent |\n| `asyncio.to_thread` | yes, it copies the context |\n| `loop.run_in_executor` | no, so lines logged from that thread lose their `request_id` |\n\n### Handlers can block the event loop\n- **Stdlib handlers write synchronously** inside the logging call.\n- **stdout/stderr in a container** is normally cheap; a file on a slow disk or a network handler (syslog,\n  HTTP) stalls every coroutine in the process while it writes.\n- **Stdlib answer:** `QueueHandler` on the hot path plus a `QueueListener` thread that owns the slow\n  handlers, *because* enqueueing is fast and the I/O moves off the event loop.\n- **Container answer:** write JSON to stdout and let the platform's log agent ship it.\n\n### Level discipline\n| Level | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| DEBUG | detail you would enable while investigating |\n| INFO | one summary event per meaningful unit of work (a request, an LLM call, a tool call) |\n| WARNING | something that recovered (a retry, a fallback model) |\n| ERROR | a failed unit of work someone may need to look at |\n\n- Levels are useful only if they mean the same thing everywhere. Alert on ERROR rate and on fields, not on\n  message text.\n\n### Integrating the two\n- **Libraries (httpx, openai, uvicorn) log through stdlib.** structlog can use stdlib loggers as its backend,\n  or render stdlib records via `structlog.stdlib.ProcessorFormatter`, so library and application lines share\n  one JSON format.\n- **Standalone speed:** `make_filtering_bound_logger(logging.INFO)` is a fast level filter, and\n  `cache_logger_on_first_use=True` avoids rebuilding loggers on every call.\n\n### Logs vs traces vs metrics\n- **Logs:** discrete events, such as one `tool_call` with its tool name, status and latency.\n- **Traces (OpenTelemetry spans):** timing and parent-child structure across services.\n- **Metrics:** cheap aggregates.\n- **Join them:** put the trace ID in every log event, and do not use logs as your only latency metric.\n- **For agents:** a tracing tool (LangSmith, Langfuse, an OpenTelemetry backend) shows one run as a tree of\n  model and tool spans; logs carry the same `run_id` so an alert on fields leads straight to that tree.","example":"### Production configuration with one event per LLM call\nJSON output, request context via contextvars, and fields you will later aggregate on. The prompt itself is\nnot logged, only its hash.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, logging, time, uuid\nimport structlog\n\nstructlog.configure(\n    processors=[\n        structlog.contextvars.merge_contextvars,     # request-scoped fields\n        structlog.processors.add_log_level,\n        structlog.processors.TimeStamper(fmt=\"iso\"),\n        structlog.processors.dict_tracebacks,        # exceptions as structured data\n        structlog.processors.JSONRenderer(),\n    ],\n    wrapper_class=structlog.make_filtering_bound_logger(logging.INFO),\n    cache_logger_on_first_use=True,\n)\nlog = structlog.get_logger()\n\ndef handle_request(user_id: str, prompt: str) -> None:\n    structlog.contextvars.clear_contextvars()\n    structlog.contextvars.bind_contextvars(request_id=str(uuid.uuid4()), user_id=user_id)\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    # ... call the model here ...\n    log.info(\"llm_call\", model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", prompt_version=\"summarize-v3\",\n             prompt_sha=hashlib.sha256(prompt.encode()).hexdigest()[:12],\n             tokens_in=812, tokens_out=164, latency_ms=round((time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000))\n\nhandle_request(\"u_42\", \"Summarize this contract ...\")\n# {\"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\", \"prompt_version\": \"summarize-v3\", \"prompt_sha\": \"…\", \"tokens_in\": 812,\n#  \"tokens_out\": 164, \"latency_ms\": 0, \"event\": \"llm_call\", \"request_id\": \"…\", \"user_id\": \"u_42\",\n#  \"level\": \"info\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-…Z\"}\n# (keys follow processor order: call kwargs, then contextvars, level, timestamp; pass\n#  JSONRenderer(sort_keys=True) if your pipeline wants a stable alphabetical order)\n```\n\n- **Do not rely on key order among context fields:** `request_id` and `user_id` can come out in either\n  order, because `merge_contextvars` reads an unordered context mapping. Query by key, or use `sort_keys=True`.\n\n### Watch request context vanish in a thread pool\nStandard library only. Two concurrent requests each set a `ContextVar`; `asyncio.to_thread` keeps it,\n`run_in_executor` does not.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, contextvars, logging\n\nrequest_id = contextvars.ContextVar(\"request_id\", default=\"-\")\n\nclass AddRequestId(logging.Filter):\n    def filter(self, record):\n        record.request_id = request_id.get()   # read at emit time, in the emitting thread\n        return True\n\nhandler = logging.StreamHandler()\nhandler.addFilter(AddRequestId())\nhandler.setFormatter(logging.Formatter(\"%(request_id)s %(message)s\"))\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, handlers=[handler])\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"demo\")\n\ndef blocking_work(label):\n    log.info(\"in thread via %s\", label)\n\nasync def handle(rid):\n    request_id.set(rid)                       # per task: each gathered task has its own copy\n    await asyncio.to_thread(blocking_work, \"to_thread\")                 # req-A / req-B\n    loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n    await loop.run_in_executor(None, blocking_work, \"run_in_executor\")  # \"-\"\n    log.info(\"back in task\")                                            # req-A / req-B\n\nasync def main():\n    await asyncio.gather(handle(\"req-A\"), handle(\"req-B\"))\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: one event per step, bound to the run\nBind `run_id` once per run and `step` per iteration, so every model call, tool call and retry line can be\ngrouped by run. Tool arguments are hashed, not logged. Needs only `pip install structlog`.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, logging, time, uuid\nimport structlog\n\nstructlog.configure(\n    processors=[structlog.contextvars.merge_contextvars, structlog.processors.add_log_level,\n                structlog.processors.TimeStamper(fmt=\"iso\"), structlog.processors.JSONRenderer()],\n    wrapper_class=structlog.make_filtering_bound_logger(logging.INFO))\nlog = structlog.get_logger()\n\ndef run_agent(task: str, steps: list[tuple[str, dict]]) -> None:\n    structlog.contextvars.clear_contextvars()\n    structlog.contextvars.bind_contextvars(run_id=str(uuid.uuid4()), agent=\"support-v2\")\n    for i, (tool, args) in enumerate(steps, start=1):\n        structlog.contextvars.bind_contextvars(step=i)          # later lines carry the step\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        status = \"ok\" if tool != \"refund\" else \"error\"          # stand-in for the real tool call\n        log.info(\"tool_call\", tool=tool, status=status,\n                 args_sha=hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(args, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()[:12],\n                 latency_ms=round((time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000))\n    log.info(\"run_end\", steps=len(steps), outcome=\"answered\")\n\nrun_agent(\"refund A17\", [(\"lookup_order\", {\"id\": \"A17\"}), (\"refund\", {\"id\": \"A17\"})])\n# {\"tool\": \"lookup_order\", \"status\": \"ok\", ..., \"run_id\": \"...\", \"agent\": \"support-v2\", \"step\": 1, ...}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: an agent that loops\nSupport reports a run that never answered. With `tool_call` events carrying `run_id`, `step`, `tool`,\n`status` and `args_sha`:\n\n| Question | Query over fields | What it showed |\n|---|---|---|\n| How many steps did the run take? | max `step` where `run_id` = the reported ID | it hit the step budget |\n| Was it the same call each time? | count by `tool`, `args_sha` for that run | one tool, one argument hash, repeated |\n| How widespread is it? | runs where one `args_sha` repeats 3+ times, by `agent` | only the new prompt version |\n\nThe fix was a guard that stops after repeated identical calls, plus an alert on that repeat count.\n\n### Worked scenario: one event vocabulary, three incident questions\nWith `llm_call` events carrying `model`, `tokens_in`, `tokens_out`, `latency_ms`, `request_id` and `user_id`:\n\n| Incident question | Query over fields |\n|---|---|\n| Which model got slow? | p95 of `latency_ms` grouped by `model` |\n| Who is burning tokens? | sum of `tokens_in + tokens_out` grouped by `user_id` |\n| What happened in this request? | all events where `request_id` = the ID from the error report |\n\nNone of these is possible from `\"call failed after 3.2s\"`.","patterns":["**Bind run and step IDs in the agent loop.** Call `bind_contextvars(run_id=..., agent=...)` at run start and `bind_contextvars(step=i)` each iteration, so tool and retry lines are grouped without passing IDs around. Alert on runs whose max `step` equals the step budget.","**Hash tool arguments, log status and latency.** Emit one `tool_call` event per call with tool name, status, `args_sha` and `latency_ms`; keep the arguments themselves in the tracing store with its access controls. Repeated identical hashes in one run are the cheapest loop detector there is.","**Bind context in middleware, clear first.** In FastAPI (or any framework) middleware call `clear_contextvars()` then `bind_contextvars(request_id=..., user_id=...)` before the handler runs. Monitor the share of log lines missing `request_id`; it should be near zero.","**Fixed event vocabulary.** Define `llm_call`, `retrieval`, `tool_call`, `guardrail_block` with consistent field names in one module, and build dashboards and alerts on those fields. Review new event names in code review like an API change.","**Redaction as a processor, with a test.** Place `redact` after `merge_contextvars` and before the renderer, and add a test that logs a fake key and asserts it never appears in output. Log a hash or a prompt version instead of the prompt.","**Stdout JSON, off-loop slow sinks.** In containers write JSON to stdout and let the log agent ship it; if you must use a file or network handler, put it behind `QueueHandler` and a `QueueListener`. Monitor event-loop lag.","**One format for library logs.** Route stdlib records through `structlog.stdlib.ProcessorFormatter` and quiet chatty libraries with `logging.getLogger(\"httpx\").setLevel(logging.WARNING)`. Monitor log volume per request.","**Trace ID on every event.** A small processor that adds the current OpenTelemetry span's trace ID lets you jump from a log line to its trace. Check that a sample of ERROR lines carries a trace ID."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** One agent run interleaves many model calls, tool calls and retries, and\n  a service runs hundreds of runs at once. Prose logs cannot tell you which run, step or tool a line\n  belongs to; structured events with bound `run_id`, `step` and `tool` can.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangSmith, Langfuse or OpenTelemetry-based tracing hold full\n  step-by-step traces with prompts; structlog JSON on stdout holds the cheap, always-on events your\n  dashboards and alerts run on. LangChain, httpx and the vendor SDKs log through stdlib, so route them\n  into the same JSON format.\n- **What you control.** The event vocabulary (`llm_call`, `tool_call`, `retrieval`, `guardrail_block`),\n  which IDs are bound per run and per step, redaction of prompts, tool arguments and secrets, and the\n  trace ID that joins a log line to its trace.\n- **What you can skip.** Custom handler classes and logging-config dict files. One `structlog.configure`\n  call plus stdout is enough for most agent services.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Debugging one bad agent run among hundreds.** Filtering JSON logs by a bound `run_id` reconstructs\n  the run's steps, tool calls and retries in order.\n- **Cost and latency dashboards.** `llm_call` events carrying model, token counts and latency feed per-run\n  cost and slow-step alerts without the expense of full traces.\n- **Guardrail and safety auditing.** `guardrail_block` and tool-denial events give a searchable record of\n  what the agent tried and what was stopped.\n- **Joining logs to traces.** A trace ID on every line links a cheap log event to its LangSmith, Langfuse or\n  OpenTelemetry trace when the full prompt is needed.","mini_project":"**Instrument an agent loop with structured events (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make every run of a small tool-calling agent queryable from its logs alone.\n- **Steps:** configure structlog once for JSON on stdout and route stdlib logging from httpx and the SDK\n  into it; bind `run_id` per run and `step` and `tool` per step with context variables; emit `llm_call`,\n  `tool_call` and `run_end` events with latency and token counts; add a processor that redacts API keys\n  and replaces raw prompts and tool arguments with hashes; run 20 concurrent runs.\n- **Done when:** a single `jq` filter on one `run_id` prints that run's steps in order, no secret appears in\n  the output, and you can compute total tokens per run from the log lines."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Middleware binds request_id\"] -.->|\"ContextVar copied into each task\"| C\n    C[\"log.info llm_call with model, tokens\"] --> E[\"Event dict\"]\n    E --> P1[\"merge_contextvars\"]\n    P1 --> P2[\"add_log_level and TimeStamper\"]\n    P2 --> P3[\"dict_tracebacks\"]\n    P3 --> P4[\"redact sensitive keys\"]\n    P4 --> R[\"JSONRenderer or ConsoleRenderer\"]\n    R --> O[\"stdout, shipped by log agent\"]\n    L[\"Library logs via stdlib: httpx, openai\"] -->|\"ProcessorFormatter\"| R\n    O --> Q[(\"Log index: query by fields\")]","caption":"Follow one `log.info` call: middleware has already called `clear_contextvars()` then `bind_contextvars(...)`,\nso request context is merged first; redaction runs before rendering; library records join at the renderer;\nand the rendered line goes to stdout (or a queue) so a slow sink never blocks the event loop."},"interview":[{"q":"What does structlog add on top of the standard `logging` module?","a":"- **Stdlib handles routing:** hierarchical loggers, levels, handlers and formatters decide who emits, at what\n  level and to where.\n- **structlog adds structure:** every call is an event dict passed through a processor chain and rendered as\n  JSON, so incident questions become queries over fields.\n- **And context:** fields bound once with `bind` or `bind_contextvars` appear on every later line without\n  being passed explicitly."},{"q":"Some log lines for a request are missing `request_id`. How do you track it down?","a":"- **Check where they were emitted:** lines logged from `loop.run_in_executor` work lose the context, because\n  that call does not copy the current context into the thread, while `asyncio.to_thread` does.\n- **Check the processor order:** `merge_contextvars` must be in the chain, before the renderer.\n- **Check middleware:** context must be bound before the handler runs, and cleared at request start."},{"q":"Where does redaction belong in the processor chain, and why there?","a":"- **After `merge_contextvars`,** otherwise a sensitive key bound in context escapes it.\n- **Before the renderer,** because after rendering there is only a string to scrub.\n- **Back it with a test** that logs a fake API key and asserts it never appears in output, and prefer logging\n  a prompt hash or version over the prompt itself."},{"q":"How would you make a misbehaving agent run debuggable from logs alone?","a":"- **Bind identity:** `run_id`, agent name and prompt version per run, `step` per iteration, via\n  contextvars so every line carries them.\n- **Fix the vocabulary:** one `llm_call` event with model, tokens and latency, and one `tool_call` event\n  with tool, status and an argument hash, per call.\n- **Join to traces:** add the trace ID so a query that finds the bad run jumps to its full trace in\n  LangSmith, Langfuse or your OpenTelemetry backend."},{"q":"Would you log full prompts, completions and tool arguments for production agents?","a":"- **The gain:** debugging is much easier with the exact input and output.\n- **The cost:** user data, retrieved documents and secrets land in a system with broad access and long\n  retention; tool arguments often carry account numbers or emails.\n- **Middle ground:** redaction, sampling and short retention, or send payloads to a dedicated LLM tracing store\n  and keep only a hash, token counts and latency in logs."},{"q":"Your async service's latency spikes whenever the log volume is high. What is a likely cause and fix?","a":"- **Likely cause:** stdlib handlers write synchronously inside the logging call, so a file on a slow disk or a\n  network handler stalls every coroutine in the process.\n- **Fix:** write JSON to stdout and let the platform's log agent ship it, or put slow handlers behind a\n  `QueueHandler` with a `QueueListener` thread.\n- **Reduce volume too:** sample DEBUG, and never log a line per streamed token."}],"tradeoffs":["**Queryable vs readable.** JSON logs aggregate cleanly but are hard to read in a terminal; render with `ConsoleRenderer` in development and switch on an environment flag rather than choosing one forever.","**Debuggability vs data exposure.** Full prompts and completions make debugging easy but store user data and secrets in a system with broad access and long retention. Acceptable only with redaction, sampling and short retention, or by sending payloads to a dedicated LLM tracing store.","**Context vs cost.** Rich context on every line costs bytes and ingestion fees at high volume; sample DEBUG and per-chunk streaming events, keep one summary event per call at INFO.","**structlog vs plain stdlib.** structlog adds a dependency and a configuration step; for a small script or a library, stdlib logging with `extra=` is enough and keeps you dependency-free."],"pitfalls":["**`logging.basicConfig` or handlers inside a library.** Symptom: duplicated lines, or log formats that change with import order in the host application. Fix: `NullHandler` only.","**Request context in a module-level global** instead of contextvars. Symptom: one user's lines carry another user's `request_id` under concurrency. Fix: `bind_contextvars`, cleared at request start.","**`log.error(str(e))` for exceptions.** Symptom: you know *that* something failed but have no traceback, because only `log.exception` or `exc_info=True` captures it. Fix: `log.exception(...)` inside `except`.","**Raw prompts containing API keys, PII or retrieved documents.** Symptom: a security review finds customer data in a log index half the company can query. Fix: a redaction processor; log a hash or prompt version.","**Logging inside `loop.run_in_executor` work.** Symptom: exactly the thread-pool lines lack `request_id` and cannot be joined to their request. Fix: `asyncio.to_thread`, or pass the ID explicitly.","**A log line per streamed token.** Symptom: volume and cost scale with output length and the useful summary line drowns in noise. Fix: one summary event per call with token counts and latency."],"when":"- **Use stdlib logging everywhere** as the routing backbone.\n- **Add structlog when:** a service handles concurrent requests or agent runs, or its logs feed a search\n  or analytics backend; that is nearly every deployed agent.\n- **Do early:** define a small event vocabulary (`llm_call`, `retrieval`, `tool_call`, `guardrail_block`)\n  with consistent field names, because dashboards and alerts are built on them.\n- **Measure:** log volume per request, and that every line carries a request or trace ID.\n- **Interviews:** explain propagation, why libraries do not configure logging, and how contextvars keep\n  async context straight.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures the processor chain, binds request and conversation IDs in middleware, and emits one structured event per model or tool call with tokens and latency.","architect":"Sets the logging contract: field names, what may never be logged, retention, and how logs join traces and cost metrics across services.","fde":"Adapts the output format and redaction rules to the customer's log pipeline and compliance rules, often their SIEM or a mandated JSON schema."},"check":{"q":"Under load, your FastAPI service's logs sometimes show a request's events tagged with another user's\nID. You store the current user in a module-level dict that middleware updates. Why does this happen and\nwhat is the fix?","a":"A module-level dict is shared by every concurrent request in the process. With async handlers,\nrequest A sets the user, awaits the model, request B overwrites the value, and A's later log lines read B's\nuser. The fix is request-scoped storage: `structlog.contextvars.bind_contextvars` (backed by a\n`ContextVar`), which asyncio copies per task, so each request sees its own values across awaits. Also clear\ncontextvars at request start so a reused worker does not inherit stale fields, and add a test that runs\nconcurrent requests and asserts each line's ID matches."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a library add only a NullHandler rather than configure logging itself?","options":["NullHandler makes the library's logging faster than a StreamHandler","Handler and format configuration belong to the application; libraries that configure logging cause duplicated or reformatted output","Libraries are not allowed to use getLogger(__name__)","Records from libraries do not propagate to the root logger"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Library records propagate to the application's handlers, so the app decides where and how they appear. A library that attaches handlers or calls basicConfig overrides that choice and often duplicates lines."},{"q":"What does a structlog processor chain receive and return?","options":["A formatted string, returning a formatted string","A stdlib LogRecord, returning a LogRecord","The event dict, returning a (possibly modified) event dict, with a renderer at the end","A list of handlers, returning the handler to use"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each processor takes the logger, method name and event dict and returns an event dict; the final renderer turns it into JSON or console text. That is what makes redaction and timestamps one-place policy."},{"q":"Why does `log.info(\"retrieved %d chunks\", n)` beat `log.info(f\"retrieved {n} chunks\")` in stdlib logging?","options":["f-strings are not supported by logging handlers","The %-style call writes JSON automatically","Formatting is deferred and skipped when the level is disabled, and the template stays constant for grouping","The f-string version is not thread-safe"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The f-string is built before logging checks the level, wasting work for disabled levels, and it produces a unique message per value, which defeats grouping by message template in log tools."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/memory-and-performance-profiling","sources":["Official docs: Python profile/cProfile and tracemalloc modules","Official docs: py-spy","Official docs: memray","Official docs: Scalene"],"why":"- **Two tickets dominate.** \"The pipeline is slow\" and \"the worker gets OOM-killed after a few hours\", and\n  intuition is a poor guide to either.\n- **Engineers fix the wrong thing.** They rewrite a loop in NumPy when a tokenizer was re-created per\n  request; add a cache when the cost was waiting sequentially on twelve embedding calls; blame \"a memory\n  leak in the model\" when an unbounded `lru_cache` or an untrimmed conversation list holds every request's\n  text.\n- **`time.time()` prints only measure code you already suspect.**\n- **`top` cannot say where.** It reports resident memory (RSS) for the whole process, mixing the Python\n  heap, native allocations, memory-mapped model weights and allocator fragmentation: it shows *that* memory\n  grows, never *where*.\n- **A profiler attributes cost systematically,** including code you did not think to suspect.","how":"### Pick the tool by the question\nThree axes decide it: CPU or wall time, Python heap or native memory, dev box or live process.\n\n| Question | Tool |\n|---|---|\n| CPU-bound or waiting? | `time.perf_counter()` vs `time.process_time()` |\n| Which functions, exact call counts (dev box) | `cProfile` + `pstats` / `snakeviz` |\n| Where is time in a live process | `py-spy` |\n| Python vs native time, memory and copies per line | Scalene |\n| Per-line timing of one known function | `line_profiler` |\n| Which lines' Python allocations grew | `tracemalloc` |\n| Native memory (C extensions, PyTorch CPU, tokenizers) | `memray` |\n| GPU memory | `torch.cuda` memory APIs |\n\n### First question: CPU-bound or waiting?\n- **Compare wall time with CPU time** (`time.perf_counter()` vs `time.process_time()`).\n- **CPU time a small fraction of wall time** means waiting on I/O, locks or a GPU; a CPU profiler will mostly\n  show time inside the event loop's selector.\n- **The fix is then** concurrency, batching or caching, and the right measurement is a trace with spans per\n  remote call, not a function profile.\n- **Agent steps are almost always in this bucket:** a step is a model call plus tool calls, each a network\n  wait, so the first lever is running independent tool calls concurrently and trimming model calls.\n\n### Deterministic profiling: cProfile\n- **How:** `cProfile` (stdlib) hooks every call and return, recording call counts, `tottime` (time inside\n  each function) and `cumtime` (including callees).\n- **Strength:** exact call counts, which is how you discover \"this tokenizer was constructed 10,000 times\".\n- **Cost:** overhead on every call inflates functions that make many small calls, and it attributes poorly\n  across `await` points and threads.\n- **Reading it:** `pstats` sorted by `cumulative` finds the expensive subtree, then by `tottime` the hot\n  leaf; `snakeviz` renders it visually.\n\n### Sampling profilers: py-spy, Scalene\n- **Why low overhead:** a sampler interrupts at intervals and records the current stack, so cost is low and\n  roughly constant, *because* it depends on the sampling rate, not on how many calls you make.\n- **`py-spy` reads the target's memory from outside,** so it attaches to a running production worker without\n  code changes:\n\n| Command / flag | Use |\n|---|---|\n| `py-spy top --pid` | live top-like view |\n| `py-spy record -o flame.svg --pid` | flame graph |\n| `py-spy dump` | stacks of a hung process |\n| `--idle` | include waiting threads |\n| `--native` | include C extension frames |\n\n- **Scalene** separates Python time from native time and reports memory and copy volume per line, which\n  answers \"is my NumPy code actually vectorized?\".\n- **`line_profiler`** (`@profile` plus `kernprof -l`) gives per-line timings once you know which function\n  matters.\n\n### Python heap memory: tracemalloc\n1. `tracemalloc` (stdlib) records a traceback for every allocation through Python's allocator.\n2. Take a snapshot, run the workload, take another.\n3. `compare_to` shows which source lines' live allocations grew: the right tool for growing lists, dicts,\n   caches and closures.\n4. **Then find the retainer.** tracemalloc answers *where* surviving bytes were allocated, not *who keeps\n   them alive*, and the second question is the one you fix. Take one leaked object and walk its referrers\n   with `gc.get_referrers(obj)`, or draw the chain to a module global with `objgraph.show_backrefs`.\n5. **Typical retainers:** a module-level list, a cache, a closure, or a bound-method callback registered on\n   a long-lived object.\n\n- **Limits:** tracemalloc slows the program, and it cannot see allocations native libraries make with their\n  own allocators unless they report them explicitly.\n- **NumPy is the exception:** it registers array data buffers with tracemalloc (via `PyTraceMalloc_Track`),\n  so large arrays do show up. PyTorch CPU tensors, Hugging Face `tokenizers` (Rust) and most C/C++ parsers\n  do not.\n\n### Native and GPU memory\n- **`memray` intercepts allocator calls** (`malloc`, `mmap` and friends), so it sees native allocations (C\n  extensions, PyTorch CPU tensors, tokenizers). It records to a file and draws flame graphs of what was live\n  at peak (`memray run`, then `memray flamegraph`).\n- **GPU memory:** use `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()`, `max_memory_allocated()` and `memory_summary()`. RSS\n  and tracemalloc know nothing about VRAM.\n- **`nvidia-smi` overstates tensor usage,** because the caching allocator keeps freed blocks reserved.\n- **GPU timing trap:** CUDA kernels launch asynchronously, so `perf_counter()` around `model(x)` measures\n  how long it took to *queue* the work. Call `torch.cuda.synchronize()` before reading the clock (or use\n  `torch.cuda.Event` timers or `torch.profiler`); otherwise the cost lands at the first line needing a\n  result on the CPU, such as `.item()`.\n\n### Measuring a fix\n- **Profile tells you where to look; a benchmark tells you whether the change helped.**\n- **Function:** `timeit` with several repeats, compare the minimum, *because* OS and process noise only ever\n  adds time.\n- **Service:** compare latency percentiles (p50 and p95) and throughput under the same load script before\n  and after, warmed up, since a single request mixes in one-off costs.\n\n### Why RSS may not go down\n- **Arenas:** CPython's small-object allocator can return an arena to the OS only when every object in it is\n  freed; native allocators behave similarly.\n- **So** a process that peaked at 6 GB and freed most objects may still report high RSS.\n- **Read the shape:** growth that plateaus is usually fragmentation or a warm cache; growth that never stops\n  under steady load is a leak.","example":"### Find a memory leak and a hot spot in a RAG worker\ntracemalloc compares two snapshots around a burst of requests; cProfile then shows where the CPU time goes.\n\n```python\nimport cProfile, pstats, tracemalloc\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nHISTORY: list[str] = []                          # the bug: grows forever\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)                         # second bug: unbounded cache keyed by full text\ndef normalize(text: str) -> str:\n    return \" \".join(text.lower().split())\n\ndef handle(i: int) -> int:\n    doc = f\"Retrieved chunk {i} \" * 200\n    HISTORY.append(doc)                          # kept \"for debugging\"\n    return len(normalize(doc).split())\n\ntracemalloc.start(10)                            # keep 10 frames per allocation\nbefore = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\nfor i in range(5_000):\n    handle(i)\nafter = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\nfor stat in after.compare_to(before, \"lineno\")[:3]:\n    print(stat)    # top lines by growth: the `doc = ...` line (strings kept alive by HISTORY),\n                   # normalize's join (cached results) and the call site (lru_cache entries)\ntracemalloc.stop()\n\nprof = cProfile.Profile()\nprof.enable()\nfor i in range(5_000, 10_000):\n    handle(i)\nprof.disable()\npstats.Stats(prof).sort_stats(\"cumulative\").print_stats(5)\n# Fix: bound the history (collections.deque(maxlen=100)) and give lru_cache a maxsize,\n# then re-run the snapshot diff and confirm growth is flat across bursts.\n```\n\n### Wall vs CPU time: the first discriminating measurement\nStandard library only. `time.sleep` stands in for waiting on a socket.\n\n```python\nimport time\n\ndef measure(fn, *args):\n    w0, c0 = time.perf_counter(), time.process_time()\n    fn(*args)\n    wall, cpu = time.perf_counter() - w0, time.process_time() - c0\n    verdict = \"waiting: fix with concurrency/batching\" if cpu < 0.5 * wall else \"CPU-bound: profile it\"\n    print(f\"{fn.__name__:<18} wall={wall:.2f}s cpu={cpu:.2f}s  {verdict}\")\n\ndef sequential_calls(n):            # stands in for n embedding calls made one after another\n    for _ in range(n):\n        time.sleep(0.05)            # time.sleep releases the CPU, like waiting on a socket\n\ndef tokenize_loop(n):               # pure-Python work: CPU time tracks wall time\n    text = \"the quick brown fox \" * 50\n    for _ in range(n):\n        sum(len(w) for w in text.split())\n\nmeasure(sequential_calls, 12)       # wall ≈ 0.6s, cpu ≈ 0.00s -> waiting\nmeasure(tokenize_loop, 20_000)      # wall ≈ cpu -> CPU-bound\n```\n\nThe 0.5 threshold is a rule of thumb for the demo, not a standard.\n\n### Agent scenario: session memory that never shrinks\nAn agent server keeps each conversation's transcript in a dict keyed by session ID. tracemalloc shows the\ngrowth line; the fix bounds both the number of sessions and the transcript per session. Standard library\nonly.\n\n```python\nimport tracemalloc\nfrom collections import OrderedDict, deque\n\nSESSIONS: dict[str, list[str]] = {}                      # the bug: no eviction, no cap per session\n\ndef agent_step(sid: str, i: int) -> None:\n    turn = f\"tool result {i}: \" + \"x\" * 2_000             # stands in for a large tool output\n    SESSIONS.setdefault(sid, []).append(turn)\n\ntracemalloc.start()\nbefore = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\nfor i in range(20_000):\n    agent_step(f\"s{i % 2_000}\", i)                        # 2,000 sessions, 10 steps each\nafter = tracemalloc.take_snapshot()\nprint(after.compare_to(before, \"lineno\")[0])              # points at the `turn = ...` line\ntracemalloc.stop()\n\nclass BoundedSessions:                                    # the fix: LRU over sessions, cap per session\n    def __init__(self, max_sessions: int = 500, max_turns: int = 50) -> None:\n        self._d: OrderedDict[str, deque] = OrderedDict()\n        self._max_s, self._max_t = max_sessions, max_turns\n    def append(self, sid: str, turn: str) -> None:\n        q = self._d.pop(sid, None) or deque(maxlen=self._max_t)\n        q.append(turn)\n        self._d[sid] = q                                  # most recent at the end\n        if len(self._d) > self._max_s:\n            self._d.popitem(last=False)                   # evict least recently used session\n```\n\nIn production, durable conversation state belongs in a store (Redis, Postgres or a LangGraph\ncheckpointer); the in-process structure should only be a bounded cache.\n\n### Worked scenario: an agent step that \"needed a faster model\"\nA support agent's step took about 8 s at p95. Wall versus CPU time and a trace told a different story\n(illustrative numbers from one run).\n\n| Measurement | Value | Inference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Step wall time | ~8 s | user-visible |\n| Step CPU time in the worker | ~0.3 s | over 95% of the step is waiting |\n| Trace: model call | ~2 s | not the main cost |\n| Trace: 3 independent tool calls, sequential | ~2 s each | ~6 s spent waiting one after another |\n| After running them with `asyncio.gather` | ~2 s for all three | step drops to ~4 s, same model |\n\n### Worked scenario: the OOM-killed ingestion worker\nSame facts as Check yourself, walked as a diagnosis.\n\n| Observation | Inference | Next step |\n|---|---|---|\n| RSS 1.5 GB → 9 GB over six hours | memory grows under steady load | find out where |\n| tracemalloc heap flat at ~300 MB | growth is outside Python's allocator (NumPy would have shown) | `memray run` in a staging replica |\n| memray peak shows parser buffers held by a document cache | native objects kept alive by a Python reference | bound or drop the cache |\n| RSS after fix | flat, or plateaus | if it plateaus, recycle workers after N jobs |","patterns":["**Trace every agent step before tuning it.** Give each model call, tool call and retrieval its own span (LangSmith, Langfuse or OpenTelemetry) and look at the waterfall; sequential independent spans are the most common fix. Monitor p95 step latency and tool-span share per agent.","**Bound agent state in the process.** Cap steps per run and turns per session, compact old turns, and evict idle sessions to an external store. Monitor RSS per worker against active sessions; RSS that grows while sessions stay flat means something else is retaining transcripts.","**Attach py-spy to a misbehaving worker.** `py-spy record -o flame.svg --pid <PID>` (add `--idle` for waiting threads) or `py-spy dump --pid <PID>` for a hang. In containers this needs ptrace permission, for example adding the `SYS_PTRACE` capability to a debug pod. Keep sessions short and watch overhead.","**Memory profiling in staging, not prod.** Replay a recorded workload under `memray run` and compare the peak flame graph with the previous release; keep tracemalloc and cProfile out of production builds.","**Recycle workers when growth plateaus.** For fragmentation you cannot remove, restart workers after N jobs: gunicorn `--max-requests` with `--max-requests-jitter`, or Celery's max-tasks-per-child setting. Monitor RSS per worker over time and alert on slope, not on a single high value.","**Log GPU peak per batch.** Call `torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()` before a batch and log `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()` after it, so capacity planning uses measured peaks rather than `nvidia-smi`.","**Gate optimizations on a benchmark.** Keep a load script and compare p50, p95 and throughput on a warmed process before and after each change; for a function, `timeit` with repeats and compare the minimum.","**Trace remote calls.** When wall time far exceeds CPU time, add spans per LLM, embedding and vector-store call; the fix is usually concurrency or batching, visible in the trace waterfall."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** \"The agent is slow\" is usually sequential tool or model calls, not\n  Python, and \"the agent server gets OOM-killed\" is usually transcripts, caches or session state that\n  never get evicted. Profiling tells you which, before you rewrite the wrong thing.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Trace waterfalls in LangSmith, Langfuse or an OpenTelemetry\n  backend answer \"where did this step's time go\" across model and tool calls; `py-spy` answers it inside\n  one Python worker; `tracemalloc` and `memray` explain RSS growth in long-lived agent servers; the\n  `torch.cuda` APIs cover self-hosted embedding or reranker models.\n- **What you control.** Whether independent tool calls run concurrently, how session memory is bounded\n  (step caps, compaction, TTL eviction), worker recycling, and which benchmarks gate a performance change.\n- **What you can skip.** Micro-optimizing prompt-building or JSON parsing code. In an agent it is rarely\n  more than a sliver of a step that waits seconds on a model.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **\"The agent is slow\" tickets.** A trace waterfall shows whether time goes to sequential tool calls,\n  model latency or Python work before anyone rewrites code.\n- **OOM-killed agent servers.** `tracemalloc` snapshots or `memray` show whether growth comes from\n  unbounded transcripts, caches or native libraries in a long-lived worker.\n- **Parallelising independent tool calls.** Profiling before and after running independent calls\n  concurrently gives a measured latency win instead of a guess.\n- **Self-hosted embedders and rerankers.** The `torch.cuda` memory APIs show whether a batch size fits on\n  the GPU next to other models.","mini_project":"**Profile a slow, leaky agent worker (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find and fix one latency problem and one memory problem in a small agent service.\n- **Steps:** build an agent with three independent fake tools that each sleep, called one after another,\n  and a session store that keeps every transcript forever; time 50 runs; attach `py-spy` with `--idle`\n  to confirm where the worker waits (without it, sleeping threads are left out); take `tracemalloc` snapshots before and after 500 sessions and compare top allocations;\n  run the independent tools concurrently and cap or evict session history.\n- **Done when:** you have a before/after table of p50 run latency and process memory, the snapshot diff\n  points at the session store, and memory stays flat after the fix."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Symptom\"] --> T{\"Slow or memory growth?\"}\n    T -->|\"slow\"| W{\"CPU time well below wall time?\"}\n    W -->|\"yes: waiting\"| TR[\"Trace spans per remote call, then batch or parallelize\"]\n    W -->|\"no: CPU-bound\"| PS[\"py-spy or Scalene sampling profile\"]\n    PS --> CP[\"cProfile or line_profiler on the hot function\"]\n    T -->|\"memory\"| R{\"RSS keeps climbing under steady load?\"}\n    R -->|\"plateaus\"| FR[\"Fragmentation or warm cache: recycle workers\"]\n    R -->|\"climbs\"| TM{\"tracemalloc snapshot diff grows?\"}\n    TM -->|\"yes\"| RT[\"Find the retainer: gc.get_referrers, objgraph\"]\n    TM -->|\"no, heap flat\"| MR[\"memray for native buffers, torch.cuda APIs for GPU\"]","caption":"The decision path: start from the symptom, use one cheap discriminating measurement, then pick the profiler\nthat can actually see the cost (Python time, native memory or GPU memory)."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a deterministic profiler and a sampling profiler, and when do you use each?","a":"- **Deterministic (`cProfile`):** hooks every call and return, giving exact call counts, which is how you find\n  a tokenizer constructed 10,000 times; the per-call overhead distorts call-heavy code.\n- **Sampling (`py-spy`, Scalene):** records the stack at intervals, so overhead is low and constant and it is\n  safe to attach to a live production worker.\n- **Typical order:** sample first to find the hot area, then a deterministic or line profile of that function."},{"q":"A RAG agent's p95 is 3 s. How do you find out where the time goes?","a":"- **Start with wall vs CPU time:** if `time.process_time()` is a small fraction of `time.perf_counter()`, the\n  request is waiting on network or GPU, not computing.\n- **If waiting:** add trace spans per LLM, embedding and vector-store call; sequential remote calls are the\n  usual culprit, fixed by concurrency or batching.\n- **If CPU-bound:** take a py-spy flame graph, then confirm the fix with p50 and p95 under the same load script."},{"q":"Your long-running agent server's RSS climbs all day and it gets OOM-killed overnight. How do you approach it?","a":"- **Confirm the slope:** RSS that climbs under steady load, not a plateau, while active sessions stay\n  roughly constant.\n- **Diff the Python heap:** tracemalloc snapshots around a burst of runs usually point at transcript,\n  tool-output or cache lines; then find the retainer, often a module-level session dict or an unbounded\n  `lru_cache`.\n- **Fix and verify:** cap turns per session, evict idle sessions to an external store, re-run the\n  diff; if the heap is flat but RSS still grows, use `memray` for native buffers."},{"q":"Why can't you trust `perf_counter()` around a PyTorch forward pass on a GPU?","a":"- **Kernels launch asynchronously,** so the timer measures how long it took to queue the work.\n- **The cost moves** to the first line that needs a CPU-side result, such as `.item()` or `.cpu()`.\n- **Fix:** call `torch.cuda.synchronize()` before reading the clock, or use `torch.cuda.Event` timers or\n  `torch.profiler`."},{"q":"When would you recycle workers instead of hunting a memory problem further?","a":"- **When growth plateaus:** under steady load that points to allocator fragmentation or a warm cache rather\n  than an unbounded leak, since CPython can return an arena to the OS only when every object in it is freed.\n- **How:** gunicorn `--max-requests` with `--max-requests-jitter`, or Celery's max-tasks-per-child setting.\n- **When not to:** growth that never stops is a real leak; recycling only hides it and the slope stays."},{"q":"`nvidia-smi` shows the GPU nearly full but your model should fit easily. What do you check?","a":"- **The caching allocator:** PyTorch keeps freed blocks reserved, so `nvidia-smi` overstates tensor usage.\n- **Measure what tensors use:** `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()`, `max_memory_allocated()` and\n  `memory_summary()`.\n- **For capacity planning,** call `torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()` before a batch and log the peak\n  after it, rather than reading `nvidia-smi`."}],"tradeoffs":["**Exact counts vs honest timings.** Deterministic profilers give exact call counts but distort call-heavy code; sampling profilers keep timings honest and are safe in production, but miss rare short calls and give no exact counts.","**Line precision vs coverage.** tracemalloc pinpoints Python-object leaks to a line but slows the program noticeably and misses native memory; switch to memray once RSS grows while tracemalloc's totals stay flat.","**Real traffic vs access.** Profiling in production (py-spy attach) shows real behaviour, but needs `ptrace`-style permissions that hardened containers often deny; locally reproduced load is easier but may not trigger the real hot path.","**Micro-optimization vs remote calls.** Halving a hot Python function's time does nothing for the user if wall time is dominated by remote calls; batching or parallelizing those calls is the larger lever."],"pitfalls":["**Profiling the agent worker when the time is in remote calls.** Symptom: a flame graph full of event loop and SSL frames, and no speedup after optimizing Python. Fix: look at the trace waterfall for sequential model and tool spans first.","**cProfile on an I/O-bound async service,** then \"optimizing\" the top entry. Symptom: the selector or event loop dominates and latency does not improve. Fix: measure wall vs CPU first, then trace.","**Treating high RSS after a peak as a leak.** Symptom: memory plateaus under steady load rather than climbing, which points to fragmentation or a cache, not unbounded growth. Fix: judge the slope over hours, and recycle workers if it plateaus.","**Measuring once, on a cold process.** Symptom: profiles dominated by imports, model loading and JIT or CUDA initialization that never recur in steady state. Fix: warm up, then profile steady-state traffic.","**Timing GPU code without `torch.cuda.synchronize()`.** Symptom: a forward pass \"takes 0.2 ms\" while an innocent `.item()` or `.cpu()` a few lines later appears to take hundreds of milliseconds. Fix: synchronize before reading the clock, or use `torch.cuda.Event` timers.","**Stopping at tracemalloc's allocation line.** Symptom: a fix to the allocating code (a smaller string, a copy avoided) shrinks each leaked object but memory climbs at the same slope. Fix: find the retainer.","**Reading `nvidia-smi` as tensor usage.** Symptom: apparent \"GPU leaks\" that are the PyTorch caching allocator holding reserved but free blocks. Fix: read `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()` instead.","**tracemalloc or a deterministic profiler left on in production.** Symptom: a large, otherwise unexplained latency regression. Fix: keep them to dev and staging; use py-spy sampling in production."],"when":"- **Use when:** before optimizing, and whenever a latency SLO, a cost target or an OOM is in play.\n- **Order for time:** wall vs CPU time, then a sampling profile, then a deterministic or line profile of the\n  function that matters.\n- **Order for memory:** watch RSS over time under steady load; if it climbs, tracemalloc snapshot diffs for\n  Python objects, memray for native buffers, the CUDA memory APIs for GPU.\n- **Measure:** after every fix, confirm it moved the number you cared about.\n- **Interviews:** distinguishing CPU-bound from waiting, and Python heap from native and GPU memory, is the\n  core skill.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs py-spy on a slow worker, uses tracemalloc diffs to find the unbounded cache, and verifies each fix with a before-and-after measurement.","architect":"Decides where profiling hooks and memory limits live, and sets the capacity model from measured per-request CPU, memory and GPU cost rather than estimates.","fde":"Diagnoses performance inside the customer's environment, often without root access, so relies on sampling, logs of wall vs CPU time and reproducible load scripts."},"check":{"q":"A document-ingestion worker's RSS climbs from 1.5 GB to 9 GB over six hours and the pod is\nOOM-killed. tracemalloc shows the Python heap stable at about 300 MB. How do you proceed?","a":"A stable traced heap with growing RSS means the growth is outside what tracemalloc sees: native\nbuffers (PDF parsers, PyTorch tensors, tokenizers; NumPy arrays would have shown up because NumPy reports\nthem to tracemalloc), memory-mapped files, or fragmentation. I would run the worker under\nmemray, or attach it in a staging replica, and look at what is live at the high-water mark, checking for\nnative objects kept alive by Python references such as a cache of parsed documents or arrays. I would also\ncheck whether growth plateaus (fragmentation, possibly mitigated by recycling workers after N jobs) or is\nunbounded (a real leak). Then fix, rerun the same workload, and confirm RSS stays flat."},"quiz":[{"q":"An async RAG endpoint's cProfile output is dominated by the event loop's select call. What does that indicate?","options":["The event loop implementation is inefficient and should be replaced","The process spends most wall time waiting on I/O, so CPU optimization will not help much","cProfile cannot run on async code at all","There is a memory leak in the selector"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Time in the selector is time spent waiting for sockets. The levers are concurrency, batching and caching of remote calls, measured with traces; replacing the loop rarely moves waiting-dominated latency."},{"q":"Why can py-spy safely profile a live production process when cProfile usually is not used there?","options":["py-spy only records memory, not CPU","cProfile requires a debug build of Python","py-spy samples stacks from outside the process at low constant overhead, while cProfile instruments every call","py-spy rewrites bytecode to remove its own overhead"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sampling cost depends on the sampling rate, not on how many calls the program makes, and py-spy reads the target's memory externally, so no code change or restart is needed."},{"q":"RSS keeps growing but tracemalloc's traced total is flat. Most likely explanation?","options":["tracemalloc is reporting incorrectly and should be restarted","Python's garbage collector is disabled","The leak is in a Python list that tracemalloc ignores","Memory is allocated by native code or the allocator is fragmented, outside what tracemalloc traces"],"answer":3,"explanation":"tracemalloc sees allocations through Python's allocator. Native libraries using their own allocators, mmaps and fragmentation all raise RSS invisibly to it, which is when a native-aware tool like memray helps."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/uv-poetry-pip","sources":["Official docs: pip","Official docs: Poetry","Official docs: uv (Astral)","PEP 621 — Storing project metadata in pyproject.toml"],"why":"- **A project needs four things.** A list of what it depends on, an exact resolved set of versions satisfying\n  all constraints, an isolated environment holding them, and a way to build and publish itself. An AI\n  service's stack (SDKs, torch, tokenizers) is deep and fast-moving, so drift between machines is the norm.\n- **pip historically did only the install step.** It has a real backtracking resolver, but out of the box it\n  records no lockfile, creates no environments, and does not distinguish \"what I asked for\" from \"what I\n  ended up with\".\n- **Standard locking is new.** `pylock.toml` was accepted as PEP 751 only in 2025, and pip's `pip lock`\n  command that writes it is still marked experimental; most teams get locking from pip-tools, Poetry or uv.\n- **`pip freeze > requirements.txt` mixes concerns.** It captures whatever is installed, including experiment\n  leftovers, for one OS and one Python version, with no record of which packages were direct requirements.\n  Upgrading one library means hand-editing pins and hoping the set is still consistent.\n- **The fix is separation.** pip-tools, Poetry and uv separate *intent* (ranges in `pyproject.toml`) from\n  *resolution* (an exact lockfile), and make recreating the environment one deterministic command.","how":"### What every tool is doing underneath\nAll three run the same pipeline, and knowing it explains most failures:\n1. **Collect constraints.** Your direct requirements (`openai>=1.40`) plus, recursively, each candidate\n   version's own declared dependencies and environment markers (`; sys_platform == \"linux\"`).\n2. **Resolve.** Choose one version per package so every constraint holds at once. This is a search\n   problem, NP-hard in general, so resolvers backtrack: pick the newest candidate, follow its\n   dependencies, and on a conflict undo choices and try older versions. pip uses the `resolvelib`\n   backtracking resolver; Poetry and uv both use PubGrub-style solvers, which record *why* a conflict\n   happened so they can skip whole families of doomed versions and print an explanation chain.\n3. **Pick artifacts.** For each chosen version, find a wheel whose tags match the interpreter and platform\n   (a filename such as `...-cp312-cp312-manylinux_2_17_x86_64.whl` encodes Python ABI and OS), else fall\n   back to the source distribution and build it locally.\n4. **Install.** Unpack into the environment's `site-packages` and write the record of what was installed.\n\nTwo consequences follow:\n- **Resolution is slow when metadata is expensive.** An sdist with a dynamic `setup.py` must be *built* just\n  to learn its dependencies, which is why a resolve can stall on one old package.\n- **The answer depends on the platform,** *because* markers and available wheels differ between a Mac laptop\n  and a Linux container: the root of \"the lock works for me but not in CI\".\n\n### pip (+ venv, + pip-tools)\n- **Environment:** `python -m venv .venv` creates an isolated interpreter environment.\n- **Install:** `pip install -r requirements.txt` resolves and installs from PyPI or another index.\n- **Wheels first:** pip prefers wheels (pre-built archives) and falls back to building source distributions,\n  which is where compiler and CUDA errors come from.\n- **pip-tools:** `pip-compile` turns a short `requirements.in` (or `pyproject.toml`) into a fully pinned,\n  optionally hashed `requirements.txt`; `pip-sync` makes the environment match it exactly.\n- **Role:** the minimal, widely understood baseline; every other tool can export to it.\n\n### Poetry\n| Command | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `poetry add httpx` | updates `pyproject.toml`, re-resolves, writes `poetry.lock` |\n| `poetry install` | creates or reuses a venv, installs exactly what the lock says |\n| dependency groups | separate dev and test tools |\n| `poetry build` / `poetry publish` | packaging |\n\n- **Standards:** recent major versions read PEP 621 `[project]` metadata, not only `[tool.poetry]` tables.\n- **Strength:** a mature, integrated workflow for libraries and applications.\n- **Historical weaknesses:** resolution speed on large graphs and friction with non-PyPI wheel indexes.\n\n### uv\nuv, from Astral, is written in Rust and covers the whole toolchain:\n\n| Area | Commands and behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| **Project workflow** | `uv init`, `uv add`, `uv lock` (writes `uv.lock`), `uv sync` (makes `.venv` match the lock), `uv run <cmd>` (syncs if needed, then runs in the environment) |\n| **CI flags** | `uv sync --locked` fails if the lock is out of date with `pyproject.toml`; `--frozen` uses the lock without checking |\n| **Universal lockfile** | `uv.lock` records resolutions for all platforms and Python versions the project supports, so one lock serves macOS laptops and Linux containers |\n| **Python versions** | `uv python install 3.12` downloads interpreters, replacing pyenv for many teams |\n| **pip-compatible interface** | `uv pip install`, `uv pip compile`, `uv pip sync` are drop-ins for requirements-based projects, so adoption is incremental |\n| **Tools and scripts** | `uvx ruff` runs a CLI in a throwaway environment; `uv run script.py` honors inline script metadata (PEP 723) |\n| **Indexes** | per-package index pinning (`[tool.uv.sources]`) installs `torch` from a CUDA-specific index while everything else comes from PyPI |\n\nuv is fast *because* of:\n- a parallel resolver and downloader;\n- a global cache whose files are cloned (copy-on-write, the default on macOS), hard-linked (the default on\n  Linux) or copied into environments instead of being re-downloaded and re-unpacked;\n- reading wheel metadata without fetching whole wheels where indexes allow.\n\nEffect: recreating an environment is cheap enough to do on every CI run and in every Docker build.","example":"### The same project, three ways\nA FastAPI RAG service needs `openai`, `httpx` and `torch` (CPU wheels in CI), plus `pytest` as a dev\ndependency.\n\n| | pip + pip-tools | Poetry | uv |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Declare | `requirements.in` with `openai>=1.40`, `httpx`, `torch`; `dev.in` with `-c requirements.txt` and `pytest` | `poetry add openai httpx torch`; `poetry add --group dev pytest` | `uv init`; `uv add openai httpx torch`; `uv add --dev pytest` |\n| Lock | `pip-compile --generate-hashes requirements.in` → pinned, hashed `requirements.txt` | `poetry.lock` | `uv.lock` |\n| CI | `python -m venv .venv` then `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt` | `poetry install` | `uv sync --locked` then `uv run pytest` |\n| Torch CPU index | an index option in the requirements file; pip cannot scope an index to one package | a configured source and an explicit source on that dependency | `[tool.uv.sources]` (below) |\n| Catch | two files per environment; a Mac developer needing different wheels may need a separately compiled file | slower resolves on large graphs | pin the uv version, since features move quickly |\n\nRoute only `torch` to the PyTorch CPU index with uv:\n\n```toml\n[[tool.uv.index]]\nname = \"pytorch-cpu\"\nurl = \"https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu\"\nexplicit = true            # used only by packages that name it, never as a general fallback\n\n[tool.uv.sources]\ntorch = { index = \"pytorch-cpu\" }\n```\n\n- **`explicit = true` matters** *because* an index searched for every package lets a same-named package on it\n  shadow the PyPI one, the dependency-confusion pattern.\n- **Commit** `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`. The Docker build copies those two files first and runs\n  `uv sync --locked --no-dev`, so the dependency layer is cached until the lock changes.\n- **The rule in all three:** humans edit ranges, a tool writes the exact lock, and CI and production install\n  only from the lock.\n\n### Docker build that caches the dependency layer\nA common uv layout: dependencies install from the two lock files before the source is copied, so code\nchanges do not reinstall packages.\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM python:3.12-slim\n# pin an exact uv release\nCOPY --from=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:0.5.11 /uv /uvx /bin/\nWORKDIR /app\nENV UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE=1 UV_LINK_MODE=copy\n# dependency layer: rebuilt only when the lock changes\nCOPY pyproject.toml uv.lock ./\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/uv \\\n    uv sync --locked --no-dev --no-install-project\n# the project itself (list .venv in .dockerignore so a local venv is not copied over)\nCOPY . .\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/uv \\\n    uv sync --locked --no-dev\nCMD [\"/app/.venv/bin/python\", \"-m\", \"myapp.entrypoints.api\"]\n```\n\n### A one-file script that declares its own dependencies\nPEP 723 inline metadata; `uv run fetch_version.py` creates a throwaway environment with `httpx` and runs it.\n\n```python\n# /// script\n# requires-python = \">=3.12\"\n# dependencies = [\"httpx>=0.27\"]\n# ///\nimport httpx\n\ninfo = httpx.get(\"https://pypi.org/pypi/openai/json\", timeout=10).json()[\"info\"]\nprint(info[\"name\"], info[\"version\"])\n```\n\n### Agent scenario: one lock for the service, the evals and the sandbox\nAn agent service built on LangGraph keeps runtime, dev and eval dependencies apart, so the production image\nstays small while eval runners use the identical runtime versions. Package names are real; version ranges\nare illustrative.\n\n```toml\n[project]\nname = \"support-agent\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.12\"\ndependencies = [\n    \"langgraph>=0.2\",\n    \"langchain-openai>=0.2\",\n    \"httpx>=0.27\",\n    \"structlog>=24\",\n]\n\n[dependency-groups]\ndev = [\"pytest>=8\", \"ruff>=0.6\"]\nevals = [\"langsmith>=0.1\", \"pandas>=2\"]\n```\n\n| Where | Command | Gets |\n|---|---|---|\n| Production image | `uv sync --locked --no-dev` | runtime dependencies only, exact lock versions |\n| CI unit tests | `uv sync --locked` then `uv run pytest` | runtime plus the default `dev` group |\n| Eval runner | `uv sync --locked --group evals` | runtime plus the default `dev` group and eval tooling (add `--no-dev` to drop dev tools), same runtime versions as production |\n| Agent-generated script | `uv run --with pandas analysis.py` in the sandbox | a throwaway environment, nothing added to the service |\n| Framework upgrade | `uv lock --upgrade-package langgraph` in its own pull request | one package moved, eval suite decides whether it merges |\n\n### Worked scenario: reading a resolution failure\nRead the explanation rather than loosening pins at random. Illustrative case:\n\n| Step | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Constraint A | the service needs `pydantic>=2` |\n| Constraint B | an older internal helper library declares `pydantic<2` |\n| Resolver | no assignment satisfies both; a PubGrub-style resolver reports the chain (\"because helper==0.3 depends on pydantic<2 and you require pydantic>=2, helper==0.3 cannot be used...\") |\n| Fix 1 (best) | upgrade the helper to a release that supports pydantic 2 |\n| Fix 2 | drop the helper |\n| Fix 3 (last resort) | an override, owning the risk of running it against a version it never declared support for |","patterns":["**Upgrade agent frameworks one at a time, behind evals.** Move `langgraph`, `langchain-*` or a provider SDK with a targeted lock upgrade in its own pull request, run the agent eval suite on it, and merge only if task success and tool-call validity hold. Track which framework versions each deployed agent runs.","**Separate groups for evals and dev.** Keep eval tooling (LangSmith client, dataframes, notebooks) in a dependency group so the production image stays lean while evals run the identical runtime lock.","**Locked installs everywhere.** CI runs `uv sync --locked` (or `poetry install` / `pip-sync` from a compiled file) so an edited `pyproject.toml` without a re-lock fails the build. Monitor for lock drift failures as a signal that developers skip `uv lock`.","**Pin the tool, not just the packages.** Pin the uv (or Poetry) version in CI and in the Docker image, for example via the version input of the setup action or an exact image tag, so a tool upgrade does not change lock behaviour under you. Upgrade it deliberately in its own pull request.","**Explicit per-package indexes.** Route `torch` and private packages to named indexes with `explicit = true` instead of `--extra-index-url`. Check images for the expected wheel variant (`torch.cuda.is_available()` on GPU hosts, image size on CPU ones).","**Export for platforms that want requirements files.** Keep uv or Poetry as the source of truth and generate a pinned `requirements.txt` for the customer's mirror or platform from the lock, never by hand.","**Lowest-bound CI job for libraries.** Run tests once with `--resolution lowest-direct` so declared lower bounds are real. Watch it like any other required check.","**Refuse installs outside a venv.** Set `PIP_REQUIRE_VIRTUALENV=1` on developer machines and build hosts so pip refuses to install into a system or base interpreter."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent's behaviour depends on library versions you did not write:\n  `langchain-core`, `langgraph`, `openai`, `anthropic`, tokenizers. A minor bump can change tool-call\n  parsing, default retries or message formats, so the agent that passed evals must run on exactly the\n  versions it was evaluated with.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `uv add langgraph langchain-openai langsmith` writes ranges to\n  `pyproject.toml` and exact versions to `uv.lock`; `uv sync --locked` rebuilds that environment in CI,\n  in the agent's Docker image and in eval runners. `uvx` and PEP 723 scripts give code-executing agents a\n  throwaway environment per script: a dependency boundary, not a security sandbox.\n- **What you control.** Which packages are direct dependencies, dependency groups for dev and evals,\n  per-package indexes (CUDA `torch` for a self-hosted embedder), the tool version, and when upgrades\n  happen: in their own pull request, gated on the eval suite.\n- **What you can skip.** Resolver internals beyond \"constraints, markers, wheel tags\". Poetry versus uv\n  arguments; pick one and lock.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Reproducible agent images.** `uv sync --locked` in the Dockerfile builds the agent service with exactly\n  the versions that passed evals.\n- **Eval runners that match production.** A dependency group for eval tooling, synced from the same lock,\n  keeps the eval harness on the service's exact framework and SDK versions.\n- **Throwaway environments for code-executing agents.** `uvx` and PEP 723 scripts give each generated\n  script its own isolated environment instead of polluting the agent's.\n- **Gated framework upgrades.** A `langgraph` or provider SDK bump lands in its own pull request and merges\n  only when the eval suite passes.","mini_project":"**Set up a locked agent project end to end (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** create an agent project whose local, CI and container environments are identical.\n- **Steps:** `uv init` a project, `uv add` an agent framework and a provider SDK, and add a dev group with\n  pytest and an eval group; commit `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`; write a Dockerfile that runs\n  `uv sync --locked` and a CI job that does the same and runs a tiny offline agent test; then upgrade one\n  package in a separate branch and compare the lock diff.\n- **Done when:** CI and the image fail if the lock is stale, the agent test passes in both, and the upgrade\n  branch shows exactly which direct and transitive versions moved."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"pyproject.toml: ranges\"] --> C[\"Collect constraints and markers\"]\n    C --> RS[\"Resolve: backtracking or PubGrub\"]\n    RS -->|\"conflict\"| X[\"Explanation chain: fix the constraint\"]\n    RS -->|\"one version per package\"| L[(\"Lockfile\")]\n    L --> D1[\"Laptop: uv sync\"]\n    L --> D2[\"CI: uv sync --locked\"]\n    L --> D3[\"Docker: uv sync --locked --no-dev\"]\n    D1 --> A[\"Pick wheel by tag, else build sdist\"]\n    D2 --> A\n    D3 --> A\n    A --> V[\"Install into .venv site-packages\"]","caption":"Humans edit intent, a resolver turns it into an exact lock (`uv.lock`, `poetry.lock` or a pinned\n`requirements.txt`), and every environment (laptop, CI, Docker) is built only from that lock; markers and\nwheel tags are what make the resolve and the artifact choice platform-specific."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between declaring dependencies and locking them?","a":"- **Declaring is intent:** ranges such as `openai>=1.40` in `pyproject.toml`, edited by humans.\n- **Locking is resolution:** one exact version (and usually hashes) for every direct and transitive package,\n  written by a tool into `uv.lock`, `poetry.lock` or a compiled `requirements.txt`.\n- **Why separate them:** upgrades re-resolve a consistent set in one command, and CI and production install\n  exactly what was tested."},{"q":"Why might a lock that works on your Mac fail in a Linux CI container?","a":"- **Markers and wheels differ by platform:** a dependency may apply only on one OS, and the available wheel\n  tags (for example `manylinux_2_17_x86_64`) differ between machines.\n- **A single-platform lock** (such as a `pip freeze` or a pip-compile run on macOS) encodes the Mac answer.\n- **Fixes:** a universal `uv.lock`, or compile the requirements file for the target platform."},{"q":"Your Docker image is several gigabytes larger than expected after adding `torch`. What happened and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the default index supplied the CUDA build of `torch` in a CPU-only image.\n- **Fix with uv:** declare the PyTorch CPU index with `explicit = true` and route only `torch` to it via\n  `[tool.uv.sources]`.\n- **Avoid `--extra-index-url`** for this, because it treats both indexes as equals and opens the\n  dependency-confusion path."},{"q":"An agent passed evals last week, but the same commit misbehaves in a fresh deploy. How do you check whether dependencies are the cause?","a":"- **Compare environments:** was the deploy built with `uv sync --locked` from the committed `uv.lock`,\n  or did it resolve fresh and pick newer `langchain-core`, `openai` or tokenizer versions?\n- **Diff installed versions** between the eval runner and the image (`uv pip list` or `pip freeze` in\n  each), focusing on the framework and provider SDKs.\n- **Fix the process:** install only from the lock everywhere, and run framework upgrades as their own\n  pull requests gated on the eval suite."},{"q":"When would you not migrate a team from Poetry to uv?","a":"- **When Poetry is used well:** locks committed, CI installs from them, and builds are fast enough; migration\n  buys speed but costs churn across repos and habits.\n- **When the platform dictates the format:** if a customer mirror accepts only requirements files, the\n  export step matters more than the tool.\n- **Migrate when** resolution speed, Python version management or per-package indexes are real pain."},{"q":"How do you keep an agent service's Docker builds fast when dependencies rarely change?","a":"- **Copy only `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` first** and run `uv sync --locked --no-dev --no-install-project`,\n  so that layer is cached until the lock changes.\n- **Copy the source afterwards** and run the final sync, so code edits do not reinstall packages.\n- **Use a cache mount** for the uv cache and pin an exact uv image tag so tool upgrades are deliberate."}],"tradeoffs":["**pip + pip-tools: portable vs assembled by hand.** Universally understood, but you assemble venv, pinning, syncing and Python versions yourself; wins when tooling is already built around requirements files.","**Poetry: integrated vs slower.** A mature workflow with good publishing support, but slower on large graphs and with its own conventions; reasonable for teams already standardized on it.","**uv: fast vs young.** Dramatically faster and replaces several tools, but new features have evolved quickly; pin the uv version in CI so a tool upgrade does not change lock behaviour under you.","**Newest versions vs tested lower bounds.** Resolving to the newest allowed versions is right for applications, but a library's lower bounds are then never tested; run CI once with uv's `--resolution lowest-direct` so `httpx>=0.23` means what it claims.","**Universal lock vs extra conflicts.** One file serves every platform, but resolves for platforms you may never deploy to, which can surface conflicts (often around CUDA wheels) a single-platform lock never hits."],"pitfalls":["**Upgrading `langchain`, `langgraph` and provider SDKs in one sweep.** Symptom: an eval regression nobody can attribute to a single package. Fix: one framework per upgrade pull request, each gated on the agent eval suite.","**Installing into the system or base conda interpreter.** Symptom: one project's upgrade breaks another, permission errors on `pip install`, or (on distro and Homebrew Pythons that adopt PEP 668) pip refusing with `externally-managed-environment`. Fix: a project environment.","**Committing `pyproject.toml` but not the lockfile** for an application. Symptom: CI and production resolve newer transitive versions than developers tested. Fix: commit the lock and install with `--locked`.","**Mixing tools in one project** (`pip install` inside a uv- or Poetry-managed venv). Symptom: the environment drifts from the lock and is silently reset, or reports a different set, on the next sync. Fix: add packages through the project tool (`uv add`, `poetry add`).","**`--extra-index-url` for one private package.** Symptom: public and private indexes are treated as equals, so a public package with the same name (and a higher version) can replace yours. Fix: a named index with `explicit = true`, pinned per package.","**Letting the default index provide `torch`.** Symptom: a multi-gigabyte CUDA wheel in a CPU-only image, or a CPU-only build on a GPU host, discovered as slow images or `torch.cuda.is_available()` being False. Fix: route `torch` to the matching PyTorch index via `[tool.uv.sources]`."],"when":"- **New agent service or application:** uv is a strong default: one tool for Python versions, environments,\n  locking and running, fast enough for CI and Docker.\n- **Team already on Poetry:** keep it if used well; migrating buys speed but costs churn.\n- **Platform expects requirements files:** pip with pip-tools, or uv's pip interface.\n- **Measure:** a fresh clone plus one command reproduces the exact environment CI tested.\n- **Interviews:** explain the separation of intent from resolution rather than tool preferences.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Uses `uv add`/`uv sync` daily, keeps dev tools in a dev group, and configures the torch index so local and CI environments get the right wheels.","architect":"Chooses one toolchain across repos and defines how locks are updated, reviewed and consumed in CI and container builds.","fde":"Works inside whatever the customer mandates, often pip against a private mirror, and exports a pinned requirements file from uv or Poetry when that is the only accepted format."},"check":{"q":"Your service's CI installs with `pip install -r requirements.txt` generated by `pip freeze` on a\ndeveloper's Mac. Linux builds fail intermittently and images contain unexpected packages. What would you\nchange, and why does it fix both symptoms?","a":"A freeze captures one machine's whole environment: experiment leftovers, macOS-specific packages and\nmarkers, and no distinction between direct and transitive dependencies. I would declare direct dependencies\nwith ranges in `pyproject.toml`, generate a lock with a resolver (uv lock, Poetry, or pip-compile run for\nthe target platform), commit it, and have CI install only from it (`uv sync --locked`). The lock contains\nonly what the declared dependencies need, so stray packages disappear, and it is resolved for Linux, so\nplatform mismatches stop."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `uv sync --locked` do in CI that plain `uv sync` does not?","options":["It skips installing dev dependencies","It fails if uv.lock is out of date with pyproject.toml instead of silently re-locking","It installs packages globally instead of into .venv","It upgrades every dependency to the newest allowed version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"--locked asserts the lockfile already matches the declared dependencies, so a developer who edited pyproject.toml without re-locking gets a failing build rather than CI resolving a new, untested set."},{"q":"Why is `pip freeze > requirements.txt` a poor lockfile for a deployed service?","options":["pip freeze cannot output version numbers","pip refuses to install files generated by freeze","It captures the whole local environment for one platform, without separating direct from transitive dependencies","It always includes hashes that break installation on other machines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A freeze records whatever is installed, including unrelated packages, resolved on one OS and Python version. A resolver-generated lock is derived from declared requirements and can target the deploy platform."},{"q":"A main reason uv installs are fast on repeated builds is that it:","options":["Skips dependency resolution entirely","Downloads only source distributions and compiles in parallel","Reuses a global cache, linking or copying files into environments instead of re-downloading them","Installs packages without metadata"],"answer":2,"explanation":"uv still resolves, but caches packages globally and materializes them into each environment cheaply, alongside parallel downloads. Building from source would be slower, not faster."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/lockfiles-and-dependency-pinning","sources":["Official docs: pip (secure installs and hash-checking mode)","Official docs: uv (locking and syncing)","PEP 440 — Version Identification and Dependency Specification","PEP 751 — A file format to record Python dependencies for installation reproducibility"],"why":"- **The graph is mostly not yours.** A typical AI service declares perhaps fifteen direct dependencies and\n  installs well over a hundred, each allowing a range of its own dependencies, with new releases daily.\n- **Ranges alone drift.** `pip install` on Tuesday and on Friday can produce different environments from the\n  same source tree.\n- **The failure is rarely loud at install time.** It appears later as a changed default, a removed keyword argument or a new\n  validation rule, in a package nobody on the team chose directly.\n- **Pinning nothing** (\"always get the latest fixes\") makes every build a new, untested combination, so a\n  green CI run on Monday says nothing about Friday's deploy.\n- **Pinning by hand fails too.** Hand pins in `requirements.txt` miss transitive dependencies unless you freeze\n  the whole environment; then upgrades are manual surgery, teams stop upgrading and accumulate vulnerable\n  versions. A resolver-generated lockfile gives exactness *and* cheap, reviewable upgrades.","how":"### Two layers: constraints and resolution\n1. **Declare intent** in `pyproject.toml` with PEP 440 specifiers: `openai>=1.40,<2`, `httpx>=0.27`.\n2. **Resolve:** a resolver (uv, Poetry, pip-compile) finds one version of every package in the transitive\n   graph that satisfies all constraints at once.\n3. **Record** the result in the lockfile.\n\nKeeping the layers separate is the point: humans reason about compatibility in ranges; machines record the\nexact solution.\n\n### Reading specifiers precisely\n| Specifier | Means |\n|---|---|\n| `>=1.40,<2` | common application range: newer minors allowed, next major excluded |\n| `~=1.4.2` | *compatible release*: `>=1.4.2, ==1.4.*` |\n| `~=1.4` | `>=1.4, ==1.*`: the number of components decides how much floats, easy to misread |\n| `==1.4.*` | prefix match |\n| `==1.4.2` | exact pin |\n| `!=1.4.3` | excludes one known-bad release |\n| pre-releases (`2.0.0rc1`) | skipped by default unless a specifier names one or you opt in |\n\n- **None of this encodes semantic versioning,** *because* PEP 440 defines ordering, not compatibility\n  promises.\n- **`0.x` packages can break on a minor bump.** Many core AI-stack packages are still on `0.x`, as the example\n  below shows. A range is a bet about the maintainer's behaviour; the lock makes that bet testable.\n- **`requires-python` is a constraint too.** A universal lock must work across the whole declared Python range,\n  so declaring `>=3.9` when you only run 3.12 can force older releases or split resolutions for some packages.\n\n### What a lockfile records\n- **Exact versions** for every package, direct and transitive.\n- **Hashes** of the distribution files. With hash checking (`pip install --require-hashes`, or tools that\n  verify lock hashes automatically), an install fails if a file's bytes differ from what was locked,\n  *because* a version number alone does not prove you got the same artifact: a compromised mirror or index\n  could serve different bytes under the same name.\n- **Markers and platforms.** Packages can differ by OS, CPU architecture or Python version. A *universal*\n  lock (uv.lock, poetry.lock) records all supported environments; a platform-specific one (a pip-compile\n  output run on Linux) is valid only where it was resolved.\n- **Sources.** Which index each package came from, which matters for torch CUDA wheels and private mirrors.\n- **Standard format:** PEP 751 defines `pylock.toml` so different tools can produce and consume the same\n  lockfile; tool-specific formats remain common.\n\n### Applications vs libraries\n| | Application | Library |\n|---|---|---|\n| Position | end of the dependency chain | installed alongside other people's dependencies |\n| Policy | lock exactly | widest range actually tested |\n| Exact pin effect | reproducibility | `httpx==0.27.2` blocks any app needing a newer httpx |\n\n- **Upper bounds (`<2`) on libraries are debated:** they prevent breakage from a future major but cause\n  unsatisfiable resolutions when that major ships and the library is not updated.\n- **Lower bounds** should reflect the oldest version you actually test.\n\n### Upgrading on purpose\n1. A bot (Dependabot, Renovate) or a scheduled job runs `uv lock --upgrade` (or `--upgrade-package openai`\n   for one package).\n2. It opens a pull request with the lock diff.\n3. CI and the eval suite decide.\n\n- **Why:** pinning is safe only if upgrades keep happening. Small, frequent upgrades are cheaper to debug than\n  a year of drift landing at once, and keep security fixes flowing.\n\n### What a lock does not pin\n| Outside the Python lock | Pin it with |\n|---|---|\n| interpreter version | a `requires-python` range plus a `.python-version` |\n| OS libraries (glibc, CUDA drivers), base container image | an image digest |\n| model weights | model revisions or checksums |\n| hosted model behind an alias | a dated model snapshot ID where the provider offers one |\n| prompts and tool schemas | a prompt version stored with the code |","example":"### Worked failure: an unlocked rebuild breaks every request\nA RAG service declares `openai>=1.0` and `httpx` with no lockfile.\n\n| Step | Without a lock | With a lock |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trigger | image rebuilt for an unrelated config change; the resolver picks a new minor of the HTTP client that removed a long-deprecated constructor argument | rebuild installs exactly the locked versions; production unaffected |\n| Where the new version lands | straight in the production image | in a PR: the weekly `uv lock --upgrade` diff shows the HTTP client moving one minor version |\n| What breaks | the resolved SDK still passes that argument, so every request fails at client construction with a `TypeError` about an unexpected keyword | CI's integration test constructs the real SDK client and hits the same `TypeError`, so the upgrade is held back |\n| Diagnosis | code, prompts and tests unchanged; only the environment moved, reconstructed from logs | cause visible as a two-line lock diff |\n\n- **Real instance:** this pattern hit older OpenAI Python SDK releases when httpx 0.28 removed its `proxies`\n  argument.\n- **Fix:** also upgrade the SDK to a release that supports the new client, or add a temporary upper bound\n  (`httpx<0.28`) with a comment and a ticket to remove it. The failure happened in a pull request, not in\n  production.\n\n### Agent scenario: same commit, different agent\nAn agent's tool-call success rate on the eval set dropped between two runs of the same commit. The team\ncompared what each run actually had (illustrative incident, rates from their eval set).\n\n| Pinned? | Run A (last week) | Run B (today) | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Python packages | unlocked rebuild | unlocked rebuild | the agent framework's core package moved one minor version |\n| Hosted model | model alias | same alias, now pointing to a newer snapshot | different tool-call style |\n| Prompt | `support-v7` | `support-v7` | unchanged |\n| Tool-call success on evals | 94% | 81% | two moving parts, impossible to attribute |\n\nThe fix was to lock packages with `uv sync --locked`, call a dated model snapshot, and write the lock\nhash, model ID and prompt version into each eval run's metadata. The next drop was attributable to one\nlock diff.\n\n### What hash checking actually does\nStandard library only: the lock records a digest at resolve time, and the installer refuses a file whose\nbytes differ, even with the same name and version.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, pathlib, tempfile\n\ndef sha256(path: pathlib.Path) -> str:\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with path.open(\"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b\"\"):   # 1 MiB blocks: wheels can be gigabytes\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\nwheel = pathlib.Path(tempfile.mkdtemp()) / \"demo-1.0-py3-none-any.whl\"\nwheel.write_bytes(b\"original bytes\")\nlocked = sha256(wheel)                       # what the lockfile recorded at resolve time\n\nwheel.write_bytes(b\"tampered bytes\")         # same name, same version, different file\nactual = sha256(wheel)\nprint(\"install\" if actual == locked else f\"refuse: expected {locked[:12]}..., got {actual[:12]}...\")\n```\n\n### Check how far a specifier floats\nWith `pip install packaging` (the library pip itself uses for PEP 440):\n\n```python\nfrom packaging.specifiers import SpecifierSet\n\nassert SpecifierSet(\"~=1.4\").contains(\"1.9\")             # surprise: 1.9 is allowed\nassert not SpecifierSet(\"~=1.4.0\").contains(\"1.9\")       # patch releases only\nassert SpecifierSet(\">=1.40,<2\").contains(\"1.99\")\n\ncandidates = [\"1.9\", \"2.0.0rc1\"]                         # what an index offers\nassert list(SpecifierSet(\">=1.0\").filter(candidates)) == [\"1.9\"]   # pre-release skipped by default\nassert list(SpecifierSet(\">=1.0\").filter(candidates, prereleases=True)) == candidates\n```\n\nUse `filter` to see what a resolver would pick: it drops pre-releases when a final release fits (and keeps\nthem only if nothing else matches). Do not test this with `contains(\"2.0.0rc1\")`, whose default changed\nbetween `packaging` releases (21.x returns False, 26.x returns True).","patterns":["**Record the full agent fingerprint with every eval and trace.** Store the lockfile hash, model snapshot ID, prompt version and embedding model revision as run metadata (LangSmith and similar tools accept arbitrary metadata on runs). Then a quality change maps to one diff instead of a guess.","**Gate dependency upgrades on agent evals, not only unit tests.** Upgrade pull requests for agent frameworks and provider SDKs run the eval suite; merge only when task success, tool-call validity and cost per run hold.","**Scheduled, small upgrade PRs.** Run `uv lock --upgrade` weekly (or let Renovate or Dependabot do it, if they support your lock format), and use `uv lock --upgrade-package openai` for urgent single fixes. Monitor lock age and the number of packages behind latest.","**Lock-is-current gate in CI.** Install with `uv sync --locked` (or run `poetry check --lock` before `poetry install`), so an edited `pyproject.toml` without a re-lock fails fast. Pin the tool version so lock diffs carry only real changes.","**Integration test that constructs real clients.** A cheap test that builds the actual SDK and HTTP clients catches constructor and import breakage from an upgrade before merge, as in the worked failure above.","**Temporary bounds with an owner.** When an upstream release breaks you, add a narrow bound such as `httpx<0.28` with a comment and a ticket link, and track open bounds so they get removed.","**Pin what the lock cannot.** Base images by digest (`FROM image@sha256:...`), the interpreter via `.python-version`, and model weights by revision (for Hugging Face, the `revision=` argument of `from_pretrained` set to a commit hash). Record all of them with eval results."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent behaviour is an eval result, and an eval result is only valid for\n  the environment it ran in. An unlocked rebuild can move `langchain-core`, a provider SDK or a tokenizer\n  and silently change message formatting, tool-call parsing or retries.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `uv.lock` or `poetry.lock` pins LangChain, LangGraph, LangSmith,\n  `openai`, `anthropic` and their transitive graph; Renovate or Dependabot opens upgrade pull requests;\n  your eval suite is the gate on each one.\n- **What you control.** Lock the service and the eval harness from one file, pin what the lock cannot\n  (dated model snapshots instead of moving aliases, prompt versions, embedding model revisions, base\n  image digests), and record all of them with every eval run.\n- **What you can skip.** PEP 440 edge cases beyond `~=` and pre-releases, and lockfile format internals.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Trustworthy eval results.** Recording the lock hash, model snapshot and prompt version with every eval\n  run means a score can be tied to the exact environment that produced it.\n- **Explaining a behaviour change after a rebuild.** Diffing two locks shows whether `langchain-core`, a\n  provider SDK or a tokenizer moved between the good and bad builds.\n- **Automated upgrade pull requests.** Renovate or Dependabot opens lock updates and the agent eval suite\n  decides whether they merge.\n- **Pinning what the lock cannot.** Dated model snapshots, embedding model revisions and base image digests\n  close the gaps a Python lockfile leaves open.","mini_project":"**Build an eval-run manifest for an agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make every agent eval result reproducible from a recorded manifest.\n- **Steps:** in a locked agent project, write a small script that collects the lock file hash, Python\n  version, model identifier, prompt version and base image digest; save it as JSON next to each eval\n  result; then upgrade one framework package, re-run a 10-case eval and diff both the manifests and the\n  scores.\n- **Done when:** each eval result has a manifest, the diff names exactly what changed between runs, and you\n  can rebuild the earlier environment from its lock and reproduce the earlier score within the run-to-run\n  noise you measured by repeating the eval (model sampling is not bit-for-bit deterministic)."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"pyproject.toml: PEP 440 ranges\"] --> R[\"Resolver: uv, Poetry, pip-compile\"]\n    R --> L[(\"Lockfile: exact versions, hashes, markers, sources\")]\n    L --> I[\"Install: uv sync --locked or pip install --require-hashes\"]\n    I --> H{\"File hash matches the lock?\"}\n    H -->|\"no\"| F[\"Install fails: tampered or substituted artifact\"]\n    H -->|\"yes\"| E[\"Same bytes on laptop, CI and container\"]\n    B[\"Weekly bot: uv lock --upgrade\"] --> P[\"Upgrade PR with lock diff\"]\n    P --> T{\"CI and eval suite pass?\"}\n    T -->|\"yes, merge\"| L\n    T -->|\"no\"| X[\"Hold back, or add a temporary bound with a ticket\"]","caption":"The lock is the only input to every install, hash checks guard the bytes, and changes reach the lock only\nthrough a reviewed upgrade pull request that CI and evals must pass."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do you need a lockfile if `pyproject.toml` already lists your dependencies?","a":"- **`pyproject.toml` holds ranges for direct dependencies only,** while a typical AI service installs well\n  over a hundred packages, each with its own ranges and new releases daily.\n- **Without a lock,** two installs of the same source tree days apart can differ, and the breakage arrives in\n  a package nobody chose directly.\n- **The lock records the exact resolved set with hashes,** so every environment installs the same bytes and\n  an incident is bisectable to a lock diff."},{"q":"A deploy broke with a `TypeError` about an unexpected keyword, but nobody changed code. How do you investigate?","a":"- **Compare environments:** diff the installed versions (or the lock) between the last good image and the\n  broken one; an unlocked rebuild often pulled a new transitive minor.\n- **Known shape:** older OpenAI Python SDK releases broke when httpx 0.28 removed its `proxies` argument.\n- **Fix and prevent:** upgrade the SDK or add `httpx<0.28` with a ticket, then lock and install only from the\n  lock, with an integration test that constructs the real clients."},{"q":"What does `~=1.4` allow, and why does that trip people up?","a":"- **It means `>=1.4, ==1.*`,** so 1.9 is allowed; the number of components decides how much floats.\n- **For patch releases only,** write `~=1.4.0` or `>=1.4,<1.5`.\n- **And remember** PEP 440 defines ordering, not compatibility, and many AI-stack packages are still `0.x`."},{"q":"How would you make an agent's eval results reproducible months later?","a":"- **Lock the code environment:** commit `uv.lock` and run evals with `uv sync --locked`, the same lock the\n  service image uses.\n- **Pin what the lock cannot:** a dated model snapshot rather than an alias, prompt and tool-schema\n  versions, embedding model revision, base image digest.\n- **Record it all per run:** store the lock hash and those IDs as eval-run metadata, so any later run can\n  be compared or rebuilt exactly."},{"q":"When does hash checking cause more friction than it is worth, and what do you do then?","a":"- **Missing hashes:** the lock lacks a hash for another platform's wheel.\n- **Rebuilt artifacts:** an internal mirror re-packages files, producing new bytes and new hashes.\n- **Unhashable sources:** direct URL and VCS dependencies pip cannot hash-check; verify at the mirror or pin by\n  commit instead of dropping verification everywhere."},{"q":"Your eval scores shifted between two runs with the same commit and the same lock. What else would you pin?","a":"- **The interpreter:** a `requires-python` range plus a `.python-version`.\n- **System layers:** the base image by digest, which also fixes OS libraries and the CUDA runtime.\n- **Model weights:** a revision or checksum, for example a commit hash as the Hugging Face `revision=`, and\n  record all of these alongside the eval results."}],"tradeoffs":["**Reproducibility vs automatic fixes.** Exact locks make builds reproducible and incidents bisectable, but you stop receiving fixes automatically; safe only when an automated upgrade process runs and its PRs get merged.","**Tamper protection vs friction.** Hash checking defends against tampered or substituted artifacts, but breaks when the lock lacks a hash for another platform's wheel, when an internal mirror rebuilds or re-packages files (new bytes, new hash), or for direct URL and VCS dependencies pip cannot hash-check; then verify at the mirror or pin by commit.","**Universal vs single-platform lock.** One file serves every developer OS and CI image, but may fail to resolve packages that exist only for some platforms; a Linux-only lock is simpler when deployment is the only target.","**Library caps vs conflicts.** Tight upper bounds protect users from untested majors but cause unresolvable conflicts in applications; testing against new releases quickly usually beats capping."],"pitfalls":["**Locking packages but calling a moving model alias.** Symptom: agent behaviour changes with no lock diff at all. Fix: use a dated model snapshot where the provider offers one, and record the model ID with every eval run.","**Lockfile not committed** (or in `.gitignore`). Symptom: \"works locally, fails in CI\" with different transitive versions in each environment. Fix: commit it and install with `uv sync --locked`.","**`pip install .` in Docker while the lock sits unused.** Symptom: images containing versions that appear nowhere in the lockfile. Fix: install from the lock.","**Exact pins in a published library.** Symptom: bug reports saying your package cannot be installed alongside another common package. Fix: declare the widest tested range.","**Locking but never upgrading.** Symptom: a security scan listing many known vulnerabilities whose fixes shipped months ago, then an upgrade that changes dozens of packages at once. Fix: scheduled upgrade PRs.","**Different tool versions locally and in CI** for `uv lock` (or `poetry lock`). Symptom: lock diffs rewriting many lines (format revision, metadata) with no dependency change, burying the one real change. Pin the tool version alongside the lock.","**Reading `~=1.4` as \"1.4.x only\".** Symptom: a 1.9 release in an upgrade PR someone assumed was excluded. Write `~=1.4.0` or `>=1.4,<1.5` when you mean patch releases only.","**Treating the Python lock as full reproducibility.** Symptom: identical locks give different results because the base image, CUDA runtime or model revision changed underneath."],"when":"- **Lock:** every agent service, tool server, notebook environment that feeds production, and evaluation\n  harness, because eval results are only comparable when the environment is fixed.\n- **Do not lock:** a library's published dependencies; lock its development environment for CI instead.\n- **Install with checks:** that the lock matches declared dependencies (`uv sync --locked`, `poetry install`\n  with a current-lock check), and hash verification where tooling allows.\n- **Measure:** lock age and the number of packages behind latest; schedule upgrades.\n- **Interviews:** separate application from library policy and explain what hashes protect against.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Commits the lock, reads lock diffs in upgrade PRs, and adds a narrowly scoped temporary bound with a ticket when an upstream release breaks something.","architect":"Defines organization-wide policy: locking for deployables, upgrade cadence, hash verification, approved indexes, and how base images and model revisions are pinned alongside.","fde":"Reproduces the customer's exact environment from their lock or image when debugging, and ships locked, mirror-compatible requirements that pass their artifact review."},"check":{"q":"You maintain both an internal Python library used by several services and one of those services.\nHow should version constraints differ between them, and what goes wrong if you use the service's policy for\nthe library?","a":"The service is a leaf: it locks every package exactly, with hashes, and installs only from the lock,\nbecause reproducibility of what runs matters most. The library is installed alongside other services'\ndependencies, so it declares ranges covering versions it is tested against, perhaps with conservative bounds,\nand locks only its own CI environment. If the library pinned exact versions, every consuming service would\nbe forced onto those versions, and any service needing a different version of a shared package (an SDK,\npydantic, httpx) would hit an unresolvable conflict."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does hash checking in a lockfile protect against that exact version pins alone do not?","options":["A dependency releasing a new minor version","Receiving different file contents under the same package name and version","Resolution conflicts between transitive dependencies","Packages that require a newer Python version"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A version number names a release; a hash identifies the exact bytes. If an index or mirror serves a tampered or substituted file, the hash no longer matches and installation fails instead of proceeding."},{"q":"Why should a widely used library avoid exact pins in its declared dependencies?","options":["Exact pins slow down import time","PyPI rejects packages with exact pins","Exact pins disable hash checking for users","Applications installing it with other packages may be unable to find a version set that satisfies everyone"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Every package in an environment must agree on one version of each dependency. A library's exact pins become everyone's constraints, causing unresolvable conflicts; applications are the right place to lock."},{"q":"Two builds from the same commit and the same lockfile give different model outputs. Which is a plausible cause?","options":["The lockfile hashes were ignored, so packages differed","Something outside the Python lock changed, such as the base image, CUDA runtime or model revision","Lockfiles do not pin transitive dependencies","The resolver re-ran and chose different versions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A lock pins Python packages, including transitive ones, but not the interpreter build, system libraries, drivers or model weights. Those need their own pins, such as image digests and model revisions."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/security-scanning-and-dependency-auditing","sources":["Official docs: pip-audit (PyPA)","Official docs: Bandit (PyCQA)","Official docs: OSV (Open Source Vulnerabilities) database"],"why":"- **An AI service is mostly third-party code:** an LLM SDK, an HTTP stack, a web framework, tokenizers,\n  PDF and HTML parsers, often torch and transformers. Vulnerabilities are disclosed in packages like these\n  regularly.\n- **Locks keep you on the vulnerable version** until someone notices, and nobody reads advisories for a\n  hundred transitive packages by hand, so without automation the gap between \"fix published\" and \"fix\n  deployed\" is unbounded.\n- **AI work makes risky code more common.** Model artifacts use pickle-based formats that execute code on\n  load; LLM output is tempting to pass to `eval`, a shell or SQL; prototypes hard-code API keys; `yaml.load`\n  and `trust_remote_code=True` slip in from tutorials.\n- **Careful review does not scale.** \"We will review carefully and update dependencies when we remember\"\n  fails because both problems grow with code volume and dependency count, while attention does not.\n- **Cheap, deterministic scanners on every pull request** close the obvious holes.","how":"### pip-audit: known vulnerabilities in dependencies\n1. **Input:** installed or declared packages: the current environment, `-r requirements.txt`, or a project\n   directory. pip-audit is maintained under PyPA.\n2. **Resolve** exact versions.\n3. **Query** a vulnerability service (PyPI's advisory data by default, or OSV with `-s osv`) for advisories\n   affecting each version.\n4. **Report** package, version, advisory ID and fixed versions; exit non-zero on findings so CI fails; emit\n   JSON or CycloneDX SBOM output on request.\n\n- **`--fix`** upgrades to fixed versions where possible; **`--ignore-vuln ID`** records a deliberate,\n  reviewed exception.\n- **Exact versions are essential,** *because* advisories are expressed as affected version ranges. Auditing a\n  lockfile or exported pinned requirements is meaningful; auditing loose ranges in `pyproject.toml` is not,\n  since the answer depends on what would be installed.\n- **The lock also reveals transitive exposure:** vulnerable packages you never chose directly.\n\n### Triage: what to do with a finding\nA finding is the start of a decision, not the decision. A useful order:\n1. **Is a fixed version available?** If yes, upgrade *through the lock*: `uv lock --upgrade-package jinja2`\n   (or the Poetry or pip-compile equivalent), then sync. `pip-audit --fix` upgrades the environment it\n   is pointed at, which in a locked project just creates drift that the next sync erases.\n2. **Is it transitive?** If the vulnerable package is pulled in by another dependency whose declared range\n   excludes the fixed version, the real fix is upgrading the parent. If that is not yet possible, a\n   constraint or override that forces the fixed version is a stopgap you must test, *because* you are\n   now running the parent against a version it did not declare support for.\n3. **Is the vulnerable path reachable?** Read the advisory: a flaw in a template sandbox does not matter if\n   you never render untrusted templates, while one in the HTTP parser of your public endpoint does.\n   Unreachable is a reason to *defer* with a written justification, not to ignore forever.\n4. **Record the exception.** `--ignore-vuln ID` with the reason and an expiry or review date next to it, so\n   the suppression is visible in review and revisited when a fix ships.\n\n### Bandit: risky patterns in your code\n- **How:** parses each Python file into an abstract syntax tree and runs plugins matching known-dangerous\n  constructs, each with a test ID, severity and confidence.\n\n| Test ID | Flags | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `B101` | assert used | asserts vanish under `python -O`, so they must not enforce security |\n| `B301` | pickle deserialization | executes code on load |\n| `B307` | `eval` | runs arbitrary expressions |\n| `B506` | unsafe `yaml.load` | full loader builds arbitrary objects |\n| `B602` | subprocess with `shell=True` | shell metacharacters from input |\n| `B105` | hard-coded password strings | secrets in source |\n| `B608` | SQL built by string formatting | SQL injection |\n\n- **Severity floor:** `bandit -r src -ll` reports medium severity and above. Some useful checks are rated low\n  (B105 and B101 among them) and disappear under that floor.\n- **B105 is a name heuristic:** it fires on literals assigned to names containing words such as `password`,\n  `secret` or `token`, so `API_KEY = \"sk-...\"` slips past it; one more reason to run a real secret scanner.\n- **Configure per project:** skips in a config file (passed explicitly); suppress a reviewed line with\n  `# nosec B602` plus a reason.\n- **Ruff alternative:** Ruff implements many of the same checks as its `S` rules (from flake8-bandit), so\n  teams can run them inside the linter they already have.\n- **Fast but blind to data flow,** *because* it matches syntax rather than tracking data. It cannot tell\n  whether the string reaching `subprocess` came from a constant or from a user, so it produces false\n  positives, and it misses vulnerabilities without a recognizable syntactic shape.\n\n### What neither tool covers\n| Gap | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| **Malicious or typosquatted packages** with no CVE | locks with hashes, approved indexes or mirrors, reviewing new dependencies |\n| **Logic and authorization bugs**, prompt injection, data leakage between tenants | design and review |\n| **Over-privileged agent tools** (a shell tool, unrestricted SQL, unscoped API tokens) | least-privilege tool design, allowlists, sandboxes, human approval for writes |\n| **Packages an agent installs at runtime**, including hallucinated names an attacker registered | no runtime installs, or an allowlisted internal index inside the sandbox |\n| **Secrets already committed** | dedicated secret scanners (gitleaks, trufflehog) search history, which Bandit's string heuristics do not |\n| **Model artifacts** | prefer safetensors over pickle-based checkpoints, pin model revisions, avoid `trust_remote_code=True` for unreviewed repositories |\n| **Reachability** (vulnerable function present but never called) | tools such as OSV-Scanner, Dependabot alerts, Safety or Snyk differ in databases and triage features; choose by workflow |","example":"### What Bandit flags in an agent, and the safer equivalents\nThe scenario: an agent that loads a classifier, runs a shell tool and stores results.\n\n```python\nimport pickle, sqlite3, subprocess, shlex\nimport yaml\n\nAPI_TOKEN = \"sk-live-123\"                                # B105 (low severity): hard-coded secret -> env/secrets\nclf = pickle.load(open(\"router.pkl\", \"rb\"))              # B301: executes code from an untrusted file\ncfg = yaml.load(open(\"agent.yaml\"), Loader=yaml.Loader)  # B506: full loader can build arbitrary objects\n\ndef run_tool(cmd_from_llm: str) -> str:                  # B602: model output reaches a shell\n    return subprocess.run(cmd_from_llm, shell=True, capture_output=True, text=True).stdout\n\ndef save(db: sqlite3.Connection, user: str, answer: str) -> None:\n    db.execute(f\"INSERT INTO answers VALUES ('{user}', '{answer}')\")   # B608: SQL injection\n\n# Safer versions\nALLOWED = {\"ls\", \"wc\"}\ndef run_tool_safe(cmd_from_llm: str) -> str:\n    argv = shlex.split(cmd_from_llm)                     # no shell: no ;, |, $(...) expansion\n    if not argv or argv[0] not in ALLOWED:               # arguments (paths) still need validating\n        raise PermissionError(f\"tool not allowed: {argv[:1]}\")\n    return subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10).stdout\n\ndef save_safe(db: sqlite3.Connection, user: str, answer: str) -> None:\n    db.execute(\"INSERT INTO answers VALUES (?, ?)\", (user, answer))\n\ncfg_safe = yaml.safe_load(open(\"agent.yaml\"))\n# Weights: store as safetensors; for any pickle-based checkpoint, load only from trusted, pinned sources.\n\n# CI (fails the build on findings):\n#   uv export --format requirements-txt --no-hashes --no-emit-project > req.txt\n#   pip-audit -r req.txt --no-deps --disable-pip   # export is already fully resolved; skip pip install\n#   bandit -r src -ll                              # medium+ only: hides B105/B101, so run -l periodically\n```\n\n- **Expect low-severity noise on the safe version too:** Bandit still reports `B603` (subprocess without a\n  shell) on `run_tool_safe`, plus `B403` for `import pickle`. Both are low severity; review them once rather\n  than suppressing them by default.\n\n### Agent scenario: what each control covers in a coding agent\nA coding agent with a shell tool, a SQL tool and community integrations. Map each risk to the control\nthat can actually see it.\n\n| Risk in the agent | pip-audit | Bandit / Ruff `S` | What actually covers it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CVE in an HTTP parser pulled in by an integration | yes, on the exported lock | no | upgrade through the lock, scheduled audits |\n| Tool runs model output with `shell=True` | no | yes, `B602` | no shell, argv allowlist, sandbox, timeout |\n| SQL tool formats model output into a query | no | yes, `B608` | parameters, read-only DB role, row limits |\n| `API_KEY = \"sk-...\"` in a tool module | no | missed (B105 matches names like `password`) | gitleaks or trufflehog, keys from a secrets manager |\n| Agent runs `pip install` for a package name it invented | no | no | block runtime installs, or an allowlisted index in the sandbox |\n| Prompt injection in a retrieved page asks the agent to email data | no | no | tool permissions, output filtering, human approval |\n\n### Scanner configuration kept in the repo\nBandit reads `[tool.bandit]` from `pyproject.toml` when you pass the file with `-c` (install `bandit[toml]`);\nthe Ruff block is the in-linter alternative. Pick one.\n\n```toml\n[tool.bandit]\nexclude_dirs = [\"tests\"]          # asserts and fixtures in tests drown real findings\n\n# Alternative: Bandit-style rules inside Ruff\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nextend-select = [\"S\"]\n\n[tool.ruff.lint.per-file-ignores]\n\"tests/**\" = [\"S101\"]             # assert is fine in tests\n```\n\nRun it with `bandit -c pyproject.toml -r src`.\n\n### Worked scenario: triaging a transitive finding\npip-audit on the exported lock reports an advisory in `jinja2`, pulled in by a web framework.\n\n| Step | Finding | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fixed version? | yes, a newer `jinja2` release | try `uv lock --upgrade-package jinja2` |\n| Transitive? | the framework's range excludes the fix | upgrade the framework; if blocked, a tested override as a stopgap |\n| Reachable? | the flaw is in the template sandbox; the service never renders untrusted templates | allowed to *defer*, with a written justification |\n| Record | `--ignore-vuln <ID>` in the CI script with reason and review date | revisit when the framework ships support |","patterns":["**Scan tool code with the strictest settings.** Run Bandit (or Ruff `S` rules) on the agent's tool modules without the `-ll` severity floor, because tools are where model output meets subprocess, SQL and the filesystem. Review every `# nosec` in a tool module like a permission grant.","**Admit agent integrations like any dependency.** Review new LangChain integration, MCP server or tool packages before adding them (maintainer, release history, what they can reach), lock them with hashes, and keep them in the scheduled audit. Track the number of tool packages per agent.","**No runtime installs from public indexes.** Code-executing agents get a sandbox image with preinstalled, audited packages, or an allowlisted internal index; models sometimes suggest package names that do not exist, and attackers can register them.","**Audit the artifact that ships.** In CI export the lock (`uv export --format requirements-txt ...`) and run `pip-audit -r req.txt --no-deps --disable-pip`, never the developer's environment. Track open findings and time from advisory to fix deployed.","**Scheduled audits, not just PR audits.** New advisories appear for code that has not changed, so also run the same audit daily or weekly against the deployed lock (for example a CI job on a `schedule` trigger) and alert the owning team.","**Expiring, reviewed suppressions.** Keep each `--ignore-vuln ID` and `# nosec Bxxx` next to a reason and a review date; count suppressions and review the list monthly so they do not become permanent.","**Audit evidence as SBOMs.** Emit CycloneDX output from pip-audit (its output-format option) and archive it per release; regulated customers often ask for exactly this.","**Secret scanning on history.** Run gitleaks or trufflehog in CI and as a pre-commit hook, because Bandit's B105 misses names like `API_KEY`. Rotate any key a scanner finds; deleting the commit is not enough.","**Model-artifact policy.** Accept only safetensors, pin model revisions, allowlist sources and keep `trust_remote_code=True` behind review, since no dependency scanner looks inside model files."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents turn model output into actions: shell commands, SQL, file paths,\n  HTTP calls. The patterns Bandit flags (`shell=True`, `eval`, string-built SQL) are exactly where model\n  output becomes code execution, and the agent's large dependency graph is where known CVEs hide.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** LangChain community integrations, MCP server packages, provider\n  SDKs, parsers for retrieved documents and model loaders all arrive through your lock; pip-audit runs on\n  that lock in CI, Bandit or Ruff `S` rules run on your tool code, and a secret scanner guards the API keys\n  agents need.\n- **What you control.** Which tool and integration packages are admitted, the CI gate and its expiring\n  suppressions, a model-artifact policy (safetensors, pinned revisions), and whether a code-executing\n  agent may install packages at runtime, from which index.\n- **What you can skip.** Building your own vulnerability database or data-flow analyzer. Prompt\n  injection and tool authorization are design problems; scanners will not find them.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Auditing an agent's dependency graph.** pip-audit on the lock catches known CVEs in framework\n  integrations, MCP server packages, document parsers and provider SDKs before deploy.\n- **Reviewing tool code that runs model output.** Bandit or Ruff `S` rules flag `shell=True`, `eval` and\n  string-built SQL in exactly the tools where model output becomes execution.\n- **Keeping agent API keys out of the repo.** A secret scanner in pre-commit and CI stops provider and tool\n  credentials from being committed.\n- **Admitting new integrations.** A review gate for new tool and integration packages limits what a\n  compromised or typosquatted package can reach.","mini_project":"**Add a security gate to an agent repo (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** fail CI on known-vulnerable dependencies and unsafe tool code in a small agent project.\n- **Steps:** write one deliberately unsafe tool that passes model output to a shell with `shell=True`;\n  add pip-audit against the lock and Bandit on the tools package to CI; add a suppression that must name a\n  reason and an expiry; fix the tool by passing an argument list and validating input against an\n  allowlist; add a secret scanner to pre-commit.\n- **Done when:** CI fails on the unsafe tool and passes after the fix, a planted fake key is blocked, and\n  every suppression in the repo has a reason and an expiry date."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    PR[\"Pull request or scheduled job\"] --> EX[\"Export the lock: uv export\"]\n    PR --> BA[\"Bandit or Ruff S rules on src\"]\n    EX --> PA[\"pip-audit -r req.txt --no-deps --disable-pip\"]\n    PA -->|\"advisory found\"| FX{\"Fixed version available?\"}\n    FX -->|\"yes, direct\"| UP[\"uv lock --upgrade-package, then sync\"]\n    FX -->|\"yes, but parent range blocks it\"| PU[\"Upgrade parent, or tested override as stopgap\"]\n    FX -->|\"no fix yet\"| RE{\"Vulnerable path reachable?\"}\n    RE -->|\"yes\"| MI[\"Mitigate in code or config now\"]\n    RE -->|\"no\"| IG[\"--ignore-vuln ID with reason and review date\"]\n    BA -->|\"finding\"| BF[\"Fix pattern, or nosec Bxxx with a reason\"]","caption":"The CI gate runs two independent scanners over two different inputs, and every pip-audit finding goes\nthrough the same triage path before it is fixed or deferred with a recorded exception."},"interview":[{"q":"What do pip-audit and Bandit each check, and why do you need both?","a":"- **pip-audit:** your exact installed or locked package versions against advisory data (PyPI's by default, OSV\n  with `-s osv`), so it finds known vulnerabilities in third-party code.\n- **Bandit:** your own source, parsed into a syntax tree, for risky patterns such as `pickle.load`, `eval`,\n  `shell=True` and SQL built by string formatting.\n- **Different blind spots:** one knows nothing about your code, the other nothing about dependencies."},{"q":"pip-audit flags a vulnerability in a transitive dependency and the parent's range excludes the fix. Walk me through it.","a":"- **Prefer upgrading the parent** to a release whose range allows the fixed version, then re-lock.\n- **If blocked,** a constraint or override forcing the fixed version is a stopgap that must be tested,\n  because the parent now runs against a version it never declared support for.\n- **If no fix exists,** check reachability in the advisory and record `--ignore-vuln ID` with a reason and a\n  review date."},{"q":"Why is a clean pip-audit run not evidence that your supply chain is safe?","a":"- **It only knows published advisories,** so a malicious or typosquatted package with no CVE passes.\n- **Defences are elsewhere:** locks with hashes, approved indexes or mirrors, and reviewing new dependencies.\n- **It also never looks inside model files,** where pickle-based checkpoints execute code on load."},{"q":"Bandit produces so many findings that the team ignores it. How do you restore signal?","a":"- **Exclude tests** (asserts and fixtures drown real findings) via `exclude_dirs` or Ruff per-file ignores.\n- **Set a severity floor** such as `-ll` for gating, and run the full set periodically, since B105 and B101\n  are rated low.\n- **Suppress narrowly** with `# nosec B602` plus a reason, and move to a data-flow tool such as Semgrep or\n  CodeQL if false positives remain the bottleneck."},{"q":"An agent passes LLM output to `subprocess.run(..., shell=True)`. What do you change?","a":"- **Drop the shell:** split with `shlex.split` and pass an argument list, so `;`, `|` and `$(...)` are not\n  expanded.\n- **Allowlist commands** and validate arguments such as paths, because a permitted binary can still be misused.\n- **Add a timeout and minimal credentials** for the process; Bandit's B602 would have flagged the original."},{"q":"Your coding agent can run `pip install` inside its sandbox. What controls do you put in place?","a":"- **Prefer no runtime installs:** bake an audited set of packages into the sandbox image, built from a\n  lock and covered by pip-audit.\n- **If installs are needed:** point the sandbox at an internal mirror or allowlisted index, never public\n  PyPI, because a model can invent a package name that an attacker has registered.\n- **Contain the blast radius:** no network beyond that index, no credentials in the sandbox, CPU, memory\n  and time limits, and logged install commands per run."}],"tradeoffs":["**Current dependencies vs blocked work.** Failing CI on any pip-audit finding keeps dependencies current but blocks unrelated work when an advisory has no fix yet; allow time-boxed, reviewed `--ignore-vuln` entries rather than disabling the check.","**Zero setup vs false positives.** Bandit catches common dangerous calls with near-zero setup, but its syntax-only view yields false positives; tune with severity thresholds and justified `nosec` comments, or move to a data-flow tool such as Semgrep or CodeQL when signal-to-noise becomes the bottleneck.","**PR scans vs drift.** Scanning every pull request catches problems early, but new advisories appear for unchanged code; also schedule a daily or weekly audit of the deployed lockfile.","**One tool vs full coverage.** Ruff's `S` rules avoid another tool but may not match Bandit's full plugin set and configuration; pick one and keep the rule list explicit."],"pitfalls":["**A clean scan treated as a safe agent.** Symptom: an agent with green pip-audit and Bandit runs is talked by injected text into calling a powerful tool. Fix: least-privilege tools, approval for writes and injection tests in the eval suite; scanners do not see tool permissions.","**Auditing `pyproject.toml` ranges or the developer's environment** instead of the lock that ships. Symptom: a clean report while the production image contains a flagged version.","**Blanket `# nosec` or skipped test IDs** to make the build green. Symptom: a security review finds `shell=True` on model output that the scanner reported months earlier.","**Clean pip-audit treated as \"no supply-chain risk\".** Symptom: surprise when a malicious lookalike package with no CVE is found in the dependency tree.","**Assuming Bandit understands context.** Symptom: the team ignores its output because findings in tests and constant strings drown real ones. Fix: a config excluding tests and a severity floor.","**`pip-audit --fix` in a lockfile-managed project.** Symptom: a green local audit followed by the same finding in CI, because the fix changed the environment but never reached the lock.","**Pickle-based model files from a public hub** because \"it is just weights\". Symptom: arbitrary code execution on the inference host, which no dependency scanner would report."],"when":"- **Use when:** an agent has a lockfile and tools that will run with real data or credentials; for agent\n  services that is usually the first deployment.\n- **Run:** pip-audit against the exact lock on every PR and on a schedule; Bandit (or Ruff `S` rules) on\n  source directories, with tests excluded or treated leniently.\n- **Measure:** open findings, time from advisory to fix deployed, and the count of suppressions.\n- **Regulated customers:** the JSON or CycloneDX output often becomes audit evidence.\n- **Interviews:** explain what each tool can and cannot see rather than listing tools.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Fixes or justifies each finding, replaces pickle, `eval` and `shell=True` patterns, and keeps suppressions narrow with a reason next to them.","architect":"Sets the policy: which scanners gate merges, severity thresholds, exception expiry, approved indexes and model-artifact formats.","fde":"Produces SBOMs and audit reports in the format the customer's security team requires, and adapts scanning to their private mirrors and air-gapped build systems."},"check":{"q":"Your service passes pip-audit and Bandit with zero findings. A security reviewer still blocks the\nlaunch, citing the agent's ability to download and load community fine-tuned models. Is the reviewer right?","a":"Yes. pip-audit only knows published advisories for Python packages, and Bandit only matches syntax in\nour source; neither inspects model artifacts. Pickle-based checkpoints, and repositories loaded with\n`trust_remote_code=True`, execute code chosen by whoever published the model, so loading community models is\nremote code execution by design unless constrained. Mitigations: accept only safetensors, pin model revisions\nor checksums, allowlist sources, scan or review artifacts, and run loading in a sandbox with minimal\ncredentials. Clean scanner output narrows risk; it does not certify the system."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should pip-audit run against the lockfile rather than the version ranges in pyproject.toml?","options":["pip-audit cannot parse pyproject.toml files","Advisories apply to specific versions, so only the exact resolved set says what is actually vulnerable","Ranges are always newer than the lockfile and therefore safe","The lockfile contains the vulnerability database"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Vulnerability data is expressed as affected version ranges. A range like httpx>=0.27 could install many versions; the lock records exactly what ships, including transitive packages, so the audit is meaningful."},{"q":"What kind of problem is Bandit structurally unable to detect well?","options":["subprocess calls with shell=True","use of pickle.load","whether a value reaching a dangerous call came from untrusted input across functions","hard-coded password-like string literals"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bandit matches patterns in each file's syntax tree; it does not track data flow. It flags the dangerous call itself, but cannot tell a constant from user-controlled input reaching it, hence false positives."},{"q":"A team wants to defend against a typosquatted package with no CVE. Which measure helps most?","options":["Running pip-audit more frequently","Lowering Bandit's severity threshold","Reviewing new dependencies and installing only from a lockfile with hashes from an approved index","Adding nosec comments to import statements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"pip-audit only reports known advisories, and Bandit scans your code, not packages. Controlling what enters the lock, and verifying hashes from trusted sources, is what stops an unknown malicious package."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pytest","sources":["Official docs: pytest","Okken, 2022 — Python Testing with pytest (2nd ed.)"],"why":"- **unittest is a JUnit port with ceremony.** Every test lives in a `TestCase` subclass, every assertion\n  is a method (`assertEqual`, `assertIn`, `assertRaises`, dozens more), and setup is `setUp`/`tearDown`\n  per class with no way to compose shared resources.\n- **Failures are opaque.** A failing `assertEqual(a, b)` prints `3 != 4` and nothing about where 3 came\n  from. A plain `assert a == b` is worse: Python's own `AssertionError` carries no message at all.\n- **Agent code needs many cases.** Chunkers with boundary conditions, prompt builders that escape user\n  text, parsers for possibly fenced JSON, tool dispatchers that reject malformed arguments, retry\n  wrappers that stop after N attempts. Each needs a table of inputs plus shared, expensive setup.\n- **Friction cuts coverage.** If each table row costs a class and each setup costs a method, people\n  write three tests where they needed thirty.\n- **pytest answers both problems.** It rewrites `assert` so a bare comparison yields a useful failure,\n  and treats setup as dependency injection with lifetimes. Everything else (async, parallelism) arrives\n  as plugins through the same hook mechanism.","how":"### What to test in an agent repo\n| Layer | Deterministic? | How pytest covers it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool functions and their argument validation | yes | plain unit tests, parametrized bad inputs |\n| Dispatcher: tool name to function, errors fed back | yes | scripted fake model replies |\n| Loop control: step budget, stop condition, retries | yes | fake model that never says \"final\" |\n| Prompt and message assembly | yes | assert on the exact messages sent |\n| The model's judgement | no | eval suites and recorded runs, in a separate lane |\n\n- **Rule of thumb:** the unit lane never calls a model. It feeds scripted replies through the real loop,\n  because that is where the deterministic bugs live.\n\n### Discovery and collection\n1. `pytest` walks `testpaths` (or the current directory).\n2. It imports files matching `test_*.py` or `*_test.py`.\n3. It collects functions prefixed `test_` and methods of classes prefixed `Test` that have no `__init__`.\n4. It also collects `unittest.TestCase` subclasses, which is how a legacy suite migrates incrementally.\n- **Collection runs before any test**, so a syntax error anywhere fails the whole session early.\n- **`--collect-only`** shows the exact test ids that would run.\n\n### Assertion rewriting\n- **An import hook does it.** When pytest imports a test module (plus `conftest.py` files and any\n  package registered with `pytest.register_assert_rewrite`), it rewrites the AST so each `assert expr`\n  evaluates sub-expressions, keeps their values and builds a message on failure.\n- **Result:** `assert len(chunks) == n` fails with `assert 4 == 3` and ` +  where 4 = len([...])`.\n  For an agent test, `assert calls[1][-1][\"role\"] == \"tool\"` shows the actual message list.\n- **Import time, not tracing**, because tracing would slow every test and could not see inside a single\n  expression.\n- **Test modules only**, because changing bytecode in production code would be a surprising side effect.\n- **Cached** as `.pyc` files tagged with the pytest version.\n\n### Fixtures\nA fixture is a function decorated with `@pytest.fixture`. A test that names it as a parameter gets its\nreturn value; pytest resolves the dependency graph (fixtures can depend on fixtures) per test.\n\n| Scope | Lifetime | Agent example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `function` (default) | rebuilt per test | scripted fake LLM, message history |\n| `class` | once per test class | rarely needed |\n| `module` | once per test file | a seeded in-memory vector index |\n| `package` | once per package | shared fixtures for one tool package |\n| `session` | once per run | loaded tokenizer, vector-DB container |\n\n- **`yield` splits setup from teardown**, and teardown runs even when the test fails.\n- **`conftest.py` sharing.** Fixtures declared there are visible to every test in that directory tree\n  without imports; that is how a project shares its `fake_llm` or `db` fixture.\n- **Built-ins:** `tmp_path` (fresh directory, ideal as an agent's scratch workspace), `monkeypatch`\n  (undoable attribute and env-var changes), `capsys` and `caplog` (captured stdout and log records),\n  `request` (introspection of the calling test).\n- **Scope rule.** A higher-scoped fixture cannot depend on a lower-scoped one, because a session-wide\n  object cannot be rebuilt once per test; violating it raises `ScopeMismatch` at setup.\n\nThree more rules explain most fixture surprises:\n- **Teardown runs in reverse order of setup**, because a fixture's dependencies must still exist while it\n  cleans up (the client closes before the server it talks to stops).\n- **`autouse=True` applies a fixture to every test in its scope** without being named. It suits \"always\n  block real network calls\" or \"always reset settings\", but is invisible to the reader of a test.\n- **Nearer definitions shadow outer ones.** A same-named fixture in a nearer `conftest.py` (or the test\n  module) wins, so `tests/integration/conftest.py` can replace the root `llm` fake with a real client\n  for that directory alone.\n\n### Parametrization\n- **`@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"raw,expected\", cases, ids=[...])`** expands one function into one test\n  per row, each with its own id such as `test_parse[fenced-json]`.\n- **Stacking two decorators** yields the Cartesian product, for example every tool times every malformed\n  argument shape.\n- **`pytest.param(..., marks=pytest.mark.xfail)`** marks a single row, such as a known model-output\n  shape your parser does not handle yet.\n- **Fixtures can be parametrized too** (`params=`): every dependent test runs once per parameter. A\n  `store` fixture parametrized over `[\"memory\", \"pgvector\"]` runs the whole store suite on both backends.\n\n### Markers and selection\n- **Known gaps:** `@pytest.mark.skip`, `skipif`, and `xfail(strict=True)`.\n- **Custom lanes:** markers such as `integration` or `e2e` are registered in configuration and selected\n  with `-m \"not e2e\"`.\n- **`--strict-markers`** turns a marker typo into an error, because an unregistered marker otherwise\n  produces only a `PytestUnknownMarkWarning` that is easy to miss. The misspelled test is then not\n  matched by `-m`, so it runs in the wrong lane and may bill real tokens.\n\n| Option | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `-k` | select by substring or boolean expression over test names |\n| `-x` | stop at the first failure |\n| `--lf` | rerun only the last failures |\n| `--durations=10` | list the slowest tests |\n\n### Configuration and plugins\n- **Config** lives under `[tool.pytest.ini_options]` in `pyproject.toml`: `testpaths`, `addopts`,\n  `markers`, `filterwarnings`.\n- **Plugins** are ordinary packages that implement `pytest_*` hook functions and register an entry point;\n  `conftest.py` can implement the same hooks locally.\n\n| Plugin an agent builder meets weekly | What it adds |\n|---|---|\n| `pytest-asyncio` | coroutine tests, for async agent loops and SDK clients |\n| `pytest-xdist` | `-n auto` distributes tests across one worker process per CPU core |\n| `pytest-cov` | coverage |\n| `pytest-timeout` | kills hung tests, such as a stuck model call |\n| `pytest-mock` | the `mocker` fixture |\n| Hypothesis | registers its own settings and database handling |\n\n- **xdist changes fixture semantics.** Each worker is a separate process with its own session, so a\n  \"session\" fixture that starts a container runs once *per worker*.\n- **Order-dependent tests break under xdist**, because a test relying on another test's side effects may\n  land on a different worker.","example":"### Agent scenario: testing a tool-calling loop with a scripted model\nSelf-contained; save as `test_agent_loop.py` and run `pytest -q`. The fake returns pre-written replies\nand records every request, so tests assert on what the loop sent.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport pytest\n\nclass ScriptedLLM:\n    def __init__(self, replies):\n        self.replies, self.calls = list(replies), []\n    def complete(self, messages):\n        self.calls.append([dict(m) for m in messages])     # snapshot what the loop sent\n        return self.replies.pop(0)\n\nTOOLS = {\"get_weather\": lambda city: {\"city\": city, \"temp_c\": 18}}\n\ndef run_agent(llm, user_msg, max_steps=5):\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_msg}]\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        reply = llm.complete(messages)\n        if reply[\"type\"] == \"final\":\n            return reply[\"text\"], messages\n        fn = TOOLS.get(reply[\"name\"])\n        result = fn(**reply[\"args\"]) if fn else {\"error\": f\"unknown tool {reply['name']}\"}\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"name\": reply[\"name\"], \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n    raise RuntimeError(\"step budget exhausted\")\n\nCALL = {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"get_weather\", \"args\": {\"city\": \"Oslo\"}}\nFINAL = {\"type\": \"final\", \"text\": \"18 C in Oslo\"}\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef llm():\n    return ScriptedLLM([CALL, FINAL])\n\ndef test_tool_result_reaches_model(llm):\n    text, _ = run_agent(llm, \"weather in Oslo?\")\n    assert text == \"18 C in Oslo\"\n    assert json.loads(llm.calls[1][-1][\"content\"])[\"temp_c\"] == 18\n\ndef test_unknown_tool_is_reported_not_raised():\n    llm = ScriptedLLM([{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"rm_rf\", \"args\": {}}, FINAL])\n    run_agent(llm, \"clean up\")\n    assert \"unknown tool\" in llm.calls[1][-1][\"content\"]\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"budget\", [1, 3, 5])\ndef test_loop_stops_at_budget(budget):\n    llm = ScriptedLLM([CALL] * budget)          # a model that never finishes\n    with pytest.raises(RuntimeError, match=\"budget\"):\n        run_agent(llm, \"loop forever\", max_steps=budget)\n    assert len(llm.calls) == budget\n```\n\n- **No tokens, no flakiness.** Five tests cover the happy path, the error-feedback path and the runaway\n  loop, which are exactly the behaviours a real model run would hit only occasionally.\n- **Assert on the transcript.** `llm.calls` is the message list the model saw; checking it catches a\n  dropped tool result or a wrong role before a model ever does.\n\n### Chunker suite: session fixture, parametrized ids, `pytest.raises`\nThe RAG chunker counts whitespace tokens so the arithmetic in the ids is checkable by hand.\n\n```python\n# tests/conftest.py\nimport pytest\nfrom myapp.chunking import Chunker\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef chunker():                              # built once per run: construction is the slow part\n    return Chunker(max_tokens=50, overlap=5)\n\n# tests/test_chunking.py\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"text,n_chunks\", [\n    (\"\", 0),\n    (\"short\", 1),\n    (\"word \" * 120, 3),                     # windows 0-49, 45-94, 90-119 -> three chunks\n], ids=[\"empty\", \"single\", \"overlapping\"])\ndef test_chunk_count(chunker, text, n_chunks):\n    assert len(chunker.split(text)) == n_chunks\n\ndef test_every_chunk_respects_limit(chunker):\n    chunks = chunker.split(\"word \" * 1000)\n    assert all(chunker.count_tokens(c) <= 50 for c in chunks)\n\ndef test_overlap_is_shared_tokens(chunker):\n    text = \" \".join(f\"w{i}\" for i in range(120))   # distinct tokens, so overlap is observable\n    first, second = chunker.split(text)[:2]\n    assert first.split()[-5:] == second.split()[:5] == [\"w45\", \"w46\", \"w47\", \"w48\", \"w49\"]\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"bad\", [None, 42, b\"bytes\"])\ndef test_rejects_non_text(chunker, bad):\n    with pytest.raises(TypeError):\n        chunker.split(bad)\n```\n\n- **Run** `pytest -q tests/test_chunking.py`: a wrong overlap implementation fails as\n  `test_chunk_count[overlapping]` with `assert 4 == 3` followed by `where 4 = len([...])`. You get the\n  case and the value without a debugger.\n- **Distinct tokens on purpose.** With `\"word \" * 120` every window looks identical, so the overlap\n  assertion would pass even with zero overlap.\n- **`pytest -k overlap`** runs just the two ids containing the word: `test_chunk_count[overlapping]` and\n  `test_overlap_is_shared_tokens`.\n\n### Runnable: a model-output parser as a case table\nSelf-contained; save as `test_parse.py` and run `pytest -q test_parse.py`. This is the parser between\nthe model's reply and your tool dispatcher.\n\n```python\nimport json, re\nimport pytest\n\nFENCE = \"`\" * 3                      # a Markdown code fence, built so this file nests cleanly\nFENCED = re.compile(FENCE + r\"(?:json)?\\s*(.*?)\" + FENCE, flags=re.S)\n\ndef parse_json_reply(text: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Accept bare JSON or JSON inside a fence; reject anything that is not an object.\"\"\"\n    m = FENCED.search(text)\n    body = m.group(1) if m else text\n    data = json.loads(body.strip())\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"expected a JSON object\")\n    return data\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"raw,expected\", [\n    ('{\"a\": 1}', {\"a\": 1}),\n    (f'{FENCE}json\\n{{\"a\": 1}}\\n{FENCE}', {\"a\": 1}),\n    (f'Sure!\\n{FENCE}\\n{{\"a\": [1, 2]}}\\n{FENCE}', {\"a\": [1, 2]}),\n], ids=[\"bare\", \"fenced-json\", \"chatty-fence\"])\ndef test_parse_ok(raw, expected):\n    assert parse_json_reply(raw) == expected\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"raw,exc\", [\n    (\"[1, 2]\", ValueError),\n    (\"not json\", json.JSONDecodeError),\n], ids=[\"array\", \"prose\"])\ndef test_parse_rejects(raw, exc):\n    with pytest.raises(exc):\n        parse_json_reply(raw)\n```\n\nFive ids such as `test_parse_ok[fenced-json]` show up in the report, so a regression names the input\nshape that broke. `json.JSONDecodeError` is a subclass of `ValueError`, so the \"prose\" row asserts the\nnarrower class on purpose. When production logs show a new reply shape, add it as a row.\n\n### Worked scenario: a session fixture under xdist\nAn agent's retrieval tests use a session fixture that starts a vector-DB container in ~20 s\n(illustrative figure).\n\n| Run | Workers | Container starts | Fixed setup cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `pytest` | 1 | 1 | ~20 s |\n| `pytest -n auto` on 8 cores | 8 | 8 | ~20 s each, in parallel, plus 8× the memory |\n| `-n auto` + file lock, shared container | 8 | 1 | ~20 s once; others wait and reuse it |\n\nThe fix that teams use is the xdist docs pattern: guard startup with a file lock under\n`tmp_path_factory.getbasetemp().parent`, which all workers share, and isolate data per worker with the\n`worker_id` fixture (for example a collection name suffixed with it).\n\n- **Teardown is the hard part.** No worker knows when the others have finished, so the lock pattern\n  suits read-only data you can leave behind; for a container, many teams start it once outside pytest\n  (a CI service container or `docker compose up`) and pass its address in.","patterns":["**Lane split with strict markers.** Register `integration` and `e2e` under `markers`, set `addopts = \"-ra --strict-markers\"`, run `-m \"not integration and not e2e\"` on every pull request and the slower, model-calling lanes nightly or on merge. Monitor PR-lane wall time with `--durations=10`.","**No real network in unit tests.** An `autouse` fixture in the root `conftest.py` uses `monkeypatch` to replace `socket.socket.connect` with a function that raises. A test that reaches a real LLM API then fails loudly instead of billing tokens. Monitor: any unit test that needs an opt-out marker.","**Production failures become case rows.** When an agent trace shows a reply your parser or dispatcher mishandled, paste the raw reply into a parametrized table with a descriptive id. The table grows into a regression suite of real model behaviour. Monitor how many rows came from incidents.","**Warnings as errors.** Set `filterwarnings = [\"error\"]` and whitelist known third-party warnings one by one, so deprecations from SDK upgrades fail CI before they become breakages. Review the whitelist on each dependency bump.","**Hang protection.** With `pytest-timeout`, set a suite-wide `timeout` in config (or `--timeout=60`), because a stuck model call or event loop otherwise hangs CI until the job limit. Track which tests hit the timeout.","**Order-independence and shared fakes.** Run with `pytest-randomly` so order changes every run and replay failures with `--randomly-seed=<n>`; monitor the flake rate per test id. Keep `fake_llm`, settings overrides and a temp workspace in the root `conftest.py`, and shadow them in `tests/integration/conftest.py` with real clients."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The model is nondeterministic, but everything around it is not: the\n  loop that stops after N steps, the dispatcher that rejects malformed tool arguments, the parser for\n  fenced JSON. Those bugs cause most agent incidents, and pytest pins them down without a single token.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A `fake_llm` fixture in the root `conftest.py`, parametrized\n  tables of model replies, marker lanes (`integration`, `e2e`) that keep real-API tests off pull\n  requests, and offline eval suites that many teams also run under pytest.\n- **What you control.** Fixture scope for costly resources (tokenizer, vector store container), which\n  lane may call a real model, a suite-wide block on network access, and timeouts so a hung model call\n  cannot stall CI.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing plugins and hook functions yourself. Know that plugins exist and change\n  fixture behaviour (xdist, asyncio); you rarely author one.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agent loop guards.** Parametrized tests pin the step limit, the stop condition and the handling of\n  a reply with no tool call, so a refactor of the loop cannot quietly remove a safety stop.\n- **Tool dispatcher hardening.** A table of malformed tool calls (unknown name, missing argument, wrong\n  type, fenced JSON) checks that each one becomes an error message to the model, not a crash.\n- **Prompt and context builders.** Tests assert that user text is escaped, that retrieved chunks are\n  ordered and trimmed to budget, and that the system prompt stays byte-stable across runs.\n- **Lane separation in CI.** Markers keep real-model tests in a nightly lane while pull requests run the\n  offline suite in seconds.","mini_project":"**Build an offline pytest suite for a tiny tool-calling agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** cover an agent loop with two tools using no network and no real model.\n- **Steps:** write a `fake_llm` fixture in `conftest.py` that replays a scripted list of replies;\n  parametrize at least eight cases (normal answer, one tool call, two chained calls, unknown tool,\n  malformed arguments, step limit reached, empty reply, tool raising); mark one real-model test\n  `integration` and exclude it by default.\n- **Done when:** the default run passes in under a few seconds with no network access, every case has a\n  readable id, and breaking the step limit in the loop makes exactly one test fail."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"pytest invoked\"] --> C[\"Collect: walk testpaths, import test_*.py\"]\n    C -->|\"import hook\"| R[\"Rewrite assert statements in test modules\"]\n    R --> P[\"Expand parametrize rows into test ids\"]\n    P --> F[\"Select lane with -k and -m\"]\n    F --> G[\"Resolve fixture graph by argument name\"]\n    G --> CF[(\"conftest.py: fake_llm, nearest wins\")]\n    CF --> U[\"Setup fixtures up to yield\"]\n    U --> T[\"Run test: loop with fake model\"]\n    T --> D[\"Teardown in reverse setup order\"]\n    D --> O[\"Report pass, fail, skip, xfail\"]\n    D -->|\"next test reuses cached higher-scope fixtures\"| G","caption":"Follow one run: collection and assertion rewriting happen before any test executes, then each test\nresolves its fixture graph (here, the fake model), runs, and tears fixtures down in reverse order."},"interview":[{"q":"How do you unit test an agent loop when the model is nondeterministic?","a":"- **Separate the deterministic shell from the model.** The loop, dispatcher, parser and stop conditions\n  are ordinary code; inject the model client so a test can pass a scripted fake.\n- **Script the replies, assert on the transcript.** The fake returns a tool call, then a final answer,\n  and records every message list it received, so the test checks the tool result reached the model.\n- **Cover the rare paths on purpose:** unknown tool, malformed arguments, a model that never finishes,\n  each as a parametrized case."},{"q":"What is a fixture's scope, and how do you choose one?","a":"- **Definition:** scope sets how often pytest rebuilds the fixture: `function` (default), `class`,\n  `module`, `package` or `session`. The value is cached and shared for that lifetime.\n- **Choose by cost and mutability.** Use session scope for expensive, read-only resources such as a loaded\n  tokenizer or a container; keep anything a test mutates, such as agent memory, at function scope,\n  because shared mutable state leaks between tests.\n- **Constraint:** a wider-scoped fixture cannot depend on a narrower one, or setup raises `ScopeMismatch`."},{"q":"A test passes alone but fails in the full suite. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Suspect shared state first:** a session or module fixture, a global, or an environment variable that an\n  earlier test changed without `monkeypatch`.\n- **Reproduce the order:** run with `pytest-randomly`, note the printed seed, and replay with\n  `--randomly-seed=<n>`; then run the suspected pair alone to confirm.\n- **Fix the cause:** narrow the fixture scope, reset state in a `yield` teardown, or use `monkeypatch` so\n  the change is undone."},{"q":"Your suite uses a session fixture that starts a container. What changes under pytest-xdist?","a":"- **Each worker is its own process with its own session**, so `-n auto` on 8 cores starts the container 8\n  times, with 8 times the memory.\n- **Share it with a file lock** under `tmp_path_factory.getbasetemp().parent`, which every worker sees, so\n  only the first worker starts it.\n- **Isolate data per worker** with the `worker_id` fixture, for example a collection name suffixed with it."},{"q":"How do you stop agent tests from silently calling a paid model API in CI?","a":"- **Block the network by default** with an `autouse` fixture in the root `conftest.py` that patches\n  `socket.socket.connect` to raise, so any accidental real call fails loudly.\n- **Put real-model tests in a registered lane** (`integration` or `e2e`) with `--strict-markers`, so a\n  typo cannot move them into the pull-request lane.\n- **Trade-off:** autouse fixtures hide setup, which is acceptable for a suite-wide invariant like this\n  and not for setup only some tests need."},{"q":"How would you keep a pull-request test lane fast as the suite grows to thousands of tests?","a":"- **Split lanes with registered markers** and `--strict-markers`, running `-m \"not integration and not\n  e2e\"` on each pull request and the slow lanes nightly or on merge.\n- **Measure before optimising:** `--durations=10` lists the slowest tests, which are often a mis-scoped\n  fixture rebuilt per test.\n- **Then parallelise** with `pytest-xdist` (`-n auto`), after confirming tests are order-independent."}],"tradeoffs":["**Concise injection vs hidden wiring.** Fixtures by name are short, but `conftest.py` hides where a value comes from; in a large suite you pay with `--fixtures` lookups and the occasional surprise override from a nearer `conftest.py`.","**Informative `assert` vs scope of rewriting.** It only applies to modules pytest imports; helper assertions in a non-test package get the plain `AssertionError` unless you register them.","**Session scope speed vs isolation.** It saves minutes when the resource is a loaded model or a container, but mutable state leaks between tests; the saving flips into flakiness the moment one test writes to the shared object, such as an agent memory store.","**Scripted fakes vs realism.** A scripted model makes loop tests fast and exact, but it only replays replies you imagined; it cannot tell you the real model emits a new shape. Pair it with a recorded or live lane.","**Free plugins vs interactions.** Parallelism, coverage and async support come cheap, but each adds configuration interactions (xdist and session fixtures, asyncio loop scopes) you must understand when a test passes alone and fails in the suite.","**`autouse` safety net vs readability.** It guarantees invariants (no real network, clean settings) in every test but hides setup; keep it for suite-wide invariants and name a fixture explicitly whenever only some tests need it."],"pitfalls":["**Misspelled marker, wrong lane.** Symptom: an `e2e` test hits a real API on every pull request. Fix: `--strict-markers` turns this into a collection error.","**Passes alone, fails together.** Usually a shared fixture mutated by an earlier test, such as a session-scoped agent memory. `pytest-randomly` shuffles order and prints its seed, `--randomly-seed=<n>` replays it, and running the suspected pair alone confirms the dependence.","**Floats compared with `==`.** Embedding similarities and loss values differ in the last bits across BLAS builds. Fix: `pytest.approx(expected, rel=1e-6)`.","**Catching too broadly.** `pytest.raises(Exception)` lets a `TypeError` from a typo pass as the intended `ValueError`; use the specific class and `match=` on the message.","**Fake model drifts from the real one.** Symptom: loop tests pass but production crashes on a reply shape the fake never produced. Fix: build fake replies from recorded real responses, not hand-typed dictionaries.","**Lost `print` debugging.** pytest captures stdout and only shows it for failures. `-s` disables capture; `capsys` asserts on it."],"when":"- **Use for:** every Python test in an agent codebase, including inherited unittest-style classes; no\n  modern Python scenario makes a different runner the better default.\n- **Decide:** which fixtures live in the root `conftest.py` (fake LLM, settings override, temporary\n  directory layout) versus a package-local one, which resources deserve session scope, and which markers\n  split fast unit tests from integration and model-calling lanes.\n- **Measure:** suite wall time with `--durations`; keep the pull-request lane under a few minutes,\n  because a slow suite is one people stop running.\n- **Interviews:** explain assertion rewriting, fixture scopes and the `conftest.py` lookup, and sketch a\n  test for an agent loop with a scripted model on the spot.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes parametrized tests for chunkers, prompt builders and output parsers, keeps a `fake_llm` fixture in `conftest.py`, and splits lanes with markers so unit tests stay fast.","architect":"Sets the suite's shape: which dependencies are allowed in the unit lane, the marker taxonomy, time budgets per lane, and the plugins the whole organisation standardises on.","fde":"Lands inside a customer repo with an unfamiliar suite and uses `--collect-only`, `--fixtures` and `-k` to find the tests covering the integration they are changing before touching code."},"check":{"q":"A colleague argues that `unittest` is \"good enough\" and pytest is just syntax sugar. Using the\nmechanics rather than taste, what does pytest provide that changes how a test suite is built?","a":"Two mechanisms, not sugar. First, assertion rewriting: pytest rewrites the AST of `assert`\nin test modules at import time so a bare comparison reports the intermediate values, which removes\nthe need for a dozen assertion methods and makes failures diagnosable without a debugger. Second,\nfixtures as dependency injection with scopes: a test names what it needs, pytest resolves a graph of\nfixtures with function, module or session lifetimes and runs teardown after `yield`, so expensive\nresources (a loaded tokenizer, a container) are built once and composed rather than duplicated in\n`setUp` methods. Add parametrization, which turns a table of cases into individually identifiable\ntests, and a hook system that lets plugins own concerns like async loops and parallel workers. Those\nchange the economics of writing tests, which is why suites built on pytest have more cases."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can pytest print the values inside a failing plain `assert` statement?","options":["Python's `AssertionError` always carries the evaluated sub-expressions","pytest rewrites the `assert` statements of test modules at import time into code that records intermediate values","pytest runs every test under `sys.settrace` and reconstructs the expression from the trace","The values come from the fixture that produced them, which pytest tracks by name"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Plain Python raises a bare `AssertionError`. pytest's import hook rewrites the AST of `assert` in collected test modules so the failure message includes each operand; tracing would be far too slow and could not see inside a single expression."},{"q":"A session-scoped fixture declares a dependency on a function-scoped fixture. What happens?","options":["It works; the function-scoped fixture is rebuilt for each test and injected into the shared one","It works but pytest emits a warning and caches the first value","pytest raises a `ScopeMismatch` error at setup time","The session fixture is silently downgraded to function scope"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A session-wide object is built once and cannot be rebuilt per test, so it cannot depend on something that changes per test. pytest refuses the graph with `ScopeMismatch` rather than guessing a lifetime."},{"q":"A test function carries two stacked `parametrize` decorators with 3 and 4 cases. How many tests run?","options":["4, because the inner decorator takes precedence","7, one per case across both decorators","12, the Cartesian product of the two case lists","It fails at collection because stacked parametrization is not allowed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each `parametrize` multiplies the existing set of test instances by its own cases, so stacking produces every combination. Use a single decorator with explicit tuples when only specific pairs are meaningful."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pytest-asyncio","sources":["Official docs: pytest-asyncio","Official docs: Python asyncio"],"why":"- **pytest calls tests synchronously.** An `async def` test is just a coroutine object nobody awaits.\n- **Agents are mostly I/O.** One agent step fans out to an LLM, a vector store, a reranker and several\n  tools, usually through `httpx.AsyncClient`, async database drivers and `asyncio.gather`.\n- **The interesting bugs only exist while the loop schedules tasks.** A semaphore that does not cap\n  concurrency, a timeout that leaves the request running, a cancelled stream that never closes its\n  connection, a retry that swallows `CancelledError`. Calling sync wrappers cannot reveal them.\n- **`def test_x(): asyncio.run(main())` is not enough.** It works for one coroutine but creates and\n  destroys a loop per call, offers no shared async setup and teardown (a client opened before the test\n  and closed after it), and cannot share an expensive resource such as a connection pool across tests.\n- **A bare `async def test_x()` is worse.** pytest has no idea how to run it: older versions warned and\n  skipped such tests, recent versions fail them. The plugin turns a coroutine into a test run.","how":"### What the plugin hooks into\n1. It registers hooks that recognise coroutine test functions and async fixtures.\n2. For a test it treats as async, it obtains an event loop for the configured scope.\n3. It drives the coroutine to completion on that loop and reports an ordinary pass, fail or error.\n4. Async fixtures (plain `async def` returning a value, or an async generator using `yield`) are wrapped\n   the same way: code before `yield` is setup on the loop, code after is teardown on the same loop,\n   because closing an async client on a different loop than it was opened on fails.\n\n### Strict vs auto mode\nSet with `asyncio_mode` in pytest config.\n\n| Mode | What runs as asyncio | Fixture decorator | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **strict** (default) | only tests marked `@pytest.mark.asyncio` | `@pytest_asyncio.fixture` | a project may use several async frameworks (asyncio, trio via anyio); the plugin should not claim tests it does not own |\n| **auto** | every `async def` test | plain `@pytest.fixture` works on async functions | removes boilerplate in an asyncio-only project |\n\n### Loop scope\n- **Scopes:** function (a fresh loop per test, the default), class, module, package or session.\n- **A fresh loop isolates tests**, because leftover tasks and callbacks from one test cannot leak into\n  the next.\n- **A wider loop is needed** when a wide-scoped async fixture (a session-wide database pool) is used by\n  tests, because those tests must run on the loop the pool was created on.\n- **Recent API:** `loop_scope` arguments on the marker and on `pytest_asyncio.fixture`, plus a\n  default-fixture-loop-scope config key (`asyncio_default_fixture_loop_scope`).\n- **Old API:** an overridable `event_loop` fixture, deprecated and since removed. Check the docs for your\n  pinned version, because this API changed more than once.\n\n### What an agent test suite needs from it\n| Agent behaviour | What the test asserts | Needs a running loop because |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parallel tool calls in one step | results keep call order, one failure does not sink the rest | `gather` schedules the tools concurrently |\n| Per-tool timeout | slow tool returns an error result and is cancelled | only the loop can cancel a pending await |\n| User aborts a streamed answer | the stream and its HTTP response close | cleanup runs on cancellation |\n| Concurrency cap on model calls | peak in-flight calls never exceed the limit | overlap exists only while tasks interleave |\n\n### What it does not do\n- **No concurrency between tests.** pytest still runs one test at a time and the plugin runs each\n  coroutine to completion before the next, so async does not speed up a slow suite (use pytest-xdist).\n  Concurrency exists only *inside* a test, among the tasks it creates.\n- **No fake clock.** `asyncio.sleep(10)` really waits ten seconds, which is why timeout and retry tests\n  use tiny delays or inject the delay or clock as a parameter.\n- **No cleanup of your tasks.** Tasks a test spawned and never awaited are still pending when the loop\n  closes; they surface as warnings or as errors attributed to a later test.\n- **Alternative loops (uvloop).** Use the plugin's documented mechanism for your pinned version (an\n  event-loop policy fixture in recent releases) rather than constructing and swapping loop objects\n  yourself. Python 3.14 deprecates loop policies, so expect this to move again.\n\n### The alternative: anyio's plugin\n- **`@pytest.mark.anyio`** can run the same test under asyncio and trio. If your code is written against\n  anyio (as Starlette and httpx internals are), it is a reasonable alternative.\n- **Pick one per suite.** With pytest-asyncio in auto mode both plugins can try to claim the same\n  coroutine tests, and the resulting errors are confusing to debug.","example":"### Agent scenario: parallel tool calls with a per-tool timeout\nThe agent runs every tool call the model requested in one step. A slow tool must come back as an error\nresult the model can read, not hang the step, and must actually be cancelled.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport pytest\n\nasync def run_tool_calls(calls, tools, timeout_s):\n    async def one(call):\n        try:\n            async with asyncio.timeout(timeout_s):           # Python 3.11+\n                return {\"id\": call[\"id\"], \"content\": await tools[call[\"name\"]](**call[\"args\"])}\n        except TimeoutError:\n            return {\"id\": call[\"id\"], \"error\": f\"{call['name']} timed out after {timeout_s}s\"}\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(one(c) for c in calls))    # keeps call order\n\ndef make_tools(state):\n    async def search(q):\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n        return f\"results for {q}\"\n    async def browse(url):\n        state[\"browse_open\"] = True\n        try:\n            await asyncio.sleep(10)                          # a hung page load\n        finally:\n            state[\"browse_open\"] = False                     # runs on cancellation\n    return {\"search\": search, \"browse\": browse}\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\nasync def test_slow_tool_becomes_error_result_and_is_cancelled():\n    state = {}\n    calls = [{\"id\": \"c1\", \"name\": \"search\", \"args\": {\"q\": \"kv cache\"}},\n             {\"id\": \"c2\", \"name\": \"browse\", \"args\": {\"url\": \"https://example.com\"}}]\n    out = await run_tool_calls(calls, make_tools(state), timeout_s=0.05)\n    assert out[0] == {\"id\": \"c1\", \"content\": \"results for kv cache\"}\n    assert \"timed out\" in out[1][\"error\"]\n    assert state[\"browse_open\"] is False                     # no tool left running\n```\n\n- **Why it matters:** without the timeout one hung tool stalls the whole agent; without cancellation the\n  hung task keeps a connection open after the step has moved on.\n- **Fast:** total runtime is about 0.05 s because the delays are injected, not production-sized.\n\n### Fan-out limit and timeout cancellation\nA fan-out helper must never exceed a concurrency limit, and a timeout must leave no request in flight.\nBoth properties exist only while the loop runs. A fake LLM records peak concurrency; the async fixture\nchecks in teardown that nothing leaked.\n\n```python\n# pyproject.toml: [tool.pytest.ini_options] asyncio_mode = \"strict\"\nimport asyncio\nimport pytest\nimport pytest_asyncio\n\nclass FakeLLM:\n    def __init__(self, delay: float):\n        self.delay, self.in_flight, self.peak = delay, 0, 0\n    async def complete(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n        self.in_flight += 1\n        self.peak = max(self.peak, self.in_flight)\n        try:\n            await asyncio.sleep(self.delay)\n            return prompt.upper()\n        finally:\n            self.in_flight -= 1          # runs on success and on cancellation\n\nasync def fan_out(llm, prompts, limit: int):\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(limit)\n    async def one(p):\n        async with sem:\n            return await llm.complete(p)\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(one(p) for p in prompts))\n\n@pytest_asyncio.fixture\nasync def llm():\n    client = FakeLLM(delay=0.01)\n    yield client\n    assert client.in_flight == 0          # teardown, same loop as the test\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\nasync def test_fan_out_respects_limit(llm):\n    out = await fan_out(llm, [f\"p{i}\" for i in range(20)], limit=4)\n    assert out == [f\"P{i}\" for i in range(20)]   # gather preserves order\n    assert llm.peak == 4\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\nasync def test_timeout_cancels_inner_call(llm):\n    llm.delay = 10\n    with pytest.raises(TimeoutError):\n        async with asyncio.timeout(0.05):        # Python 3.11+\n            await llm.complete(\"x\")\n    assert llm.in_flight == 0\n```\n\n- **Fast:** the suite runs in well under a second because the delays are tiny.\n- **What a bug looks like:** a broken limit shows up as `peak == 20`; a timeout that does not cancel\n  shows up as `in_flight == 1`.\n\n### Streams must close on early stop and on cancel\nA token stream wraps an HTTP response; when the agent stops early (a stop sequence, a guardrail) or the\nuser disconnects, its cleanup must run.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import aclosing        # Python 3.10+\nimport pytest\n\nasync def stream_tokens(state: dict, n: int = 100):\n    state[\"open\"] = True\n    try:\n        for i in range(n):\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.001)      # stands in for a network read\n            yield f\"t{i}\"\n    finally:\n        state[\"open\"] = False           # stands in for closing the HTTP response\n\nasync def first_k(state: dict, k: int) -> list[str]:\n    out: list[str] = []\n    async with aclosing(stream_tokens(state)) as agen:\n        async for tok in agen:\n            out.append(tok)\n            if len(out) == k:\n                break                   # aclosing runs the generator's finally now\n    return out\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\nasync def test_early_stop_closes_stream():\n    state: dict = {}\n    assert await first_k(state, 3) == [\"t0\", \"t1\", \"t2\"]\n    assert state[\"open\"] is False       # without aclosing, still True here\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\nasync def test_cancel_mid_stream_closes_stream():\n    state: dict = {}\n    task = asyncio.create_task(first_k(state, 50))\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.01)\n    task.cancel()\n    with pytest.raises(asyncio.CancelledError):\n        await task\n    assert state[\"open\"] is False\n```\n\n- **Why `aclosing`:** breaking out of `async for` leaves the async generator suspended; its `finally`\n  runs only when it is finalized later. `aclosing` closes it at the end of the block.\n- **The cancel test** proves the cleanup also runs when the consumer task is cancelled mid-stream.\n\n### Worked incident: \"attached to a different loop\" only in CI\n| Step | What happened |\n|---|---|\n| Fixture | session-scoped async fixture opens a connection pool on the first test's loop |\n| Locally | developer runs one test; only one loop exists, so it passes |\n| CI, full suite | test 2 runs on a new function-scoped loop and reuses a pooled connection |\n| Symptom | `RuntimeError` mentioning a Future \"attached to a different loop\" |\n| Fix | `loop_scope=\"session\"` on both the fixture and the marker of tests that use it |\n\n```python\n@pytest_asyncio.fixture(scope=\"session\", loop_scope=\"session\")\nasync def pool():\n    p = await create_pool(DSN)        # your driver's pool factory\n    yield p\n    await p.close()\n\npytestmark = pytest.mark.asyncio(loop_scope=\"session\")   # every test in this module\n```","patterns":["**Every tool call has a timeout under test.** For each async tool, one test runs it with a fake that never returns and asserts the step yields an error result and the task is cancelled. Monitor production tool-timeout rates per tool; a tool with no timeout test is the one that hangs an agent.","**Explicit, pinned config.** Pin the plugin version and set `asyncio_mode = \"strict\"` and `asyncio_default_fixture_loop_scope = \"function\"` in `[tool.pytest.ini_options]`, so behaviour does not shift on upgrade. Monitor deprecation warnings from the plugin in CI output after each bump.","**Leaked-task guard.** An autouse `@pytest_asyncio.fixture` that, after `yield`, collects `asyncio.all_tasks()` except `asyncio.current_task()`, cancels any not done and raises, so pytest reports a teardown error. Leaks then land on the test that caused them, not a later one. Watch for tests that need an opt-out.","**asyncio debug mode in CI.** Run the async lane with `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` (or `python -X dev`): asyncio then logs callbacks slower than `loop.slow_callback_duration` (0.1 s by default) and gives \"never awaited\" warnings a traceback. Grep CI logs for \"Executing\" warnings to catch blocking calls.","**Session pool, session loop, one module.** Put tests that share a costly async pool in one module with `pytestmark = pytest.mark.asyncio(loop_scope=\"session\")`, and keep everything else on function loops. Monitor order-dependent failures with randomized order.","**Injected delays and clocks.** Pass retry backoff and timeouts as parameters (or a `sleep` callable) so tests use 0.01 s where production uses seconds. Track async-lane wall time; a jump usually means a real delay crept in."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A step that runs three tool calls with `asyncio.gather`, a per-tool\n  timeout, a token stream the user can abort: each has bugs that only appear while the loop schedules\n  tasks. A sync test of the same code proves nothing about them.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Async SDK clients (`httpx.AsyncClient` underneath most LLM SDKs),\n  async agent frameworks, FastAPI streaming endpoints, MCP clients and servers. Their tests run under this\n  plugin or anyio's.\n- **What you control.** `asyncio_mode`, the loop scope of each fixture and test, injected delays so retry\n  and backoff tests stay fast, and a guard that fails any test leaving a tool task pending.\n- **What you can skip.** Custom loop policies and the old `event_loop` override. Pin the plugin, set the\n  config keys explicitly and use the defaults.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Parallel tool calls.** Tests check that a step running tools with `asyncio.gather` respects its\n  concurrency cap and returns results in the order the model asked for them.\n- **Per-tool timeouts.** A fake tool that never returns proves the timeout fires, the task is cancelled\n  and the model receives a timeout message instead of the loop hanging.\n- **Streaming and abort.** Tests of a streaming endpoint check that a user abort closes the upstream\n  stream and that `CancelledError` is not swallowed by a retry wrapper.\n- **Async clients and servers.** MCP clients, async SDK wrappers and FastAPI agent endpoints are tested\n  with shared async fixtures that open and close their connections cleanly.","mini_project":"**Test the concurrency of an async agent step (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove that one agent step runs tool calls concurrently, caps them and handles a slow tool.\n- **Steps:** write an async step function that fans out tool calls behind a semaphore; build fake tools\n  that `await asyncio.sleep` and record peak concurrency; add tests for the cap, for result ordering,\n  for a tool that exceeds its timeout, and for cancellation of the whole step; set `asyncio_mode`\n  explicitly in config.\n- **Done when:** all tests pass in well under a second, raising the semaphore limit makes the cap test\n  fail, and replacing an `await asyncio.sleep` with `time.sleep` makes the concurrency test fail."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant P as pytest\n    participant A as pytest-asyncio\n    participant L as Event loop\n    participant F as Async fixture\n    participant T as Test coroutine\n    P->>A: collect test marked asyncio\n    A->>L: get or create loop for the configured loop_scope\n    A->>F: run setup up to yield on L\n    A->>T: drive coroutine to completion on L\n    T-->>A: pass, fail or error\n    A->>F: run teardown after yield on the same L\n    A->>L: close loop when its scope ends\n    Note over L: pending tasks left here surface as warnings in later tests\n    A-->>P: ordinary test report","caption":"One async test under strict mode with function loop scope: setup, the test body and teardown all run on\nthe same loop, which is closed when the test ends."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't pytest run an async def test without a plugin?","a":"- **pytest calls tests synchronously**, so calling an `async def` test only creates a coroutine object\n  that nobody awaits; recent pytest fails such tests and older versions warned and skipped them.\n- **The plugin supplies the loop:** it recognises coroutine tests and async fixtures, obtains a loop for\n  the configured scope and drives each coroutine to completion.\n- **Why not `asyncio.run` in a sync test:** it gives no async setup and teardown and cannot share a pool\n  across tests."},{"q":"How would you test that a fan-out helper never exceeds its concurrency limit?","a":"- **Use a fake dependency that awaits**, for example `asyncio.sleep(0.01)`, and records `in_flight` and\n  `peak` counters in a `try`/`finally`.\n- **Run enough calls to saturate the limit**, such as 20 prompts with `limit=4`, and assert `peak == 4`.\n- **Never block in the fake:** `time.sleep` stops task switching, so calls never overlap and the test\n  passes even when the limit is broken."},{"q":"An agent step runs tool calls in parallel. How do you test that one hung tool cannot stall the agent?","a":"- **Give each call a timeout** (`asyncio.timeout`) and turn `TimeoutError` into an error result the model\n  can read, so the step completes with partial results.\n- **Test it with a fake tool that sleeps far longer than the timeout** and records cleanup in a\n  `finally`; assert the error result, the order of results, and that the cleanup ran.\n- **Keep the timeout tiny in the test**, such as 0.05 s, so the case costs nothing in CI."},{"q":"When would you choose auto mode over strict mode, and when not?","a":"- **Auto mode suits an asyncio-only project:** every `async def` test is claimed and plain\n  `@pytest.fixture` works on async functions, which removes boilerplate.\n- **Strict mode suits mixed suites:** when anyio or trio tests exist, auto mode claims coroutine tests it\n  does not own and the errors are confusing.\n- **Either way, set it explicitly** with `asyncio_mode` and pin the plugin, because defaults and APIs have\n  changed between versions."},{"q":"Async tests leave \"Task was destroyed but it is pending\" warnings in CI. How do you find the culprit?","a":"- **Cause:** a test called `asyncio.create_task` and never awaited or cancelled it, so the task is still\n  pending when its loop closes and the warning lands on a later test.\n- **Locate it** with an autouse async fixture that, after `yield`, checks `asyncio.all_tasks()` minus\n  `asyncio.current_task()` and fails the test that leaked.\n- **Get tracebacks** by running the lane with `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` or `python -X dev`."},{"q":"A user closes the browser mid-answer. How do you prove the agent stops spending tokens?","a":"- **Model the disconnect as cancellation** of the task consuming the stream, as a server does when the\n  client goes away.\n- **Test it:** start the consumer with `asyncio.create_task`, cancel it after a few tokens, expect\n  `CancelledError`, and assert the stream's `finally` closed the response.\n- **Also cover early stop** with `aclosing`, since a `break` alone leaves the generator suspended.\n- **Check retries do not undo it:** a retry wrapper that catches `CancelledError` (or a bare `except`)\n  restarts the stream; one test cancels mid-retry and asserts no new model call was made."}],"tradeoffs":["**Function-scoped loops: isolation vs rebuild cost.** Every test is isolated from leaked tasks, but expensive async resources are rebuilt per test; widen loop scope only for genuinely costly fixtures.","**Auto mode: less boilerplate vs claiming everything.** It removes markers and special fixture decorators but claims every coroutine test, so it fits asyncio-only projects and breaks once anyio or trio tests appear in the same suite.","**Real `asyncio.sleep`: faithful vs slow.** Real delays match production timing but make suites slow and timing-sensitive; injecting a clock or tiny delays wins once the suite grows past a few minutes.","**Real event loop: catches scheduling bugs vs harder tests.** It finds bugs sync wrappers hide, but tests are harder to read and debug. If the property does not depend on scheduling (a prompt builder that happens to be `async`), extract it and test it synchronously.","**Session-scoped loops: one pool vs shared poison.** One connection pool serves the whole suite, but one test's leaked task or closed client poisons every later test. Switch to function scope as soon as failures appear only in particular test orders."],"pitfalls":["**Session async fixture, function-loop tests.** Symptom: `RuntimeError` mentioning a Future or Task \"attached to a different loop\", often only in CI ordering. Fix: matching `loop_scope`.","**Missing marker or decorator in strict mode.** A missing marker makes recent pytest fail the test as an unsupported `async def` (older pytest warned and skipped it). A missing `pytest_asyncio.fixture` hands the test an un-awaited coroutine or async generator object, so it fails on first attribute access or with a \"never awaited\" warning.","**Blocking calls in async tests.** `time.sleep`, sync HTTP or sync DB drivers inside an async test or fixture: the test passes but serialises everything, so concurrency assertions are silently meaningless.","**Orphaned tasks.** `asyncio.create_task` without awaiting or cancelling, common in agents that start background tool calls or memory writes. Symptom: \"Task was destroyed but it is pending\" warnings and assertions failing in a later, unrelated test.","**Stale `event_loop` override.** Copied from an old blog post into a pinned recent version: on later 0.2x releases it gives a deprecation warning; on 1.x the plugin no longer consults that fixture at all, so your custom loop or policy silently stops applying.","**Fake-clock assumptions in retry tests.** The plugin has no fake clock, so a retry test with production backoff really waits `asyncio.sleep(10)`. Fix: inject a `sleep` callable or the delays and pass 0.01 s, and use `asyncio.timeout(0.05)` for timeout paths."],"when":"- **Use when:** the unit under test is a coroutine and the property depends on scheduling: parallel tool\n  calls, concurrency caps, timeouts, cancellation, streaming, ordering of `gather` results, backpressure.\n- **Avoid when:** pure logic merely sits inside async code; extract it into a sync function and test it\n  without a loop.\n- **Configure:** pin the plugin and set `asyncio_mode` and the default fixture loop scope explicitly, so\n  behaviour does not change on upgrade.\n- **Per-test hang limit:** wrap risky awaits in `asyncio.timeout(...)` inside the test, or use\n  `pytest-timeout`, so a never-resolving future fails one test instead of hanging the CI job.\n- **FastAPI apps:** pair it with an async HTTP client against the ASGI app, for streaming agent endpoints.\n- **Interviews:** explaining the \"attached to a different loop\" error from first principles signals you\n  understand both asyncio and pytest fixtures.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes async tests for fan-out, retry and streaming code, choosing loop scopes so shared pools work without leaking state between tests.","architect":"Sets the project-wide async testing convention: one async framework, pinned plugin, explicit mode and loop scope, and a rule that blocking calls in async code fail lint or review.","fde":"Reproduces a customer's intermittent timeout or hung stream as a small async test with a fake slow dependency, so the fix can be shown working before redeploying."},"check":{"q":"Your suite has a session-scoped async fixture that opens an `httpx.AsyncClient`. Tests pass\nwhen run one at a time but fail in CI with \"attached to a different loop\". What is happening and how\ndo you fix it?","a":"The client lives for the whole session, but each test by default runs on its own\nfunction-scoped loop that is closed when the test ends. The client's pooled connections are bound to\nwhichever loop was running when they were opened, typically the first test that made a request. The\nnext test pulls those keep-alive connections from the pool and awaits I/O tied to a dead loop. Run\nalone, only one test loop ever exists, so nothing is shared across loops and it passes. Fixes: run the\nfixture and the tests that use it on the same wider loop (`loop_scope=\"session\"` on both\n`pytest_asyncio.fixture` and the `asyncio` marker), or make the fixture function-scoped and accept\nthe cost, or create the client inside each test. The principle is that async resources are bound to\nthe loop they were made on, so fixture lifetime and loop lifetime must match."},"quiz":[{"q":"In pytest-asyncio's strict mode, what happens to an unmarked `async def` test?","options":["It is run automatically on a fresh event loop","The plugin does not claim it, so it is not run as an asyncio test","It runs on the session event loop instead of a function-scoped one","It is converted into a synchronous test by wrapping it in asyncio.run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Strict mode only treats tests with `@pytest.mark.asyncio` as asyncio tests, so multiple async plugins can coexist; an unclaimed coroutine test is then failed (or, on older pytest, skipped) by pytest itself. Automatic claiming of every coroutine test is what auto mode does."},{"q":"Why does an async generator fixture run its teardown on the same loop as its setup?","options":["Because pytest requires all fixtures to share one thread","Because a new loop would rerun the setup code","Because async resources such as clients and pools are bound to the loop they were created on","Because teardown code is not allowed to await anything"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Closing a connection or awaiting a future from another loop raises errors, so setup, the test and teardown must all execute on one loop. Thread sharing is a separate concern from loop binding."},{"q":"A concurrency-limit test passes, yet production exceeds the limit. Which test flaw best explains it?","options":["The test used a function-scoped event loop","The fake dependency blocked with time.sleep, so calls never overlapped and peak concurrency stayed low","The test used auto mode instead of strict mode","The fixture was declared with pytest_asyncio.fixture"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A blocking sleep stops the loop from switching tasks, so the test never produced concurrent calls to count. Loop scope and plugin mode affect which loop runs the test, not whether calls overlap."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/test-doubles-mocks-fixtures","sources":["Meszaros, 2007 — xUnit Test Patterns","Fowler, 2007 — Mocks Aren't Stubs","Official docs: unittest.mock","Official docs: pytest"],"why":"- **Real dependencies are unaffordable in unit tests.** A real LLM call costs money, takes hundreds of\n  milliseconds to seconds, returns different text each run, rate-limits you in CI, and needs a secret\n  the runner may not have. A vector database needs a server; a payment or ticketing API has side effects.\n- **Through the real thing, the suite dies.** Tests of retry logic, prompt assembly or tool dispatch get\n  slow, flaky and expensive, and people stop running them.\n- **You cannot make a live service misbehave on demand.** \"The model returned an empty string\", \"the API\n  timed out on the second attempt\", \"the tool returned malformed JSON\" are the branches that matter most\n  and the ones a live service rarely produces when you want.\n- **Patching everything with `MagicMock` fails differently.** A bare mock accepts any attribute and call,\n  so a test passes while code calls a method the real client lacks; asserting on every internal call\n  breaks on every refactor even when behaviour is unchanged.\n- **The vocabulary picks the smallest stand-in** that verifies what you actually care about.","how":"### The kinds of double\nMeszaros's taxonomy, which most of the industry uses loosely:\n\n| Kind | What it does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Dummy** | fills a parameter; never used | `client=None` when the path under test does not touch the client |\n| **Stub** | returns canned answers to drive a branch; you assert on the code's output | \"Whatever you are asked, reply `'{\"label\": \"spam\"}'`.\" |\n| **Spy** | a stub that also records calls, checked afterwards | \"was the second attempt sent with a lower temperature?\" |\n| **Mock** | pre-programmed with call expectations; fails if interactions differ | `unittest.mock.Mock` is technically a spy you assert on after the fact |\n| **Fake** | a working, simplified implementation | in-memory vector store ranking by cosine similarity; dict cache with real TTL; a model server speaking the provider's HTTP API |\n\n- **Fakes cost more code** but make tests read like usage and can be shared across suites.\n- **The \"fake LLM\" naming caveat.** One that pops scripted replies off a list is strictly a stub that\n  records calls (a spy); it has no behaviour of its own. Teams call it a fake anyway. The real question:\n  does this double *compute* anything, or only replay what the test told it?\n\n### Doubles in an agent stack\n| Dependency | Usual double | Verify |\n|---|---|---|\n| The model | scripted stub-with-spy emitting tool calls and final answers | the loop's outcome and the transcript it sent |\n| Read-only tools (search, fetch) | fake returning canned results, recording arguments | the result reached the model |\n| Side-effecting tools (send, delete, pay) | spy or autospecced mock | the call happened, or never happened, as required |\n| Vector store | in-memory fake ranking by cosine similarity | retrieved chunks and their order |\n| Memory store | dict-backed fake with the real interface | what was written and read back |\n| Clock, ids, randomness | injected callables | timeouts, retries, stable ids |\n\n### State vs behaviour verification\n| | Doubles | Test checks | Survives refactoring? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **State verification** | stubs, fakes | the result | yes |\n| **Behaviour verification** | mocks, spies | the calls | no; right only when the call *is* the behaviour |\n\nCalls that *are* the behaviour: an email was sent, a tool was invoked with these arguments, a charge was\nnot made twice.\n\n### unittest.mock mechanics\n- **`Mock` / `MagicMock`** create attributes on access and record every call in `call_args_list`.\n  `MagicMock` also supports dunder methods (`len`, iteration, context managers). `AsyncMock` returns\n  awaitables, for async clients.\n- **Scripting.** `return_value` and `side_effect`; `side_effect` can be an exception (raised every call),\n  a callable, or an iterable consumed one item per call in which any exception item is raised.\n  `side_effect=[TimeoutError(), \"ok\"]` simulates \"timeout, then success\".\n- **Spec it.** `Mock(spec_set=RealClass)` rejects attributes the real class lacks (and assignment of new\n  ones); `create_autospec(RealClass, instance=True)` also checks method call signatures. This matters\n  because an unspecced mock happily accepts `client.compelte(...)` and the typo ships.\n- **`patch(\"pkg.module.name\")` replaces a name *where it is looked up*.** If `app/summarize.py` does\n  `from app.llm import call_llm`, patch `app.summarize.call_llm`; patching `app.llm.call_llm` changes\n  nothing the code sees, because `from x import y` copies a reference into the importing namespace at\n  import time and `patch` rebinds only one entry.\n- **`patch.object(obj, \"attr\")`** avoids the string path entirely.\n- **`Mock(wraps=real)` is Python's true spy.** Calls go through to the real object and are recorded:\n  \"did the retry wrapper call the real tokenizer exactly once?\"\n\n### Fixtures\n- **Injection.** A pytest fixture's return (or `yield`ed) value is injected into any test naming it as a\n  parameter. Code after `yield` is teardown and runs even if the test fails.\n- **Scope** (`function`, `class`, `module`, `package`, `session`) sets how often it is rebuilt: wide for\n  expensive immutable things (a loaded tokenizer), function scope for anything a test can mutate.\n- **`conftest.py`** fixtures are shared by every test below that directory.\n- **Factory fixture.** Returns a function, so each test builds its own variant (\"an LLM that replies with\n  these three strings\").\n- **Built-ins:** `monkeypatch` sets env vars and attributes and undoes them; `tmp_path` gives an isolated\n  directory.\n\n### Prefer injection over patching\n- **Injected client, no patching.** If a function (or constructor) takes its client as a parameter, the\n  test passes a fake.\n- **Patching is for code you cannot restructure**; dependency injection is what makes doubles cheap.\n\n### Where to put the seam\nFor an LLM call there are three places to cut, and each decides what the test still exercises:\n1. **Your own interface** (`LLMClient.complete`): a fake replaces everything below it. Fastest and\n   simplest, but the SDK, request building, retries and response parsing are not tested at all.\n2. **The HTTP transport**: `respx` (for httpx) or recorded cassettes (vcrpy, pytest-recording) intercept\n   requests below the SDK. The real SDK serialises your request and parses a realistic response, so a\n   wrong field name or a changed response shape fails here.\n3. **A fake server process**: the code talks to a real socket. This is the integration level, covered in\n   the integration-testing lesson.\n\nCut as high as the test allows, because higher seams are faster and less brittle. Move the seam down\nonly for code whose correctness depends on what is below it, such as response parsing.","example":"### Agent scenario: tool dispatch with a scripted model and recording tools\nThe model is a scripted stub; tools are fakes that record what they ran with. State checks cover the\nnormal path; a call check covers the one requirement that is an interaction: a destructive tool never\nruns without approval.\n\n```python\nclass FakeTool:\n    def __init__(self, result, destructive=False):\n        self.result, self.destructive, self.runs = result, destructive, []\n    def __call__(self, **kwargs):\n        self.runs.append(kwargs)\n        return self.result\n\nclass ScriptedLLM:\n    def __init__(self, *actions): self.actions, self.seen = list(actions), []\n    def next_action(self, transcript):\n        self.seen.append(list(transcript))\n        return self.actions.pop(0)\n\ndef run_step(llm, tools, transcript, approve):\n    action = llm.next_action(transcript)\n    tool = tools.get(action[\"tool\"])\n    if tool is None:\n        return {\"error\": f\"unknown tool {action['tool']}\"}\n    if tool.destructive and not approve(action):\n        return {\"error\": \"denied by approval policy\"}\n    return {\"result\": tool(**action[\"args\"])}\n\ndef test_read_tool_result_is_returned():\n    search = FakeTool([\"doc-7\", \"doc-2\"])\n    llm = ScriptedLLM({\"tool\": \"search\", \"args\": {\"q\": \"refund policy\"}})\n    assert run_step(llm, {\"search\": search}, [], approve=lambda a: True) == {\"result\": [\"doc-7\", \"doc-2\"]}\n    assert search.runs == [{\"q\": \"refund policy\"}]\n\ndef test_destructive_tool_never_runs_without_approval():\n    delete = FakeTool(\"deleted\", destructive=True)\n    llm = ScriptedLLM({\"tool\": \"delete_file\", \"args\": {\"path\": \"/data/a.csv\"}})\n    out = run_step(llm, {\"delete_file\": delete}, [], approve=lambda a: False)\n    assert out == {\"error\": \"denied by approval policy\"}\n    assert delete.runs == []                 # the interaction is the requirement\n\ndef test_hallucinated_tool_is_an_error_result():\n    llm = ScriptedLLM({\"tool\": \"rm_rf\", \"args\": {}})\n    assert run_step(llm, {}, [], approve=lambda a: True) == {\"error\": \"unknown tool rm_rf\"}\n```\n\n- **No mocks of the registry or the loop:** the real dispatch code runs, so refactoring it does not\n  break these tests unless behaviour changes.\n- **One behaviour assertion, on purpose:** `delete.runs == []` is the safety property itself.\n\n### Summarizer: scripted fake, autospec mock, monkeypatch\nA summarizer retries once on empty output and truncates to a limit read from the environment. Three\ndoubles: a scripted stub-with-spy (what most teams call a fake LLM) for state checks, an autospecced\nmock for one interaction that matters, and `monkeypatch` for configuration.\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport pytest\nfrom unittest.mock import create_autospec\n\nclass LLMClient:                                   # the real client's interface\n    def complete(self, messages: list[dict], *, model: str) -> str: ...\n\ndef summarize(text: str, *, client, model: str = \"large\") -> str:\n    limit = int(os.environ.get(\"SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS\", \"500\"))\n    for _ in range(2):\n        out = client.complete([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Summarize: {text}\"}], model=model)\n        if out.strip():\n            return out[:limit]\n    raise RuntimeError(\"model returned empty output twice\")\n\nclass FakeLLM:                                     # stub + spy: scripted replies, records calls\n    def __init__(self, replies): self.replies, self.calls = list(replies), []\n    def complete(self, messages, *, model):\n        self.calls.append((messages, model))\n        return self.replies.pop(0)\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef make_llm():                                    # factory fixture\n    return lambda *replies: FakeLLM(replies)\n\ndef test_retries_once_on_empty(make_llm):\n    llm = make_llm(\"\", \"a summary\")\n    assert summarize(\"long text\", client=llm) == \"a summary\"\n    assert len(llm.calls) == 2\n\ndef test_gives_up_after_two_empties(make_llm):\n    with pytest.raises(RuntimeError):\n        summarize(\"x\", client=make_llm(\"\", \"  \"))\n\ndef test_forwards_model_name():\n    llm = create_autospec(LLMClient, instance=True)    # rejects calls the real class would reject\n    llm.complete.return_value = \"ok\"\n    summarize(\"text\", client=llm, model=\"small\")\n    assert llm.complete.call_args.kwargs[\"model\"] == \"small\"\n\ndef test_truncates_to_env_limit(monkeypatch, make_llm):\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\"SUMMARY_MAX_CHARS\", \"5\")       # undone after the test\n    assert summarize(\"x\", client=make_llm(\"abcdefgh\")) == \"abcde\"\n```\n\nNone of these tests hit the network, and none breaks if `summarize` changes how it builds its prompt.\n\n### Transport seam: `httpx.MockTransport` scripts a 429, then a reply\nCutting at the HTTP layer exercises request building, status handling and response parsing. Runnable\nwith `pip install httpx pytest`.\n\n```python\nimport httpx\nimport pytest\n\ndef classify(text: str, http: httpx.Client) -> str:\n    for attempt in range(3):\n        r = http.post(\"/v1/chat\", json={\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text}]})\n        if r.status_code == 429 and attempt < 2:\n            continue                                   # real code would back off here\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        return r.json()[\"choices\"][0][\"message\"][\"content\"].strip().lower()\n    raise AssertionError(\"unreachable\")\n\ndef scripted(*responses):\n    seen, queue = [], list(responses)\n    def handler(request: httpx.Request) -> httpx.Response:\n        seen.append(request)\n        return queue.pop(0)\n    return httpx.MockTransport(handler), seen\n\ndef reply(content):\n    return httpx.Response(200, json={\"choices\": [{\"message\": {\"content\": content}}]})\n\ndef test_retries_rate_limit_then_parses():\n    transport, seen = scripted(httpx.Response(429), reply(\"  SPAM\\n\"))\n    with httpx.Client(transport=transport, base_url=\"https://llm.test\") as http:\n        assert classify(\"win a prize\", http) == \"spam\"\n    assert len(seen) == 2\n    assert seen[0].url.path == \"/v1/chat\"\n\ndef test_gives_up_after_three_429s():\n    transport, _ = scripted(*[httpx.Response(429)] * 3)\n    with httpx.Client(transport=transport, base_url=\"https://llm.test\") as http:\n        with pytest.raises(httpx.HTTPStatusError):\n            classify(\"x\", http)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: which patch target?\n| Code under test in `app/summarize.py` | Lookup at call time | Correct target |\n|---|---|---|\n| `from app.llm import call_llm` then `call_llm(...)` | `app.summarize` namespace | `app.summarize.call_llm` |\n| `import app.llm` then `app.llm.call_llm(...)` | `app.llm` module attribute | `app.llm.call_llm` |\n| `self.client.complete(...)` with injected client | the object passed in | no patch: pass a fake |\n\nThe wrong target in row 1 shows up as a test that still makes real network calls or fails with a missing\nAPI key despite the patch.","patterns":["**One shared fake client.** Keep a `FakeLLM` and a factory fixture (`make_llm`) in the root `conftest.py`, scripting replies, exceptions and timeouts (`AsyncMock(side_effect=[TimeoutError(), \"ok\"])` for async). Every new branch test then costs one line. Watch for teams forking their own fakes.","**Autospec by default.** Use `create_autospec(RealClient, instance=True)` (or `spec_set=`) for any remaining mock, and flag bare `MagicMock()` for clients in review. Monitor production `AttributeError` or `TypeError` from client calls that tests missed.","**Record real responses, replay in CI.** With pytest-recording, mark tests `@pytest.mark.vcr`, record once locally, and run CI with `--record-mode=none` so any unrecorded request fails. Strip secrets via a `vcr_config` fixture returning `{\"filter_headers\": [\"authorization\"]}`. Re-record on SDK upgrades.","**Realistic stub data.** Store sanitised real model outputs (fenced JSON, preambles, trailing prose) as fixture files and feed parsers from them, so stubs look like what models return, mess included. Add every production parse failure as a new fixture file.","**Keep fakes honest.** Run one behaviour suite parametrized over `[\"fake\", \"real\"]`, with the real backend marked `integration` and run nightly. A fake that drifts fails the shared suite.","**Turn agent traces into scripts.** When a production run misbehaves, export its model replies and tool results from your tracing tool and feed them to the scripted fake and fake tools, so the exact run replays offline as a regression test. Track how many tests came from traces."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent bugs live in the branches a live model rarely takes: a malformed\n  tool call, an empty reply, a timeout on the second attempt, a destructive tool requested without\n  approval. Doubles let you trigger each one on demand, for free, in milliseconds.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A scripted \"fake LLM\" that emits tool-call messages, fake tools\n  that record their arguments, an in-memory vector store for RAG tests, `AsyncMock` for async SDK\n  clients, and `httpx.MockTransport` or cassettes under the provider SDK.\n- **What you control.** Where the seam sits (your client interface, the HTTP transport or a fake\n  server), whether a test checks the outcome or the call, and whether stub replies look like real model\n  output, mess included.\n- **What you can skip.** Arguing taxonomy. Know the difference between replaying and computing, and\n  between asserting on state and on calls; that decides every test.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Scripted fake LLM.** A fake that emits a fixed sequence of tool-call and text messages drives the\n  agent loop through rare branches (malformed call, empty reply, refusal) on every run.\n- **Recording fake tools.** Fake tools store the arguments they received, so a test asserts that a\n  destructive tool was never called without approval.\n- **In-memory retrieval.** A small in-memory vector store makes RAG prompt-assembly tests fast and\n  deterministic, with real stores left to integration tests.\n- **Transport-level doubles.** `httpx.MockTransport` or cassettes under the provider SDK test retries,\n  rate-limit handling and response parsing without a network.","mini_project":"**Build a fake-LLM harness for an approval-gated agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** test an agent that must ask for approval before calling a `delete_record` tool.\n- **Steps:** define a client interface and a scripted fake that implements it; write fake tools that\n  record calls; add tests where the model requests the delete with and without prior approval, returns\n  malformed arguments, and times out on the second attempt; use an autospecced double for one SDK\n  client and confirm a misspelled method fails the test.\n- **Done when:** each test asserts on outcome or recorded tool calls, not on internal call order, and\n  removing the approval check from the agent makes exactly the approval tests fail."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Dependency in the code under test\"] --> Q1{\"Crosses the process boundary?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"no, your own code\"| R[\"Call the real code\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"yes: LLM, HTTP, DB, clock\"| Q2{\"Is the call the requirement?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"no, check the result\"| S[\"Stub or fake, assert state\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"yes, e.g. never charge twice\"| M[\"Autospecced mock or spy\"]\n    S --> Q3{\"Depends on SDK or wire format?\"}\n    M --> Q3\n    Q3 -->|\"no\"| I[\"Inject fake at your interface\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Cut at the HTTP transport\"]\n    I --> K[\"Nightly shared suite vs real backend\"]\n    H --> K","caption":"The decision path for one agent dependency (the model, a tool, a store): first ask whether to double it\nat all, then what you verify, then where to cut the seam (the transport seam means `httpx.MockTransport`,\n`respx` or cassettes). Every doubled path ends at the same honesty check: one shared suite run nightly against the\nreal backend."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a stub, a mock and a fake?","a":"- **Stub:** returns canned answers to drive a branch, and the test asserts on the code's output (state\n  verification).\n- **Mock:** pre-programmed with call expectations and fails if interactions differ (behaviour\n  verification); Python's `Mock` is really a spy you assert on afterwards.\n- **Fake:** a working, simplified implementation, such as an in-memory vector store that really ranks by\n  cosine similarity. The question to ask is whether the double computes anything or only replays."},{"q":"You patched call_llm but the test still makes a real network call. What went wrong?","a":"- **Most likely the wrong patch target.** If `app/summarize.py` does `from app.llm import call_llm`, the\n  name is looked up in `app.summarize`, so patch `app.summarize.call_llm`, not `app.llm.call_llm`.\n- **Why:** `from x import y` copies a reference at import time and `patch` rebinds only one entry.\n- **Better fix:** inject the client as a parameter or use `patch.object`, so no string path is needed."},{"q":"When would you deliberately assert on calls instead of results?","a":"- **Only when the interaction is the requirement:** an email was sent, a tool ran with these arguments, a\n  charge was not made twice, a destructive tool never ran without approval.\n- **Otherwise prefer state checks**, because asserting on every internal call breaks on refactors that\n  change no behaviour, and teams learn to update assertions without reading them.\n- **Spec the mock** with `create_autospec(..., instance=True)` so signature mistakes still fail."},{"q":"How do you test that an agent never runs a destructive tool without approval?","a":"- **Run the real dispatcher** with a scripted model that requests the destructive tool, and an approval\n  callback that denies.\n- **Make the tool a recording fake** and assert it has no recorded runs and the step returned an error\n  result; here the absence of a call is the requirement, so a behaviour check is right.\n- **Add the approved case too**, asserting the arguments reached the tool unchanged."},{"q":"How would you test a parser for LLM output so it survives real responses?","a":"- **Feed it realistic stub data:** sanitised real outputs with markdown fences, preambles and trailing\n  prose, stored as fixture files, because idealised stubs hide exactly the cases that fail in production.\n- **Add each production parse failure as a new fixture file**, so the suite grows from incidents.\n- **Cut at the transport when the SDK parses the response**, using `httpx.MockTransport` or recorded\n  cassettes, so a changed response shape fails the test."},{"q":"How do you stop a shared fake LLM from drifting away from the real provider?","a":"- **Run one behaviour suite against both:** parametrize a fixture over `[\"fake\", \"real\"]`, mark the real\n  backend `integration` and run it nightly, so drift fails the shared suite.\n- **Back key doubles with contract or recorded-response tests**, re-recording cassettes on SDK upgrades.\n- **Monitor production `AttributeError` or `TypeError` from client calls** that the tests missed; each one\n  is a hole in a double."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fakes: readable vs drift.** They make tests readable and refactor-proof, but are code you maintain and can drift from the real service; pair them with a contract or integration test once drift has bitten you.","**Mocks: precise vs coupled.** They verify interactions precisely but couple tests to implementation; they flip from liability to asset only when the interaction is the requirement, such as \"never call the payment API twice\".","**Wide fixture scope: fast vs leaky.** Suites get faster, but one test's mutation leaks into another; use wide scope only for objects nothing mutates, and function scope for everything else.","**Patching: reach vs hidden dependency.** It lets you test code you cannot restructure but hides the dependency; when you own the code, injecting the collaborator removes the need for patching.","**Scripted model vs real model judgement.** A scripted fake makes loop and dispatch tests exact, but it cannot tell you whether the model picks the right tool; that question belongs to an eval suite, not a unit test."],"pitfalls":["**Unspecced `MagicMock` as a client.** Symptom: a green test while production raises `AttributeError` or `TypeError` because the method name or signature is wrong. Fix: autospec.","**Patching where defined, not where imported.** Symptom: a test that still makes real network calls or fails with a missing API key despite the patch.","**Asserting on every call and argument.** Symptom: dozens of tests fail after a refactor that changed no behaviour, so the team learns to update assertions without reading them.","**Idealised LLM stubs.** Perfect JSON, no fences, no preamble. Symptom: a parser that passes every test and fails on the first real response wrapped in markdown fences.","**Hand-typed tool-call messages.** A scripted reply whose shape differs from the provider's real tool-call format (wrong key, arguments as a dict instead of a JSON string). Symptom: dispatch tests pass and production fails to parse the first real call. Fix: build scripted replies from recorded ones.","**Mutating a session-scoped fixture.** Symptom: tests that pass alone and fail depending on order, or only under parallel runs.","**Plain `Mock` for an async client.** Awaiting its result raises `TypeError: object Mock can't be used in 'await' expression`, or, worse, code that never awaits passes silently. Use `AsyncMock` or autospec, which creates async methods for coroutine functions."],"when":"- **Use doubles at the process boundary:** model APIs, tools that call out, HTTP services, databases,\n  clocks and randomness.\n- **Do not double your own code:** call your pure functions and value objects directly.\n- **Default:** fakes and stubs with state assertions; spec'd mocks only when the call is the\n  requirement; recorded real model responses as stub data, including their mess.\n- **Back the important doubles:** every double is a claim about the real dependency, so cover key ones\n  with a contract or integration test.\n- **Interviews:** for \"how do you test code that calls an LLM?\", separate deterministic logic tested\n  with doubles from model quality measured with evals.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds a shared fake LLM client and factory fixtures so every test can script model replies, failures and timeouts without network access or keys.","architect":"Defines client interfaces and injection points so doubles are cheap, and decides which boundaries also need contract or integration tests to keep fakes honest.","fde":"Captures real responses from the customer's systems (sanitised) and turns them into stub data, so a failure seen on site becomes a fast local regression test."},"check":{"q":"A teammate's tests for an agent's tool dispatcher mock the LLM, the tool registry and every tool,\nthen assert the exact sequence of calls. They break weekly. What would you change, and what would you\nkeep?","a":"Those tests verify implementation, not behaviour, so any refactor of call order breaks them. I\nwould replace the mocked LLM with a scripted fake that emits specific tool-call messages, use real tool\nregistry code, and use fake tools that record what they received. Tests then assert outcomes: the right\ntool ran with validated arguments, malformed arguments were rejected, the final answer included the\ntool result. I would keep interaction assertions only where the call is the requirement, such as a\ndestructive tool never running without approval, and spec every remaining mock against the real class."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which double is best described as a working, simplified implementation?","options":["A stub that always returns the same canned response","A fake, such as an in-memory vector store that really ranks documents by similarity","A mock configured with expected calls","A dummy passed only to satisfy a parameter"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fake actually behaves like the dependency, just simplified. A stub only returns canned answers and does not model behaviour, which is why fakes read more like real usage in tests."},{"q":"`app/agent.py` does `from app.llm import call_llm`. Which patch target replaces it for code in agent.py?","options":["app.llm.call_llm","app.agent.call_llm","call_llm","app.llm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The import copied a reference into app.agent's namespace, so that is where the name is looked up. Patching app.llm.call_llm rebinds the original module's name, which agent.py no longer consults."},{"q":"Why prefer `create_autospec(RealClient, instance=True)` over a bare `MagicMock()` for a client?","options":["It runs faster than MagicMock","It automatically records real responses from the API","It rejects attributes and call signatures the real class does not have","It makes the test independent of the client's interface"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An autospecced double mirrors the real interface, so a typo or wrong argument fails the test. A bare MagicMock accepts anything, letting interface mistakes pass tests and fail in production."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/property-based-testing","sources":["Claessen & Hughes, 2000 — QuickCheck: A Lightweight Tool for Random Testing of Haskell Programs","MacIver et al., 2019 — Hypothesis: A new approach to property-based testing","Official docs: Hypothesis"],"why":"- **Examples test only what the author imagined.** A chunker passes \"a paragraph\", \"exactly one chunk\n  long\" and \"three chunks\", then production hits an empty document, combining characters, a chunk size\n  of 1, or an overlap of chunk size minus one, and it crashes or silently drops text.\n- **The bug was always there.** The example set simply never visited that part of the input space.\n- **AI glue code is full of such functions.** Tokenisation-adjacent splitting, prompt templating that\n  escapes user text, model-output parsers, JSON round-trips for tool arguments, deduplication, score\n  normalisation, pagination over retrieval results: huge input spaces, rules easy to state, hard to\n  enumerate.\n- **\"Random inputs in a loop\" falls short twice.** Uniform random data rarely hits the boundaries that\n  matter (an empty list, zero, the maximum value, a lone surrogate), and a failure arrives as a\n  1,400-character random string you must reverse-engineer by hand.\n- **Property-based testing fixes both:** generators are biased toward edge cases, and failures are shrunk\n  automatically to the smallest input that still fails.","how":"### Properties, not examples\nA property is a universally quantified claim: for all valid inputs *x*, some predicate holds. Common\nshapes, roughly in order of how often they pay off:\n\n| Shape | Claim | AI-code example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Round-trip** | `decode(encode(x)) == x` | JSON, serialisers, tokenizer encode/decode where lossless |\n| **Invariant** | output always satisfies a condition | every chunk length ≤ size; scores in [0, 1]; output sorted; no duplicate IDs |\n| **Idempotence** | `f(f(x)) == f(x)` | normalisation, deduplication, cleaning |\n| **Oracle / differential** | fast version agrees with a slow obviously-correct one, or new agrees with old | rewritten ranker vs reference |\n| **Metamorphic** | a known input change gives a known output change | reordering documents does not change the set retrieved |\n| **Total function** | never raises, always returns a result or a typed error | tool-argument parser on any model output |\n\n- **The agent-specific shape is the last one.** A tool dispatcher that raises on bad arguments crashes\n  the loop; one that returns an error result lets the model retry. \"Never raises\" is cheap to state and\n  Hypothesis finds the inputs that break it.\n\n### Strategies\n- **Primitives:** `st.integers(0, 100)`, `st.text()`, `st.lists(...)`, `st.dictionaries(...)`.\n- **Combinators:** `.map`, `.filter`, `st.one_of`, `st.recursive` and `st.builds` (call a constructor\n  with generated arguments).\n- **`st.from_type`** derives a strategy from type hints; works with dataclasses and many annotated classes.\n- **`st.data()`** draws values interactively, so one parameter can depend on another (an overlap smaller\n  than the chunk size).\n- **Edge cases are over-sampled on purpose:** empty collections, boundary integers, unusual Unicode.\n\n### Running, shrinking and remembering\n1. **`@given(...)` turns a test into a loop.** By default about 100 examples per test (tunable with\n   `@settings(max_examples=...)`); a deadline setting flags examples that run too long.\n2. **On failure, Hypothesis shrinks:** it repeatedly tries simpler inputs and keeps any that still fail.\n3. **It shrinks choices, not values.** Classic QuickCheck shrinks values by type-specific rules;\n   Hypothesis shrinks the underlying sequence of random choices that produced the value. The payoff:\n   shrinking works for any composed strategy, including `.map` and `.filter`, with no shrinker to write.\n4. **Failures are remembered.** They are saved in a local database (the `.hypothesis/` directory) and\n   replayed first next run, so a found bug is checked every time until fixed.\n5. **`@example(...)` pins an input permanently**, turning a counterexample into a documented regression.\n\n- **`assume(cond)`** discards inputs that miss a precondition. Use it sparingly, because heavy filtering\n  makes generation slow and Hypothesis reports a health-check failure.\n- **Construct valid inputs instead** (draw `size` first, then `overlap` below it) rather than generating\n  freely and throwing most away; it is almost always cheaper.\n\n### Profiles: different budgets for different lanes\nThe example count is a dial between thoroughness and suite time, so set it per environment, not per test.\n- **Register** `settings.register_profile(\"ci\", max_examples=1000)` and\n  `settings.register_profile(\"dev\", max_examples=50)` in `conftest.py`.\n- **Select** with `--hypothesis-profile=ci` or `settings.load_profile(...)` from an environment variable.\n- **`derandomize=True` in CI** makes examples a fixed function of the test, so a red build is reproducible\n  and never an unlucky draw. The price: CI explores the same inputs every run, which is why some teams\n  keep a randomised nightly job alongside it.\n- **`derandomize=True` implies `database=None`**, so a derandomized lane neither saves nor replays\n  failures; the example database only helps randomised runs. Recent Hypothesis versions also detect CI\n  and default to derandomized settings there, so check your version before assuming randomness in CI.\n\n### Stateful testing\n- **The bug is often in a *sequence* of calls** for objects with operations (a cache, a conversation\n  memory, a rate limiter), not in any single input.\n- **`RuleBasedStateMachine`:** declare each operation as a `@rule` with argument strategies and each\n  invariant as an `@invariant`.\n- **Random programs vs a model.** Hypothesis generates programs (put, put, evict, get, ...), runs each\n  against the real object and a deliberately trivial model (a plain `dict` standing in for an LRU cache),\n  and checks after every step that they agree.\n- **It shrinks the *program*.** A 40-step failure is typically reported as the three or four calls that\n  matter, such as \"insert at capacity, read the oldest key, insert again\".","example":"### Agent scenario: a tool-argument parser that never crashes the loop\nThe model's tool call arrives as a JSON string. The parser must return `{\"ok\": args}` or\n`{\"error\": message}` for any input, so the agent can feed errors back instead of crashing; valid\narguments must survive unchanged.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom hypothesis import given, strategies as st\n\nSCHEMA = {\"city\": str, \"days\": int}\n\ndef parse_tool_args(raw: str) -> dict:\n    try:\n        args = json.loads(raw)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n        return {\"error\": f\"arguments are not valid JSON: {e.msg}\"}\n    if not isinstance(args, dict):\n        return {\"error\": \"arguments must be a JSON object\"}\n    missing = [k for k in SCHEMA if k not in args]\n    if missing:\n        return {\"error\": f\"missing: {', '.join(missing)}\"}\n    bad = [k for k, t in SCHEMA.items() if type(args[k]) is not t]   # rejects True for int\n    if bad:\n        return {\"error\": f\"wrong type: {', '.join(bad)}\"}\n    return {\"ok\": {k: args[k] for k in SCHEMA}}\n\nscalars = st.none() | st.booleans() | st.integers() | st.text()\n\n@given(st.text())\ndef test_any_text_gives_result_or_error(raw):\n    assert set(parse_tool_args(raw)) in ({\"ok\"}, {\"error\"})\n\n@given(st.dictionaries(st.text(), scalars))\ndef test_any_object_gives_result_or_error(args):\n    assert set(parse_tool_args(json.dumps(args))) in ({\"ok\"}, {\"error\"})\n\n@given(city=st.text(), days=st.integers(), extra=st.dictionaries(st.text(), scalars))\ndef test_valid_args_pass_through(city, days, extra):\n    raw = json.dumps({**extra, \"city\": city, \"days\": days})\n    assert parse_tool_args(raw) == {\"ok\": {\"city\": city, \"days\": days}}\n```\n\n- **Total, not just correct:** the first two properties encode \"the loop never crashes on model output\",\n  which is the property agent incidents usually violate.\n- **Extra keys on purpose:** models add fields you did not ask for; the third property states that they\n  are dropped, not that they break the call.\n\n### Chunker and tool-argument round-trip\nA retrieval chunker with overlap. The contract: no chunk exceeds `size`, only the last may be short,\nconsecutive chunks really share `overlap` characters, and dropping each later chunk's overlap rejoins\nthe text exactly.\n\nAn earlier version used `range(0, len(text), step)`; Hypothesis failed it at once and shrank the\ncounterexample to `text=''`, where that version returned no chunks at all.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom hypothesis import given, strategies as st\n\ndef chunk(text: str, size: int, overlap: int) -> list[str]:\n    if not 0 <= overlap < size:\n        raise ValueError(\"need 0 <= overlap < size\")\n    step = size - overlap\n    return [text[i:i + size] for i in range(0, max(len(text) - overlap, 1), step)]\n\n@given(text=st.text(max_size=2000), size=st.integers(1, 200), data=st.data())\ndef test_chunk_properties(text, size, data):\n    overlap = data.draw(st.integers(0, size - 1), label=\"overlap\")\n    chunks = chunk(text, size, overlap)\n    assert chunks, \"always at least one chunk\"\n    assert all(len(c) <= size for c in chunks)\n    assert all(len(c) == size for c in chunks[:-1])           # only the last chunk may be short\n    assert all(a[len(a) - overlap:] == b[:overlap] for a, b in zip(chunks, chunks[1:]))\n    assert chunks[0] + \"\".join(c[overlap:] for c in chunks[1:]) == text\n\njson_values = st.recursive(\n    st.none() | st.booleans() | st.integers() | st.text(),        # no floats: NaN != NaN\n    lambda inner: st.lists(inner) | st.dictionaries(st.text(), inner),\n)\n\n@given(st.dictionaries(st.text(min_size=1), json_values))\ndef test_tool_args_round_trip(args):\n    assert json.loads(json.dumps(args)) == args\n\ndef test_empty_text_regression():          # the shrunk counterexample, pinned as a plain test\n    assert chunk(\"\", size=4, overlap=1) == [\"\"]\n```\n\n- **Five assertions encode the spec.** The full-length and shared-overlap checks matter: without them a\n  chunker emitting one character per chunk would still rejoin correctly and pass whenever `overlap` is 0.\n- **Coverage no example set gives:** size 1, overlap `size - 1`, empty text and non-ASCII text, all at once.\n- **Floats excluded on purpose** from the round-trip, because `NaN` breaks equality; property tests force\n  you to decide such facts explicitly.\n\n### Stateful: an LRU cache against a dict-and-list model\nRunnable with `pip install hypothesis pytest`; pytest collects `TestLRU`.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\nfrom hypothesis import strategies as st\nfrom hypothesis.stateful import RuleBasedStateMachine, invariant, rule\n\nclass LRU:                                         # the code under test\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        self.capacity, self.data = capacity, OrderedDict()\n    def get(self, k):\n        if k not in self.data:\n            return None\n        self.data.move_to_end(k)                   # delete this line to see the bug found\n        return self.data[k]\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        self.data[k] = v\n        self.data.move_to_end(k)\n        if len(self.data) > self.capacity:\n            self.data.popitem(last=False)          # evict least recently used\n\nclass LRUMachine(RuleBasedStateMachine):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.cache, self.model, self.recency = LRU(3), {}, []   # model: dict + list\n\n    def _touch(self, k):\n        if k in self.recency:\n            self.recency.remove(k)\n        self.recency.append(k)\n\n    @rule(k=st.integers(0, 5), v=st.integers())\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        self.cache.put(k, v)\n        self.model[k] = v\n        self._touch(k)\n        if len(self.recency) > 3:\n            del self.model[self.recency.pop(0)]\n\n    @rule(k=st.integers(0, 5))\n    def get(self, k):\n        assert self.cache.get(k) == self.model.get(k)\n        if k in self.model:\n            self._touch(k)\n\n    @invariant()\n    def within_capacity(self):\n        assert len(self.cache.data) <= 3\n\nTestLRU = LRUMachine.TestCase\n```\n\nWith the marked line deleted, one run reported this shrunk program (exact keys vary run to run):\n\n| Step | Call | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1-3 | `put(1)`, `put(0)`, `put(3)` | fill capacity 3 |\n| 4 | `get(1)` | 1 should become most recent |\n| 5 | `put(2)` | evicts; the buggy cache drops 1, the model drops 0 |\n| 6 | `get(0)` | cache returns a value, model says `None`: assertion fails |\n\n### Worked scenario: example budgets per lane\nRough orders of magnitude for a suite of 40 property tests at ~2 ms per example (illustrative).\n\n| Profile | `max_examples` | Per test | Whole suite |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `dev` | 50 | ~0.1 s | ~4 s |\n| default | 100 | ~0.2 s | ~8 s |\n| `ci` (+ `derandomize=True`) | 1000 | ~2 s | ~80 s |\n| nightly, randomised | 10,000 | ~20 s | ~13 min |","patterns":["**Profiles selected by environment.** In `conftest.py`, register `dev`, `ci` and `nightly` profiles and call `settings.load_profile(os.getenv(\"HYPOTHESIS_PROFILE\", \"dev\"))`. PRs run the deterministic `ci` profile; a nightly job runs a large randomised budget. Monitor property-lane wall time per profile.","**Pin every shrunk counterexample.** Add it as `@example(...)` on the property and, for important bugs, as a plain named regression test like `test_empty_text_regression`. The bug then stays covered even if the `.hypothesis/` database is wiped.","**Persist the example database in randomised CI lanes.** Cache the `.hypothesis/` directory between nightly runs with your CI system's cache step, so failures found on one run are replayed first on the next. Skip it for the derandomized PR lane, which does not use the database.","**Reproduce from CI output.** Enable the `print_blob=True` setting in the CI profile; a failure then prints an `@reproduce_failure(...)` line you paste locally to replay the exact case.","**Tune deadlines, not tolerance for slowness.** For legitimately slow properties set `@settings(deadline=None)` or a larger `timedelta`, rather than leaving deadline flakes in the suite. Track which tests carry such overrides.","**Stateful tests for anything with memory.** Caches, conversation memories and rate limiters get a `RuleBasedStateMachine` against a plain dict or list model. For agent memory, rules such as add turn, summarise, trim to budget and recall check that pinned facts survive every trim. Re-run it whenever eviction or expiry logic changes.","**A \"never raises\" property per tool.** Every tool the model can call gets one property feeding it arbitrary argument JSON and asserting a result or a typed error. Monitor production tool exceptions that escaped the dispatcher; each is a missing property."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The model is an adversarial input generator you do not control: it\n  emits truncated JSON, wrong types, empty strings and odd Unicode as tool arguments. \"The dispatcher\n  never raises on any model output\" is a property, and Hypothesis checks it far better than examples.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool-argument parsers and validators, RAG chunkers, prompt\n  templates that escape user text, JSON round-trips for tool results, and stateful pieces such as\n  conversation memory, caches and rate limiters.\n- **What you control.** The properties (round-trip, invariant, idempotence, oracle), strategies that\n  build valid inputs directly, and per-lane example budgets so the pull-request lane stays fast.\n- **What you can skip.** Judging model quality with it. Generated inputs test your deterministic code;\n  whether the model chooses well is an eval question.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool-argument parsing.** The property \"the dispatcher never raises on any model output\" is checked\n  against generated truncated JSON, wrong types and odd Unicode.\n- **RAG chunkers.** Invariants such as \"chunks rejoin to the original text\" and \"no chunk exceeds the\n  size limit\" hold for every document, chunk size and overlap.\n- **Tool-result round-trips.** Serialising a tool result to JSON and parsing it back returns an equal\n  value for every generated result.\n- **Stateful agent memory.** Rule-based state machines exercise conversation memory, caches and rate\n  limiters with random sequences of operations.","mini_project":"**Fuzz an agent's tool dispatcher with Hypothesis (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show that a tool dispatcher turns every possible model output into a result or a structured\n  error, never an exception.\n- **Steps:** write a dispatcher for two tools with typed arguments; build a strategy that mixes valid\n  tool calls, valid calls with wrong types, and arbitrary text; assert the dispatcher returns a result\n  or an error object; add a round-trip property for the tool-result JSON; keep a small example budget\n  for the pull-request lane.\n- **Done when:** Hypothesis has found and shrunk at least one real bug you then fixed, the failing input\n  is kept as an explicit example, and the suite passes in seconds."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"@given test starts\"] --> DB[(\".hypothesis/ example database\")]\n    DB -->|\"replay saved failures first\"| G[\"Generate edge-biased input\"]\n    G --> R[\"Run the property\"]\n    R -->|\"passes\"| N{\"Reached max_examples?\"}\n    N -->|\"no\"| G\n    N -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Test passes\"]\n    R -->|\"fails\"| SH[\"Shrink: try simpler choice sequences\"]\n    SH -->|\"still fails, keep it\"| SH\n    SH -->|\"no simpler failure found\"| M[\"Report minimal counterexample\"]\n    M -->|\"save for next run\"| DB\n    M --> E[\"Pin it with @example\"]","caption":"The loop behind one `@given` test, such as a tool-argument parser property: replay saved failures\nfirst, generate edge-biased inputs, and on a failure shrink the choice sequence until the smallest\nfailing input remains."},"interview":[{"q":"What does property-based testing find that example-based tests miss?","a":"- **Definition:** you state a rule that must hold for all valid inputs and Hypothesis generates the\n  inputs, about 100 per test by default, biased toward empty collections, boundary integers and unusual\n  Unicode.\n- **Why it finds more:** examples cover only what the author imagined, while generators visit the corners\n  (chunk size 1, overlap of size minus one, empty text) where parsing and chunking bugs live.\n- **Shrinking makes failures readable**, reporting `text=''` instead of a 1,400-character random string."},{"q":"Your agent crashes on some tool calls the model emits. How would property-based testing help?","a":"- **State the property the crash violates:** for any argument string, the parser or dispatcher returns a\n  result or an error result the model can read, and never raises.\n- **Generate the inputs models produce:** arbitrary text, arbitrary JSON objects, wrong types, missing and\n  extra keys; Hypothesis shrinks a crash to the smallest such input.\n- **Pin each shrunk input** with `@example(...)` and keep the property in the PR lane, since it needs no\n  network or model."},{"q":"Which properties would you write for a retrieval chunker with overlap?","a":"- **Invariants:** at least one chunk, no chunk longer than `size`, only the last chunk short.\n- **Overlap really shared:** the tail of each chunk equals the head of the next for `overlap` characters.\n- **Round-trip:** dropping each later chunk's overlap rejoins the original text exactly.\n- **Generate valid inputs directly:** draw `size` first, then `overlap` below it with `st.data()`, rather\n  than generating freely and filtering with `assume`."},{"q":"A property test fails in CI but you cannot reproduce it locally. What do you do?","a":"- **Make CI deterministic:** use a `ci` profile with `derandomize=True`, so a red build is a fixed\n  function of the test, not an unlucky draw.\n- **Get the exact case:** with `print_blob=True` the failure prints a `@reproduce_failure(...)` line you\n  paste locally.\n- **Keep it covered:** pin the shrunk input with `@example(...)`, because a derandomized CI lane has no\n  example database to replay it from."},{"q":"When is property-based testing the wrong tool?","a":"- **When you cannot state the rule:** for code whose correctness is not expressible as an invariant, a few\n  good examples are the better investment.\n- **When judging model quality:** outputs are non-deterministic and correctness is graded, which is the\n  job of evals, not Hypothesis.\n- **When the property restates the implementation:** it never fails, even when you break the code, so it\n  proves nothing."},{"q":"How would you budget example counts for a suite of 40 property tests?","a":"- **Per lane, not per test:** register `dev` (50 examples), `ci` (1000, derandomized) and a randomised\n  nightly profile, and select with `--hypothesis-profile` or an environment variable.\n- **Rough arithmetic at ~2 ms per example (illustrative):** about 4 s for the suite in dev, about 80 s in\n  CI and about 13 minutes nightly at 10,000 examples.\n- **Monitor property-lane wall time** and raise or lower budgets when the PR lane becomes a bottleneck."}],"tradeoffs":["**Coverage vs effort.** Properties cover far more of the input space but are harder to write; for code whose correctness you cannot state as a rule, a handful of good examples is the better investment.","**More examples vs suite time.** Larger counts find rarer bugs but slow the suite; run the default count locally and a larger count in a nightly or CI profile, and flip that once the suite is a bottleneck.","**Exploration vs surprise failures.** Generated inputs explore edge cases you did not imagine, but a new failure can surface on an unrelated commit; the example database and pinned `@example`s keep that manageable.","**Easy property vs real property.** Restating the implementation as the property is easy but proves nothing; an independent oracle or invariant costs more thought and is the only version that catches bugs."],"pitfalls":["**Re-implementing the function as the property.** Symptom: a test that has never failed, even when you deliberately break the code.","**Overusing `filter` or `assume`.** Symptom: slow tests and health-check errors about too much data being filtered out. Fix: construct valid inputs directly.","**Floats in equality properties.** NaN, infinities and rounding cause spurious failures on `nan != nan` or tiny representation differences. Exclude them or compare with tolerance.","**Function-scoped fixtures inside `@given`.** `tmp_path` and friends are created once per *test function* and shared by all examples, so one example's state leaks into the next. Hypothesis raises a health-check error; create the resource inside the test body instead.","**Real LLM or network calls inside `@given`.** Symptom: a hundred paid, slow, non-deterministic calls per run, failing on flaky responses rather than bugs.","**Deleting `.hypothesis/` in CI without pinning failures.** Symptom: a bug that reproduced once, then mysteriously stops reproducing until it hits production."],"when":"- **Use when:** deterministic code has a large input space and an easy-to-state rule: model-output\n  and tool-argument parsers, chunkers and splitters, serialisers, prompt template escaping, ID and deduplication logic,\n  ranking and score normalisation, and stateful things like caches and memories.\n- **Avoid when:** judging model quality. Outputs are not deterministic and \"correctness\" is a graded\n  judgement, which evals and metamorphic evaluation address.\n- **Pair with:** a few readable examples, which serve as documentation.\n- **Interviews:** naming the property for a given function (round-trip, invariant, idempotence, oracle)\n  is the skill being tested.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes Hypothesis properties for parsers, chunkers and tool-argument serialisation, and pins every shrunk counterexample as a permanent regression test.","architect":"Requires property tests on boundary code that turns untrusted text into structured data, because that is where malformed inputs enter the system.","fde":"Turns a customer's odd documents (encodings, empty pages, enormous tables) into strategies that generate similar inputs, proving the pipeline handles the whole class."},"check":{"q":"You must test a function that extracts a JSON object from a model reply that may include markdown\nfences and surrounding prose. What properties would you write, and what would you not use Hypothesis for?","a":"Generate an arbitrary JSON-compatible dict, serialise it, then wrap it with generated prose\nbefore and after, optionally inside a fenced block; the property is that extraction returns the\noriginal dict. A second property: text with no JSON object raises a specific parse error rather than\nreturning garbage. A third: extraction is unchanged by leading and trailing whitespace. Generated\nprose should be filtered so it cannot itself contain braces, or the property must define which object\nwins. I would not use Hypothesis to decide whether the model produces good JSON in the first place;\nthat is an eval over real prompts, and replies would be recorded examples, not generated ones."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does Hypothesis shrinking do after a property fails?","options":["Reruns the failing input until it passes, to detect flakiness","Searches for a simpler input that still fails and reports the minimal one","Reduces max_examples so the test runs faster next time","Removes the failing input from future runs to avoid noise"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shrinking turns a large random failure into a small, readable counterexample such as an empty string. Failing inputs are saved and replayed first next time, not removed."},{"q":"Which is the strongest property for a JSON serialiser of tool arguments?","options":["The output is a non-empty string","The output is shorter than ten kilobytes","Deserialising the output returns a value equal to the original input","Serialising twice produces the same string"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The round-trip property checks that no information is lost or corrupted. Non-empty output and determinism can hold while values are silently mangled, so they are much weaker checks."},{"q":"Why can Hypothesis shrink values built with `.map` and `.filter` without a custom shrinker?","options":["It requires every strategy to define its own shrink function","It only shrinks integers and strings, not composed values","It shrinks the underlying sequence of random choices, then regenerates the value from it","It stores every generated value and picks the smallest one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Shrinking the choice sequence works for any strategy composition automatically. Type-based shrinking, as in classic QuickCheck, needs a shrinker per type and struggles with mapped values."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/contract-testing","sources":["Robinson, 2006 — Consumer-Driven Contracts: A Service Evolution Pattern","Official docs: Pact","Official docs: Schemathesis"],"why":"- **AI systems are services talking over interfaces.** An agent service calls a retrieval service, a\n  tool gateway, a model gateway and customer APIs; tools exchange JSON with the model; an MCP server\n  exposes schemas to clients. Each side is tested in isolation with doubles.\n- **Mocks drift from the real service silently.** The agent's tests stub retrieval as\n  `{\"hits\": [{\"doc_id\", \"score\", \"text\"}]}` and pass. The retrieval team renames `text` to `content`.\n  Both suites stay green, because each checks only its own assumptions, and production shows the agent\n  quietly answering without context.\n- **Shared end-to-end environments are too slow and fragile.** They catch the rename, but they are flaky,\n  expensive to keep in sync, and report the break long after the commit that caused it.\n- **A spec document nobody executes does not help.** Asking teams to \"follow it\" fails because nothing\n  runs the spec against the code. The provider also cannot know which fields consumers use, so every\n  change feels risky and APIs ossify.\n- **Contract testing makes the agreement executable and splits it in two.** Each consumer's expectations\n  become a file the provider runs against its real code on every build, so a breaking change fails the\n  provider's CI in the commit that caused it, naming the consumer it would break.","how":"### Consumer-driven contracts\n1. **Consumer test.** The consumer's test talks to a mock provider configured with expected\n   interactions: \"given the index contains a refunds document, `POST /search` with this body returns\n   200 and a list of hits each having a string `doc_id`, a number `score` and a string `text`.\"\n   - The consumer code runs for real against that mock, so the expectations are exactly what the code\n     uses.\n2. **Contract file.** The run writes the interactions to a contract (a pact file in Pact's terms).\n   - Matchers express \"a string\" or \"a list of at least one like this\" rather than exact values, because\n     the consumer depends on shape, not on today's data.\n3. **Provider verification.** The provider's CI fetches every consumer's contract, puts its real\n   application into each named **provider state** (seeds the test index), replays each request, and\n   checks the response matches.\n   - Extra fields are fine; missing or retyped ones fail.\n4. **Broker and deploy gate.** Contracts are published to a shared store (the Pact Broker or similar)\n   tagged by version and environment.\n   - The provider verifies against what is actually deployed, and a \"can I deploy\" check blocks\n     releasing a version that breaks a live consumer.\n\n### Why the direction matters\n- **The consumer states only what it uses**, so the provider learns exactly which fields are\n  load-bearing and is free to change everything else.\n- **Asynchronous messages work the same way**, though there is no request to replay. The consumer records\n  the event payload it expects (a `document.indexed` event on a queue), and the provider's verification\n  calls the producing code and checks the message it would publish.\n\n### Evolving a contract without a flag day\nContracts make breaking changes visible; they do not make them free. Renaming `text` to `content`\nsafely is an expand-and-contract sequence, and the broker's version matrix says when each step is\nallowed.\n1. **Expand.** The provider returns both `text` and `content`. Every existing contract still passes,\n   because extra fields are allowed.\n2. **Migrate.** Each consumer switches to `content` and publishes a new contract that no longer\n   mentions `text`.\n3. **Contract.** The provider removes `text`. Verification now passes only if *every deployed*\n   consumer version has stopped relying on it, so the \"can I deploy\" check blocks the removal until the\n   last old consumer has actually been redeployed, not merely merged.\n\n- **The order matters because services deploy independently.** At any moment several versions of each\n  are live, and the contract that must hold is the one between the versions actually running together.\n\n### Provider-driven, schema-based contracts\n- **The alternative:** the provider publishes a schema (OpenAPI, JSON Schema, protobuf) and both sides\n  test against it.\n- **Schemathesis** generates requests from an OpenAPI document and checks the real service's responses\n  conform, which is property-based testing applied to an API.\n- **What each catches:** schema checks catch \"the service does not do what its spec says\"; they do not\n  tell you which consumers break when the spec changes, which is what consumer-driven contracts add.\n\n| Approach | Who writes it | Catches | Cannot tell you |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Consumer-driven (Pact) | each consumer | removed or retyped fields a consumer reads | whether the provider meets its own spec elsewhere |\n| Schema-based (OpenAPI + Schemathesis) | the provider | responses that violate the published schema | which consumer a change breaks |\n\n### Contracts you cannot verify\nYou cannot run provider verification against a model vendor's API. For third-party providers:\n- **Validate every response at your boundary** with strict models.\n- **Keep recorded real responses** as consumer test fixtures.\n- **Pin SDK versions.**\n- **Run a small scheduled canary** against the live API that validates real responses against your\n  models, so a vendor-side change fails a check before users notice.\n- **Tool schemas and structured-output schemas shown to a model are contracts too**, verified the same\n  way: by validating what actually comes back.\n\n### The agent's contract map\n| Boundary | Agent's role | Contract style | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Retrieval or tool service another team owns | consumer | consumer-driven (Pact or hand-rolled) | provider CI plus deploy gate |\n| Your tool service used by several agents | provider | consumer-driven, one contract per agent | your CI |\n| MCP server's tool list and result shapes | consumer | schema snapshot plus strict validation | your CI and a live canary |\n| Tool schemas shown to the model | provider (the model consumes) | committed schema snapshot | review on every change |\n| Vendor model API | consumer, unverifiable | strict boundary models | recorded fixtures and a canary |","example":"### Agent scenario: snapshot the tool schemas the model sees\nA renamed argument or a tightened limit changes what the model is told, so it is an interface change\nwith the model as consumer. This test fails on any schema change until someone reviews the diff and\nregenerates the committed file. Runnable with `pip install pydantic pytest`.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport pytest\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass SearchDocs(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"Search the help-centre index and return the top passages.\"\"\"\n    query: str = Field(description=\"What to search for, in the user's words\")\n    k: int = Field(3, ge=1, le=10, description=\"How many passages to return\")\n\nTOOLS = {\"search_docs\": SearchDocs}\nSNAPSHOT = Path(__file__).with_name(\"tool_schemas.json\")\n\ndef current_schemas() -> dict:\n    return {name: model.model_json_schema() for name, model in TOOLS.items()}\n\ndef test_tool_schemas_match_snapshot():\n    if not SNAPSHOT.exists():                      # first run: write it, commit it\n        SNAPSHOT.write_text(json.dumps(current_schemas(), indent=2, sort_keys=True))\n        pytest.skip(\"snapshot created; commit tool_schemas.json\")\n    assert current_schemas() == json.loads(SNAPSHOT.read_text()), (\n        \"tool schema changed: review what the model will now see, then regenerate the snapshot\")\n\ndef test_model_tool_call_honours_schema():\n    raw = '{\"query\": \"refund policy\", \"k\": 3}'      # a recorded real tool call\n    assert SearchDocs.model_validate_json(raw).k == 3\n```\n\n- **Descriptions are part of the contract.** Rewording a field description changes model behaviour, so\n  the snapshot flags it for review just like a renamed field.\n- **Recorded tool calls as fixtures** check the other direction: what the model actually sends still\n  validates against the schema you now publish.\n\n### Hand-rolled contract: consumer model, provider verification\nAn agent service consumes a retrieval service. The consumer models only the fields it reads and\npublishes one interaction; the provider's test replays it against the real app in a seeded state. This\nhand-rolled version shows the mechanism that Pact automates with matchers and a broker.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict\n\nclass Hit(BaseModel):                      # consumer side: only fields the agent reads\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\", strict=True)   # strict: \"0.9\" is not a float\n    doc_id: str\n    score: float\n    text: str\n\nclass SearchResponse(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\", strict=True)\n    hits: list[Hit]\n\nCONTRACT = {                               # published by the consumer's CI\n    \"consumer\": \"agent-service\", \"provider\": \"retrieval-service\",\n    \"interactions\": [{\n        \"description\": \"search returns scored hits\",\n        \"provider_state\": \"index contains doc d1 about refunds\",\n        \"request\": {\"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"/search\", \"json\": {\"query\": \"refund policy\", \"k\": 3}},\n        \"response\": {\"status\": 200, \"schema\": SearchResponse},  # shipped as model_json_schema()\n    }],\n}\n\n# provider side, in the retrieval service's own test suite\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom retrieval.app import app, seed_state  # the provider's real code\n\ndef test_retrieval_honours_agent_contract():\n    client = TestClient(app)\n    for it in CONTRACT[\"interactions\"]:\n        seed_state(it[\"provider_state\"])\n        req = it[\"request\"]\n        r = client.request(req[\"method\"], req[\"path\"], json=req[\"json\"])\n        assert r.status_code == it[\"response\"][\"status\"]\n        body = it[\"response\"][\"schema\"].model_validate_json(r.content)  # missing/retyped field raises\n        assert body.hits, \"provider state promised at least one hit\"\n```\n\n- **What fails and what passes.** If the retrieval team renames `text` to `content`, the provider test\n  fails in their pull request; adding a new `chunk_id` field passes because the consumer ignores extras.\n- **Why `strict=True` matters.** Pydantic's default lax mode would coerce a `score` that became the\n  string `\"0.9\"` back into a float. The retype would pass verification while breaking any consumer that\n  does not coerce.\n- **In a real setup** the consumer publishes the JSON Schema (or Pact matchers) rather than a Python\n  class.\n\n### Consumer side: the real adapter against a stub built from the interaction\nThe matching consumer test runs the agent's real retrieval adapter against a stub server that returns a\nresponse built from the same interaction. The contract then cannot list a field the adapter does not\nparse, or omit one it does; that pairing is what Pact's mock provider automates. Runnable with\n`pip install httpx pydantic pytest`.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport httpx\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict\n\nclass Hit(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\", strict=True)\n    doc_id: str\n    score: float\n    text: str\n\nclass SearchResponse(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"ignore\", strict=True)\n    hits: list[Hit]\n\ndef retrieve_context(http: httpx.Client, query: str, k: int = 3) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"The agent's real adapter: the passages it will put in the prompt.\"\"\"\n    r = http.post(\"/search\", json={\"query\": query, \"k\": k})\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    return [h.text for h in SearchResponse.model_validate_json(r.content).hits]\n\nINTERACTION = {\n    \"request\": {\"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"/search\", \"json\": {\"query\": \"refund policy\", \"k\": 3}},\n    \"response\": {\"status\": 200,\n                 \"json\": {\"hits\": [{\"doc_id\": \"d1\", \"score\": 0.9, \"text\": \"Refunds take 5 days.\"}]}},\n}\n\ndef stub_provider(it: dict) -> httpx.MockTransport:\n    def handler(request: httpx.Request) -> httpx.Response:\n        exp = it[\"request\"]\n        assert (request.method, request.url.path) == (exp[\"method\"], exp[\"path\"])\n        assert json.loads(request.content) == exp[\"json\"]     # adapter sends what the contract says\n        return httpx.Response(it[\"response\"][\"status\"], json=it[\"response\"][\"json\"])\n    return httpx.MockTransport(handler)\n\ndef test_adapter_matches_contract():\n    with httpx.Client(transport=stub_provider(INTERACTION), base_url=\"http://retrieval.test\") as http:\n        assert retrieve_context(http, \"refund policy\") == [\"Refunds take 5 days.\"]\n```\n\nIf the adapter starts sending `\"top_k\"` instead of `\"k\"`, the request assertion fails here, before the\nprovider ever sees the change.\n\n### Worked scenario: removing `text` across independent deploys\nIllustrative version numbers; the point is which check blocks which step.\n\n| Day | Provider | Agent on main | Agent in production | Gate result for removing `text` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | v41 returns `text` and `content` | v12 reads `text` | v12 | not attempted; all contracts pass |\n| 3 | PR removes `text` | v13 reads `content` | v12 | **blocked**: deployed v12 still reads `text` |\n| 5 | same PR, re-checked | v13 | v13, deployment recorded | allowed |\n\nVerifying only against main (v13) on day 3 would have passed and caused an outage for the v12 still\nrunning.","patterns":["**Gate releases on deployed versions.** When a service ships, record it with `pact-broker record-deployment`; before each release run `pact-broker can-i-deploy --pacticipant <name> --version <sha> --to-environment production`. Monitor blocked releases and how long contracts sit unverified.","**Verify when a contract changes, not only when the provider changes.** Configure a broker webhook that triggers the provider's verification build when a consumer publishes a changed contract, so the consumer learns within one build whether its new expectation is met. Track verification lag.","**Schema fuzzing in provider CI.** Run Schemathesis against the running app's OpenAPI document (`schemathesis run <url-to-openapi.json>`) on each pull request. It catches responses that violate the spec, 500s on edge-case inputs and undocumented status codes. Watch for endpoints excluded from the run.","**Canary the vendor APIs you cannot verify.** A scheduled job sends a fixed small prompt to each model provider, validates the response with the same strict models used at the boundary, and alerts on validation failure. Pair it with pinned SDK versions and re-recorded fixtures on upgrade.","**Snapshot tool and structured-output schemas.** A test compares each tool's `model_json_schema()` with a committed JSON file, so any change to what the model is shown needs an explicit, reviewed update. Monitor schema validation failures on model tool calls after each change.","**Pin and validate MCP servers you consume.** Snapshot the tool list and input schemas a third-party MCP server advertises, validate every tool result with strict models, and run a scheduled check that lists its tools and diffs them against the snapshot. Alert on any added, removed or changed tool."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** When a tool or retrieval service renames a field, an agent rarely\n  crashes; it quietly answers without context or calls a tool with arguments that no longer mean\n  anything. Contracts turn that silent degradation into a failed build in the commit that caused it.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The agent as consumer of retrieval, tool gateways and MCP\n  servers; your tool service as provider to several agents; tool and structured-output JSON schemas,\n  which are a contract with the model; vendor model APIs, which you can only validate and canary.\n- **What you control.** Which boundaries get consumer-driven contracts versus a shared schema, strict\n  validation at every boundary, a snapshot of each tool schema the model sees, and a deploy gate on\n  deployed versions.\n- **What you can skip.** Running a broker for interfaces one team owns end to end; shared Pydantic\n  models and a schema snapshot are enough there.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agent and retrieval service.** A consumer contract records the fields the agent reads from search\n  hits, so a rename on the retrieval side fails the retrieval team's build.\n- **Shared tool services.** A tool gateway used by several agents runs each agent's contract, and can\n  remove a field only when no deployed agent still reads it.\n- **Tool schemas the model sees.** A snapshot test of every tool's JSON schema makes a change to what the\n  model is offered a reviewed diff, not an accident.\n- **MCP servers.** The schemas an MCP server exposes are validated in CI so clients do not discover a\n  breaking change at runtime.","mini_project":"**Add a contract between an agent and its search tool (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** catch a breaking change in a search service before the agent quietly loses its context.\n- **Steps:** build a small search service and an agent client that reads `doc_id`, `score` and `text`;\n  write the consumer's expectations as a contract file from the agent's tests; verify the contract\n  against the real provider code in its own test; snapshot the agent's tool schema; then rename `text`\n  to `content` in the provider.\n- **Done when:** the rename fails the provider's check with a message naming the agent as the broken\n  consumer, and changing the tool schema shows up as a snapshot diff."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Consumer CI\n    participant M as Mock provider\n    participant B as Contract broker\n    participant P as Provider CI\n    participant G as Deploy gate\n    C->>M: run real adapter against expected interactions\n    M-->>C: canned responses built from type matchers\n    C->>B: publish contract tagged with consumer version\n    B->>P: fetch contracts of every deployed consumer version\n    P->>P: seed provider state, replay requests against real app\n    P->>B: publish verification result\n    G->>B: can I deploy this provider version to production\n    B-->>G: yes only if every live consumer contract passes","caption":"Follow one contract from the consumer's build to the provider's deploy gate; the provider never needs\nthe consumer running, only its published file."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a consumer-driven contract, and why is the consumer the one writing it?","a":"- **Definition:** the consumer's tests run its real code against a mock provider and record the\n  interactions it relies on; the provider replays them against its real application in its own CI.\n- **Why the consumer:** it states only the fields it actually reads, so the provider learns which fields\n  are load-bearing and can change everything else freely.\n- **Result:** a breaking change fails the provider's build in the commit that caused it, naming the\n  consumer."},{"q":"How would you rename a response field that three consumers read, without an outage?","a":"- **Expand:** return both the old and new field; every existing contract still passes because extra fields\n  are allowed.\n- **Migrate:** each consumer switches and publishes a contract without the old field.\n- **Contract:** remove the old field only when the \"can I deploy\" check passes against every *deployed*\n  consumer version, because merged-but-undeployed consumers still read it."},{"q":"When would you choose OpenAPI plus Schemathesis over Pact?","a":"- **One team owns both sides, or the API is public** with unknown consumers: a provider-published schema\n  is simpler and needs no broker or cross-team process.\n- **What you give up:** a schema cannot tell you which consumer a change breaks, because it records what the\n  provider offers, not what each consumer uses.\n- **Pact pays off** when separately deployed services owned by different teams have shipped interface\n  breaks to production."},{"q":"Your agent parses responses from a vendor LLM API. How do you get contract-like safety without provider verification?","a":"- **Validate every response at the boundary** with strict Pydantic models, so a shape change fails loudly\n  instead of degrading answers.\n- **Keep recorded real responses as fixtures and pin the SDK**, re-recording on upgrades.\n- **Run a scheduled canary** that calls the live API with a small fixed prompt and validates the shape, so a\n  vendor-side change alerts before users notice."},{"q":"Your agent depends on a third-party MCP server. How do you protect it from changes on their side?","a":"- **Treat it as an unverifiable provider:** you cannot run verification in their CI, so validate every\n  tool result with strict models at your boundary.\n- **Snapshot what it advertises:** the tool names and input schemas, diffed by a scheduled job that\n  alerts on any change.\n- **Keep recorded results as fixtures** for your own tests, and pin the server version where you can."},{"q":"Provider verification passes, yet the consumer in production broke. What are the likely causes?","a":"- **Verified against the wrong version:** the provider checked the consumer's main-branch contract, not\n  the version actually deployed.\n- **Lax validation:** a field was retyped (a `score` that became the string `\"0.9\"`) and Pydantic's lax mode\n  coerced it back, so verification passed while a non-coercing consumer failed.\n- **Missing provider state:** verification ran against an empty index and only exercised the \"no results\"\n  path."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast interface checks vs narrow coverage.** Contracts catch interface breaks in seconds per build, but only interface breaks; a provider that returns the right shape with the wrong documents still passes, which integration tests or evals cover.","**Precise consumer knowledge vs process cost.** Consumer-driven contracts tell providers exactly which fields matter, but need a broker, versioning discipline and buy-in from both teams; with one team owning both sides, a shared schema is often enough.","**Schema-first simplicity vs blind spots.** Schema-first contracts are simple to adopt and serve many unknown consumers, but cannot report which consumer a change breaks; they flip to the better choice for public APIs with external clients.","**Loose matchers vs semantic gaps.** Type-not-value matchers keep contracts stable but can admit semantically wrong data; tighten them only where a value is genuinely part of the agreement, such as an enum of error codes."],"pitfalls":["**Pinning exact values from the consumer's fixtures.** Symptom: provider verification fails whenever test data changes, until teams stop trusting and then stop running it. Fix: type matchers.","**Recording fields the consumer does not read.** Symptom: a provider that cannot remove or rename anything, because every field appears load-bearing. Fix: generate the contract from the real adapter.","**Missing provider states.** Verification runs against an empty database. Symptom: contracts that only ever verify the \"no results\" path while the populated path breaks unnoticed.","**Treating a vendor LLM API as stable because your mocks are.** Symptom: a production parse error after an SDK or response-format change that no test of yours could have seen. Fix: boundary validation and a live canary.","**Removing a field as soon as consumers' main branches stop using it.** Symptom: an outage in the window before the last consumer is redeployed, which a deploy gate on deployed versions prevents.","**Verifying against the main-branch contract instead of the deployed one.** Symptom: a provider release that passes CI and still breaks the consumer version currently running in production.","**Tool schema edits shipped without review.** A renamed argument or reworded description reaches the model unnoticed. Symptom: tool-call validation errors or a drop in tool-selection accuracy right after a deploy. Fix: a committed schema snapshot plus an eval run on schema changes."],"when":"- **Use consumer-driven contracts when** separately deployed services are owned by different teams and\n  interface breaks have actually reached production; that is when the broker and process pay for\n  themselves.\n- **Use schema-based checks when** one team owns both sides or the API is public: OpenAPI plus\n  Schemathesis, or shared Pydantic models.\n- **For third-party model and SaaS APIs:** boundary validation plus a live canary.\n- **Avoid when:** you want to test behaviour; contracts are not a substitute for integration tests of\n  behaviour.\n- **Interviews:** contract testing is the expected answer to \"how do you stop mocks drifting from the real\n  service?\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes consumer tests that record only the fields the code reads, and provider tests with explicit states, so contract failures point at real breaks.","architect":"Decides where contracts sit (service APIs, tool schemas, MCP surfaces, event payloads) and wires a deploy gate so breaking changes cannot ship to a live consumer.","fde":"Captures the customer system's real request and response shapes as a contract early, so an upstream change in their API fails a check instead of a live integration."},"check":{"q":"Your agent calls an internal tool service owned by another team and a public LLM API. Mocks on\nboth sides keep passing while production breaks. Design the testing for each boundary.","a":"For the internal tool service, I would use consumer-driven contracts: the agent's tests run\nagainst a mock built from recorded interactions that use type matchers and name provider states; the\ncontract is published to a broker; the tool team's CI verifies it against their real service and a\ndeploy gate checks deployed versions. For the LLM API, I cannot run provider verification, so I would\nvalidate every response with strict models at the boundary, keep recorded real responses as fixtures,\npin the SDK, and run a scheduled canary that calls the live API and validates the shape. Neither\nreplaces evals of answer quality; contracts only guarantee the shape of the conversation."},"quiz":[{"q":"In consumer-driven contract testing, who verifies the contract against real code?","options":["The consumer, by calling the real provider in its test suite","The provider, by replaying the consumer's recorded interactions against its real application","A shared end-to-end environment that runs both services together","Nobody; the contract file is documentation reviewed by humans"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The consumer runs against a mock to produce the contract; the provider verifies it in its own build. That split is what removes the need for a shared environment to catch interface breaks."},{"q":"Why should consumer contracts use type matchers rather than exact values?","options":["Exact values make contract files too large to store","The consumer depends on response shape, so exact values cause failures whenever test data changes","Type matchers let the provider omit fields the consumer uses","Exact values cannot be serialised to JSON"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pinning values couples the contract to fixtures rather than to the interface, making verification brittle. Matchers still fail when a used field is missing or changes type."},{"q":"What can schema-based contract checks not tell a provider that consumer-driven contracts can?","options":["Whether responses match the published OpenAPI schema","Whether a field has the declared type","Which specific consumers would break if a field were removed","Whether the service handles malformed requests"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A schema describes what the provider offers, not what each consumer uses. Consumer-driven contracts record actual usage, so a removal fails against exactly the consumers that read that field."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/integration-and-end-to-end-testing","sources":["Cohn, 2009 — Succeeding with Agile","Official docs: Testcontainers","Official docs: pytest"],"why":"- **Unit tests only prove logic given your assumptions.** Doubles check your code against your model of\n  the outside world; integration and end-to-end tests check that model against reality.\n- **A RAG service with perfect unit tests can fail on its first real query:**\n  - the fake vector store returned exactly *k* results, but the real approximate index with a tenant\n    filter returns fewer;\n  - the fake database accepted 1,536-dimensional vectors into a column declared for 768;\n  - the SQL the ORM generates differs from what the mocked repository implied;\n  - the migration that creates the index was never run in tests;\n  - the streaming endpoint works in isolation, but the reverse proxy buffers the stream.\n- **None of these are logic bugs.** They are disagreements between your code and the real systems it\n  touches, and a double cannot reveal them because the double *is* your assumption.\n- **\"Test everything end to end against staging\" fails the other way.** End-to-end runs are slow (minutes,\n  not milliseconds), depend on shared state others change, fail for unrelated reasons (a network blip, a\n  model rate limit, a redeploy), and rarely say *where*. A suite built mainly from them becomes slow,\n  flaky and ignored.\n- **The test pyramid is the compromise:** many fast unit tests for logic, fewer integration tests at each\n  real boundary, and a thin layer of end-to-end tests for the critical user journeys.","how":"### Where the lines are\n| Level | What runs | Typical cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Unit** | one piece of your code, collaborators replaced by doubles | milliseconds per test |\n| **Integration** | your code plus one or more *real* collaborators you control in the test: a database in a container, a real HTTP client against a local server, the real framework routing and serialisation | usually seconds per test, mostly startup |\n| **End-to-end** | the assembled system, deployed roughly as in production, exercised only through its public interface (HTTP API, UI, queue) | minutes per journey |\n\nAn end-to-end test checks a full journey: upload a document, ask a question, get a cited answer.\n\n### Real dependencies, disposable\n- **Testcontainers** (with a Python library) starts a throwaway Docker container from a pinned image for\n  the duration of a fixture, waits until it is ready, and gives you its connection URL.\n- **Same engine as production**, not SQLite or an in-memory imitation, because dialect, indexing and\n  extension behaviour are exactly what you are trying to verify.\n- **Docker Compose** does the same for a multi-service stack.\n- **Scope the container per session** and isolate each test within it, because container startup is the\n  expensive part.\n- **Under pytest-xdist, \"per session\" means per worker process**, so eight workers start eight databases.\n  That is usually fine, and it is also why tests must not assume they share rows with any other test.\n\n### Isolation inside a shared dependency\nEach option has its own failure mode. Never rely on test order.\n\n| Option | Speed | Breaks when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wrap each test in a transaction and roll back | fast | the code commits or uses several connections |\n| Unique namespace per test (tenant ID, schema or collection name) | medium | rarely; works across connections and services |\n| Truncate tables between tests | slower | simple, but serialises tests on shared tables |\n\n### The model in the loop\nThe LLM breaks the usual playbook, because it is non-deterministic, paid and rate-limited. Choices for\nintegration and e2e tests:\n1. **A fake model server** that speaks the provider's HTTP API and returns scripted replies. The full HTTP\n   path, SDK, retries and parsing run for real; only the model is fixed.\n2. **Record and replay:** capture real responses once (cassette libraries such as vcrpy do this for HTTP)\n   and replay them, refreshing deliberately.\n3. **Live calls**, kept few, run on a schedule or before release, asserting structure and invariants\n   (valid JSON, cited document IDs exist) rather than exact text.\n- **Judging answer *quality* belongs in the eval suite**, not the test suite.\n\n### What an agent test asserts at each level\n| Level | Model | Assert |\n|---|---|---|\n| Integration | fake model server behind the real SDK | tool results reach the model in the right message format; retries and parsing work |\n| Integration | none (tools tested directly) | each tool against its real backend: SQL, vector search, file system sandbox |\n| End-to-end | fake server, scripted per scenario | trajectory: which tools ran, in what order, within the step budget |\n| End-to-end, scheduled | live | structure and invariants: valid JSON, cited IDs exist, no tool errors |\n\n### End-to-end tests that are not flaky\nMost e2e flakiness is self-inflicted, and each cause has a mechanical fix:\n- **Starting before the system is ready.** Wait on a readiness endpoint that checks dependencies, not on\n  a fixed sleep, because startup time varies with the CI machine.\n- **Racing asynchronous work.** Ingestion in a RAG system is usually a background job, so \"upload, then\n  immediately ask\" sometimes runs before indexing finishes.\n  - Poll for the observable outcome (the document's status is `indexed`) with a deadline, and fail with\n    the last status seen.\n  - A guessed sleep is either too short on a slow day or wasteful on every run.\n- **Shared data.** Create uniquely named tenants, users and documents per run, so concurrent runs and\n  leftovers from crashed runs cannot collide; clean up best-effort, but never depend on cleanup.\n- **Undiagnosable failures.** Attach the request IDs or trace IDs of every call the test made to the\n  failure message, so a red run points at the logs of the service that misbehaved.\n- **The payoff:** such a test fails only when the system is broken or genuinely slower than its budget,\n  which is the only kind of failure worth paging a developer about.\n\n### Running them\n- **Mark slow tests** (`@pytest.mark.integration`, `@pytest.mark.e2e`) and register the markers.\n- **Select them with `-m`**, so the default local run stays fast while CI runs integration tests on every\n  change and e2e on merge or deploy.","example":"### Agent scenario: real SDK, real HTTP, scripted model\nA local server speaks the chat-completions shape and replays a script: one tool call, then a final\nanswer. The real OpenAI SDK, the agent loop and the tool run unmodified, so a tool result dropped or\nmis-shaped in serialisation fails here. Runnable with `pip install openai pytest`.\n\n```python\nimport json, threading\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer\nimport pytest\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"search_docs\", \"parameters\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"query\"]}}}]\nSCRIPT = [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": None, \"tool_calls\": [{\"id\": \"call_1\", \"type\": \"function\",\n           \"function\": {\"name\": \"search_docs\", \"arguments\": '{\"query\": \"refund window\"}'}}]},\n          {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Refunds take 14 days [d1].\"}]\n\nclass FakeModel(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    seen = []                                           # every request body the SDK sent\n    def do_POST(self):\n        body = json.loads(self.rfile.read(int(self.headers[\"Content-Length\"])))\n        FakeModel.seen.append(body)\n        msg = SCRIPT[len(FakeModel.seen) - 1]\n        data = json.dumps({\"id\": \"x\", \"object\": \"chat.completion\", \"created\": 0, \"model\": body[\"model\"],\n            \"choices\": [{\"index\": 0, \"message\": msg,\n                         \"finish_reason\": \"tool_calls\" if msg.get(\"tool_calls\") else \"stop\"}]}).encode()\n        self.send_response(200); self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(data))); self.end_headers(); self.wfile.write(data)\n    def log_message(self, *args): pass\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef model_url():\n    srv = HTTPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", 0), FakeModel)\n    threading.Thread(target=srv.serve_forever, daemon=True).start()\n    yield f\"http://127.0.0.1:{srv.server_port}/v1\"\n    srv.shutdown()\n\ndef search_docs(query):                                 # in a real suite: the container-backed retriever\n    return [{\"doc_id\": \"d1\", \"text\": \"Refunds are issued within 14 days.\"}]\n\ndef run_agent(client, question, max_steps=4):\n    messages, trace = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}], []\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        msg = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"any\", messages=messages, tools=TOOLS).choices[0].message\n        if not msg.tool_calls:\n            return msg.content, trace\n        messages.append(msg.model_dump(exclude_none=True))\n        for tc in msg.tool_calls:\n            trace.append(tc.function.name)\n            result = search_docs(**json.loads(tc.function.arguments))\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": tc.id, \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n    raise RuntimeError(\"step budget exhausted\")\n\ndef test_agent_trajectory_over_real_http(model_url):\n    client = OpenAI(base_url=model_url, api_key=\"test\", max_retries=0)\n    answer, trace = run_agent(client, \"How long do refunds take?\")\n    assert trace == [\"search_docs\"]                                 # trajectory\n    assert \"[d1]\" in answer                                         # structure, not wording\n    assert FakeModel.seen[1][\"messages\"][-1][\"role\"] == \"tool\"      # result went back to the model\n```\n\n- **What it catches that a unit test cannot:** the assistant message re-sent in the wrong shape, a\n  missing `tool_call_id`, or an SDK upgrade that changes how tool calls parse.\n- **Scale it up** by swapping `search_docs` for the container-backed retriever below and scripting one\n  `SCRIPT` per journey.\n\n### Integration test: a pgvector retriever against the real extension\nThe embedder is a deterministic fake so results are reproducible; everything else (SQL, index,\nmigrations, tenant filter) is real. `PgRetriever` and `HashEmbedder` stand for the application's own code.\n\n```python\nimport uuid\nimport pytest\nfrom testcontainers.postgres import PostgresContainer\nfrom app.retriever import PgRetriever     # code under test: migrate(), add(), search()\nfrom app.embed import HashEmbedder        # deterministic fake: hashes text into an 8-dim vector\n\npytestmark = pytest.mark.integration      # run with: pytest -m integration\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef retriever():\n    with PostgresContainer(\"pgvector/pgvector:0.8.0-pg16\", driver=None) as pg:  # pin both versions\n        r = PgRetriever(pg.get_connection_url(), embedder=HashEmbedder(dim=8))\n        r.migrate()                       # the real migrations: extension, table, HNSW index\n        yield r\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef tenant():\n    return f\"t-{uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]}\"    # unique namespace per test: no cleanup, no cross-talk\n\ndef test_search_never_crosses_tenants(retriever, tenant):\n    retriever.add(tenant, \"d1\", \"refunds are issued within 14 days\")\n    retriever.add(tenant + \"-other\", \"d2\", \"refunds are issued within 14 days\")\n    hits = retriever.search(tenant, \"refund window\", k=5)\n    assert [h.doc_id for h in hits] == [\"d1\"]\n\ndef test_wrong_dimension_is_rejected_by_the_database(retriever, tenant):\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError):       # adapter maps the DB error to a domain error\n        retriever.add_vector(tenant, \"d3\", [0.1] * 7)\n\ndef test_filtered_search_returns_all_matches(retriever, tenant):\n    for i in range(50):\n        retriever.add(tenant, f\"doc{i}\", f\"policy section {i}\")\n    assert len(retriever.search(tenant, \"policy\", k=20)) == 20\n```\n\n- **The last test is the one doubles cannot write.** With a shared approximate index and a selective\n  tenant filter, a naive query can return fewer than *k* rows, which is a property of the real engine and\n  its settings, not of your Python.\n- **Why it happens.** pgvector's HNSW scan gathers a bounded candidate list (`hnsw.ef_search`, 40 by\n  default) and the `WHERE` filter is applied afterwards; version 0.8.0 added iterative index scans that keep\n  searching when too few rows survive, but they are off until you set `hnsw.iterative_scan`.\n- **Make the test meaningful.** Seed enough rows for other tenants that the planner actually uses the\n  index; on a near-empty table Postgres may choose an exact sequential scan and the test passes for the\n  wrong reason.\n- **Pin both versions.** The image tag pins pgvector as well as Postgres: a bare `pg16` tag moves when\n  pgvector releases.\n\n### End-to-end journey: poll with a deadline, report what you saw\nA smoke test against a deployed stack. The URL comes from the environment and the endpoints are the\napplication's own API; the polling helper is plain Python and replaces every guessed sleep.\n\n```python\nimport os, time, uuid\nimport httpx\nimport pytest\n\npytestmark = pytest.mark.e2e\n\ndef wait_for(check, *, timeout: float, interval: float = 0.5):\n    \"\"\"Call check() until it returns (True, value); fail with the last value seen.\"\"\"\n    deadline, last = time.monotonic() + timeout, None\n    while time.monotonic() < deadline:\n        done, last = check()\n        if done:\n            return last\n        time.sleep(interval)\n    raise AssertionError(f\"not done after {timeout}s; last seen: {last!r}\")\n\ndef test_upload_then_ask_cites_the_document():\n    base = os.environ[\"E2E_BASE_URL\"]\n    tenant = f\"e2e-{uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]}\"              # unique per run, never shared\n    req_ids = []\n    with httpx.Client(base_url=base, timeout=30, headers={\"X-Tenant\": tenant}) as http:\n        wait_for(lambda: (http.get(\"/ready\").status_code == 200, None), timeout=60)\n        r = http.post(\"/documents\", json={\"text\": \"Refunds are issued within 14 days.\"})\n        req_ids.append(r.headers.get(\"x-request-id\"))\n        doc_id = r.json()[\"id\"]\n\n        def indexed():\n            s = http.get(f\"/documents/{doc_id}\").json()[\"status\"]\n            return s == \"indexed\", s\n\n        wait_for(indexed, timeout=120)\n        r = http.post(\"/ask\", json={\"question\": \"How long do refunds take?\"})\n        req_ids.append(r.headers.get(\"x-request-id\"))\n        assert doc_id in r.json()[\"citations\"], f\"request ids: {req_ids}\"   # structure, not exact text\n```\n\nThe assertion checks a cited document ID, not the answer text, so a model or prompt change cannot break\nit while the plumbing still works.\n\n### Worked scenario: fixed sleep vs polling for background ingestion\nIllustrative indexing times on a shared CI runner: about 4 s typically, up to 25 s on a busy day.\n\n| Strategy | Typical wait | Busy-day outcome | Over 200 CI runs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `time.sleep(10)` then ask | 10 s every run | fails whenever indexing takes over 10 s, passes on rerun | ~33 min spent sleeping; flaky reds teach people to rerun |\n| `time.sleep(30)` then ask | 30 s every run | passes | ~100 min spent sleeping |\n| poll every 0.5 s, 120 s deadline | ~4.5 s | passes at ~25 s | ~15 min waiting; a red means indexing really exceeded 120 s |","patterns":["**Lanes by marker and trigger.** Register `integration` and `e2e` markers; run unit plus integration on every pull request and e2e on merge or deploy, with locally `pytest -m \"not integration and not e2e\"`. Monitor wall time per lane and keep the PR lane within a few minutes.","**Pinned, session-scoped containers.** One Testcontainers fixture per dependency at session scope, with images pinned to a full tag such as `pgvector/pgvector:0.8.0-pg16`, and the real migrations applied in the fixture. Cache images in CI and track container startup time separately from test time.","**Point the SDK at a fake model server.** Run a small HTTP server that speaks the provider's chat API with scripted replies, and set the client's base URL to it through configuration (the OpenAI and Anthropic Python clients both accept `base_url=`). The SDK, retries and parsing stay real. Review the fake whenever the SDK is upgraded.","**Unique namespaces plus a janitor.** Prefix every test tenant, collection and user with `e2e-` and a random suffix, and run a scheduled job that deletes prefixed data older than a day. Monitor leftover counts, which reveal crashed runs.","**Flake tracking instead of blind retries.** Record per-test pass/fail-then-pass on rerun with no code change, quarantine a flaky test with an owner and a deadline, and fix or delete it. Do not auto-retry e2e tests to green, because retries hide real races and timeouts near p99 latency.","**Scheduled live-model smoke.** A handful of real calls on a schedule or before release, asserting valid JSON and existing cited IDs. Alert on structure failures; track cost and rate-limit errors per run.","**Trajectory assertions from traces.** Have the agent service return or log a trace per run (tools called, step count, request IDs) and assert on it in e2e tests: expected tools ran, no tool errored, the step budget held. Monitor step count per journey; a jump means the agent is looping."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent with green unit tests can still retrieve nothing for one\n  tenant, lose a tool result in SDK serialisation, or answer before ingestion finished. Those are\n  disagreements with real systems, and only tests against real systems find them.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Testcontainers for the vector store and memory database, a fake\n  model server behind the real provider SDK (`base_url=`), recorded cassettes, and a few e2e journeys\n  against the deployed agent API.\n- **What you control.** Which boundaries get a real dependency, where the model is faked, replayed or\n  called live, and what an agent e2e test asserts: trajectory and structure (tools called, cited IDs),\n  never exact wording.\n- **What you can skip.** Judging answer quality here. That is the eval suite's job; these tests check the\n  plumbing the model depends on.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tenant-filtered retrieval.** An integration test against a real vector store in a container checks\n  that a tenant filter returns only that tenant's documents and copes with fewer than *k* hits.\n- **SDK serialisation.** A fake model server behind the real provider SDK (`base_url=`) proves tool\n  results and tool-call messages survive the round trip intact.\n- **Ingestion to answer.** An end-to-end journey uploads a document, waits for ingestion, asks a question\n  and asserts the answer cites that document's ID.\n- **Streaming through the proxy.** An e2e test against the deployed agent API checks that tokens arrive\n  incrementally, not buffered into one response.","mini_project":"**Write an integration test for a RAG agent with a fake model server (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** test a RAG agent's real plumbing (vector store, SDK, tool dispatch) with a scripted model.\n- **Steps:** start a vector store with Testcontainers; ingest documents for two tenants; run a small\n  HTTP server that speaks the provider's chat API and returns a scripted tool call then a final answer;\n  point the real SDK at it with `base_url=`; assert on the trajectory (search tool called with the\n  tenant filter) and on cited IDs, not on wording.\n- **Done when:** the test passes locally and in CI, a broken tenant filter makes it fail, and no real\n  model API key is needed."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Code change\"] --> U[\"Unit lane: doubles\"]\n    U --> I[\"Integration lane: every PR\"]\n    I --> PG[(\"Postgres plus pgvector, pinned\")]\n    I --> FM[\"Fake model server\"]\n    I --> E[\"E2E lane: merge or deploy\"]\n    E --> FM\n    E --> RD[\"Poll status with a deadline\"]\n    S[\"Scheduled live-model check\"] -.->|\"structure and invariants only\"| E\n    E -.->|\"answer quality\"| EV[\"Eval suite, not tests\"]","caption":"Which lane catches what: each step down uses more real systems, runs less often and costs more per test.\nUnit tests use doubles on every save, integration runs real containers per PR, and e2e drives the\ndeployed API on merge or deploy, polling readiness instead of sleeping."},"interview":[{"q":"Where do you draw the line between unit, integration and end-to-end tests?","a":"- **Unit:** your code with collaborators replaced by doubles, milliseconds per test, for logic.\n- **Integration:** your code plus at least one real collaborator you control in the test, such as Postgres\n  in a container or the real framework routing, to check your assumptions about it.\n- **End-to-end:** the deployed system through its public interface only, for a few critical journeys,\n  because it is slow and poor at locating faults."},{"q":"How would you keep integration tests isolated when they share one database container?","a":"- **Prefer a unique namespace per test**, such as a tenant ID or collection name from `uuid4`, because it\n  works across connections and services and needs no cleanup.\n- **Transaction rollback is faster** but breaks once the code commits or uses several connections.\n- **Never rely on order:** under pytest-xdist each worker has its own container, and tests must not assume\n  shared rows."},{"q":"An end-to-end test fails one run in ten and passes on rerun. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Look for self-inflicted causes first:** a fixed sleep racing background ingestion, starting before\n  readiness, or data shared with concurrent runs.\n- **Replace guesses with polling** on the observable outcome (status `indexed`) with a deadline, failing\n  with the last status seen.\n- **Attach request or trace IDs to the failure message**, and do not add retries, because they would hide a\n  real race or a timeout near p99 latency."},{"q":"How do you include the LLM in integration and e2e tests without flakiness or cost?","a":"- **Default to a fake model server** that speaks the provider's HTTP API, so the SDK, retries and parsing\n  run for real while replies are scripted.\n- **Use record and replay** for realistic responses, refreshing cassettes deliberately.\n- **Keep a few scheduled live calls** asserting structure (valid JSON, cited IDs exist), and leave answer\n  quality to evals."},{"q":"What would an end-to-end test of a tool-using agent assert?","a":"- **Trajectory, not prose:** which tools ran, in what order, that none returned an error, and that the\n  run finished within its step budget.\n- **Structure of the answer:** cited document IDs exist, output parses, no internal error text leaks.\n- **Make it deterministic** with a fake model server scripted per journey behind the real SDK, and keep\n  a few scheduled live runs for drift; quality stays in evals."},{"q":"Why might a filtered vector search test pass locally but miss a real production bug?","a":"- **On a near-empty table Postgres may choose an exact sequential scan**, so the HNSW index and its\n  post-filtering are never exercised.\n- **In production the HNSW scan gathers a bounded candidate list** (`hnsw.ef_search`, 40 by default) before\n  the `WHERE` filter, so a selective tenant filter can return fewer than *k* rows.\n- **Fix the test:** seed enough rows for other tenants that the planner uses the index, pin the pgvector\n  version, and assert the full *k* is returned."}],"tradeoffs":["**Real dependencies vs startup cost.** They catch dialect, index and migration bugs that fakes hide, but add container startup time and a Docker requirement in CI; session-scoped containers keep that cost roughly constant.","**Whole-system confidence vs fault location.** End-to-end tests are the only check that the deployed whole works, but are slow, flaky and poor at locating faults; keep them to a few critical journeys and push everything else down a level.","**Deterministic fake models vs reality.** Fake model servers make AI e2e tests deterministic and free, but test only your handling of replies you anticipated; scheduled live checks flip that, at the cost of money, rate limits and noise.","**Rollback isolation vs generality.** Transaction rollback is very fast, but breaks once code commits or spans connections; unique namespaces per test are slower to set up and work everywhere."],"pitfalls":["**SQLite or an in-memory imitation in place of Postgres with pgvector.** Symptom: green tests and production errors on vector operators, JSON functions or index behaviour.","**Tests sharing rows or collections.** Symptom: a suite that passes serially and fails under parallel runs or in a different order, which teams misdiagnose as random flakiness.","**Asserting exact LLM output text in end-to-end tests.** Symptom: failures after any model or prompt change even though the system still works, until the tests are disabled.","**Unpinned container image tags such as `latest`.** Symptom: a suite that breaks overnight with no code change because the database or extension version moved.","**Fixed `sleep` calls waiting for background ingestion or deployment.** Symptom: a test that fails on busy CI runners and passes on rerun, while the suite's wall time grows by the sum of every guess.","**Retrying flaky e2e tests until green.** Symptom: a real intermittent bug (a race, a timeout too close to p99 latency) that the retry hides and production users then hit.","**Agent e2e tests against a live model as the default lane.** Symptom: red runs that change with the model's mood, a token bill per pull request, and rate-limit failures. Fix: a scripted fake server in the default lane and a few scheduled live journeys asserting structure only."],"when":"- **Write an integration test at every boundary** where your code meets something you did not write and\n  cannot fully fake: databases and vector stores, queues, caches, the web framework's request handling,\n  and the HTTP layer to model providers via a fake server.\n- **Keep end-to-end tests to a handful of journeys** that would be a severity-one incident if broken, and\n  run them on merge or deploy rather than on every save.\n- **Measure:** suite time per lane and flake rate (the share of runs that fail and then pass on rerun with\n  no code change). Fix or delete flaky tests quickly, because each one teaches the team to ignore red.\n- **Interviews:** \"how would you test a RAG service?\" expects this layering, plus a clear separation from\n  evals that measure quality.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes container-backed integration tests with unique per-test namespaces, marks them, and keeps the default local test run fast.","architect":"Defines the test layering, what runs on every commit versus before deploy, and where the model is faked, replayed or called live, with an explicit budget for each.","fde":"Builds an end-to-end smoke test against the customer's environment (their SSO, network, data store) that proves the deployment works before handing it over."},"check":{"q":"Your RAG service's unit tests all pass, but after deploying, answers for some tenants have no\ncitations. How would you change the test suite so this class of failure is caught before deploy?","a":"Missing citations for some tenants smells like a real-engine behaviour: a tenant filter combined\nwith an approximate index returning fewer rows, a migration not applied, or a dimension mismatch. I would\nadd container-backed integration tests against the same Postgres and pgvector version, running the real\nmigrations, with per-test tenant namespaces and cases for selective filters and large tenants. Then one\nend-to-end test that uploads a document and asks a question through the public API against a fake model\nserver, asserting that the response cites an existing document ID. Model answer quality stays in evals;\nthese tests check the plumbing that the model depends on."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes an integration test from a unit test in this layering?","options":["Integration tests are written in a different framework","Integration tests run your code against at least one real collaborator instead of a double","Integration tests always go through the public UI","Integration tests cannot be run with pytest"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The defining feature is the real dependency, such as a containerised database. Driving the whole deployed system through its public interface describes end-to-end tests, not integration tests."},{"q":"Why is SQLite a poor stand-in for Postgres with pgvector in integration tests?","options":["SQLite is too slow for tests","SQLite cannot be started from pytest fixtures","The test exists to verify engine-specific behaviour, which a different engine does not have","SQLite does not support transactions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vector operators, index behaviour and SQL dialect are exactly what integration tests check, so a different engine silently skips the assumptions you meant to verify. SQLite is fast, not slow."},{"q":"Which approach keeps an AI end-to-end test deterministic while still exercising the HTTP path?","options":["Calling the live model with temperature zero and asserting exact text","Pointing the system at a fake model server that speaks the provider's API with scripted replies","Mocking the service's handler function inside the test process","Retrying the test until the model produces the expected answer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A fake server keeps SDK, retries and parsing real while fixing the model's output. Temperature zero does not guarantee identical text, and mocking the handler is no longer end to end."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/mypy","sources":["Official docs: mypy","PEP 484 — Type Hints","PEP 561 — Distributing and Packaging Type Information"],"why":"- **Python ignores hints at runtime.** `def call_model(prompt: str) -> str` happily accepts a list and\n  returns `None`; you find out when a downstream `.strip()` raises `AttributeError` in production, often\n  on a rare path: the model returned no content, an env var was unset, a tool call had no arguments.\n- **AI code is especially exposed.** It passes loosely shaped data between layers: provider responses\n  where `content` may be `None`, dicts of tool arguments, optional fields in config, results that are\n  either a value or an error.\n- **Tests and Pydantic leave gaps.** Tests cover only the paths you test, and the `None` paths are the\n  ones people forget. Runtime validation catches bad data at boundaries, but not inside your own code\n  between boundaries.\n- **Unchecked annotations are comments that drift.** \"We have annotations, that is enough\" gives you\n  documentation that silently becomes wrong after the third refactor, because nothing checks it.\n- **A checker is a cheap whole-program test.** It runs in seconds, covers every path including those no\n  test exercises, and makes refactoring safe: change a signature and it lists every caller you broke.","how":"### Checking\n1. **Parse** each module.\n2. **Build types** for every definition from annotations and from inference (`x = []` followed by\n   `x.append(1)` infers `list[int]`).\n3. **Check every expression** against them.\n4. **Report errors with a code** in brackets (`[arg-type]`, `[union-attr]`, `[return-value]`), which you\n   use for targeted ignores and configuration.\n\n### Narrowing\n- **Narrowing makes optional types usable:** after `if content is None: return`, mypy knows `content` is\n  `str`.\n- **Same mechanism** for `isinstance`, comparisons against `Literal` values, and `TypeGuard`/`TypeIs`\n  functions.\n- **Exhaustiveness:** calling `assert_never(x)` in the final branch makes mypy prove a chain of checks is\n  exhaustive, so adding a new literal value breaks the build where it is not handled.\n\n### Subtyping: nominal, structural, and variance\n- **Nominal by default:** a value is compatible with a class if it is an instance of that class or a\n  subclass.\n- **`Protocol` switches to structural typing:** any object with matching methods qualifies, without\n  inheriting anything. That is how you type \"anything with an async `complete` method\", so a real client\n  and a test fake both satisfy one interface.\n- **`list[int]` is *not* a `list[float]`**, even though `int` is accepted where `float` is expected. The\n  reason is soundness: a function taking `list[float]` may append `0.5`, which would corrupt the caller's\n  `list[int]`.\n\n| Container | Variance | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `list`, `dict` (mutable) | invariant | `list[int]` rejected where `list[float]` is expected |\n| `Sequence`, `Mapping` values (read-only) | covariant | `Sequence[float]` accepts a `list[int]` |\n\n- **The usual fix** is to accept `Sequence[float]` when the function only reads.\n- **`reveal_type(expr)`** prints what mypy inferred at that point, and is the first tool when a message\n  seems wrong.\n\n### Gradual typing\n- **`Any` is compatible with everything, in both directions**, so errors stop propagating through it.\n- **Unannotated functions are skipped by default:** mypy does not check the bodies of functions with no\n  annotations at all, because adopting a checker on an untyped codebase would otherwise produce\n  thousands of errors.\n- **Both choices make adoption possible and both hide bugs**, which is why `--strict` exists, along with\n  flags such as `disallow_untyped_defs`, `check_untyped_defs`, `warn_return_any` and `warn_unused_ignores`.\n\n### Third-party types\n- **Inline hints** are used if the package ships a `py.typed` marker (PEP 561).\n- **Otherwise stub packages:** the standard library's from typeshed (bundled), others from `types-*`\n  packages.\n- **Untyped libraries** produce \"missing library stubs\" errors, silenced per module with\n  `ignore_missing_imports`.\n- **Plugins** teach mypy about some libraries, such as Pydantic's mypy plugin for model constructors.\n\n### Configuration and speed\nConfigure in `pyproject.toml`; per-module overrides let you be strict in new code and lenient in legacy:\n\n```toml\n[tool.mypy]\nstrict = true\nplugins = [\"pydantic.mypy\"]\n\n[[tool.mypy.overrides]]\nmodule = [\"legacy.*\"]\ndisallow_untyped_defs = false\n\n[[tool.mypy.overrides]]\nmodule = [\"some_untyped_sdk.*\"]\nignore_missing_imports = true\n```\n\n- **Run it in the real environment.** mypy reads types from the packages installed where it runs, so it\n  needs the project's real dependencies and the same target Python version (`python_version`) as\n  production. In a bare CI image every third-party import becomes a missing-stubs error or silently `Any`.\n- **Incremental:** results are cached in `.mypy_cache` and only what changed is rechecked; `dmypy` keeps a\n  daemon running for faster editor-style feedback.\n\n| Checker | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| mypy | long-standing reference, plugin system |\n| pyright (engine behind Pylance) | faster, infers more aggressively, so it disagrees with mypy at the edges |\n| Astral's ty, Meta's Pyrefly | Rust-based and much faster but younger; check current maturity and typing-spec conformance before making one a gate |\n\n- **Pick one checker as the CI gate.**\n\n### Where types pay off in an agent\n| Agent boundary | Type it as | What mypy then catches |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model reply content | `str \\| None`, narrowed before use | `.strip()` on a tool-call reply with no text |\n| Tool arguments after parsing | `TypedDict` or a Pydantic model, not `dict[str, Any]` | misspelled argument keys |\n| Step results of the loop | a union of dataclasses (tool call, final, refusal) | a new step type left unhandled |\n| Model client | a `Protocol` with the methods the loop uses | a test fake whose methods do not match |\n| `json.loads` output | parse straight into a model | `Any` silently disabling checks downstream |","example":"### Agent scenario: an exhaustive step handler\nThe loop turns each model reply into one of three step types. Check with `mypy --strict agent_step.py`;\nmessages are abridged and were produced with a recent mypy.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import assert_never\n\n@dataclass\nclass ToolCall:\n    id: str\n    name: str\n    arguments: str          # a JSON string, as providers send it\n\n@dataclass\nclass Final:\n    text: str | None        # a reply can arrive with no text content\n\n@dataclass\nclass Refusal:\n    reason: str\n\nStep = ToolCall | Final | Refusal\n\ndef run_tool(name: str, arguments: str) -> str:\n    return \"ok\"\n\ndef handle(step: Step) -> str:\n    match step:\n        case ToolCall(name=name, arguments=args):\n            return run_tool(name, args)\n        case Final(text=text):\n            return text.strip()\n            # error: Item \"None\" of \"str | None\" has no attribute \"strip\"  [union-attr]\n        case Refusal():\n            return \"refused\"\n        case _:\n            assert_never(step)\n\ndef handle_v2(step: Step) -> str:          # written before Refusal existed\n    match step:\n        case ToolCall():\n            return run_tool(step.name, step.arguments)\n        case Final():\n            return step.text or \"\"\n        case _:\n            assert_never(step)\n            # error: Argument 1 to \"assert_never\" has incompatible type \"Refusal\"; expected \"Never\"  [arg-type]\n```\n\n- **Two agent incidents prevented:** the `None` reply that would crash the final step, and the new\n  `Refusal` step type that an older handler silently mishandled.\n- **Add a step type, get a to-do list:** every `assert_never` that now fails is a place in the loop,\n  tracing or UI that must learn the new case.\n\n### Bugs a test suite misses, and one mypy misses\nEach reported by mypy with its error code. The comments show mypy's messages (paths and line numbers\nomitted).\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom typing import Literal, TypedDict, assert_never   # assert_never: Python 3.11+\n\nclass ToolCall(TypedDict):\n    name: Literal[\"search\", \"fetch_url\"]\n    arguments: dict[str, str]\n\ndef run(model: str, prompt: str) -> str: ...\n\ndef answer(prompt: str) -> str:\n    model = os.environ.get(\"MODEL\")          # str | None\n    return run(model, prompt)\n    # error: Argument 1 to \"run\" has incompatible type \"str | None\"; expected \"str\"  [arg-type]\n\ndef first_text(content: str | None) -> str:\n    return content.strip()\n    # error: Item \"None\" of \"str | None\" has no attribute \"strip\"  [union-attr]\n\ndef query_of(call: ToolCall) -> str:\n    return call[\"argumnets\"][\"query\"]\n    # error: TypedDict \"ToolCall\" has no key \"argumnets\"  [typeddict-item]\n\ndef route(call: ToolCall) -> str:\n    name = call[\"name\"]\n    if name == \"search\":\n        return \"search-service\"\n    elif name == \"fetch_url\":\n        return \"fetcher\"\n    else:\n        assert_never(name)   # adding \"run_sql\" to the Literal makes this line an error\n\ndef helper(resp):                  # unannotated: mypy skips this body by default\n    return first_text(resp) + 1    # str + int: reported only with check_untyped_defs (or strict)\n    # resp.choises would pass even then: an unannotated parameter is Any until you annotate it\n```\n\n- **The fixes are one line each:** `os.environ[\"MODEL\"]` or a default, an `is None` branch, the correct\n  key. mypy 1.19 also attaches a `Did you mean \"arguments\"?` note to the TypedDict error; under\n  `--strict` the same line additionally reports `[no-any-return]`, because the bad key's value is `Any`.\n- **Why `strict` matters:** unannotated bodies are skipped by default, and even `check_untyped_defs` cannot\n  see through parameters that are implicitly `Any`. Strict mode's `disallow_untyped_defs` forces the\n  annotation that makes the checker useful there.\n- **A side effect of the stub:** `def run(...) -> str: ...` in a regular module is itself reported as\n  `Missing return statement  [empty-body]`; real code has a body or lives in a `.pyi` stub.\n\n### Protocols and variance: a fake that type-checks, a fake that does not\nCheck with `mypy --strict fakes.py`; the file is for the checker, not for running. Messages are from\nmypy 1.19, abridged.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Protocol, Sequence\n\nclass Completer(Protocol):\n    async def complete(self, prompt: str) -> str: ...\n\nclass FakeLLM:                                  # no inheritance: matches structurally\n    async def complete(self, prompt: str) -> str:\n        return prompt.upper()\n\nclass BadFake:\n    def complete(self, prompt: str) -> str:     # sync: does not match the protocol\n        return prompt\n\nasync def summarize(llm: Completer, text: str) -> str:\n    return await llm.complete(f\"Summarize: {text}\")\n\ndef mean(xs: list[float]) -> float:\n    return sum(xs) / len(xs)\n\ndef mean_ro(xs: Sequence[float]) -> float:\n    return sum(xs) / len(xs)\n\nasync def main() -> None:\n    await summarize(FakeLLM(), \"doc\")           # ok\n    await summarize(BadFake(), \"doc\")\n    # error: Argument 1 to \"summarize\" has incompatible type \"BadFake\"; expected \"Completer\"  [arg-type]\n    # note: Expected: def complete(self, prompt: str) -> Coroutine[Any, Any, str]\n    scores: list[int] = [3, 4, 5]\n    mean(scores)\n    # error: Argument 1 to \"mean\" has incompatible type \"list[int]\"; expected \"list[float]\"  [arg-type]\n    # note: \"list\" is invariant ... Consider using \"Sequence\" instead, which is covariant\n    mean_ro(scores)                             # ok: Sequence is covariant\n    reveal_type(scores)                         # note: Revealed type is \"builtins.list[builtins.int]\"\n```\n\nmypy 2.x prints the same errors but shorter revealed types (`list[int]`). The sync fake would have failed at runtime with \"object str can't be used in 'await' expression\", but\nonly on the path that awaits it; mypy reports it before any test runs.\n\n### Worked scenario: adopting mypy on a legacy service\nIllustrative counts for a 60-module service; the shape of the ratchet is what matters.\n\n| Step | Config change | Errors to fix | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | default mode, `ignore_missing_imports` per untyped SDK | ~40, mostly `union-attr` and `arg-type` | real `None` bugs fixed; gate turned on in CI |\n| 2 | `strict = true`, all old modules in a `legacy.*`-style override | 0 new; new modules strict from day one | strict code grows with every PR |\n| 3 | move 5 modules per sprint out of the override | ~20 per module | override list shrinks; track it as a metric |\n| 4 | `warn_unused_ignores` on | stale ignores removed | suppressions stay honest |","patterns":["**Gate CI in the project's environment.** Install the real dependencies (for example `uv run mypy src/` or `pip install -e .` first) and set `python_version` to production's version. Cache `.mypy_cache` between CI runs. Monitor error count on main, which must stay at zero.","**Strict for new code, ratchet the rest.** `strict = true` globally, with legacy packages listed in a `[[tool.mypy.overrides]]` block that relaxes `disallow_untyped_defs`. Remove modules from the list as they are typed, and track the list's length as the adoption metric.","**Honest suppressions.** Require error codes on every ignore (`# type: ignore[union-attr]`) by adding `enable_error_code = [\"ignore-without-code\"]`, and set `warn_unused_ignores = true` so stale ones fail the build. Review the ignore count in each release.","**Measure `Any` leakage.** Run `mypy --any-exprs-report <dir>` periodically; it writes a per-module table of how many expressions are `Any` and why (unannotated, unimported, explicit). Model the worst boundaries with `TypedDict` or Pydantic.","**Typed agent boundaries.** Parse every tool call's arguments straight into a Pydantic model or `TypedDict`, type reply content as optional, and model loop steps as a union handled with `assert_never`. Run `--any-exprs-report` on the agent package first, because that is where `Any` from `json.loads` concentrates. Monitor production `AttributeError` and `KeyError` in the loop.","**pre-commit needs its dependencies.** The `mirrors-mypy` hook runs in its own isolated environment, so list typed libraries and stub packages under `additional_dependencies`. Otherwise imports silently become `Any` and pre-commit passes code that CI rejects."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The crashes that end agent runs are rarely logic errors; they are a\n  `.strip()` on a reply with no text, a misspelled tool-argument key, or a new step type the loop never\n  handles. mypy finds all three across every path, including the rare ones no test reaches.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Provider SDK response types (most ship `py.typed`), `TypedDict` and\n  Pydantic models for tool arguments, a `Protocol` for the model client so fakes type-check, and a union of\n  step types the agent loop must handle exhaustively.\n- **What you control.** Strictness per module, how precisely you type the boundaries where model output\n  enters, and whether `Any` from `json.loads` leaks into the loop.\n- **What you can skip.** Typing theory beyond variance and narrowing; runtime validation of model output\n  is Pydantic's job, not mypy's.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Optional model content.** mypy forces a check before `.strip()` on a reply whose content may be\n  `None`, the classic crash after a tool-call-only response.\n- **Typed tool arguments.** `TypedDict` or Pydantic models for tool arguments turn a misspelled key into\n  a type error instead of a `KeyError` in the middle of an agent run.\n- **Exhaustive step handling.** A union of step types with `assert_never` makes adding a new step type\n  fail type checking until the agent loop handles it.\n- **Swappable model clients.** A `Protocol` for the model client lets the fake used in tests and the\n  real SDK wrapper both type-check against one interface.","mini_project":"**Type-harden an agent loop with mypy (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make mypy catch the three common agent crashes: `None` content, a wrong tool-argument key,\n  and an unhandled step type.\n- **Steps:** take a small untyped agent loop; add a `Protocol` for the model client, `TypedDict` models\n  for two tools' arguments and a union of step types; run mypy in strict mode on the agent package;\n  stop `Any` from `json.loads` leaking by validating at the boundary; add an `assert_never` branch.\n- **Done when:** mypy passes, and each of three deliberate bugs (removing a `None` check, misspelling an\n  argument key, adding a new step type) produces a mypy error at the right line."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    SRC[\"Your modules and annotations\"] --> P[\"Parse and build types\"]\n    PKG[(\"Installed packages and stubs\")] --> P\n    CFG[\"pyproject.toml: strict, overrides\"] --> P\n    P --> I[\"Infer unannotated locals\"]\n    I --> Q{\"Function annotated?\"}\n    Q -->|\"no, default\"| SK[\"Body skipped\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| C[\"Check expressions, narrow types\"]\n    C --> A{\"Value typed Any?\"}\n    A -->|\"yes\"| SL[\"Checks switched off downstream\"]\n    A -->|\"no\"| E[\"Errors with codes, e.g. union-attr\"]\n    SK -.->|\"check_untyped_defs or strict\"| C\n    E --> CA[(\".mypy_cache for incremental reruns\")]","caption":"What mypy consumes and where checking silently stops: annotated code is checked end to end, while `Any`\nand unannotated bodies bypass the checks unless strict flags close them. Types come from `py.typed`\npackages, typeshed and `types-*` stubs; narrowing works on `None`, `isinstance` and `Literal` checks."},"interview":[{"q":"Your agent reads tool arguments from json.loads. What is gradual typing, and why is that Any contagious?","a":"- **Gradual:** unannotated functions are not checked by default and `Any` is compatible with everything in\n  both directions, so a large codebase can adopt mypy module by module.\n- **Contagious:** any value derived from an `Any` (an attribute, an index, a call result) is also `Any`, so\n  one `dict[str, Any]` of tool arguments switches off checking for everything the tool does with it.\n- **Close the gap** with `--strict`, boundary models (`TypedDict`, Pydantic) that parse the arguments, and\n  `--any-exprs-report`."},{"q":"Why is list[int] rejected where list[float] is expected, and how do you fix the signature?","a":"- **Mutable containers are invariant** for soundness: a function taking `list[float]` could append `0.5`\n  and corrupt the caller's `list[int]`.\n- **State the real requirement:** if the function only reads, accept `Sequence[float]` (or\n  `Mapping[str, float]`), which is covariant.\n- **Do not paper over it** with `cast` or `# type: ignore[arg-type]`; mypy's own note suggests `Sequence`."},{"q":"How would you use types to make an agent loop safe to extend with new step types?","a":"- **Model steps as a union** of small dataclasses (tool call, final answer, refusal) instead of a dict\n  with a `type` key.\n- **Handle them with `match` and `assert_never`** in the final branch, so adding a step type makes mypy\n  report every handler that does not cover it.\n- **Type reply content as `str | None`** so the checker forces a branch for replies with no text, the\n  case that otherwise crashes the last step."},{"q":"mypy passes locally but CI reports dozens of missing-stubs errors. What happened?","a":"- **mypy reads types from the installed environment**, so a CI image without the project's dependencies\n  makes every third-party import a missing-stubs error, or silently `Any`.\n- **Fix the environment, not the config:** install real dependencies and `types-*` stubs before running,\n  and set `python_version` to production's.\n- **The reverse also happens** with pre-commit's isolated hook environment, unless typed libraries are\n  listed under `additional_dependencies`."},{"q":"How would you introduce mypy to a 200-module untyped codebase without stopping feature work?","a":"- **Start lenient and gate CI immediately** on the codes that find real bugs (`union-attr`, `arg-type`,\n  `return-value`), with `ignore_missing_imports` per untyped SDK, never globally.\n- **Make new code strict** via `strict = true` plus an override listing legacy packages, so the strict share\n  grows with every pull request.\n- **Ratchet:** remove a few modules from the override each sprint, turn on `warn_unused_ignores`, and track\n  the override list and `Any` share as metrics."},{"q":"Would you use mypy or pyright as the CI gate, and would you run both?","a":"- **Either works as a gate:** mypy is the long-standing reference with a plugin system (Pydantic's\n  plugin, for example); pyright is faster and infers more aggressively, which suits editor feedback.\n- **Run one as the gate**, because the two disagree at the edges and dual gates mostly produce arguments\n  about edge cases.\n- **Treat newer Rust-based checkers** (ty, Pyrefly) as candidates to evaluate on maturity and typing-spec\n  conformance before they gate anything."}],"tradeoffs":["**Strict mode: most bugs caught vs annotation effort.** It fights dynamic code, and flips from burden to bargain once a codebase is refactored often or shared by several teams.","**Gradual typing: incremental adoption vs holes.** `Any` and unannotated functions leave holes that look checked; track the untyped share and ratchet strictness module by module.","**Static checks: every path vs only your process.** They cover every path at near-zero runtime cost, but say nothing about data from outside the process; model output and HTTP payloads still need runtime validation with Pydantic or similar.","**pyright vs mypy.** pyright is faster and more aggressive at inference, while mypy is the long-standing reference with a plugin system; mixing both as CI gates mostly produces arguments about edge cases."],"pitfalls":["**Sprinkling bare `# type: ignore` comments.** Symptom: suppressed real errors that outlive their reason. Fix: `warn_unused_ignores` and error-code-specific ignores make them visible.","**Typing provider responses or tool arguments as `dict[str, Any]`.** Symptom: mypy passes code that reads misspelled keys, because `Any` turns off every check downstream of it.","**Leaving functions unannotated and assuming they are checked.** Symptom: obvious misuse of typed values in those bodies that mypy never reports until `check_untyped_defs` is on, and attribute typos on their implicitly `Any` parameters that stay invisible until the parameters are annotated.","**Running mypy only in editors, not in CI.** Symptom: a main branch with hundreds of errors that nobody can turn on as a gate without a week of cleanup.","**Annotating a read-only parameter as `list[float]` or `dict[str, float]`.** Symptom: `[arg-type]` errors at callers passing `list[int]`, \"fixed\" with casts or ignores, when `Sequence[float]` or `Mapping[str, float]` states the real requirement.","**Ignoring missing stubs globally rather than per module.** Symptom: a typo in an import or a wrongly named function from a library that no longer produces any error."],"when":"- **Use on every service that will be maintained beyond a prototype:** mypy (or one chosen alternative)\n  as a CI gate alongside ruff and pytest.\n- **New projects:** start in strict mode.\n- **Existing projects:** start lenient, fix the error codes that find real bugs (`union-attr`, `arg-type`,\n  `return-value`), and tighten per module.\n- **Pair with boundary types:** `TypedDict`, `Literal` and Pydantic models where untyped data enters,\n  because types are only as good as the boundary that establishes them.\n- **Interviews:** explain gradual typing and why `Any` is contagious, not just which flags you set.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Annotates provider and tool boundaries precisely, narrows optional values explicitly, and treats every `type: ignore` as a reviewed exception with an error code.","architect":"Sets the typing policy: which checker gates CI, strictness for new versus legacy modules, and how typed contracts flow between services and agent tools.","fde":"Adds typed adapters around a customer's loosely shaped API early, so changes in their payloads surface as type errors in the integration code rather than as runtime failures."},"check":{"q":"A team says \"we have type hints everywhere and mypy passes, yet we keep getting `NoneType has no\nattribute` errors in production.\" Give two plausible causes and how to confirm each.","a":"First, `Any` leakage: responses or JSON are typed `dict[str, Any]` or come from an untyped library,\nso attribute access on them is never checked. Confirm with `reveal_type` at the failing site or mypy's\nany-expression reports, and fix by modelling the data with `TypedDict` or Pydantic at the boundary.\nSecond, gradual-typing gaps: the failing code is in unannotated functions or under a lenient override,\nso its body is not checked; turn on `check_untyped_defs` or strict for that module and see what appears.\nA third possibility is that runtime data violates the declared types, such as a field the provider\ndocuments as present arriving as null, which only runtime validation catches."},"quiz":[{"q":"By default, what does mypy do with the body of a function that has no annotations?","options":["Infers all parameter types from call sites and checks the body fully","Treats it as dynamically typed and does not report errors inside the body","Refuses to check the module until annotations are added","Converts the parameters to object and reports every attribute access"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Skipping unannotated bodies makes gradual adoption possible; `check_untyped_defs` or strict mode changes that. mypy does not infer parameter types from call sites; unannotated parameters are treated as Any."},{"q":"Why does typing a model response as `dict[str, Any]` weaken mypy's checking so much?","options":["mypy cannot check dictionaries at all","It forces mypy into a slower checking mode","Values typed Any are compatible with everything, so errors downstream of them are not reported","It makes mypy treat the whole module as untyped"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`Any` switches off checks wherever it flows, so a misspelled key or wrong attribute passes silently. Modelling the response with TypedDict or Pydantic restores checking downstream."},{"q":"What does calling `assert_never(x)` in a final `else` branch let mypy verify?","options":["That x is never None at runtime","That every possible value of x's union or Literal type was handled by earlier branches","That the function never raises an exception","That the else branch is executed at least once in tests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"After narrowing, x in the final branch should have no remaining type; if a new Literal value is added and not handled, mypy reports an error at the assert_never call."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/ruff","sources":["Official docs: Ruff","Official docs: flake8-bugbear"],"why":"- **The old toolchain was five or six tools.** flake8 for errors and style, several flake8 plugins\n  (bugbear for likely bugs, comprehensions, simplify), isort for import order, pyupgrade for modern\n  syntax, Black for formatting, sometimes pylint and bandit.\n- **Each had its own config, pins and parse.** Each had its own config file or section, its own version\n  pins, its own ideas about line length, and its own parse of every file.\n- **Slow, fragmented linting gets skipped.** On a large repository people disabled the pre-commit hook and\n  let CI find problems, minutes later instead of while the code is still on screen. Fast enough to run on\n  every save makes it a habit.\n- **In AI code some lint rules catch real defects, not style:**\n  - a blocking `time.sleep` or `requests.get` inside an `async def` stalls every concurrent request;\n  - an `asyncio.create_task` whose result is not stored can be garbage-collected mid-flight;\n  - a mutable default argument shares state across calls;\n  - SQL built with f-strings is an injection risk when a tenant ID comes from a request;\n  - a blind `except Exception` swallows unexpected errors and hides failures.\n  All are cheap to detect statically and expensive to debug in production.\n- **\"Code review will catch it\" does not scale:** reviewers miss mechanical issues, and review time spent\n  on import order is time not spent on design.","how":"### One parse, many rules\n1. **Parse once:** each file becomes an AST and token stream.\n2. **Run every enabled rule** over that single representation.\n3. **In parallel across files.**\n4. **With a per-file cache**, so files unchanged since the last run (under the same settings) are skipped.\n\n- **Why it is fast:** native Rust code plus that architecture; the old toolchain ran each Python tool as\n  its own process and re-parsed each file once per tool.\n- **Benchmarks:** the maintainers publish benchmarks showing large speedups over flake8; treat any\n  specific multiple as illustrative and measure on your own repository.\n\n### What it can and cannot see\n- **One file at a time, with a semantic model** of that file's scopes, bindings and imports: enough to\n  know that `requests.get` refers to the `requests` package, or that a name is unused.\n- **No cross-module type inference.** That is why Ruff recognises a blocking call by its qualified name\n  but cannot follow it through your own wrapper function in another module.\n- **Hence no whole-program pylint checks:** those that depend on whole-program inference are not among\n  the ones it re-implements.\n\n### Rules are namespaced by origin\nEach rule code's prefix names the tool it re-implements.\n\n| Prefix | Origin | Catches |\n|---|---|---|\n| `F` | pyflakes | unused imports, undefined names |\n| `E`/`W` | pycodestyle | style errors and warnings |\n| `I` | isort | import order |\n| `B` | bugbear | likely bugs |\n| `UP` | pyupgrade | outdated syntax |\n| `S` | bandit | security checks |\n| `ASYNC` | flake8-async | blocking calls in coroutines |\n| `PT` | pytest style | test conventions |\n| `SIM`, `C4` | simplify, comprehensions | simpler equivalent code |\n| `RUF` | Ruff's own rules | e.g. dangling tasks, stale `noqa` |\n\n- **Enable by prefix** (`select = [\"B\"]`) or single code; `extend-select` adds to the inherited selection\n  instead of replacing it.\n- **Preview gate.** Newly added rules start in **preview** and run only with `preview = true`, so\n  upgrading Ruff does not silently add findings to your CI; rules graduate to stable in later releases.\n- **Small default set**, covering pyflakes and a subset of pycodestyle errors, because a linter that\n  floods a new project with warnings gets switched off.\n- **`ruff rule B006`** prints the rationale for any rule.\n\n### Fixes\n- **Safe fixes** are behaviour-preserving and applied by `ruff check --fix`.\n- **Unsafe fixes** may change behaviour (such as removing an import with side effects) and are applied\n  only with `--unsafe-fixes`.\n- **Why the split:** a tool that silently changes semantics cannot be trusted in a pre-commit hook.\n\n### Formatter\n- **`ruff format`** is designed as a near drop-in replacement for Black's style, so teams can switch\n  without reformatting debates.\n- **Separate commands:** linting and formatting are run separately; when the formatter owns layout,\n  disable lint rules that fight it (line length is the usual one).\n\n### Configuration\n- **One place:** `pyproject.toml` (or `ruff.toml`). `[tool.ruff]` holds shared settings such as\n  `target-version` and `line-length`; `[tool.ruff.lint]` holds `select`, `ignore` and `per-file-ignores`.\n- **Suppressions:** inline `# noqa: B006` suppresses one rule on one line, and `RUF100` flags `noqa`\n  comments that no longer suppress anything, so suppressions do not outlive the code they excused.\n- **Not a type checker:** Ruff does not know that a value may be `None`, which is mypy's or pyright's job,\n  so the two run side by side.\n\n### Rules that map to agent incidents\n| Agent incident | Typical code | Rule (family) |\n|---|---|---|\n| One slow tool stalls every concurrent agent run | `requests.get` or `subprocess.run` inside an async tool | `ASYNC210`, `ASYNC221` (`ASYNC`) |\n| A background memory or trace write vanishes | `asyncio.create_task(...)` with no stored reference | `RUF006` (`RUF`) |\n| Prompt injection becomes command execution | shell tool using `shell=True` | `S602` (`S`) |\n| Model-written \"math\" runs arbitrary code | calculator tool calling `eval` | `S307` (`S`) |\n| Tool failures hidden from the model | `except Exception: return \"\"` | `BLE001` (`BLE`, not in the baseline below) |\n| Tenant data leaks across agents | SQL tool built with f-strings | `S608` (`S`) |\n\n- **Rules are a floor, not a sandbox.** A lint rule catches `eval` and `shell=True` by name; isolating\n  what a tool can reach is still the sandbox's and permission layer's job.","example":"### Agent scenario: linting a tool module\nFour tools an agent can call, linted with the configuration in the next example. Findings below are\nfrom `ruff check --output-format concise tools.py` on Ruff 0.17.1.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport subprocess\n\nimport requests\n\n\nasync def save_memory(note: str) -> None: ...\n\n\nasync def run_shell(command: str) -> str:\n    out = subprocess.run(command, shell=True, capture_output=True, text=True)  # ASYNC221, S602\n    asyncio.create_task(save_memory(command))                                  # RUF006\n    return out.stdout\n\n\nasync def fetch_page(url: str) -> str:\n    return requests.get(url, timeout=10).text                                  # ASYNC210\n\n\ndef calculator(expression: str) -> float:\n    return eval(expression)                                                    # S307\n\n\ndef read_file(path: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        return open(path).read()\n    except Exception:                      # BLE001 only if you add the BLE family\n        return \"\"\n```\n\n| Finding | Why it matters in an agent | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `S602` | the model chooses `command`, so injected text runs in a shell | argument list without a shell, an allowlist, a sandbox |\n| `ASYNC221`, `ASYNC210` | each call blocks the loop for every concurrent run | `asyncio.create_subprocess_exec`, an async HTTP client |\n| `RUF006` | the memory write can be garbage-collected mid-flight | keep the task in a set or a `TaskGroup` |\n| `S307` | model-written expressions execute as Python | a restricted parser such as `ast.literal_eval` or a math library |\n\n- **Five findings, all design changes:** none has a safe autofix, which is why tool modules deserve the\n  `S` and `ASYNC` families even when the rest of the repo uses a lighter selection.\n\n### Config and snippet for an async AI service\nA configuration, then a snippet with the findings Ruff reports under it. The comments name the rule\ncodes; each is a real defect class, not cosmetics.\n\n```toml\n[tool.ruff]\ntarget-version = \"py312\"\nline-length = 100\n\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nselect = [\"E\", \"F\", \"I\", \"B\", \"UP\", \"ASYNC\", \"S\", \"RUF\"]\nignore = [\"E501\"]                          # the formatter owns line length\n\n[tool.ruff.lint.per-file-ignores]\n\"tests/**\" = [\"S101\"]                      # assert is the point of a test\n```\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json, time                 # F401 json unused; I001 import block unsorted (E401 too)\nfrom typing import List                    # UP035 deprecated typing alias import\nimport requests\n\nasync def log_done(n: int) -> None: ...\n\nasync def fetch_all(urls: List[str], seen: set = set()) -> list:   # UP006 List; B006 mutable default\n    results = []\n    for url in urls:\n        if url in seen:\n            continue\n        time.sleep(0.1)                    # ASYNC251 blocks the event loop for every request\n        r = requests.get(url, timeout=10)  # ASYNC210 blocking HTTP call inside async def\n        results.append(r.json())\n    asyncio.create_task(log_done(len(results)))   # RUF006 task reference not kept\n    return results\n\ndef docs_for(cur, tenant: str):\n    try:\n        cur.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM docs WHERE tenant = '{tenant}'\")   # S608 SQL from string\n    except Exception as e:                 # F841 e unused\n        raise RuntimeError(\"query failed\")   # B904 re-raise without `from e`\n```\n\n- **What `--fix` handles:** `ruff check --fix` resolves the import, `List` and some other findings safely.\n- **What needs a human:** the async, SQL and default-argument findings, because the fix is a design\n  change (an async client, a parameterised query, a `None` default).\n\n### Worked run: what Ruff actually reported\nRunning `ruff check svc.py` with the config above (Ruff 0.17.1) gives 12 findings. `ruff check --fix`\nthen fixes 6 and leaves 6, and reports 1 more fix available only with `--unsafe-fixes`:\n\n| Group | Codes | Who fixes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Imports and syntax | `E401`, `I001`, `F401`, `UP006` | `--fix`, safely |\n| Unused binding | `F841` | `--fix` drops `as e` |\n| Deprecated import | `UP035` | gone after `--fix`, because the now-unused `List` import is removed |\n| Mutable default | `B006` | unsafe fix offered (a `None` default plus `if seen is None`); review it |\n| Likely bugs | `B904`, `RUF006` | human: `raise ... from e`, keep a task reference |\n| Event-loop blockers | `ASYNC251`, `ASYNC210` | human: `await asyncio.sleep`, an async HTTP client |\n| Security | `S608` | human: parameterised query |\n\nHalf of the findings are design decisions, which is why lint findings belong in review, not in a silent\nautofix job.\n\n### Hook and CI order\nIn a pre-commit hook, run `ruff check --fix` before `ruff format`, because a lint fix (removing an\nimport, rewriting a comprehension) can leave code the formatter would lay out differently. In CI, run\n`ruff check` and `ruff format --check`; neither writes files, so CI fails on findings instead of quietly\nfixing them in a throwaway checkout.\n\n```yaml\n# .pre-commit-config.yaml\nrepos:\n  - repo: https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff-pre-commit\n    rev: v0.17.1               # same version as CI and the editor\n    hooks:\n      - id: ruff-check\n        args: [--fix]          # lint fixes first\n      - id: ruff-format        # then layout\n```","patterns":["**One pinned version everywhere.** Pin Ruff in the dev dependencies, use the same `rev` in the pre-commit hook, and run that version in CI, so findings and fixes do not flip between machines. Monitor CI-only findings, which signal version drift.","**Shared baseline rule selection.** Keep one `[tool.ruff.lint]` block (for example `E`, `F`, `I`, `B`, `UP`, `ASYNC`, `S`, `RUF`) across services, and use `extend-select` for per-repo additions so the baseline is not silently replaced. Review it when Ruff graduates preview rules to stable.","**Baseline a legacy repo, then burn it down.** Enable the families you want, run `ruff check --add-noqa` once to suppress existing findings with per-code comments, and keep `RUF100` on so comments are removed as code is fixed. Track the count of `noqa` comments over time.","**CI annotations, no writes.** Run `ruff check --output-format github` and `ruff format --check` in the pipeline, so findings appear inline on the pull request and nothing is fixed in a throwaway checkout.","**Stricter rules for tool code.** Agent tools run on model-chosen input, so give the tools package the full `S`, `ASYNC` and `BLE` families with a nested `ruff.toml` in that directory that sets `extend = \"../pyproject.toml\"` and uses `extend-select`, because a nested config replaces the root one unless it extends it. Require review of any `noqa` there. Monitor `noqa` comments in the tools package separately from the rest of the repo.","**Triage with statistics.** Before enabling a new family, run `ruff check --select ASYNC --statistics` to see counts per rule, fix the defect classes first (`ASYNC`, `B006`, `RUF006`, `S608`), then enable the family as an error."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent tools are where model-chosen input meets your process: a shell\n  tool with `shell=True`, a calculator built on `eval`, a sync HTTP call inside an async tool, a memory\n  write fired as an unreferenced task. Ruff flags each by rule code before the model ever reaches it.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** One `[tool.ruff.lint]` block in `pyproject.toml`, the editor, a\n  pre-commit hook and CI, all on one pinned version, next to mypy and pytest.\n- **What you control.** The rule families: `ASYNC` for async agent services, `S` for tool code that runs\n  processes, SQL or `eval`, `B` and `RUF` for likely bugs. Also which findings are fixed automatically and\n  which need a design change.\n- **What you can skip.** Style debates and the full rule catalogue; `ruff rule <code>` explains any rule\n  when it appears.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Async agent services.** `ASYNC` rules flag a blocking `time.sleep` or `requests.get` inside an async\n  tool before it stalls every concurrent session.\n- **Tools that run code or queries.** `S` rules flag `shell=True`, `eval` and SQL built with f-strings in\n  tools that receive model-chosen input.\n- **Fire-and-forget tasks.** `RUF` rules flag an `asyncio.create_task` result that is not stored, a\n  memory write that could vanish mid-flight.\n- **One fast gate.** A single pinned Ruff in the editor, pre-commit and CI replaces several linters, so\n  agent repositories get checked on every save.","mini_project":"**Lint an agent's tool package and fix what Ruff finds (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find and fix the agent-specific defects Ruff catches in a small set of tools.\n- **Steps:** write four tools with planted defects (a shell tool with `shell=True`, a calculator using\n  `eval`, an async tool calling `requests.get`, an unstored `asyncio.create_task`); enable `ASYNC`, `S`,\n  `B` and `RUF` in `[tool.ruff.lint]`; run `ruff check`; use `ruff rule <code>` to read each finding;\n  fix each by design, not by suppression.\n- **Done when:** `ruff check` passes with no inline suppressions on the planted defects, and the\n  pre-commit hook and CI run the same pinned version."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Python file\"] --> CH{\"Unchanged since last run, same settings?\"}\n    CH -->|\"yes\"| SKIP[\"Skip, reuse cached result\"]\n    CH -->|\"no\"| P[\"One parse: AST plus tokens\"]\n    P --> SM[\"Per-file semantic model: scopes, bindings, imports\"]\n    CFG[\"pyproject.toml: select, extend-select, ignore, preview\"] --> R\n    SM --> R[\"Run every enabled rule, files in parallel\"]\n    R --> FD[\"Findings with codes, e.g. B006, ASYNC251, S608\"]\n    FD -->|\"safe fix\"| FX[\"Applied by ruff check --fix\"]\n    FD -->|\"unsafe fix\"| UF[\"Only with --unsafe-fixes, reviewed\"]\n    FD -->|\"design change needed\"| H[\"Human: async client, parameterised query, None default\"]\n    FX --> FMT[\"ruff format lays out the result\"]","caption":"Follow one `ruff check` run: a single parse per file feeds every enabled rule, and each finding ends in a\nsafe fix, an opt-in unsafe fix, or a human decision."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is Ruff so much faster than the flake8-based toolchain it replaces?","a":"- **One parse per file:** every enabled rule runs over a single AST and token stream, where the old chain\n  ran each Python tool as its own process and re-parsed every file once per tool.\n- **Parallel and cached:** files are linted in parallel, and unchanged files under the same settings are\n  skipped.\n- **Native Rust code** adds the rest; quote your own repository's measurement, not a published multiple."},{"q":"Which Ruff rules would you enable for an async LLM service, and why those?","a":"- **`ASYNC`:** flags blocking sleeps and HTTP calls inside `async def`, which stall every concurrent\n  request; the default set does not include it.\n- **`B` and `RUF`:** mutable defaults (`B006`), lost exception context (`B904`) and fire-and-forget tasks\n  that can be garbage-collected (`RUF006`).\n- **`S`:** SQL built from strings (`S608`) and other bandit checks, plus the defaults `E` and `F`, `I` for\n  imports and `UP` for current syntax."},{"q":"What would Ruff flag in an agent's tool implementations, and what would it miss?","a":"- **It flags dangerous calls by name:** `S602` for a shell tool with `shell=True`, `S307` for `eval` in a\n  calculator tool, `ASYNC210` and `ASYNC221` for blocking calls in async tools, `RUF006` for\n  unreferenced background tasks.\n- **It misses anything indirect:** a dangerous call behind your own helper in another module, or a tool\n  that is safe in code but has too broad a permission.\n- **So pair it with a sandbox and an allowlist**, and treat its findings in tool code as review blockers."},{"q":"You turn on ASYNC and B in a legacy repo and get 900 findings. How do you roll it out?","a":"- **Triage first** with `ruff check --statistics`, and fix the defect classes that matter in production\n  (blocking calls, dangling tasks, SQL injection) before cosmetic ones.\n- **Baseline the rest** with `ruff check --add-noqa`, which writes per-code `noqa` comments, so CI can\n  gate new code immediately.\n- **Burn down** with `RUF100` enabled, so each suppression disappears once its line is fixed, and track the\n  `noqa` count."},{"q":"What can Ruff not catch, and what do you pair it with?","a":"- **Types:** it does not know a value may be `None`, so `.strip()` on `str | None` needs mypy or pyright.\n- **Cross-module behaviour:** a blocking call hidden behind your own helper in another module, or a\n  CPU-heavy function in a coroutine, is invisible because rules see one file at a time.\n- **Dependencies:** vulnerable packages need a dependency auditor; runtime blocking is confirmed with asyncio\n  debug mode or profiling."},{"q":"When would you not apply Ruff's fixes automatically?","a":"- **Unsafe fixes:** they may change behaviour, such as removing an import with side effects, so they stay\n  behind `--unsafe-fixes` and get reviewed.\n- **In CI:** run `ruff check` and `ruff format --check`, which never write files, so findings fail the build\n  instead of being fixed in a throwaway checkout.\n- **Design findings:** async, SQL and default-argument findings have no autofix because the fix is a design\n  change."}],"tradeoffs":["**One fast tool vs re-implementations.** It replaces a fragmented chain and runs on every save, but you depend on Ruff's re-implementations; a niche flake8 plugin or pylint check it lacks still needs the original tool.","**Broad rule families vs legacy noise.** Selecting broad families catches more real bugs, but floods legacy code with findings; start with `F`, `B`, `ASYNC`, `S` and grow the selection once the backlog is cleared.","**Autofix in pre-commit vs behaviour changes.** Autofix removes mechanical review churn, but unsafe fixes can change behaviour; keep unsafe fixes manual and reviewed, which flips only for rules you have audited.","**Formatter switch vs one big diff.** Ruff's formatter matches Black closely enough to switch in place, but small output differences mean one large reformatting commit; do it once, then add that commit to the blame-ignore list."],"pitfalls":["**Running only the default rules and assuming the code is checked.** Symptom: blocking calls in async handlers and mutable defaults shipping, because `ASYNC` and `B` were never enabled.","**Blanket `# noqa` without a code.** Symptom: a line that silently suppresses every future finding, including the real bug someone adds to it later.","**Different Ruff versions locally, in pre-commit and in CI.** Symptom: findings that appear only in CI or fixes that flip back and forth between machines. Fix: pin one version everywhere.","**Treating a clean Ruff run as tool safety.** Symptom: a tool that passes lint because it calls a wrapper around `subprocess` defined in another module, which Ruff cannot follow. Fix: sandbox and allowlist tools regardless, and keep dangerous calls inside one reviewed module.","**Treating a clean Ruff run as type safety.** Symptom: `None` attribute errors in production in code Ruff passed, because Ruff does not reason about types.","**A line-length lint rule and the formatter with different limits.** Symptom: findings the formatter can never fix, and contributors adding `noqa` comments to get past them."],"when":"- **Use in every Python project, from the first commit:** it is cheap enough to run in the editor, in a\n  pre-commit hook and in CI with the same pinned version.\n- **Choose rule families deliberately:** `F` and `E` for correctness, `B` and `RUF` for likely bugs,\n  `ASYNC` for any service built on asyncio, `S` for code that touches SQL, subprocesses or secrets, `UP`\n  to keep syntax current with your minimum Python.\n- **Run it next to mypy, not instead of it**, and next to dependency auditing, which it does not do.\n- **Interviews:** a good answer explains which rules catch production defects in async AI services, not\n  just that Ruff is fast.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Fixes findings as they appear in the editor and knows why `ASYNC`, `B006` and `RUF006` matter in an event-loop service, rather than silencing them.","architect":"Owns the shared rule selection and pinned version across repositories, so every service enforces the same correctness and security baseline in CI.","fde":"Runs Ruff on a customer's existing Python integration code on day one to surface blocking calls, injection risks and swallowed errors before building on top of it."},"check":{"q":"An async FastAPI service for LLM calls has latency spikes under load, yet CPU is low. The team\nalready runs Ruff with its default rules. How could Ruff have helped, and what are its limits here?","a":"Low CPU with spikes under concurrency suggests the event loop is being blocked: a `time.sleep`,\na sync HTTP client or a sync database driver called inside `async def`. The default rule set does not\ninclude the flake8-async family, so enabling `ASYNC` would flag blocking sleeps and HTTP calls inside\ncoroutines, and `RUF006` would flag fire-and-forget tasks. The limits: Ruff only recognises known\nblocking calls by name; a sync call hidden behind your own helper, or a CPU-heavy function such as\ntokenising a huge document, is invisible to it. You confirm with asyncio debug mode or profiling."},"quiz":[{"q":"What mainly explains Ruff's speed advantage over the older multi-tool lint chain?","options":["It skips checking files larger than a fixed size","It parses each file once and runs all rules on that parse, in parallel, with caching","It only runs rules on lines changed in the last commit","It uses type inference to prune rules that cannot apply"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The old chain re-parsed every file once per tool. Ruff shares one parse across all rules, runs files in parallel and caches unchanged files; it does not do type inference."},{"q":"Which issue is Ruff not designed to catch?","options":["A blocking time.sleep call inside an async function","A mutable list used as a default argument","Calling .strip() on a value whose declared type is str | None","An SQL query assembled with an f-string"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Optional-type errors need a type checker such as mypy or pyright. The other three are covered by Ruff's ASYNC, bugbear and bandit-derived rule families when those are enabled."},{"q":"Why does Ruff separate safe fixes from unsafe fixes?","options":["Unsafe fixes are slower to compute","Safe fixes only apply to formatting, never to lint findings","Unsafe fixes may change program behaviour, so they are not applied by default","Unsafe fixes require an internet connection to download rule data"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A fix such as removing an import with side effects can alter runtime behaviour. Keeping such fixes opt-in lets `--fix` run automatically in hooks without silently changing semantics."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/python-dotenv","sources":["Official docs: python-dotenv","Wiggins, 2011 — The Twelve-Factor App"],"why":"- **Config belongs in the environment.** An agent service needs values that differ per environment and\n  must never be committed: the OpenAI or Anthropic API key, a vector-database URL, a Langfuse key, the\n  model you are trialling. Twelve-Factor says read them from env vars, because Docker, Kubernetes, a PaaS\n  and CI all inject them with no code changes.\n- **The friction is the laptop.** Exporting eight variables in every new terminal is tedious, and an\n  agent with five tools easily needs that many.\n- **The workarounds are worse.** Hardcoding the key \"just for now\" ends in a leaked key in git history;\n  `~/.zshrc` leaks one project's key into every other project and every subprocess your agent spawns; a\n  `config.py` with real values gets committed by accident.\n- **What people want.** A per-project file that behaves like environment variables, is easy to edit and\n  is ignored by git.\n- **python-dotenv is that bridge.** The code path stays `os.environ[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]` everywhere; only\n  the *source* changes. Locally a file fills it in; in production the platform does and the library steps\n  aside.","how":"### Finding the file\n1. `load_dotenv()` with no arguments calls `find_dotenv()`.\n2. The search starts in the calling module's directory (or the current directory in an interactive\n   session) and walks *up* parent directories to the first `.env`. Upward, because agent scripts often\n   run from `scripts/`, `evals/` or `notebooks/` while the file sits at the project root.\n3. Alternatives: pass an explicit path, or `find_dotenv(usecwd=True)` to search from the working\n   directory.\n4. No file found: `load_dotenv()` returns `False` rather than raising, because \"no file\" is the normal\n   production case.\n\nThe walk is a convenience with two sharp corners and one fix:\n- **Monorepos.** It stops at the *first* `.env` going up, so a parent directory's file can be picked up by\n  a child project, for example the agent picking up the web app's keys.\n- **Installed packages.** When the calling code lives under `site-packages`, the walk starts there and\n  never reaches your project.\n- **Fix for both.** Pass an explicit path in the entry point.\n\n### Parsing\n| Syntax | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| `KEY=value` | One variable per line |\n| `export ` prefix | Allowed, so the same file can be `source`d by a shell |\n| `#` | Starts a comment |\n| Single quotes | Literal value |\n| Double quotes | Escapes such as `\\n`; can span lines, which is how people store PEM keys |\n| `${OTHER}` | Expands from earlier lines or the existing environment (interpolation is on by default for `load_dotenv`) |\n| `${VAR:-default}` | Fallback when neither the environment nor the file defines `VAR` |\n\nSo `DB_URL=postgres://${DB_USER}@localhost/app` works, and so does a memory-store URL built from parts.\n\n### The precedence rule\n- **Default is `load_dotenv(override=False)`.** A variable already in the process environment wins over\n  the file, because the file is meant to be the laptop's fallback.\n- **Why that matters.** In CI or production the platform-injected value is authoritative, and a stray\n  `.env` baked into an agent image must not silently replace it.\n- **`override=True`** only when you deliberately want the file to win.\n- **First source to set a key wins.** With several files, load the most specific first\n  (`load_dotenv(\".env.local\")` then `load_dotenv(\".env\")`), the opposite of the \"later overrides\n  earlier\" order most config systems use. Interpolation follows the same precedence.\n\n### Lifecycle\n- **A one-time copy, not a live binding.** Editing `.env` changes nothing in a running agent server;\n  restart it.\n- **Early captures keep stale values.** Anything that read a value before `load_dotenv()` ran (a\n  module-level `client = OpenAI()`, a `settings` object built at import, a tracing client configured at\n  import) keeps what it saw, which is why the load belongs on the first lines of the entry point.\n\n### Two ways to use the values\n| Call | Effect | Best for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `load_dotenv()` | Mutates `os.environ` | Libraries that read env vars themselves (the OpenAI SDK reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` on its own) and subprocesses you spawn |\n| `dotenv_values(\".env\")` | Returns a plain dict, touches nothing | Tests and merging files, e.g. `{**dotenv_values(\".env.shared\"), **dotenv_values(\".env.local\")}`, because global mutable state is avoided |\n| `dotenv run -- python app.py` | CLI (from the `cli` extra) loads the file then execs the command | Tools you cannot edit, such as a third-party MCP server you launch |\n\n### What it does not do\n- **Every value is a string.** `DEBUG=false` gives the non-empty string `\"false\"`, which is truthy, and\n  `MAX_STEPS=8` is `\"8\"` until you convert it.\n- **No schema, no required-field check, no secret masking.** That is the job of a settings layer such as\n  pydantic-settings, which can itself read `.env` files.","example":"### Agent entry point that loads, then fails fast\nLoad local config, check every key the agent's tools need, then build clients. Missing keys fail at\nstartup, not on step 7 of a run when the search tool is first called.\n\n```python\n# .env (gitignored)                     .env.example (committed, no real values)\n#   OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-local-dev           OPENAI_API_KEY=\n#   MODEL=gpt-4o-mini                     MODEL=gpt-4o-mini\n#   MAX_TOKENS=512                        MAX_TOKENS=512\n#   DEBUG=false                           DEBUG=false\n#   SEARCH_API_KEY=local-search           SEARCH_API_KEY=\n#   MAX_STEPS=8                           MAX_STEPS=8\nimport os\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv, dotenv_values\n\nfound = load_dotenv()          # walks up to find .env; existing env vars win\nprint(\"loaded .env:\", found)   # False in production, where the platform sets vars\n\nREQUIRED = [\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"MODEL\", \"SEARCH_API_KEY\"]   # model + every tool's key\nmissing = [k for k in REQUIRED if not os.environ.get(k)]\nif missing:\n    raise SystemExit(f\"missing config: {missing}; copy .env.example to .env\")\n\nmodel = os.environ[\"MODEL\"]\nmax_tokens = int(os.environ.get(\"MAX_TOKENS\", \"256\"))      # strings only: convert yourself\nmax_steps = int(os.environ.get(\"MAX_STEPS\", \"8\"))          # loop budget for the agent\ndebug = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", \"false\").lower() in {\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\"}\nprint(model, max_tokens, max_steps, debug)  # bool(\"false\") would have been True\n\nfrom openai import OpenAI      # import clients AFTER load_dotenv\nclient = OpenAI()              # reads OPENAI_API_KEY from os.environ itself\n\n# Without touching os.environ (good in tests):\ncfg = dotenv_values(\".env.example\")\nassert set(REQUIRED) <= cfg.keys(), \"example file is out of date\"\n```\n\n- Running `MODEL=claude-test python app.py` prints `claude-test`, because the shell's variable was set\n  before `load_dotenv()` ran and `override` defaults to `False`.\n\n### Worked scenario: who wins with two files and a shell export\nThe shell still has an old `export MODEL=gpt-4o` from yesterday's eval run. The agent's entry point runs\n`load_dotenv(\".env.local\")` then `load_dotenv(\".env\")`, both with the default `override=False`.\n\n| Key | Shell | `.env.local` | `.env` | Final value | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `MODEL` | `gpt-4o` | `gpt-4o-mini` | `gpt-4o-mini` | `gpt-4o` | Already set; files never override |\n| `OPENAI_API_KEY` | unset | `sk-local-dev` | `sk-shared` | `sk-local-dev` | First file to set it wins |\n| `MAX_TOKENS` | unset | unset | `512` | `512` | Only `.env` defines it |\n\nThe \"I changed the model in .env and the agent still behaves the same\" bug is the first row; `unset\nMODEL` or a new terminal fixes it. In an eval comparison it is worse: both \"variants\" silently ran the\nsame model, so the scores match for the wrong reason.\n\n### Worked scenario: an agent's tool subprocess sees too much\nAn agent runs a code-execution tool as a subprocess. With `load_dotenv()`, `os.environ` holds every key.\n\n| Approach | What the sandboxed tool process can read |\n|---|---|\n| Inherit the parent environment | The model key, the search key, the tracing key: all of them |\n| Pass `env={\"PATH\": ...}` explicitly to the subprocess | Only what you listed |\n\nThe fix is in how you spawn the tool, not in python-dotenv: give tool processes an explicit, minimal\nenvironment.","patterns":["**Fail fast on every tool's key.** At startup, check a `REQUIRED` list covering the model and each enabled tool against `os.environ` and exit with the missing names, as in the first example. Monitor crash-loops on deploy, which now name the key instead of surfacing as a `None` API key mid-run.","**Keep `.env.example` honest in CI.** Add a test that loads `dotenv_values(\".env.example\")` and asserts every key the code requires is present. Watch for that test failing when someone adds a tool without documenting its key.","**Exclude the file from git and images.** List `.env` in both `.gitignore` and `.dockerignore`, and run a secret scanner in pre-commit and CI. Monitor scanner hits and provider leaked-key alerts; any hit means rotate, because history keeps the value.","**Load once, from an explicit path.** In `main`, the eval runner or `conftest.py`, call `load_dotenv()` with the path next to the entry point and log its return value. Never call it in library code or inside a tool, which should read only the environment it is given.","**Treat production as \"no file\".** Run staging with no `.env` present and variables injected by the platform or a secrets manager. The agent should behave identically; a `True` return from `load_dotenv()` in production logs is a red flag.","**Use separate dev keys with low limits.** Put a low-spend, dev-only provider key in `.env`, so a runaway local agent loop or a leaked laptop file costs little. Monitor spend per key."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent touches more credentials than a chat app: the LLM provider, a\n  search or browser tool, a vector database, a tracing service such as LangSmith or Langfuse. Each SDK\n  reads its own env var, so one `.env` loaded early wires all of them locally.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The OpenAI and Anthropic SDKs, LangChain chat model classes and\n  tracing clients read their keys from the environment on their own; `load_dotenv()` on the first lines of\n  the agent's entry point, notebook or `conftest.py` is how those keys arrive in development.\n- **What you control.** Which file loads, from which path, and the precedence: an already-set variable\n  wins under the default `override=False`, so the platform's injected secret beats any stray file.\n- **What you can skip.** Parser edge cases beyond quoting and `${VAR}` expansion. Know the override\n  default, the one-time copy, and that every value is a string.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local agent development.** One `.env` holds the LLM provider key, a search tool key and a tracing key,\n  so every SDK the agent touches is wired with a single `load_dotenv()` call.\n- **Agent test suites.** `conftest.py` loads a test `.env` pointing at a sandbox vector database and a\n  cheap model, so tests never hit production resources.\n- **Notebooks for prompt and tool experiments.** A notebook calls `load_dotenv()` at the top and reuses\n  the same keys as the agent service without pasting them into cells.\n- **Onboarding a new teammate.** A committed `.env.example` lists every variable the agent needs, so a\n  fresh clone runs after copying it and filling in values.","mini_project":"**Wire a two-tool agent's secrets with dotenv (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** run a small tool-calling agent locally with every key coming from a `.env` file, and prove\n  that a platform-injected variable wins over the file.\n- **Steps:** write a `.env.example` listing the model key, a search tool key and a model name; add\n  `.env` to `.gitignore`; call `load_dotenv()` first in the entry point; add a startup check that fails\n  fast with the names of any missing variables; export one variable in the shell and confirm it beats\n  the file under the default `override=False`.\n- **Done when:** a fresh clone runs after copying `.env.example`, a missing key fails at startup with a\n  clear message instead of mid-run, and `git status` never shows `.env`."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Agent entry point calls load_dotenv()\"] --> B[\"find_dotenv walks up from caller\"]\n    B -->|\"no .env found\"| N[\"Return False, env untouched\"]\n    B -->|\"first .env found\"| P[\"Parse lines, expand references\"]\n    P --> K{\"Key already set?\"}\n    K -->|\"yes, override=False\"| S[\"Keep existing value\"]\n    K -->|\"no, or override=True\"| W[\"Write file value\"]\n    S --> E[(\"os.environ, strings only\")]\n    W --> E\n    N --> E\n    E --> C[\"LLM SDK, tool clients, tracing\"]","caption":"Follow one `load_dotenv()` call at agent startup: the upward file search, then the per-key precedence\ncheck that lets an already-set variable win unless `override=True`."},"interview":[{"q":"What does python-dotenv actually do, and why is it not a secrets manager?","a":"- **Definition:** it parses `KEY=value` lines from a `.env` file and copies them into `os.environ` once,\n  so code reads config the same way locally and in production.\n- **Not a secrets manager:** the file is plaintext on disk, with no rotation, audit trail, access control\n  or masking. Production values should come from the platform or a secrets store.\n- **Why it still helps:** the code path stays `os.environ[...]` everywhere; only the source of the values\n  changes between a laptop and a cluster."},{"q":"A developer changed `MODEL` in `.env`, but the agent still uses the old model. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Stale shell export:** an earlier `export MODEL=...` is already in the environment, and under the\n  default `override=False` the file never replaces it. Check with `echo $MODEL`.\n- **Process not restarted:** loading is a one-time copy, so a running agent server never sees file edits.\n- **Captured too early or wrong file:** a module-level client may have read the value before\n  `load_dotenv()` ran, or the upward search found another project's `.env`. Log the return value and\n  pass an explicit path."},{"q":"Your agent has a code-execution tool that runs as a subprocess. How do you keep it from seeing your API keys?","a":"- **Know the default:** `load_dotenv()` writes keys into `os.environ`, and a child process inherits the\n  whole environment unless you pass one explicitly.\n- **Pass a minimal environment:** spawn the tool with an explicit env containing only what it needs, or\n  load values with `dotenv_values` into a dict that never touches the process environment.\n- **Defence in depth:** run the tool in a sandbox with no network access to your providers, and keep\n  production keys out of `.env` entirely."},{"q":"Why is `override=False` the default, and when would you set `override=True`?","a":"- **The environment is authoritative:** in CI and production the platform injects values, and a stray\n  `.env` baked into an image must not silently replace them.\n- **The file is a laptop fallback:** it fills only gaps, which is why the first source to set a key wins.\n- **`override=True` is a deliberate choice:** use it only where you want the file to beat the shell, for\n  example a local script, and never in code that also runs in production."},{"q":"How do you keep a `.env` file out of git history and container images?","a":"- **Ignore it twice:** list `.env` in `.gitignore` and `.dockerignore`, because `COPY . .` would otherwise\n  bake real keys into an image layer anyone who pulls it can read.\n- **Scan continuously:** run a secret scanner in pre-commit and CI, and commit only a `.env.example` with\n  key names and no real values.\n- **Assume a leak is permanent:** if a key was ever committed, rotate it, because removing the file does\n  not remove it from history."},{"q":"When would you replace python-dotenv with pydantic-settings in an agent service?","a":"- **When settings need types:** python-dotenv yields only strings, so `\"false\"` is truthy and every call\n  site converts step budgets, timeouts and toggles by hand.\n- **When you need validation:** pydantic-settings checks required fields and constraints at startup,\n  supports nested models (one per tool) and masks secrets with `SecretStr`.\n- **Migration cost is low:** it reads the same `.env` file, so the switch is a new `Settings` class, not a\n  new config workflow."}],"tradeoffs":["**One code path vs a second source of truth.** `os.environ` works in every environment, but the file is an implicit source that can drift from what production injects; a committed `.env.example` keeps the drift visible.","**Zero friction vs hand-rolled typing.** Trivial to adopt with no other dependencies, but you write type conversion and required checks yourself. Past a few settings (model, step budget, timeouts, tool toggles), pydantic-settings pays for itself.","**Visibility vs leakage.** Mutating `os.environ` lets third-party SDKs and subprocesses see values, which is convenient until a tool subprocess or crash dump exposes a key it never needed; `dotenv_values` avoids that.","**Plaintext file vs secrets manager.** Fine for a dev key with low limits; for production secrets a secrets manager with rotation and audit wins regardless of team size."],"pitfalls":["**Committing `.env` because `.gitignore` came later.** Symptom: a provider's leaked-key alert or a secret scanner hit. Fix: rotate the key (mandatory, because git history keeps it) and add the ignore.","**Expecting the file to beat a stale export.** Symptom: \"I changed the key in .env and nothing changed\", because an old `export` takes precedence under `override=False`. Fix: unset it or open a new shell.","**Loading after an import that already read `os.environ`.** Symptom: a `None` key even though the file is correct, or traces missing because the tracing client configured itself at import. Fix: load first thing in the entry point.","**Treating `\"false\"` or `\"0\"` as falsy.** Symptom: debug mode, verbose tracing or a tool toggle stuck on. Fix: convert explicitly.","**Copying `.env` into a Docker image with `COPY . .`.** Symptom: real keys visible in a pulled image layer. Fix: `.dockerignore`.","**Relying on the upward search in a nested repo or installed package.** Symptom: values from the wrong project's `.env`, or `load_dotenv()` returning `False` where the file plainly exists. Fix: log the return value and pass an explicit path."],"when":"- **Use when:** local agent development, notebooks, eval scripts, demos and small scripts where the\n  alternative is exporting variables by hand. Call it once at the top of the entry point; commit a\n  `.env.example` listing every key.\n- **Avoid in library code and tools:** they should only read the environment they are given.\n- **Move on when:** settings need types, validation, nesting or per-environment profiles; switch to\n  pydantic-settings, which reads the same file.\n- **Never for production secret delivery:** production gets variables from the platform or a secrets\n  manager, and your code should work identically when no `.env` exists.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is knowing the override default and why.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Loads `.env` once in `main` or `conftest.py`, keeps `.env.example` current, and converts string values explicitly instead of trusting truthiness.","architect":"Sets the rule that `.env` is a local fallback only, production secrets come from a managed store, and environment precedence is documented.","fde":"In a customer VPC, ships a `.env.example` and runbook, and checks the customer's platform injects variables rather than mounting a plaintext file."},"check":{"q":"Your service reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` via python-dotenv. In staging it uses the wrong key even\nthough the Kubernetes secret is correct. What are the likely causes and how do you fix it?","a":"First check whether a `.env` file got into the image (no `.dockerignore`) and whether the code\ncalls `load_dotenv(override=True)`; together those make the baked file beat the injected secret. If\n`override` is the default `False`, the injected variable should win, so the next suspect is that the pod\ndoes not actually receive it (wrong secret key name or namespace) and the file supplies a fallback. Fix\nby excluding `.env` from images, keeping the default precedence, and failing fast at startup when a\nrequired variable is absent, so no silent fallback exists."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `load_dotenv()` do by default when `OPENAI_API_KEY` is already set in the shell?","options":["Replaces it with the value from `.env`","Leaves the existing value in place and only fills variables that are not already set","Raises an error about a conflicting definition","Keeps both values and joins them with a separator"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The default is `override=False`, so the process environment wins. That lets production inject real values while `.env` only fills gaps on a laptop; `override=True` reverses the precedence."},{"q":"A `.env` has `ENABLE_CACHE=false`, and the code does `if os.environ[\"ENABLE_CACHE\"]:`. What happens?","options":["The branch is skipped because python-dotenv parses booleans","A TypeError is raised because the value is not a bool","The branch runs, because the value is the non-empty string \"false\"","The variable is ignored because lowercase booleans are invalid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"python-dotenv only produces strings, and any non-empty string is truthy in Python. Convert explicitly or use a typed settings layer such as pydantic-settings that parses \"false\" into False."},{"q":"When is `dotenv_values()` a better choice than `load_dotenv()`?","options":["When you need third-party SDKs to pick up the key automatically","When you want the values as a dict without mutating global process state, such as in tests","When the file uses `export` prefixes that `load_dotenv` cannot parse","When running in production without a `.env` file present"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`dotenv_values` returns a dict and leaves `os.environ` untouched, which avoids leakage between tests and makes merging files explicit. SDKs that read the environment themselves need `load_dotenv` instead."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pydantic-settings","sources":["Official docs: pydantic-settings","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"- **Everything is a string.** With `os.environ.get` scattered through the code, `TIMEOUT=\"30\"` must be\n  converted at each call site, and `\"false\"` is truthy, so a disabled tool stays enabled.\n- **Nothing is checked up front.** A missing `VECTOR_DB_URL` or a typo in `RERANK_TOP_K` surfaces only\n  when the first agent run reaches the retrieval step, possibly hours after deploy.\n- **No single list of what the service needs.** A new environment is configured by trial and error.\n- **A hand-written `config.py` hits a wall.** It works until thirty fields, nested groups (retrieval,\n  model, tracing, one per tool), lists of allowed models and secrets that must stay out of logs and\n  traces; then you are reimplementing a validation library.\n- **Pydantic already is one.** pydantic-settings reuses its coercion, constraints and error messages,\n  aimed at the process environment. It was split out with Pydantic v2, so it is a separate install\n  (`pip install pydantic-settings`).","how":"### Declaring settings\nSubclass `BaseSettings`, annotate fields as in any Pydantic model, and set behaviour with\n`model_config = SettingsConfigDict(...)`:\n\n| Option | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `env_prefix` | `APP_MODEL` fills `model`, avoiding collisions with other software's variables |\n| `env_file` | One path or a tuple, later files winning |\n| `case_sensitive` | Off by default |\n| `env_nested_delimiter` | Lets one env var set one field of a nested model, such as one tool's timeout |\n| `extra` | What to do with unknown keys from dotenv files |\n\n### Where values come from, in priority order\nWhen you instantiate `Settings()`, each field is resolved from the highest-priority source that has it:\n\n1. Keyword arguments passed to the constructor (used by tests and eval variants).\n2. Environment variables.\n3. The dotenv file(s).\n4. Files in `secrets_dir`, one file per field, which is how Docker and Kubernetes secrets are mounted.\n5. Field defaults.\n\n- **Why this order.** The platform's environment beats a local file, matching python-dotenv's default,\n  so a stale `.env` cannot override production.\n- **Customisable.** Reorder or add sources (a vault, a YAML file) by overriding the\n  `settings_customise_sources` classmethod.\n\n### The merge mechanism\n1. Each source is a callable returning a dict of raw values for the fields it knows about.\n2. pydantic-settings calls them and deep-merges the dicts, a higher-priority source overwriting a lower\n   one key by key.\n3. One ordinary Pydantic validation runs over the merged dict.\n\n- **Consequence.** Everything Pydantic can do (coercion, validators, nested models, aliases) works\n  unchanged for config, and a custom source only has to produce a dict.\n- **Asymmetry with python-dotenv.** Within `env_file=(\".env\", \".env.local\")` the *later* file wins,\n  whereas repeated `load_dotenv` calls keep the first value.\n- **Names fixed by someone else.** For the OpenAI SDK's `OPENAI_API_KEY` or a platform's `DATABASE_URL`,\n  use `validation_alias=\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"` (or `AliasChoices` for several accepted names) instead of\n  bending your prefix convention.\n\n### Parsing beyond strings\n| Field type | Env value | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `float` | `\"30\"` | `30.0` |\n| `bool` | `\"false\"` | `False` |\n| `list`, `dict`, nested model | JSON string, e.g. `APP_ALLOWED_MODELS='[\"a\",\"b\"]'` | Parsed list |\n| Nested field with `env_nested_delimiter=\"__\"` | `APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K=20` | Sets one field, rest keep defaults |\n\n- **Constraints apply.** `Field(gt=0)` and `Literal[\"dev\",\"prod\"]` are enforced exactly as for API input,\n  so `max_steps=0` or an unknown model name never reaches the loop.\n- **All errors at once.** A failure raises a single `ValidationError` listing every bad field, because one\n  deploy should report all problems rather than one per restart.\n\n### Secrets\n- **Type sensitive fields as `SecretStr`.** The value is masked in `repr`, `str` and default\n  serialization, so `print(settings)`, a crash log or a config dump attached to a trace shows\n  `**********`.\n- **Unwrap at the point of use** with `.get_secret_value()`, when constructing the model or tool client.\n- **No encryption.** It prevents the most common leak, which is logging a config object.\n\n### Where it lives at runtime\n- **Construct once and share:** a cached `get_settings()` function, or an object built in the FastAPI\n  lifespan and injected as a dependency into the agent endpoint and tool factories.\n- **Payoff:** one validation at startup and an easy override in tests (construct a `Settings(...)` with\n  explicit kwargs and `_env_file=None`).","example":"### Settings for a tool-calling RAG agent\nOne class holds the model, the loop budget, retrieval knobs and per-tool config. Every agent component\nreads from it.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, SecretStr\nfrom pydantic_settings import BaseSettings, SettingsConfigDict\n\nclass Retrieval(BaseModel):\n    top_k: int = Field(8, ge=1, le=100)\n    rerank: bool = True\n\nclass SearchTool(BaseModel):\n    enabled: bool = True\n    timeout_s: float = Field(10.0, gt=0)\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    model_config = SettingsConfigDict(\n        env_prefix=\"APP_\", env_file=\".env\", env_nested_delimiter=\"__\", extra=\"ignore\")\n    env: Literal[\"dev\", \"staging\", \"prod\"] = \"dev\"\n    openai_api_key: SecretStr                     # required: no default\n    model: str = \"gpt-4o-mini\"\n    max_steps: int = Field(8, ge=1, le=50)        # agent loop budget\n    request_timeout_s: float = Field(30.0, gt=0)\n    allowed_models: list[str] = [\"gpt-4o-mini\"]\n    retrieval: Retrieval = Retrieval()\n    search: SearchTool = SearchTool()\n\n@lru_cache\ndef get_settings() -> Settings:\n    return Settings()\n\n# Environment:\n#   APP_OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-live-...      APP_ENV=prod\n#   APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K=20             APP_ALLOWED_MODELS='[\"gpt-4o-mini\",\"gpt-4o\"]'\n#   APP_SEARCH__ENABLED=false\ns = get_settings()\nprint(s.retrieval)          # top_k=20 rerank=True   (nested override, default kept)\nprint(s.search.enabled)     # False                  (parsed bool, not the truthy string)\nprint(s.openai_api_key)     # **********             (masked)\nclient_key = s.openai_api_key.get_secret_value()\ntools = [t for t, on in {\"search\": s.search.enabled, \"retrieve\": True}.items() if on]\n\n# In a test, ignore the developer's .env (real env vars still apply, so\n# kwargs or monkeypatch.delenv must cover anything the shell might set):\nt = Settings(_env_file=None, openai_api_key=\"test\", env=\"dev\", max_steps=2)\n```\n\n- **Bad staging values fail loudly.** If staging sets `APP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_S=thirty` and\n  `APP_ENV=production`, startup fails with one `ValidationError` reporting two errors:\n  `request_timeout_s` is not a valid number and `env` is not one of `'dev', 'staging', 'prod'`.\n- **Result.** The pod crash-loops visibly instead of accepting agent runs with a broken timeout.\n\n### Worked scenario: what a config typo costs at runtime vs at startup\nAn agent averages 6 steps per run; the search tool is first called on step 4. Someone sets\n`APP_SEARCH__TIMEOUT_S=ten`.\n\n| Approach | When it fails | Cost before the failure is visible |\n|---|---|---|\n| `float(os.environ[...])` inside the tool | Step 4 of every run | 4 model calls per run wasted (steps 1-3 plus the call that requested search), user sees an error |\n| Typed `Settings()` at startup | Deploy | Zero runs; the rollout stops and names the field |\n\n### Making mounted secret files authoritative\nWhen a team rotates keys by updating mounted secret files, a leftover env var would silently shadow the\nnew key. Reordering sources makes the file win.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import SecretStr\nfrom pydantic_settings import BaseSettings, SettingsConfigDict\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    model_config = SettingsConfigDict(env_prefix=\"APP_\", secrets_dir=\"/run/secrets\")\n    openai_api_key: SecretStr\n\n    @classmethod\n    def settings_customise_sources(cls, settings_cls, init_settings, env_settings,\n                                   dotenv_settings, file_secret_settings):\n        # default order is init, env, dotenv, secrets; put secret files ahead of env vars\n        return (init_settings, file_secret_settings, env_settings, dotenv_settings)\n```\n\n| Source present | Default order picks | Reordered picks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stale `APP_OPENAI_API_KEY` env var + new secret file | Stale env var | New secret file |\n| Only the env var | Env var | Env var |\n| Neither | Startup fails: required field | Startup fails: required field |","patterns":["**Validate config before agent traffic.** Run a step that only constructs `Settings()` (a CI job against the target environment's variables, or the container's first startup line) so a bad value fails the deploy. Monitor deploy failures and crash-loops that name a field, not mid-run `KeyError`s.","**One cached settings dependency.** Expose `get_settings()` with `@lru_cache` and inject it with FastAPI `Depends(get_settings)`. In tests set `app.dependency_overrides[get_settings]` to return `Settings(_env_file=None, ...)`, and call `get_settings.cache_clear()` when env changes.","**Eval variants as constructor kwargs.** An eval runner builds `Settings(model=\"...\", max_steps=4)` per variant instead of mutating env vars, so two configs can run in one process. Record the non-secret settings with each eval result.","**Prefix everything, alias the fixed names.** Set `env_prefix=\"APP_\"` so fields like `path` cannot pick up system variables, and use `validation_alias` only for names others own, such as `OPENAI_API_KEY`. Review new fields for collisions.","**Secrets as `SecretStr`, delivered by the platform.** Mount them via `secrets_dir` or inject env vars from a secrets manager; never put real values in `.env.example`. Log which settings are present at startup, never their values, and grep logs and traces for unmasked keys.","**Tolerate shared dotenv files.** Use `extra=\"ignore\"` when the same `.env` feeds other tools (a tracing SDK, an MCP server), so their keys do not fail validation. Keep a documented `.env.example` that mirrors the `Settings` fields."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent service has many knobs: model name, max steps, per-tool\n  timeouts, which tools are enabled, retrieval `top_k`, tracing on or off, several API keys. A typo in any\n  of them otherwise surfaces deep inside a run, after tokens and tool calls are already spent.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The `Settings` class behind a FastAPI agent server, a LangGraph or\n  plain-Python worker, an eval runner and a background job queue; one class shared by all of them.\n- **What you control.** The prefix, the source order (env vs secret files), nested groups per tool,\n  constraints such as `Field(gt=0)`, and `SecretStr` so a logged config or trace never shows a key.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing custom sources. The four default sources (init kwargs, env vars, dotenv,\n  secret files) cover most deployments, and the package ships optional ones (YAML, TOML, JSON files and\n  cloud secret stores) you enable in `settings_customise_sources`.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agent API servers.** A FastAPI agent service validates model name, max steps, tool timeouts and keys\n  at startup, so a bad deploy fails the health check instead of the first user run.\n- **Per-environment agent behaviour.** Staging runs a cheap model with tracing on; production runs a\n  stronger model with tighter step limits, all from the same `Settings` class.\n- **Tool feature flags.** Boolean fields enable or disable individual tools per environment or\n  deployment, parsed correctly so `\"false\"` really means off. Per-tenant choices belong in request data.\n- **Eval runners.** The eval harness reuses the agent's `Settings`, overriding only the model and dataset\n  path, so evaluated and deployed configs cannot drift apart.","mini_project":"**Typed config for a tool-calling agent (1-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** replace scattered `os.environ.get` calls in a small agent with one validated `Settings` class.\n- **Steps:** define fields for model name, `max_steps` with `Field(gt=0)`, a nested retrieval group with\n  `top_k`, a per-tool timeout and the provider key as `SecretStr`; set an env prefix and a nested\n  delimiter; load it once and pass it into the agent loop; log the settings object at startup.\n- **Done when:** a typo or a negative step limit stops the process before any model call, the startup\n  log shows the key masked, and one nested field can be overridden from the environment alone."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Init kwargs, priority 1\"] --> M[\"Deep-merge, higher priority wins\"]\n    E[\"Environment vars, priority 2\"] --> M\n    D[\"Dotenv files, priority 3\"] --> M\n    S[\"secrets_dir files, priority 4\"] --> M\n    M --> V[\"One Pydantic validation pass\"]\n    F[\"Field defaults, priority 5\"] -->|\"fill unset fields\"| V\n    V -->|\"all valid\"| OK[\"Typed agent Settings, cached\"]\n    V -->|\"any invalid\"| ERR[\"One ValidationError, all fields\"]\n    ERR --> X[\"Startup fails before any run\"]","caption":"Each source returns a dict; they are merged key by key in priority order, then one validation pass either\nyields a typed agent config or fails startup with every bad field listed."},"interview":[{"q":"Why use pydantic-settings instead of `os.environ.get` calls spread through the code?","a":"- **One typed schema:** every setting is declared once with a type and constraint, so `\"30\"` becomes\n  `30.0` and `\"false\"` becomes `False` without per-call-site conversion.\n- **Fail at startup, not mid-request:** a missing or malformed value raises one `ValidationError` naming\n  every bad field when `Settings()` is built, instead of a `KeyError` hours after deploy.\n- **Self-documenting:** the class is the list of what a new environment must provide."},{"q":"How would you structure configuration for an agent with a dozen tools?","a":"- **Nested model per tool:** each tool gets a small `BaseModel` (enabled, timeout, limits) on the\n  `Settings` class, and `env_nested_delimiter=\"__\"` lets ops change one field without rewriting JSON.\n- **Keys as `SecretStr`, owned names aliased:** each tool's credential is typed `SecretStr`, with\n  `validation_alias` where the vendor fixes the variable name.\n- **Build tools from settings:** a factory reads the settings once and registers only enabled tools, so a\n  disabled tool never appears in the model's tool list."},{"q":"A field named `path` holds a strange value in production. What happened and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Likely cause:** there is no `env_prefix`, and matching is case-insensitive by default, so the field\n  was filled from the operating system's own `PATH` variable.\n- **Fix:** set `env_prefix=\"APP_\"` so only `APP_PATH` can fill it, and use `validation_alias` only for\n  names other software owns, such as `OPENAI_API_KEY`.\n- **Prevent it:** review new field names for collisions with common system variables like `HOME`."},{"q":"How do you pass lists and nested config through environment variables, and when would you stop?","a":"- **Lists and dicts:** pass a JSON string, for example `APP_ALLOWED_MODELS='[\"a\",\"b\"]'`.\n- **Nested models:** set `env_nested_delimiter=\"__\"` so `APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K=20` overrides one field and\n  the rest keep their defaults; without the delimiter the override is silently ignored.\n- **When to stop:** quoting JSON inside shell, YAML and Helm is error-prone, so past a few nested fields a\n  mounted config file read by a custom source is cleaner."},{"q":"How do you test code that depends on `Settings` without the developer's `.env` leaking in?","a":"- **Construct explicitly:** `Settings(_env_file=None, openai_api_key=\"test\", ...)` skips the dotenv file;\n  real environment variables still apply, so cover them with kwargs or `monkeypatch.delenv`.\n- **Override the dependency:** in FastAPI set `app.dependency_overrides[get_settings]` to return the test\n  object.\n- **Mind the cache:** `get_settings` is wrapped in `lru_cache`, so call `get_settings.cache_clear()`\n  after changing environment variables in a test."},{"q":"What does `SecretStr` protect against in an agent that logs traces, and what does it not?","a":"- **Protects:** accidental logging. The value is masked in `repr`, `str` and default serialization, so\n  `print(settings)`, a crash log or a config dump in a trace shows `**********`.\n- **Does not:** encrypt, rotate or audit anything. The plaintext is in process memory and returned by\n  `.get_secret_value()`, which you call only at the point of use.\n- **So:** pair it with a secrets manager or mounted secret files for delivery and rotation."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fail fast vs convenience.** Startup validation turns silent misconfiguration into a crash, right for agent services but annoying for scripts that need one value; there plain `os.environ` is simpler.","**One typed object vs coercion surprises.** It documents every setting but couples config to Pydantic's coercion rules, so lax coercion (`\"1\"` to `True`) can surprise; strict mode trades convenience for exactness.","**JSON-in-env vs quoting pain.** Lists and nested models stay in variables, yet quoting JSON inside shell, YAML and Helm templates is error-prone; past a few nested fields (a dozen tools each with options) a mounted config file is cleaner.","**Masking vs ceremony.** `SecretStr` masks accidental logging but adds `.get_secret_value()` noise, and it is no substitute for a secrets manager with rotation; it only governs how the value is printed.","**Startup-time config vs per-run config.** Settings fix values for the process lifetime; per-tenant or per-request choices (which model this user gets) belong in request data, not in env vars."],"pitfalls":["**Instantiating `Settings()` at import time in a library module.** Symptom: tests fail to import because a required key is missing. Fix: construct lazily or in the entry point.","**Forgetting `env_prefix` and naming a field `path` or `home`.** Symptom: the field is mysteriously filled from the system's own `PATH` or `HOME` variable. Fix: add a prefix.","**Leaving `extra` at its default (`\"forbid\"`) with a shared `.env`.** Symptom: a validation error about unexpected keys from the dotenv file (environment variables never trigger it). Fix: `extra=\"ignore\"`.","**Expecting a nested override without `env_nested_delimiter`.** Symptom: `APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K` is silently ignored and the default used, so the agent retrieves 8 chunks instead of 20. Fix: set the delimiter.","**Caching with `lru_cache`, then changing env vars in a test.** Symptom: old values. Fix: `get_settings.cache_clear()` or dependency overrides.","**Dumping settings into a trace.** Symptom: keys visible in the tracing UI because a field was typed `str`. Fix: type every key as `SecretStr` and export only an allow-listed subset."],"when":"- **Use when:** any long-running agent service or worker with more than a few settings, anything with\n  secrets, and anything deployed to more than one environment.\n- **Default in FastAPI:** settings plug straight into dependency injection.\n- **Avoid when:** throwaway scripts and notebooks; keep plain `os.environ` or python-dotenv.\n- **Measure:** how config failures surface. They should appear as startup errors naming the field, never\n  as mid-run `KeyError`s or wrong agent behaviour.\n- **Interviews:** explain the source precedence and why secrets are typed as `SecretStr`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the `Settings` class, injects it through a cached dependency, and overrides it in tests with explicit kwargs instead of patching environment variables.","architect":"Decides which settings exist per environment, the prefix convention, which values come from a secrets store, and that services fail closed on invalid config.","fde":"Hands the customer a documented settings schema and `.env.example`, and reads the startup `ValidationError` to diagnose their environment in minutes."},"check":{"q":"Your platform team rotated the API key by updating a Kubernetes secret mounted as a file in\n`secrets_dir`. The pods restarted, but they still call the provider with the old key. An old ConfigMap\nalso sets `APP_OPENAI_API_KEY`. Explain what happened and two ways to fix it.","a":"Sources are merged by priority: init kwargs, environment, dotenv, secret files, defaults. The\nstale ConfigMap value arrives as an environment variable, which outranks the mounted secret file, so the\nrotation was silently shadowed; the restart re-read both and the env var won again. Fix one: delete the\nvariable from the ConfigMap so the secret file is the only source. Fix two, if the team wants files to\nbe authoritative by design: override `settings_customise_sources` to return the file-secret source\nahead of the environment source. Either way, add a startup log of *which source* supplied each secret\n(never the value), so the next shadowing is visible, and remember a running process never re-reads its\nsettings without a restart."},"quiz":[{"q":"`APP_MODEL` is set in the environment, `.env` sets `APP_MODEL` differently, and the field has a default. Which value wins?","options":["The `.env` value, because files are read last","The environment variable, because it outranks dotenv files and defaults","The default, because explicit defaults always win","Startup fails because of the conflicting definitions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Priority is constructor kwargs, then environment variables, then dotenv, then secret files, then defaults. This matches the deployment model: the platform's injected value beats a local file."},{"q":"Why type an API key as `SecretStr` rather than `str`?","options":["It encrypts the value in memory","It validates that the key has the provider's format","It masks the value when the settings object is printed, logged or serialized","It loads the value from a secrets manager automatically"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`SecretStr` only changes representation, so logging the config shows asterisks. You still need a real secrets store and must call `.get_secret_value()` where the key is actually used."},{"q":"How would you set only `retrieval.top_k` from the environment for a nested `Retrieval` model?","options":["`APP_RETRIEVAL.TOP_K=20`, which works by default","Set `env_nested_delimiter=\"__\"` and use `APP_RETRIEVAL__TOP_K=20`","Nested models cannot be configured from the environment at all","Set `APP_TOP_K=20`, since nested fields are flattened automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without a configured delimiter, nested models are only settable as a whole JSON string. With `env_nested_delimiter`, a single nested field can be overridden while the others keep their defaults."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/json-jsonl","sources":["Bray, 2017 — RFC 8259: The JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) Data Interchange Format","Official docs: Python json module","Official docs: JSON Lines"],"why":"- **Agent data is nested records.** A transcript with a variable number of messages, a tool call with\n  arguments, a model response with metadata, a trace with one span per step.\n- **The alternatives fail.** CSV cannot represent nesting without inventing an escaping scheme; binary\n  formats need a schema up front and are not readable in a terminal. JSON represents nested objects and\n  arrays directly, every language parses it, and LLMs produce it reliably enough that providers expose\n  JSON modes.\n- **One JSON array is a bad container for many records.** `[{...}, {...}, ...]` must be parsed in full\n  before you use the first record, cannot be appended to without rewriting the closing bracket, cannot be\n  split across workers without parsing, and is corrupted as a whole if a writer crashes before the final\n  `]`. With a million eval cases you hold the whole dataset in memory just to read one.\n- **JSONL's one rule.** Each line is a self-contained JSON value, separated by `\\n`. Appending is writing\n  a line, reading is iterating lines, splitting is `split -l`, and a crash damages at most the last line.\n- **It costs no expressiveness.** Standard encoders escape newlines inside strings as `\\n`, so any JSON\n  value fits on one line, including a long tool result.","how":"### JSON's data model\nRFC 8259 defines six types: object, array, string, number, `true`/`false`, `null`.\n\n| JSON | Python `json` maps to |\n|---|---|\n| object / array | `dict` / `list` |\n| string | `str` |\n| number | `int`/`float` |\n| `true`/`false` / `null` | `bool` / `None` |\n\n- **Not in JSON:** comments, trailing commas, dates, bytes, NaN or Infinity.\n- **Encoding:** strings are Unicode and, for interchange, the text must be UTF-8.\n- **Anything else raises `TypeError` on `dumps`:** datetimes, `Decimal`, sets, most numpy scalars such as\n  `np.int64` and `np.float32`. (`np.float64` slips through only because it subclasses Python `float`.)\n- **Convert first:** with a `default=` function, or serialize a Pydantic model with `model_dump_json()`.\n  This matters most in tool results, which often come straight from a database row or a pandas frame.\n\n### Tool calls are JSON in both directions\n1. **You send** each tool's parameters as a JSON Schema (often generated from a Pydantic model).\n2. **The model returns** arguments; in OpenAI-style APIs they arrive as a JSON string you `json.loads`,\n   and the model can produce malformed or schema-violating JSON.\n3. **You validate** the parsed arguments against the same model, and on failure send the error back as\n   the tool result so the model can retry, instead of crashing the loop.\n4. **You return** the tool's result serialized as a string, so it must be valid, compact JSON.\n\n### The edges where Python's defaults bite\n- **NaN and Infinity.** `json.dumps(float(\"nan\"))` emits `NaN`, which is *not* valid JSON; JavaScript's\n  `JSON.parse` and strict parsers reject it. Pass `allow_nan=False` to fail at write time instead.\n- **Big integers.** Python round-trips any integer exactly, but parsers that use IEEE doubles (JavaScript)\n  lose precision above 2^53. IDs from Snowflake-style generators should travel as strings, including\n  order IDs a tool returns to the model.\n- **Non-ASCII.** `ensure_ascii=True` is the default, so `café` is written as `caf\\u00e9`: valid, but it\n  inflates files and hurts readability for multilingual data, and escapes cost extra tokens in a prompt.\n  `ensure_ascii=False` writes UTF-8, which is correct as long as the file is opened with\n  `encoding=\"utf-8\"`.\n- **Duplicate keys.** The RFC leaves them undefined; Python keeps the last one silently.\n\n### JSONL mechanics\n1. **Write** `f.write(json.dumps(obj) + \"\\n\")` per record, with no pretty-printing, because `indent=`\n   inserts newlines and breaks one-record-per-line.\n2. **Read** by iterating the file object line by line and calling `json.loads` on each, so memory stays\n   proportional to one record.\n3. **Writers** treat the file as append-only and flush per record or per batch.\n4. **Readers** report errors with line numbers so a bad record can be located, and decide explicitly\n   whether to skip or stop.\n5. **Compress** with `gzip.open(path, \"wt\")` for `.jsonl.gz`; line semantics are unchanged.\n\n### Three details that decide crash isolation\n- **What counts as a line.** Iterating a text file splits only on real line endings (`\\n`, `\\r\\n`, and a\n  lone `\\r` in default text mode), all of which encoders escape inside strings, so that is what JSONL\n  means. `str.splitlines()` also splits on U+2028 and U+0085, which `json.dumps(..., ensure_ascii=False)`\n  leaves unescaped inside strings, so `text.splitlines()` can cut a valid record in half. Iterate the\n  file, or split on `\"\\n\"` explicitly.\n- **The torn last line.** A writer killed mid-write leaves a final line with no trailing newline that\n  usually fails to parse. A resuming reader should tolerate exactly that case (drop it, then truncate or\n  rewrite the file) and treat a bad line anywhere else as real corruption.\n- **One writer per file.** Several processes appending to one file can interleave bytes when records are\n  large or the file is on network storage, producing two half records on a line. Give each worker its own\n  shard (`results-00.jsonl`, `results-01.jsonl`) or funnel writes through one process.\n\n### Where you meet it\n| Use | Shape |\n|---|---|\n| OpenAI-style fine-tuning data | One `{\"messages\": [...]}` object per line, tool calls included for agent traces |\n| OpenAI Batch API | JSONL of requests, each with a `custom_id` so results match back |\n| Eval harnesses | Cases and results as JSONL |\n| Agent traces and structured logs | Usually one JSON object per line: one step, one span or one event |\n\nFor analytics over millions of rows, JSONL is usually converted to a columnar format such as Parquet,\nsince re-parsing text on every query is slow.","example":"### Parse tool-call arguments, run the tool, log the step as JSONL\nThe model's arguments are untrusted JSON; validate them, feed errors back to the model, and append each\nstep to a trace file.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\nclass LookupOrder(BaseModel):\n    order_id: str            # string, not int: IDs above 2^53 break JS consumers\n    include_items: bool = False\n\ndef run_tool(raw_args: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        args = LookupOrder.model_validate_json(raw_args)   # parse + validate in one step\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        return json.dumps({\"error\": e.errors(include_url=False)}, default=str)  # model retries\n    row = {\"order_id\": args.order_id, \"status\": \"shipped\", \"total\": 42.5}\n    return json.dumps(row, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False)\n\ndef log_step(f, step: int, name: str, raw_args: str, result: str) -> None:\n    rec = {\"step\": step, \"tool\": name, \"args\": raw_args, \"result\": result}\n    f.write(json.dumps(rec, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False) + \"\\n\")\n    f.flush()                                           # a crash loses at most this line\n\nwith open(\"trace.jsonl\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n    for step, raw in enumerate(['{\"order_id\": \"9007199254740993\"}', '{\"order_id\": 5'], 1):\n        log_step(f, step, \"lookup_order\", raw, run_tool(raw))   # 2nd is malformed JSON\n```\n\n- **The malformed second call** becomes a JSON error string that goes back to the model as the tool\n  result, and the trace still gets a complete line for step 2.\n\n### Write an eval set safely, read it as a stream\nWriting an eval set safely and reading it back as a stream, with failures pinned to a line.\n\n```python\nimport json, math\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ncases = [\n    {\"id\": \"q1\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Refund policy?\\nAsk twice.\"}],\n     \"expected\": \"policy\", \"score\": 0.9},\n    {\"id\": \"q2\", \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Café opening hours\"}],\n     \"expected\": \"hours\", \"score\": math.nan},          # a bad value we must catch\n]\n\ndef write_jsonl(path: Path, rows) -> None:\n    with path.open(\"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for row in rows:\n            # allow_nan=False refuses to emit the invalid token NaN\n            f.write(json.dumps(row, ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False) + \"\\n\")\n\ndef read_jsonl(path: Path):\n    with path.open(encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1):\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            try:\n                yield json.loads(line)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{path}:{lineno}: {e.msg}\") from e\n\ncases[1][\"score\"] = None                               # fix: null is valid JSON\np = Path(\"eval.jsonl\")\nwrite_jsonl(p, cases)\nprint(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").count(\"\\n\"))       # 2: the embedded newline was escaped\nfor case in read_jsonl(p):                             # one record in memory at a time\n    print(case[\"id\"], len(case[\"messages\"]))\n```\n\n- **Without the fix,** `write_jsonl` raises `ValueError: Out of range float values are not JSON compliant`\n  on the second record, at write time, instead of producing a file a TypeScript dashboard later refuses\n  to parse.\n\n### Worked scenario: a resumable agent eval run that survives a torn last line\nA worker runs the agent on each case, appends one result per `custom_id`, and can be killed at any time.\nOn restart it drops a torn tail, skips finished IDs and appends the rest.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef resume(path: Path) -> set[str]:\n    done, good_bytes = set(), 0\n    if not path.exists():\n        return done\n    with path.open(\"rb\") as f:\n        for raw in f:                          # binary iteration splits on b\"\\n\" only\n            if not raw.endswith(b\"\\n\"):        # torn last line: writer died mid-record\n                break\n            if raw.strip():                    # a bad *complete* line is real corruption: let it raise\n                done.add(json.loads(raw)[\"custom_id\"])\n            good_bytes += len(raw)\n    with path.open(\"r+b\") as f:\n        f.truncate(good_bytes)                 # cut the torn tail before appending again\n    return done\n\ndef run_eval(cases, out: Path, run_agent):\n    done = resume(out)\n    with out.open(\"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        for case in cases:\n            if case[\"custom_id\"] in done:\n                continue                       # finished: do not pay for it twice\n            result = run_agent(case)           # several model and tool calls\n            f.write(json.dumps({\"custom_id\": case[\"custom_id\"], **result},\n                               ensure_ascii=False, allow_nan=False) + \"\\n\")\n            f.flush()                          # durability matches progress\n```\n\n| Event | Effect on `results.jsonl` |\n|---|---|\n| Pod killed while writing record 1,800,001 | 1,800,000 complete lines plus a partial one |\n| Restart calls `resume` | Partial line truncated; 1,800,000 IDs marked done |\n| Job continues | Only the remaining cases are sent, so it is idempotent |\n\n- **Why it matters for agent evals:** each case may cost several model and tool calls, so re-running\n  finished cases after a crash multiplies spend. A bad *complete* line anywhere else should raise, since\n  that is real corruption.","patterns":["**Strict writer by default.** Wrap `json.dumps` with `allow_nan=False`, an explicit `default=` for datetimes and numpy scalars, and `encoding=\"utf-8\"` on every open; use it for tool results and traces alike. Monitor write-time `ValueError` and `TypeError` counts; they are bugs caught early.","**Validate every tool call and every line at the boundary.** Define a Pydantic model per tool and per record type and call `Model.model_validate_json(line)`, raising with the file and line number (or returning the error to the model). Track rejected-argument rates per tool; schema drift shows up there.","**Shard by worker, merge by concatenation.** Each eval or batch process writes `results-{worker:02d}.jsonl`, never a shared file, and downstream jobs read the shard glob. Watch for shards with no trailing newline, which mark workers that died mid-write.","**Key every record for idempotent resume.** Carry a stable `custom_id` (strings, not big integers) and resume as in the eval scenario. Monitor duplicate-ID counts after merges.","**Promote to Parquet for analytics.** Keep JSONL as the append and interchange format for traces and evals, and convert finished files (for example `pd.read_json(path, lines=True).to_parquet(...)`) before repeated queries. Watch query latency and file size; a gzip-compressed `.jsonl.gz` is the cheaper archive."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every tool call is a JSON round trip: you send a JSON Schema, the model\n  returns arguments as JSON, your tool returns a result you serialize back. A tool result with a NaN, a\n  `datetime` or a numpy scalar breaks the loop at step 5 instead of at your desk.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** OpenAI-style `tool_calls` carry arguments as a JSON *string* you\n  must parse; structured-output modes return JSON; agent traces, eval datasets, fine-tuning files and\n  Batch API inputs and outputs are JSONL.\n- **What you control.** A strict writer (`allow_nan=False`, a `default=` hook), a validating reader with\n  line numbers, one writer per file, and a stable ID per record for resumable eval and batch jobs.\n- **What you can skip.** The RFC's grammar details. Know the six types, what Python emits that is not\n  valid JSON, and why JSONL beats one big array.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool-call round trips.** Parsing the model's argument string, validating it and serializing the tool\n  result back is JSON on every step of every agent run.\n- **Agent trace logs.** One JSONL record per step (run ID, tool, arguments, status, tokens) is easy to\n  append, tail and load into pandas or Polars later.\n- **Eval datasets.** Each test case is one JSONL line with a stable ID, so a crashed eval run resumes by\n  skipping IDs already present in the results file.\n- **Fine-tuning and batch jobs.** Training files built from good agent transcripts and batch inputs and\n  outputs are JSONL, one request or example per line.","mini_project":"**A strict JSONL trace logger for an agent (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** log every step of a small tool-calling agent to JSONL safely enough that bad values fail at\n  write time, not at analysis time.\n- **Steps:** write a logger that serializes with `allow_nan=False` and a `default=` hook for datetimes\n  and numpy scalars; write one record per step with a run ID and step number; write a reader that\n  reports the line number of any bad record; feed it a tool result containing NaN and a `datetime`.\n- **Done when:** the NaN case raises at the writer, the datetime is stored as a string, the reader loads\n  a 100-step log and names the exact line of a hand-corrupted record."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Agent step: dict or Pydantic model\"] --> W[\"json.dumps, allow_nan=False, no indent\"]\n    W --> A[\"Append line plus newline, flush\"]\n    A --> F[(\"Shard file per worker\")]\n    F --> I[\"Reader iterates line by line\"]\n    I --> T{\"Line ends with newline?\"}\n    T -->|\"no, last line\"| X[\"Torn tail: drop or truncate\"]\n    T -->|\"yes\"| L[\"json.loads, then validate\"]\n    L -->|\"valid\"| U[\"Use one record, flat memory\"]\n    L -->|\"invalid\"| E[\"Raise with path and line\"]","caption":"The agent's trace writer keeps one record per line; the reader streams line by line, tolerates only a\ntorn final line, and pins any other failure to a line number."},"interview":[{"q":"What is JSONL, and why do fine-tuning, eval and batch APIs use it instead of a JSON array?","a":"- **Definition:** one self-contained JSON value per line, separated by `\\n`; encoders escape newlines\n  inside strings, so any value fits on one line.\n- **Why:** a reader handles one record at a time, a writer appends without rewriting a closing `]`, a file\n  splits by line across workers, and a crash damages at most the last line.\n- **Example:** OpenAI-style fine-tuning data is one `{\"messages\": [...]}` object per line."},{"q":"Your agent crashes intermittently when the model calls a tool. How do you make the loop robust?","a":"- **Treat arguments as untrusted input:** parse the JSON string and validate it with the tool's Pydantic\n  model, because models sometimes emit truncated or off-schema JSON.\n- **Return errors, do not raise:** send the validation error back as the tool result so the model can\n  correct itself, and cap retries per step.\n- **Serialize results strictly:** `allow_nan=False` and a `default=` hook, so a NaN or a `datetime` in a\n  database row cannot break the next model call. Track rejected-argument rate per tool."},{"q":"Python reads your results file fine, but a TypeScript dashboard rejects it. What do you check?","a":"- **NaN or Infinity first:** Python's `json.dumps` emits `NaN` by default, which is not valid JSON, so\n  strict parsers like `JSON.parse` fail. Write with `allow_nan=False` and map NaN to `null`.\n- **Big integers next:** JavaScript parses numbers as IEEE doubles and loses precision above 2^53, so IDs\n  should travel as strings.\n- **Then encoding:** confirm the file is UTF-8 and was opened with `encoding=\"utf-8\"` on both sides."},{"q":"A reader fails with \"Unterminated string\" on a few records of scraped web text. Why?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the code splits with `str.splitlines()`, which also breaks on U+2028 and U+0085.\n  `ensure_ascii=False` leaves those unescaped inside strings, so a valid record is cut in half.\n- **Fix:** iterate the file object, which splits only on `\\n` and `\\r\\n`, or split on `\"\\n\"` explicitly.\n- **Prevent it:** keep a regression case containing U+2028 in the reader's tests."},{"q":"How should several eval workers write agent results without corrupting the output?","a":"- **One writer per file:** concurrent appends to one file can interleave bytes for large records or on\n  network storage, leaving two half records on one line.\n- **Shard and concatenate:** each worker writes `results-00.jsonl`, `results-01.jsonl` and so on, and\n  downstream jobs read the glob.\n- **Monitor:** a shard without a trailing newline marks a worker that died mid-write; duplicate\n  `custom_id` counts after a merge reveal double processing."},{"q":"When would you not keep data in JSONL?","a":"- **Repeated analytics:** text JSON is larger and slower to parse than columnar formats, so finished\n  datasets queried column by column across millions of rows belong in Parquet or a database.\n- **Typed joins and schemas:** JSONL has no enclosing schema, so drift is found only when a reader hits\n  the bad line.\n- **Keep JSONL for:** append-heavy interchange, such as eval cases, batch inputs and outputs, and traces."}],"tradeoffs":["**Streaming vs document guarantees.** JSONL streams and appends record by record but has no enclosing schema, so a malformed middle line is only found when a reader reaches it.","**Readable vs fast.** Text JSON is human-readable and universal, yet several times larger and slower to parse than columnar binary formats; for repeated analytical queries over millions of rows, Parquet wins.","**Schemaless vs drift.** Adding fields to eval, trace or log data is easy, but readers drift silently unless a Pydantic model or JSON Schema validates each line at the boundary.","**`ensure_ascii=False` vs safety.** Multilingual data stays compact and readable (and cheaper in tokens when the JSON goes back into a prompt), but a consumer that guesses the wrong encoding mangles it; ASCII escapes are safer when you do not control the reader."],"pitfalls":["**`json.dump(obj, f, indent=2)` in a JSONL writer.** Symptom: `JSONDecodeError` on the very first line (a lone `{` is not a complete value) because each record spans many lines. Fix: no `indent`.","**Serializing NaN from pandas or numpy metrics.** Symptom: a JavaScript or Go consumer rejects the file while Python reads it fine. Fix: `allow_nan=False` and map NaN to `null`.","**Trusting tool-call arguments.** Symptom: the agent loop crashes with `JSONDecodeError` or `KeyError` when the model emits truncated or off-schema arguments. Fix: validate with a Pydantic model and return the error to the model as the tool result.","**`f.readlines()` or `[json.loads(l) for l in f]` on a multi-gigabyte file.** Symptom: memory climbs to several times the file size (parsed objects are far larger than their text) and the process is OOM-killed. (`json.load` on JSONL fails outright with \"Extra data\".) Fix: a generator reader.","**Reading with `path.read_text().splitlines()`.** Symptom: `JSONDecodeError` (\"Unterminated string\") on a handful of records containing U+2028 or similar, often scraped web text. Fix: iterate the file.","**Opening files without `encoding=\"utf-8\"`.** Symptom: `UnicodeDecodeError` or mojibake on Windows or in containers with a non-UTF-8 locale.","**Passing numpy types into `json.dumps`.** Symptom: `TypeError: Object of type float32 is not JSON serializable` deep inside an eval run or a tool result. Fix: `.item()`, `.tolist()` or a `default=` hook."],"when":"- **JSON for single payloads:** API requests and responses, tool schemas and arguments, tool results,\n  config documents, a model's structured output.\n- **JSONL for growing or one-at-a-time collections:** eval datasets, fine-tuning data, batch-inference\n  inputs and outputs, agent traces and logs.\n- **Validate at ingestion** with a Pydantic model so bad records fail with a line number.\n- **Move to Parquet or a database** for column-wise queries, joins or typed schemas across millions of\n  rows.\n- **Interviews:** \"why JSONL rather than a JSON array?\" The answer is streaming, appending and crash\n  isolation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes JSONL readers as generators with line-numbered errors, sets `allow_nan=False`, and validates every record against a Pydantic model before it enters an eval or training run.","architect":"Chooses where JSONL is the interchange format and where data is promoted to Parquet or a database, and defines the record schema and versioning for datasets.","fde":"Converts a customer's CSV or ticket export into a validated JSONL eval set, catching encoding and malformed-row problems before they poison results."},"check":{"q":"Your batch-inference job writes 2 million results to a single JSON array file, and a pod restart at\n90% leaves it unusable. Redesign it and explain why each choice helps.","a":"Write results as JSONL, one object per line, each carrying the request's `custom_id`. A restart\nthen damages at most the final partial line, and the reader can drop a last line that fails to parse. On\nresume, read the existing file, collect completed IDs and only submit the rest, which makes the job\nidempotent. Flush after each batch so durability matches progress. Downstream consumers can stream the\nfile without loading 2 million objects, and shards can be split by line for parallel scoring. If the\nresults feed analytics, convert the finished JSONL to Parquet."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is JSONL preferred over a single JSON array for a large, growing eval dataset?","options":["JSONL files are always smaller than equivalent JSON arrays","Each line parses independently, so the file can be streamed, appended to and split without parsing the whole thing","JSONL supports comments and trailing commas that JSON forbids","JSONL stores numbers in binary, which is faster to parse"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The size and syntax are essentially the same as JSON. The advantage is structural: one record per line means constant memory reads, appends without rewriting, line-based sharding and crash damage limited to one line."},{"q":"What does Python's `json.dumps({\"x\": float(\"nan\")})` produce by default?","options":["`{\"x\": null}`, because NaN maps to null","A `TypeError` because NaN is not serializable","`{\"x\": NaN}`, which is not valid JSON and strict parsers reject","`{\"x\": \"NaN\"}`, a string representation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Python defaults to `allow_nan=True` and emits the JavaScript-style token NaN, which RFC 8259 does not allow. Setting `allow_nan=False` raises a ValueError instead, catching the bad value at write time."},{"q":"A JSONL writer uses `json.dumps(record, indent=2)`. What breaks?","options":["Nothing; readers ignore whitespace inside JSON values","Only non-ASCII characters are affected","The file becomes valid JSON instead of JSONL","Each record spans several lines, so line-by-line readers fail to parse"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Indentation inserts newlines between keys. JSON parsers tolerate whitespace inside one document, but JSONL readers split on newlines first, so each fragment is an incomplete value and parsing fails."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/numpy","sources":["Harris et al., 2020 — Array programming with NumPy","Official docs: NumPy"],"why":"- **A Python list of floats is pointers to boxed objects.** Each element is a separate heap object with\n  a type and a reference count. A dot product costs, per element, two pointer chases, two type checks, an\n  allocation for the result and an interpreter dispatch.\n- **That is slow at agent scale.** Scoring a query against 100,000 embeddings of 768 dimensions is about\n  77 million multiply-adds: seconds to minutes in pure Python versus milliseconds in compiled code\n  (illustrative orders of magnitude; measure on your machine). In an agent loop that runs every step.\n- **It wastes memory too.** A boxed float is far larger than its 8 bytes of payload, so lists use several\n  times the memory of the raw values and scatter them across the heap, ruining CPU cache behaviour.\n- **NumPy's answer.** Store numbers unboxed in one buffer with one dtype, and expose whole-buffer\n  operations. The loop still exists, but runs in C (or an optimized BLAS library for matrix products) over\n  contiguous memory.\n- **It is the common substrate.** Nearly the whole scientific Python stack, pandas, scikit-learn, and the\n  tensor interop in PyTorch, is built on or speaks this array model.","how":"### The ndarray: buffer, dtype, shape, strides\nAn array is a pointer to raw bytes plus metadata:\n\n| Field | Example | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `dtype` | `float32` | 4 bytes per element |\n| `shape` | `(100000, 768)` | Size of each axis |\n| `strides` | `(3072, 4)` | Bytes to step one position along each axis (C-ordered, row-major) |\n\n- **Addressing.** Element `[i, j]` lives at `offset + i*3072 + j*4`.\n- **Memory is exactly `n × d × itemsize`:** 100,000 × 768 × 4 = 307,200,000 bytes, about 307 MB. In\n  float64 it would be twice that, which is why embeddings are usually kept in float32.\n\n### Views versus copies\nIndexing is arithmetic on strides, so many operations return a *view* sharing the buffer.\n\n| Returns a view | Returns a copy |\n|---|---|\n| Basic slicing: `a[:10]`, `a[:, ::2]` | Fancy indexing: `a[[1, 5, 9]]` |\n| `reshape` when possible | Boolean masks: `a[a > 0]` |\n| Transpose: `a.T` just swaps the strides | |\n\n- **Why views.** Speed: slicing a 307 MB array costs nothing.\n- **The consequence.** Writing into a view writes into the original, for example a memory store shared by\n  two agent sessions.\n- **Why fancy indexing copies.** It must gather scattered elements into new memory.\n\n### Contiguity and layout\n- **Views leave arrays non-contiguous.** `a.T` of a C-ordered matrix is Fortran-ordered; `a[:, ::2]`\n  skips every other element.\n- **Strides cost cache.** NumPy accepts any strides, but a large stride wastes cache lines, so reducing\n  along the contiguous axis is faster than along the strided one.\n- **C extensions are stricter.** Code taking a raw pointer (FAISS, many C/CUDA kernels, some\n  serialization paths) expects C-contiguous memory of a specific dtype. Check `a.flags[\"C_CONTIGUOUS\"]`\n  and convert once with `np.ascontiguousarray(a, dtype=np.float32)` at the boundary; that is the one place\n  a copy is correct.\n- **`reshape` may copy silently.** It returns a view only when existing strides can express the new\n  shape, so after a transpose a reshape may cost a full copy.\n\n### Vectorization and ufuncs\n- **Ufuncs.** Element-wise operations (`+`, `np.exp`, comparisons) are compiled loops over every element.\n- **Reductions take an `axis`,** the dimension that collapses: `a.sum(axis=1)` on `(N, d)` gives `(N,)`.\n  Same for `sum`, `max`, `argmax`.\n- **Matrix products (`@`) dispatch to BLAS,** which uses SIMD and multiple cores.\n- **Many release the GIL,** so threads can overlap NumPy work, for example scoring memories while an\n  async agent server waits on a model call.\n\n### Broadcasting\n1. Align shapes from the right.\n2. Each pair of dimensions must be equal or one of them 1.\n3. Size-1 dimensions are stretched virtually (stride 0), with no copy.\n\n| Expression | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `(N, d) / (N, 1)` | Divides each row by its own norm |\n| `(N, d) - (d,)` | Subtracts a mean vector from every row |\n| `(N,) - (N, 1)` | Silently becomes an `(N, N)` matrix: the top source of silent bugs |\n\n### Dtypes and numerical behaviour\n- **Integers wrap on overflow** without an exception: a `uint8` pixel of 250 plus 10 becomes 4.\n- **float32 has about 7 significant decimal digits:** enough for similarity scores, risky for long\n  accumulations.\n- **NumPy scalars are not Python floats.** `float32` values do not serialize to JSON until you call\n  `.item()` or `.tolist()`, which bites when a similarity score goes into a tool result or a trace.\n- **Mixed dtypes promote to the wider one.** `float32_array * float64_array` produces float64, so one\n  float64 array (a weights vector built with `np.array([...])`, a result from an older file) can silently\n  double the memory of everything downstream.\n- **Scalars differ.** A plain Python scalar does not force the upcast (`emb * 0.5` stays float32). A\n  NumPy `float64` scalar, such as a value pulled out of another array, does under NumPy 2's promotion\n  rules, whereas NumPy 1.x let it slide, so upgrades can change dtypes. Check `result.dtype` at stage\n  boundaries.\n\n### Randomness\n- **Use `rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)` and pass `rng` around** rather than seeding global state,\n  because explicit generators make experiments (and sampled eval subsets) reproducible and do not\n  interfere across libraries.","example":"### Agent tool routing: pick 5 of 200 tools by embedding similarity\nSending 200 tool schemas every step wastes context; embed the tool descriptions once and send only the\nclosest few to the user's request.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\ntool_names = [f\"tool_{i}\" for i in range(200)]\n# In production: embeddings of each tool's description from your embedding model, done once.\ntool_vecs = rng.standard_normal((200, 768), dtype=np.float32)\ntool_vecs /= np.linalg.norm(tool_vecs, axis=1, keepdims=True)    # (200, 768) / (200, 1)\n\ndef route(query_vec: np.ndarray, k: int = 5) -> list[tuple[str, float]]:\n    q = np.asarray(query_vec, dtype=np.float32)\n    q = q / np.linalg.norm(q)          # new array: never mutate the caller's vector\n    scores = tool_vecs @ q                                     # (200,)\n    top = np.argpartition(-scores, k)[:k]\n    top = top[np.argsort(-scores[top])]\n    return [(tool_names[i], scores[i].item()) for i in top]    # .item(): JSON-safe float\n\npicked = route(rng.standard_normal(768))\nassert tool_vecs.dtype == np.float32 and tool_vecs.shape == (200, 768)\nprint(picked)    # pass only these 5 schemas to the model this step\n```\n\n- **Why it pays:** 5 schemas instead of 200 shrinks every prompt and reduces wrong-tool picks; log the\n  routed set per step so an eval can check the right tool was in the top 5.\n\n### Exact top-k cosine search\nExact top-k cosine search over 100,000 embeddings, the core of every retrieval prototype and agent\nsemantic-memory store.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\ndocs = rng.standard_normal((100_000, 768), dtype=np.float32)   # ~307 MB\nquery = rng.standard_normal(768, dtype=np.float32)\nprint(docs.nbytes, docs.strides)          # 307200000 (3072, 4)\n\n# Normalize rows: (N, 768) / (N, 1) broadcasts per row; /= writes into docs in place\ndocs /= np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1, keepdims=True)\nquery /= np.linalg.norm(query)\n\nscores = docs @ query                     # (100000,) via one BLAS matrix-vector product\nk = 5\ntop = np.argpartition(-scores, k)[:k]     # O(N) selection of the k best, unordered\ntop = top[np.argsort(-scores[top])]       # sort only those k\nprint(top, scores[top].round(3))\n\n# Pitfall demo: forgetting keepdims gives shape (N,), which cannot broadcast against (N, 768)\nnorms = np.linalg.norm(docs, axis=1)      # (100000,)\n# docs / norms would raise: operands could not be broadcast together\nhead = docs[:10]                          # a view: head[:] = 0 would zero rows of docs\npicked = docs[[0, 5, 9]]                  # fancy indexing: a copy, safe to modify\nprint(np.shares_memory(head, docs), np.shares_memory(picked, docs))  # True False\n```\n\n- **`argpartition`** avoids sorting 100,000 scores to find 5.\n- **`keepdims=True`** keeps the norm as `(N, 1)` so the division broadcasts per row.\n- **Scale limit.** Past a few million vectors, exact search like this is replaced by an approximate index.\n\n### Worked scenario: batched exact search as the recall baseline for an agent's retriever\n`Q @ D.T` for many eval queries materialises a huge block. Processing queries in batches bounds the\ntemporary, keeping only k results per row; the output is ground truth to compare with the vector\ndatabase's answers.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef batched_topk(Q, D, k=10, batch=1_000):\n    \"\"\"Q (m, d) and D (n, d), both L2-normalized float32. Returns (m, k) indices and scores.\"\"\"\n    idx = np.empty((len(Q), k), dtype=np.int64)\n    val = np.empty((len(Q), k), dtype=np.float32)\n    for s in range(0, len(Q), batch):\n        S = Q[s:s + batch] @ D.T                        # (b, n) block of scores\n        part = np.argpartition(-S, k, axis=1)[:, :k]     # (b, k), unordered\n        top = np.take_along_axis(S, part, axis=1)\n        order = np.argsort(-top, axis=1)                 # sort only k per row\n        idx[s:s + batch] = np.take_along_axis(part, order, axis=1)\n        val[s:s + batch] = np.take_along_axis(top, order, axis=1)\n    return idx, val\n\ndef recall_at_k(exact_ids, db_ids):\n    \"\"\"Share of the exact top-k that the vector database also returned, per query, averaged.\"\"\"\n    return np.mean([len(set(e) & set(r)) / len(e) for e, r in zip(exact_ids, db_ids)])\n```\n\n| Plan (768-d float32, 1,000,000 docs) | Peak score block |\n|---|---|\n| 50,000 queries at once | 50,000 × 1,000,000 × 4 B = 200 GB: does not fit |\n| batch=1,000 | 4 GB, plus a temporary of the same size from `-S` |\n| batch=100 | 0.4 GB per block |\n\n| Embedding store (768-d) | float64 | float32 | float16 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 100,000 vectors | 614 MB | 307 MB | 154 MB |\n| 1,000,000 vectors | 6.1 GB | 3.1 GB | 1.5 GB |\n\n- **For an agent memory store:** an agent that writes one 768-d float32 memory per turn reaches 100,000\n  memories at 307 MB; that still fits in process, but at 1,000,000 (3.1 GB per replica) move to a vector\n  database.","patterns":["**Normalize and pin layout at ingestion.** Convert once with `np.ascontiguousarray(x, dtype=np.float32)`, divide by row norms, and store that. Monitor `np.isnan(x).any()` and the min/max of row norms per batch; a zero or NaN norm usually means an empty input text upstream, such as an empty tool result embedded.","**Assert shapes and dtypes at stage boundaries.** Put `assert x.shape == (n, d) and x.dtype == np.float32` where arrays enter and leave a component, including after every embedding call. Track `x.nbytes` in logs, so a float64 upcast shows up as a doubled number before it shows up as an OOM.","**Memory-map big arrays.** Save with `np.save` and open with `np.load(path, mmap_mode=\"r\")`, so agent workers share pages from the OS cache instead of each loading a copy. Monitor resident memory per worker and disk read throughput.","**Bound BLAS threads per process.** When running several agent worker processes, set `OMP_NUM_THREADS` (or the BLAS-specific variable, such as `OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS`) so cores are not oversubscribed. Watch CPU load average against core count and matrix-product latency.","**Keep exact search as the recall baseline.** Run `batched_topk` on a sample of real agent queries and compare with the vector database's results. Alert when recall@k drops after an index rebuild, an embedding-model change or a parameter change.","**Convert scores before they leave NumPy.** Call `.item()` or `.tolist()` before a similarity score goes into a tool result, a trace or an API response. Watch for serialization `TypeError`s in agent logs."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every embedding your agent produces (a query, a memory, a tool\n  description, a retrieved chunk) comes back as a float vector. Scoring it against stored vectors, picking\n  the top-k memories or the 5 most relevant tools out of 200, and measuring retrieval recall are NumPy\n  one-liners.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Embedding API responses turned into `float32` arrays, in-process\n  semantic memory for prototypes, LangChain or LlamaIndex in-memory vector stores, FAISS inputs, eval\n  scripts for recall@k, and the arrays inside pandas and scikit-learn.\n- **What you control.** Dtype (float32, not the float64 default), normalization once at ingestion, shapes\n  you assert at boundaries, batch sizes for big score matrices, and when to hand off to a real index.\n- **What you can skip.** Linear algebra beyond dot products and norms, and most of the API. Shapes,\n  broadcasting, views, dtype promotion and `argpartition` are the working set.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Tool selection.** Embed the user request and 200 tool descriptions, score them with one matrix\n  product, and expose only the top 5 tools to the model.\n- **In-process semantic memory.** A prototype agent keeps past facts as a normalized `float32` matrix and\n  recalls the top-k by dot product before each step.\n- **Retrieval evals.** Recall@k over a labelled query set is a few array operations once scores and\n  gold IDs are arrays.\n- **Deduplicating memories or chunks.** Pairwise cosine similarity above a threshold flags near-duplicate\n  entries before they crowd the context window.","mini_project":"**Embedding-based tool router (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** pick the most relevant tools for a request using NumPy alone, and measure how often the right\n  tool makes the shortlist.\n- **Steps:** write 30 short tool descriptions and 50 labelled requests; embed both and store them as\n  `float32`; normalize once; score requests against tools with one matrix product; take the top 5 with\n  `argpartition` and sort only those; assert shapes at each boundary.\n- **Done when:** you report recall@5 for the 50 requests, the scoring step runs without Python loops,\n  and you can name which requests failed and why their descriptions overlap."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[(\"Embedding buffer, one dtype\")] --> M[\"dtype, shape, strides\"]\n    M --> O{\"Which operation?\"}\n    O -->|\"slice, transpose, reshape\"| V[\"View: same buffer\"]\n    O -->|\"fancy index or mask\"| C[\"Copy: new memory\"]\n    O -->|\"ufunc, reduction, matmul\"| K[\"Compiled loop or BLAS\"]\n    V -.->|\"writes go through\"| B\n    K --> R[\"Result: a full temporary\"]\n    V --> X{\"Passing to a C extension?\"}\n    X -->|\"strided or wrong dtype\"| A[\"ascontiguousarray once\"]\n    X -->|\"contiguous float32\"| P[\"Pass pointer as is\"]","caption":"One buffer, many behaviours: the operation you choose decides whether you get a free view, a gathered\ncopy or a compiled loop, and handing vectors to FAISS or another C extension is where a copy is correct."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is a NumPy dot product so much faster than the same loop over Python lists?","a":"- **Lists hold pointers to boxed objects:** each element costs pointer chases, type checks, a result\n  allocation and an interpreter dispatch.\n- **An ndarray is one unboxed, typed buffer:** the loop runs in C, or in BLAS for `@`, over contiguous\n  memory with SIMD and often several cores.\n- **Memory follows:** values take exactly `n × d × itemsize` bytes and stay cache-friendly instead of\n  being scattered across the heap."},{"q":"Your agent has 200 tools and picks the wrong one too often. How would you use embeddings to help?","a":"- **Route before the model call:** embed each tool's description once into a normalized float32 matrix,\n  embed the request, and pass only the top 5 by cosine score (`argpartition`) to the model.\n- **Measure it:** build an eval of requests labelled with the correct tool and track whether it lands in\n  the routed set (recall@5); tune k from that.\n- **Watch the failure mode:** a tool missing from the routed set can never be called, so keep a fallback\n  such as a generic search tool, or raise k for ambiguous requests."},{"q":"A recall metric looks plausible but is wrong after a refactor. How could broadcasting cause that?","a":"- **The classic bug:** `(N,) - (N, 1)` broadcasts to an `(N, N)` matrix with no error, so a mean is\n  silently taken over N² pairs.\n- **How to find it:** print or assert `shape` at each step; an unexpected 2-D intermediate, or a sudden\n  `MemoryError` at large N, gives it away.\n- **Fix:** keep reduced axes with `keepdims=True`, or reshape explicitly so both operands have the shape\n  you wrote down."},{"q":"When would you keep an agent's long-term memory in a NumPy array, and when move it to a vector database?","a":"- **NumPy is enough when:** one process, up to roughly hundreds of thousands of vectors (100,000 768-d\n  float32 vectors are 307 MB), exact results wanted, and no per-user filtering or concurrent writers.\n- **Move when:** several replicas must share memory, you need metadata filters or tenant isolation,\n  writes are concurrent, or the store passes a few million vectors.\n- **Keep NumPy anyway:** as the exact recall baseline that checks the database's approximate results."},{"q":"Memory doubled after a pipeline change. What do you check?","a":"- **Dtype promotion:** one float64 operand, such as a weights vector built with `np.array([...])`, turns\n  a float32 pipeline into float64. Under NumPy 2 a NumPy `float64` scalar also upcasts.\n- **The numbers:** 1,000,000 768-d embeddings take 3.1 GB in float32 but 6.1 GB in float64.\n- **Prevent it:** assert `x.dtype == np.float32` at stage boundaries and log `x.nbytes`, so the doubling\n  shows up in logs before it shows up as an OOM."},{"q":"When is a view dangerous, and why use `argpartition` rather than `argsort` for top-k?","a":"- **Aliasing:** basic slices share the buffer, so `head = docs[:10]` followed by `head[:] = 0` zeroes rows\n  of `docs`; call `.copy()` before mutating shared data. Slicing a 307 MB array otherwise costs nothing.\n- **C-extension boundaries:** transposed or strided views are not C-contiguous; convert once with\n  `np.ascontiguousarray(a, dtype=np.float32)`.\n- **Top-k:** `argpartition` selects the k best in O(N) without ordering them, while a full sort of 100,000\n  scores is O(N log N) to find 5; then sort only k. With `axis=1` and `np.take_along_axis`, the same trick\n  keeps only k results per query row in batched search."}],"tradeoffs":["**Compiled speed vs temporaries.** Whole-array operations are fast, but every intermediate is a full temporary, so memory can peak at several copies of your data; in-place operators and `out=` arguments help.","**Free views vs aliasing.** Slicing and transposes cost nothing, but a mutation through a view changes the original, a correctness hazard in shared code paths such as a memory store used by many sessions.","**float32 vs float64.** float32 halves memory and speeds up BLAS, but loses precision in long sums or ill-conditioned linear algebra, where float64 is worth the cost.","**In-process arrays vs a vector database.** NumPy needs no service, no network hop and is exact, but has no persistence, filtering, multi-tenant isolation or concurrent writes. Past one process, millions of vectors or per-user filters, a vector database wins.","**One CPU process vs scale.** Once arrays exceed RAM or you need GPUs or autograd, PyTorch, memory-mapped arrays or a vector database take over."],"pitfalls":["**Python loops over array elements.** Symptom: a NumPy script no faster than pure Python; a profiler shows time in the interpreter, not in NumPy calls. Fix: vectorize.","**Accidental broadcasting of `(N,)` against `(N, 1)`.** Symptom: an unexpectedly huge array, a `MemoryError`, or a plausible-looking metric averaged over N² pairs. Fix: `keepdims=True` or reshape.","**Modifying a slice and corrupting the source.** Symptom: embeddings or memories change \"by themselves\" after an unrelated function ran. Fix: `.copy()` before mutating.","**Integer overflow in fixed-width dtypes.** Symptom: negative counts or wrapped pixel values with no warning. Fix: widen the dtype before arithmetic.","**Default float64 or one float64 operand.** `np.array(list_of_floats)` for embeddings returned by an API as Python lists, or a float64 operand promoting a float32 pipeline. Symptom: double the expected memory and slower matrix products, visible in `arr.dtype` and `arr.nbytes`.","**Mixing embedding models in one array.** Symptom: retrieval quality drops after a model upgrade with no error, because old and new vectors are not comparable even at the same dimension. Fix: version vectors by model and re-embed.","**Transposed or sliced arrays into a C extension.** Symptom: an explicit \"array is not C-contiguous\" error or, with careless bindings, garbage results. Fix: `np.ascontiguousarray` at the boundary."],"when":"- **Use when:** anything numerical that fits in memory on one machine: embedding similarity, tool\n  routing, prototype agent memory, retrieval metrics, feature arrays for scikit-learn, image and audio\n  buffers, and interchange between libraries.\n- **Think shapes first:** write the expected shape next to each line.\n- **Switch when:** data is tabular with named, mixed-type columns (pandas or Polars); you need GPUs or\n  gradients (PyTorch); exact search over millions of vectors is too slow (a vector index).\n- **Measure:** with `nbytes`, a profiler and `%timeit` rather than guessing.\n- **Interviews:** often ask you to vectorize a loop or explain broadcasting.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Vectorizes scoring and metrics, tracks shapes and dtypes explicitly, and uses float32 and `argpartition` for retrieval prototypes.","architect":"Uses `n × d × bytes` arithmetic to size in-memory indexes and decides when exact NumPy search must give way to an approximate vector store.","fde":"Loads a customer's embedding dump with NumPy, checks norms, dtypes and NaNs, and runs exact search as a ground-truth baseline for their vector database."},"check":{"q":"You need cosine similarity between 50,000 queries and 1,000,000 documents, both 768-d float32.\nA colleague writes `Q @ D.T` in one line. What happens and what would you do?","a":"The result matrix is 50,000 × 1,000,000 float32 values, 200 GB, so it will not fit in memory, on\ntop of 3 GB for D and about 154 MB for Q. Normalize both once, then process queries in batches (say\n1,000 at a time, a 4 GB result per batch, or smaller), and for each batch take `argpartition` top-k per\nrow and keep only those indices and scores, discarding the full block. If this runs regularly, an\napproximate nearest-neighbour index is the right tool, with the exact batched computation kept as the\nrecall baseline."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which operation returns a view sharing memory with the original array `a` of shape (1000, 64)?","options":["`a[[0, 10, 20]]`","`a[a[:, 0] > 0]`","`a[:, ::2]`","`np.sort(a, axis=0)`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Basic slicing with step sizes only changes offset and strides, so it shares the buffer. Integer and boolean indexing must gather scattered rows into new memory, and sort returns a new array."},{"q":"`x` has shape (500,) and `y` has shape (500, 1). What is the shape of `x - y`?","options":["(500,)","(500, 1)","(500, 500)","An error, because the shapes differ"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Broadcasting aligns trailing dimensions: (1, 500) against (500, 1) stretches both to (500, 500). No error is raised, which is why this silent shape blow-up is a common bug in metric code."},{"q":"Why is `np.argpartition` preferred over `np.argsort` for top-5 retrieval over a million scores?","options":["It returns the results already sorted by score","It finds the k best in linear time without fully sorting the array","It runs on the GPU automatically","It handles NaN scores while argsort cannot"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A full sort is O(N log N) work for an answer that needs only k items. Partitioning is linear and leaves the top k unordered, so you sort just those k afterwards, which is cheap."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pandas","sources":["McKinney, 2010 — Data Structures for Statistical Computing in Python","Official docs: pandas"],"why":"- **Agent data is mixed-type rows.** An eval run produces a case ID, model name, category string, pass/fail\n  boolean, latency, token counts, steps taken, tools called and a timestamp. NumPy arrays hold one dtype\n  and address data by integer position.\n- **The questions are tabular.** \"Pass rate and p95 latency per model per category\", \"error rate per\n  tool\", join in a price table, filter to failures to read them.\n- **The naive tools fail.** Lists of dicts mean hand-written loops for every group-by and join, slow and\n  easy to get wrong; raw NumPy means keeping parallel arrays aligned by hand.\n- **SQL is friction here.** Loading data into a database for a ten-minute analysis is overhead, and SQL is\n  awkward for the iterative, look-at-it-and-adjust workflow of a notebook.\n- **pandas brought R's data-frame model into Python.** Named columns of different types, row labels and a\n  large vocabulary of vectorized operations, in process with your other code, which is why so much of the\n  ecosystem, from scikit-learn inputs to plotting libraries, accepts DataFrames.","how":"### Columns, dtypes and the index\n- **Structure.** An ordered collection of columns (each a `Series`) sharing one `Index` of row labels.\n- **Storage.** Each column is a NumPy array or an extension array, so numeric columns get NumPy speed.\n\n| Column kind | Storage | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Numeric | NumPy array | Compiled speed |\n| Strings, historically | `object`: pointers to Python objects | Slow and memory-hungry; trace text (prompts, tool outputs) lives here |\n| Strings, newer | Dedicated string dtypes | pandas 3.0 makes a string dtype the default for text |\n| Arrow-backed (since pandas 2.0, optional) | `dtype_backend=\"pyarrow\"` | Arrow's columnar format |\n\n### Missing values\n- **Float columns use `NaN`,** so an integer column that gains one missing value silently becomes\n  `float64` (a historical compromise), for example `tokens` on a step that timed out.\n- **Nullable dtypes (`Int64`, `boolean`, `string`)** use a separate mask instead, keeping the type.\n\n### Alignment\n- **Arithmetic aligns on index *labels*, not positions.** `a + b` matches rows with the same label and\n  yields `NaN` where a label exists on only one side.\n- **Why.** It was designed for time series, where aligning by date is what you want.\n- **The cost.** On freshly filtered frames with gappy indexes it causes silent NaNs, which is why\n  `reset_index(drop=True)` appears so often.\n\n### Vectorized operations and their escape hatch\n- **Column expressions** such as `df[\"cost\"] = df[\"tokens\"] / 1000 * df[\"price\"]` run as NumPy operations\n  over whole columns.\n- **`df.apply(f, axis=1)`** calls a Python function per row, bringing back interpreter speed: a last resort.\n- **String and datetime operations** live under `.str` and `.dt`, for example\n  `df[\"tool_output\"].str.contains(\"timeout\")` to tag failed steps.\n\n### Group-by, joins, reshaping\n| Tool | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `groupby` | Split-apply-combine: split rows by key, aggregate per group, combine into a new frame |\n| Named aggregation `agg(pass_rate=(\"passed\", \"mean\"))` | Keeps output columns explicit |\n| `merge` | SQL-style joins; `validate=` checks cardinality so a duplicate key cannot silently multiply rows |\n| `pivot_table` / `melt` | Switch between wide and long shapes, e.g. one column per model for a side-by-side eval |\n\nHow `groupby` runs:\n1. **Factorize** the key columns: one hash pass maps each distinct key to an integer group code.\n2. **Aggregate.** Built-ins named by string (`\"mean\"`, `\"sum\"`, `\"size\"`) run a compiled loop adding each\n   row into its group's accumulator, so cost is roughly linear in rows whatever the number of groups.\n3. **Custom functions are slower.** A lambda is called once per group with a Series: fine for tens of\n   groups, slow for a million (one group per agent run is easily that many).\n\nTwo defaults matter: `dropna=True` drops rows whose key is missing, and for categorical keys `observed=`\ndecides whether empty categories appear as rows.\n\n### Indexing, copies and views\n- **Select and assign with `.loc[row_mask, \"col\"]`** for labels, `.iloc` for positions.\n- **Chained indexing** such as `df[mask][\"col\"] = x` may assign into a temporary copy and leave `df`\n  unchanged, the origin of the `SettingWithCopyWarning`.\n- **pandas 3.0 made Copy-on-Write the default.** Every derived object behaves as a copy and real copying\n  is deferred until a write.\n- **That removes the ambiguity in one direction.** Chained assignment *never* modifies `df`, and pandas\n  emits a `ChainedAssignmentError` warning instead. Code for pandas 2.x that relied on chained assignment\n  silently stops working.\n\n### Execution model\n- **Eager.** Each operation materializes a full result immediately, mostly on a single core.\n- **RAM rule of thumb.** With intermediates, have several times the dataset's size available; traces with\n  full prompts and tool outputs are mostly text and get large fast.\n- **Bigger than memory?** Chunked reading (`chunksize=`), a columnar engine such as Polars or DuckDB, or a\n  database.","example":"### Agent trace analysis: which tool fails, and what does a run cost?\nOne row per agent step, as exported from a tracing tool or your own JSONL. Two group-bys answer the\nquestions an agent team asks every week.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\nsteps = pd.DataFrame({       # in practice: pd.read_json(\"traces.jsonl\", lines=True)\n    \"run_id\":   [\"r1\", \"r1\", \"r1\", \"r2\", \"r2\", \"r3\", \"r3\", \"r3\", \"r3\"],\n    \"step\":     [1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4],\n    \"tool\":     [\"search\", \"lookup_order\", None, \"search\", None,\n                 \"lookup_order\", \"lookup_order\", \"lookup_order\", None],\n    \"status\":   [\"ok\", \"error\", \"ok\", \"ok\", \"ok\", \"error\", \"error\", \"ok\", \"ok\"],\n    \"in_tokens\":  [3000, 3600, 4100, 2900, 3400, 3100, 3700, 4300, 4900],\n    \"out_tokens\": [80, 60, 220, 70, 180, 50, 50, 60, 240],\n}).astype({\"tool\": \"string\", \"status\": \"category\"})\n\nper_tool = (steps.dropna(subset=[\"tool\"])\n                 .groupby(\"tool\")\n                 .agg(calls=(\"step\", \"size\"),\n                      error_rate=(\"status\", lambda s: (s == \"error\").mean()))\n                 .reset_index())\n\nper_run = (steps.groupby(\"run_id\")\n                .agg(n_steps=(\"step\", \"max\"), in_tokens=(\"in_tokens\", \"sum\"),\n                     out_tokens=(\"out_tokens\", \"sum\"))\n                .reset_index())\nper_run[\"cost_usd\"] = (per_run[\"in_tokens\"] / 1000 * 0.00015\n                       + per_run[\"out_tokens\"] / 1000 * 0.0006)   # illustrative prices\nprint(per_tool, per_run, sep=\"\\n\")\n```\n\n| Finding | Number | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lookup_order` error rate | 3 of 4 calls = 0.75 | Read those tool results; likely a bad argument schema |\n| Run `r3` | 4 steps, 16,000 input tokens | Two retries of the same failing tool added two steps of re-sent context |\n| Input tokens per step | grows ~500-600 per step | The transcript grows each step; see prompt caching |\n\n### Eval summary per model and category\nSummarising an agent eval run: pass rate, tail latency and cost per model and category.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({          # in practice: pd.read_json(\"results.jsonl\", lines=True)\n    \"case_id\":    [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3],\n    \"model\":      [\"small\", \"large\"] * 3,\n    \"category\":   [\"refund\", \"refund\", \"billing\", \"billing\", \"refund\", \"refund\"],\n    \"passed\":     [False, True, True, True, False, True],\n    \"latency_ms\": [420, 1310, 380, 1250, 450, 1400],\n    \"out_tokens\": [120, 180, 90, 150, 130, 200],\n})\nprice = pd.DataFrame({\"model\": [\"small\", \"large\"],\n                      \"usd_per_1k_out\": [0.0006, 0.01]})     # illustrative prices\n\ndf = df.merge(price, on=\"model\", how=\"left\", validate=\"many_to_one\")\ndf[\"cost_usd\"] = df[\"out_tokens\"] / 1000 * df[\"usd_per_1k_out\"]   # column math, no loop\n\nsummary = (df.groupby([\"model\", \"category\"])\n             .agg(pass_rate=(\"passed\", \"mean\"),\n                  p95_ms=(\"latency_ms\", lambda s: s.quantile(0.95)),\n                  cost_usd=(\"cost_usd\", \"sum\"),\n                  n=(\"case_id\", \"size\"))\n             .reset_index())\nprint(summary)\n\n# Wrong: chained indexing may write into a temporary copy\n# df[df[\"model\"] == \"small\"][\"passed\"] = True\ndf.loc[df[\"model\"] == \"small\", \"reviewed\"] = True             # right: one .loc call\nfailures = df.loc[~df[\"passed\"], [\"case_id\", \"model\", \"category\"]]\n```\n\n| model | category | pass_rate | cost_usd |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| small | refund (2 cases) | 0.0 | 0.00015 |\n| small | billing | 1.0 | |\n| large | refund | 1.0 | (180 + 200) / 1000 × 0.01 = 0.0038 |\n| large | billing | 1.0 | |\n\nThat is the shape of a real model-choice discussion: the cheap model fails one category, so route that\ncategory to the large one.\n\n### Worked scenario: loading a large usage log without exhausting memory\nA token-usage export from a busy agent fleet is too big to load with default dtypes. Read only needed\ncolumns, pin compact dtypes, and aggregate chunk by chunk.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\ncols = [\"ts\", \"model\", \"route\", \"out_tokens\", \"latency_ms\"]\ndtypes = {\"model\": \"category\", \"route\": \"category\",\n          \"out_tokens\": \"int32\", \"latency_ms\": \"float32\"}\n\nparts = []\nfor chunk in pd.read_csv(\"usage.csv\", usecols=cols, dtype=dtypes,\n                         parse_dates=[\"ts\"], chunksize=1_000_000):\n    parts.append(chunk.groupby([\"model\", \"route\"], observed=True)\n                      .agg(calls=(\"latency_ms\", \"size\"), out_tokens=(\"out_tokens\", \"sum\")))\n\nusage = pd.concat(parts).groupby(level=[\"model\", \"route\"], observed=True).sum()  # combine partial sums\nprint(usage.sort_values(\"out_tokens\", ascending=False).head())\n```\n\nWhy the dtypes matter, per 10 million rows (bytes are exact for fixed-width types):\n\n| Column | Default dtype | Pinned dtype | Memory |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `out_tokens` | int64, 8 B/row = 80 MB | int32, 4 B/row | 40 MB |\n| `latency_ms` | float64 = 80 MB | float32 | 40 MB |\n| `model` (a handful of values) | `object`: 8 B pointer per row plus Python string objects (~50 B each) | `category`: 1 B code per row | ~10 MB |\n\nCheck the real numbers with `df.memory_usage(deep=True)`.","patterns":["**Weekly trace error analysis.** Export a sample of production traces, group by tool and by category, read the failing rows, and file fixes against the tool or prompt. Track per-tool error rate and steps per run week over week; a rising step count often means a tool that silently started failing.","**Pin dtypes and columns at read time.** Pass `usecols=`/`dtype=` to `read_csv`, or `columns=` to `read_parquet`, and use `category` for repeated strings such as tool and model names. Log `df.memory_usage(deep=True).sum()` per stage; a jump means a column fell back to `object` or `float64`.","**Guard every join.** Use `merge(..., validate=\"many_to_one\")` and assert the row count is unchanged after a left join to a price, label or rubric table. Monitor rows-before versus rows-after in pipeline logs, since inflated totals are the classic silent failure.","**Nullable IDs everywhere.** Read ID columns as `Int64` or `string` (`dtype={\"case_id\": \"Int64\"}`, or `df.convert_dtypes()`), so one missing value cannot turn IDs into `1234.0`. Watch join match rates.","**Opt into Copy-on-Write before upgrading.** On pandas 2.x set `pd.set_option(\"mode.copy_on_write\", True)` in tests and fix every chained assignment it flags. Track `ChainedAssignmentError` and `SettingWithCopyWarning` counts in CI output; aim for zero.","**Hand off as Parquet, not CSV.** Write `df.to_parquet(path, index=False)` so dtypes survive the trip to the next notebook or job. Monitor file sizes and read times, and keep `dropna=False` explicit in report group-bys so missing categories (and steps with no tool) stay visible."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent quality is found in the traces: one row per step or per run with\n  tool name, status, latency, tokens and cost. Error analysis (\"which tool errors most in the refund\n  category?\") is one `groupby`, and model-choice decisions come from the summary table.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Notebooks over trace exports from LangSmith, Langfuse or your own\n  JSONL logs; eval harness outputs; cost reports; scikit-learn inputs. Some agents also expose a\n  DataFrame as a tool, such as LangChain's experimental pandas agent, which executes model-written Python\n  and needs a sandbox.\n- **What you control.** Dtypes at read time, `validate=` on every join (a price or label table), explicit\n  `dropna=False` in reports, and vectorized column math instead of `apply`.\n- **What you can skip.** MultiIndex gymnastics, time-series resampling and most of the API. Reading,\n  joining, grouping and safe assignment cover agent analysis.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agent error analysis.** Group step-level traces by tool and status to find which tool fails most,\n  then filter to those rows and read the transcripts.\n- **Model comparison.** Pass rate, p95 latency and cost per run, per model and per category, from one\n  eval results table.\n- **Cost reports.** Join token counts to a per-model price table with `validate=\"many_to_one\"` and sum\n  cost per tenant or per day.\n- **Building eval sets.** Sample failed production runs by category into a labelled dataset for the next\n  eval round.","mini_project":"**Error analysis notebook over agent traces (1-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn a JSONL trace export of an agent into a table that says where it fails and what it\n  costs.\n- **Steps:** load the traces with explicit dtypes; compute per-run steps, tokens and pass or fail; join\n  a price table with `validate=`; group by model and category with `dropna=False`; list the five tools\n  with the highest error rate; pull ten failing runs for manual reading.\n- **Done when:** one summary table shows pass rate, p95 latency and cost per model and category, the\n  join raises if the price table has duplicate keys, and you have written down two concrete fixes from\n  the failing runs."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Read trace JSONL, pinned dtypes\"] --> J[\"merge, validate=many_to_one\"]\n    J --> C[\"Vectorized column math\"]\n    C --> G[\"groupby: keys to group codes\"]\n    G --> Q{\"Aggregation kind?\"}\n    Q -->|\"built-in: mean, sum, size\"| F[\"Compiled loop, linear in rows\"]\n    Q -->|\"lambda or custom\"| S[\"One Python call per group\"]\n    F --> O[\"Summary frame, reset_index\"]\n    S --> O\n    O --> P[(\"Parquet hand-off\")]","caption":"A typical agent-trace analysis pipeline, with the group-by split shown: built-in aggregations stay in\ncompiled code, while a lambda drops back to one Python call per group."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a DataFrame, and why reach for pandas instead of lists of dicts or raw NumPy?","a":"- **Definition:** an ordered set of typed columns, each a `Series`, sharing one `Index` of row labels,\n  with numeric columns stored as NumPy arrays.\n- **Why:** eval and log data are mixed-type rows, and the questions (pass rate per model per category,\n  join a price table) are one `groupby` or `merge` instead of hand-written loops or parallel arrays kept\n  aligned by hand.\n- **Ecosystem:** scikit-learn, plotting libraries and most readers accept or produce DataFrames."},{"q":"Your agent's average cost per run rose 40% this week. How would you use traces to find out why?","a":"- **Shape the data:** load one row per step with run ID, tool, status and token counts; aggregate per run\n  (steps, input and output tokens, cost) and compare the two weeks' distributions, not just means.\n- **Slice:** group by tool and status to find a tool whose error rate rose, since failing tools cause\n  retries and longer runs; group by route or category to see whether traffic mix changed.\n- **Confirm in rows:** read the failing traces themselves before fixing, then add those cases to the eval\n  set so the regression is caught next time."},{"q":"Cost totals inflated after a join. How do you find and prevent the cause?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the key is not unique on the side you assumed, so a left join multiplied rows.\n- **Find it:** compare row counts before and after the merge, and check duplicate keys with\n  `df.duplicated(subset=[key]).sum()` on the lookup table.\n- **Prevent it:** pass `validate=\"many_to_one\"` so pandas raises instead of multiplying rows, and log\n  rows-before versus rows-after in the pipeline."},{"q":"Why does `df[mask][\"col\"] = x` fail to update `df`, and what changed in pandas 3.0?","a":"- **Chained indexing:** the first `[]` may return a temporary copy, so the assignment lands there and\n  `df` is unchanged; pandas 2.x warns with `SettingWithCopyWarning`.\n- **pandas 3.0 Copy-on-Write:** every derived object behaves as a copy, so chained assignment never\n  modifies `df` and pandas emits `ChainedAssignmentError` instead.\n- **Fix:** one `.loc[mask, \"col\"] = x` call; enable `mode.copy_on_write` on 2.x in tests before upgrading."},{"q":"Would you give an agent a pandas DataFrame as a tool? What are the risks?","a":"- **The appeal:** a model writing pandas code can answer open-ended questions over a table without you\n  pre-building every query.\n- **The risk:** it is arbitrary code execution driven by model output (and by any text the model read),\n  so it needs a sandbox with no secrets, no network and resource limits.\n- **The usual alternative:** a few typed tools (filter, aggregate by column, top-n) with validated\n  arguments, which are safer and easier to evaluate when the question types are known."},{"q":"A 10-million-row usage CSV exhausts memory. How do you cut it down, and when would you leave pandas?","a":"- **Read less:** pass `usecols=` for the needed columns and `chunksize=` to aggregate chunk by chunk.\n- **Pin dtypes:** `int32` and `float32` take 40 MB per column instead of 80 MB in int64 or float64, and a\n  repeated string as `category` costs about 1 byte per row; `df.memory_usage(deep=True)` shows offenders.\n- **Leave pandas when:** data nears RAM size or the pipeline repeats (Polars or DuckDB optimize lazily and\n  stream), or data is shared and concurrently updated (a database)."}],"tradeoffs":["**Huge API vs consistency.** Most tasks are one line away, but there are many ways to do the same thing and inconsistent historical defaults, so code quality varies wildly across a team.","**Eager vs lazy.** Eager execution is easy to inspect cell by cell, yet materializes every intermediate; near RAM size, lazy engines such as Polars or DuckDB optimize and stream instead.","**Label alignment vs surprise NaNs.** Ideal for time series, but on ad-hoc tables it silently introduces NaNs, so many teams treat the index as a plain row number and join explicitly.","**`object` flexibility vs cost.** It holds anything and tolerates messy data (nested tool arguments, long outputs) at the cost of speed and memory; converting to string, categorical or Arrow dtypes flips that once data grows.","**DataFrame as an agent tool vs safety.** Letting the model write pandas code answers open questions over a table, but it is arbitrary code execution; a fixed set of query tools is safer when the questions are known."],"pitfalls":["**`apply(axis=1)` or `iterrows()` for arithmetic.** Symptom: a cell taking minutes on a few million rows where the vectorized expression takes under a second. Fix: column expressions, `map` or `merge`.","**Chained assignment like `df[mask][\"col\"] = x`.** Symptom: `SettingWithCopyWarning` (pandas 2.x) or `ChainedAssignmentError` (pandas 3.x, Copy-on-Write) and a frame unchanged afterwards. Fix: one `.loc`.","**Merging on a key not unique on the side you assumed.** Symptom: row count grows after a join and totals inflate. Fix: `validate=\"many_to_one\"` catches it.","**One missing value turning an ID column into `float64`.** Symptom: IDs printed as `1234.0` and failed joins against integer keys. Fix: nullable `Int64`.","**A multi-gigabyte CSV with default dtypes.** Symptom: memory exhaustion. Fix: specify dtypes, read only needed columns, or switch to Parquet; `df.memory_usage(deep=True)` shows which columns (usually `object` strings) are responsible.","**Grouping a column with missing values under default `dropna=True`.** Symptom: per-group counts sum to fewer rows than the frame, because cases with no category (or steps with no tool, the final answer) vanished from the report. Fix: pass `dropna=False` or fill the key first."],"when":"- **Use when:** exploratory analysis and reporting on data that fits comfortably in memory: agent traces,\n  eval results, annotation exports, token and cost logs, small training sets, anything feeding\n  scikit-learn or a plot. It is also the lingua franca for handing tables between libraries.\n- **Prefer Polars or DuckDB when:** data approaches RAM size, a pipeline runs repeatedly and speed\n  matters, or you want lazy, query-optimized execution.\n- **Prefer a database when:** data is shared, updated concurrently or needs durability, such as live agent\n  state or memory.\n- **Interviews:** expect group-by, joins, vectorization and why chained assignment fails.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Loads JSONL eval and trace data into DataFrames, slices failures by category, and writes vectorized, `.loc`-based transformations that survive Copy-on-Write.","architect":"Decides where notebook-scale pandas analysis ends and production pipelines in Polars, Spark or SQL begin, and standardizes Parquet as the hand-off format.","fde":"Pulls a customer's CSV or ticket export into pandas on day one to profile volumes, categories and data quality before scoping the solution."},"check":{"q":"A notebook computes per-ticket cost with `df.apply(lambda r: price[r.model] * r.tokens, axis=1)`\nover 5 million rows and takes many minutes. Why is it slow and how do you fix it?","a":"`apply` with `axis=1` builds a Series for each row and calls the Python lambda 5 million times,\nso the work runs in the interpreter rather than in compiled column operations. Fix it by expressing the\nlookup as a join or a vectorized map: `df[\"unit\"] = df[\"model\"].map(price)` (or merge a price table),\nthen `df[\"cost\"] = df[\"unit\"] * df[\"tokens\"]`, both of which operate on whole columns. Also convert\n`model` to a categorical dtype if it repeats, and check the merge or map left no NaNs for unknown models."},"quiz":[{"q":"`a` has index [0, 1, 2] and `b` has index [1, 2, 3]. What does `a + b` return?","options":["An error, because the indexes differ","Three values computed by position","Four rows labelled 0 to 3, with NaN for labels present on only one side","Two rows, labels 1 and 2 only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"pandas aligns on labels and takes the union of the indexes, so labels 0 and 3 have no partner and become NaN. Positional addition requires `.to_numpy()` or resetting the indexes first."},{"q":"Why does `df[df.score < 0.5][\"flag\"] = True` often fail to modify `df`?","options":["Boolean masks are read-only in pandas","The first indexing step can return a new object, and the assignment goes into that temporary copy","The column must exist before assignment","pandas requires `inplace=True` for all assignments"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chained indexing performs two separate operations; the second writes into whatever the first returned, which may be a copy (under pandas 3's Copy-on-Write it always behaves as one). A single `df.loc[mask, \"flag\"] = True` modifies the original frame."},{"q":"After a merge, an eval summary reports more rows than there were test cases. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The merge defaulted to an outer join","groupby dropped NaN keys","The join key was duplicated in the right-hand table, multiplying matching rows","Copy-on-Write duplicated the frame"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A many-to-many match repeats each left row once per matching right row. Passing `validate=\"many_to_one\"` raises an error when the right side's keys are not unique, catching the problem at the join."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/polars","sources":["Official docs: Polars","Official docs: Apache Arrow"],"why":"- **pandas was designed for one core and a few hundred megabytes.** Each operation runs eagerly,\n  materializes a full intermediate, mostly on one core, and strings have historically been arrays of\n  Python objects.\n- **That breaks at trace scale.** Point it at 50 GB of agent traces to compute daily token usage per\n  model and you read every column of every file into memory, filter afterwards, and wait on one core\n  while the others idle.\n- **Fix one: an analytics memory layout.** Typed, contiguous columns with a proper string representation\n  and real null handling, so scans and aggregations are cache-friendly and vectorizable. Apache Arrow\n  defines exactly that.\n- **Fix two: a query engine, not a function library.** Seeing the whole computation first lets it skip\n  unused columns, apply filters while reading instead of after, and split work across threads.\n- **Databases have done this for decades.** Polars brings it to a DataFrame API inside a Python process,\n  with no server to run.","how":"### Expressions, not row functions\n- **Expressions describe a column computation, not a result:** `pl.col(\"latency_ms\").quantile(0.95)`,\n  `(pl.col(\"out_tokens\") / 1000 * pl.col(\"price\")).alias(\"cost\")`,\n  `pl.when(cond).then(a).otherwise(b)`.\n- **Contexts apply them:** `select`, `with_columns`, `filter`, and `group_by(...).agg(...)`.\n- **Why declarative.** Polars can run independent expressions in parallel and optimize them, which a\n  Python lambda executed per row would prevent.\n- **Escape hatch.** Python UDFs (`map_elements`) exist but drop you back to interpreter speed.\n\n### Eager and lazy\n| Call | Returns | When it reads |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pl.read_parquet` | `DataFrame` | Immediately, like pandas |\n| `pl.scan_parquet` (also `scan_csv`, `scan_ndjson`) | `LazyFrame` | Nothing until `.collect()`; each call adds a node to a logical plan |\n\nBefore executing, the optimizer applies rules such as:\n- **Projection pushdown:** only columns referenced anywhere in the plan are read from disk, so the large\n  prompt and tool-output columns of a trace are never touched by a cost rollup.\n- **Predicate pushdown:** filters move into the scan, so Parquet row groups whose statistics rule them\n  out can be skipped.\n- **Simplification and common-subexpression elimination**, so repeated expressions are computed once.\n\n`lf.explain()` prints the optimized plan, so you can verify what will be read.\n\n### Memory and parallelism\n- **Arrow-style buffers with a validity bitmap.** `null` is distinct from a float `NaN`, and an integer\n  column with missing values stays an integer column (a step with no token count stays an integer).\n- **Multi-threaded Rust, outside the GIL.** A group-by uses all cores without multiprocessing.\n- **Parallel group-by works like a distributed one.** Threads hash rows into partitions, build partial\n  aggregates independently, and merge them.\n- **So group order is not guaranteed** unless you pass `maintain_order=True` (which costs speed). That\n  matters for tests and reports that compare frames row by row.\n- **Larger than RAM.** A streaming engine processes the plan in batches, and `sink_parquet` writes a lazy\n  result straight to disk without materializing it. How you enable streaming has changed across\n  releases, so check the docs for your version.\n\n### No index, strict types\n- **No row index, no label alignment.** Rows are positions, and joins are explicit.\n- **Strict schemas.** Mixing types in a column or comparing incompatible dtypes raises an error rather\n  than silently becoming `object`. Agent traces are prone to this, because a tool's result field can be a\n  number in one step and an error string in the next.\n- **The deliberate trade:** fewer silent surprises, more errors up front.\n\n### Interop\n- **Arrow on both sides.** Conversion to and from pyarrow is typically zero-copy.\n- **`to_pandas()`** exists for libraries that only accept pandas. Many plotting and ML libraries accept\n  Polars frames directly now, but not all.","example":"### Daily token usage from agent trace files, read lazily\nDaily token usage per model from a directory of trace files, read lazily.\n\n```python\nimport polars as pl\n\nlf = (\n    pl.scan_parquet(\"traces/*.parquet\")                    # builds a plan; reads nothing\n      .filter(pl.col(\"ts\") >= pl.datetime(2026, 10, 1))\n      .filter(pl.col(\"status\") == \"ok\")\n      .with_columns(\n          (pl.col(\"output_tokens\") / 1000 * pl.col(\"usd_per_1k_out\")).alias(\"cost_usd\"))\n      .group_by(\"model\", pl.col(\"ts\").dt.date().alias(\"day\"))\n      .agg(\n          pl.len().alias(\"calls\"),\n          pl.col(\"input_tokens\").sum(),\n          pl.col(\"output_tokens\").sum(),\n          pl.col(\"cost_usd\").sum(),\n          pl.col(\"latency_ms\").quantile(0.95).alias(\"p95_ms\"),\n      )\n      .sort(\"day\", \"model\")\n)\nprint(lf.explain())   # both filters appear inside the Parquet scan; only 7 columns are projected\ndaily = lf.collect()  # multi-threaded execution happens here\ndaily.write_parquet(\"daily_usage.parquet\")\n```\n\nSuppose the trace files hold 40 columns, including large prompt and completion text, and only rows from\n1 October onward are needed:\n\n| | Eager pandas | Lazy Polars |\n|---|---|---|\n| Columns read | All 40, text included | 7 numeric or short columns |\n| Rows read | All, filtered afterwards | Row groups whose `ts` statistics fall before October are skipped |\n| Bytes read | The whole dataset | Can be a small fraction of it |\n\n### Per-tool health and steps per run, from step-level traces\nTwo rollups an agent team watches: which tools fail, and whether runs are getting longer.\n\n```python\nimport polars as pl\n\nsteps = pl.scan_parquet(\"traces/*.parquet\").filter(pl.col(\"ts\") >= pl.datetime(2026, 10, 1))\n\nper_tool = (steps.filter(pl.col(\"tool\").is_not_null())\n                 .group_by(\"tool\")\n                 .agg(pl.len().alias(\"calls\"),\n                      (pl.col(\"status\") == \"error\").mean().alias(\"error_rate\"),\n                      pl.col(\"latency_ms\").quantile(0.95).alias(\"p95_ms\"))\n                 .sort(\"error_rate\", descending=True))\n\nper_run = (steps.group_by(\"run_id\")\n                .agg(pl.len().alias(\"n_steps\"),\n                     pl.col(\"input_tokens\").sum().alias(\"in_tokens\"))\n                .select(pl.col(\"n_steps\").mean().alias(\"mean_steps\"),\n                        pl.col(\"n_steps\").quantile(0.95).alias(\"p95_steps\"),\n                        pl.col(\"in_tokens\").mean().alias(\"mean_in_tokens\")))\n\nprint(pl.collect_all([per_tool, per_run]))   # collects both plans in one call, in parallel\n```\n\n- **What to act on:** a tool at the top of `error_rate` with many calls, or a p95 step count creeping\n  toward your loop's step budget, which means runs are hitting the cap instead of finishing.\n\n### Worked scenario: turning agent transcripts into a clean fine-tuning set\nRaw JSONL with a field that is \"always an integer\" until it is not. An explicit schema fails fast and\npredictably, and the dedupe runs in parallel native code.\n\n```python\nimport polars as pl\n\nschema = {\"id\": pl.String, \"prompt\": pl.String, \"completion\": pl.String,\n          \"source\": pl.String, \"quality\": pl.Float64}\nlf = (\n    pl.scan_ndjson(\"raw/sft.jsonl\", schema=schema)       # no inference from the first rows\n      .filter(pl.col(\"quality\") >= 0.7)                  # judge or human grade per transcript\n      .with_columns(pl.col(\"prompt\").str.strip_chars().str.to_lowercase().alias(\"key\"))\n      .unique(subset=[\"key\"], keep=\"first\", maintain_order=True)   # deterministic output order\n      .drop(\"key\")\n)\nclean = lf.collect()\nprint(clean.height, clean.null_count())                  # rows kept, nulls per column\nclean.write_parquet(\"clean/sft.parquet\")\n```\n\n| Stage | Rows (illustrative) | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Raw agent transcripts | 120,000 | Every successful run last month |\n| `quality >= 0.7` (judge or human score) | 54,000 | Keep only runs graded as good |\n| Dedupe on normalised prompt | 31,000 | Popular requests repeat; duplicates overweight them |\n\n- **No `map_elements`:** normalisation uses built-in string expressions, so it stays parallel.\n- **`maintain_order=True`** keeps reruns diffable, at some speed cost.","patterns":["**Lazy scan, then read the plan.** Build trace and eval pipelines from `scan_parquet`/`scan_ndjson` and review `lf.explain()` whenever a pipeline changes, checking that filters and projections sit in the scan. Monitor bytes read and peak memory per run; a sudden jump usually means an early `.collect()` crept in.","**Explicit schemas at ingestion.** Pass `schema=` to scans of JSONL, or `schema_overrides=` for CSV, for every source you do not control, including traces exported from a tracing vendor. Track ingestion failures by source; a type error there beats a silently wrong column downstream.","**Split hot and cold trace columns.** Keep token counts, latencies, status and tool names in one set of files and prompt and tool-output text in another, joined by step ID, so dashboards never scan the text. Watch bytes read per dashboard query.","**Deterministic outputs.** End report pipelines with an explicit `.sort(...)` (or `maintain_order=True` where order matters) before writing or diffing. Monitor flaky report diffs and tests; they usually trace back to group order.","**Lay out Parquet for skipping.** Sort or partition files by the common filter key (date, customer, agent version) before `write_parquet`, so row-group statistics are tight and predicate pushdown can skip data. Watch row groups read versus total in your profiling.","**Pin the version and fail on deprecations.** Pin Polars in the lockfile and treat deprecation warnings as errors in CI, because names such as `groupby` became `group_by` and other APIs evolved quickly. Convert with `to_pandas()` or to Arrow only at library boundaries."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A production agent fleet writes one row per step, with prompts and tool\n  outputs attached; that reaches tens of gigabytes quickly. Daily cost per model, error rate per tool,\n  steps per run, and \"turn last month's good traces into a fine-tuning set\" are scheduled jobs over it.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Nightly rollups over trace Parquet or JSONL exports, eval\n  aggregation across thousands of runs, deduplicating and filtering documents before chunking and\n  embedding for RAG, and cleaning SFT data built from agent transcripts.\n- **What you control.** Lazy scans with explicit schemas, which columns the plan touches (skip the big\n  prompt text when you only need token counts), file layout for skipping, and deterministic ordering.\n- **What you can skip.** The streaming-engine internals and most of the API surface. Expressions,\n  `scan_*` plus `collect`, `explain()` and `group_by` are the working set.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Nightly trace rollups.** Daily tokens, cost and error rate per model and per tool over gigabytes of\n  step-level traces, reading only the columns the query needs.\n- **Fine-tuning set curation.** Filter last month's successful agent runs, deduplicate by prompt and\n  write the selected transcripts out for SFT.\n- **RAG corpus cleaning.** Drop empty and duplicate documents and normalize text before chunking and\n  embedding.\n- **Large eval aggregation.** Combine results from thousands of eval runs into per-model, per-category\n  scores without loading everything into memory.","mini_project":"**Lazy daily rollup over agent traces (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** compute daily cost and tool error rates from a large trace dataset with a lazy Polars query.\n- **Steps:** generate or export a few million step rows to Parquet (run ID, date, model, tool, status,\n  tokens, prompt text) with explicit column types; write a `scan_parquet` query that groups by date,\n  model and tool and ends with an explicit `.sort(...)`; check with `explain()` that the prompt text\n  column is not read; run it with `collect`.\n- **Done when:** the rollup is deterministic across two runs, `explain()` shows only the needed columns,\n  and you have timed it against the same query in eager pandas on the same files."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"scan_parquet on traces, nothing read\"] --> P[\"filter, with_columns, group_by\"]\n    P --> O[\"collect: optimizer rewrites plan\"]\n    O --> PP[\"Projection pushdown: needed columns\"]\n    O --> PR[\"Predicate pushdown: skip row groups\"]\n    PP --> X[\"Rust threads: partial aggregates, merged\"]\n    PR --> X\n    X --> Q{\"Output order matters?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| SO[\"sort or maintain_order=True\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| R[\"DataFrame or sink_parquet\"]\n    SO --> R","caption":"A lazy trace rollup is only a plan until `.collect()`; the optimizer pushes columns and filters into the\nscan before the parallel engine runs, and group order is not guaranteed unless you ask for it."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a Polars expression, and why is it faster than a pandas `apply` with a lambda?","a":"- **Definition:** an expression such as `pl.col(\"latency_ms\").quantile(0.95)` describes a column\n  computation; contexts like `select`, `with_columns`, `filter` and `group_by(...).agg(...)` apply it.\n- **Why faster:** because it is declarative, Polars can optimize it and run independent expressions in\n  parallel in Rust, outside the GIL, while a lambda runs once per row in the interpreter.\n- **The escape hatch costs the same:** `map_elements` with a Python function drops back to interpreter\n  speed and one core."},{"q":"Design the nightly job that turns raw agent traces into a cost and reliability dashboard.","a":"- **Ingest with a schema:** lazy `scan_parquet` or `scan_ndjson` with explicit types, filtered to the\n  previous day, so a tool that returned a string where a number was expected fails loudly at ingestion.\n- **Read only hot columns:** token counts, latency, status, tool, model and run ID; never the prompt or\n  output text, which projection pushdown leaves on disk.\n- **Roll up and order:** group by model and by tool for cost, error rate and p95 latency, and per run for\n  step counts; sort before writing so diffs are stable. Monitor bytes read and peak memory per run."},{"q":"A daily report diff fails intermittently on identical input. What is the likely cause?","a":"- **Group order:** a parallel `group_by` hashes rows into partitions per thread and merges partial\n  aggregates, so groups come back in a different order from run to run.\n- **Fix:** end the pipeline with an explicit `.sort(...)`, or pass `maintain_order=True` where order\n  matters, accepting some speed cost.\n- **Same idea for dedupe:** `unique(..., maintain_order=True)` keeps reruns diffable."},{"q":"After migrating to Polars, `drop_nulls()` leaves bad rows and an average comes out NaN. Why?","a":"- **Two kinds of missing:** Polars uses an Arrow validity bitmap, so `null` is distinct from a float\n  `NaN`; `drop_nulls()` removes only nulls.\n- **Where NaN comes from:** upstream float maths or a pandas source that used `NaN` for missing values.\n- **Fix:** handle both explicitly, and settle at ingestion which one the dataset uses for missing."},{"q":"How would you lay out Parquet files so Polars filters skip most of the data?","a":"- **Sort or partition by the common filter key,** such as date or customer, before `write_parquet`, so\n  each row group has tight min and max statistics.\n- **Then predicate pushdown can skip row groups** whose statistics rule them out; unsorted data gives wide\n  ranges and little skipping.\n- **Verify:** check `lf.explain()` for the filter inside the scan, and compare bytes read and peak memory\n  before and after the layout change."},{"q":"When would you stay with pandas or pick DuckDB instead of Polars?","a":"- **Stay with pandas:** small notebook data, code that depends on pandas-only libraries, or a team whose\n  skills make pandas cheaper; conversion with `to_pandas()` at each boundary also costs time.\n- **Pick DuckDB:** when the team prefers SQL for the same in-process analytical work.\n- **Move to a warehouse:** when many users share data and need concurrency control, durability or\n  access management; decide with wall time and peak memory measured on real data."}],"tradeoffs":["**Speed vs debuggability.** Lazy plans with pushdown and parallelism are fast on large data, but debugging means reading a query plan instead of inspecting intermediate frames; for small exploratory data, eager mode or pandas is just as quick to write.","**Strictness vs ingestion friction.** Strict types and no index remove pandas' silent alignment and dtype drift, at the cost of more upfront casting and errors on messy JSON or CSV, which agent traces usually are.","**Performance vs ecosystem.** Faster and leaner than pandas on most analytical workloads, yet younger, so some libraries still expect pandas and you pay a conversion at the boundary.","**No server vs no governance.** An in-process engine needs no server, but has no concurrency control, durability or access management; once many users share the data, a warehouse or database wins."],"pitfalls":["**pandas habits such as `map_elements` with Python lambdas.** Symptom: Polars code no faster than pandas and using one core. Fix: built-in expressions like `when/then` or string methods.","**Calling `.collect()` early and continuing eagerly.** Symptom: memory spikes, because pushdown only applies to the plan built before collecting. Fix: stay lazy until the end.","**Confusing `null` and `NaN`.** Symptom: `drop_nulls()` leaves NaN rows behind, or averages come out NaN. Fix: handle both explicitly.","**Code copied from older blog posts.** Symptom: `AttributeError` or deprecation warnings because names such as `groupby` became `group_by` and other APIs evolved quickly. Fix: check the current docs.","**Relying on `group_by` output order.** Symptom: a test or daily-report diff fails intermittently with the same data, because groups come back in a different order each run. Fix: sort explicitly or pass `maintain_order=True`.","**Wide JSONL with inferred schemas.** Symptom: a type error deep in the file when an always-integer field suddenly holds a string, typically a tool result that returned an error message. Fix: pass an explicit schema."],"when":"- **Use when:** data is in the gigabytes; a transformation runs on a schedule (token and cost rollups,\n  eval aggregation, dataset deduplication and filtering before fine-tuning, corpus cleaning before RAG\n  ingestion); or pandas is running out of memory or time. Use lazy scans over Parquet whenever possible.\n- **Stay with pandas when:** small notebooks, code leaning on pandas-only libraries, or team skills make\n  it the cheaper choice.\n- **Consider DuckDB when:** the team prefers SQL; it occupies similar ground.\n- **Measure:** wall time and peak memory on your real data before migrating a pipeline.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes lazy Polars pipelines over Parquet trace and eval data, checks `explain()` for pushdown, and converts to pandas only at library boundaries.","architect":"Chooses between pandas, Polars, DuckDB and a warehouse based on data size, run frequency and sharing needs, and standardizes Arrow and Parquet as the interchange layer.","fde":"Processes a customer's large log export on a single VM without provisioning a cluster, getting usage and quality numbers within hours of receiving access."},"check":{"q":"A pandas job reads 30 GB of Parquet traces, filters to one customer, and sums tokens per day. It\nOOMs on a 64 GB machine. Why might a Polars lazy rewrite succeed, and what would you verify?","a":"As written, pandas reads every column of every file into memory before filtering (its\n`read_parquet` accepts `columns=` and `filters=`, but the naive job uses neither), and decompression,\nPython string objects and intermediates add more, so 30 GB on disk can exceed 64 GB in RAM. A lazy Polars plan built with `scan_parquet` pushes the\ncustomer filter and the column selection into the scan, so only the few needed columns are read and\nrow groups for other customers can be skipped using Parquet statistics. The aggregation then runs in\nparallel. I would verify with `explain()` that the filter and projection are in the scan, measure peak\nmemory, and if data is not sorted or partitioned by customer, expect less skipping and consider the\nstreaming engine."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does projection pushdown do in a Polars lazy query over Parquet?","options":["It moves the group-by to run before filters","It reads only the columns the query actually references","It caches the result for later queries","It converts the plan into SQL for an external database"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The optimizer sees the whole plan, so it knows which columns are used and asks the Parquet reader for only those. Predicate pushdown is the related rule that moves filters into the scan."},{"q":"Why can a Polars group-by use all CPU cores without multiprocessing?","options":["It forks one Python process per core automatically","It uses asyncio to schedule groups concurrently","The engine runs in Rust threads outside the Python interpreter, so the GIL does not serialize the work","It sends the work to a local Spark cluster"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Polars executes expressions in native code with its own thread pool. Python only builds the plan, so the GIL is not held during the heavy computation and parallelism needs no extra processes."},{"q":"A teammate's Polars pipeline is no faster than the pandas version. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Polars is slower than pandas for CSV files in general","Lazy mode adds planning overhead that dominates every query","The machine has too few cores for Rust code","Row-wise Python functions via `map_elements` force interpreter-speed execution"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Expressions run in native, parallel code, but a Python UDF must be called per element under the GIL. Rewriting the logic as built-in expressions such as `when/then` or string methods restores the speedup."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pyarrow","sources":["Official docs: Apache Arrow","Official docs: Apache Parquet"],"why":"- **Every tool had its own memory layout.** Moving a table from a database client to pandas, then Spark,\n  then a model's data loader meant converting each value at every hop: row-by-row serialization, often\n  through Python objects, that could cost more than the analysis itself.\n- **Text files waste reads.** CSV and JSONL store numbers as text with no types, so every read re-parses\n  every byte and must read every column even when the query needs two (say, IDs and embeddings, not the\n  chunk text).\n- **Arrow fixes memory with a shared spec.** If every library lays out a column the same way, a table\n  moves from C++ to Python to Rust by passing pointers to buffers.\n- **Parquet fixes storage.** A columnar file format with types, encodings, compression and statistics, so\n  a reader can skip columns and whole blocks of rows.\n- **They are complementary.** Parquet is optimized for being small on disk and selectively readable,\n  Arrow for fast in-memory compute, and pyarrow converts between them efficiently. You rarely write much\n  pyarrow directly, but it sits under most of your data stack.","how":"### The Arrow memory layout\nAn Arrow array is a few contiguous buffers:\n\n| Column type | Buffers |\n|---|---|\n| Fixed-width (`int64`, `float32`) | One values buffer + optional *validity bitmap*, one bit per row marking nulls |\n| Variable-length (`string`) | Adds an *offsets* buffer: row *i* is the bytes between `offsets[i]` and `offsets[i+1]` in one shared data buffer |\n| Nested (`list`, `struct`, `fixed_size_list`) | Compose the above |\n\n- **Nulls without type changes.** The validity bitmap is why missing values do not force a type change as\n  NaN does in pandas.\n- **Strings are three buffers,** not a million Python objects for a million strings.\n- **Embeddings fit naturally.** A fixed-size list of 768 float32 values is one contiguous float buffer\n  that converts to a NumPy `(n, 768)` array without copying.\n- **Nested traces fit too.** A step's tool arguments can be a `struct` and a run's steps a `list`, though\n  most teams flatten traces to one row per step for easier querying.\n\n### Tables, batches and chunks\n- **`RecordBatch`:** a set of equal-length arrays with a `Schema`.\n- **`Table`:** a schema plus columns that are `ChunkedArray`s (sequences of arrays), *because* tables are\n  built by concatenating batches from files or streams, and chunking avoids copying them into one buffer.\n- **Chunk-wise by default.** Many operations work chunk by chunk; `combine_chunks()` makes a single\n  contiguous array when a consumer needs one.\n\n### Sharing without copying\n- **In process.** The Arrow C Data Interface lets libraries exchange buffers by pointer, which is how\n  Polars, DuckDB and pandas' Arrow-backed dtypes interoperate cheaply.\n- **In Python,** this is increasingly exposed through the Arrow PyCapsule protocol (`__arrow_c_stream__`\n  and friends), so a library can accept \"anything Arrow-shaped\" without importing the producer.\n- **Zero-copy holds only while both sides keep the Arrow layout.** `table.to_pandas()` with default\n  settings turns a string column into an `object` column of Python strings: a full copy, often several\n  times larger. `to_pandas(types_mapper=pd.ArrowDtype)` keeps Arrow-backed columns instead.\n- **Across processes.** The Arrow IPC format (also called Feather v2 on disk) writes the in-memory layout\n  directly, so a file can be memory-mapped and read with almost no decoding.\n- **Hugging Face `datasets`** stores data as Arrow files and memory-maps them, which is why a dataset\n  larger than RAM can still be indexed randomly, for example an SFT set of agent transcripts.\n\n### Parquet on disk\n1. **Row groups.** A file is split into *row groups*, each holding a *column chunk* per column.\n2. **Encoding.** Dictionary encoding for repetitive values such as tenant IDs, tool names or model names;\n   run-length and bit-packing for small integers.\n3. **Compression.** Snappy by default in pyarrow, Zstandard for a better ratio.\n4. **Footer.** Stores the schema and per-column-chunk statistics such as min and max.\n5. **Selective reads.** Asked for two columns with a filter, a reader reads the footer, skips row groups\n   whose statistics cannot match, and fetches only the needed column chunks. That is why one file serves\n   cheap selective queries.\n\nThe `pyarrow.dataset` module extends this to directories of files, including hive-style partitions such\nas `tenant=acme/`, which prune whole files.\n\n### Compute\n- **`pyarrow.compute`** provides vectorized kernels (filters, arithmetic, string functions, aggregations)\n  over Arrow arrays.\n- **Light work only.** For analytical queries you usually reach for Polars or DuckDB on top of Arrow.","example":"### A RAG chunk-and-embedding table in Parquet\nStoring chunk text and embeddings for an agent's retrieval corpus in Parquet with an explicit schema, then\nreading back only what retrieval needs.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport pyarrow as pa\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\n\nn, d = 10_000, 384\nemb = np.random.default_rng(0).standard_normal((n, d), dtype=np.float32)  # 15.4 MB\n\nschema = pa.schema([\n    (\"doc_id\", pa.string()),\n    (\"tenant\", pa.string()),                       # Parquet dictionary-encodes it on disk\n    (\"chunk\", pa.large_string()),                  # 64-bit offsets: no 2 GB-per-array limit\n    (\"embedding\", pa.list_(pa.float32(), d)),      # fixed_size_list<float32>[384]\n])\ntable = pa.table({\n    \"doc_id\": [f\"doc-{i}\" for i in range(n)],\n    \"tenant\": [f\"t{i % 3}\" for i in range(n)],\n    \"chunk\": [f\"text of chunk {i}\" for i in range(n)],\n    \"embedding\": pa.FixedSizeListArray.from_arrays(pa.array(emb.ravel()), d),\n}, schema=schema)\n\npq.write_table(table, \"chunks.parquet\", compression=\"zstd\", row_group_size=2_000)\nprint(pq.ParquetFile(\"chunks.parquet\").metadata.num_row_groups)    # 5\n\nt = pq.read_table(\"chunks.parquet\", columns=[\"doc_id\", \"embedding\"],\n                  filters=[(\"tenant\", \"=\", \"t1\")])                 # chunk text never read\nvecs = t.column(\"embedding\").combine_chunks().flatten().to_numpy().reshape(-1, d)\nprint(t.num_rows, vecs.shape, vecs.dtype)                           # 3333 (3333, 384) float32\n```\n\nWhat the read skips depends on layout:\n\n| Layout | `chunk` column | Row groups read |\n|---|---|---|\n| As written (tenants interleaved) | Skipped entirely | All 5: every row group contains all three tenants |\n| Sorted by tenant before writing | Skipped | `t1` occupies rows 3,334 to 6,666 (0-based), in the 2nd, 3rd and 4th groups, so min/max statistics skip the other 2 of 5 |\n| Partitioned (`tenant=t1/` directories) | Skipped | Only `t1`'s files; the two-thirds of data for other tenants is never opened |\n\n### Worked scenario: rebuilding a tenant's vector index after an embedding-model change\nThe agent's retriever moves to a new embedding model. Parquet is the source of truth; the vector\ndatabase is rebuilt from it, one tenant at a time. The production version of the third row above: write\none directory per tenant with `ds.write_dataset(table, \"chunks/\", format=\"parquet\",\npartitioning=[\"tenant\"], partitioning_flavor=\"hive\", existing_data_behavior=\"overwrite_or_ignore\")`.\n\n| Step | What is read | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Re-embed tenant `t1` | `doc_id`, `chunk` from `chunks/tenant=t1/` only | New vectors need the text; other tenants' directories are never opened |\n| Write new vectors | A new `embedding_v2` column or a new dataset version | Old and new vectors are not comparable, so never mix them |\n| Load the vector DB | `doc_id`, `embedding_v2` | The large chunk text is skipped by projection |\n| Recall check | A sample of eval queries | Compare top-k against exact search before switching the agent over |\n\n- **The partition column lives in the path,** not in the files, and is restored on read.\n- **Watch cardinality.** Partition by a column with tens to thousands of values; one directory per\n  document would recreate the tiny-files problem.\n\n### Partitioned dataset for per-tenant reads\nRead one tenant with a filter that prunes whole files.\n\n```python\nimport pyarrow.dataset as ds\n\nds.write_dataset(table, \"chunks/\", format=\"parquet\",\n                 partitioning=[\"tenant\"], partitioning_flavor=\"hive\",   # chunks/tenant=t1/...\n                 existing_data_behavior=\"overwrite_or_ignore\")\n\ndset = ds.dataset(\"chunks/\", format=\"parquet\", partitioning=\"hive\")\nt1 = dset.to_table(columns=[\"doc_id\", \"embedding\"],\n                   filter=ds.field(\"tenant\") == \"t1\")                 # other directories never opened\nprint(t1.num_rows)                                                    # 3333\n```","patterns":["**Parquet as the source of truth for RAG.** Keep chunks, metadata and embeddings (with the embedding model's name per column or dataset version) in Parquet, and treat the vector database as a rebuildable serving index. Monitor row counts in Parquet versus the index after each load.","**Explicit schema on every write.** Define a `pa.schema` per table (with `large_string` for big text and `pa.list_(pa.float32(), d)` for embeddings) and pass it to `pa.table` or the writer. Monitor write-time type errors; they replace dataset reads failing weeks later on one odd file.","**Partition by the dominant filter.** Use `ds.write_dataset(..., partitioning=[\"tenant\"], partitioning_flavor=\"hive\")` for tenant or date filters, and sort within partitions by the next filter key. Watch files opened and bytes read per query.","**Compact small trace files on a schedule.** Streaming trace writers produce many tiny files; a periodic job rewrites them with `ds.write_dataset` using its `max_rows_per_file` and `min_rows_per_group` limits. Monitor file count and average file size per partition.","**Keep Arrow types across hand-offs.** Convert with `to_pandas(types_mapper=pd.ArrowDtype)`, or hand the table to Polars or DuckDB, instead of default `to_pandas()` on text-heavy tables. Track peak memory against `table.nbytes`.","**Zstandard for archives, Feather for local caches.** Write long-lived Parquet with `compression=\"zstd\"`; for scratch data reread many times on one machine, write Arrow IPC with `pyarrow.feather.write_feather` (pass `compression=\"uncompressed\"`, since its default LZ4 must be decoded on read) and memory-map it on read. Compare file size and read time per format."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The data around an agent outlives any one run: the chunk-and-embedding\n  table behind RAG, months of step-level traces, eval datasets and fine-tuning sets built from them. Stored\n  as Parquet with a schema, each is cheap to re-read selectively, for example only one tenant's vectors.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Under pandas and Polars Parquet I/O, Hugging Face `datasets` (Arrow\n  files, memory-mapped), DuckDB, LanceDB (built on Arrow), and the export or backfill jobs that load a\n  vector database from a Parquet source of truth.\n- **What you control.** Explicit schemas (`large_string` for chunk text, `fixed_size_list` for\n  embeddings), partitioning by tenant or date, row-group and file sizes, compression, and conversions\n  that keep zero-copy.\n- **What you can skip.** The C Data Interface details and most of `pyarrow.compute`; let Polars or DuckDB\n  do the queries.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **RAG source of truth.** Chunks, metadata and embeddings live in Parquet with a fixed schema, so a\n  vector database can be rebuilt or re-embedded from it at any time.\n- **Per-tenant agent memory exports.** Partitioning by tenant lets a job read or delete one tenant's\n  rows without touching the rest.\n- **Long-term trace storage.** Months of agent steps stored compressed and columnar, cheap to query for\n  only IDs, tools and token counts.\n- **Handing data between tools.** Polars, DuckDB and Hugging Face `datasets` read the same Arrow data,\n  often without copying.","mini_project":"**Parquet store for an agent's RAG chunks (1-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** keep a small agent's retrieval corpus in Parquet so its vector index can be rebuilt from disk.\n- **Steps:** chunk 200 documents; define an explicit schema with `large_string` for text, a\n  `fixed_size_list` of floats for embeddings and a tenant column; write a dataset partitioned by tenant;\n  read back only one tenant's IDs and embeddings; load them into an in-memory index and run a few queries.\n- **Done when:** the reread touches only the requested columns and tenant, the schema rejects an\n  embedding of the wrong length, and the rebuilt index returns the same top results as before."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"read_table: columns and filters\"] --> F[\"Footer: schema, min-max stats\"]\n    F --> RG{\"Row group can match?\"}\n    RG -->|\"no\"| SK[\"Skip row group\"]\n    RG -->|\"yes\"| CC[\"Fetch requested column chunks\"]\n    CC --> DE[\"Decode into Arrow buffers\"]\n    DE --> T[\"Arrow Table of ChunkedArrays\"]\n    T -->|\"zero-copy\"| L[\"Polars, DuckDB, NumPy vectors\"]\n    T -->|\"default to_pandas\"| C[\"Full copy: Python strings\"]","caption":"A selective read of a RAG chunk table: footer statistics decide which row groups are fetched, only the\nrequested column chunks are decoded, and the Arrow table moves on by pointer unless a conversion copies it."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between Arrow and Parquet, and why do you need both?","a":"- **Arrow is an in-memory columnar spec:** contiguous values buffers, a validity bitmap for nulls and\n  offsets for strings, so libraries share tables by passing pointers instead of serializing.\n- **Parquet is an on-disk columnar file format:** row groups, dictionary and run-length encoding,\n  compression and min and max statistics, so readers skip columns and row groups.\n- **pyarrow bridges them:** Parquet is small on disk and selectively readable, Arrow is fast to compute\n  on, and pyarrow converts efficiently between the two."},{"q":"How would you store the corpus behind a multi-tenant RAG agent so you can rebuild the vector index safely?","a":"- **Parquet as source of truth:** one dataset of chunk ID, tenant, text, metadata and embeddings, with an\n  explicit schema and the embedding model recorded per column or version.\n- **Partition by tenant:** `tenant=x/` directories, so re-embedding or reloading one tenant never opens\n  another's files, which also helps isolation audits.\n- **Rebuild, then verify:** load the vector database from the ID and embedding columns only, compare\n  counts, and run an exact-search recall check on eval queries before switching the agent over."},{"q":"Peak memory is several times `table.nbytes` after converting to pandas. Why, and what do you do?","a":"- **Default `to_pandas()` copies strings:** each Arrow string becomes a Python object in an `object`\n  column, a full copy that is often several times larger.\n- **Keep Arrow types:** use `to_pandas(types_mapper=pd.ArrowDtype)`, or hand the table to Polars or\n  DuckDB, which read Arrow buffers directly.\n- **Check other copies too:** `to_numpy()` on columns with nulls or several chunks also copies or fails;\n  `combine_chunks()` and null handling come first."},{"q":"Your agent's trace-ingestion job produces thousands of tiny Parquet files and dashboards are slow. What happened?","a":"- **Cause:** each file has its own footer to open and parse, so per-file overhead dominates and row\n  groups are too small to compress well or skip usefully.\n- **Fix:** a scheduled compaction job that rewrites partitions with `ds.write_dataset` using its\n  `max_rows_per_file` and `min_rows_per_group` limits.\n- **Monitor:** file count and average file size per partition, plus files opened per query."},{"q":"How do you choose row-group size and partitioning?","a":"- **Large row groups** compress better and scan faster; **small ones** skip at finer grain and use less\n  memory per read, so pick by whether workloads scan or seek.\n- **Statistics only help when data is sorted:** on unsorted files every row group's min and max overlap\n  the predicate, and a filtered read costs a full scan.\n- **Partition by the dominant filter,** such as `tenant=acme/`, with tens to thousands of values; one\n  directory per document recreates the tiny-files problem."},{"q":"A write fails with an offset overflow on a large text column. What is going on?","a":"- **32-bit offsets:** Arrow's `string` type stores row boundaries as 32-bit offsets into one shared data\n  buffer, so a single array's text cannot exceed about 2 GB.\n- **Fix:** declare the column as `pa.large_string()`, which uses 64-bit offsets, or write in smaller\n  chunks.\n- **Prevent it:** put `large_string` for chunk text in the explicit schema, as in the RAG example."}],"tradeoffs":["**Cheap selective reads vs immutability.** Parquet's compression and column pruning make selective reads cheap, but files are immutable and poor at single-row updates; frequently mutated data, such as live agent memory or session state, belongs in a database or a table format on top.","**Free hand-offs vs type matching.** Zero-copy only holds when types match; nulls, string columns or non-contiguous chunks can force a copy when converting to NumPy or pandas.","**Large vs small row groups.** Large ones compress better and scan faster; small ones allow finer skipping and lower memory when reading. The right size depends on whether workloads scan or seek.","**Explicit schemas vs maintenance.** They catch type drift at write time but must evolve with fields; inference is convenient until two files disagree and a dataset read fails.","**Parquet store vs vector database.** Parquet is cheap, portable and exact to rebuild from, but has no ANN index; serve queries from a vector database built from it."],"pitfalls":["**Many tiny Parquet files from a streaming job.** Symptom: slow reads dominated by opening files and parsing footers. Fix: compact into fewer, larger files.","**32-bit-offset `string` for huge text columns.** Symptom: an offset overflow error once one array's text exceeds about 2 GB, common with long documents or full tool outputs. Fix: `large_string` or smaller chunks.","**Expecting filters to skip data on unsorted files.** Symptom: a \"filtered\" read takes as long as a full scan, because row-group statistics overlap every predicate. Fix: sort or partition first.","**Letting schemas be inferred per file.** Symptom: a dataset read fails because one file has an int column as double or a field missing entirely. Fix: an explicit schema.","**Assuming `to_numpy()` is always zero-copy.** Symptom: an error or memory spike on columns with nulls or multiple chunks. Fix: `combine_chunks()` and handle nulls first.","**Default `to_pandas()` on a large text table.** Symptom: peak memory far above the Arrow table's `nbytes`, because every string became a Python object. Fix: `types_mapper=pd.ArrowDtype`."],"when":"- **Parquet via pyarrow by default** for any tabular dataset read more than once: eval results, traces,\n  processed training data, chunk and embedding tables for RAG.\n- **Arrow IPC or Feather** when you want fast, memory-mappable local files with no decoding.\n- **pyarrow directly** for explicit schemas, control over row groups and compression, or moving data\n  between libraries; otherwise let pandas, Polars or DuckDB call it for you.\n- **Keep JSONL** for human-readable interchange and append-only logs, and convert to Parquet for analysis.\n- **Measure:** file size, read time for typical queries and peak memory.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes Parquet with explicit schemas and `fixed_size_list` embeddings, reads only needed columns, and converts to NumPy without copies for scoring.","architect":"Makes Arrow and Parquet the interchange and storage contract across ingestion, training and analytics, including partitioning and file-size policy.","fde":"Asks the customer for a Parquet export instead of CSV, cutting transfer size and avoiding a week of type and encoding bugs."},"check":{"q":"Your embedding store is 20 million rows of text plus 768-d float32 vectors in one directory of\nParquet files. Retrieval for one tenant reads everything and is slow. What do you change, and why?","a":"Vectors alone are 20 M × 768 × 4 bytes, about 61 GB, so full scans are expensive. First, project:\nread only the ID and embedding columns, never the text, until results are chosen. Second, make filters\nskip data: partition the dataset by tenant (`tenant=x/` directories) or sort by tenant before writing so\nrow-group statistics exclude other tenants. Third, tune file and row-group sizes so there are not\nthousands of tiny files. If queries are frequent and latency matters, Parquet is the source of truth but\nnot the serving layer; build an ANN index per tenant from it."},"quiz":[{"q":"What lets a Parquet reader skip entire row groups when a query filters on `tenant = \"acme\"`?","options":["The validity bitmap marks irrelevant rows as null","Per-column-chunk min/max statistics in the file footer show no row in the group can match","Dictionary encoding stores each tenant in a separate file","Snappy compression indexes every distinct value"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Readers consult footer statistics before fetching data. If a row group's tenant range excludes \"acme\", it is skipped, which only works well when data is sorted or partitioned by that column."},{"q":"How does Arrow represent a column of one million variable-length strings?","options":["As one million Python string objects referenced by pointers","As fixed-width padded strings sized to the longest value","As an offsets buffer, a contiguous data buffer and an optional validity bitmap","As a compressed Parquet column chunk kept in memory"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Offsets mark where each string starts and ends inside one shared byte buffer, so the column is a few contiguous buffers that any Arrow-speaking library can read without conversion."},{"q":"What is the key difference between Arrow and Parquet?","options":["Arrow is row-oriented and Parquet is columnar","Arrow is an in-memory format for fast computation and exchange; Parquet is an encoded, compressed on-disk format","Parquet is a Python library and Arrow is a file format","They are the same format with different file extensions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both are columnar. Arrow optimizes random access and zero-copy sharing in memory, while Parquet uses encodings, compression and statistics to be small and selectively readable on disk."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/scikit-learn-basics","sources":["Pedregosa et al., 2011 — Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python","Buitinck et al., 2013 — API design for machine learning software: experiences from the scikit-learn project","Official docs: scikit-learn"],"why":"- **The same chores in every project.** Scale features, encode categories, vectorize text, fit a model,\n  tune hyperparameters, estimate performance on unseen data.\n- **Before a common library, glue everywhere.** Each algorithm had its own calling convention, so swapping\n  logistic regression for a random forest meant rewriting glue code, and every team rebuilt evaluation\n  loops by hand.\n- **Hand-rolled glue is where leakage lives.** Fit a scaler or TF-IDF vocabulary on the full dataset, then\n  split, and test-row statistics have shaped the training features. The reported score is optimistic, the\n  production score is not, and nobody notices until after launch.\n- **The answer is an API contract more than an algorithm collection.** Every component exposes the same\n  methods, learned state is separate from configuration, and pipelines, cross-validators and grid searches\n  treat any estimator uniformly. With preprocessing inside a pipeline, the library refits it per fold, so\n  the honest procedure becomes the easy one.\n- **The agent questions recur.** Is the LLM router better than a TF-IDF classifier? Can a logistic\n  regression on embeddings replace a per-request LLM call for intent detection or tool-set selection?","how":"### The estimator contract\n- **Constructor = hyperparameters only.** `LogisticRegression(C=1.0, max_iter=1000)` stores settings\n  and touches no data, *because* that lets tools clone an estimator and refit it on new folds.\n- **`fit(X, y)` learns state** and returns `self`. Learned attributes end with an underscore (`coef_`,\n  `classes_`, `vocabulary_`), so you can tell configuration from fitted state at a glance.\n- **Predictors** add `predict`, and usually `predict_proba` or `decision_function`; **transformers** add\n  `transform` (and `fit_transform`). `X` is a 2-D array-like of shape `(n_samples, n_features)` (NumPy,\n  sparse matrices, or pandas frames); `y` is 1-D.\n\n### Pipelines and column transformers\n1. **`Pipeline` (or `make_pipeline`)** chains transformers ending in a model, and is itself an estimator.\n2. **`fit`** fits each step on the training data and transforms it for the next step.\n3. **`predict`** only transforms, then predicts.\n\n- **`ColumnTransformer`** applies different preprocessing per column: one-hot encode `plan_tier`, scale\n  `tokens_used`, TF-IDF the `text`.\n- **Why one object.** Cross validation and deployment use exactly the same preprocessing, and nothing is\n  fit on held-out data; the router your agent calls in production is the one you evaluated.\n\n### Honest evaluation\n| Tool | What it guarantees |\n|---|---|\n| `train_test_split(..., stratify=y)` | Class proportions kept in both splits, which matters with imbalanced intents |\n| `cross_val_score` / `cross_validate` with `StratifiedKFold` | A mean and spread across folds, not one lucky split |\n| `GridSearchCV` / `RandomizedSearchCV` | Hyperparameters tuned by cross validation on the training set; the test set is touched once, at the end |\n| `GroupKFold` | All rows of one customer or conversation on the same side, preventing near-duplicate leakage |\n\nThe concepts (leakage, splits, imbalance) are covered in depth in the ML fundamentals module; here the\npoint is that the library encodes them.\n\n### Background: what the standard text baseline computes\nKnowing the math tells you what the baseline can and cannot learn.\n\n- **`TfidfVectorizer`** turns each document into a sparse vector with one dimension per vocabulary term\n  (and n-gram).\n- **Weights.** Term *t* in document *d* gets `tf(t, d) × idf(t)`. With the default `smooth_idf=True`:\n\n```\nidf(t) = ln((1 + n) / (1 + df(t))) + 1      n = training documents, df(t) = documents containing t\n```\n\n- **Rare terms get large weights** because they discriminate; terms in every document get weight near 1.\n- **`sublinear_tf=True`** replaces raw counts with `1 + ln(tf)`, so a word repeated ten times does not\n  count ten times. Each row is then L2-normalized so long tickets do not dominate.\n- **Both the vocabulary and *df* are learned state,** which is exactly why fitting the vectorizer on test\n  documents leaks.\n\n`LogisticRegression` then learns one weight per (class, feature):\n\n```\nz_k = w_k · x + b_k\np_k = exp(z_k) / Σ_j exp(z_j)        (multiclass softmax; predict returns the argmax)\n```\n\n- **Regularization.** Fitting minimizes log-loss plus an L2 penalty of strength `1/C`, so a smaller `C`\n  means stronger regularization, which matters when n-gram features far outnumber tickets.\n- **The ceiling.** The model is linear in word evidence: it cannot represent negation or word order beyond\n  the n-grams you gave it. That is where embeddings or an LLM start to earn their cost.\n\n### Metrics and baselines\n- **`sklearn.metrics`** provides `classification_report`, `confusion_matrix`, `f1_score`,\n  `roc_auc_score`, `precision_recall_curve` and regression metrics; many LLM eval harnesses call them\n  underneath rather than reimplementing them. In agent evals they score tool choice (predicted tool vs\n  expected tool), refusal decisions, and LLM-judge labels against human labels.\n- **`DummyClassifier`** gives the majority-class baseline every result should beat.\n\n### What it is not\n- **CPU, mostly in memory,** with no GPU deep learning.\n- **Persistence is version-bound.** Saving with `joblib` or pickle ties a model to the library version that\n  saved it, and loading untrusted pickles executes code.\n- **`skops`** exists to load models without arbitrary code execution when artifacts cross a trust boundary.","example":"### A router in front of the agent: classifier first, LLM agent on low confidence\nThe classifier handles confident, simple intents; only uncertain or complex requests reach the\ntool-calling agent.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import classification_report\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\n# X: (n_samples, dim) float32 embeddings of past requests; y: routed intent labels\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nX = rng.standard_normal((4000, 384)).astype(np.float32)\ny = rng.choice([\"billing\", \"faq\", \"order_status\", \"needs_agent\"], size=4000)\n\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2, stratify=y, random_state=42)\nrouter = CalibratedClassifierCV(\n    LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, class_weight=\"balanced\"), cv=5).fit(X_tr, y_tr)\nprint(classification_report(y_te, router.predict(X_te)))\n\nTHRESHOLD = 0.8          # choose from a precision-vs-coverage curve on validation data\n\ndef route(embedding: np.ndarray) -> str:\n    p = router.predict_proba(embedding.reshape(1, -1))[0]\n    label, conf = router.classes_[p.argmax()], p.max()\n    if label == \"needs_agent\" or conf < THRESHOLD:\n        return \"agent\"           # full tool-calling loop\n    return label                 # cheap handler: FAQ answer, status lookup, billing form\n```\n\n- **Random data here, real embeddings in practice:** with random features the classifier learns nothing,\n  which is itself a useful sanity check that the baseline catches.\n\n### Worked scenario: what the router saves\n10,000 requests a day; an agent run averages 4 model calls at an illustrative $0.01 per call.\n\n| Setup | Requests reaching the agent | Model-call cost per day |\n|---|---|---|\n| Every request runs the agent | 10,000 | 10,000 × 4 × $0.01 = $400 |\n| Router handles 60% confidently | 4,000 | 4,000 × 4 × $0.01 = $160 |\n\n- **The quality side.** If precision above the threshold measures 0.95, then 5% of the 6,000 requests\n  the router keeps are wrong: 300 bad cheap answers a day to review.\n- **The extra cost.** Each request still needs one embedding call for the router, usually a small\n  fraction of an agent run; include it in the comparison.\n- **The decision is the threshold.** Raising it sends more traffic to the agent (cost) and fewer wrong\n  cheap answers (quality). Monitor both.\n\n### Intent classifier baseline for support tickets\nAn intent classifier for support tickets, the kind of baseline you build before deciding an LLM router\nis needed.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyClassifier\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import classification_report\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import StratifiedKFold, cross_val_score, train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n\ndf = pd.read_json(\"tickets.jsonl\", lines=True)          # columns: text, intent\nX_tr, X_te, y_tr, y_te = train_test_split(\n    df[\"text\"], df[\"intent\"], test_size=0.2, stratify=df[\"intent\"], random_state=42)\n\npipe = make_pipeline(                                    # vectorizer is refit inside every fold\n    TfidfVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2), min_df=2, sublinear_tf=True),\n    LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, class_weight=\"balanced\"),\n)\ncv = StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)\nf1 = cross_val_score(pipe, X_tr, y_tr, cv=cv, scoring=\"f1_macro\")\nbase = cross_val_score(DummyClassifier(strategy=\"most_frequent\"), X_tr, y_tr,\n                       cv=cv, scoring=\"f1_macro\")\nprint(f\"model macro-F1 {f1.mean():.3f} ± {f1.std():.3f} | baseline {base.mean():.3f}\")\n\npipe.fit(X_tr, y_tr)                                     # final fit on all training data\nprint(classification_report(y_te, pipe.predict(X_te)))   # test set used once\nprobs = pipe.predict_proba([\"I was charged twice this month\"])[0]\nprint(dict(zip(pipe.classes_, probs.round(2))))\n```\n\nSuppose 4,000 tickets across 6 intents, with `billing` at 40% of them:\n\n| Metric for the dummy baseline | Value | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Accuracy | 40% | Always predicts `billing` |\n| `billing` F1 | 2 × 0.4 × 1.0 / (0.4 + 1.0) ≈ 0.57 | Precision 0.4, recall 1.0 |\n| Other 5 classes F1 | 0 | Never predicted |\n| Macro-F1 | ≈ 0.57 / 6, near 0.1 | One class partly right, five wrong |\n\n- **Macro-F1 is the headline metric** for exactly this reason.\n- **The decision.** If the pipeline reaches a macro-F1 close to an LLM prompt on the same test set, the\n  classifier is the cheaper, faster router.\n- **Swapping in embeddings is a small change:** drop `TfidfVectorizer` and pass the `(n_samples, dim)`\n  embedding matrix as `X`; splits, scoring and baseline stay identical, as the next example shows.\n\n### Grouped evaluation on precomputed embeddings\nSame task with sentence embeddings, evaluated so one customer's tickets never sit on both sides of a\nsplit.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GroupKFold, cross_validate\n\ndf = pd.read_parquet(\"tickets_embedded.parquet\")   # columns: customer_id, intent, embedding\nX = np.vstack(df[\"embedding\"].to_numpy()).astype(np.float32)   # (n_samples, dim)\ny, groups = df[\"intent\"].to_numpy(), df[\"customer_id\"].to_numpy()\n\nclf = LogisticRegression(C=1.0, max_iter=1000, class_weight=\"balanced\")\nres = cross_validate(clf, X, y, groups=groups, cv=GroupKFold(n_splits=5),\n                     scoring=[\"f1_macro\", \"accuracy\"])\nprint(res[\"test_f1_macro\"].mean(), res[\"test_f1_macro\"].std())\n```\n\n### Worked TF-IDF weights\nFour training documents (`n` = 4), default `smooth_idf=True`:\n\n| Term appears in | idf = ln((1 + 4) / (1 + df)) + 1 |\n|---|---|\n| 1 document | ln(5/2) + 1 ≈ 1.92 |\n| 2 documents | ln(5/3) + 1 ≈ 1.51 |\n| All 4 documents | ln(5/5) + 1 = 1.00 |\n\nWith `sublinear_tf=True`, a term used 10 times weighs `1 + ln(10)` ≈ 3.30 times its idf before row\nnormalization, not 10 times.","patterns":["**Classifier gate before the agent loop.** Route confident, simple intents to cheap handlers and send the rest to the tool-calling agent, with the threshold set from a validation precision-coverage curve. Monitor fallback rate, per-class precision above the threshold, and cost per request.","**Ship the whole pipeline as one artifact.** Persist the fitted `Pipeline` (vectorizer and model together) with `joblib.dump`, and record `sklearn.__version__` beside it. Pin that version in the serving image; watch for `InconsistentVersionWarning` at load. Use `skops` when artifacts cross a trust boundary.","**Gate releases on a baseline.** In CI, score the candidate and a `DummyClassifier` on the same frozen test set and fail if macro-F1 does not clear the baseline and the previous model. Track per-class F1 from `classification_report`, not just the mean.","**Group and time-aware holdouts.** Split with `GroupKFold` keyed on customer or conversation, and keep a holdout of the most recent tickets (`TimeSeriesSplit` for time-ordered data). Monitor the gap between offline and live metrics; a widening gap signals leakage or drift.","**Calibrate before thresholding.** Route requests whose top `predict_proba` is below a threshold to the LLM agent, after calibrating with `CalibratedClassifierCV` (needed if you used `class_weight=\"balanced\"`). Re-check the threshold whenever the model is retrained.","**Watch prediction mix in production.** Log predicted intent counts per day and compare with the training distribution. A class that suddenly dominates or vanishes is the cheapest drift alarm."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Not every decision in an agent needs a model call. Routing a request to\n  the right agent or tool set, flagging abuse before the loop starts, or deciding \"answer from FAQ vs run\n  the agent\" can be a logistic regression on embeddings: milliseconds, no tokens, and measurable.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A router or guardrail step before the agent graph (for example a\n  LangGraph conditional edge that calls a classifier), quality classifiers over agent outputs, and\n  `sklearn.metrics` inside eval harnesses for tool-choice accuracy, per-class F1 and judge agreement.\n- **What you control.** The pipeline (so evaluation is leak-free), grouped splits by user or\n  conversation, the confidence threshold for falling back to the LLM, calibration, and the pinned\n  version of the artifact you ship.\n- **What you can skip.** Most of the algorithm zoo. Pipelines, logistic regression, splitters, metrics\n  and `DummyClassifier` cover the agent use cases.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Request routing.** A logistic regression on embeddings sends each request to the right agent or\n  tool set in milliseconds, with no tokens spent.\n- **Pre-loop guardrails.** A cheap classifier flags abuse or out-of-scope requests before the agent loop\n  starts, falling back to the LLM only when confidence is low.\n- **Eval metrics.** `sklearn.metrics` computes tool-choice accuracy, per-class F1 and agreement between\n  an LLM judge and human labels.\n- **Output quality checks.** A classifier over agent answers flags likely failures for human review.","mini_project":"**Embedding router with an LLM fallback (1-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** route requests to one of four agent skills with a classifier, and hand only uncertain cases to\n  the LLM.\n- **Steps:** label 400 requests across four routes (draft them with an LLM, then review the labels by\n  hand); embed them; build a `Pipeline` with logistic regression; split by user so one user's requests never appear in both train and test; compare macro-F1\n  against `DummyClassifier`; pick a probability threshold below which the request goes to the LLM router.\n- **Done when:** you report macro-F1 versus the dummy baseline, the share of requests sent to the LLM at\n  your threshold, and the accuracy of the requests the classifier keeps."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[(\"Labelled requests\")] --> S[\"train_test_split, stratify=y\"]\n    S -->|\"20 percent\"| TE[\"Test set, held back\"]\n    S -->|\"80 percent\"| CV[\"StratifiedKFold or GroupKFold\"]\n    CV --> TR[\"Training folds\"]\n    CV --> VA[\"Validation fold\"]\n    TR --> FIT[\"Pipeline.fit on training folds\"]\n    FIT --> PR[\"Pipeline.predict: transform, predict\"]\n    VA --> PR\n    PR --> M[\"Macro-F1 vs DummyClassifier\"]\n    M --> FF[\"Final fit on all training data\"]\n    FF -->|\"score once\"| TE","caption":"Honest evaluation of an agent's intent router: preprocessing is refit on the training folds only, every\nfold is scored against a dummy baseline, and the test set is touched once at the end."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the scikit-learn estimator contract, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Constructor holds hyperparameters only:** `LogisticRegression(C=1.0)` touches no data, so tools can\n  clone an estimator and refit it on each fold.\n- **`fit(X, y)` learns state** and returns `self`; learned attributes end with an underscore, such as\n  `coef_`, `classes_` and `vocabulary_`.\n- **Why it matters:** pipelines, cross-validators and grid searches treat any estimator uniformly, so\n  swapping logistic regression for a random forest is a one-line change."},{"q":"Would you put a classical classifier in front of a tool-calling agent? How would you design it?","a":"- **Yes, for frequent simple intents:** a logistic regression on request embeddings answers in\n  milliseconds with no tokens, and sends only uncertain or complex requests into the agent loop.\n- **Design the fallback:** calibrate with `CalibratedClassifierCV`, pick the threshold from a validation\n  precision-coverage curve, and include an explicit \"needs agent\" class.\n- **Operate it:** log fallback rate, per-class precision above the threshold and cost per request, and\n  retrain when the daily prediction mix drifts from the training distribution."},{"q":"Would you build a classical baseline before an LLM router for ticket intents? Why?","a":"- **Yes, first:** a TF-IDF plus logistic regression pipeline trains in seconds, runs on CPU and costs\n  nothing per request, and it is the number any LLM solution must beat on cost and accuracy.\n- **Know its ceiling:** the model is linear in word evidence, so negation and word order beyond the given\n  n-grams are out of reach; that is where embeddings or an LLM earn their cost.\n- **Decide on one test set:** if macro-F1 is close to the LLM prompt's, the classifier is the cheaper,\n  faster router."},{"q":"How would you use scikit-learn to evaluate an agent's tool selection and an LLM judge?","a":"- **Tool choice as classification:** for each eval case compare the tool the agent called first with the\n  expected tool, then read `classification_report` and `confusion_matrix` to see which tools get confused.\n- **Judge agreement:** score the LLM judge's labels against a human-labelled sample with per-class F1 or\n  an agreement statistic, before trusting the judge on the full set.\n- **Same discipline as any model:** a frozen test set, a dummy baseline, and per-class numbers, not one\n  average."},{"q":"Your classifier reports 90% accuracy on imbalanced intents. Is that good?","a":"- **Not by itself:** with `billing` at 40% of 4,000 tickets, a `DummyClassifier` that always predicts it\n  already scores 40% accuracy, so accuracy hides classes never predicted.\n- **Use macro-F1:** that dummy gets about 0.57 F1 on `billing` and 0 on the other 5 classes, a macro-F1\n  near 0.1, which exposes the failure.\n- **Report per-class F1** from `classification_report` against the dummy baseline, not just the mean."},{"q":"How do you ship a scikit-learn model to production safely, and what can go wrong with `predict_proba` thresholds?","a":"- **One artifact:** persist the fitted `Pipeline` with `joblib.dump`, record `sklearn.__version__`\n  beside it, and watch for `InconsistentVersionWarning` at load; use `skops` when artifacts come from\n  elsewhere, because loading an untrusted pickle executes code.\n- **Shifted probabilities:** `class_weight=\"balanced\"` distorts predicted probabilities, so a fixed\n  threshold no longer means what you think; calibrate with `CalibratedClassifierCV` first.\n- **Monitor:** fallback rate, per-class precision above the threshold, cost per ticket, and the daily mix\n  of predicted intents."}],"tradeoffs":["**Uniform API vs in-memory assumptions.** Swapping models is trivial, but the abstractions assume in-memory, tabular `X`; data beyond RAM or GPU-scale deep learning needs PyTorch or a distributed framework.","**Fast linear models vs capability ceiling.** Linear models on TF-IDF or embeddings train in seconds and are easy to inspect, but cap out on tasks needing reasoning, multi-step tool use or long context, where an LLM agent or fine-tuned model wins despite cost.","**Cross validation vs compute.** It gives a far more honest estimate than one split, at k times the training time; for slow models or huge data, a single well-built validation split is acceptable.","**`class_weight=\"balanced\"` vs calibrated scores.** It raises recall on rare intents but shifts predicted probabilities, so scores used as confidence thresholds need recalibration.","**Classifier router vs LLM router.** A classifier costs nothing per request and is deterministic, but needs labelled data and retraining when intents change; an LLM router adapts from a prompt edit at a per-request cost."],"pitfalls":["**Fitting a scaler or vectorizer on all data before splitting.** Symptom: cross-validation scores clearly higher than performance on genuinely new data. Fix: put preprocessing in the `Pipeline`.","**Random row splits when a customer or conversation repeats.** Symptom: near-perfect validation scores that collapse in production. Fix: `GroupKFold`.","**Reporting accuracy on imbalanced classes.** Symptom: a model that \"scores 90%\" while never predicting the rare intents that matter. Fix: macro-F1 against a dummy baseline.","**Tuning hyperparameters against the test set.** Symptom: a test score that keeps creeping up across experiments while live metrics stay flat. Fix: tune by cross validation, touch the test set once.","**Loading a pickled model with a different scikit-learn version.** Symptom: inconsistent-version warnings or subtly different predictions. Fix: pin versions with the artifact.","**Training the router on the agent's own past routing decisions.** Symptom: the router copies earlier mistakes and offline scores look great. Fix: label from outcomes or human review, not from what the previous system did."],"when":"- **Use when:** classification, regression or clustering on features you already have: TF-IDF text,\n  embeddings, tabular usage data.\n- **In agent products:** intent routing in front of the agent, spam or abuse filters, quality classifiers\n  over agent outputs, and baselines any LLM solution must beat on cost and accuracy.\n- **Inside agent evals:** use its metrics and splitters even when the model is an LLM, for tool-choice\n  accuracy, per-class F1 and judge-vs-human agreement.\n- **Avoid when:** training neural networks or data that does not fit in memory.\n- **Interviews:** explain pipelines and leakage, and justify a baseline before proposing an LLM.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds pipeline-based baselines and embedding classifiers, evaluates with stratified or grouped cross validation, and uses `sklearn.metrics` inside eval code.","architect":"Decides when a cheap classical model should front or replace an LLM call, and sets the evaluation protocol every candidate must pass.","fde":"Trains a quick classifier on a customer's labelled tickets to show what is achievable and to quantify where an LLM adds value."},"check":{"q":"A teammate reports 0.97 macro-F1 for a ticket classifier. They TF-IDF-vectorized all 10,000\ntickets, then ran `train_test_split`, and many customers filed several near-identical tickets. Do you\ntrust the number? What do you change?","a":"No. Two leaks inflate it. The vectorizer learned vocabulary and IDF weights from test tickets,\nso features already encode the test set; putting the vectorizer in a `Pipeline` refits it inside each\nfold. Worse, random row splits place a customer's near-duplicate tickets on both sides, so the model is\npartly memorizing. Use `GroupKFold` or a group-aware split keyed on customer ID, and ideally a\ntime-based holdout of the most recent tickets. Report macro-F1 against a `DummyClassifier` baseline. A\nreal score will be lower but will predict production."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does scikit-learn require that constructors only store hyperparameters and never touch data?","options":["It makes estimators smaller when pickled","Tools such as cross validation and grid search can clone an unfitted estimator and refit it on each fold","It forces all data to be passed as NumPy arrays","It allows models to train on the GPU"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cloning works by reading constructor parameters and creating a fresh, unfitted copy. If constructors learned from data, every fold would inherit state from data it should never have seen."},{"q":"What is the main reason to put a `TfidfVectorizer` inside a `Pipeline` before cross validation?","options":["Pipelines run the vectorizer faster in parallel","Vectorizers cannot be used outside a pipeline","The vectorizer is then fit only on each fold's training portion, preventing leakage of test vocabulary and IDF statistics","Pipelines automatically choose the best n-gram range"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fitting the vectorizer on all rows lets held-out documents shape the feature space. Inside a pipeline, each fold refits it on its own training data, so the validation score reflects truly unseen text."},{"q":"Your intent dataset is 85% one class. Which evaluation choice best reveals whether the model helps?","options":["Plain accuracy on a random split","Training accuracy, since it has the most data","Accuracy averaged over unstratified folds","Macro-F1 with stratified cross validation, compared against a majority-class dummy baseline"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Accuracy rewards predicting the majority class, which already scores 85%. Macro-F1 weights every class equally, stratification keeps rare classes in each fold, and the dummy baseline shows the floor to beat."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/pytorch","sources":["Paszke et al., 2019 — PyTorch: An Imperative Style, High-Performance Deep Learning Library","Official docs: PyTorch"],"why":"- **NumPy lacks three things training needs.** The derivative of a scalar loss with respect to millions\n  or billions of parameters, execution on accelerators whose memory is separate from the CPU's, and a way\n  to keep a GPU busy while the next batch is loaded and decoded.\n- **Hand-derived and numeric gradients do not scale.** Hand derivation is error-prone past a few layers.\n  Finite differences need one extra forward pass per parameter: a billion forward passes per step for a\n  1B-parameter model.\n- **Reverse-mode autodiff needs a record of the computation.** It computes all gradients in one backward\n  pass costing a small constant multiple of the forward pass (commonly quoted as about two to three\n  times), independent of parameter count.\n- **Static graphs made that record painful.** TensorFlow 1.x had you declare a graph, then run it in a\n  session: easy to optimise, miserable to debug, because errors surfaced inside a runtime you could not\n  step through, and data-dependent control flow (a loop that runs a variable number of times) needed\n  special graph operators.\n- **Define-by-run fixes it, and is now the lingua franca.** The graph is recorded as Python executes,\n  every iteration, so control flow is just Python and you debug with print statements and `pdb`, not a\n  separate graph compiler; that is why it won.\n- **For agent builders, literacy is the payoff.** Every Hugging Face model, LoRA adapter, embedding model\n  and most local inference stacks are PyTorch modules. When the agent's retriever OOMs, a fine-tuned\n  router produces NaNs, or a tool model runs on the CPU by accident, you need to know which of the five\n  pieces is responsible.","how":"### Where agents meet PyTorch\n| Agent component | PyTorch role | What to get right |\n|---|---|---|\n| RAG embedder and reranker | forward pass per query or batch of chunks | `inference_mode`, batching, bf16 |\n| Intent or tool router (small classifier) | `nn.Module` loaded at startup | `model.eval()`, flat memory per request |\n| LoRA fine-tune on agent traces | training loop via PEFT/TRL | zeroed grads, precision, memory budget |\n| Self-hosted agent LLM | weights loaded by vLLM or SGLang | dtype and device; the engine owns the loop |\n\n### Tensors\n- **What a tensor is.** A typed, strided n-dimensional array: a pointer to storage plus `shape`,\n  `stride`, `dtype` and `device`.\n- **Views are free.** `.view`, `.transpose` and slicing share storage and only change strides. That is\n  also why `.view` fails on a non-contiguous tensor and you need `.reshape` or `.contiguous()`.\n- **Broadcasting** follows NumPy rules.\n- **`dtype` matters more than in NumPy.**\n\n| dtype | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| `float32` | the default |\n| `bfloat16`, `float16` | halve memory and run on tensor cores |\n| mixed in one op | raises or silently upcasts, depending on the op |\n\n### Autograd\n1. **Recording.** Any tensor with `requires_grad=True` (all `nn.Parameter`s) makes later ops record a\n   node in a directed acyclic graph. Each output holds a `grad_fn` pointing to the function that made it\n   and to its inputs.\n2. **Backward.** `loss.backward()` walks the graph in reverse, applying the chain rule, and\n   *accumulates* into each leaf's `.grad`.\n3. **Freeing.** The graph is freed after `backward()`, because holding saved activations is the main\n   memory cost of training.\n\nDesign choices and their reasons:\n- **Accumulate, not assign.** Gradient accumulation across micro-batches works for free; the price is\n  calling `optimizer.zero_grad()` every step.\n- **No full Jacobian.** Starting from d(loss)/d(loss) = 1, each node takes the gradient w.r.t. its output,\n  multiplies by its local derivative (a vector-Jacobian product) and passes it to its inputs. So cost is a\n  small multiple of forward, and `backward()` with no argument only works on a scalar; for a vector\n  output you supply the vector to multiply by.\n- **Stop recording for inference.** `torch.no_grad()` (or `torch.inference_mode()`) records nothing, so\n  no activations are saved for a backward pass that will never happen. Every agent tool that calls a\n  local model is inference, so this is the setting that matters most.\n\nThree lines make accumulation concrete:\n\n```python\nw = torch.tensor(3.0, requires_grad=True)\ny = 2 * w**2; y.backward(); print(w.grad)   # tensor(12.)  since dy/dw = 4w\ny = 2 * w**2; y.backward(); print(w.grad)   # tensor(24.)  accumulated, not replaced\n```\n\n- **Only leaves keep `.grad`.** Tensors you created, such as parameters. Intermediate gradients are freed\n  unless you call `retain_grad()`.\n- **`.detach()` cuts the graph.** It shares storage but stops gradients flowing, for example into a frozen\n  target.\n- **In-place edits are guarded.** Backward reuses inputs each op saved, so modifying one in place before\n  `backward()` would silently corrupt the gradient. Autograd keeps a version counter per tensor and\n  raises \"modified by an inplace operation\" instead.\n\n### nn.Module\n- **Auto-registration.** Attributes that are `nn.Parameter`s or other modules register automatically.\n- **What registration buys.** `model.parameters()` (for the optimizer), `model.to(device)`,\n  `model.state_dict()` (for saving) and `model.train()` / `model.eval()` all work across an arbitrarily\n  nested tree. A LoRA adapter is just extra registered parameters, which is why PEFT can save only them.\n- **`train()`/`eval()` flip layer behaviour** such as dropout and batch norm. They do not disable\n  gradients.\n- **Call it, don't call forward.** You write `forward`, and call the module as `model(x)` so hooks run.\n\n### DataLoader\n| Piece | Job |\n|---|---|\n| `Dataset` | answers `len()` and `__getitem__(i)`, for example one agent trace per index |\n| `DataLoader` | batching and shuffling |\n| `collate_fn` | stacks samples, or pads variable-length sequences (traces vary a lot in length) |\n| `num_workers` | subprocesses that prefetch, so tokenisation or image decoding overlaps GPU compute |\n| `pin_memory=True` | page-locked host memory, so the GPU copy is async with `non_blocking=True` on `.to(device)` |\n\n- **Processes, not threads**, because of the GIL.\n\n### device\n- **One device per tensor:** `cpu`, `cuda:0`, `mps`. Ops require all inputs on the same device.\n- **Moves are explicit** (`.to(device)`), because a host-to-GPU copy crosses PCIe and is slow relative to\n  GPU compute, and PyTorch refuses to hide that cost.\n- **CUDA calls are asynchronous.** Python returns before the kernel finishes, so naive timing measures\n  launch time unless you call `torch.cuda.synchronize()`. Time agent tool latency only after a sync.","example":"### Agent tool: a local router model that does not leak\nAn agent decides per user turn whether to call the search tool. A small PyTorch classifier scores the\nturn embedding; the handler is safe to call thousands of times.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom torch import nn\n\ndevice = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\n\nclass Router(nn.Module):                         # embedding (384-d) -> [no_tool, search]\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(384, 64), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(64, 2))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)\n\nrouter = Router().to(device).eval()              # eval: dropout off; does NOT stop autograd\n\n@torch.inference_mode()                          # no graph, no saved activations\ndef route(turn_embeddings: torch.Tensor) -> list[str]:\n    probs = router(turn_embeddings.to(device, non_blocking=True)).softmax(-1)\n    return [\"search\" if p[1] > 0.5 else \"answer\" for p in probs.cpu().tolist()]\n\n# inside the agent loop: batch all pending turns from concurrent sessions into one call\ndecisions = route(torch.randn(16, 384))          # 16 sessions, one forward pass\nprint(decisions[:4])\n```\n\n### Minimal training loop\nEvery line maps to one of the five pieces. Swap the toy data for embedded agent turns and the same loop\ntrains the router above.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom torch import nn\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader, TensorDataset\n\ndevice = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\nX = torch.randn(1024, 16)                       # 1024 samples, 16 features\ny = (X.sum(dim=1, keepdim=True) > 0).float()    # toy binary label\nloader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(X, y), batch_size=64, shuffle=True)\n\nclass MLP(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(16, 32), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(32, 1))\n    def forward(self, x):\n        return self.net(x)                       # logits, shape [batch, 1]\n\nmodel = MLP().to(device)                         # moves every registered parameter\nopt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\nloss_fn = nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()\n\nfor epoch in range(5):\n    model.train()\n    for xb, yb in loader:\n        xb, yb = xb.to(device), yb.to(device)    # data must be on the model's device\n        opt.zero_grad()                          # grads accumulate otherwise\n        loss = loss_fn(model(xb), yb)\n        loss.backward()                          # fills p.grad for every parameter\n        opt.step()\n    model.eval()\n    with torch.no_grad():                        # no graph, less memory\n        acc = ((model(X.to(device)) > 0).float() == y.to(device)).float().mean()\n    print(epoch, round(loss.item(), 4), round(acc.item(), 3))\n```\n\n- **Parameter count:** 16×32+32 + 32×1+1 = 577.\n- **`loss.item()` forces a sync** because it copies one scalar back to the CPU; in a real loop, log it\n  every N steps rather than every step.\n\n### Leak-free inference handler\nThe fix for the Check-yourself bug, reusing `model` and `device` from the loop above on a CUDA GPU: no\ngraph, plain Python results, flat memory. This is the shape of every agent tool that wraps a local model.\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\nmodel.eval()                                     # dropout off; does NOT stop autograd\n\n@torch.inference_mode()                          # no graph, no saved activations\ndef predict(batch):\n    logits = model(batch.to(device, non_blocking=True))\n    return (logits > 0).squeeze(-1).cpu().tolist()   # binary labels as Python bools, no graph kept alive\n\nbefore = torch.cuda.memory_allocated()\nfor _ in range(100):\n    predict(torch.randn(8, 16))\nprint(torch.cuda.memory_allocated() - before)    # should stay ~0 across requests\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: will full fine-tuning of a 1B model fit?\nPlain fp32 training with AdamW, before activations, for example a 1B model tuned on tool-calling traces:\n\n| Item | Bytes per parameter | 1B parameters |\n|---|---|---|\n| Weights (fp32) | 4 | 4 GB |\n| Gradients in `.grad` (fp32) | 4 | 4 GB |\n| AdamW state (two moments, fp32) | 8 | 8 GB |\n| **Total before activations** | **16** | **16 GB** |\n\n- **Activations come on top.** They grow with batch size and sequence length, and agent traces are long\n  (multi-turn tool calls and results); gradient checkpointing trades roughly one extra forward pass for\n  much lower activation memory.\n- **Inference alone needs only the weights:** 4 GB in fp32, 2 GB in bf16, because no `.grad`, optimizer\n  state or saved activations exist under `inference_mode()`.\n- **Why agent teams use LoRA instead.** The same arithmetic for an 8B model gives 16 × 8 = 128 GB before\n  activations. LoRA freezes the base (2 bytes × 8B = 16 GB in bf16) and trains only small adapter\n  matrices, so gradients and optimizer state shrink to the adapter's size (illustrative arithmetic, not a\n  benchmark).","patterns":["**Inference mode in every tool handler.** Wrap request handlers in `@torch.inference_mode()` plus `model.eval()` at startup, and return `.cpu().tolist()` values to the agent, never tensors. Monitor `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()` per request; it should stay flat over a long-running agent session.","**Keep model calls off the agent's event loop.** A forward pass is blocking Python; inside an async agent framework it stalls every other session. Run it in a worker thread or, better, behind a separate model server (Text Embeddings Inference, vLLM, a small FastAPI service). Monitor event-loop lag and tool-call p95 latency.","**Micro-batch tool calls across concurrent agents.** Many sessions embedding or reranking at once should share one forward pass instead of running batch 1 each, because GPUs are underused at batch 1. Monitor batch size and queue wait per tool.","**bf16 autocast for training and serving.** On hardware that supports it, wrap forward in `torch.autocast(device_type=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bfloat16)`; unlike fp16 it needs no loss scaling. Watch the loss curve and eval metrics against an fp32 baseline run.","**Feed the GPU before buying a bigger one.** Raise `num_workers`, set `pin_memory=True` and `persistent_workers=True` on the `DataLoader`, and copy with `non_blocking=True`. Monitor GPU utilisation and samples per second; low utilisation with busy CPUs means the loader is the bottleneck.","**Compile hot models.** Apply `torch.compile(model)` once the code is stable, to fuse kernels and cut per-op Python overhead. Track compile time on startup and graph breaks (`TORCH_LOGS=\"graph_breaks\"`).","**Track peak memory per job.** Call `torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()` before a step and log `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()` after it, so batch-size and context changes have a measured memory cost. Alert when peak approaches card capacity.","**Sync-free logging.** Call `.item()` only every N steps, and time GPU work only after `torch.cuda.synchronize()`, so metrics neither stall the loop nor lie."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Hosted LLM calls hide PyTorch, but the moment an agent runs a local\n  embedding model, a reranker, a small intent router or a LoRA fine-tuned on agent traces, a PyTorch\n  module is in the request path. Its memory and latency become your agent's memory and latency.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Under Hugging Face Transformers, sentence-transformers, PEFT and\n  TRL for fine-tuning, under LangChain or LlamaIndex embedding wrappers that load local models, and\n  inside engines like vLLM and SGLang that serve self-hosted agent models.\n- **What you control.** `model.eval()` plus `torch.inference_mode()` in every tool handler, `dtype`\n  (bf16 halves memory), `device` placement, batch size across concurrent agents, and whether the model\n  runs in-process or behind its own server.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing training loops from scratch for standard LLM fine-tuning, and the\n  autograd internals beyond \"gradients accumulate and graphs hold memory\". Literacy is enough to debug\n  OOMs, NaNs and accidental CPU fallbacks.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local tool models in the agent path.** Embedders, rerankers and intent routers run as PyTorch modules\n  inside tool handlers, so their latency and memory add directly to every agent step.\n- **Fine-tuning on agent traces.** LoRA or SFT runs on successful tool-calling transcripts are PyTorch\n  training loops under PEFT and TRL; reading their loss curves and OOMs needs PyTorch literacy.\n- **Debugging a slow or crashing agent worker.** Accidental CPU fallback, missing `torch.inference_mode()`\n  or growing GPU memory across concurrent sessions are PyTorch issues that look like agent issues.\n- **Capacity planning for self-hosted agents.** Choosing `dtype`, batch size and device placement decides\n  how many concurrent agent sessions one GPU serves.","mini_project":"**Profile a local reranker tool inside an agent loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure what a PyTorch reranker costs per agent step and cut it.\n- **Steps:** wrap a small cross-encoder as a `rerank` tool; call it from a fake 10-step agent loop; log\n  per-call latency and peak GPU (or CPU) memory; then add `model.eval()`, `torch.inference_mode()`, bf16\n  and batching of candidates, and rerun.\n- **Done when:** you have a before/after table of latency and memory per step, and a note on which change\n  mattered most and why."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    DS[\"Dataset: agent traces\"] --> DL[\"DataLoader workers: batch, collate\"]\n    DL -->|\"pinned memory, non_blocking copy\"| DEV[\"Batch on device\"]\n    DEV --> F[\"model(x): forward records grad_fn graph\"]\n    F --> L[\"Scalar loss\"]\n    L -->|\"loss.backward()\"| G[\"Leaf .grad accumulates, graph freed\"]\n    G --> O[\"optimizer.step()\"]\n    O --> Z[\"optimizer.zero_grad()\"]\n    Z -->|\"next batch\"| DL\n    T[\"Agent tool call\"] -.->|\"inference_mode: no graph\"| P[\"Scores back to agent as Python values\"]","caption":"Two paths an agent team runs: the fine-tuning step (batch of traces through forward, backward and the\noptimizer) and the serving path a tool handler takes under `inference_mode`, with no graph kept."},"interview":[{"q":"What is define-by-run autograd, and why did it win over static graphs?","a":"- **Definition:** the graph is recorded as Python executes each iteration; every op on a tensor with\n  `requires_grad=True` adds a `grad_fn` node, and `backward()` walks it in reverse once.\n- **Why it won:** control flow is ordinary Python, so you debug with `print` and `pdb` instead of\n  inspecting a separately compiled graph, as TensorFlow 1.x sessions required.\n- **Cost it accepts:** per-op Python overhead, which `torch.compile` later recovers by fusing kernels."},{"q":"Your agent's GPU memory grows with the number of open sessions, though each tool call is small. What do you check?","a":"- **Graph retention first:** confirm every local-model tool runs under `torch.inference_mode()`; `eval()`\n  alone still records graphs.\n- **Then the tool boundary:** look for tensors stored in session memory, caches or tool results; convert\n  them with `.cpu().tolist()` so nothing pins device memory.\n- **Confirm with numbers:** log `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()` before and after a burst of requests; it\n  should return to the same baseline once sessions close."},{"q":"You add a local reranker to an async agent and every session's latency doubles. Why, and what is the fix?","a":"- **The forward pass blocks the event loop:** PyTorch calls are synchronous Python, so while one session\n  reranks, every other coroutine waits.\n- **Fix the placement:** run the model in a worker thread for a quick fix, or behind a model server that\n  micro-batches requests across sessions, which also raises GPU utilisation.\n- **Measure:** event-loop lag and tool p95 latency before and after; batch size per forward pass should\n  rise above 1."},{"q":"Training loss diverges after a few steps in a loop a colleague wrote. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check gradient zeroing first:** a missing `optimizer.zero_grad()` makes every step's gradients add up,\n  which matches divergence after a few steps.\n- **Then check learning rate, precision and inputs:** a learning rate too high for the batch size\n  diverges the same way; fp16 without loss scaling underflows small gradients to zero and can overflow to\n  inf or NaN; NaN or extreme values in the batch show up as sudden loss spikes.\n- **Confirm with numbers:** log the gradient norm every N steps (using `.item()` sparingly) and compare\n  against an fp32 baseline run."},{"q":"How much GPU memory does full fp32 AdamW fine-tuning of a 1B-parameter model need?","a":"- **16 bytes per parameter before activations:** 4 for weights, 4 for gradients and 8 for the two AdamW\n  moments, so about 16 GB for 1B parameters.\n- **Activations come on top** and grow with batch size and sequence length; gradient checkpointing trades\n  roughly one extra forward pass for much lower activation memory.\n- **Inference needs only weights:** 4 GB in fp32 or 2 GB in bf16, because no `.grad` or optimizer state exists."},{"q":"Your GPU shows 30 percent utilisation during training. What do you change?","a":"- **Suspect the input pipeline:** low GPU utilisation with busy CPU cores means the GPU waits for batches\n  from the `DataLoader`.\n- **Fix it in the loader:** raise `num_workers`, set `pin_memory=True` and `persistent_workers=True`, and\n  copy with `non_blocking=True` so transfers overlap compute.\n- **Remove hidden syncs:** a `.item()` call every step forces the CPU to wait for the GPU; log every N\n  steps instead and measure samples per second before and after."}],"tradeoffs":["**In-process tool model vs separate model server.** Loading the embedder or router inside the agent process is simplest and has no network hop, but it ties GPU memory to agent replicas and blocks the event loop unless offloaded to a worker thread. A dedicated server batches across agents and scales separately; it wins once several agent replicas or teams share the model.","**Eager debuggability vs per-op overhead.** Define-by-run makes debugging trivial, but per-op Python overhead hurts small models. `torch.compile` recovers speed by tracing to fused kernels, at the cost of compile time and graph breaks.","**Explicit placement vs forgotten moves.** Device placement makes data movement visible and predictable, but every boundary is a place to forget a `.to(device)`; frameworks like Accelerate hide it once you run multi-GPU.","**Lower precision vs numeric care.** bf16/fp16 halve memory and speed tensor-core math, but fp16 needs loss scaling to avoid gradient underflow; bf16 does not, so prefer it whenever hardware supports it.","**Cheap backward vs activation memory.** Storing activations makes autograd cheap in compute but dominates training memory; gradient checkpointing trades roughly one extra forward pass for much lower activation memory."],"pitfalls":["**Inference without `torch.no_grad()` or `inference_mode()`.** Every forward saves activations for a backward that never comes, so peak memory is higher. If any output is kept (cached, appended to a list, stored in agent memory) its graph stays alive, and memory climbs per request until a CUDA out-of-memory error.","**Returning tensors into agent state.** A tensor stored in conversation memory or a tool result keeps its device memory (and maybe its graph) alive for the session. Symptom: GPU memory tracks open sessions. Fix: convert to Python values with `.cpu().tolist()` at the tool boundary.","**Forgetting `optimizer.zero_grad()`.** Gradients from every step add up. Symptom: loss diverges or oscillates wildly after a few steps.","**Forgetting `model.eval()` before evaluation.** Dropout stays active, so the same input gives different outputs and validation metrics look noisy and worse than expected. In an agent, the router makes different tool choices for identical turns.","**`total += loss` instead of `loss.item()`.** You keep every step's graph referenced. Symptom: steadily rising GPU memory across an epoch.","**`torch.zeros(n)` inside `forward` after moving to GPU.** The new tensor lands on the CPU. Symptom: \"Expected all tensors to be on the same device\". Fix: pass `device=x.device` or use `torch.zeros_like(x)`.","**Python lists of strings or dicts with many DataLoader workers.** Reference counting touches every object, so copy-on-write pages get copied into each worker. Symptom: host RAM grows per worker until the machine OOMs. Fix: store data as NumPy arrays or Arrow.","**Timing GPU code without `torch.cuda.synchronize()`.** Kernels are asynchronous, so measurements look impossibly fast, then the cost appears at the next `.item()` or `.cpu()` call."],"when":"- **Use raw PyTorch when** you need control a higher-level library hides: a custom loss, a non-standard\n  training step, a small router or scorer for your agent, inspecting activations, or debugging why a\n  Hugging Face model misbehaves.\n- **Use a library on top when** fine-tuning standard LLMs on agent traces: Transformers, PEFT and TRL\n  rather than hand-written loops.\n- **Avoid for serving LLMs at scale:** use an inference engine (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM) rather than a\n  PyTorch `generate` loop behind your agent.\n- **Skip entirely when** the agent only calls hosted APIs; PyTorch only enters when a model runs on your\n  own hardware.\n- **Measure:** GPU utilisation, peak memory (`torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()`) and samples per second.\n  A GPU idling at low utilisation usually means the DataLoader is the bottleneck.\n- **Interviews:** explain why gradients accumulate, what `no_grad` saves, and the difference between\n  `eval()` and `no_grad()`.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes and debugs training or inference loops, fixes device mismatches, dtype errors and OOMs, and reads model code in Transformers to understand what a checkpoint actually does.","architect":"Decides when the team owns PyTorch-level code versus buying an API, and sets the GPU, precision and framework standards that training and serving pipelines inherit.","fde":"Gets a customer's model running on their hardware, often diagnosing CUDA/driver mismatches, CPU fallbacks, or memory limits on whatever GPU the customer actually has."},"check":{"q":"A colleague's inference service leaks GPU memory: every request adds a little until it crashes.\nThe code calls `model.eval()` at startup. What is the likely cause, and why doesn't `eval()` fix it?","a":"`eval()` only changes layer behaviour such as dropout and batch norm; it does not stop autograd\nfrom recording. If the forward pass runs without `torch.no_grad()` or `torch.inference_mode()`, each\noutput tensor carries a graph with saved activations, and if any output (or a running total of them) is\nkept referenced, for example appended to a list or stored in a cache, that memory is never released.\nThe fix is to wrap inference in `inference_mode()`, convert results to Python values or detached CPU\ntensors before storing them, and check `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()` stays flat across requests."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does PyTorch accumulate gradients into `.grad` instead of overwriting them?","options":["Because overwriting would break the chain rule inside a single backward pass","So that gradients from several backward passes can be summed, enabling gradient accumulation","Because GPU memory cannot be overwritten in place","So the optimizer can compute momentum without storing extra state"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accumulation lets several micro-batches contribute to one update without extra code. The price is that you must zero gradients each step; momentum is stored separately by the optimizer, not in `.grad`."},{"q":"What does `model.eval()` change?","options":["It disables gradient tracking for all parameters","It moves the model to the CPU for deterministic evaluation","It switches layers like dropout and batch norm to inference behaviour","It freezes parameters so the optimizer cannot update them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`eval()` sets the training flag that dropout and normalisation layers read. Gradient recording is controlled separately by `no_grad`/`inference_mode`, which is why production code needs both."},{"q":"A GPU sits at low utilisation during training while CPU cores are busy. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Increase the learning rate so each step does more work","Switch the model from bf16 to fp32","Raise DataLoader `num_workers` and enable `pin_memory` so batches are prepared in parallel","Call `torch.cuda.synchronize()` after every step"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The GPU is waiting for data. Worker processes prefetch and collate batches while the GPU computes, and pinned memory allows asynchronous copies; synchronising more often would make the stall worse."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/hugging-face","sources":["Wolf et al., 2020 — Transformers: State-of-the-Art Natural Language Processing","Hu et al., 2021 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models","Official docs: Hugging Face Transformers, Datasets, Accelerate, PEFT, TRL"],"why":"- **A model is more than a weights file.** You also need matching architecture code, the config (layer\n  count, heads, rope settings), the tokenizer with its exact vocabulary and special tokens, and the chat\n  template that formats a conversation, including how tool calls and tool results are written.\n- **Mismatches fail silently.** Before a common hub each release had its own repository and conventions,\n  so swapping models meant rewriting glue. The wrong tokenizer or a missing BOS token produced a model\n  that ran but generated garbage, with no error. In an agent, it shows up as tool calls that never parse.\n- **Training adds more glue.** Datasets of tens of gigabytes do not fit in RAM as Python lists;\n  multi-GPU needs process launching, gradient sync and mixed precision. Full fine-tuning of a 7B model at\n  about 16 bytes per parameter (bf16 weights and gradients, fp32 master weights, two fp32 AdamW moments)\n  is roughly 112 GB before any activation, more than an 80 GB GPU holds.\n- **Preference tuning is easy to get subtly wrong.** DPO and PPO-style RLHF need paired data handling, a\n  frozen reference model and specific loss functions.\n- **\"Write it in plain PyTorch\" re-solves this per project.** The libraries put it behind\n  `from_pretrained` and a few trainer classes, so the expensive knowledge (correct tokenisation, loss\n  masking, sharding) lives in one tested place.","how":"### The libraries at a glance\n| Library | Job | Agent use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `transformers` | loads any published architecture and its weights by name | local agent model, judge, evals |\n| `tokenizers` | turns text into the exact ids that model expects | token budgets, chat and tool templates |\n| `datasets` | streams and memory-maps training data | trace corpora for SFT and DPO |\n| `accelerate` | runs the same PyTorch code on one GPU or many | scaling a trace fine-tune |\n| `PEFT` | trains small adapters instead of all weights | one adapter per agent or tenant |\n| `TRL` | SFT and preference-tuning trainers | tune tool-calling on good versus bad traces |\n\n### transformers\n1. **Resolve.** `AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"org/name\")` downloads `config.json` and reads its\n   `model_type`.\n2. **Pick the class.** It chooses the matching PyTorch class. Task heads are separate classes\n   (`...ForSequenceClassification`, `...ForCausalLM`) because one backbone feeds different output layers.\n3. **Load weights** (preferably `.safetensors`) into it.\n\nLoad-time options and helpers:\n- **`device_map=\"auto\"`** (via Accelerate) spreads layers across GPUs and CPU when a model does not fit\n  on one device.\n- **`dtype`** selects precision at load, e.g. `torch.bfloat16`, or `\"auto\"` for the checkpoint's stored\n  dtype, so no fp32 copy is materialised first. Older releases call it `torch_dtype` (still accepted as a\n  deprecated alias).\n- **`generate()`** implements the decoding loop with the KV cache, sampling and stopping criteria.\n- **`pipeline()`** wraps tokenise-model-decode for quick experiments.\n\n### The Hub and the local cache\n- **Name plus `revision`.** Each `from_pretrained` resolves a repository and a `revision` (branch, tag or\n  commit hash) to files.\n- **Content-addressed cache.** Files download once into a cache under `HF_HOME` and are reused after.\n- **Pin a commit hash for reproducibility**, because a branch such as `main` can be rewritten by the\n  repository owner, and a changed chat template silently changes how your agent's tools are rendered.\n- **Offline mode.** `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` resolves loads only from the cache; that is how air-gapped\n  deployments work once the cache is pre-populated.\n\n### tokenizers and chat templates\n- **Fast tokenizers are Rust**, exposed to Python, because tokenising a large corpus in pure Python is a\n  bottleneck.\n- **`AutoTokenizer`** loads that exact model's vocabulary and merges and returns offsets for span\n  alignment.\n- **`apply_chat_template`** renders a list of messages into the model's prompt format with the Jinja\n  template stored with the tokenizer. Using it, not hand-concatenating `\"User: ...\\nAssistant:\"`, keeps a\n  chat model on-distribution.\n- **Tools go through the template too.** Recent `transformers` versions let you pass tool definitions to\n  `apply_chat_template` so the model sees them in the format it was trained on. Models that were not\n  trained for tool use have no tool section in their template; check before choosing one for an agent.\n- **Training new tokenizers** (BPE, WordPiece or Unigram) matters only when you pre-train or adapt a\n  vocabulary; for fine-tuning always reuse the base model's tokenizer.\n- **Left-pad for batched generation** with decoder-only models (`padding_side=\"left\"`), because\n  generation appends to the right end of each row and right padding would put pad tokens between the\n  prompt and the new tokens.\n\n### datasets\n- **Arrow on disk, memory-mapped.** `load_dataset` stores data as Apache Arrow files, so a 50 GB dataset\n  costs little RAM and random access is cheap.\n- **Cached preprocessing.** `.map(fn, batched=True)` tokenises in batches and caches the result keyed by\n  a fingerprint of the function and inputs, so re-running a notebook does not re-tokenise.\n- **Streaming.** `streaming=True` iterates without downloading everything first.\n\n### accelerate\n- **`accelerator.prepare(model, optimizer, loader)`** handles device placement, distributed data\n  parallel, mixed precision and FSDP/DeepSpeed backends.\n- **`accelerate launch`** starts one process per GPU.\n- **Your loop stays ordinary PyTorch**; the configuration decides the topology.\n\n### PEFT and LoRA\nPEFT freezes the base model and injects small trainable modules. LoRA's update:\n\n```\nΔW = B·A          A is r×k, B is d×r, rank r << d, k\ntrainable per matrix:  d·k  ->  r·(d+k)\nforward:   h = W·x + (α/r)·B·A·x\nmerge:     W' = W + (α/r)·B·A\n```\n\n- **`B` starts at zero**, so at step 0 the adapted model is exactly the base model and training starts\n  from known-good behaviour.\n- **The `α/r` scale** keeps the update's magnitude roughly stable when you change r.\n- **What shrinks.** Optimizer state shrinks proportionally, the saved adapter is megabytes instead of\n  gigabytes, and one base can serve many adapters (for example one per agent persona or tenant).\n- **Deploy: merge or keep separate.** Merging removes any extra inference cost but fixes one adapter\n  into the weights; separate adapters let a server swap them per request.\n- **Why LoRA is the default.** Prompt/prefix tuning and IA³ are other PEFT methods, but LoRA adds no\n  inference latency once merged.\n- **QLoRA** adds a 4-bit quantized frozen base (via bitsandbytes) to fit larger models on one GPU.\n\n### TRL\nTrainers built on the `transformers` Trainer; they accept a `peft_config`, so adapters and preference\ntuning compose.\n\n| Trainer | Use | Agent data |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SFTTrainer` | supervised fine-tuning on chat or instruction data; applies the chat template and can compute loss only on assistant tokens | successful traces with tool calls and results |\n| `DPOTrainer` | preference pairs; needs a frozen reference model to measure how far the policy moved | a good versus a bad tool choice for the same state |\n| reward and online RL trainers | reward modelling, PPO, GRPO | rewards from task success or a verifier |\n\n- **DPO with an adapter** can use the base model with the adapter disabled as the reference, instead of\n  loading a second copy.\n- **TRL's API changes frequently** across releases, so pin the version and read its docs.","example":"### Render a tool-calling trace exactly as the model sees it\nBefore fine-tuning or serving an open model in an agent, print one rendered trace. If the tool schema or\nthe tool result does not appear in the model's native format, the agent will not call tools reliably.\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\")   # pin revision= in production\ntools = [{\n    \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"get_order_status\",\n        \"description\": \"Look up an order by id\",\n        \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n                       \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                       \"required\": [\"order_id\"]},\n    },\n}]\nmessages = [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a support agent. Use tools for order data.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is order A-1042?\"},\n]\nprompt = tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tools=tools, add_generation_prompt=True, tokenize=False)\nprint(prompt)                       # check: tool schema present, roles marked, generation prompt at end\nprint(len(tok(prompt).input_ids))   # tool schemas cost tokens on every agent step\n```\n\n### LoRA SFT of a small chat model with TRL and PEFT\nArgument names follow recent TRL releases; check them against your pinned version. For an agent, each\nrow is a successful trace: user turn, assistant tool call, tool result, final answer.\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig\nfrom trl import SFTConfig, SFTTrainer\n\n# Each row: {\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", ...}, {\"role\": \"assistant\", ...}]}\n# Agent traces also hold assistant tool_calls and role \"tool\" messages; how the tool\n# schemas are passed (for example a \"tools\" column) depends on your TRL version.\nds = load_dataset(\"json\", data_files=\"support_chats.jsonl\", split=\"train\")\nds = ds.train_test_split(test_size=0.05, seed=0)\n\npeft_cfg = LoraConfig(\n    r=16, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.05,\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"k_proj\", \"v_proj\", \"o_proj\"],  # names depend on architecture\n    task_type=\"CAUSAL_LM\",\n)\nargs = SFTConfig(\n    output_dir=\"out/support-lora\",\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4, gradient_accumulation_steps=4,   # effective batch 16\n    learning_rate=2e-4, num_train_epochs=2, bf16=True,\n    logging_steps=10, eval_strategy=\"steps\", eval_steps=100,\n)\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=\"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\",   # loaded via transformers; chat template from its tokenizer\n    args=args, train_dataset=ds[\"train\"], eval_dataset=ds[\"test\"],\n    peft_config=peft_cfg,\n)\ntrainer.train()\ntrainer.save_model()   # writes only adapter weights (adapter_model.safetensors) plus config\n```\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Full update, one 4096×4096 projection | 16.8M parameters |\n| LoRA at r=16 | 16×(4096+4096) = 131,072, under 1% of that matrix |\n| Update scale with α=32 | α/r = 2 |\n| Effective batch | per-device batch × accumulation steps × GPUs; on 4 GPUs this config trains with batch 64 |\n\n- **Revisit learning rate when topology changes**, because the effective batch changes with GPU count.\n- **`trainer.save_model()` writes megabytes** of adapter weights.\n\n### Merge the adapter for serving\nTo serve without PEFT at runtime, load the base, attach the adapter and call `merge_and_unload()`.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nfrom peft import PeftModel\n\nbase_id = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\"\nbase = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(base_id, dtype=torch.bfloat16)\nmodel = PeftModel.from_pretrained(base, \"out/support-lora\")   # attach adapter\nmerged = model.merge_and_unload()                             # W' = W + (α/r)·B·A, PEFT gone\nmerged.save_pretrained(\"out/support-merged\")                  # safetensors by default\nAutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(base_id).save_pretrained(\"out/support-merged\")  # ship template too\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what fits on one 80 GB GPU for a 7B agent model\nRough weight-and-optimizer memory before activations (illustrative orders of magnitude):\n\n| Approach | What is stored | Approx. memory |\n|---|---|---|\n| Full fine-tune, mixed-precision AdamW | ~16 bytes per parameter | ~112 GB, does not fit |\n| LoRA on a bf16 base | 2 bytes per frozen parameter + tiny adapter and its optimizer state | ~14 GB + adapter |\n| QLoRA on a 4-bit base | ~0.5 bytes per frozen parameter + adapter | ~3.5 GB + adapter |\n\nActivations for the batch come on top in every row, which is why batch size and sequence length still\ndecide whether a LoRA run fits. Agent traces are long (tool schemas, calls and results), so sequence\nlength is usually the binding constraint.","patterns":["**Fine-tune on curated agent traces, not raw logs.** Keep only traces that passed your eval or a human review, render each with `apply_chat_template` and the same tool definitions the agent uses, and hold out a set of tasks. Monitor tool-call parse rate and task success on the held-out tasks after training.","**Pin and mirror every artifact.** Pass `revision=\"<commit sha>\"` to each `from_pretrained`, pre-fill the cache with `huggingface_hub.snapshot_download`, and run with `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`. Monitor for any load that reaches the network in production.","**Ban unvetted remote code.** Keep `trust_remote_code=False` by default; when a model needs it, review the code and pin it by commit hash. Alert on any config change that flips it.","**Lock library versions per training job.** Pin `transformers`, `peft`, `trl`, `datasets` and `accelerate` in a lockfile next to the config, because renamed arguments or changed defaults silently alter training. Record versions with every run.","**Mask loss to assistant tokens and verify it.** Use your TRL version's assistant-only or completion-only loss option, then decode one batch and check that prompt and tool-result positions have label -100 (the ignore index). Watch held-out task metrics, not training loss.","**Merge for one tenant, swap for many.** Merge with `merge_and_unload()` when one adapter serves all traffic; keep adapters separate on an engine that loads LoRA per request (for example vLLM with `--enable-lora`) for adapter-per-tenant. Monitor per-adapter latency and quality.","**Export to an inference engine for traffic.** Use `generate()` for evals and batch jobs, and serve concurrent agents from vLLM or SGLang behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so the agent code does not change. Monitor time to first token and throughput under load."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** When a hosted model is too expensive, too slow or not allowed, the\n  replacement is an open model from the Hub. Making it call tools reliably means rendering tool schemas\n  with its own chat template and, often, fine-tuning on your agent's successful traces.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `transformers` and `tokenizers` for local agent models and evals,\n  `datasets` for trace corpora, PEFT and TRL for SFT and DPO on traces, `langchain-huggingface` wrappers\n  in LangChain, Hugging Face's own `smolagents` library, and vLLM or SGLang loading the same Hub repos.\n- **What you control.** The pinned `revision`, `apply_chat_template` with your tool definitions, loss\n  masking to assistant (and tool-call) tokens, LoRA rank and targets, and whether adapters are merged or\n  swapped per tenant.\n- **What you can skip.** Training tokenizers, writing Accelerate configs by hand for a first run, and\n  serving traffic from `generate()`; export to an inference engine instead.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Replacing a hosted model with an open one.** When cost, latency or data rules rule out a hosted API,\n  teams load an open model from the Hub and make it call tools with its own chat template.\n- **Fine-tuning on agent traces.** PEFT and TRL turn successful tool-calling transcripts into a LoRA\n  adapter that calls your tools more reliably than the base model.\n- **Offline agent evals.** `datasets` holds trace corpora and eval sets, and `transformers` runs candidate\n  models over them before a model swap ships.\n- **Per-tenant adapters.** One base model with swapped LoRA adapters serves several agents or customers\n  from the same GPUs.","mini_project":"**Render and test tool calls with an open model (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** get a small open instruct model to emit parseable tool calls for two tools.\n- **Steps:** pick a Hub model whose chat template supports tools and pin its `revision`; define two tool\n  schemas; render prompts with `apply_chat_template` and inspect the raw string; generate for 20 test\n  requests; parse the tool calls and count how many are valid against the schema.\n- **Done when:** you have a parse-rate number, examples of failures, and a note on whether the template,\n  the prompt or the model caused them."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    HUB[(\"Hub repo at a pinned revision\")] --> CACHE[(\"Local cache under HF_HOME\")]\n    CACHE --> M[\"transformers: config, class, safetensors weights\"]\n    CACHE --> TK[\"tokenizers: vocab plus chat template\"]\n    DATA[(\"datasets: agent traces as Arrow\")] -->|\"map with apply_chat_template\"| TR[\"TRL SFTTrainer or DPOTrainer\"]\n    M --> TR\n    TK --> TR\n    PEFT[\"PEFT LoraConfig: frozen base, trainable B and A\"] --> TR\n    ACC[\"accelerate: devices, mixed precision, multi-GPU\"] --> TR\n    TR --> AD[\"Adapter weights, megabytes\"]\n    AD -->|\"merge_and_unload\"| MERGED[\"Merged model for one tenant\"]\n    AD -->|\"keep separate\"| ENG[\"Inference engine loading adapters per request\"]","caption":"The path of a LoRA fine-tune on agent traces: a pinned Hub repository feeds the model and tokenizer,\n`datasets` feeds templated traces, TRL trains only the adapter, and the result is merged or served."},"interview":[{"q":"What does `from_pretrained` actually resolve and load, and why does that matter?","a":"- **It resolves a repository plus a `revision`** to files, downloads them once into the cache under\n  `HF_HOME`, reads `config.json` for `model_type`, picks the matching class and loads the weights.\n- **The tokenizer and chat template travel separately**, so loading a model with the wrong tokenizer runs\n  without error but generates garbage.\n- **A branch such as `main` can be rewritten**, so production code pins a commit hash."},{"q":"Walk through the LoRA parameter arithmetic for a 4096 by 4096 projection at rank 16.","a":"- **Full update:** 4096 × 4096 is about 16.8M trainable parameters for that one matrix.\n- **LoRA:** r·(d+k) = 16 × (4096 + 4096) = 131,072, under 1% of the matrix, and `B` starts at zero so\n  step 0 equals the base model.\n- **Scale:** with `lora_alpha=32` the update is multiplied by α/r = 2, which keeps magnitude stable if\n  you later change r."},{"q":"When would you fine-tune an open model for your agent, and when would you not?","a":"- **Not while prompting and retrieval still improve the task metric**, because both are cheaper to\n  change than weights, and a hosted model with good tool use may already be enough.\n- **LoRA by default** when cost, latency or data residency force an open model: far less memory,\n  megabyte-sized adapters, many adapters per base and no latency once merged.\n- **Full fine-tuning** when the task needs broad behaviour change that LoRA underfits and you can afford\n  roughly 16 bytes per parameter of training memory."},{"q":"After LoRA SFT on agent traces, the model's tool calls fail to parse 20 percent of the time in production but not in evals. What do you check?","a":"- **Rendering parity:** are tools passed to `apply_chat_template` at inference exactly as they were when\n  the traces were rendered for training, with the same pinned tokenizer revision?\n- **Serving path:** does the inference engine apply the same chat template and tool parser as your eval\n  harness, or a default one?\n- **Data coverage:** do training traces include the tools and argument shapes now failing? Log failing\n  raw outputs and diff them against the training format."},{"q":"A customer runs in an air-gapped network. How do you deliver a fine-tuned open model?","a":"- **Pre-fetch everything outside:** `huggingface_hub.snapshot_download` at a pinned commit for the base,\n  plus the adapter or merged weights, the tokenizer files, datasets and Python wheels.\n- **Load offline inside:** populate the cache, set `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` so loads resolve only from it, and\n  keep `trust_remote_code=False` unless the code was reviewed.\n- **Verify with a smoke test** that generates with the chat template and stops at the end-of-turn token."},{"q":"Does a 7B full fine-tune fit on an 80 GB GPU? What are the alternatives?","a":"- **No:** about 16 bytes per parameter for mixed-precision AdamW is roughly 112 GB before activations.\n- **LoRA on a bf16 base** stores about 2 bytes per frozen parameter, roughly 14 GB plus the adapter.\n- **QLoRA on a 4-bit base** is roughly 3.5 GB plus the adapter; in every case activations still decide\n  the batch size and sequence length that fit."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fine-tuned open model vs prompted hosted model.** A LoRA on traces can match a narrow agent task at lower cost and latency, and keeps data in-house, but you own evals, serving and every base-model upgrade. Prompting a hosted model wins while the task still changes weekly.","**Instant access vs supply-chain risk.** `from_pretrained` by name reaches thousands of models but fetches weights at runtime (and, with `trust_remote_code=True`, Python code too) from whatever the branch currently holds. Pin a revision hash and mirror artifacts once you leave experimentation.","**Trainer defaults vs control.** Trainer classes remove boilerplate and encode correct defaults but hide the loop; for a custom loss or schedule, subclassing can be harder than writing the PyTorch loop.","**LoRA efficiency vs capacity.** LoRA cuts memory and storage dramatically and allows many adapters per base, but can underfit tasks needing broad behaviour change, where full fine-tuning (if affordable) does better.","**`generate` simplicity vs serving throughput.** `transformers.generate` is fine for experiments and batch jobs; many concurrent users are better served by an inference engine such as vLLM or SGLang, which add continuous batching and a paged KV cache.","**Runs vs runs fast.** `device_map=\"auto\"` makes a too-large model run, but layers offloaded to CPU make generation dramatically slower; it trades failure for latency, acceptable only offline."],"pitfalls":["**Hand-formatted prompts instead of `apply_chat_template`.** The model answers, but quality is oddly poor, it ignores the system prompt, or keeps generating past where it should stop. With tools, it writes tool calls in a format your parser rejects.","**Training and serving with different tool renderings.** Traces rendered one way at training time and tools passed another way at inference. Symptom: tool-call accuracy on evals drops after deployment. Fix: one function renders both, through the same template and tool list.","**Loss on prompt tokens as well as completions during SFT.** The model learns to imitate users (and tool outputs). Symptom: outputs that echo or continue the question instead of answering it.","**`trust_remote_code=True` on an unvetted repository.** It executes arbitrary Python from the hub at load time; the symptom may be nothing visible at all, which is the danger.","**Unpinned library versions.** TRL or transformers upgrades rename arguments or change defaults, and a script that worked last month fails or silently trains differently.","**Right-padding batched prompts for a decoder-only model in `generate()`.** Shorter prompts give degraded or nonsensical outputs and transformers warns that right-padding was detected. Fix: `tokenizer.padding_side = \"left\"` for generation.","**Mismatched tokenizer and model** (e.g. new special tokens without resizing embeddings). Symptom: index errors on new token ids, or generations that never emit the end-of-turn token."],"when":"- **Use `transformers` + `tokenizers`** whenever you run an open-weight model in Python for experiments,\n  offline batch work, evaluation, an LLM judge or embedding.\n- **Use `datasets`** when training or eval data (agent traces, eval tasks) exceeds a few hundred\n  megabytes or needs reproducible preprocessing.\n- **Use PEFT + TRL** when prompting and retrieval have plateaued and you have a few thousand high-quality\n  examples of the target behaviour; use Accelerate when one GPU is not enough.\n- **Avoid** serving high-concurrency traffic from this stack; export the model (merged weights or base\n  plus adapter) to an inference engine.\n- **Measure** eval loss and a task metric on a held-out set (for agents, tool-call validity and task\n  success), not training loss.\n- **Interviews:** LoRA's parameter arithmetic, why chat templates matter, and when to fine-tune instead\n  of prompt.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Loads checkpoints with correct dtype and template, writes SFT/DPO jobs with PEFT, and debugs tokenisation, loss masking and version drift.","architect":"Decides fine-tune versus prompt-and-retrieve, sets the hub mirroring and revision-pinning policy, and chooses adapter-per-tenant versus merged models for serving.","fde":"Fine-tunes or evaluates an open model inside a customer's air-gapped environment, which means pre-downloading weights, datasets and wheels, and loading them offline."},"check":{"q":"A team fine-tuned a 7B instruct model with LoRA on 5,000 support transcripts. Training loss fell\nnicely, but in testing the model rambles, sometimes writes the customer's next message, and ignores the\nsystem prompt. What do you check first?","a":"These symptoms point at data formatting, not capacity. First, was the data rendered with the base\nmodel's own chat template via `apply_chat_template`, including its end-of-turn token? If not, the model\nlearned a new format that conflicts with its instruction tuning and never learned to stop. Second, was\nloss masked to assistant tokens only? Training on user turns teaches the model to write user turns. Third,\nwas the system prompt present in training examples? Then verify inference uses the same template. Only\nafter formatting is correct would I tune rank, learning rate or epochs, and I would judge with a task\nmetric on held-out conversations, since falling training loss says little about behaviour."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does LoRA reduce GPU memory during fine-tuning so much more than it reduces inference memory?","options":["Because LoRA quantizes the base model to 4 bits automatically","Because gradients and optimizer states are only kept for the small adapter matrices, not the frozen base","Because LoRA removes the KV cache during training","Because the adapter replaces the attention layers entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Training memory is dominated by gradients and AdamW states for every trainable weight. Freezing the base removes those for almost all parameters; the frozen weights still must be loaded, so inference barely changes."},{"q":"What does `datasets` gain by storing data as memory-mapped Arrow files?","options":["It compresses text so tokenisation is faster","It guarantees the data is deduplicated","Large datasets can be accessed randomly without loading them fully into RAM","It encrypts the data for compliance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Memory mapping lets the operating system page in only the rows you touch, so a dataset far larger than RAM is usable. Compression and deduplication are separate concerns that Arrow does not imply."},{"q":"Why use `tokenizer.apply_chat_template` instead of building the prompt string yourself?","options":["It makes the prompt shorter so it costs fewer tokens","It is required for the model to accept a system prompt at all","It reproduces the exact special tokens and turn format the model was trained on","It automatically removes prompt-injection attempts from user messages"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chat models are trained on one specific format of role markers and end-of-turn tokens. Deviating keeps the model running but off-distribution, which degrades quality and stopping behaviour without any error."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/sentence-transformers","sources":["Reimers & Gurevych, 2019 — Sentence-BERT: Sentence Embeddings using Siamese BERT-Networks","Official docs: Sentence Transformers"],"why":"- **Token vectors are not sentence vectors.** A BERT-style encoder emits one vector per token. Taking\n  `[CLS]` or averaging tokens from a model trained only on masked-language modelling gives vectors whose\n  cosine similarity correlates poorly with meaning; it was never trained to put paraphrases close.\n- **Pairwise scoring does not scale.** A cross-encoder (both sentences in one model, classify the pair) is\n  accurate but costs a forward pass per pair: a million forward passes per query over a million documents.\n- **Sentence-BERT's fix: siamese training.** Two copies with shared weights encode each sentence\n  independently, and a simple similarity between the two vectors is trained to reflect relatedness.\n- **Independent encoding is the key property.** Document vectors are computed once and stored in a vector\n  index; each query (or each agent tool call) costs one forward pass plus a nearest-neighbour lookup.\n- **One library prevents silent drift.** It packages loading, pooling, batching, multi-GPU encoding,\n  contrastive losses and rerankers. Without it, teams re-implement pooling and small differences (mean vs\n  CLS pooling, forgetting to normalise, missing query prefixes) silently degrade retrieval.","how":"### Where it sits in an agent\n| Agent need | Component | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `search_docs` tool over a knowledge base | bi-encoder plus vector index, then `CrossEncoder` | retrieve wide, rerank narrow |\n| Long-term memory (\"what did the user tell me last week\") | bi-encoder over stored memories | same versioning rules as documents |\n| Tool selection over many tools | embed tool descriptions, pick the closest few | keeps the prompt short when tools number in the dozens |\n| Semantic cache of past answers | embed queries, reuse an answer above a tuned threshold | threshold must be re-tuned per model |\n\n### Bi-encoder: `SentenceTransformer`\nA model is a pipeline of modules saved together:\n\n| Module | Role |\n|---|---|\n| Transformer | a Hugging Face encoder with its tokenizer and max sequence length |\n| Pooling | mean of token vectors masked by attention, CLS, or last-token for decoder-based embedders |\n| Dense (optional) | projection |\n| Normalize (optional) | unit length |\n\n`encode(texts, batch_size=..., normalize_embeddings=True)`:\n1. Tokenises and sorts inputs by length to reduce padding waste.\n2. Runs batches on the chosen device under no-grad.\n3. Pools and returns a NumPy array or tensor of shape `[n, dim]`.\n\n- **Normalise for speed.** At unit length, dot product equals cosine similarity, which most vector indexes\n  compute fastest.\n\n### Mean pooling and why the mask matters\n```\ne = Σᵢ mᵢ·hᵢ / Σᵢ mᵢ        hᵢ = token i's output vector, mᵢ = 1 for real tokens, 0 for padding\n```\n\n- **Padding vectors are not zero**, so averaging them in would make the same sentence embed differently\n  depending on what else was in its batch.\n- **Pooling is fixed by training, not a free choice**, which is why the library stores the pooling config\n  with the weights instead of asking you to pick.\n\n### Prompts and asymmetry\n- **Asymmetric models need prefixes.** Queries and passages get different instructions, for example E5's\n  `\"query: \"` and `\"passage: \"`.\n- **The library applies them.** Prompts live in the model config and are applied via `prompt_name`, and,\n  from v5, through `encode_query` / `encode_document`, which pick the right prompt for you.\n- **Why bother:** forgetting the prefix measurably hurts retrieval for those models while raising no error.\n- **Agent queries are queries.** The search string the LLM writes in its tool call goes through the query\n  path; the chunks you indexed went through the document path.\n\n### Cross-encoder: `CrossEncoder`\n- **Input:** the concatenated `(query, document)` pair; **output:** one relevance score.\n- **More accurate, nothing precomputable.** Attention runs across both texts, capturing interactions a dot\n  product cannot.\n- **Retrieve-then-rerank.** A bi-encoder fetches the top 50-200 candidates cheaply; a cross-encoder\n  rescores only those, and the agent sees only the few that survive.\n\n### Training with contrastive losses\n`MultipleNegativesRankingLoss` takes (anchor, positive) pairs and treats every other positive in the batch\nas a negative. For anchor `aᵢ` in a batch of B pairs it is a B-way softmax cross-entropy:\n\n```\nloss_i = -log( exp(s·cos(aᵢ, pᵢ)) / Σⱼ exp(s·cos(aᵢ, pⱼ)) )      # j = 1..B, s is a scale (default 20)\n```\n\n- **Each step asks \"pick your true positive out of B candidates\".**\n- **Batch size matters more than in classification**, because larger batches mean more, and on average\n  harder, negatives for free.\n- **`CachedMultipleNegativesRankingLoss`** reaches large effective batches without the memory by\n  computing embeddings in chunks.\n- **Mined hard negatives** (anchor, positive, negative): a passage that looks relevant but is not teaches\n  distinctions random in-batch negatives never test.\n- **Trap: duplicates become false negatives.** Two pairs with the same positive in a batch; deduplicate.\n- **Trainer.** Recent versions use a `SentenceTransformerTrainer` built on the Hugging Face Trainer, with\n  evaluators reporting retrieval metrics such as recall@k and NDCG on a held-out set.\n- **Agent logs are training data.** The search queries your agent issued, paired with the passage it\n  ended up citing in a successful answer, make in-domain (anchor, positive) pairs.\n\n### Matryoshka embeddings\n- **Leading dimensions carry the most.** Models trained with `MatryoshkaLoss` let you keep, say, the first\n  256 of 768 dimensions (`truncate_dim=256`) for a 3× smaller index and a modest recall loss.\n- **Only for models trained that way.** Others degrade badly when truncated; check the model card, and\n  re-normalise after truncation.\n\n### Truncation\n- **Each model has a `max_seq_length`**; longer inputs are silently truncated.\n- **That limit, not the model's theoretical context, decides your chunk size.**","example":"### A `search_docs` tool for an agent\nThe function an agent framework registers as a tool: retrieve wide, rerank narrow, return short passages\nwith ids the agent can cite. `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` produces 384-dimensional vectors.\n\n```python\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer, CrossEncoder\n\ndocs = [\n    \"Refunds are issued within 14 days of a returned item being received.\",\n    \"Our warehouse ships orders Monday to Friday.\",\n    \"To reset your password, use the link on the sign-in page.\",\n    \"Returned items must be unused and in original packaging.\",\n]\nbi = SentenceTransformer(\"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\nce = CrossEncoder(\"cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2\")\ndoc_emb = bi.encode(docs, normalize_embeddings=True, batch_size=64)   # shape (4, 384), computed once\n\ndef search_docs(query: str, pool: int = 50, k: int = 2) -> list[dict]:\n    \"\"\"Tool: find passages that answer the query. Returns [{id, text, score}].\"\"\"\n    q_emb = bi.encode(query, normalize_embeddings=True)\n    scores = doc_emb @ q_emb                       # dot product == cosine because vectors are unit length\n    cand = scores.argsort()[::-1][:pool]           # cheap first stage\n    rr = ce.predict([(query, docs[i]) for i in cand])   # one forward pass per candidate pair\n    best = sorted(zip(cand, rr), key=lambda t: -t[1])[:k]\n    return [{\"id\": f\"doc-{i}\", \"text\": docs[i], \"score\": round(float(s), 3)} for i, s in best]\n\n# what the agent receives after calling the tool with its own search string\nprint(search_docs(\"how long until I get my money back after returning something\"))\nprint(bi.max_seq_length)                           # inputs beyond this many tokens are truncated\n```\n\n- **What to look for:** both refund-related passages score well with the bi-encoder because they share\n  the topic of returns. The cross-encoder reads query and passage together, so it should separate the one\n  that answers \"how long\" (the 14-day passage) from the one that only mentions returns.\n- **Exact scores vary by model version**; the ordering is the lesson.\n- **Cost shape:** with N documents and k candidates, a query costs 1 bi-encoder pass plus k cross-encoder\n  passes instead of N. At N = 1,000,000 and k = 50 that is 51 forward passes per query instead of a\n  million.\n\n### Worked scenario: retrieval cost and context budget per agent task\nA research agent averages 4 `search_docs` calls per task, with a pool of 50 and top 5 returned, and\npassages of about 200 tokens (illustrative figures).\n\n| Quantity | Per call | Per task (4 calls) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bi-encoder passes | 1 | 4 |\n| Cross-encoder passes | 50 | 200 |\n| Passages added to context | 5 | 20 |\n| Tokens added to the LLM context | ~1,000 | ~4,000, re-sent on every later step |\n\n- **Rerank cost is the pool, not the corpus.** Halving the pool to 25 halves cross-encoder work; check\n  recall@25 first, because a passage outside the pool can never be returned.\n- **Top-k is an LLM cost lever.** Each passage you return is paid for again on every later step of the\n  loop, so 5 well-reranked passages beat 20 loosely ranked ones.\n\n### Fine-tune on in-domain pairs without false negatives\nSentence Transformers v3+ trainer API; the no-duplicates sampler keeps repeated texts out of one batch.\nPairs can come from agent logs: the agent's search query and the passage it cited in a good answer.\n\n```python\nfrom datasets import Dataset\nfrom sentence_transformers import (SentenceTransformer, SentenceTransformerTrainer,\n                                   SentenceTransformerTrainingArguments, losses)\nfrom sentence_transformers.training_args import BatchSamplers\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\ntrain = Dataset.from_dict({                        # toy data: real runs use thousands of pairs\n    \"anchor\":   [\"how long do refunds take\", \"reset my password\"],\n    \"positive\": [\"Refunds are issued within 14 days of a returned item being received.\",\n                 \"To reset your password, use the link on the sign-in page.\"],\n})\nargs = SentenceTransformerTrainingArguments(\n    output_dir=\"out/support-embedder\",\n    per_device_train_batch_size=64,                 # bigger batch = more in-batch negatives\n    batch_sampler=BatchSamplers.NO_DUPLICATES,      # avoid false negatives from duplicates\n    num_train_epochs=1,\n)\nloss = losses.MultipleNegativesRankingLoss(model)\nSentenceTransformerTrainer(model=model, args=args, train_dataset=train, loss=loss).train()\n```\n\nAfter training, re-embed the whole corpus with the new model, because its vectors are not comparable with\nthe old ones.\n\n### Worked scenario: index size with Matryoshka truncation\n10 million passages stored as fp32 (4 bytes per dimension), vectors only, before index overhead:\n\n| Dimensions kept | Bytes per vector | Total |\n|---|---|---|\n| 768 (full) | 3,072 | ~30.7 GB |\n| 256 (`truncate_dim=256`) | 1,024 | ~10.2 GB |\n\nThe 3× saving is real only for a Matryoshka-trained model; measure recall@k at both sizes on your labelled\nqueries before switching.","patterns":["**Return ids and short passages from the tool.** The agent's search tool returns a fixed top-k with stable passage ids and source metadata, so the agent can cite and you can trace which passage drove an answer. Monitor citation rate and how often the cited id was in the reranked top-k.","**Evaluate retrieval separately from the agent.** Keep a labelled set of real agent search queries with their correct passages, and run recall@k and MRR on every embedder or chunking change before running end-to-end agent evals. Monitor both; an agent regression with stable recall points elsewhere.","**Version every vector.** Store the model name and version with each embedding, and re-index into a new collection on upgrade, then switch reads over in one step. Monitor that no index mixes versions, including the agent's memory store.","**Retrieve wide, rerank narrow.** Pull the top 50-200 with the bi-encoder, rerank with a `CrossEncoder`, keep a fixed top-k such as 5. Monitor recall@50 on labelled queries and p95 rerank latency.","**Chunk to the real limit.** Count chunk tokens with the model's own tokenizer and keep each under `max_seq_length`. Monitor the share of chunks that would be truncated; it should be zero.","**Let the library apply prompts.** Use `encode_query` / `encode_document` (v5) or `prompt_name` instead of hand-written prefixes, so query and passage formats cannot drift apart. Spot-check recall against the model card's claim.","**Batch offline embedding.** Pass whole lists to `encode` with a tuned `batch_size` on GPU, never one text per call. Monitor documents per second and GPU utilisation during backfills."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent's `search_docs` tool and its long-term memory lookups are only\n  as good as the embedder behind them. When the agent cites the wrong passage or \"forgets\" a stored fact,\n  the cause is usually here: a missing prefix, no normalisation, truncation or mixed model versions.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Directly in your retrieval tool, behind LangChain's\n  `HuggingFaceEmbeddings` and LlamaIndex's Hugging Face embedding wrappers, as the reranker step in RAG\n  pipelines, and in embedding servers that load the same models.\n- **What you control.** Which model, query versus document prompts, `normalize_embeddings`, chunk size\n  versus `max_seq_length`, candidate pool size for reranking, top-k returned to the agent, and whether\n  to fine-tune on your own queries.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing contrastive losses yourself and the Sentence-BERT paper's details. Know\n  bi-encoder versus cross-encoder cost, and why vectors from different models never mix.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **An agent's `search_docs` tool.** A bi-encoder embeds the corpus once and each tool call costs one query\n  embedding plus a nearest-neighbour lookup.\n- **Reranking before the agent reads.** A cross-encoder rescores a candidate pool so only the best few\n  chunks reach the context window.\n- **Long-term agent memory.** Stored facts and past conversation summaries are embedded and recalled by\n  similarity when a new turn arrives.\n- **Tool selection for large toolsets.** Embedding tool descriptions lets an agent shortlist relevant tools\n  instead of sending every schema on every step.","mini_project":"**Build and measure a retrieval tool for an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** ship a `search_docs` tool and prove reranking improves what the agent sees.\n- **Steps:** chunk 100-300 short documents; embed them with `normalize_embeddings` on and the model's\n  query and document prompts; write 20 test questions with a known correct chunk; measure top-5 hit rate\n  with the bi-encoder alone, then with a cross-encoder reranking the top 50.\n- **Done when:** the tool returns JSON results an agent can call, and you have a hit-rate table for both\n  setups plus per-query latency."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    D[\"Chunks under max_seq_length\"] -->|\"passage prompt, offline\"| BI[\"Bi-encoder: pool, normalize\"]\n    BI --> IDX[(\"Vector index with model version\")]\n    A[\"Agent emits search_docs call\"] -->|\"query prompt, one pass\"| QE[\"Query vector\"]\n    QE -->|\"dot product equals cosine\"| IDX\n    IDX -->|\"top 50 to 200 candidates\"| CE[\"CrossEncoder scores each pair\"]\n    CE -->|\"keep fixed top-k such as 5\"| OUT[\"Tool result: passages with ids\"]\n    OUT --> A","caption":"An agent's search tool: documents are embedded once offline, each tool call costs one bi-encoder pass\nplus a nearest-neighbour lookup, and only the candidates pay for a cross-encoder pass before a few\npassages go back into the agent's context."},"interview":[{"q":"Why can't you just mean-pool a plain BERT model to get sentence embeddings?","a":"- **It was never trained for it:** a masked-language-modelling encoder was not trained to put paraphrases\n  close, so pooled `[CLS]` or averaged token vectors give cosine scores that correlate poorly with meaning.\n- **Siamese contrastive training fixes that:** Sentence-BERT trains shared-weight encoders so a simple\n  similarity between independently encoded vectors reflects relatedness.\n- **Pooling is fixed by training**, so the library stores the pooling config with the weights."},{"q":"After an embedder upgrade, your agent stops recalling facts users told it weeks ago. What do you check?","a":"- **Mixed vector versions:** old memories were embedded with the previous model and new queries with the\n  new one, so similarities are meaningless; check that every stored vector carries its model name and\n  version.\n- **Prompts and normalisation:** confirm the new model's required query and passage prefixes are applied\n  and that `normalize_embeddings=True` is set if the index uses dot product.\n- **Fix:** re-embed memories and documents into a new collection, measure recall@k on labelled queries,\n  then switch reads over in one step."},{"q":"How would you design the retrieval tool an agent calls, and what would you measure?","a":"- **Shape:** the tool takes the agent's query string, retrieves 50 candidates with a bi-encoder, reranks\n  with a cross-encoder and returns a fixed top-k such as 5, each with an id and source.\n- **Measure retrieval alone:** recall@50 and MRR on labelled agent queries, plus p95 tool latency.\n- **Measure in the loop:** searches per task, citation correctness and context tokens added, because\n  every returned passage is re-sent on later steps."},{"q":"When is a cross-encoder reranker not worth adding?","a":"- **When top-k precision is already fine:** if the right passage already ranks in the top few, a reranker\n  adds latency for little gain.\n- **When the candidate pool misses the answer:** reranking cannot recover a passage the bi-encoder never\n  retrieved, so fix recall@50 first.\n- **When latency budgets are tight:** cost grows with candidate count because each pair is one forward\n  pass; measure p95 rerank latency against the precision gained."},{"q":"Why does batch size matter so much when training with `MultipleNegativesRankingLoss`?","a":"- **In-batch negatives:** every other positive in the batch is a negative, so a batch of B pairs makes\n  each step a B-way softmax.\n- **Bigger batches give more and harder negatives for free**; `CachedMultipleNegativesRankingLoss`\n  reaches large effective batches without the memory.\n- **Duplicates become false negatives**, so use the no-duplicates batch sampler."},{"q":"How much storage does a 10M-passage index need, and how would you shrink it?","a":"- **Full 768 dimensions in fp32:** 3,072 bytes per vector, about 30.7 GB before index overhead.\n- **Matryoshka truncation to 256 dimensions:** 1,024 bytes per vector, about 10.2 GB, a 3× saving, but\n  only for a model trained with `MatryoshkaLoss` and only after measuring recall@k at both sizes.\n- **Re-normalise after truncation**, because the shorter vector is no longer unit length."}],"tradeoffs":["**Precomputed speed vs fine interactions.** Bi-encoders allow precomputed document vectors and millisecond search but compress each text to one vector and miss fine query-document interactions; add a cross-encoder when top-k precision matters.","**Reranker latency vs agent step count.** A reranker adds latency to every search call, but better top-k often saves the agent a second or third search; measure total task latency, not per-call latency.","**Local vs hosted.** Running embeddings locally avoids per-token API cost and keeps data in-house, but you own GPU capacity and batching; hosted embedding APIs win when volume is small or ops capacity scarce.","**Small and fast vs larger and better.** MiniLM-class models are fast enough for CPU, but larger or instruction-tuned embedders usually retrieve better; measure the gap on your queries, not a public leaderboard.","**Fine-tuned vs bigger generic.** Fine-tuning on in-domain pairs often beats a bigger generic model but requires re-embedding the whole corpus, since vectors from different models are not comparable."],"pitfalls":["**Forgetting `normalize_embeddings=True` with a dot-product index**, on a model that does not already end in a Normalize module. Large-magnitude vectors get inflated scores. Symptom: the same few documents win for every query, so the agent cites the same passage for unrelated questions.","**Omitting a model's required query or passage prefix.** No error, just noticeably worse recall than the model card claims.","**Chunks longer than `max_seq_length`.** The tail is silently dropped, so facts near the end of a chunk are never retrievable.","**Mixing vectors from two model versions in one index after an upgrade.** Similarity scores become meaningless. Symptom: a sudden, unexplained retrieval quality drop, or an agent that stops recalling memories written before the upgrade.","**Thresholding cross-encoder scores (\"keep passages scoring above 0.5\") then swapping the model.** Scores are model-specific logits or sigmoid outputs, not calibrated probabilities, so the reranker suddenly keeps nothing or everything. Fix: re-tune thresholds per model, or keep a fixed top-k.","**Encoding one text at a time in a loop.** Throughput collapses because the GPU processes batches of one; pass lists and let `encode` batch."],"when":"- **Use when** you need embeddings or reranking from open models: a self-hosted agent search tool or\n  memory, data that cannot leave the network, high-volume offline embedding where API costs add up,\n  semantic deduplication, clustering, or fine-tuning an embedder on your domain.\n- **Prefer a hosted embedding API when** volume is modest and you do not want to run GPUs.\n- **Measure before choosing:** build a small labelled set of real queries with their correct passages,\n  measure recall@k and MRR for two or three candidates, choose on that, and record the model name and\n  version alongside every stored vector.\n- **Interviews:** explain bi-encoder versus cross-encoder cost and why in-batch negatives make batch size\n  matter.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Embeds corpora in batches, applies correct prefixes and normalisation, adds a cross-encoder reranker, and fine-tunes with contrastive loss when generic models miss domain terms.","architect":"Owns the embedding model choice and versioning policy, the re-index plan for upgrades, and the decision between self-hosted encoders and a hosted embedding API.","fde":"Runs local embedding inside a customer's network when documents cannot be sent to an API, and evaluates candidate models on the customer's own queries before committing."},"check":{"q":"Your RAG system uses a bi-encoder for top-5 retrieval. Users complain answers cite related but\nwrong passages. Why might adding a cross-encoder fix this, and how would you size it?","a":"A bi-encoder compresses each passage into one vector before seeing the query, so it ranks by\ntopical closeness and can miss which passage actually answers the specific question. A cross-encoder\nreads query and passage together, so attention can match details such as the exact entity, date or\nnegation. I would retrieve a larger candidate set, say top 50, with the bi-encoder to protect recall,\nrerank with a cross-encoder and keep the top 5. Latency grows with the candidate count, so I would\nmeasure recall@50 to make sure the right passage is in the pool, then tune the pool size against p95\nlatency and a precision metric on labelled queries."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a bi-encoder search a million documents quickly while a cross-encoder cannot?","options":["Bi-encoders use smaller transformer models than cross-encoders","Bi-encoders encode documents independently of the query, so document vectors are precomputed once","Cross-encoders cannot run on GPUs","Bi-encoders skip tokenisation entirely"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent encoding lets you store document vectors and answer queries with one forward pass plus a vector lookup. A cross-encoder needs the query and each document together, so nothing can be precomputed."},{"q":"With `MultipleNegativesRankingLoss`, why does increasing batch size often improve the embedder?","options":["Larger batches reduce the learning rate automatically","Larger batches prevent truncation of long inputs","Every other positive in the batch acts as a negative, so larger batches supply more negatives per example","Larger batches make embeddings higher-dimensional"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The loss contrasts each anchor with its positive against all other in-batch positives. More items per batch means a harder, more informative contrast; dimension and truncation are fixed by the model."},{"q":"Dot-product scores in your index favour the same few documents regardless of the query. What is the likely cause?","options":["The cross-encoder was trained on short texts","The model's max sequence length is too large","Embeddings were not normalised, so vector magnitude influences the score","The tokenizer is case-sensitive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without unit normalisation, dot product mixes direction with magnitude, and magnitude can correlate with text properties such as length or rarity, so high-norm documents win everywhere. Normalising makes dot product equal cosine similarity, which compares direction only."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/tiktoken","sources":["Sennrich et al., 2016 — Neural Machine Translation of Rare Words with Subword Units","Official docs: tiktoken"],"why":"- **Every API limit is in tokens.** The context window, maximum output, rate limits (tokens per minute)\n  and the bill are all measured in tokens, not characters or words.\n- **Counting after the request is too late.** The response's usage field cannot prevent a prompt that\n  overflows the window and is rejected, retrieved context that crowds out the answer budget, or a batch\n  job whose cost you could not estimate in advance.\n- **\"About four characters per token\" breaks where it hurts.** It is a fair average for English prose,\n  but code, JSON, URLs, numbers and most non-English scripts produce many more tokens per character. A\n  heuristic fine for a blog post overflows on a stack trace or a Japanese contract, and agent tool\n  results are mostly JSON and code.\n- **Counting via an API** adds latency and a network dependency to every prompt you assemble.\n- **tiktoken ships the exact vocabulary and merge ranks** for OpenAI encodings and applies them locally,\n  fast enough for every request and for whole corpora during chunking.","how":"### Where tokens go in an agent step\n| Part of the request | Grows? | Budget lever |\n|---|---|---|\n| System prompt | fixed | keep it short and stable |\n| Tool schemas (JSON) | fixed per run, scales with tool count | expose only the tools this step needs |\n| Conversation and tool-call history | every step | compact or summarise old turns |\n| Tool results (JSON, code, pages) | every step, often the largest | truncate or summarise before appending |\n| Retrieved chunks | per search | fixed top-k, token-sized chunks |\n| Reserved output | fixed | the model's answer or next tool call |\n\n### Byte-level BPE\n1. **Pre-split.** A regular expression splits text into chunks (words, numbers, punctuation, whitespace\n   runs) in an encoding-specific way.\n2. **Bytes.** Each chunk becomes UTF-8 bytes.\n3. **Merge.** Repeatedly merge the adjacent pair with the lowest rank in the learned merge table until no\n   mergeable pair remains.\n4. **Map.** Each final piece is one token id.\n\n- **No unknown token.** Every byte is in the base vocabulary, so any string, including emoji or\n  invalid-looking input, encodes without a fallback.\n\n### Toy walk-through (made-up ranks)\nRanks: `(l, o)` = 1, `(lo, w)` = 2, `(e, r)` = 3. Chunk: `lower`.\n\n| Step | Lowest-ranked pair present | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| start | — | `l o w e r` |\n| 1 | `(l, o)` | `lo w e r` |\n| 2 | `(lo, w)` | `low e r` |\n| 3 | `(e, r)` | `low er` |\n| stop | (`low`, `er`) has no rank | two tokens |\n\n- **Ranks come from training.** The most frequent pairs in the training corpus merged first, so common\n  words collapse into single tokens and rare strings stay in many small pieces.\n- **That is why counts depend on how \"typical\" your text is.**\n\n### Why the regex pre-split exists\nMerges never cross certain boundaries:\n- **Leading spaces attach to the next word:** `\" world\"` is one token, not a space plus `world`.\n- **Punctuation does not fuse onto words.**\n- **Digits are grouped:** in `cl100k_base` and `o200k_base` digit runs split into groups of at most three.\n- **Without it**, the vocabulary would waste entries on cross-word fragments and numbers would tokenise\n  unpredictably.\n\n### Encodings\nAn encoding is the regex plus the merge ranks plus special tokens.\n\n| Encoding | Models |\n|---|---|\n| `r50k_base`, `p50k_base` | GPT-2/GPT-3-era |\n| `cl100k_base` | GPT-3.5/GPT-4 generation |\n| `o200k_base` | GPT-4o and later |\n\n- **Use `tiktoken.encoding_for_model(name)`**, not a hard-coded encoding, because newer models may use a\n  different one.\n- **Larger vocabularies** tend to encode the same text in fewer tokens, especially non-English text.\n\n### API surface\n| Call | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `enc.encode(text)` | list of ints |\n| `enc.decode(ids)` | text |\n| `enc.encode_ordinary(text)` | ignores special tokens entirely |\n| `encode_batch` | parallelises over many strings |\n\n- **Special tokens raise by default.** `encode` errors if text contains a special-token string such as\n  `<|endoftext|>`. This is deliberate: silently encoding user text as a real control token would let\n  input inject document boundaries. Tool results (scraped pages, files) are untrusted input too.\n- **Opt in** with `allowed_special`, or treat them as plain text with `disallowed_special=()`.\n\n### Chat and tool overhead\n- **Messages add tokens you did not write:** role markers and separators.\n- **Tool definitions are rendered into the prompt** by the provider in a format you do not see, so\n  counting `json.dumps(tools)` is an estimate of their cost, not the exact number.\n- **Not a stable public contract.** The overhead depends on the model, so treat local content counts as\n  close estimates and reconcile against the `usage` field the API returns.\n\n### Caching\n- **First use downloads the rank file** and caches it on disk (set the location with the\n  `TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR` environment variable).\n- **Air-gapped environments** fail on the first call unless the file is pre-placed in that cache.","example":"### Agent context manager: trim history before each step\nBefore every model call, estimate the request and drop the oldest tool results (keeping the system\nprompt, tool schemas and the latest turns) until it fits.\n\n```python\nimport json, tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.encoding_for_model(\"gpt-4o\")          # resolves to o200k_base\nWINDOW, RESERVED_OUTPUT, PER_MSG = 128_000, 4_000, 4  # PER_MSG: rough allowance for role markers\n\ndef n(s: str) -> int:\n    return len(enc.encode(s, disallowed_special=()))  # tool results are untrusted text\n\ndef estimate(messages: list[dict], tools: list[dict]) -> int:\n    body = sum(n(m.get(\"content\") or \"\") + n(json.dumps(m.get(\"tool_calls\", \"\"))) + PER_MSG\n               for m in messages)\n    return body + n(json.dumps(tools))                # estimate: provider renders tools its own way\n\ndef fit(messages: list[dict], tools: list[dict], keep_last: int = 6) -> list[dict]:\n    msgs = list(messages)\n    budget = WINDOW - RESERVED_OUTPUT\n    while estimate(msgs, tools) > budget:\n        old = [i for i, m in enumerate(msgs[1:-keep_last], 1) if m[\"role\"] == \"tool\"\n               and m[\"content\"] != \"[result elided]\"]\n        if not old:\n            raise ValueError(\"cannot fit: compact history or shrink tool schemas\")\n        msgs[old[0]] = {**msgs[old[0]], \"content\": \"[result elided]\"}  # keep tool_call_id pairing\n    return msgs\n```\n\n- **Elide, do not delete, tool messages.** Providers require every tool call to have a matching result,\n  so replace the content and keep the message and its id.\n- **Trim at a boundary.** Each edit to earlier turns breaks provider prompt caching from that point, so\n  compact in larger steps rather than a little on every call.\n\n### Budgeting a RAG prompt so chunks never crowd out the answer\n```python\nimport tiktoken\n\nenc = tiktoken.encoding_for_model(\"gpt-4o\")      # resolves to o200k_base\nCONTEXT, RESERVED_OUTPUT, OVERHEAD = 128_000, 4_000, 200   # overhead: rough allowance for chat formatting\n\ndef n_tokens(s: str) -> int:\n    return len(enc.encode(s, disallowed_special=()))   # treat '<|endoftext|>' in user text as plain text\n\ndef pack(system: str, question: str, chunks: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n    budget = CONTEXT - RESERVED_OUTPUT - OVERHEAD - n_tokens(system) - n_tokens(question)\n    kept = []\n    for c in chunks:                              # chunks already sorted by relevance\n        cost = n_tokens(c)\n        if cost > budget:\n            break\n        kept.append(c)\n        budget -= cost\n    return kept\n\nfor s in [\"hello world\", '{\"user_id\": 98213, \"ok\": true}', \"東京都の天気\"]:\n    ids = enc.encode(s)\n    print(len(s), \"chars ->\", len(ids), \"tokens\", [enc.decode_single_token_bytes(t) for t in ids])\n# \"hello world\" -> [b'hello', b' world']: the space belongs to the second token.\n# For the Japanese string some pieces may be partial UTF-8 byte sequences, not whole characters.\n# The chars-to-tokens ratio differs sharply between English, JSON and Japanese; that spread is\n# exactly why a fixed chars/4 heuristic misbudgets prompts.\n```\n\n- **Truncating by tokens can split a character.** `enc.decode(ids[:n])` may cut a multi-byte character\n  in half, and the decoder then emits a replacement character; truncate on chunk or sentence boundaries\n  where possible.\n\n### The merge loop in pure Python\nRunnable without tiktoken; it reproduces the toy walk-through above.\n\n```python\nRANKS = {(\"l\", \"o\"): 1, (\"lo\", \"w\"): 2, (\"e\", \"r\"): 3}   # toy merge table, lower rank merges first\n\ndef bpe(chunk: str) -> list[str]:\n    parts = list(chunk)                                   # real BPE starts from UTF-8 bytes\n    while True:\n        pairs = [(RANKS.get((a, b)), i) for i, (a, b) in enumerate(zip(parts, parts[1:]))]\n        ranked = [(r, i) for r, i in pairs if r is not None]\n        if not ranked:\n            return parts                                  # no mergeable pair left\n        _, i = min(ranked)                                # lowest rank wins\n        parts[i:i + 2] = [parts[i] + parts[i + 1]]\n        print(\" \".join(parts))\n\nprint(bpe(\"lower\"))    # lo w e r -> low e r -> low er -> ['low', 'er']\nprint(bpe(\"slower\"))   # 's' never merges: ['s', 'low', 'er']\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: how many chunks fit\nUsing the constants above, with an illustrative 1,500-token system prompt, a 300-token question and\n800-token chunks:\n\n| Line item | Tokens |\n|---|---|\n| Context window | 128,000 |\n| − reserved output | 4,000 |\n| − chat overhead allowance | 200 |\n| − system prompt | 1,500 |\n| − question | 300 |\n| **= chunk budget** | **122,000** |\n| Chunks of 800 tokens that fit | 152 |\n\nIf those chunks were sized with chars/4 on code or Japanese, each would really cost far more than 800\ntokens and far fewer would fit; count with the real encoder.\n\n### Worked scenario: when does an agent hit the window?\nIllustrative coding agent on a 128,000-token window: 1,500-token system prompt, 3,000 tokens of tool\nschemas, 4,000 reserved for output, and each step appends a tool call plus a result averaging 2,500\ntokens.\n\n| Quantity | Tokens |\n|---|---|\n| Fixed prefix (system + tools) | 4,500 |\n| Available for history: 128,000 − 4,000 − 4,500 | 119,500 |\n| Steps before overflow at 2,500 per step | 47 |\n| Same agent if results are capped at 1,000 tokens | 119 steps |\n\nCapping or summarising tool results is usually a bigger lever than trimming the system prompt.","patterns":["**Budget every agent step, not just the first.** Run a `fit`-style check before each model call, with an explicit output reserve. Log local estimate next to the API's reported input tokens per step and alert when the gap grows.","**Cap tool results at the tool boundary.** Each tool returns at most a set number of tokens (truncate, paginate or summarise), counted with the real encoder, because one large page or log dump can consume the window. Monitor p95 tool-result tokens per tool.","**Count before you send.** Run `pack`-style budgeting on every dynamically assembled prompt and trim or reject before the call, rather than discovering overflow from an error.","**Resolve the encoding, with a deliberate fallback.** Call `tiktoken.encoding_for_model(model)` and catch `KeyError` for names it does not know, falling back to an explicitly chosen encoding such as `o200k_base`. Log every fallback so new models get checked.","**Pre-seed the cache at build time.** Set `TIKTOKEN_CACHE_DIR` in the image and call `tiktoken.get_encoding(\"o200k_base\")` during the build, so the rank file ships with the container. Monitor first-request failures in offline environments.","**Treat user text and tool output as plain text.** Pass `disallowed_special=()` (or use `encode_ordinary`) for any untrusted input. Never put `<|endoftext|>` into `allowed_special` for user content.","**Batch corpus counting.** Use `enc.encode_batch(texts)` when chunking or costing large corpora, and load the encoding once at module level rather than per request. Monitor chunking throughput."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent's context grows every step: system prompt, tool schemas, each\n  tool call and result. Without a local count you find out about overflow from a rejected request on\n  step 30, after paying for steps 1 to 29.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Your context manager and history trimmer, chunkers in RAG\n  pipelines, LangChain's OpenAI token counting and text splitters that can measure length with tiktoken,\n  LiteLLM's cost and token helpers for OpenAI models, and pre-flight cost estimates for batch evals.\n- **What you control.** The encoding (resolve it per model), the output reserve, how much of the window\n  retrieval and tool results may take, when to compact old turns, and how untrusted text with special\n  tokens is handled.\n- **What you can skip.** Training BPE, and the exact regex of each encoding. Know that counts depend on\n  text type, that chat and tool overhead exist, and that `usage` is the ground truth after the call.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Context budgeting per agent step.** Count system prompt, tool schemas, history and tool results before\n  each call, and trim or compact before the request is rejected.\n- **Capping tool results.** Truncate a large JSON or log tool output to a token budget instead of letting\n  one result crowd out the rest of the transcript.\n- **Chunking for RAG tools.** Split documents by token count so chunks fit retrieval and context budgets.\n- **Pre-flight cost estimates.** Estimate input tokens for a batch eval or a fleet of agent runs before\n  spending money, then reconcile with `usage`.","mini_project":"**Add a token budgeter to an agent loop (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** keep a multi-step agent transcript under a fixed budget without losing the task.\n- **Steps:** build a fake agent loop that appends a verbose JSON tool result each step; count tokens per\n  component with tiktoken before every call; when the total passes a threshold, truncate old tool results\n  first, then summarise old turns; compare your local count with the API's `usage` on a few real calls.\n- **Done when:** a 30-step run never exceeds the budget, and you have a table of local count versus\n  reported usage with the gap explained."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Input text or tool result\"] --> S{\"Contains a special-token string?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes, default encode\"| E[\"Raise error\"]\n    S -->|\"no, or disallowed_special set to empty\"| R[\"Regex pre-split into chunks\"]\n    R --> B[\"Each chunk to UTF-8 bytes\"]\n    B --> M[\"Merge lowest-ranked adjacent pair\"]\n    M --> C{\"Any ranked pair left?\"}\n    C -->|\"yes\"| M\n    C -->|\"no\"| I[\"Map pieces to token ids\"]\n    I --> N[\"len(ids) feeds the agent context budget\"]","caption":"How one string becomes token ids, and where the special-token check sits: the regex split and the rank\ntable decide the count your agent's context manager budgets with."},"interview":[{"q":"What is byte-level BPE, and why do token counts depend on the kind of text?","a":"- **Definition:** text is pre-split by a regex, turned into UTF-8 bytes, and adjacent pairs are merged in\n  order of learned rank until no ranked pair remains; each final piece is one token id.\n- **Ranks come from corpus frequency**, so common English words collapse into single tokens while code,\n  numbers and many non-English scripts stay in more, smaller pieces.\n- **No unknown token exists**, because every byte is in the base vocabulary."},{"q":"Your agent fails with a context-length error on step 30 although your per-step count said it fit. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the encoding first:** hard-coded `cl100k_base` on a newer `o200k_base` model, or tiktoken used\n  for a non-OpenAI model, gives the wrong count.\n- **Then check what you did not count:** role markers, separators and tool definitions also consume\n  tokens, so content-only counts run low near the limit; a single large tool result can also jump the\n  total in one step.\n- **Confirm with data:** log the local count next to the API's reported input tokens per step and\n  inspect where the gap opens."},{"q":"How would you keep a long-running agent inside its context window?","a":"- **Reserve and cap:** fix an output reserve, and cap every tool result at the tool boundary with the\n  real encoder, because tool results usually dominate growth.\n- **Compact deliberately:** when the estimate nears the budget, elide or summarise old tool results in\n  one larger step, keeping call/result pairing and the stable prefix so prompt caching still hits.\n- **Measure:** tokens per step, steps per task, and how often compaction fires, alongside task success."},{"q":"When would you not use tiktoken to count tokens?","a":"- **For Anthropic, Gemini or open models:** tiktoken counts are only an approximation there; use the\n  provider's token-counting endpoint or the model's Hugging Face tokenizer.\n- **For billing:** the `usage` field returned by the API is authoritative, so use local counts for\n  admission control and `usage` for reconciliation.\n- **When a rough number is enough:** a one-off cost guess for English prose may not need exact counting."},{"q":"With a 128,000-token window, how many 800-token chunks fit in a RAG prompt?","a":"- **Subtract fixed costs:** 4,000 reserved output, a 200-token chat overhead allowance, a 1,500-token\n  system prompt and a 300-token question leave a 122,000-token chunk budget.\n- **Divide:** 122,000 ÷ 800 gives 152 chunks that fit.\n- **Caveat:** chunks sized with chars/4 on code or Japanese really cost far more than 800 tokens, so far\n  fewer would fit; count each chunk with the real encoder."},{"q":"Why does `encode` reject `<|endoftext|>` by default, and what do you do with untrusted input?","a":"- **Security reason:** encoding user text as the real control token would let input inject document\n  boundaries, so tiktoken raises instead of guessing.\n- **For untrusted input** pass `disallowed_special=()` or call `encode_ordinary`, which treat the string\n  as plain text.\n- **Never** put `<|endoftext|>` into `allowed_special` for user content."}],"tradeoffs":["**Exact and free vs OpenAI-only.** Local counts are fast and exact for OpenAI models, but only estimates for Anthropic, Gemini or open models; use those providers' own token-counting endpoints or tokenizers when exactness matters. A multi-provider agent needs a per-provider counter.","**Prevention vs CPU per request.** Counting every prompt prevents context-overflow errors before they happen; the CPU cost is negligible for chat, but batch with `encode_batch` over large corpora.","**Trim often vs cache hits.** Trimming a little every step keeps context minimal but breaks prompt caching each time; compacting rarely, in larger steps, keeps the prefix stable and cheaper.","**Predictable chunk sizes vs sentence integrity.** Token-based chunking fits model limits reliably but cuts across sentences unless combined with structural splitting, which can hurt retrieval quality.","**Local estimate vs `usage` field.** `usage` is authoritative and free but arrives after the money is spent; use local counts for admission control and `usage` for billing and reconciliation."],"pitfalls":["**Budgeting a non-OpenAI model with tiktoken.** Prompts that \"fit\" get rejected or truncated by the provider, especially for code and non-English text where tokenizers diverge most.","**Hard-coding `cl100k_base` for every model.** Counts drift for newer models on a different encoding. Symptom: cost estimates that disagree with billed usage.","**`encode` on raw user input without special-token handling.** A document containing `<|endoftext|>` raises an exception and the request fails with a confusing error; in an agent, a scraped page can crash the loop mid-task.","**Forgetting chat-format and tool-schema overhead.** Requests near the limit fail even though your content-only count said they fit, because tool definitions and role markers also consume tokens.","**Deleting tool messages to save tokens.** Removing a tool result but keeping its call breaks the provider's call/result pairing and the request is rejected. Fix: elide the content, keep the message.","**Deploying without internet access.** The first encode call fails trying to download the encoding file, unless the cache directory was pre-populated."],"when":"- **Use when** you assemble prompts dynamically for OpenAI models: budgeting each agent step, packing\n  retrieved context, trimming conversation history, capping tool results, enforcing per-user token\n  limits, chunking documents to a token size, and estimating cost for a batch job before you run it.\n- **Other providers:** only as a rough estimate; prefer their official counter, or the Hugging Face\n  tokenizer for an open model.\n- **Measure** the gap between your local estimate and the API's reported input count\n  (`usage.prompt_tokens` in Chat Completions, `usage.input_tokens` in the Responses API) on real traffic.\n  A stable small gap is chat overhead; a large or growing gap means the wrong encoding.\n- **Interviews:** why character heuristics fail for code and non-English text.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Counts tokens to pack context, trims history to a budget, and chunks corpora by token length using the encoding that matches the target model.","architect":"Sets context-budget policy (reserved output tokens, retrieval share) and decides where local estimates suffice versus where provider-reported usage drives billing.","fde":"Estimates a customer's monthly token spend from a sample of their real documents, and pre-seeds the tiktoken cache for offline deployments."},"check":{"q":"A team estimates monthly cost for a code-review assistant by counting characters in sample\ndiffs and dividing by four. Actual bills come in much higher. Explain why and how you would fix the\nestimate.","a":"The four-characters-per-token rule is an average for English prose. Code has short identifiers,\nsymbols, indentation runs and numbers that BPE splits into more tokens per character, so the heuristic\nundercounts. The fix is to tokenise a representative sample of real diffs with the target model's\nencoding, via `encoding_for_model`, measure the actual token distribution including system prompt,\ntool definitions and chat overhead, and multiply by expected volume and output length. Then reconcile\nwith `usage` numbers from a pilot week, because billed input also includes formatting and any retries."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does tiktoken's `encode` raise an error by default when text contains `<|endoftext|>`?","options":["Because that string cannot be represented in UTF-8","To avoid silently turning user-supplied text into a real control token","Because special tokens are only valid in the output, never the input","Because the encoding file does not contain special tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encoding the string as the actual special token would let untrusted text insert document boundaries. The default forces you to choose: allow it explicitly, or treat it as ordinary text."},{"q":"Your tiktoken count says a prompt is 7,500 tokens, but a non-OpenAI model reports 9,000. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["tiktoken skips whitespace when counting","The provider double-counts the system prompt","The other model uses a different tokenizer, so the same text produces a different number of tokens","tiktoken counts only the last message in a conversation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Token counts are a property of a specific vocabulary and merge table. tiktoken is exact only for OpenAI encodings; for other providers, use their own token-counting tool when the number matters."},{"q":"Why does byte-level BPE never need an \"unknown token\"?","options":["Because its vocabulary includes every word in every language","Because unknown characters are removed before encoding","Because every possible byte is in the base vocabulary, so any string can be encoded","Because it falls back to character-level encoding using Unicode code points"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Text is converted to UTF-8 bytes and all 256 byte values are base tokens, so any input decomposes into known pieces. Merges only make common sequences shorter; they are never required for coverage."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/safetensors","sources":["Official docs: safetensors","Official docs: PyTorch serialization semantics"],"why":"- **Pickle checkpoints are programs.** `torch.save` uses Python's `pickle`, which is not a data format: it\n  is a small program the unpickler executes to rebuild objects, and it can call any importable function,\n  including `os.system`. A downloaded `.bin` or `.pt` checkpoint is executable code.\n- **Hubs made that routine.** Once pulling weights from strangers was normal, \"load a model\" became \"run\n  an untrusted program\", and malicious pickles were found in the wild.\n- **Pickle loading is also slow and all-or-nothing.** Unpickling copies data into freshly allocated\n  memory, sometimes twice when moving to GPU, and you cannot read one tensor without parsing the whole\n  file, so partial loading (sharding across GPUs, inspecting one layer) was awkward.\n- **`weights_only=True` narrows but does not remove the risk.** `torch.load(..., weights_only=True)`\n  restricts the unpickler to an allow-list of types; it is the right default for legacy files (recent\n  PyTorch releases made it the default). But it still runs a pickle interpreter over untrusted input, and\n  its safety rests on the allow-list having no holes.\n- **safetensors removes the interpreter.** The format cannot express code, so there is nothing to\n  restrict. (`torch.load(..., mmap=True)` on the newer zip-based checkpoint format narrows the speed gap,\n  but not the security one.)\n\nThe mechanism fits in four lines: any object can tell pickle how to rebuild it via `__reduce__`, and the\nrebuild recipe is \"call this function with these arguments\".\n\n```python\nimport pickle\nclass Payload:\n    def __reduce__(self):\n        return (print, (\"this ran during load\",))   # an attacker would return (os.system, (\"...\",))\npickle.loads(pickle.dumps(Payload()))               # prints: the call happens inside loads()\n```\n\nNothing in a checkpoint file distinguishes \"rebuild a tensor\" from \"call os.system\"; both are calls.","how":"### What the agent builder does with it\n| Situation | Action |\n|---|---|\n| Serving an open agent model | load only safetensors, staged on local NVMe |\n| Tenants upload LoRA adapters | accept only `adapter_model.safetensors`, audit the header, check shapes match the base |\n| Taking in a legacy `.bin` | convert in a sandbox job, ship only the result |\n| Agent with code execution can download files | never let it `torch.load` an untrusted path; treat it like any untrusted input |\n| Autoscaling model replicas | memory-mapped loads plus local disk keep cold start short |\n\n### Background: file layout\n1. **8 bytes:** an unsigned little-endian 64-bit integer N, the size of the header.\n2. **N bytes:** a UTF-8 JSON object. Each key is a tensor name mapping to\n   `{\"dtype\": \"BF16\", \"shape\": [4096, 4096], \"data_offsets\": [begin, end]}`. An optional `\"__metadata__\"`\n   key holds string-to-string metadata such as the format or training info.\n3. **The rest:** one contiguous byte buffer. Each tensor occupies `[begin, end)` relative to the start of\n   this buffer, stored row-major with no padding inside a tensor.\n\n- **Aligned data.** The reference writer pads the header with spaces so the data buffer starts 8-byte\n  aligned, letting loaders view tensors in place without an alignment copy.\n- **JSON can only describe data.** The header has no way to name a function or class to call, so parsing\n  it cannot run code, unlike a pickle's rebuild instructions.\n- **Validate before touching bytes.** The header is parsed and checked first (offsets in bounds and\n  non-overlapping, sizes matching `dtype × shape`), which blocks malformed files that try to trigger\n  out-of-bounds reads. The design rule: the loader should only ever read.\n\n### Background: zero-copy, lazy loading and sharding\n- **Memory-mapped.** Each tensor sits raw at a known offset, so the OS maps the file into the address space\n  and pages in only the bytes actually read.\n- **Slices are pure arithmetic.** For a row-major `[rows, cols]` tensor of element size `b` starting at\n  `begin`, row `i` lives at bytes `begin + i·cols·b` to `begin + (i+1)·cols·b`; no payload parsing.\n- **`safe_open` handles.** `get_tensor(name)` materialises one tensor and `get_slice(name)[a:b]` reads only\n  a slice. That is how tensor-parallel loaders give each GPU just its shard of a weight matrix without\n  reading the rest.\n- **Sharding: several files plus an index:** `model-00001-of-00004.safetensors`, ... and\n  `model.safetensors.index.json`, which maps each tensor name to its shard. Loaders read the index, then\n  open only the shards they need.\n- **Framework neutral.** Raw bytes plus dtype, not Python objects, so one file loads into PyTorch, NumPy,\n  JAX/Flax, TensorFlow or Rust code (`safetensors.torch`, `safetensors.numpy`, ...).\n\n### What it deliberately does not store\n- **No optimizer state objects, no model class, no arbitrary Python.**\n- **No aliases.** Tensors sharing memory (tied embedding weights) cannot be saved as aliases: `save_file`\n  refuses them, while helpers such as `save_model` deduplicate them and rely on the loading code to re-tie.","example":"### Intake check for a tenant's LoRA adapter\nAn agent platform lets each customer upload a LoRA adapter for their agent. Before it reaches the model\nserver, audit it as plain data with the standard library: no ML library, no code trusted.\n\n```python\nimport json, struct\n\nALLOWED_DTYPES = {\"BF16\", \"F16\", \"F32\"}\n\ndef audit_adapter(path: str, expected_rank: int, max_bytes: int = 500_000_000) -> dict:\n    with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n        n = struct.unpack(\"<Q\", f.read(8))[0]          # header size\n        if n > 10_000_000:\n            raise ValueError(\"header too large\")\n        header = json.loads(f.read(n))\n    meta = header.pop(\"__metadata__\", {})\n    total = 0\n    for name, t in header.items():\n        if \"lora_\" not in name:\n            raise ValueError(f\"unexpected tensor {name}\")   # adapters hold only LoRA matrices\n        if t[\"dtype\"] not in ALLOWED_DTYPES:\n            raise ValueError(f\"bad dtype {t['dtype']} for {name}\")\n        if expected_rank not in t[\"shape\"]:\n            raise ValueError(f\"rank mismatch in {name}: {t['shape']}\")\n        begin, end = t[\"data_offsets\"]\n        total += end - begin\n    if total > max_bytes:\n        raise ValueError(\"adapter larger than policy allows\")\n    return {\"tensors\": len(header), \"bytes\": total, \"metadata\": meta}\n```\n\n- **Only `.safetensors` is accepted.** A `.bin` upload is rejected outright, or converted in a sandbox.\n- **Passing the audit is not passing review.** The adapter still runs an eval suite (including safety\n  prompts) before the router sends that tenant's traffic to it.\n\n### Save, load lazily, and read the header by hand\n```python\nimport json, struct, torch\nfrom safetensors.torch import save_file, load_file\nfrom safetensors import safe_open\n\nweights = {\n    \"embed.weight\": torch.randn(32000, 512, dtype=torch.bfloat16),\n    \"head.weight\": torch.randn(10, 512),\n}\nsave_file(weights, \"model.safetensors\", metadata={\"format\": \"pt\", \"run\": \"exp-42\"})\n\nwith open(\"model.safetensors\", \"rb\") as f:            # no library needed to inspect\n    n = struct.unpack(\"<Q\", f.read(8))[0]\n    header = json.loads(f.read(n))\ne = header[\"embed.weight\"]      # {'dtype': 'BF16', 'shape': [32000, 512], 'data_offsets': [begin, end]}\nprint(e[\"data_offsets\"][1] - e[\"data_offsets\"][0])     # 32768000 bytes\n\nwith safe_open(\"model.safetensors\", framework=\"pt\", device=\"cpu\") as f:\n    rows = f.get_slice(\"embed.weight\")[:1000]          # reads 1000 rows, not the whole matrix\n    print(rows.shape, f.metadata())\n\nfull = load_file(\"model.safetensors\")                  # dict[str, Tensor]\n```\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Embedding size | 32,000 × 512 × 2 bytes = 32,768,000 bytes, exactly `end - begin` in its `data_offsets` |\n| 1,000-row slice | 1,000 × 512 × 2 = 1,024,000 bytes, about 3% of the tensor |\n\n- **Read offsets, never assume them**, because where each tensor starts depends on the writer's ordering.\n- **Lazy loading pays off** when each of eight tensor-parallel ranks needs only an eighth of every matrix.\n- **Auditable with the standard library.** `json` and `struct` alone list every tensor, dtype and shape,\n  with no ML library installed and no code trusted.\n\n### Convert a legacy checkpoint in a sandbox\nThe intake step for an external `.bin`: load with the restricted unpickler in an isolated job, write\nsafetensors, ship only the result.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom safetensors.torch import save_file\n\nsd = torch.load(\"pytorch_model.bin\", map_location=\"cpu\", weights_only=True)   # allow-list unpickler\nsd = {k: v.contiguous() for k, v in sd.items()}        # save_file requires contiguous tensors\nsave_file(sd, \"model.safetensors\", metadata={\"format\": \"pt\", \"source\": \"pytorch_model.bin\"})\n# Tied weights raise a shared-tensor error here; for a full model use safetensors.torch.save_model.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: one weight matrix across eight ranks\nA `[4096, 4096]` BF16 projection split row-wise for tensor parallelism, as when an agent model is\nserved across 8 GPUs:\n\n| Item | Bytes |\n|---|---|\n| Whole matrix: 4096 × 4096 × 2 | 33,554,432 |\n| One rank's 512 rows: 512 × 4096 × 2 | 4,194,304 |\n| Read by all 8 ranks with `get_slice` | 33,554,432 in total, each byte once |\n| Read by all 8 ranks with full loads | 268,435,456, then 7/8 discarded |","patterns":["**Reject external pickles at the door.** CI or the model registry blocks `.bin`/`.pt`/`.pkl` artifacts from outside sources, including tenant-uploaded adapters, unless converted in a sandbox (as above). Monitor rejection counts and who tried.","**Force safetensors at load.** Pass `use_safetensors=True` to `from_pretrained` so Transformers errors instead of silently falling back to a pickle file in the repo. Fail CI on any `weights_only=False` in the codebase, because that is the usual \"fix\" for a load error.","**Stage weights on local NVMe.** Copy checkpoints from network or object storage to local disk before memory-mapped loading, so a new agent-model replica serves quickly during a traffic spike. Monitor cold-start time to first token per storage path.","**Stamp provenance in metadata.** Write source repo, revision and training run (for example the agent-trace dataset version) into `__metadata__` (string-to-string), and read it back with `f.metadata()` at deploy time. Fail the deploy if it is missing.","**Audit headers before promotion.** Parse the header with `json` and `struct`, and check tensor names, dtypes and shapes against the expected config. Flag unexpected tensors or dtypes."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** If you self-host an agent model, serve per-tenant LoRA adapters, or let\n  an agent platform pull models from a hub, every load is a supply-chain decision. A pickle checkpoint is\n  a program; a safetensors file is data.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `model.safetensors` and shard index files in every Hub repo,\n  `adapter_model.safetensors` written by PEFT, and the weights vLLM, SGLang and Text Embeddings Inference\n  load when your agent's model server starts or scales out.\n- **What you control.** Requiring safetensors at load (`use_safetensors=True`), rejecting pickle uploads,\n  converting legacy files in a sandbox, stamping provenance metadata, and staging weights on local disk\n  for fast autoscaling.\n- **What you can skip.** The byte layout details and writing loaders yourself. Remember three facts: no\n  code on load, lazy memory-mapped reads, and \"safe to load\" is not \"safe to run\".","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosted agent models.** Model servers load `model.safetensors` shards, and memory-mapping speeds\n  cold starts when agent traffic scales out.\n- **Per-tenant LoRA adapters.** PEFT writes `adapter_model.safetensors`, so accepting adapters from teams or\n  customers does not mean running their code on load.\n- **Model intake policy.** Agent platforms that pull from a hub can require safetensors and reject pickle\n  checkpoints as a supply-chain control.\n- **Provenance tracking.** Header metadata can record which trace dataset and run produced a fine-tuned\n  agent model.","mini_project":"**Write a safe model intake check for an agent platform (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** accept only safe, traceable weights before an agent model server loads them.\n- **Steps:** write a script that rejects `.bin`, `.pt` and `.pkl` files in a model folder; for each\n  safetensors file, read the header only and list tensor names, dtypes and shapes; require a provenance\n  key in the metadata; load the model with `use_safetensors=True`.\n- **Done when:** the script passes a clean folder, fails a folder containing a pickle file, and prints a\n  short manifest you could attach to a deployment."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Checkpoint or uploaded adapter\"] --> K{\"Format?\"}\n    K -->|\"pickle .bin or .pt\"| P[\"Unpickler executes rebuild calls\"]\n    P -.->|\"any importable function, even os.system\"| X[\"Code runs during load\"]\n    K -->|\"safetensors\"| H[\"Read 8-byte header size N\"]\n    H --> J[\"Parse N bytes of JSON: dtype, shape, data_offsets\"]\n    J --> V{\"Offsets in bounds, sizes match dtype times shape?\"}\n    V -->|\"no\"| R[\"Reject malformed file\"]\n    V -->|\"yes\"| MM[\"Memory-map the data buffer\"]\n    MM --> G[\"get_tensor: one whole tensor\"]\n    MM --> SL[\"get_slice: only this rank's rows\"]","caption":"Compare the two load paths an agent model server might take: a pickle checkpoint runs an interpreter\nover the file, while a safetensors load only reads, validates and maps bytes."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is loading a pickle checkpoint a security risk, and how does safetensors remove it?","a":"- **Pickle is a program, not data:** the unpickler executes rebuild instructions, and `__reduce__` can\n  name any importable callable, including `os.system`, so a downloaded `.bin` is executable code.\n- **safetensors cannot express code:** an 8-byte header size, a JSON header and raw bytes, validated\n  before any tensor is touched.\n- **So there is no interpreter to restrict**, unlike `weights_only=True`, which still parses untrusted\n  pickle through an allow-list."},{"q":"Your agent platform lets each customer upload a LoRA adapter. How do you make that safe?","a":"- **Format gate:** accept only safetensors, so loading cannot run code; reject or sandbox-convert pickles.\n- **Header audit:** parse with `json` and `struct`, and check tensor names, dtypes, rank and total size\n  against the base model's expected adapter shapes before the server sees the file.\n- **Behaviour gate:** run the tenant's agent evals and safety prompts on base plus adapter before routing\n  traffic, because a file that loads safely can still behave badly."},{"q":"A load fails on a legacy checkpoint and a teammate proposes `weights_only=False`. What do you do?","a":"- **Refuse the quick fix:** it re-enables arbitrary code execution, and the error was the safety check\n  working.\n- **Convert in a sandbox instead:** load with `weights_only=True` in an isolated job, make tensors\n  contiguous, write with `save_file` (or `save_model` for tied weights) and ship only the safetensors file.\n- **Then enforce it:** pass `use_safetensors=True` to `from_pretrained` so pickle fallbacks error."},{"q":"When is safetensors not the format you ship?","a":"- **Internal training-resume checkpoints:** optimizer and scheduler state are Python objects, which\n  safetensors deliberately does not store, so `torch.save` stays common there.\n- **Condition:** only for files your own jobs wrote and never for anything from an untrusted location.\n- **Local llama.cpp-style inference** uses GGUF instead, which also embeds tokenizer metadata and\n  quantized types, so conversion from safetensors is a separate deployment step."},{"q":"How many bytes does each of 8 tensor-parallel ranks read for a 4096 by 4096 BF16 matrix?","a":"- **Whole matrix:** 4096 × 4096 × 2 = 33,554,432 bytes.\n- **One rank's 512 rows with `get_slice`:** 512 × 4096 × 2 = 4,194,304 bytes, so all 8 ranks read\n  33,554,432 bytes in total, each byte once.\n- **With full loads** they read 268,435,456 bytes and discard 7/8, which is why offsets plus memory\n  mapping matter for cold start."},{"q":"New agent-model replicas take minutes to serve during traffic spikes, even with safetensors. Where do you look?","a":"- **Storage path first:** memory-mapped loading pages in bytes on demand, and page faults over network\n  filesystems or object storage mounts can be slow.\n- **Fix:** stage checkpoints on local NVMe before loading (or bake them into the node image) and compare\n  time to first token per storage path.\n- **Also check sharding:** a missing `model.safetensors.index.json` makes loaders fail or load only part\n  of the model."}],"tradeoffs":["**No code on load vs tensors only.** It removes a whole class of supply-chain attacks, but the format only holds tensors; tokenizer files, configs and training state need their own (also non-executable) formats, and chat templates in tokenizer configs still shape how your agent's tools are rendered.","**Memory-mapped speed vs slow storage.** Loading is fast and lazy on local disk, but on network filesystems or object storage mounts page faults can be slow; copying to local NVMe first often wins for large models.","**Portability vs object graphs.** Framework-neutral raw bytes travel anywhere, but you lose pickling an entire object graph; full training checkpoints with optimizer and scheduler state still often use `torch.save`.","**Transformers standard vs GGUF.** safetensors is the standard for Transformers-style checkpoints, but llama.cpp-style local inference uses GGUF, which also embeds tokenizer metadata and quantized types; conversion is a deployment step."],"pitfalls":["**Assuming safetensors makes a model trustworthy.** It only guarantees loading runs no code; weights can still be backdoored, and `trust_remote_code` model classes can still execute arbitrary Python. A backdoored agent model could pick harmful tool calls on a trigger phrase, which only evals reveal.","**Loading legacy `.bin` files with `weights_only=False` because a load error appeared.** This re-enables arbitrary code execution; the symptom you were fixing was the safety check working.","**Saving tied weights via `save_file` directly.** It raises an error about shared tensors; naive workarounds that clone the tensors store the bytes twice, and the tie then survives only if loading code re-ties the parameters, which `save_model` / `load_model` handle for you.","**Forgetting the index file when copying sharded checkpoints.** Loaders fail to find tensors or load only part of the model, and the error names a missing key rather than a missing file."],"when":"- **Use for** any weights you publish, download, mirror or move between teams, including LoRA adapters\n  trained on agent traces; convert legacy pickle checkpoints when you take them in.\n- **Policy:** \"no pickle from outside\". CI or the model registry rejects `.bin`/`.pt`/`.pkl` artifacts\n  from external sources unless converted in a sandbox.\n- **Keep `torch.save` only** for internal training-resume checkpoints holding optimizer state, and never\n  load those from untrusted locations.\n- **Skip the details when** your agents only call hosted APIs; the format matters once you hold weights.\n- **Measure** time to first token after a cold start with memory-mapped local files versus network\n  storage, when loading time matters.\n- **Interviews:** explain why pickle is unsafe and what memory mapping buys.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Saves and loads weights with safetensors, converts legacy checkpoints, and uses `safe_open` slices to inspect or shard weights without loading the whole file.","architect":"Sets the model-artifact policy: allowed formats, conversion and scanning in a sandbox, provenance metadata, and storage layout that keeps cold-start loading fast.","fde":"Meets customers whose security teams block pickle files, and shows that safetensors artifacts can be audited as plain data before they enter the customer's network."},"check":{"q":"A security reviewer says, \"We switched to safetensors, so models from the hub are now safe to\nrun.\" What is right and what is wrong about that statement?","a":"It is right that loading a safetensors file cannot execute code: the format is a JSON header plus\nraw bytes, so the pickle-based remote-code-execution path is gone. It is wrong to conclude the model is\nsafe. Weights can encode backdoors or poisoned behaviour that only an evaluation reveals; repositories\ncan still require `trust_remote_code`, which runs Python model code; tokenizers and configs come from the\nsame source; and other files in the repo may still be pickles. A sound policy combines safetensors-only\nweights, pinned revisions, disallowing remote code unless reviewed, provenance checks, and behavioural\nevaluation before deployment."},"quiz":[{"q":"What makes loading a pickle-based PyTorch checkpoint dangerous?","options":["Pickle files are not compressed, so they can exhaust disk space","Unpickling executes instructions that can call arbitrary Python functions","Pickle stores tensors in a format GPUs cannot read","Pickle files cannot be checksummed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pickle is a small program run by the unpickler to rebuild objects, and it may invoke any importable callable. safetensors stores only a JSON header and raw bytes, so there is no program to run."},{"q":"How does safetensors let each GPU load only its shard of a large weight matrix?","options":["It splits every tensor into one file per GPU at save time","It compresses tensors so they decompress faster","Tensors are stored raw at known offsets, so a slice can be read from a memory-mapped file","It stores the model class, which knows how to shard itself"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Known offsets plus row-major raw storage let a loader compute and read only the bytes for the requested rows. Sharded files exist too, but slicing works within a single tensor."},{"q":"Why might you still use `torch.save` inside a training job?","options":["Because safetensors cannot store bf16 tensors","Because safetensors files cannot exceed 2 GB","To checkpoint optimizer and scheduler state, which are Python objects rather than plain tensor dicts","Because safetensors loads slower than pickle in every case"],"answer":2,"explanation":"safetensors deliberately stores only named tensors. Training-resume checkpoints often include non-tensor state, so `torch.save` remains common internally, as long as those files never come from untrusted sources."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/openai-sdk","sources":["Official docs: OpenAI API reference","Official docs: openai-python"],"why":"- **Raw HTTP breaks on the details.** 429 rate limits and transient 5xx errors are routine at volume and\n  need backoff; requests can hang for minutes without a timeout.\n- **Payloads are awkward to parse safely.** Streaming arrives as server-sent events parsed incrementally;\n  tool calls arrive as JSON strings inside nested structures whose shape changes as features land; every\n  dict field you read is an unchecked guess about the schema.\n- **Hand-rolled clients fail predictably.** Teams reinvent retries badly (retrying non-idempotent work, or\n  retrying 400s forever) and discover schema changes as `KeyError`s in production.\n- **The SDK encodes the contract once.** Generated from the OpenAPI specification, it gives typed,\n  autocompletable parameters and responses, a precise exception hierarchy, and one implementation of\n  pooling, retry with backoff, timeouts and streaming.\n- **Most agent debugging happens here.** What this client sent and received, meaning the exact messages,\n  the tool schema, the `usage` counts and the request ID you give to support, is why to learn it before\n  hiding it behind a framework.","how":"### Client and transport\n- **Env-configured.** `OpenAI()` reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` (and optionally organisation and project) and\n  builds an `httpx` client with connection pooling.\n- **Async twin.** `AsyncOpenAI` exposes the same methods as coroutines for asyncio servers, which is how\n  you run many agent sessions, or parallel tool-using sub-agents, in one process.\n- **One client per process**, reused, because each client owns a connection pool; one per request defeats\n  keep-alive.\n- **`base_url`** points the same client at any compatible server.\n- **Azure.** A dedicated `AzureOpenAI` client exists because classic deployment-based URLs, the\n  `api-version` parameter and Entra ID auth differ. Azure's newer v1-style endpoints can also be reached\n  with the plain client and a `base_url`; check which one your deployment exposes.\n\n### Two generation APIs\n| | Chat Completions | Responses |\n|---|---|---|\n| Call | `client.chat.completions.create` | `client.responses.create` |\n| Input | role-tagged `messages` | `input` plus `instructions` |\n| Output | `choices[0].message` | list of typed output items; `response.output_text` concatenates text |\n| State | stateless: resend the whole history each call | optional server-side conversation state, built-in tools |\n| Best for | portability; the shape other providers copied | new OpenAI-specific features, which tend to arrive here first |\n\nServer-side state (`previous_response_id`, or a conversation object):\n- **Saves resending history over the wire, not paying for it.** The model still reads earlier turns, and\n  they are billed as input tokens on each call, which is why long agent runs cost more per step.\n- **Moves the transcript into provider storage**, which matters for retention policies. `store=False` opts\n  out, at the cost of managing history yourself.\n\n### Typed responses and structured output\n- **Pydantic responses.** `resp.usage.prompt_tokens` is checked attribute access, not a dict lookup.\n- **`parse` helpers** take a Pydantic class, convert it to a strict JSON Schema, and return the validated\n  object (`output_parsed` on Responses, `message.parsed` on Chat Completions).\n- **Strict mode constrains decoding** to the schema, stronger than asking for JSON in the prompt. You still\n  handle refusals and truncated outputs. Agents use it for plans, routing decisions and final answers.\n\n### Tools: the agent loop\n1. You pass JSON Schema function definitions.\n2. The model may return tool calls whose `arguments` field is a JSON string, possibly several in one turn.\n3. Your code parses and validates the arguments and runs the function.\n4. You send the result back in a follow-up message tied to the call's ID.\n5. Repeat until the model answers without tool calls, or your step limit or deadline is reached.\n\n- **The SDK does not execute tools.** That loop is your agent code, and so are its guards: a maximum step\n  count, an overall deadline, an allow-list of tool names and argument validation.\n- **Every call must get a result.** Each `tool_call_id` in an assistant turn needs a matching tool\n  message, even if the result is an error, or the next request is rejected.\n\n### Streaming\n- **`stream=True`** returns an iterator of chunks (Chat Completions) or typed events (Responses) parsed from\n  server-sent events, so you can forward tokens as they arrive.\n- **Usage arrives only at the end.** Chat Completions includes it in a final chunk only with\n  `stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}`; Responses carries it on the closing `response.completed`\n  event. Log usage from the final event.\n\n### Errors, retries, timeouts\n| Exception (all subclass `openai.APIError`) | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `APIConnectionError`, `APITimeoutError` | transport: no HTTP response arrived |\n| `RateLimitError` | 429 |\n| `BadRequestError` | 400 |\n| `AuthenticationError` | 401 |\n\n- **Status errors share a parent.** `RateLimitError`, `BadRequestError` and `AuthenticationError` are all\n  `APIStatusError` subclasses carrying `status_code`, so catch the specific class first and\n  `APIStatusError` last.\n- **Default retries:** connection errors, 408, 409, 429 and 5xx are retried twice (`max_retries=2`) with\n  exponential backoff and jitter, honouring a `retry-after` header when sent, because those failures are\n  plausibly transient.\n- **No retry on 4xx like 400**, because resending the same bad request cannot succeed.\n- **Default timeout is 600 seconds**, far too long for an interactive path; set your own per client or per\n  call with `with_options`.\n- **Worst-case wall clock** is roughly `timeout × (max_retries + 1)` plus backoff, per model call; an agent\n  multiplies that by its step count.\n- **Generation is not idempotent.** If a timeout fires after the server started work, the retry is a\n  second, separately billed generation, so aggressive timeouts on long outputs raise cost as well as\n  latency. Tool side effects are not retried by the SDK, but your loop must not re-run them blindly.\n\n### Raw responses and headers\n- **Typed objects hide the HTTP layer**, usually what you want, except for the headers.\n- **`client.with_raw_response.chat.completions.create(...)`** returns the raw response; its headers carry\n  the request ID and rate-limit state (`x-ratelimit-remaining-requests`, `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens`),\n  and `.parse()` gives the usual typed object.\n- **Throttle before the 429.** Reading remaining-token headers lets a batch worker slow down before it hits\n  429s instead of after.","example":"### A bounded agent loop (Chat Completions)\nThe SDK only transports each call; the step limit, deadline, tool allow-list, argument validation and the\nreply for every tool call are yours. Model names change; read them from configuration.\n\n```python\nimport json, os, time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nMODEL = os.environ.get(\"AGENT_MODEL\", \"gpt-4o-mini\")\nclient = OpenAI(timeout=20.0, max_retries=1)          # per step: worst case ~2 × 20 s + backoff\n\ntools = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_order\", \"description\": \"Look up an order by id\", \"strict\": True,\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"order_id\"], \"additionalProperties\": False}}}]\n\ndef get_order(order_id: str) -> dict:\n    return {\"order_id\": order_id, \"status\": \"shipped\"}\n\nTOOLS = {\"get_order\": get_order}\n\ndef run_agent(question: str, max_steps: int = 8, deadline_s: float = 90.0) -> str:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    start = time.monotonic()\n    for step in range(max_steps):\n        if time.monotonic() - start > deadline_s:\n            return \"Sorry, that took too long. Please try again.\"\n        resp = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, tools=tools)\n        print(step, resp._request_id, resp.usage.prompt_tokens, resp.usage.completion_tokens)\n        msg = resp.choices[0].message\n        if not msg.tool_calls:\n            return msg.content                         # final answer\n        messages.append(msg)                           # the assistant turn that asked for tools\n        for call in msg.tool_calls:                    # may be several, in one turn\n            fn = TOOLS.get(call.function.name)\n            try:\n                args = json.loads(call.function.arguments)        # model-generated, may be invalid\n                result = fn(**args) if fn else {\"error\": \"unknown tool\"}\n            except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError) as e:\n                result = {\"error\": f\"bad arguments: {e}\"}         # let the model correct itself\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id, \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n    return \"Step limit reached.\"\n\nprint(run_agent(\"Where is order A123?\"))\n```\n\n- **Errors go back to the model.** Returning `{\"error\": ...}` as the tool result lets the model fix its\n  arguments on the next step, instead of crashing the loop.\n- **Local server:** the same loop runs against vLLM with `OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\",\n  api_key=\"unused\")`, provided that server implements the endpoint you call and tool-call parsing for\n  that model is enabled.\n\n### Structured output for a routing step, with explicit errors\nA classifier step at the front of an agent: decide the category before choosing which tools to expose.\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nimport openai\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nMODEL = os.environ.get(\"CLASSIFIER_MODEL\", \"gpt-4o-mini\")\nclient = OpenAI(timeout=20.0, max_retries=3)        # one client per process; worst case ~4 × 20 s + backoff\n\nclass Triage(BaseModel):\n    category: Literal[\"billing\", \"bug\", \"how_to\", \"other\"]\n    urgent: bool\n    summary: str\n\ndef triage(ticket: str) -> Triage | None:\n    try:\n        resp = client.responses.parse(\n            model=MODEL,\n            instructions=\"Classify the support ticket.\",\n            input=ticket,\n            text_format=Triage,                     # Pydantic class -> strict JSON Schema\n        )\n    except openai.RateLimitError:\n        raise                                       # already retried; let the queue back off\n    except openai.BadRequestError as e:\n        print(\"bad request, not retrying:\", e.status_code, e.message)\n        return None\n    print(\"request id:\", resp._request_id, \"tokens:\", resp.usage.input_tokens, resp.usage.output_tokens)\n    return resp.output_parsed                       # a validated Triage, or None on refusal\n\nprint(triage(\"I was charged twice this month and need it fixed today.\"))\n```\n\n- **On the wire:** `parse` turns `Triage` into a JSON Schema (an enum for `category`, a boolean, a string,\n  every field required, no extra properties) sent as a strict text format. The server constrains decoding\n  to matching JSON; the SDK validates it back into a `Triage`.\n- **Retry accounting:** a 429 is retried up to three times before `RateLimitError` reaches the `except`, so\n  re-raising hands the failure to an outer queue instead of retrying a fourth time in-process.\n\n### Worked scenario: worst-case time a worker is held\nUsing `timeout × (max_retries + 1)`, before backoff:\n\n| Settings | Worst case per call |\n|---|---|\n| Defaults: 600 s, `max_retries=2` | 1,800 s (30 minutes) |\n| Classifier above: 20 s, `max_retries=3` | 80 s |\n| Interactive path: 10 s, `max_retries=1` | 20 s |\n| Queued batch job: 60 s, `max_retries=0`, queue owns backoff | 60 s per attempt |\n| Agent above: 20 s, `max_retries=1`, up to 8 steps | 40 s per step, 320 s per task without a deadline |\n\nDuring a provider incident, the first row is how a few slow calls exhaust a worker pool, and the last row\nis why an agent needs a task-level deadline (90 s above) on top of per-call timeouts.","patterns":["**Bound every agent run.** Enforce a maximum step count, a wall-clock deadline and a token budget per task, and return a graceful answer when one trips. Monitor steps per task and the share of runs hitting each limit; a rising share signals a prompt or tool regression.","**Trace each step, not just each task.** Record `resp._request_id`, model, input and output tokens, latency and the tool calls per step in structured logs or a tracer (LangSmith, Langfuse or OpenTelemetry). Use them to answer \"why did the agent do that\" and \"why this bill\".","**Strict tool schemas plus server-side validation.** Use `additionalProperties: false` and required fields, then validate again in the tool (types, ranges, permissions), because the model chooses the arguments. Monitor invalid-argument rate per tool.","**Budget timeouts per path.** Create one client, then derive variants with `client.with_options(timeout=10.0, max_retries=1)` for user-facing calls and longer budgets for batch. Monitor `APITimeoutError` and `RateLimitError` rates separately, because they need different fixes.","**Throttle batch workers from headers.** Use `with_raw_response` and read `x-ratelimit-remaining-tokens`, slowing the worker as it approaches zero, for example during an offline eval run over thousands of agent tasks. Monitor 429 count per hour; it should trend to zero.","**Stream with usage on.** Pass `stream=True` with `stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}` on Chat Completions, forward deltas to the user, and record usage from the final chunk. Monitor time to first token.","**Async client in async servers.** Use `AsyncOpenAI` in FastAPI or other asyncio apps, created once at startup, so many agent sessions share one process. Monitor event-loop lag and concurrent in-flight calls."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent is a loop of model calls: send history plus tool schemas, get\n  tool calls back, run them, append results, repeat. This SDK transports every step; the loop, the\n  argument validation, the step limit and the deadline are your code.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Directly in hand-written agents, under LangChain's `ChatOpenAI`\n  (the `langchain-openai` package) and therefore LangGraph nodes, under OpenAI's own Agents SDK, inside\n  LiteLLM for OpenAI models, and wrapped by tracing tools such as LangSmith's OpenAI wrapper.\n- **What you control.** Timeouts and `max_retries` per path, the tool schemas (strict, with\n  `additionalProperties: false`), Chat Completions versus Responses, `store=False`, streaming, and what you\n  log per step (request ID, usage, tool calls).\n- **What you can skip.** Raw HTTP and SSE parsing, and most Responses-only features if you need\n  portability. Learn the tool-call round trip and retry semantics before any framework hides them.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Hand-written tool-calling agents.** The SDK carries every step of the loop: send history and tool\n  schemas, read tool calls, append results with their call IDs, repeat.\n- **Structured extraction steps.** Strict schemas with `additionalProperties: false` give parseable output\n  for planners, classifiers and form-filling agents.\n- **One client for many backends.** Changing `base_url` points the same agent code at vLLM, Ollama or a\n  gateway for cheaper or private steps.\n- **Agent observability.** Logging request ID, usage and tool calls per step turns a failed run into a\n  readable trace.","mini_project":"**Build a guarded tool-calling agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a small agent that answers questions with two tools and never runs away.\n- **Steps:** define two tools with strict schemas; write the loop yourself; validate arguments before\n  running a tool and return errors as tool results; add a step limit, a per-call timeout and\n  `max_retries`; log request ID, usage and tool calls per step.\n- **Done when:** 10 test questions complete within the step limit, a malformed argument is recovered\n  from, and you have a per-step log with total tokens per run."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant A as Agent loop\n    participant C as OpenAI client\n    participant S as API or compatible server\n    A->>C: create with model, messages, tools, timeout\n    C->>S: POST over pooled httpx connection\n    S-->>C: 429 or 5xx with retry-after\n    Note over C: backoff with jitter, up to max_retries\n    C->>S: retry same request\n    S-->>C: 200 with tool_calls, usage, request id\n    C-->>A: typed response object\n    Note over A: validate arguments, run tool, check step limit\n    A->>C: create again with tool result and tool_call_id\n    C->>S: POST full history plus tool result\n    S-->>C: 200 final message, usage\n    C-->>A: typed final message to log","caption":"One agent step through the SDK: retries happen inside the client, the tool loop happens in your code,\nand the request ID and usage come back for logging on every iteration."},"interview":[{"q":"Why use the official SDK instead of calling the HTTP API with `requests`?","a":"- **It encodes the contract once:** generated from the OpenAPI specification, it gives typed,\n  autocompletable parameters and responses that your editor and type checker verify, instead of unchecked\n  dict lookups that fail as `KeyError`s in production.\n- **It ships the hard parts:** connection pooling, retries with exponential backoff and jitter that honour\n  `retry-after`, timeouts, server-sent-event parsing and a precise exception hierarchy.\n- **Hand-rolled clients** tend to retry 400s or non-idempotent work, or forget timeouts entirely."},{"q":"Walk me through the tool-calling loop you would write on this SDK, and its guards.","a":"- **Loop:** send messages plus tool schemas; if the reply has tool calls, append that assistant turn, run\n  each allowed tool with validated arguments, append one tool message per `tool_call_id`, and call again.\n- **Guards:** a step limit, a task deadline, a token budget, an allow-list of tool names, and errors\n  returned to the model as tool results so it can self-correct.\n- **Observability:** log request ID, usage and tool calls per step, so a bad run can be replayed."},{"q":"Token spend on long generations rose after you lowered the client timeout. Why?","a":"- **Generation is not idempotent:** if the timeout fires after the server started work, the SDK's retry\n  starts a second, separately billed generation.\n- **So short timeouts on long outputs raise cost as well as latency**, and the first attempt still bills.\n- **Fix:** size timeouts to the output length per path, and stream long responses: the client's read\n  timeout then measures the gap between chunks, not the whole generation. For queued jobs set\n  `max_retries=0` so the queue owns backoff."},{"q":"When would you choose Chat Completions over the Responses API for an agent?","a":"- **Portability:** Chat Completions is the shape vLLM, Ollama and gateways copied, so the same agent loop\n  reaches them via `base_url`.\n- **Data control:** Responses can keep server-side conversation state, which moves transcripts into\n  provider storage; `store=False` opts out at the cost of managing history yourself.\n- **Choose Responses** when you need new OpenAI-specific features or built-in tools, which tend to arrive\n  there first."},{"q":"What is the worst-case time one agent task can hold a worker?","a":"- **Per call:** roughly `timeout × (max_retries + 1)` plus backoff; defaults of 600 seconds and\n  `max_retries=2` give 1,800 seconds, 30 minutes.\n- **Per task:** multiply by the step count; 20 seconds, `max_retries=1` and 8 steps is 320 seconds.\n- **Fix:** a short interactive variant (10 seconds, `max_retries=1`, 20 seconds per call) from\n  `client.with_options`, plus a task-level deadline that ends the loop."},{"q":"How do you get token usage when streaming?","a":"- **Chat Completions:** usage appears in a final chunk only when you pass\n  `stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}`.\n- **Responses:** usage is carried on the closing `response.completed` event.\n- **In both cases** log usage from the final event, and monitor time to first token separately from total\n  latency, because streaming changes what users perceive."}],"tradeoffs":["**Hand-written loop vs framework.** A raw SDK loop is small, debuggable and has no hidden prompts; LangGraph or the OpenAI Agents SDK add state graphs, handoffs, persistence and tracing. Start raw, adopt a framework when you need durable state or multi-agent control flow.","**Immediate features vs provider coupling.** The official SDK tracks new features immediately but couples code to one provider's shapes; a thin internal interface or a router like LiteLLM pays off once you genuinely use several providers.","**Free retries vs multiplied latency.** Built-in retries handle transient failures with no code, but multiply latency and cost on overload; in queued batch jobs prefer `max_retries=0` and let the queue own backoff.","**Responses features vs portability.** Server-side state and built-in tools are not mirrored by other providers, so Chat Completions is better when portability matters more than new features.","**Schema guarantee vs schema expressiveness.** Strict structured outputs guarantee conformance but restrict JSON Schema features; complex unions or open-ended dicts may need a looser schema plus Pydantic validation."],"pitfalls":["**An unbounded tool loop.** The model keeps calling tools (often the same one) and the task never ends. Symptom: runaway token spend on a few sessions. Fix: step limit, deadline, and detection of repeated identical calls.","**Dropping a tool result.** An assistant turn with tool calls and a missing matching tool message makes the next request fail with a 400. Fix: always append one result per `tool_call_id`, errors included.","**A new client per request.** Connection setup on every call raises latency; under load you see socket exhaustion or many TLS handshakes in traces.","**The default long timeout in a user-facing endpoint.** One stuck upstream call ties up a worker for minutes. Symptom: request pile-ups during provider incidents.","**The sync client inside an async web server.** The event loop blocks and every concurrent request stalls while one LLM call is in flight; use `AsyncOpenAI`.","**Trusting tool-call `arguments` as valid.** They are model-generated JSON strings. Symptom: occasional `JSONDecodeError` or wrong-typed fields crashing the tool layer.","**Not logging request IDs and usage.** When a customer reports a bad answer or a billing spike, you cannot match it to a call or explain the cost."],"when":"- **Use directly when** OpenAI (or Azure OpenAI) is your primary provider and you want new features as they\n  ship; use it with `base_url` against OpenAI-compatible servers for local or self-hosted models.\n- **Wrap it** behind a small interface (one function per capability: classify, generate, embed, agent\n  step) so tests can fake it and a second provider can be added without touching callers.\n- **Move to LiteLLM or a gateway when** you need routing, fallbacks or central spend tracking across\n  providers; to LangGraph or an agents SDK when you need durable, multi-step state.\n- **Measure** latency percentiles, retry counts, error rates by type, tokens per call, and steps per task.\n- **Interviews:** retry semantics, streaming, and how you validate structured output.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the client wrapper with timeouts, retries, structured output and streaming, and logs request IDs and usage for every call.","architect":"Decides whether product code calls the SDK directly or through an internal interface or gateway, and which API surface (Chat Completions or Responses) the platform standardises on.","fde":"Points the client at a customer's Azure deployment or private compatible endpoint, often behind proxies and custom certificates configured through the underlying HTTP client."},"check":{"q":"During a provider incident your API's p99 latency jumps to several minutes and workers are\nexhausted, even though most calls eventually fail. What is happening in the SDK and what do you change?","a":"Each failing call may wait for the client's long default timeout and then be retried several\ntimes with backoff, so one user request can hold a worker for the timeout multiplied by the attempts.\nI would set an explicit per-call timeout sized to the endpoint's latency budget, lower `max_retries` for\ninteractive paths (keeping more retries for background jobs), use the async client so waiting does not\nblock workers, and add a circuit breaker or fallback model so that after repeated failures requests fail\nfast or route elsewhere. I would also alert on `APITimeoutError` and `RateLimitError` rates separately,\nbecause they need different fixes."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the SDK retry 429 and 5xx responses by default but not 400?","options":["Because 400 errors are always caused by network problems","Because 429 and 5xx are plausibly transient, while resending an invalid request will fail the same way","Because the API forbids retrying 400 responses","Because 400 responses are retried by the server automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rate limits and server errors often clear after a short wait, so backoff retries help. A 400 means the request itself is wrong; retrying wastes time and quota without any chance of success."},{"q":"What does changing `base_url` on the OpenAI client accomplish?","options":["It enables the Responses API on older models","It switches the client to a different OpenAI region for lower latency","It lets the same client call any server implementing the compatible endpoints, such as a local vLLM instance","It disables authentication for local testing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Many servers implement OpenAI's request and response shapes. Pointing `base_url` at them reuses the client, though only the endpoints and features that server actually implements will work."},{"q":"A model returns a tool call. What is the SDK's responsibility versus yours?","options":["The SDK executes the function and returns the final answer automatically","The SDK returns the call with its JSON-string arguments; your code validates, executes and returns the result","The SDK validates the arguments against your Python function signature","The SDK sends the tool result back to the model on the next request automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The client only transports the model's request to call a tool. Parsing arguments, running the tool and sending results back with the call ID is the agent loop you write and must guard."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/anthropic-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic API (Messages API)","Official docs: anthropic-sdk-python"],"why":"- **The usual transport problems.** Authentication, retries on 429 and 5xx, timeouts and server-sent-event\n  streaming, as with any LLM API.\n- **Responses are mixed lists.** One response can hold reasoning blocks, text and one or more tool calls.\n  Agent loops must echo assistant content back faithfully, including blocks your code does not display,\n  because the API expects history to contain them.\n- **Caching is exacting.** Prompt caching pays off only if the cached prefix is byte-identical across\n  requests and the breakpoints sit in the right places.\n- **Naive clients lose data.** Hand-rolled HTTP clients treat the response as one string, silently\n  dropping tool calls, losing reasoning blocks needed for the next turn, or mis-concatenating stream\n  deltas. Generic multi-provider wrappers often lag or flatten provider features such as cache\n  breakpoints, citations, batches or extended reasoning controls.\n- **The SDK makes the real shape the easy path.** Typed parameters, content-block classes you branch on\n  with `.type`, streaming helpers that assemble the final message, an exception hierarchy separating\n  retryable from fatal failures, and helpers for token counting, batches and the tool loop. Client classes\n  for Amazon Bedrock, Google Vertex AI and Microsoft Foundry keep calling code the same (feature\n  availability still differs per platform).","how":"### Client\n- **Credentials from the environment.** `anthropic.Anthropic()` typically reads `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`;\n  `AsyncAnthropic` is the asyncio twin. Create it once per process, like any pooled HTTP client.\n- **Defaults.** Timeout ten minutes; `max_retries` 2, covering connection errors, 408, 409, 429 and 5xx\n  with exponential backoff. Set them on the client or per call via `client.with_options(...)`.\n- **Timeouts are retried too**, so worst-case wall-clock time is the timeout times the number of attempts.\n\n### The Messages call\n`client.messages.create(model=..., max_tokens=..., system=..., messages=[...])`\n\n| Design choice | Because |\n|---|---|\n| **`system` is top-level**, not a message | it is operator configuration rather than a turn, and separating it makes a stable, cacheable prefix |\n| **`max_tokens` is required** | every call gets an explicit output ceiling; hitting it ends with `stop_reason == \"max_tokens\"`, which code must treat as truncation |\n| **`messages` alternates user and assistant turns** | consecutive same-role turns are merged; each `content` is a string or a list of blocks (text, image, document, tool_result) |\n\n### Content blocks\n`response.content` is a list; branch on `block.type`:\n- **`\"text\"`** — the answer.\n- **`\"thinking\"`** — reasoning; opt-in on older models, on by default on recent ones. Its visible text\n  may be summarised or empty depending on the display setting.\n- **`\"tool_use\"`** — with `id`, `name`, and `input` already parsed as a dict.\n\n### Stop reasons\n| `stop_reason` | Do next |\n|---|---|\n| `end_turn` | done |\n| `tool_use` | run tools and continue |\n| `max_tokens` | truncated |\n| `stop_sequence` | a stop sequence matched |\n| `pause_turn` | a long server-side tool turn to resume |\n| `refusal` | declined; check it before reading content |\n\n### Thinking signatures and append-only history\n- **Echo thinking blocks unchanged** on the next turn with the same model. They carry a cryptographic\n  `signature` the server verifies and uses to continue reasoning across tool calls.\n- **Treat history as append-only.** Rewriting or trimming earlier assistant turns can invalidate their\n  thinking.\n- **Enforcement varies** by model and account on recent models; check the current docs before building a\n  harness that edits history.\n\n### Tool loop\n1. **Declare tools** as `name`, `description`, `input_schema` (JSON Schema).\n2. **On `tool_use`**, append the assistant's full `response.content` as an assistant message.\n3. **Run each requested tool.**\n4. **Reply with one user message** containing a `tool_result` block per call, each with the matching\n   `tool_use_id`. Set `is_error: True` for failures rather than dropping them.\n\n- **All results for one turn in one message**; splitting them breaks the parallel-call pattern.\n- **Beta tool runner.** The SDK's beta tool runner can drive this loop for decorated Python functions.\n- **`tool_choice`** controls whether the model may or must call tools (`auto`, `none`, and on some models\n  forced `any` or a named tool).\n- **Portable well-formed calls.** Forcing is not supported on every current model, so use `auto` plus a\n  clear instruction, with `strict: true` on the tool so `input` always validates against `input_schema`\n  (strict mode needs the schema to list `required` fields and set `additionalProperties: false`).\n- **Your guards still apply.** Schema-valid input is not permission: cap iterations, set a task\n  deadline, allow-list tool names, and check what each call may touch before running it.\n\n### Structured output\n- **Schema-constrained JSON.** `output_config={\"format\": ...}` with a JSON Schema constrains the response.\n- **Pydantic helper.** `client.messages.parse(...)` accepts a Pydantic model and returns the validated\n  object, replacing the old trick of a fake tool just to get structured arguments.\n- **Still check `stop_reason`:** a refusal or `max_tokens` cut-off means no complete object to parse.\n\n### Prompt caching\n- **Mark a breakpoint** with `cache_control={\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}` on a block, or use top-level automatic\n  caching.\n- **Prefix in render order:** tools, then system, then messages, up to the breakpoint.\n- **Byte-identical prefixes** read it later at a large discount and lower latency.\n- **Verify** with `usage`: `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens`.\n- **Any earlier change invalidates everything after** it: a timestamp in the system prompt, reordered\n  tools.\n\n### Streaming, counting, batches\n- **Streaming helper.** `with client.messages.stream(...) as s:` yields events and `s.text_stream`;\n  `s.get_final_message()` returns the assembled message, so you never hand-merge deltas.\n- **Stream long outputs.** Recommended for long outputs and large `max_tokens`, where a non-streaming\n  request can hit HTTP timeouts. The Python SDK refuses a non-streaming request whose `max_tokens` implies\n  an expected duration beyond its timeout and tells you to stream instead.\n- **Exact counts.** `client.messages.count_tokens(...)` returns the exact input token count, the right\n  tool for Claude because local OpenAI tokenizers do not match.\n- **Batches.** `client.messages.batches` submits many requests for asynchronous processing at a discount,\n  results keyed by your `custom_id`.","example":"### Minimal tool-use loop with a cached system prompt\nModel IDs change; take them from configuration.\n\n```python\nimport os, anthropic\n\nMODEL = os.environ.get(\"CLAUDE_MODEL\", \"claude-opus-5-5\")\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic(max_retries=3)\nSYSTEM = [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"You are an order-support agent. Use tools for facts.\",\n           \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}}]  # caches only above the model's minimum prefix length\nTOOLS = [{\"name\": \"get_order\", \"description\": \"Look up an order by id.\",\n          \"input_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                           \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}}]\n\ndef get_order(order_id: str) -> str:\n    return f'{{\"order_id\": \"{order_id}\", \"status\": \"shipped\", \"eta\": \"2 days\"}}'\n\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is order A-1042?\"}]\nwhile True:\n    resp = client.messages.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=8192, system=SYSTEM,   # thinking counts too\n                                  tools=TOOLS, messages=messages)\n    print(resp._request_id, resp.usage.input_tokens, resp.usage.cache_read_input_tokens)\n    if resp.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":\n        break\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": resp.content})   # echo every block back\n    results = []\n    for block in resp.content:\n        if block.type == \"tool_use\":\n            out = get_order(**block.input) if block.name == \"get_order\" else \"unknown tool\"\n            results.append({\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": block.id, \"content\": out})\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": results})               # all results, one message\n\nif resp.stop_reason == \"refusal\" or resp.stop_reason == \"max_tokens\":\n    print(\"did not finish normally:\", resp.stop_reason)\nprint(\"\".join(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == \"text\"))\n```\n\nTrace of a typical run:\n\n| Call | Returns | History after |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `stop_reason=\"tool_use\"`, content such as `[thinking, tool_use(id=\"toolu_…\", name=\"get_order\", input={\"order_id\": \"A-1042\"})]` | three messages: the user question, the assistant turn with every block intact, a user turn with one `tool_result` whose `tool_use_id` matches |\n| 2 | resends all three; the model reads the result and returns `stop_reason=\"end_turn\"` with a text block | done |\n\n- **Input grows every iteration**, because each call re-reads the whole history; that is exactly the cost\n  caching the stable prefix attacks.\n- **This toy prompt likely will not cache.** Its tools and system prompt are probably shorter than the\n  minimum cacheable prefix (model-dependent, from a few hundred to a few thousand tokens), so expect\n  `cache_read_input_tokens` to stay at zero with no error. With a realistic multi-thousand-token system\n  prompt and tool list it rises from the second call.\n- **A production loop also** caps iterations, validates `block.input` before running a tool, and catches\n  `anthropic.RateLimitError`, `anthropic.APIStatusError` and `anthropic.APIConnectionError` separately.\n\n### Streaming a long answer and pricing it first\nReuses `client`, `MODEL` and `SYSTEM` from the loop above.\n\n```python\nmsgs = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a migration guide for our v2 API.\"}]\n\nn = client.messages.count_tokens(model=MODEL, system=SYSTEM, messages=msgs).input_tokens\nprint(\"input tokens:\", n)                                  # exact, before spending anything\n\nwith client.messages.stream(model=MODEL, max_tokens=64000, system=SYSTEM, messages=msgs) as s:\n    for text in s.text_stream:                             # forward deltas to the user\n        print(text, end=\"\", flush=True)\n    final = s.get_final_message()                          # assembled Message, no hand-merging\nprint(\"\\n\", final.stop_reason, final.usage.output_tokens)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what caching saves in an agent loop\nA 6,000-token system prompt and tool list, resent on 10 consecutive calls within the cache lifetime.\nTypical multipliers on the base input price: cache writes ~1.25× with the default 5-minute lifetime (2×\nfor the 1-hour option), cache reads ~0.1× (illustrative; the read multiplier is lower on some models, so\ncheck current pricing).\n\n| | Prefix cost in input-token equivalents |\n|---|---|\n| No caching: 10 × 6,000 | 60,000 |\n| Call 1 writes: 1.25 × 6,000 | 7,500 |\n| Calls 2-10 read: 9 × 0.1 × 6,000 | 5,400 |\n| **With caching** | **12,900, about 78% less** |\n\nIf a timestamp sits at the top of the system prompt, every call is a write and the saving goes negative.\n\n### Worked scenario: a 15-step coding agent with and without a warm prefix\nIllustrative: 8,000 tokens of tools and system prompt, each step appends ~1,500 tokens of tool call and\nresult, same multipliers as above, all steps inside the cache lifetime. History is not cached here, only\nthe prefix.\n\n| Step | Prefix cost, no caching | Prefix cost, cached |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 8,000 | 10,000 (write at 1.25×) |\n| 2-15 (14 steps) | 14 × 8,000 = 112,000 | 14 × 800 = 11,200 (reads at 0.1×) |\n| **Total prefix** | **120,000** | **21,200** |\n\n- **The history still grows** by ~1,500 tokens a step; a second breakpoint on the latest turn lets\n  earlier history be read from cache on the next step too, if the history is append-only.\n- **Editing earlier turns** (trimming tool results mid-run) moves the cache miss to that point, so\n  compact rarely and in one step.","patterns":["**Trace every agent step.** Log `resp._request_id`, `stop_reason`, tool names, `usage` including cache fields, and latency per call, to a tracer such as LangSmith, Langfuse or OpenTelemetry. Replaying one bad trajectory beats reading aggregate metrics.","**Lay out prompts for the cache.** Put tools and a frozen system prompt first with one `cache_control` breakpoint after them, and volatile data (dates, user names, retrieved text) after it. Alert when `cache_read_input_tokens` / input tokens drops on a route that should be warm.","**Stream anything long.** Use `client.messages.stream(...)` with `get_final_message()` for large `max_tokens`, instead of non-streaming calls that hit HTTP timeouts. Monitor time to first token and `stop_reason == \"max_tokens\"` rate.","**Batch offline work.** Send evals and backfills through `client.messages.batches` at a discount, poll until `processing_status` is `\"ended\"`, and join results on `custom_id` because order is arbitrary. Track errored and expired counts per batch.","**Count before you send.** Use `client.messages.count_tokens(...)` for admission control and cost estimates on long documents, not tiktoken. Log estimate vs `usage.input_tokens`.","**Guard the tool loop.** Cap iterations, set `strict: true` on tools, validate `block.input`, and return failures as `tool_result` with `is_error: True`. Monitor loop iterations per task and tool error rate.","**Budget timeouts per path.** Derive `client.with_options(timeout=..., max_retries=...)` variants: short and few retries for interactive calls, generous for background jobs. Alert on `RateLimitError` and `APIConnectionError` separately."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A Claude agent is a loop driven by `stop_reason`: on `tool_use`, echo\n  every block back, run the tools, return all results in one user message, repeat. Most broken Claude\n  agents break one of those rules, and most expensive ones miss the prompt cache.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Directly in hand-written agents, under LangChain's `ChatAnthropic`\n  (the `langchain-anthropic` package) and so LangGraph nodes, under Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK, inside\n  LiteLLM for Claude models, and on Bedrock, Vertex AI or Microsoft Foundry through this SDK's platform\n  clients.\n- **What you control.** Prompt layout for caching (tools, then system, then messages), `max_tokens`,\n  `tool_choice` and `strict` tools, append-only history, streaming, batches for evals, and per-path\n  timeouts and retries.\n- **What you can skip.** Raw SSE parsing and hand-merging stream deltas; the streaming helper and\n  `get_final_message()` do it. Learn the content-block shapes before trusting a wrapper to translate them.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Claude tool-calling agents.** The loop runs on `stop_reason`: on `tool_use`, echo the assistant blocks,\n  run every tool and return all results in one user message.\n- **Long agent sessions at lower cost.** `cache_control` on a stable tools-then-system prefix keeps later\n  steps cheap when history is append-only.\n- **Batch agent evals.** Message batches run many eval prompts without blocking an interactive budget.\n- **Cloud-hosted Claude.** The same SDK reaches Claude on Bedrock, Vertex AI or Microsoft Foundry through\n  its platform clients.","mini_project":"**Build a cached Claude agent loop (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a Claude agent with two tools whose later steps reuse the prompt cache.\n- **Steps:** define two tools; write the `stop_reason` loop, handling `tool_use`, `end_turn` and\n  `max_tokens`; put `cache_control` on the end of the system prompt; keep history append-only; run a\n  10-step task and log input, cache-read and output tokens per step from `usage`.\n- **Done when:** the loop handles parallel tool calls, treats `max_tokens` as truncation, and your log\n  shows cache reads climbing after step 1."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P[\"Prefix: tools, then system, cache_control breakpoint\"] --> CALL[\"messages.create with max_tokens and history\"]\n    H[(\"History: append-only messages\")] --> CALL\n    CALL --> SR{\"stop_reason?\"}\n    SR -->|\"tool_use\"| EC[\"Append full response.content as assistant turn\"]\n    EC --> RUN[\"Run each tool_use block, validate input\"]\n    RUN --> TR[\"One user message: tool_result per tool_use_id\"]\n    TR --> H\n    SR -->|\"end_turn\"| DONE[\"Read text blocks, log usage and cache reads\"]\n    SR -->|\"max_tokens\"| TRUNC[\"Truncated: retry larger or stream\"]\n    SR -->|\"refusal\"| REF[\"Declined: handle before reading content\"]\n    SR -->|\"pause_turn\"| CALL","caption":"The agent loop is driven by `stop_reason`: on a tool call, echo every content block back, then answer\nall calls in one user message; the cached prefix (tools, then system) stays fixed across iterations."},"interview":[{"q":"How does the Messages API shape differ from OpenAI's Chat Completions, and why does it matter?","a":"- **`system` is top-level**, not a message, because it is operator configuration and a stable, cacheable\n  prefix.\n- **`max_tokens` is required** and responses are typed content blocks (`text`, `thinking`, `tool_use`)\n  rather than one string, so code must branch on `block.type`.\n- **Tool results go back as user `tool_result` blocks** keyed by `tool_use_id`; porting OpenAI code\n  without these changes is the source of most integration bugs."},{"q":"The second request in your tool loop fails validation. What went wrong?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the code appended only the assistant's text instead of the full `response.content`, so\n  the `tool_result` blocks reference `tool_use` ids no longer in history.\n- **Other suspects:** results split across several user messages, a missing result for one of several\n  parallel calls, or thinking blocks dropped or edited, since their `signature` is verified.\n- **Fix:** treat history as append-only and return every result, including failures with `is_error: True`."},{"q":"Your Claude agent runs 30 steps per task and costs keep climbing per step. How do you design for it?","a":"- **Cache the stable prefix:** tools and a frozen system prompt first with a `cache_control` breakpoint,\n  volatile data after it; verify `cache_read_input_tokens` rises from step 2.\n- **Keep history append-only** and compact at a boundary when it nears the window, accepting one cache\n  miss rather than one per step; echo thinking blocks unchanged.\n- **Cap the loop:** iteration limit, deadline and per-tool result size, and measure tokens and steps per\n  task alongside task success."},{"q":"When would you not use the native Anthropic SDK?","a":"- **When several providers are genuinely in production** and you need routing, fallbacks or central spend\n  tracking, a router or gateway may fit better.\n- **But check feature survival:** generic wrappers often lag or flatten cache breakpoints, citations,\n  batches or reasoning controls.\n- **For Bedrock or Vertex contracts** you usually still use this SDK's platform client classes, keeping\n  calling code the same."},{"q":"How much does caching save on a 6,000-token prefix resent on 10 calls?","a":"- **Without caching:** 10 × 6,000 = 60,000 input-token equivalents.\n- **With caching, using illustrative multipliers:** one write at ~1.25× is 7,500, nine reads at ~0.1× are\n  5,400, total 12,900, about 78% less; check current pricing per model.\n- **It only works** if the prefix is byte-identical and above the model's minimum cacheable length;\n  confirm with `cache_read_input_tokens`."},{"q":"A long generation fails with a timeout or the SDK refuses the request. What do you change?","a":"- **Stream it:** the Python SDK refuses a non-streaming request whose `max_tokens` implies a duration\n  beyond its timeout, and long non-streaming calls can hit HTTP timeouts.\n- **Use the helper:** `client.messages.stream(...)` with `s.text_stream` for deltas and\n  `get_final_message()` for the assembled message and usage.\n- **Remember retries:** timeouts are retried, so worst-case wall clock is the timeout times attempts."}],"tradeoffs":["**Full fidelity vs portability.** The native SDK exposes Claude-specific features fully (cache breakpoints, content blocks, batches, reasoning controls), but the code is not portable; a router helps once several providers are genuinely in production.","**Explicit breakpoints vs automatic caching.** Explicit placement gives precise control over what is cached and what it costs but requires disciplined prompt layout; automatic caching is simpler when you do not need per-block placement.","**Batch discount vs latency.** Batches cut cost substantially for offline work, but results arrive asynchronously and in any order, so they suit evals and backfills, not interactive requests.","**Free retries vs worst-case latency.** Built-in retries absorb transient failures cheaply, but on long timeouts they multiply worst-case latency; interactive paths should set short timeouts and few retries."],"pitfalls":["**Reading `response.content[0].text` blindly.** When the first block is thinking or a tool call, you get an attribute error or an empty answer, and tool calls are silently ignored.","**Appending only the text of an assistant turn instead of `response.content`.** The next request fails validation because `tool_result` blocks reference `tool_use` ids no longer in history.","**A timestamp or request ID at the top of the system prompt.** `cache_read_input_tokens` stays at zero on every call and costs never drop.","**A small `max_tokens` without checking `stop_reason`.** Answers or JSON are cut off mid-way. Symptom: parse errors that look like model mistakes.","**Budgeting Claude prompts with tiktoken.** Counts differ and requests near the context limit fail; use `count_tokens` instead."],"when":"- **Use when** Claude is a primary model and you want its features at full fidelity: tool-using agents,\n  long cached system prompts or documents, citations, batch evaluation, or reasoning controls.\n- **Wrap it** behind your own narrow interface so tests can fake it.\n- **Use the Bedrock or Vertex client classes** when the customer's contract or data residency requires\n  those platforms, keeping the same calling code.\n- **Choose LiteLLM or a gateway only when** multi-provider routing is a real requirement, and check that\n  the features you depend on survive the translation.\n- **Measure** cache read ratio, stop-reason distribution, tool-loop iterations per task and error rates\n  by class.\n- **Interviews:** walk through the tool loop and explain why a cache hit rate is zero.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the tool loop with correct block handling, streams long outputs, lays out prompts so caching hits, and logs request IDs and usage for every call.","architect":"Decides provider abstraction boundaries, which platform (first-party, Bedrock, Vertex) each workload uses, and where batches replace synchronous calls to cut cost.","fde":"Integrates Claude through the customer's cloud account on Bedrock or Vertex, and debugs auth, region and quota differences while keeping application code unchanged."},"check":{"q":"A team's Claude agent has a 6,000-token system prompt and tool list, and they enabled prompt\ncaching, yet `cache_read_input_tokens` is always zero. Walk through how you would diagnose it.","a":"Caching is a prefix match over tools, then system, then messages, so anything that varies early in\nthat order breaks every hit. I would diff two consecutive request bodies byte for byte: typical culprits\nare a timestamp or user name interpolated into the system prompt, tools built from an unordered set so\ntheir order changes, or JSON serialised without sorted keys. I would check the breakpoint is placed after\nthe stable content and that the prefix exceeds the model's minimum cacheable length. I would confirm\nrequests arrive within the cache lifetime and use the same model, since caches are model-scoped. Then I\nwould move volatile data after the last breakpoint and watch the read counter rise."},"quiz":[{"q":"After a response with `stop_reason == \"tool_use\"`, what must the next request contain?","options":["Only the tool result text appended to the system prompt","A new user message summarising the tool call","The assistant's full content blocks, then a user message with a `tool_result` block per `tool_use` id","A request with `tool_choice` reset so the model cannot call tools again"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The API validates that each `tool_result` matches a `tool_use` in the preceding assistant turn. Echoing the full content and returning every result in one user message keeps history consistent."},{"q":"Why might a correctly marked cache breakpoint still produce no cache reads?","options":["Because caching only works for streaming requests","Because something earlier in the prefix changes between requests, such as a timestamp in the system prompt","Because cache reads are reported only in the billing dashboard","Because caching requires the Batches API"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Caching matches the exact prefix up to the breakpoint. Any byte that varies before it, in tools or system, creates a new prefix, so every request writes a cache entry and none read one."},{"q":"Why is `max_tokens` a required parameter, and what should code do with it?","options":["It sets the input context limit; code should truncate prompts to fit it","It controls billing tier; higher values cost more per token","It caps output length explicitly; code must check for `stop_reason == \"max_tokens\"` as truncation","It limits the number of tool calls per turn"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Requiring an output ceiling makes cost and latency bounds explicit. When the model hits it, the response is incomplete, so treating that stop reason as success produces cut-off answers and broken JSON."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/litellm","sources":["Official docs: LiteLLM"],"why":"- **Teams rarely stay on one model.** A cheap model for classification, a strong one for reasoning, a\n  second provider for outages or rate limits, a self-hosted model for sensitive data, and new models to\n  evaluate without rewriting call sites.\n- **Every provider differs.** Each has its own SDK, message format, tool-call shape, error classes,\n  streaming events and usage fields.\n- **Direct calls spread the cost everywhere.** Every new provider touches every call site, fallbacks are\n  ad hoc per service, and nobody can answer \"what did we spend on model X for feature Y last month\",\n  because usage is logged in five shapes.\n- **An internal wrapper grows into a product.** Fine for two providers and one capability, it becomes a\n  maintained translation layer for tool schemas, streaming, error mapping and pricing tables: the boring,\n  fast-changing work a shared library amortises.\n- **Proxy mode moves it out of apps entirely.** Services talk to one OpenAI-compatible endpoint; the\n  platform team controls which models exist, who may use them and how much they may spend. The trade: a\n  dependency on every LLM call, whose correctness, latency and update cadence become yours.","how":"### Unified call\n`litellm.completion(model=\"<provider>/<model>\", messages=[...])` (and `acompletion` for asyncio):\n1. **Parse the provider prefix:** `openai/`, `anthropic/`, `bedrock/`, `vertex_ai/`, `ollama/`,\n   `hosted_vllm/` and many more.\n2. **Translate** the OpenAI-format request into that provider's native request and call it.\n3. **Translate back** into an OpenAI-format object: `choices[0].message`, `tool_calls` and `usage`.\n\n- **Same shape whichever provider served it:** `completion(model=\"anthropic/...\", messages=...)` returns\n  the same response object as an OpenAI call.\n- **Credentials** come from provider-specific environment variables.\n- **Unsupported parameters raise by default.** `drop_params=True` silently drops them instead, which is\n  convenient and dangerous in equal measure.\n\n### What translation actually does (OpenAI format to `anthropic/...`)\nRequest, OpenAI format in, Anthropic request out:\n\n| You send (OpenAI) | LiteLLM sends (Anthropic) |\n|---|---|\n| `system` message in `messages` | top-level `system` field |\n| `max_tokens` omitted | a value is supplied, because Anthropic requires it |\n| `tools[].function` | `name`/`description`/`input_schema` tool |\n| assistant `tool_calls` | `tool_use` blocks |\n| `role: \"tool\"` message | `tool_result` block inside a user message |\n\nResponse, Anthropic reply in, OpenAI shape out:\n\n| Anthropic returns | You receive (OpenAI) |\n|---|---|\n| `tool_use` blocks | `tool_calls` with `arguments` re-serialised as a JSON string |\n| `stop_reason`: `end_turn`, `tool_use`, `max_tokens` | `finish_reason`: `stop`, `tool_calls`, `length` |\n| `input_tokens` / `output_tokens` | `prompt_tokens` / `completion_tokens` |\n\n- **Every mapping is a fidelity risk.** A feature with no OpenAI equivalent (thinking blocks, cache\n  breakpoints, citations) must ride in an extra field or is lost, which is why translation fidelity is the\n  thing to test.\n- **Agent history is the hardest case.** A multi-step transcript with parallel tool calls, tool results\n  and reasoning blocks must round-trip through translation on every step; test full multi-step traces,\n  not single calls.\n\n### Errors\n- **One hierarchy.** Provider errors map onto OpenAI-style exception types (rate limit, context window\n  exceeded, authentication, bad request), so retry and fallback logic is written once.\n\n### Router\n`litellm.Router` takes a `model_list` where several deployments share one logical `model_name` (for\nexample two Azure regions plus OpenAI direct).\n\n| Setting | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `routing_strategy` | load balancing: random `simple-shuffle` by default, or prefer the least busy, lowest latency or least used deployment |\n| `allowed_fails`, `cooldown_time` | a deployment exceeding `allowed_fails` goes into cooldown for `cooldown_time` seconds |\n| `num_retries` | retries within the group |\n| `fallbacks` | map one logical model to others |\n| `context_window_fallbacks` | overflow to a longer-context model |\n| `content_policy_fallbacks` | reroute on content-policy errors |\n\n- **Callers ask for the logical name; topology lives in config.**\n- **Order matters.** Retries happen within the group first; fallback to another group only when the whole\n  group has failed, because a different model is a bigger behavioural change than a different deployment\n  of the same one.\n\n### Cost and callbacks\n- **Price map.** `completion_cost` computes per-call cost from usage: prompt tokens × input price +\n  completion tokens × output price, looked up by model name.\n- **Custom names need `base_model`.** An Azure deployment called `my-gpt4o-mini-eu` is not in the map, so\n  declare its `base_model` in the deployment's `model_info` for the lookup to work.\n- **Callbacks** send each call's inputs, outputs, latency and cost to logging and observability backends.\n\n### Proxy (gateway) mode\n- **Same machinery as a server** exposing OpenAI-compatible endpoints, configured by a YAML file of models,\n  routing and fallbacks.\n- **Governance features:** virtual API keys mapped to teams or users, budgets and rate limits per key,\n  spend logs in a database, and central guardrail hooks.\n- **Any client, any language**, pointed at the proxy's `base_url`.\n- **Licensing.** The core is open source, but some enterprise proxy features (for example SSO and certain\n  audit and admin controls) need a commercial licence; check which you need before standardising.","example":"### One agent loop, a model per role\nThe same tool loop runs whichever provider serves each step; a cheap model decides whether tools are\nneeded, a stronger one does the work. Model names are illustrative; take them from config.\n\n```python\nimport json, litellm\n\nROLES = {\"router\": \"openai/gpt-4o-mini\", \"worker\": \"anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5\"}\ntools = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_order\", \"description\": \"Look up an order by id\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"order_id\"]}}}]\n\ndef get_order(order_id: str) -> dict:\n    return {\"order_id\": order_id, \"status\": \"shipped\"}\n\ndef run(question: str, tenant: str, max_steps: int = 6) -> str:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    for step in range(max_steps):\n        model = ROLES[\"router\"] if step == 0 else ROLES[\"worker\"]\n        resp = litellm.completion(model=model, messages=messages, tools=tools, timeout=20,\n                                  metadata={\"agent\": \"support\", \"tenant\": tenant, \"step\": step})\n        msg = resp.choices[0].message\n        print(step, resp.model, resp.usage.prompt_tokens, litellm.completion_cost(resp))\n        if resp.choices[0].finish_reason != \"tool_calls\":\n            return msg.content                     # same finish_reason names for every provider\n        messages.append(msg.model_dump())\n        for call in msg.tool_calls:\n            args = json.loads(call.function.arguments)   # validate before running in production\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id,\n                             \"content\": json.dumps(get_order(**args))})\n    return \"Step limit reached.\"\n```\n\n- **Switching models mid-run is a design choice.** History written by one model is read by another;\n  provider-specific blocks (reasoning, cache markers) do not carry over, so eval the mixed trajectory.\n- **Per-step cost** comes from `completion_cost`, so you can report cost per task and per agent role.\n\n### Router with two deployments, retries and a cross-provider fallback\nModel names are illustrative; take them from config.\n\n```python\nimport litellm\nfrom litellm import Router\n\nrouter = Router(\n    model_list=[\n        {\"model_name\": \"chat\", \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"openai/gpt-4o-mini\"}},\n        {\"model_name\": \"chat\", \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"azure/my-gpt4o-mini-eu\",\n                                                  \"api_base\": \"https://example-eu.openai.azure.com\"}},\n        {\"model_name\": \"chat-backup\", \"litellm_params\": {\"model\": \"anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5\"}},\n    ],\n    num_retries=2,                                # retries within the group; may pick another deployment\n    fallbacks=[{\"chat\": [\"chat-backup\"]}],        # if all \"chat\" deployments fail\n    timeout=20,\n)\n\nresp = router.completion(\n    model=\"chat\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summarise: the refund was issued on 3 May.\"}],\n    metadata={\"feature\": \"ticket-summary\", \"tenant\": \"acme\"},   # for cost attribution in callbacks\n)\nprint(resp.model, resp.choices[0].message.content)\nprint(resp.usage.prompt_tokens, resp.usage.completion_tokens, litellm.completion_cost(resp))\n```\n\nScenario: OpenAI returns 429s for a minute.\n1. By default the router spreads \"chat\" traffic across both deployments (simple shuffle).\n2. Failing calls are retried, and the OpenAI deployment goes into cooldown after repeated failures.\n3. Traffic lands on the EU Azure deployment.\n4. Only if the \"chat\" group as a whole fails does the request go to the Anthropic backup; the caller still\n   receives an OpenAI-shaped response.\n\n### The same topology as a proxy config\nApplications then call the proxy with any OpenAI-compatible client and a virtual key.\n\n```yaml\nmodel_list:\n  - model_name: chat\n    litellm_params:\n      model: openai/gpt-4o-mini\n      api_key: os.environ/OPENAI_API_KEY\n  - model_name: chat\n    litellm_params:\n      model: azure/my-gpt4o-mini-eu\n      api_base: https://example-eu.openai.azure.com\n      api_key: os.environ/AZURE_API_KEY\n    model_info:\n      base_model: azure/gpt-4o-mini      # so the price map can cost this custom name\n  - model_name: chat-backup\n    litellm_params:\n      model: anthropic/claude-haiku-5-5\n      api_key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\nrouter_settings:\n  num_retries: 2\n  timeout: 20\n  allowed_fails: 3\n  cooldown_time: 30\n  fallbacks: [{\"chat\": [\"chat-backup\"]}]\n```\n\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:4000\", api_key=\"sk-team-virtual-key\", max_retries=0)\nclient.chat.completions.create(model=\"chat\", messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"hi\"}])\n```\n\n`max_retries=0` on the app client leaves retries to the proxy, so they live in exactly one layer.\n\n### Worked scenario: how stacked retries amplify an incident\nIllustrative settings, during an outage where every upstream call fails:\n\n| Layer | Attempts |\n|---|---|\n| Application client retries 2 times | 3 |\n| Router `num_retries=2` per group | × 3 |\n| Groups tried: \"chat\", then \"chat-backup\" | × 2 |\n| **Upstream calls for one user request** | **18** |\n\nEighteen calls for one user request, sent exactly when the provider is already rate limiting you. With\nretries owned only by the router (`max_retries=0` on the app client), the same request makes 3 × 2 = 6\ncalls.\n\n### Worked scenario: the same outage, inside an agent\nAn agent task averages 8 model calls (illustrative). With the stacked setup above, each call can become\n18 upstream attempts.\n\n| Setup | Upstream calls for one agent task, worst case |\n|---|---|\n| Stacked retries: 8 × 18 | 144 |\n| Router-owned retries only: 8 × 6 | 48 |\n| Router-owned retries plus a task deadline that stops after 3 failed steps: 3 × 6 | 18 |\n\nAgents multiply every per-call retry policy by their step count, which is why the loop needs its own\ndeadline and failure budget on top of the router.","patterns":["**One logical name per agent role.** Call `planner`, `router`, `judge` instead of provider model names, and map them in router or proxy config, so swapping a model is a config change plus an eval run. Monitor cost and latency per role.","**Budget each agent with its own key.** In proxy mode give each agent or tenant a virtual key with a spend budget and rate limit, so one runaway loop cannot spend the whole month. Monitor budget burn per key and alert well before the cap.","**Own retries in one layer.** Set `num_retries` and `timeout` in the router or proxy, and `max_retries=0` on clients that call the proxy. Monitor upstream calls per user request during incidents.","**Eval every fallback pair.** Run the eval suite on each `fallbacks` target before enabling it, and alert on fallback rate per route, so a silent permanent failover with worse quality is noticed.","**Make cost attribution complete.** Declare `base_model` for every custom deployment, pass `metadata` (feature, tenant) on each call, and reconcile computed spend against provider invoices monthly before using it for chargeback.","**Run the proxy highly available.** At least two replicas behind a load balancer, sharing one database for keys and spend logs, with the proxy's health endpoints wired to the orchestrator's liveness and readiness probes. Monitor proxy-added latency and error rate.","**Pin the version, keep `drop_params` off globally.** Lock the LiteLLM version and upgrade deliberately with an eval run; enable dropping only where a known parameter gap is accepted. Watch for cost or format changes after upgrades."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents make many calls per task, often to different models (a cheap\n  router step, a strong planner, a judge for evals). One call shape means one tool-calling loop for every\n  provider, and one place for retries, fallbacks and spend caps per agent or tenant.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** As the model layer under hand-written agent loops, via LangChain's\n  LiteLLM chat model integration (and so LangGraph), inside other agent frameworks that accept a LiteLLM\n  model string, as a proxy that every agent service calls with an OpenAI client, and in eval harnesses\n  that compare models on the same agent tasks.\n- **What you control.** Logical model names per agent role, `fallbacks` and `num_retries`, which layer\n  owns retries, `drop_params`, virtual keys with budgets per agent or tenant, `metadata` for cost\n  attribution, and callbacks to tracing backends.\n- **What you can skip.** The internals of each provider translation. Test the parts your agent depends on\n  (tool calls, streaming usage, caching) on every provider in the route instead.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Model routing per agent role.** A cheap model for routing steps, a strong one for planning and a judge\n  model for evals, all behind one call shape.\n- **Fallbacks during outages.** `fallbacks` and `num_retries` keep an agent running when one provider\n  rate-limits or fails.\n- **Spend control per agent or tenant.** Proxy virtual keys with budgets and `metadata` for attribution\n  answer \"what did this agent cost last month\".\n- **Cross-provider agent evals.** The same agent tasks run against several models to compare quality,\n  latency and cost before a switch.","mini_project":"**Route one agent across two providers (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** run the same tool-calling agent on two providers and prove the fallback works.\n- **Steps:** write one agent loop with a single tool using LiteLLM; configure a primary and a fallback\n  model in a `Router`; run 10 tasks on each; force a primary failure (point the primary deployment at an\n  unreachable `api_base`) and confirm the fallback serves; log per-task cost and tool-call parse success.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-provider table of success, cost and latency, plus a note on any parameter\n  that behaved differently or was dropped."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    APP[\"Agent step: OpenAI-format call, model chat\"] --> R[\"Router or proxy: logical name to deployments\"]\n    R -->|\"routing_strategy, simple-shuffle default\"| D1[\"openai/gpt-4o-mini\"]\n    R --> D2[\"azure/my-gpt4o-mini-eu\"]\n    D1 -->|\"429 or 5xx\"| RT{\"num_retries left in group?\"}\n    RT -->|\"yes, pick a healthy deployment\"| D2\n    D1 -.->|\"over allowed_fails\"| CD[\"Cooldown for cooldown_time seconds\"]\n    RT -->|\"no, whole group failed\"| FB[\"fallbacks: chat-backup, anthropic model\"]\n    D2 --> TX[\"Translate response to OpenAI shape\"]\n    FB --> TX\n    TX --> COST[\"completion_cost and callbacks log spend with metadata\"]\n    COST --> APP","caption":"A request for the logical name \"chat\": retries stay inside the group first, a failing deployment cools\ndown, and only when the whole group fails does the call fall back to another provider's model."},"interview":[{"q":"What does LiteLLM translate, and where does translation lose fidelity?","a":"- **It maps requests and responses:** for `anthropic/...` the OpenAI `system` message becomes top-level\n  `system`, `tools[].function` becomes `input_schema` tools, and `role: \"tool\"` messages become\n  `tool_result` blocks inside a user message.\n- **On the way back** `stop_reason` maps to `finish_reason` and `input_tokens` to `prompt_tokens`, so\n  parsing and logging are written once.\n- **Lost or hidden:** features with no OpenAI equivalent, such as thinking blocks, cache breakpoints or\n  citations, which must ride in extra fields."},{"q":"After a provider outage, your agent's task success dropped and support tickets spiked, but logs showed no errors. What happened?","a":"- **Likely a silent fallback:** the router moved traffic to `chat-backup`, so requests succeeded on a\n  model with different style, tool-calling accuracy or instruction following on your prompts.\n- **Check `drop_params`:** if it is on globally, a stop sequence or response format may have been dropped\n  only for the fallback provider, changing output format.\n- **Fix:** run the eval suite on every fallback pair before enabling it and alert on fallback rate per route."},{"q":"A fallback fires halfway through a 10-step agent run. What can go wrong, and how do you design for it?","a":"- **Mixed trajectories:** steps 1 to 5 were written by one model and steps 6 to 10 by another; reasoning\n  blocks and cache state do not carry over, and the new model may call tools differently.\n- **Design choices:** either pin a task to one model group for its whole run and retry the task, or allow\n  mid-run fallback only to pairs whose mixed trajectories passed your agent evals.\n- **Observe it:** tag each step with the serving model in traces and alert on fallback rate per agent."},{"q":"When would you not introduce LiteLLM?","a":"- **One strategic provider:** if you depend on its newest features, a native SDK exposes them first and\n  at full fidelity.\n- **Small call volume:** an extra abstraction on every LLM call, with its own correctness, latency and\n  update cadence, may not be worth owning.\n- **Library versus proxy:** choose the proxy only when many services and languages need shared keys,\n  budgets and spend logs, because it is an extra hop you must run highly available."},{"q":"How many upstream calls can one user request make during a full outage?","a":"- **Stacked layers multiply:** 3 application attempts × 3 router attempts per group × 2 groups gives 18\n  upstream calls in the illustrative setup.\n- **With retries owned only by the router** the same request makes 3 × 2 = 6 calls.\n- **So set `max_retries=0`** on clients that call the proxy and monitor upstream calls per user request\n  during incidents."},{"q":"Cost reports show zero spend for one Azure deployment. How do you fix attribution?","a":"- **Cause:** `completion_cost` looks up price by model name, and a custom name such as\n  `my-gpt4o-mini-eu` is not in the price map.\n- **Fix:** declare `base_model` in that deployment's `model_info` and pass `metadata` (feature, tenant)\n  on each call so callbacks can attribute spend.\n- **Then reconcile** computed spend against provider invoices before chargeback, because price maps can\n  lag price changes and negotiated rates."}],"tradeoffs":["**Per-step model choice vs trajectory consistency.** Routing cheap steps to a small model cuts agent cost, but each switch hands a transcript written by one model to another; it pays off only when evals on mixed trajectories hold up.","**One interface vs lowest common denominator.** Switching and A/B testing become cheap, but features get hidden; Claude cache breakpoints or Responses-API tools may need provider-specific params.","**Library vs proxy.** The in-process library adds no network hop, but every service needs credentials and config; the proxy centralises keys, budgets and logs at the price of an extra hop and a component you must run highly available.","**Availability vs behaviour drift.** Automatic fallbacks raise availability, but a fallback model may behave differently on your prompts; without evals per fallback pair, an outage silently becomes a quality regression.","**Instant cost visibility vs accuracy.** Built-in price maps show cost immediately but can lag price changes and negotiated rates; reconcile against provider invoices before using them for chargeback."],"pitfalls":["**Testing translation with single calls only.** A one-shot prompt works on every provider, but the agent fails on step 3 when a parallel tool-call history is translated. Fix: replay full multi-step agent traces against each provider in the route.","**`drop_params=True` globally.** A parameter you rely on (structured output, a stop sequence) is quietly discarded for one provider, and outputs change format only when a fallback fires.","**Assuming tool calling and streaming behave identically everywhere.** Translation edge cases show up as malformed tool arguments or missing usage in streamed responses from one provider.","**Fallback chains without evals.** During an incident users see a different model's style or lower accuracy, and support tickets spike with no error in the logs.","**Unpinned versions of a fast-moving dependency on every LLM call.** An upgrade changes defaults or pricing data. Symptom: a behaviour or cost change nobody deployed deliberately.","**Stacking retry layers.** Application retries × router `num_retries` × any retries the underlying client performs × each fallback group. Symptom: one user request becomes a dozen upstream calls during an incident, amplifying the provider's rate limiting. Own retries in exactly one layer.","**Cost reports showing zero or raising for some deployments.** The custom deployment name is not in the price map; declare `base_model` so spend attribution is not silently undercounted.","**Running the proxy as a single instance.** It becomes the single point of failure for every AI feature, and one restart takes them all down."],"when":"- **Use when** you genuinely use, or must be able to switch between, several providers: routing by cost or\n  capability, cross-provider fallbacks, eval harnesses comparing many models on the same agent tasks, or\n  agents that use different models for different roles.\n- **Use proxy mode when** a platform team needs central keys, budgets and spend attribution across many\n  services.\n- **Stay with a native SDK when** one provider is strategic and you depend on its newest features, or call\n  volume is small and an extra abstraction is not worth owning.\n- **Measure** fallback rate, per-model latency and error rates, cost per feature and tenant, and quality on\n  an eval set for each fallback pair.\n- **Interviews:** how you would build provider failover and where the abstraction should stop.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Calls models through one interface, writes retry and fallback config, attaches metadata for cost attribution, and handles provider-specific parameters where translation falls short.","architect":"Decides library versus gateway, which providers and fallback pairs are allowed, how budgets and keys are governed, and keeps a vendor-exit path that does not depend on one SDK.","fde":"Deploys the proxy inside a customer's network to route between their approved cloud models and a local model, giving their security team one place to audit and cap usage."},"check":{"q":"Your product runs on one provider. Leadership asks for \"no single point of failure for AI\". A\ncolleague proposes adding LiteLLM with a fallback to a second provider. What else must be true for this\nto actually deliver resilience?","a":"The fallback must be tested, not just configured. Prompts, tool schemas and output parsing need\nto work on the second model, so I would run the eval suite against it and fix prompt or schema\ndifferences first. Features that do not translate, such as provider-specific caching or structured\noutput modes, need equivalents or graceful degradation. Rate limits and quotas on the backup must cover\nfailover traffic. The routing layer itself must not become the new single point of failure, so the\nproxy runs with multiple replicas or the library is embedded. Finally, alert on fallback rate, so a\nsilent permanent failover with worse quality is noticed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does LiteLLM's response translation give application code?","options":["A guarantee that every provider produces identical outputs for the same prompt","One OpenAI-format response shape, including usage and tool calls, regardless of provider","Automatic prompt rewriting optimised for each model","Access to every provider-specific feature through standard OpenAI parameters"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Translation normalises the shape of requests and responses, so parsing and logging are written once. It cannot make models behave the same, and some provider-specific features have no standard equivalent."},{"q":"When is LiteLLM's proxy mode a better choice than the in-process library?","options":["When a single Python service calls one provider","When the lowest possible latency is the only goal","When many services and languages need shared keys, budgets, rate limits and spend logs","When you need offline access to models without any server"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The proxy centralises governance behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint any client can use. For one service and one provider, the extra hop and operational burden rarely pay off."},{"q":"Why is `drop_params=True` risky as a global default?","options":["It doubles the token cost of each request","It disables retries on rate-limit errors","Unsupported parameters are silently removed, so behaviour can change only when a different provider serves the call","It prevents streaming responses from working"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Silently dropping a parameter such as a response format or stop sequence turns a loud error into a quiet behaviour change, often visible only during a fallback when debugging is hardest."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/instructor","sources":["Official docs: Instructor","Official docs: Pydantic"],"why":"- **Most LLM features, and most agent sub-steps, are extraction or classification.** Pull an invoice's\n  line items, label a ticket's intent, turn an email into a calendar event, choose the next tool.\n  Downstream code wants a typed object, not prose.\n- **\"Respond in JSON\" plus `json.loads` fails in many distinct ways.** A Markdown fence or preamble\n  around the JSON; `\"twelve\"` instead of `12`; an invented enum value; a missing required field; a date\n  in the wrong format; JSON truncated because the output hit the token limit.\n- **Every team writes the same bundle by hand.** Build a schema from the type, put it in the request,\n  strip fences, parse, validate, and on failure tell the model what was wrong and try again.\n- **Provider-native structured output closes only part of the gap.** JSON mode, strict schemas and\n  forced tool calls can guarantee syntactically valid JSON of the schema's shape. They cannot enforce\n  *business* rules: `end_date >= start_date`, a cited quote actually in the source, line items summing\n  to the total, a tool name that exists in this agent's registry.\n- **Those rules live in Pydantic validators.** Only a validate-then-retry loop can use them to correct\n  the model. Instructor is that loop, packaged once, across many providers.","how":"### Patching the client\n- **Wrap an existing SDK client:** `instructor.from_openai(OpenAI())` or\n  `instructor.from_anthropic(Anthropic())`. Recent versions also offer a `from_provider(\"openai/...\")`\n  shortcut, handy when an agent routes steps to different providers.\n- **Same call signature, a few extra kwargs,** chiefly `response_model` and `max_retries`.\n- **It wraps rather than replaces the SDK** *because* the SDK already handles auth, HTTP, streaming and\n  provider quirks; Instructor only owns the schema and validation layer.\n\n### Schema in, object out\n1. **Schema generation.** Pydantic's `model_json_schema()` turns your `BaseModel` into JSON Schema,\n   including field descriptions, enums from `Literal` or `Enum`, and nested models. The class docstring\n   and each `Field(description=...)` become prompt text the model reads, which is why good descriptions\n   matter (measure it on your own eval set).\n2. **Mode selection.** The `mode` decides how the schema reaches the model (table below). Tool calling is\n   preferred where available *because* providers train models to emit well-formed tool arguments.\n3. **Parse and validate.** The reply's arguments are parsed with `model_validate_json`, which runs type\n   coercion and every `field_validator` and `model_validator`.\n4. **Reask.** If validation raises, Instructor appends the model's previous output and the\n   `ValidationError` text as a new message and calls again. The error is the correction signal: a\n   validator raising `ValueError(\"end_date is before start_date\")` tells the model precisely what to fix.\n5. **Give up cleanly.** After the last attempt it raises an `InstructorRetryException` carrying the\n   number of attempts and the last completion, which your agent loop catches and handles.\n\n| Mode | How the schema is sent |\n|---|---|\n| `Mode.TOOLS` | Single tool definition, tool call forced (the usual default for OpenAI) |\n| Provider tool modes | Equivalent for Anthropic and others |\n| `Mode.JSON` | The provider's JSON mode |\n| `Mode.MD_JSON` | Asks for JSON in a Markdown block, for models with no native support |\n\n### Instructor versus the agent's own tool loop\n- **Instructor forces one schema per call.** In tool mode it declares a single tool and forces it, so the\n  model cannot choose among tools or answer in prose that turn.\n- **A tool-calling agent loop is different:** the model sees many tools and decides whether and which to\n  call. That loop usually uses the provider's native tool calling directly.\n- **So use Instructor for the typed sub-steps around the loop:** routing, planning, extraction from tool\n  results, guardrail classification and eval judges. You can also model \"next action\" as a union schema\n  (example below) when you want validators on every decision.\n\n### Retries and `max_retries`\n- **Retries are driven by Tenacity,** so `max_retries` accepts an integer or a full Tenacity retry policy.\n- **An integer means total attempts.** It becomes a stop-after-N-*attempts* rule, counting all calls\n  including the first, not extra reasks on top of it (check this against the version you pin).\n\n### What a reask actually sends\n- **In tool mode, retry request =** the original messages + an assistant message with the failed tool\n  call + a message carrying the `ValidationError` text and an instruction to fix it.\n- **Every retry re-sends the whole prompt *and* grows it,** so retry *k* costs more input tokens than\n  the first call.\n- **Long documents hurt most:** reasking over a 20k-token contract repeats the 20k tokens each time.\n- **Long agent transcripts hurt the same way.** A reask on step 15 re-sends the whole transcript so far,\n  and the failed attempt is appended to the request it sends.\n\n### Expected retry cost\nIf each attempt failed independently with probability *p* and you allow *N* attempts:\n\n```\nexpected calls   = 1 + p + p² + … + p^(N−1)\nresidual failure = p^N\np = 0.1, N = 3  ->  ~1.11 calls, 0.1% residual failures\n```\n\n- **Real failures are *not* independent.** An input that defeats the model once tends to defeat it again\n  with the same error, so the residual rate is far higher than p^N.\n- **Most retry spend lands on a small set of hard inputs.** Measure retry success rate per attempt\n  number; if attempt two rarely fixes what attempt one broke, lower `max_retries` and route failures to\n  review or a stronger model.\n- **In an agent, failures compound over steps.** A run with many structured steps succeeds only if every\n  step does, so a per-step residual failure rate that looks tiny matters at the run level.\n\n### Giving validators something to check against\n- **Validators only see the data you give them.** To check an extracted quote appears in the source,\n  pass the source as a context dict at call time (`context=` in recent versions, `validation_context=`\n  in older ones). It reaches validators through Pydantic's `ValidationInfo.context`.\n- **This turns a prompt instruction into an enforced rule:** \"the quote must be verbatim\" now triggers a\n  reask. In an agent, pass the tool registry or the retrieved chunks so a planner cannot name a missing\n  tool and an answer cannot cite a chunk that was never retrieved.\n- **Make fields the source may omit `Optional`.** A required field forces the model to produce\n  *something*, and that something is a plausible invention.\n\n### Beyond a single object\n| Method | Returns | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `create_partial` | A model whose fields fill in as tokens arrive | UIs rendering a form or an agent's plan progressively |\n| `create_iterable` | A stream of objects | Every person mentioned in a document |\n| `create_with_completion` | Parsed object + raw provider response | Token usage and logging per agent step |\n\n### What it is not\n- **Instructor does not constrain decoding.** The model can still emit anything; Instructor detects bad\n  output after the fact and pays for another call.\n- **Constrained decoding is a different tool.** Libraries such as Outlines mask tokens during generation\n  so invalid output is impossible, but that needs access to the logits, which hosted APIs do not expose.","example":"### Agent scenario: a validated next-action step\nA planner that must either call a registered tool with the right arguments or finish. The registry is\npassed as validation context, so an invented tool name triggers a reask instead of a crash.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nimport instructor\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationInfo, model_validator\n\nclass NextAction(BaseModel):\n    reasoning: str = Field(description=\"One or two sentences: why this action now\")   # first: reason, then act\n    action: Literal[\"call_tool\", \"finish\"]\n    tool: str | None = Field(None, description=\"Tool name when action is call_tool\")\n    args: dict = Field(default_factory=dict)\n    answer: str | None = Field(None, description=\"Final answer when action is finish\")\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def check_against_registry(self, info: ValidationInfo):\n        tools = (info.context or {}).get(\"tools\", {})\n        if self.action == \"call_tool\" and self.tool not in tools:\n            raise ValueError(f\"unknown tool {self.tool!r}; choose one of {sorted(tools)}\")\n        if self.action == \"finish\" and not self.answer:\n            raise ValueError(\"action is finish but answer is empty; write the answer\")\n        return self\n\nTOOLS = {\"search_docs\": lambda query: f\"3 hits for {query}\",\n         \"get_ticket\": lambda ticket_id: {\"id\": ticket_id, \"status\": \"open\"}}\nclient = instructor.from_openai(OpenAI())\nmessages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Is ticket 42 still open? Answer in one line.\"}]\n\nfor step in range(8):                                    # hard step budget for the run\n    act = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", response_model=NextAction, max_retries=2,\n        context={\"tools\": TOOLS}, messages=messages)\n    if act.action == \"finish\":\n        print(act.answer); break\n    result = TOOLS[act.tool](**act.args)                 # wrap in try/except in real code\n    messages += [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": act.model_dump_json()},\n                 {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Tool {act.tool} returned: {result}\"}]\n```\n\n- **`reasoning` comes first** because field order is generation order; the decision is made after the\n  model has written why.\n- **Two checks in one validator:** the tool exists, and a finish carries an answer. Both are rules a JSON\n  schema cannot express on its own.\n- **Bad tool arguments still raise at call time.** Validate them too (for example a Pydantic model per\n  tool) or return the exception text to the model as the tool result.\n\n### Meeting extraction with a cross-field rule\nExtracting a meeting from an email, with a business rule the schema alone cannot express. An assistant\nagent would run this on an email a tool just fetched.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom typing import Literal\nimport instructor\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field, model_validator\n\nclass Meeting(BaseModel):\n    title: str = Field(description=\"Short title, max 8 words\")\n    start: date\n    end: date\n    kind: Literal[\"internal\", \"customer\", \"interview\"]\n    attendees: list[str] = Field(description=\"Email addresses only\")\n\n    @model_validator(mode=\"after\")\n    def end_not_before_start(self):\n        if self.end < self.start:\n            raise ValueError(f\"end {self.end} is before start {self.start}; re-read the dates\")\n        return self\n\nclient = instructor.from_openai(OpenAI())\n\nmeeting, raw = client.chat.completions.create_with_completion(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",                 # any tool-calling model\n    response_model=Meeting,\n    max_retries=3,                       # at most 3 calls: the first try plus up to 2 reasks\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":\n        \"Offsite with Acme from 3 to 5 March 2025. Invite ana@acme.com and raj@us.io.\"}],\n)\nprint(meeting.model_dump())\nprint(raw.usage.total_tokens)            # recent versions sum usage across all attempts here\n```\n\n- **If the first reply sets `end` before `start`,** the validator's message goes back and the second\n  call usually fixes it.\n- **If all three attempts fail,** the exception tells you which validation kept failing.\n- **Usage accounting:** recent versions accumulate token usage from every attempt into the returned\n  completion's `usage` for the major providers; confirm that for your version and provider before you\n  build cost reporting on it.\n\n### Grounded quotes via validation context\nThe validator rejects any quote not found verbatim in the source, which forces a reask. In a RAG agent the\nsource is the retrieved chunks, so the final answer cannot cite text that was never retrieved.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationInfo, field_validator\n\nclass Evidence(BaseModel):\n    claim: str\n    quote: str\n\n    @field_validator(\"quote\")\n    @classmethod\n    def quote_in_source(cls, v: str, info: ValidationInfo) -> str:\n        source = (info.context or {}).get(\"source\", \"\")\n        if v not in source:\n            raise ValueError(\"quote is not verbatim in the source; copy it exactly\")\n        return v\n\ncontract = open(\"contract.txt\").read()\nev = client.chat.completions.create(       # client from the example above\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    response_model=Evidence,\n    max_retries=2,\n    context={\"source\": contract},           # validation_context= on older versions\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"What is the termination notice period?\\n\\n{contract}\"}],\n)\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what retries cost on a long contract\nIllustrative token counts for one 20k-token contract that fails validation on every attempt, with\n`max_retries=3`. A long agent transcript behaves the same way.\n\n| Attempt | Input tokens sent | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ~20,000 | Prompt plus contract (or the transcript so far) |\n| 2 | ~20,600 | Same, plus failed tool call (~500) and error text (~100) |\n| 3 | ~21,200 | Grows again by the second failure |\n| **Total** | **~61,800** | About 3× a single call, for an input that still fails |\n\n- **Under independence** (p = 0.1) the average would be ~1.11 calls, about 22k tokens per contract.\n- **In practice** the same hard contracts fail repeatedly, so a few inputs dominate spend; cap retries and\n  escalate them.\n- **Per agent run** (illustrative): a run with 10 structured steps at ~1.11 expected calls each makes about\n  11 calls rather than 10. But one stubborn late step at three attempts re-sends the longest transcript\n  three times, so it can cost as much as several early steps combined.","patterns":["**Cap retries per agent step with an explicit policy.** For long inputs pass a small integer or a Tenacity policy, e.g. `max_retries=Retrying(stop=stop_after_attempt(2))` with both names imported from `tenacity`. Monitor retries per step type and cost per successful run, not just per call.","**Escalate instead of looping.** Catch `InstructorRetryException`, then send the input to a stronger model, ask the user a clarifying question, or queue it for human review with the last completion attached. Monitor escalation rate and the validators that triggered it.","**Log every attempt into the agent trace.** Use `create_with_completion` for usage, and Instructor's hooks (for example `client.on(\"parse:error\", handler)`) to record each failed attempt and its `ValidationError` as a span in your tracing tool. Reconcile monthly against the provider invoice.","**Ground with context validators.** Pass the source, the retrieved chunks or the tool registry via `context=` and check quotes, IDs, totals or tool names against it, so hallucinated values cause a reask. Monitor the rate of grounding failures per step.","**Type your eval judges.** Make LLM-as-judge steps return a model with a `Literal` verdict and a reasoning field, so agent eval scores aggregate cleanly and a malformed judgement never silently counts as a pass.","**Gate schema changes on an eval set.** Field names, docstrings and descriptions are prompt text; rerun a labelled set before merging a model change. Track first-pass validation rate, since a fall signals prompt or model drift."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every non-chat step in an agent (route the request, pick the next\n  action, extract tool arguments from a document, score a trace as an LLM judge) must return data your\n  code branches on. A malformed or invented value there derails the whole run, not one answer.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** As `instructor.from_openai(...)` or `from_anthropic(...)` around\n  the SDK client inside planner, router, extractor and eval-judge steps; frameworks offer the same idea\n  (LangChain's `with_structured_output`, the typed outputs of PydanticAI), so the concepts transfer.\n- **What you control.** The schema (field order, `Literal` labels, `Optional` fields), validators that\n  check against run state such as the tool registry, `max_retries` per step, and what happens when the\n  step still fails.\n- **What you can skip.** The provider adapter internals and every `Mode` you do not use. Know that a\n  reask is a full, longer LLM call that counts against your agent's latency and token budget.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Intent routers.** A support agent classifies each message into a `Literal` label and a confidence,\n  then branches to the billing, refund or human-handoff sub-agent on a checked value.\n- **Planner steps.** A planner returns a typed list of next actions whose tool names a validator checks\n  against the live tool registry, so an invented tool triggers a reask instead of a crash.\n- **Document extraction tools.** An invoice or contract agent extracts line items with validators that\n  enforce totals and date order before anything is written to a database.\n- **LLM-as-judge evals.** Judges return a typed verdict with a score and a reason, so eval pipelines\n  aggregate clean rows instead of parsing prose.","mini_project":"**Typed router and planner for a small agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** replace free-text routing and planning in a toy agent with Instructor models and measure how\n  often reasks fire.\n- **Steps:** define a `Route` model with a `Literal` intent and a `Plan` model whose steps name a tool;\n  add a validator that rejects tool names missing from your registry; wrap your SDK client with\n  Instructor and set `max_retries`; run 30 varied user messages and log attempts and tokens per step.\n- **Done when:** every run returns a validated route and plan or a handled failure, and you have a table\n  of reask rate and extra tokens per step with one note on which schema change cut reasks most."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Pydantic model as response_model\"] --> S[\"model_json_schema() to tool or JSON schema\"]\n    S --> C[\"LLM call: messages plus schema\"]\n    C --> P[\"Parse with model_validate_json\"]\n    P --> V{\"Types and validators pass?\"}\n    V -->|\"yes\"| O[\"Return validated instance to agent loop\"]\n    V -->|\"no\"| R{\"Attempts left?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Append failed output and ValidationError text\"]\n    A -->|\"longer prompt, full-price call\"| C\n    R -->|\"no\"| E[\"Raise InstructorRetryException, escalate\"]","caption":"Follow one structured agent step: the schema goes out with the transcript, and every validation failure\nloops back as a longer request until it passes or `max_retries` attempts are used up."},"interview":[{"q":"What does Instructor add on top of a provider SDK, and why not just call `json.loads`?","a":"- **It packages the validate-then-reask loop.** It builds a schema from a Pydantic model, sends it as a\n  tool or JSON schema, parses and validates the reply, and on failure sends the `ValidationError` back.\n- **`json.loads` only checks syntax.** It misses Markdown fences, wrong types, invented enum values,\n  missing fields and every business rule, so each team ends up rewriting the same glue.\n- **It wraps rather than replaces the SDK,** so auth, HTTP, streaming and provider quirks stay with the\n  client you already use."},{"q":"Your agent's planner sometimes names a tool that does not exist and the run crashes. How do you fix it?","a":"- **Validate against the registry.** Make the planner return a Pydantic model and pass the tool registry\n  as `context=`; a validator raising \"unknown tool, choose one of ...\" turns the crash into a reask.\n- **Constrain where you can.** If the tool set is fixed, a `Literal` of tool names puts the legal values\n  in the schema itself, so strict mode or the model's training already helps.\n- **Handle the last failure.** Catch `InstructorRetryException`, then fall back to a clarifying question\n  or a stronger model, and log the step in the trace."},{"q":"Extraction retries are spiking cost on one route. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Find who pays.** Log every attempt with its usage and the `ValidationError` that triggered the reask,\n  then group by input; a few hard documents usually dominate spend.\n- **Check whether retries fix anything.** If attempt two rarely succeeds where attempt one failed, the\n  failures are correlated, so lower `max_retries` to 2 and escalate.\n- **Read the validator messages.** A vague \"invalid\" gives the model nothing to act on; rewrite it to say\n  what is wrong and how to fix it."},{"q":"When would you choose constrained decoding or native tool calling over Instructor?","a":"- **When you self-host and need validity on the first pass.** Constrained decoding (Outlines, engine\n  guided decoding) masks invalid tokens, so no retry is ever needed.\n- **When latency is tight.** Every Instructor reask is another full LLM call, which multiplies tail\n  latency across agent steps.\n- **When the model must choose among many tools,** native tool calling fits the main agent loop better\n  than one forced schema per call.\n- **Instructor still wins on hosted APIs and business rules,** because it needs no logit access and can\n  enforce any Python check, such as line items summing to the total."},{"q":"How do you budget the token cost of retries on long inputs or long agent transcripts?","a":"- **Retries re-send and grow the prompt.** For a 20k-token contract that fails every time with\n  `max_retries=3`, illustrative input is ~20,000 + ~20,600 + ~21,200, about 61,800 tokens.\n- **Independence math is optimistic.** With p = 0.1 and three attempts the expected count is ~1.11 calls,\n  but hard inputs fail repeatedly, so the real cost sits in a small tail.\n- **So** cap retries per step, catch `InstructorRetryException`, and route the input to a stronger model\n  or human review instead of looping."},{"q":"How do you stop well-typed hallucinations in extracted fields?","a":"- **Ground validators in the source.** Pass the document or retrieved chunks as `context=` and reject any\n  quote or ID not found in it, which turns \"must be verbatim\" into a reask.\n- **Make omittable fields `Optional`.** A required field forces the model to invent a plausible value.\n- **Order fields for reasoning.** Put reasoning before the answer, because field order is generation order."}],"tradeoffs":["**Less glue vs another dependency.** One `response_model` argument replaces schema, parsing and retry glue, but mode defaults and provider adapters change between releases; pin it and test upgrades.","**Arbitrary rules vs extra calls.** Validate-and-reask enforces any Python rule, at the cost of extra full LLM calls that add latency to every affected agent step. When failures are mostly syntactic, native strict structured output removes them for free and the reask loop sits idle.","**Validated decisions vs model freedom.** Forcing one schema per call makes each agent decision checkable, but removes the model's native choice among many tools; for an open-ended tool loop, native tool calling is usually the better fit.","**No logit access needed vs no guarantee.** Hosted APIs work, but nothing guarantees validity. If you self-host and need validity on the first pass every time, constrained decoding (Outlines, vLLM guided decoding) wins.","**Rich schemas vs tokens.** Rich Pydantic schemas improve extraction quality, but deep nesting and long descriptions add input tokens on every call; for high-volume classification or routing, a flat model with a `Literal` field is cheaper."],"pitfalls":["**High `max_retries` with no budget.** One pathological input triggers several full-price calls. Symptom: a cost spike traced to a handful of requests or agent runs with repeated identical errors. Fix: cap attempts per route and escalate.","**Vague validator messages like \"invalid\".** The model cannot act on them. Symptom: retries returning the same wrong value each time. Fix: say what is wrong and how to fix it, including the legal values.","**Cost reporting that misses failed attempts.** Per-call logs, or a provider adapter that does not accumulate usage across retries, make failed attempts vanish. Symptom: dashboards under-report against the provider invoice. Verify with a forced validation failure, or log each attempt through hooks.","**Answer field before the reasoning field.** Field order is generation order, so the answer is committed before the reasoning exists and accuracy drops on harder cases. Fix: declare a `reasoning` field first.","**Every field required when the source often omits some.** The model must fill them. Symptom: plausible but absent values (a made-up phone number, a guessed date) that pass validation.","**Treating a validated object as true.** Validation proves the shape and your rules, not that the facts are in the source. Symptom: confident, well-typed hallucinations in evals.","**An uncaught retry exception ending the agent run.** Symptom: whole runs crash on one hard step. Fix: catch `InstructorRetryException` in the loop and choose a fallback action."],"when":"- **Use when:** you call hosted LLMs and need typed objects with rules beyond what a JSON schema can say,\n  especially across more than one provider: agent routers, planners, extractors and eval judges.\n- **Consider the SDK alone when:** your only need is valid JSON of a fixed shape from one provider; native\n  structured output (for example OpenAI's `parse` helper) may be enough without another dependency.\n- **Prefer native tool calling when:** the model must choose freely among many tools in the main agent\n  loop.\n- **Avoid when:** you self-host and need hard guarantees or very low latency; prefer constrained decoding.\n- **Measure:** first-pass validation rate, retries per request, cost per successful extraction or run, and\n  the fix rate of each retry attempt. A falling first-pass rate is an early signal of prompt or model drift.\n- **Interviews:** explain why validation-plus-reask and constrained decoding solve different halves of\n  the structured-output problem.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the Pydantic models, validators with actionable error messages, and retry limits, and logs every attempt with its token usage.","architect":"Decides where structure is enforced: provider strict mode, reask loops or constrained decoding, and sets the retry and cost budget per feature.","fde":"Turns a customer's messy documents into a typed schema quickly, using validators to encode their business rules so extraction errors surface as explicit failures."},"check":{"q":"Your extraction pipeline uses provider strict JSON mode and still produces wrong records. A\ncolleague says adding Instructor's retries is pointless because the JSON is already valid. Are they right?","a":"Partly. Strict mode guarantees syntax and schema shape, so Instructor's reask will never fire on\nthose failures. But the wrong records are semantic: dates swapped, totals not matching line items,\nvalues not present in the source. Those can be expressed as Pydantic validators, and Instructor feeds\neach validator's message back so the model can correct itself. The trade is extra calls per failure, so\nyou would cap retries, measure first-pass and post-retry accuracy on a labelled set, and keep a\nhuman-review path for records that still fail validation."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does Instructor do when the model's output fails Pydantic validation?","options":["It masks invalid tokens and resamples only the failing field","It sends the validation error back to the model in a new message and calls again, up to max_retries","It silently fills missing fields with their Pydantic defaults and returns the object","It switches automatically to a larger model for the next attempt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Instructor works on hosted APIs without logit access, so it cannot mask tokens. It reasks: the ValidationError text becomes a corrective message, and each retry is a full new LLM call."},{"q":"Why is the tool-calling mode usually preferred over plain JSON-in-prompt mode?","options":["Tool calls are billed at a lower token rate than normal completions","Tool calls let Instructor skip Pydantic validation entirely","Providers train models to emit well-formed arguments for declared tools, so first-pass validity is higher","JSON-in-prompt mode cannot represent nested models"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tool modes send the schema as a tool definition the provider is trained to honour, which reduces malformed output. Validation still runs afterwards; nothing about billing or nesting differs."},{"q":"Which requirement can Instructor enforce but provider strict schema mode alone cannot?","options":["That a field is an integer rather than a string","That an enum field only takes listed values","That the sum of extracted line items equals the stated invoice total","That every required field is present"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Types, enums and required fields are expressible in JSON Schema and strict mode enforces them. A cross-field arithmetic rule needs code, which lives in a Pydantic validator that drives the reask."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/outlines","sources":["Willard & Louf, 2023 — Efficient Guided Generation for Large Language Models","Official docs: Outlines"],"why":"- **Sampling knows nothing about your format.** A model samples one token at a time over its whole\n  vocabulary; nothing in that process knows you wanted a JSON object with an integer `age`. Prompting\n  raises the odds of valid output but never makes it certain.\n- **Small open models break formats most.** The ones you can afford to self-host break formats far more\n  often than frontier APIs, and an agent multiplies that risk by every step it takes.\n- **Parse-and-retry has two costs.** Each retry is another full generation, so tail latency multiplies.\n  And retries do not converge on hard inputs: a model that keeps writing `\"age\": \"about 40\"` will keep\n  doing it.\n- **If you control sampling, refuse invalid tokens.** At each step compute which tokens could continue a\n  valid string, set every other logit to negative infinity, and sample from what remains. Output is valid\n  on the first pass, always.\n- **The naive check is too slow.** Testing every token against the pattern at every step scans a\n  vocabulary of tens or hundreds of thousands of tokens per generated token. Outlines' contribution was\n  making that check effectively a lookup.","how":"### From pattern to automaton\n1. **Compile the constraint to a regular expression.** A regex is used directly. A JSON Schema becomes a\n   regex matching exactly the JSON strings that satisfy it (keys in schema order, types as sub-patterns,\n   enums as alternations). A `choice([\"yes\",\"no\"])` is an alternation.\n2. **Regex to finite-state machine.** The regex becomes a deterministic finite automaton over\n   *characters*: states, and transitions labelled by characters.\n3. **Index the vocabulary against the FSM (the key step).** For every state, walk each token's string\n   character by character; if the walk never falls off the automaton, the token is allowed there, and you\n   record where it lands. Result: a map `state -> {allowed token -> next state}`.\n4. **Build once, cache.** The index is built once per (pattern, tokenizer) pair *because* it is\n   expensive, then reused across every request with the same schema, such as every call of one tool.\n5. **Generate.** Each step: look up the current state's allowed set, mask all other logits, sample\n   (greedy, temperature, whatever you configured) from the survivors, follow the transition. Per-step\n   overhead is a lookup and a mask, not a vocabulary scan.\n\n### The maths\nWith allowed set A(s) in state *s*, the sampled distribution is\n`p'(t) = p(t) · 1[t ∈ A(s)] / Σ_{u ∈ A(s)} p(u)`: the model's probabilities restricted to legal tokens\nand renormalized.\n\n```\nindex build cost ≈ (number of FSM states) × (vocabulary size) × (average token length) character steps\n```\n\n- **More states, longer compile.** Long enumerations or many optional fields multiply states, so such a\n  schema takes visibly longer to compile than a two-field one; that is why the work happens once.\n- **Token level, not character level,** *because* tokens span several characters. A single token such as\n  `name` moves the FSM four characters forward in one step, and the index also records tokens valid only\n  because they straddle a boundary, such as `\":\"`.\n\n### Constraining an agent's tool call\n- **One schema per tool, or one union schema per step.** Give each tool's argument model its own\n  compiled index, or compile a single schema whose `tool` field is a `Literal` of tool names with\n  matching argument objects.\n- **The engine applies it per request.** With vLLM you send the schema in `response_format` and the\n  engine masks tokens for that request only, so different agent steps can use different schemas.\n- **Decide \"call a tool or answer\" first.** A tiny choice constraint (for example `call` or `answer`)\n  routes the step, then a second constrained call fills the arguments; or fold both into one schema.\n\n### Grammars\n- **Regexes cannot describe arbitrary nesting.** Recursive JSON schemas, or code, need a context-free\n  grammar, which requires a pushdown automaton (a state plus a stack).\n- **Grammar mode exists but costs more.** Outlines supports it, but it is costlier and less mature than\n  the regex path; engines such as vLLM also offer alternative grammar backends for this reason.\n- **Backends move.** The core was later rewritten as a separate `outlines-core` package, and recent\n  versions can delegate to other backends; which one runs is a version detail to check.\n\n### Where it runs\n- **Local runtimes:** Outlines wraps Hugging Face Transformers, llama.cpp, MLX and others.\n- **Serving engines:** its core FSM logic is used inside them; vLLM's guided decoding has offered an\n  Outlines backend. This is where most agent teams meet it.\n- **The API changed between major versions.** Older code uses `outlines.generate.json(model, Schema)`;\n  the 1.x API passes the output type when calling the model. Check the version you install.\n\n### What constraining does to quality\nMasking renormalizes over allowed tokens. If the model strongly wanted a forbidden token, it is pushed\nonto a path it considered unlikely, and content there can be worse even though the format is perfect.\nTwo mechanisms make this worse than it sounds:\n- **The mask is greedy, not look-ahead.** It asks \"can this token still lead to a valid string?\", not\n  \"is this a good continuation?\". If the model's mass sits on prose like `Sure! {\"intent\"...`, the mask\n  removes `Sure` and renormalizes into a state the model was not planning for.\n- **Fix: describe the format in the prompt** (or show an example). The model's own preference then\n  aligns with the mask, so little probability is removed. For tool calls, list the tools and their\n  arguments in the system prompt as you would without constraints.\n- **Non-canonical tokenization.** The index allows any token sequence that spells a valid string,\n  including splits the tokenizer would never produce (for example `\"` + `int` + `ent` where training data\n  always had `\"intent`). The model has rarely seen these, so later predictions degrade.\n- **Still measure accuracy, not just parse rate.** Mature implementations handle forced boundaries\n  carefully, but constraints guarantee syntax, never correctness: a valid call to the wrong tool is\n  still a wrong step.","example":"### Agent scenario: constrained tool calls on a self-hosted model\nA support agent on a self-hosted model served by vLLM. Each step asks for one of two tool calls; the\nengine constrains decoding so the reply always parses.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom typing import Literal, Union\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass SearchDocs(BaseModel):\n    tool: Literal[\"search_docs\"]\n    query: str = Field(max_length=200)          # bounded: no runaway strings\n\nclass GetTicket(BaseModel):\n    tool: Literal[\"get_ticket\"]\n    ticket_id: int\n\nclass Step(BaseModel):\n    reasoning: str = Field(max_length=400)      # reason first, then act\n    call: Union[SearchDocs, GetTicket]\n\nllm = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://vllm.internal:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\nresp = llm.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"my-7b-instruct\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Tools: search_docs(query), get_ticket(ticket_id). \"\n                                            \"Reply with JSON: reasoning, then call.\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is the status of ticket 42?\"}],\n    response_format={\"type\": \"json_schema\",\n                     \"json_schema\": {\"name\": \"step\", \"schema\": Step.model_json_schema()}},\n    max_tokens=300,                             # above the longest valid output\n)\nstep = Step.model_validate_json(resp.choices[0].message.content)   # parses; may still be the wrong tool\nprint(step.call)\n```\n\n- **The schema is the tool registry.** `Literal` tool names plus per-tool argument models mean the model\n  cannot invent a tool or an argument type.\n- **Bounded strings** (`max_length` becomes `maxLength`) stop a looping model from filling the window.\n- **Which backend masks tokens** (Outlines or another) is an engine setting whose name varies by version.\n\n### Worked scenario: parse failures across a multi-step run\nA self-hosted 7B agent averages 6 tool-calling steps per run. Suppose 4% of unconstrained steps fail to\nparse (illustrative; measure your own).\n\n| | Unconstrained plus retry | Constrained |\n|---|---|---|\n| Runs with at least one bad step | 1 − 0.96^6 ≈ 22% | 0% from parsing |\n| Extra generations | One or more retries on those steps | None |\n| p99 run latency | Extra steps' worth of generation | One generation per step |\n| What can still go wrong | Wrong tool, wrong arguments | Wrong tool, wrong arguments |\n\nThe constraint removes the format failures; the remaining errors are decision errors your agent evals\nmust catch.\n\n### Toy FSM index and one masked step (pure Python)\nA choice constraint, a tiny vocabulary, the precomputed index, and one renormalized sampling step.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\nchoices = [\"yes\", \"no\", \"not sure\"]                    # constraint: one of these strings\nvocab = [\"y\", \"ye\", \"yes\", \"n\", \"no\", \"not\", \" sure\", \"s\", \"x\", \"Sure\"]\n\n# FSM states = every prefix of a valid string; build the index once.\nstates = {c[:i] for c in choices for i in range(len(c) + 1)}\nindex = {s: {t: s + t for t in vocab if s + t in states} for s in states}\n\nprint(index[\"\"])      # tokens allowed at the start, and the state each lands in\nprint(index[\"not\"])   # only ' sure' can continue 'not'\n\n# One decoding step: mask disallowed logits, renormalize, pick.\nlogits = {\"Sure\": 3.0, \"yes\": 1.0, \"no\": 0.5, \"x\": 0.2, \"y\": 0.1}\nallowed = {t: v for t, v in logits.items() if t in index[\"\"]}\nz = sum(math.exp(v) for v in allowed.values())\nprobs = {t: round(math.exp(v) / z, 3) for t, v in allowed.items()}\nprint(probs)          # 'Sure' had the most mass but is masked out\n```\n\nOutput: `index[\"\"]` allows `y`, `ye`, `yes`, `n`, `no`, `not`; the step gives roughly\n`{'yes': 0.497, 'no': 0.301, 'y': 0.202}`. That is the greedy-mask problem in miniature: the model's\nfavourite token is gone and its mass is spread over paths it ranked lower.\n\n### Ticket classifier on a self-hosted model\nClassifying 50,000 support tickets on a self-hosted 7B model into\n`{\"intent\": one of 6 labels, \"urgent\": true|false}`, the routing step in front of a support agent.\n\n| | Parse-and-retry | Outlines |\n|---|---|---|\n| Parse failures | Suppose 4% (illustrative; measure your own) | None: every output parses |\n| Extra work | 2,000 tickets need at least one more generation, some several | No retries exist |\n| p99 latency | Two or three generations long | One generation |\n| Compile | None | JSON Schema compiles once into an index |\n\n- **What the model may emit.** Apart from whatever optional whitespace the pattern permits:\n  `{\"intent\":\"`, then the tokens of one of the six labels, then the closing quote, `,\"urgent\":`, then\n  `true` or `false`, then `}`.\n- **Far fewer free tokens.** Most structure is forced. When only one token is allowed there is nothing to\n  sample, and some implementations append such forced tokens directly and process several in one forward\n  pass.\n- **Field order is generation order.** To make the model reason, put a bounded `reasoning` string\n  *before* `intent`; otherwise the label is chosen with no reasoning to condition on.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nimport outlines\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\n\nclass Ticket(BaseModel):\n    intent: Literal[\"billing\", \"bug\", \"how_to\", \"account\", \"feature\", \"other\"]\n    urgent: bool\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\"   # any local causal LM\nmodel = outlines.from_transformers(\n    AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name), AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name))\nraw = model(\"Classify: 'I was charged twice this month, fix it today.'\", Ticket, max_new_tokens=50)\nprint(Ticket.model_validate_json(raw))   # always parses; may still be the wrong label\n```","patterns":["**Constrain tool calls in the serving engine.** When agents run on vLLM or a similar engine, request structured output per call through its OpenAI-compatible API (`response_format` with `\"type\": \"json_schema\"`) instead of embedding Outlines in app code; which decoding backend runs is an engine setting whose name varies by version. Monitor per-token latency with and without constraints.","**Compile at startup, cache by schema.** Keep a fixed set of tool schemas so each compiles once; build generators when the worker starts so no agent step pays compile latency, and for dynamic schemas cache by a hash of the schema plus tokenizer. Monitor compile time and cache hit rate.","**Bound every free-text field.** Use `Field(max_length=...)` (JSON Schema `maxLength`) on strings and set `max_new_tokens` above the longest valid output. Monitor how often generation stops at the token limit; it should be near zero.","**Prompt for the format and use the chat template.** Build the prompt with `tokenizer.apply_chat_template(..., add_generation_prompt=True)` and describe the JSON and tools in it, so the mask removes little probability. Watch for degenerate values on easy inputs.","**Gate on task success, not parse rate.** Before rollout, run the agent eval set constrained and unconstrained and keep the better configuration. Track tool-selection accuracy and run success per release, since parse rate will read 100%."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A small self-hosted model drives an agent only if its tool arguments\n  parse every step. One malformed call per few dozen steps breaks a meaningful share of multi-step runs;\n  constrained decoding removes that failure class entirely.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Inside serving engines rather than your app: vLLM's guided\n  decoding has offered an Outlines backend, reached through the OpenAI-compatible `response_format`.\n  Directly, it wraps Transformers, llama.cpp or MLX for local agents and offline batch steps.\n- **What you control.** The schema (bounded strings, field order, `Literal` labels), the prompt that\n  describes the format, the chat template, and whether you constrain at all for a given step.\n- **What you can skip.** Building FSM indexes by hand and grammar internals. Know that the constraint\n  fixes syntax, never correctness, and that compile cost lands on the first request per schema.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Local tool-calling agents.** A small open model on a laptop or single GPU emits tool calls that\n  always parse, so the loop never stalls on malformed arguments.\n- **Self-hosted agent platforms.** Teams serving open models behind vLLM use guided decoding through the\n  OpenAI-compatible `response_format` so every agent step returns schema-shaped JSON.\n- **Routing and classification steps.** A choice list constrains a router to the exact labels the agent\n  branches on, with no free-text drift.\n- **Offline batch steps.** Bulk extraction or labelling feeding an agent's knowledge base runs with no\n  parse-and-retry pass.","mini_project":"**Constrained versus unconstrained tool calls on a small model (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** measure how constrained decoding changes tool-call validity for a small local model in a\n  short agent loop.\n- **Steps:** pick a small open model you can run locally; define a tool-call schema with a `Literal`\n  tool name and bounded string arguments; run 50 prompts unconstrained and parse the output, then run the\n  same prompts with Outlines constraining to the schema; record parse rate, correct-tool rate and latency\n  including the first-request compile cost.\n- **Done when:** you have a table comparing both runs and a short note separating syntax gains (parse\n  rate) from correctness (right tool, right arguments), which the constraint does not fix."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Tool schema, regex or choice list\"] --> R[\"Compile to regex\"]\n    R --> F[\"Character-level FSM\"]\n    F --> I[(\"Index: state to allowed tokens and next state\")]\n    T[\"Tokenizer vocabulary\"] --> I\n    I --> L[\"Look up allowed set for current state\"]\n    G[\"Model logits for next token\"] --> K[\"Mask others to minus infinity, renormalize\"]\n    L --> K\n    K --> P[\"Sample from survivors\"]\n    P -->|\"follow transition\"| L\n    P --> O[\"Tool call valid by construction\"]","caption":"The top half (pattern to index) runs once per (pattern, tokenizer) pair, for example once per tool\nschema; the loop below it runs every token and only does a lookup and a mask."},"interview":[{"q":"What is constrained decoding, and why can't prompting alone give the same guarantee?","a":"- **It filters the vocabulary before each sample.** Tokens that cannot continue a valid string get a logit\n  of negative infinity, and the model samples from what remains, renormalized.\n- **Prompting only shifts probabilities.** The sampler knows nothing about your format, so a prompt raises\n  the odds of valid JSON but never makes it certain, especially on small self-hosted models."},{"q":"Your self-hosted agent fails about one run in five on malformed tool calls. How would you fix it?","a":"- **Compute the run-level effect.** With six steps and an illustrative 4% per-step parse failure,\n  1 − 0.96^6 ≈ 22% of runs hit at least one bad call, which matches the symptom.\n- **Constrain in the engine.** Send each step's schema (a `Literal` of tool names plus argument models)\n  as `response_format` with `\"type\": \"json_schema\"`, so every call parses on the first pass.\n- **Then measure decisions.** Parse failures go to zero; track tool-selection accuracy and run success on\n  the agent eval set, since wrong-but-valid calls remain."},{"q":"Your constrained classifier parses 100% of outputs, but accuracy dropped versus free-form output. What do you check?","a":"- **The prompt.** If it does not describe the JSON, the model's mass sits on prose like `Sure!`, and the\n  greedy mask pushes it onto paths it ranked low.\n- **The chat template.** Instruct models need `apply_chat_template(..., add_generation_prompt=True)`; a\n  bare prompt degrades quality.\n- **Field order.** If `intent` comes before any `reasoning` field, the label is committed with nothing to\n  condition on.\n- **Non-canonical token splits,** which the index allows but the model has rarely seen in training."},{"q":"When would you not use Outlines?","a":"- **On closed hosted APIs,** because masking needs the logits before sampling; use native structured output\n  or a validate-and-retry library like Instructor.\n- **For per-request dynamic schemas you cannot cache,** such as tools generated per user, because each new\n  schema pays index compile time.\n- **For deeply recursive structures,** which need grammar mode; it costs more per step and is less mature\n  than the regex path."},{"q":"Where does the cost of constrained decoding go, and how do you keep it off an agent's request path?","a":"- **Into the index build.** Roughly FSM states × vocabulary size × average token length character steps,\n  so long enums and many optional fields compile visibly slower.\n- **Per token it is a lookup and a mask,** not a scan of tens or hundreds of thousands of tokens.\n- **So** keep a fixed set of tool schemas, compile them at worker startup, cache dynamic ones by a hash of\n  schema plus tokenizer, and monitor compile time and cache hit rate."},{"q":"Why do unbounded string fields cause truncated output even under a constraint?","a":"- **Any length is valid.** A looping model keeps emitting legal characters until `max_new_tokens`, leaving\n  the JSON unclosed.\n- **Fix:** set `maxLength` (Pydantic `Field(max_length=...)`) on every free-text field, set\n  `max_new_tokens` above the longest valid output, and alert on generations that stop at the limit."}],"tradeoffs":["**Guaranteed validity vs owning the stack.** Validity is guaranteed on the first pass and retries vanish, but only when you own the inference stack; on hosted APIs you rely on the provider's structured output or on validate-and-retry tools.","**One-time compile vs dynamic schemas.** Index construction is paid once per schema and tokenizer; for a few fixed tool schemas it amortizes to nothing, but per-request dynamic schemas (tools generated per user) pay compile latency each time unless cached.","**No format errors vs content quality.** Hard constraints can lower content quality when the mask fights the model's preferences; a looser pattern plus validation sometimes scores better on accuracy evals.","**Regex speed vs grammar reach.** Regex-based JSON is fast and robust for flat or bounded schemas; deeply recursive structures need grammar mode, which costs more per step and is where alternative backends may perform better."],"pitfalls":["**Unbounded strings or whitespace in the pattern.** The model can fill tokens until `max_new_tokens`. Symptom: outputs truncated at the limit, which then fail to parse despite the constraint. Fix: bound strings with `maxLength`.","**Assuming constrained output is correct output.** Symptom: a perfect parse rate alongside poor accuracy on a labelled set or failing agent runs, because format and truth are different properties.","**Answer field before any reasoning field.** The label or tool is committed first. Symptom: lower accuracy than the same model achieves in free-form chain-of-thought. Fix: put a bounded `reasoning` field first.","**Copying tutorial code across major versions.** The `outlines.generate.*` functions and the old `outlines.models.transformers(...)` loader are pre-1.0 idioms. Symptom: `AttributeError` or `TypeError` on those calls after an upgrade.","**Constraining without describing the format in the prompt.** The mask fights the model's preferred opening. Symptom: valid JSON with odd or degenerate field values on easy inputs.","**Forgetting the chat template for instruct models.** A bare prompt degrades quality. Symptom: worse labels than the same model gives through its usual chat interface."],"when":"- **Use when:** you self-host open models and need structured output at volume: classification,\n  extraction, routing, agent tool arguments on a small model.\n- **Most valuable when:** retries are expensive or latency-bound, small models break formats often, and\n  agent runs chain many structured steps.\n- **Avoid when:** you use closed APIs, which do not expose logits; use their native structured output or a\n  validate-and-retry library like Instructor.\n- **Measure:** parse rate (should become 100%), task accuracy and agent run success with and without the\n  constraint, and compile latency for new schemas.\n- **Interviews:** explain the FSM index and why constraining fixes syntax but not semantics.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines schemas with bounded strings and sensible field order, wires constrained decoding into local inference, and compares accuracy with and without the constraint.","architect":"Decides whether structure is enforced at decode time on self-hosted models or by validation on hosted APIs, and which serving engine backend carries it.","fde":"In air-gapped or on-prem deployments with only small open models, uses constrained decoding to get reliable structured output where retries would blow latency budgets."},"check":{"q":"Why does Outlines precompute an index from FSM states to allowed tokens instead of checking the\nregex against each candidate token at generation time, and what does that cost you?","a":"Checking at generation time means testing every vocabulary token, tens or hundreds of thousands,\nagainst the pattern from the current state on every step, which adds a large per-token overhead. Since the\nset of valid tokens depends only on the FSM state, the pattern and the tokenizer, it can be computed once:\nfor each state, walk each token's characters through the automaton and record whether it survives and\nwhere it lands. Generation then becomes lookup plus masking. The cost moves to compile time and memory\nper schema, which is cheap for a few stable schemas but painful for per-request dynamic schemas, so you\ncache indexes and avoid generating schemas on the fly."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't Outlines enforce a schema on a closed hosted model API?","options":["Hosted models use a different tokenizer format that Outlines cannot parse","It must mask logits before each token is sampled, and hosted APIs do not expose that step","Hosted APIs reject requests containing regular expressions","JSON Schema is only supported by open-weight models"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Constrained decoding intervenes inside the sampling loop. Hosted APIs return finished tokens, so the only options there are the provider's own structured output or validating and retrying afterwards."},{"q":"A constrained classifier now parses 100% of outputs but its accuracy did not improve. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The FSM index was built for the wrong tokenizer","Constrained decoding always lowers temperature to zero","Constraints guarantee format, not correctness, so the model can still choose the wrong valid label","The schema compiled to a context-free grammar instead of a regex"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Masking removes invalid tokens but leaves the model's choice among valid ones untouched. Previous parse failures may also have been the hard cases, so accuracy can stay flat or even drop."},{"q":"Which schema design choice tends to cause truncated, unparseable outputs under constrained decoding?","options":["Using a Literal field with six allowed values","Putting a boolean field last in the object","An unbounded free-text string field, which lets generation run until max_new_tokens","Declaring all fields as required"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The constraint allows any length string, so a looping model keeps emitting valid characters until the token limit, leaving the JSON unclosed. Bounding length with maxLength prevents it."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/mcp-python-sdk","sources":["Official docs: Model Context Protocol","Official docs: MCP Python SDK"],"why":"- **Every host defined tools its own way.** An OpenAI function schema here, a LangChain tool class there,\n  a custom plugin format in each IDE.\n- **So integrations multiplied.** A team wanting internal search, ticketing and database tools in three\n  assistants wrote three integrations and kept them in sync.\n- **MCP fixes the interface.** A server advertises tools, resources and prompts over a standard protocol,\n  and any compliant host, including your own agent, can use them.\n- **The protocol is not trivial by hand.** JSON-RPC 2.0, version and capability negotiation during\n  `initialize`, JSON Schema for every tool input, request routing, notifications and cancellation, over at\n  least two transports with different framing.\n- **Hand-rolled versions get details wrong.** Schemas drift from the function signature, a stray `print`\n  corrupts the stdio stream, version negotiation is skipped. The SDK handles the protocol once, correctly,\n  so your code is just the tool logic.","how":"### Two layers\n- **FastMCP (high level).** `FastMCP(\"name\")` creates a server; decorators register capabilities. It\n  inspects each function's signature and type hints, builds a Pydantic model, and emits its JSON Schema\n  as the tool's `inputSchema`.\n- **Why derive the schema:** the signature is already the source of truth, so the two cannot drift.\n  Incoming arguments are validated against the same model before your function runs. The docstring\n  becomes the tool description the LLM reads when choosing a tool.\n- **Low-level `Server`.** Explicit handlers for `list_tools`, `call_tool` and so on, for dynamic tool\n  lists or full control over the wire format.\n- **Name clash to watch.** A separately maintained project, also called FastMCP, extends the original API\n  that was folded into the official SDK; imports differ, so check which one a codebase uses.\n\n### Three primitives\n| Decorator | Exposes | Controlled by |\n|---|---|---|\n| `@mcp.tool()` | An action the model may choose to call, e.g. `create_ticket` | The model |\n| `@mcp.resource(\"tickets://{id}\")` | Readable data addressed by URI | The host application (not necessarily the model) |\n| `@mcp.prompt()` | A reusable prompt template a user can pick | The user |\n\nThe split exists *because* control differs, and hosts present the three differently. In your own agent,\ntools go into the model's tool list; resources are context your code decides to load.\n\n### The session lifecycle\n1. **Connect.** The client starts or connects to the server through a transport.\n2. **`initialize`.** Client and server exchange protocol versions and capabilities.\n3. **Discovery.** `tools/list`, `resources/list`, `prompts/list`. Your agent turns the tool list into\n   the model's tool definitions.\n4. **Use.** `tools/call` with JSON arguments the model produced. The result is a list of content items\n   (text, image, embedded resource), with an `isError` flag for tool-level failures. Recent protocol\n   versions also allow structured output validated against an output schema, which the SDK can derive\n   from a typed return.\n\nOn the wire, step 4 is one JSON-RPC request and one response matched by `id`, which is what lets many\ncalls be in flight on one connection (for example parallel tool calls in one agent turn):\n\n```\n-> {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":3,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\n    \"params\":{\"name\":\"create_ticket\",\"arguments\":{\"title\":\"VPN down\",\"priority\":\"high\"}}}\n<- {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":3,\"result\":{\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"{\\\"id\\\": 1, ...}\"}],\n    \"isError\":false}}\n```\n\n### Two failure channels\n| Failure | Returned as | Handled by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Malformed request, unknown tool | JSON-RPC `error` | The host (protocol failure) |\n| Tool ran and failed | Normal `result` with `isError: true` | The model, which can read the message and try something else |\n\n- **Notifications** (messages with no `id`) carry logs and progress and need no reply.\n- **In your agent loop,** feed `isError` results back to the model as the tool result, and handle\n  JSON-RPC errors in code (retry, drop the tool, or fail the step).\n\n### Transports\n| | stdio | Streamable HTTP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Start | `mcp.run()` (the default) | `mcp.run(transport=\"streamable-http\")` |\n| Mechanics | Host spawns your server as a subprocess; newline-delimited JSON-RPC over stdin and stdout | Same protocol over HTTP |\n| Fits | Local tools; simple, no network | Remote, multi-client servers shared by many agents |\n| Auth | Not needed | Your concern |\n\n- **The older HTTP+SSE transport is deprecated** in favour of Streamable HTTP.\n- **Streamable HTTP is *stateful* by default.** It issues a session ID header after `initialize`, and later\n  requests must reach the process holding that session, so behind a load balancer you need sticky routing.\n- **Stateless mode** (`stateless_http=True`) treats each request independently. It scales horizontally\n  but gives up server-initiated messages that depend on a live session.\n\n### Context, lifespan and server-to-client requests\n- **`ctx: Context` parameter.** FastMCP injects it (and hides it from the schema); use it to send log\n  messages and progress notifications back to the client during long operations.\n- **MCP is bidirectional.** A server can *ask the client* for capabilities the client advertised:\n  **sampling** (the host's LLM generates text, so the server needs no model key of its own) and\n  **elicitation** (ask the user for missing input mid-call). Hosts vary, so check capabilities rather\n  than assuming.\n- **Shared resources go in a `lifespan`.** Pass an async context manager to `FastMCP(...)`; it runs once\n  at startup and shutdown and its yielded object is reachable from the context, *because* opening a\n  connection per tool call repeats setup and leaks under load.\n\n### Client side\n- **`ClientSession` plus a transport helper such as `stdio_client`** lets Python code act as a host:\n  initialize, list tools, call them. This is how your own agent consumes MCP servers.\n- **Everything is async,** built on `anyio`.","example":"### Agent scenario: MCP tools in your own tool-calling loop\nThe agent connects to the ticket server below, exposes its tools to the model, executes the calls the\nmodel makes, and feeds results (including `isError` ones) back until the model answers.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\nllm = AsyncOpenAI()\n\nasync def run_agent(task: str, max_steps: int = 6) -> str:\n    params = StdioServerParameters(command=\"python\", args=[\"server.py\"])\n    async with stdio_client(params) as (read, write), ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n        await session.initialize()\n        tools = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": t.name, \"description\": t.description,\n                  \"parameters\": t.inputSchema}} for t in (await session.list_tools()).tools]\n        messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}]\n        for _ in range(max_steps):                         # step budget\n            msg = (await llm.chat.completions.create(\n                model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", messages=messages, tools=tools)).choices[0].message\n            if not msg.tool_calls:\n                return msg.content\n            messages.append(msg)\n            for call in msg.tool_calls:\n                res = await session.call_tool(call.function.name, json.loads(call.function.arguments))\n                text = \"\".join(c.text for c in res.content if c.type == \"text\")\n                messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id,\n                                 \"content\": (\"ERROR: \" if res.isError else \"\") + text})\n        return \"step budget exhausted\"\n\nprint(asyncio.run(run_agent(\"Open a high-priority ticket: VPN is down for the Berlin office.\")))\n```\n\n- **The MCP schema is passed straight through** as the function's `parameters`, so the type hints on the\n  server are what constrain the model.\n- **`isError` results go back to the model,** which is the channel for recovering from a bad call.\n\n### Ticket server and stdio client\nA small ticket server and a client that calls it over stdio.\n\n```python\n# server.py\nfrom typing import Literal\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP, Context\n\nmcp = FastMCP(\"tickets\")\nDB: dict[int, dict] = {}\n\n@mcp.tool()\nasync def create_ticket(title: str, priority: Literal[\"low\", \"high\"], ctx: Context) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Create a support ticket. Use only after confirming the issue with the user.\"\"\"\n    tid = len(DB) + 1\n    DB[tid] = {\"id\": tid, \"title\": title, \"priority\": priority}\n    await ctx.info(f\"created ticket {tid}\")      # goes to the client, never to stdout\n    return DB[tid]\n\n@mcp.resource(\"tickets://{tid}\")\ndef read_ticket(tid: int) -> str:\n    return str(DB.get(int(tid), \"not found\"))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    mcp.run()                                    # stdio by default\n\n# client.py\nimport asyncio\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def main():\n    params = StdioServerParameters(command=\"python\", args=[\"server.py\"])\n    async with stdio_client(params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            print([t.name for t in (await session.list_tools()).tools])\n            res = await session.call_tool(\"create_ticket\", {\"title\": \"VPN down\", \"priority\": \"high\"})\n            print(res.content[0].text, res.isError)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **The `priority` hint becomes an `enum`** in the schema, so the model sees exactly two legal values,\n  and a call with `\"urgent\"` is rejected before your code runs.\n- **Generated `inputSchema` for `create_ticket`** is roughly\n  `{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"title\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"priority\":{\"enum\":[\"low\",\"high\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"title\",\"priority\"]}`;\n  `ctx` is absent because FastMCP injects it.\n- **Inspect before any LLM is involved.** With the SDK's CLI extra installed, `mcp dev server.py` opens\n  the MCP Inspector, where you can see that schema and call the tool by hand.\n\n### Production shape: lifespan pool, actionable errors, stateless HTTP\nOne database pool per process, errors the model can act on, and a transport that scales behind a load\nbalancer when many agents share the server.\n\n```python\nimport sys, logging\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nimport asyncpg\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP, Context\n\nlogging.basicConfig(stream=sys.stderr, level=logging.INFO)   # never stdout\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(server: FastMCP):\n    pool = await asyncpg.create_pool(\"postgresql://app@db/tickets\")\n    try:\n        yield {\"pool\": pool}                     # reachable from every tool's ctx\n    finally:\n        await pool.close()\n\nmcp = FastMCP(\"tickets\", lifespan=lifespan, stateless_http=True)\n\n@mcp.tool()\nasync def get_ticket(ticket_id: int, ctx: Context) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Fetch one ticket by numeric id. Use search_tickets first if you only have a title.\"\"\"\n    pool = ctx.request_context.lifespan_context[\"pool\"]\n    row = await pool.fetchrow(\"SELECT id, title, status FROM tickets WHERE id = $1\", ticket_id)\n    if row is None:\n        raise ValueError(f\"ticket {ticket_id} does not exist; call search_tickets with a title\")\n    return dict(row)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    mcp.run(transport=\"streamable-http\")\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: \"session not found\" after scaling out\nA stateful Streamable HTTP server goes from 1 replica to 3 behind a round-robin load balancer.\n\n| Replicas | Chance a follow-up request reaches the process holding the session | Symptom |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 100% | Works |\n| 3, round-robin | roughly 1 in 3 | Intermittent \"session not found\" or 4xx errors on about two-thirds of follow-ups |\n| 3, sticky on session ID header | 100% | Works, but a replica restart drops its sessions |\n| 3, `stateless_http=True` | Not needed: no session to find | Works; server-initiated messages that need a live session are gone |\n\n### Worked scenario: what a large tool list costs an agent\nIllustrative: an agent connects to four MCP servers exposing 40 tools, each schema plus description about\n150 tokens.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Tool definitions per model call | 40 × ~150 ≈ 6,000 tokens |\n| Over a 20-step run | ~120,000 input tokens on tool definitions alone |\n| Exposing only the 8 tools this task needs | ~1,200 tokens per call, ~24,000 per run |\n\nPrompt caching softens the price when the tool list is byte-identical and first, but selection accuracy\nstill falls as near-duplicate tools pile up.","patterns":["**Keep stdout clean on stdio.** Configure `logging` to `sys.stderr` at startup, log through `ctx` for client-visible messages, and silence libraries that print progress bars. Monitor the host's MCP logs for parse errors and unexpected disconnects.","**Curate the tool list per agent.** Load only the servers and tools a given agent needs, and keep their order stable so the prefix caches. Monitor tokens spent on tool definitions per call and the rate of wrong-tool selections in agent evals.","**Pick a scaling mode for HTTP explicitly.** Either run `stateless_http=True` behind any load balancer, or route on the session ID header with sticky sessions. Alert on \"session not found\" and 4xx rates per replica.","**Own shared resources in `lifespan`.** Create pools and clients once in the lifespan context manager and read them via `ctx.request_context.lifespan_context`. Monitor pool saturation and per-tool latency.","**Write errors for the model.** Raise exceptions whose message says what was wrong and what to call instead; FastMCP returns them as `isError` results. Monitor `isError` rate per tool and repeated identical calls, which signal an unhelpful message.","**Contract-test the schema and authenticate HTTP.** Snapshot `list_tools()` output (names, descriptions, `inputSchema`) under a real `ClientSession` in CI and fail on unreviewed changes, because a renamed parameter or reworded docstring changes model behaviour. Put Streamable HTTP behind auth and origin checks, and audit-log every `tools/call` with caller identity."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Tools are what make an agent act. MCP gives them one interface, so a\n  ticket or search tool you write once works in IDE assistants, desktop chat apps and your own agents,\n  and your agent can use third-party servers without bespoke glue.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Server side: `FastMCP` with `@mcp.tool()`. Client side:\n  `ClientSession` feeding `list_tools()` into your model's tool definitions; agent frameworks such as the\n  OpenAI Agents SDK and LangChain (through its MCP adapters package) can load MCP servers as tools.\n- **What you control.** Tool names, docstrings and type hints (the model reads them), error messages the\n  model recovers from, the transport, auth, and how many tools you expose per agent.\n- **What you can skip.** Hand-writing JSON-RPC, capability negotiation and transport framing; the SDK\n  owns those. Know the lifecycle, the two failure channels and why stdout is sacred on stdio.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Internal tools for every assistant.** A team publishes ticketing, search or database lookups once as\n  an MCP server and uses them from IDE assistants, desktop chat apps and in-house agents.\n- **Agents that consume third-party servers.** Your agent loop connects a `ClientSession`, calls\n  `list_tools()` and passes the result to the model as tool definitions, with no bespoke glue per tool.\n- **Local developer agents over stdio.** A desktop host spawns your server as a subprocess for file,\n  git or project-specific actions on the developer's machine.\n- **Shared remote tool services.** One Streamable HTTP server with authentication serves many agents and\n  users, so tool logic and access control live in one place.","mini_project":"**Build an MCP tool server and call it from your own agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** expose two tools with FastMCP and drive them from a minimal agent loop you write.\n- **Steps:** write a `FastMCP` server with two `@mcp.tool()` functions (for example a note search and a\n  note create) with clear docstrings and type hints; run it over stdio, logging to stderr only; write a\n  client with `ClientSession` that lists tools, maps them to your model's tool format, runs a tool-calling\n  loop and returns tool errors to the model; test one call that fails on purpose.\n- **Done when:** the agent completes a two-step task using both tools, recovers from the deliberate tool\n  error, and the same server also works unchanged in one existing MCP host."},"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant H as Agent host with ClientSession\n    participant S as FastMCP server\n    H->>S: initialize with protocol version and capabilities\n    S-->>H: server version and capabilities\n    H->>S: tools/list\n    S-->>H: tools with inputSchema from type hints\n    Note over H: model picks create_ticket from the tool list\n    H->>S: tools/call create_ticket, id 3\n    Note over S: validate arguments against the Pydantic model\n    S-->>H: notification from ctx.info, no id\n    S-->>H: result for id 3, content list and isError false","caption":"One session over stdio inside an agent: negotiation first, then discovery feeding the model's tool list,\nthen a tool call whose log message travels back as a notification, never through `print`."},"interview":[{"q":"What does FastMCP do for you that a hand-written MCP server would not?","a":"- **It derives each tool's `inputSchema` from type hints** through a Pydantic model, so the schema cannot\n  drift from the signature, and arguments are validated before your function runs.\n- **It handles the protocol:** JSON-RPC 2.0 framing, `initialize` version and capability negotiation,\n  routing, notifications and the transports.\n- **The docstring becomes the tool description,** which is the text the model uses to choose the tool."},{"q":"How do you wire MCP servers into your own tool-calling agent?","a":"- **Discover, then translate.** Open a `ClientSession`, call `list_tools()`, and map each tool's name,\n  description and `inputSchema` into your model provider's tool definition format.\n- **Execute and feed back.** For each tool call the model makes, run `call_tool` and append the content\n  as the tool result, prefixing or flagging `isError` results so the model can recover.\n- **Budget it.** Cap steps, expose only the tools the task needs, and log every call in the trace."},{"q":"Explain the difference between a JSON-RPC error and an `isError` result.","a":"- **JSON-RPC `error` is a protocol failure,** such as a malformed request or unknown tool, and the host\n  handles it.\n- **`isError: true` is a tool failure inside a normal `result`.** The model reads the message and can try\n  something else, which is why exception text should say what to call instead.\n- **FastMCP maps a raised exception to `isError`,** so a vague `Exception(\"failed\")` produces retry loops."},{"q":"You scaled a Streamable HTTP server from 1 to 3 replicas and see intermittent \"session not found\". Why, and what are the fixes?","a":"- **Stateful mode pins a session to one process.** After `initialize` the server issues a session ID\n  header, and round-robin sends roughly two-thirds of follow-ups to a replica that never saw it.\n- **Fix one: sticky routing** on the session ID header; a replica restart still drops its sessions.\n- **Fix two: `stateless_http=True`,** which scales behind any load balancer but gives up server-initiated\n  messages that need a live session."},{"q":"When would you not wrap a function as an MCP tool?","a":"- **When one in-process agent is the only caller,** because a plain function call avoids the process\n  boundary and protocol overhead.\n- **When the tool list is already large,** because every schema occupies context on every turn; past a few\n  dozen tools, selection accuracy and token cost both suffer."},{"q":"How would you test an MCP server before any LLM is involved?","a":"- **Manually:** `mcp dev server.py` (with the CLI extra) opens the MCP Inspector to view schemas and call\n  tools by hand.\n- **In CI:** start the server under a real `ClientSession`, snapshot `list_tools()` output and fail on\n  unreviewed changes, since a renamed parameter or reworded docstring changes model behaviour.\n- **Exercise failure paths** and assert they return `isError` with an actionable message."}],"tradeoffs":["**Every host vs a process boundary.** One MCP server makes a tool usable from every compliant host, but you pay a process boundary and protocol overhead; for a tool used by exactly one in-process agent, a plain function call is simpler.","**stdio vs Streamable HTTP.** stdio is zero-config with no network attack surface, but runs one server process per client and cannot be shared; Streamable HTTP is shareable and remote, at the cost of auth, TLS and deployment.","**Derived schemas vs expressiveness.** FastMCP derives schemas from type hints so they never drift, but anything hints cannot express (cross-field rules) must be enforced in the function; drop to the low-level server for dynamic tools.","**Precise tools vs context cost.** Many small, focused tools give the model precise choices, but every schema occupies context on every turn; past a few dozen tools, selection accuracy and token cost both suffer.","**Third-party servers vs trust.** Plugging in someone else's MCP server is fast, but its tool descriptions and results enter your agent's context unfiltered; treat them as untrusted input."],"pitfalls":["**`print()` in a stdio server.** stdout is the protocol channel. Symptom: the host reports parse errors or the server \"disconnects\" right after a log line. Fix: log to stderr or through `ctx`.","**Docstrings written for humans, not models.** The model picks tools from these descriptions. Symptom: the wrong tool chosen, or a destructive tool called without the intended precondition.","**Vague exceptions for expected failures.** FastMCP catches a tool's exception and returns it as an `isError` result carrying its message, so `raise Exception(\"failed\")` gives the model nothing to act on. Symptom: the same bad call retried in a loop. Fix: say what was wrong and what to do instead.","**Blocking calls inside async tools.** Sync database drivers or `time.sleep` stall the whole server. Symptom: every tool times out whenever one slow call is in flight. Fix: use an async driver and `await asyncio.sleep`, or push sync work to a thread with `anyio.to_thread.run_sync`.","**Stateful Streamable HTTP behind a round-robin load balancer.** Requests land on a process that never saw the session. Symptom: intermittent \"session not found\" or 4xx errors that vanish with one replica. Fix: sticky routing on the session ID header, or `stateless_http=True`.","**No authentication or origin checks on Streamable HTTP.** Symptom: discovering that anything that can reach the port can invoke your tools.","**Dropping `isError` in the agent loop.** Symptom: the model believes a failed call succeeded and reports a ticket that was never created. Fix: pass the error text back as the tool result."],"when":"- **Use when:** tools or data must work across several agent hosts (IDE assistants, desktop chat apps,\n  your own agents), or a customer's platform already speaks MCP.\n- **Transport choice:** stdio for local, single-user tools; Streamable HTTP for shared services behind\n  proper auth.\n- **Avoid when:** one application calls the function in-process; do not wrap every internal function as\n  an MCP tool.\n- **Measure:** tool-call error rate, latency per tool, tokens spent on tool definitions, and how often the\n  model picks the intended tool.\n- **Go deeper / interviews:** the agents module's MCP lessons cover protocol design, the security model and\n  when MCP is the right boundary; expect to explain the lifecycle and why stdout matters on stdio.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes FastMCP tools with precise type hints and model-facing docstrings, logs through the context or stderr, and tests servers with a real client session.","architect":"Decides which capabilities become shared MCP servers versus in-process functions, which transport each uses, and where authentication and authorization are enforced.","fde":"Wraps a customer's internal APIs as an MCP server so their existing assistant hosts can use them without bespoke integrations per tool."},"check":{"q":"A stdio MCP server works when you run it by hand but the host shows \"connection closed\" as soon\nas one particular tool is called. What do you suspect, and how do you confirm it?","a":"On stdio, stdout carries the JSON-RPC stream, so anything else written there, a `print`, a\nlibrary's progress bar, a warning sent to stdout, corrupts the framing and the host drops the session.\nRunning by hand hides this because you are not parsing the stream. Confirm by checking the host's MCP\nlogs for a parse error, grepping that tool's code path for prints, or running it under a real\n`ClientSession`. Fix by logging to stderr or through `ctx` logging, and redirecting noisy libraries.\nA second suspect is the process itself dying in that code path (a native-extension crash, `sys.exit`,\nan out-of-memory kill), which the server's stderr or exit code would show; an ordinary Python exception\ninside a FastMCP tool does not do this, because it is returned to the client as an `isError` result."},"quiz":[{"q":"How does FastMCP produce a tool's input schema?","options":["You must write the JSON Schema by hand in the decorator","It builds a Pydantic model from the function's type hints and emits that model's JSON Schema","The host infers the schema by calling the tool with sample arguments","It parses the docstring for parameter types"],"answer":1,"explanation":"FastMCP inspects the signature and type hints, so the schema cannot drift from the code. The docstring supplies the description the model reads, not the types."},{"q":"Why should a stdio MCP server never write logs to stdout?","options":["stdout is buffered and logs would arrive out of order","The protocol forbids any logging during a session","stdout carries the JSON-RPC messages, so extra output corrupts the stream the host parses","Hosts discard stdout, so the logs would be lost"],"answer":2,"explanation":"On stdio the two processes talk over stdin and stdout. Logging belongs on stderr or in protocol log notifications through the context; stdout output breaks message framing."},{"q":"When is Streamable HTTP the better transport than stdio?","options":["When the tool runs on the same laptop as a single desktop host","When you want to avoid writing any authentication code","When the server must print debugging output freely","When one server should be shared by many clients or run remotely as a service"],"answer":3,"explanation":"stdio spawns a private subprocess per client, which is ideal locally. A shared or remote server needs HTTP, which also brings authentication and network security into scope."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/httpx","sources":["Official docs: HTTPX"],"why":"- **`requests` is synchronous.** It defined the ergonomic Python HTTP API, but an AI service fanning out\n  to an LLM, a vector store and two tools per request spends nearly all its time waiting on the network.\n  Inside an async framework each blocking wait stalls the event loop, so the server handles one request\n  at a time.\n- **The workarounds fall short.** Threads scale poorly to thousands of concurrent streams. `aiohttp` is\n  async-only, with a different API, so code and tests cannot be shared with sync scripts.\n- **LLM responses stream for many seconds.** They arrive as server-sent events, so the client must expose\n  bytes or lines incrementally rather than buffer the body.\n- **LLM requests are expensive and long.** Timeouts, connection reuse and pool limits stop being defaults\n  you ignore and become things you must set deliberately.\n- **httpx gives one API for both.** `httpx.Client` and `httpx.AsyncClient` have identical methods,\n  streaming, explicit fine-grained timeouts and a transport layer you can swap for a mock in tests.","how":"### Clients own connection pools\n- **`httpx.get(...)` is a convenience** that opens and discards a connection.\n- **A `Client`/`AsyncClient` keeps a pool of keep-alive connections,** so repeated calls to the same host\n  skip TCP and TLS handshakes, often the largest part of a short request's latency, and an agent makes\n  many short tool calls.\n- **So create one client per process (or per upstream) and reuse it,** closing it on shutdown with a\n  context manager or `aclose()`.\n- **Pool size:** `httpx.Limits(max_connections=..., max_keepalive_connections=...)`, defaults 100 and 20.\n  When the pool is full, further requests wait for a free connection, and that wait has its own timeout.\n- **The pool is your concurrency limit to that upstream.** By Little's law, sustainable throughput is\n  about `max_connections ÷ average request duration`: 50 connections holding 10-second streams carry about\n  5 requests per second, whatever the CPU could do.\n\n### Timeouts are per phase\n| Phase | Bounds | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| **connect** | Establishing the socket | 5 seconds |\n| **read** | Waiting for the next chunk of data | 5 seconds |\n| **write** | Sending a chunk | 5 seconds |\n| **pool** | Waiting for a free connection | 5 seconds |\n\n- **A read timeout bounds the gap *between* chunks, not the total duration.** All four live in\n  `httpx.Timeout`.\n- **Steady streams never trip it.** A completion sending a token every 100 ms passes a 5 s read timeout\n  even if it runs for ten minutes.\n- **Slow first bytes trip it at once.** A reasoning model that thinks for 30 s before its first byte fails\n  immediately.\n- **For a total deadline,** wrap the call in `asyncio.timeout(...)` (or anyio's equivalent). In an\n  agent, give each tool call its own deadline and the whole run a larger one.\n\n### Parallel tool calls\n- **Models can request several tool calls in one turn.** Run them concurrently with `asyncio.gather`\n  over one shared `AsyncClient`, so the step costs the slowest call, not the sum.\n- **Each call needs its own deadline and error capture,** so one hung tool does not stall the step and\n  one failure becomes a tool result the model can read instead of an exception that kills the run.\n\n### Streaming\n1. `client.stream(\"POST\", url, json=...)` is a context manager that returns once headers arrive.\n2. Iterate `aiter_bytes()`, `aiter_text()` or `aiter_lines()`.\n3. Leaving the context closes the response and returns the connection to the pool, *which is why* the\n   context-manager form matters: a streamed response never closed keeps its connection checked out.\n\n### Behaviour that differs from requests\n- **Redirects are not followed by default** (`follow_redirects=True` to enable).\n- **Timeouts are on by default;** `requests` waits forever unless told otherwise.\n- **Error types:** `raise_for_status()` raises `httpx.HTTPStatusError`; network-level failures are\n  `httpx.RequestError` subclasses such as `ConnectError`, `ReadTimeout` and `PoolTimeout`.\n\n### Transports\nEvery client sends through a transport.\n\n| Transport | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `httpx.HTTPTransport(retries=n)` (`AsyncHTTPTransport` for an `AsyncClient`) | Retries only failures to establish a connection, not HTTP 429 or 5xx |\n| `httpx.MockTransport(handler)` | Routes requests to a Python function: fast, deterministic agent tests, no network |\n| `httpx.ASGITransport(app=app)` | Sends requests straight into an ASGI app such as FastAPI, in-process |\n\n- **Application retries with backoff remain your job** (Tenacity, or the SDK's built-in retries).\n- **HTTP/2** needs the optional extra (`httpx[http2]`) and `http2=True`, and multiplexes many requests over\n  one connection.\n\n### Hooks and SDK integration\n- **`event_hooks={\"request\": [...], \"response\": [...]}`** runs your functions on every request and\n  response: the usual place for trace headers, latency per upstream or payload redaction, *because* it\n  covers every call through the client, including calls an SDK makes for you.\n- **Provider SDKs accept a pre-built client:** `OpenAI(http_client=httpx.Client(...))`,\n  `AsyncOpenAI(http_client=httpx.AsyncClient(...))`, so you control proxies, certificates, limits and hooks.\n- **The SDK still applies its own timeout and retry settings on top,** so set those on the SDK rather than\n  assuming httpx's 5 s default is in force.","example":"### Agent scenario: parallel tool calls with deadlines\nA tool executor for one agent step. It runs every tool call the model requested concurrently on a shared\nclient, gives each a deadline, and turns failures into text the model can read.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, httpx\n\nhttp = httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"https://tools.internal\",\n                         timeout=httpx.Timeout(connect=2.0, read=10.0, write=5.0, pool=1.0),\n                         limits=httpx.Limits(max_connections=50, max_keepalive_connections=20))\n\nasync def call_tool(name: str, args: dict, deadline_s: float = 8.0) -> str:\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(deadline_s):          # total budget for this tool call\n            r = await http.post(f\"/tools/{name}\", json=args)\n            r.raise_for_status()\n            return r.text\n    except TimeoutError:\n        return f\"ERROR: {name} took longer than {deadline_s}s; try a narrower query\"\n    except httpx.HTTPStatusError as e:\n        return f\"ERROR: {name} returned {e.response.status_code}: {e.response.text[:200]}\"\n    except httpx.RequestError as e:\n        return f\"ERROR: {name} unreachable ({type(e).__name__}); try another tool\"\n\nasync def run_step(tool_calls: list[tuple[str, dict]]) -> list[str]:\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(call_tool(n, a) for n, a in tool_calls))   # cost = slowest call\n```\n\n- **Errors become tool results,** so the model can retry with different arguments or pick another tool\n  instead of the run crashing.\n- **The shared client** keeps connections warm across all steps of all runs in this process.\n\n### Worked scenario: one agent step, sequential versus parallel\nIllustrative latencies for a step where the model asked for three tools.\n\n| Tool | Latency |\n|---|---|\n| `search_docs` | 1.2 s |\n| `get_customer` | 0.8 s |\n| `get_invoices` | 2.0 s |\n| Sequential step | 1.2 + 0.8 + 2.0 = 4.0 s |\n| Parallel step with `gather` | max = 2.0 s |\n| Parallel, `get_invoices` hangs, no deadline | until the read timeout, or forever on a trickle |\n| Parallel, 8 s deadline per call | at most 8 s, with an error result the model can act on |\n\n### Streaming with limits, a deadline and a mock\nStreaming an OpenAI-compatible completion with deliberate limits, a total deadline and a mock for tests.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json, httpx\n\nTIMEOUT = httpx.Timeout(connect=5.0, read=60.0, write=10.0, pool=2.0)  # slow first token is normal\nLIMITS = httpx.Limits(max_connections=50, max_keepalive_connections=20)\n\nasync def stream_chat(client: httpx.AsyncClient, prompt: str) -> str:\n    body = {\"model\": \"my-model\", \"stream\": True,\n            \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]}\n    parts = []\n    async with asyncio.timeout(120):                      # total deadline (Python 3.11+)\n        async with client.stream(\"POST\", \"/v1/chat/completions\", json=body) as r:\n            r.raise_for_status()\n            async for line in r.aiter_lines():\n                if line.startswith(\"data: \") and line != \"data: [DONE]\":\n                    delta = json.loads(line[6:])[\"choices\"][0][\"delta\"]\n                    parts.append(delta.get(\"content\") or \"\")\n    return \"\".join(parts)\n\ndef fake(request: httpx.Request) -> httpx.Response:       # test double, no network\n    chunk = {\"choices\": [{\"delta\": {\"content\": \"hi\"}}]}\n    return httpx.Response(200, text=f\"data: {json.dumps(chunk)}\\n\\ndata: [DONE]\\n\\n\")\n\nasync def main():\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(base_url=\"https://llm.internal\", timeout=TIMEOUT, limits=LIMITS,\n                                 transport=httpx.MockTransport(fake)) as client:\n        print(await asyncio.gather(*(stream_chat(client, f\"q{i}\") for i in range(3))))\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\nDrop the `transport=` argument and the same code talks to a real server.\n\n### Worked scenario: a burst against the pool\n200 concurrent calls whose streams last about 8 s each hit the client above, for example 200 agent runs\nstarting together.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Get connections immediately | First 50 |\n| Wait in the pool | The other 150 |\n| Throughput | 50 ÷ 8 ≈ 6 requests per second |\n| Time to drain the burst | about 32 s |\n| Wait for the last request | about 24 s for a connection |\n| With `pool=2.0` | Every request still waiting after 2 s fails with `PoolTimeout`: roughly 150 of the 200 fail fast |\n\n- **This is a deliberate choice:** fail fast and shed load (return 503, let the caller retry) versus a long\n  pool timeout where users wait in line.\n- **If you want queueing,** put an explicit `asyncio.Semaphore` or job queue in front so the wait is\n  visible and bounded, rather than hiding it in the pool.\n\n### Production client for an SDK, with hooks\nOne shared client carries trace headers and logs for every call the SDK makes. Hooks on an\n`AsyncClient` must be async functions.\n\n```python\nimport logging, uuid, httpx\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"upstream\")\n\nasync def add_trace(request: httpx.Request) -> None:\n    request.headers[\"x-request-id\"] = uuid.uuid4().hex\n\nasync def log_response(response: httpx.Response) -> None:\n    req = response.request\n    log.info(\"%s %s -> %s\", req.method, req.url.host, response.status_code)\n\nhttp = httpx.AsyncClient(\n    timeout=httpx.Timeout(connect=5.0, read=60.0, write=10.0, pool=2.0),\n    limits=httpx.Limits(max_connections=50, max_keepalive_connections=20),\n    event_hooks={\"request\": [add_trace], \"response\": [log_response]},\n)\nllm = AsyncOpenAI(http_client=http, timeout=120.0, max_retries=2)   # SDK settings win on top\n```","patterns":["**One client per upstream, owned by the app lifespan.** Create the `AsyncClient` at startup (for example in a FastAPI lifespan), share it across agent runs, and `await client.aclose()` on shutdown. Monitor open sockets and TLS handshakes per second, which should stay low and flat.","**Separate pools for model streams and tool calls.** Give long LLM streams their own client and `Limits` sized by Little's law, so they cannot starve fast tool or vector-store calls. Monitor `PoolTimeout` count and pool wait time per client.","**Per-phase timeouts plus layered deadlines.** Set read to cover worst-case time to first token, keep connect short, wrap each tool call in `asyncio.timeout(...)`, and give the whole agent run a larger budget. Monitor time to first byte and total duration per upstream and per tool.","**Retry status codes in application code.** Retry 429 and 5xx with backoff (Tenacity, or the SDK's `max_retries`) and honour `Retry-After`; transport retries only cover connects. Retry only idempotent tool calls automatically. Monitor retries per request and rate-limit errors reaching users.","**Test agents with `MockTransport`, not patching.** Inject the transport into the same client factory used in production, so agent tests replay fixed model and tool responses through real timeout, header and streaming code paths with no network.","**Trace every upstream call.** Add a run or trace ID header in a request hook so tool and model calls from one agent run can be joined in your tracing tool."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** One agent step can stream a completion and then run three tool calls\n  in parallel. Whether that step takes 2 s or hangs for ten minutes is decided by the connection pool\n  and per-phase timeouts (httpx settings) plus the outer deadline you add with `asyncio.timeout`.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Inside provider SDKs (`AsyncOpenAI(http_client=...)`), in your\n  own tool functions that call internal APIs, in streaming from self-hosted model servers, and in tests\n  through `MockTransport`.\n- **What you control.** One shared `AsyncClient` per upstream, `httpx.Timeout` per phase,\n  `httpx.Limits` sized for your concurrency, a total deadline per tool call, and where retries live.\n- **What you can skip.** HTTP/2 internals and transport plumbing beyond the three transports below.\n  Know that a read timeout is not a deadline and that the pool is your concurrency limit.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Parallel tool calls.** An agent step fans out three tool calls through one shared `AsyncClient`,\n  with `httpx.Limits` capping concurrency and a deadline per call so one slow API cannot stall the step.\n- **Tuning provider SDK clients.** Passing a configured client as `AsyncOpenAI(http_client=...)` sets\n  pooling and per-phase timeouts for every model call the agent makes.\n- **Streaming from self-hosted models.** Agents read server-sent events from vLLM or similar servers\n  incrementally, showing tokens as they arrive and cancelling cleanly.\n- **Deterministic agent tests.** `MockTransport` returns canned tool and model responses, so agent loops\n  run in CI with no network and no spend.","mini_project":"**A resilient HTTP layer for an agent's tools (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make an agent's tool calls fast in parallel and safe when an upstream hangs or rate-limits.\n- **Steps:** create one shared `AsyncClient` with an `httpx.Timeout` per phase and `httpx.Limits` sized\n  for your concurrency; wrap each tool call in a total deadline; add application-level retries with\n  backoff for 429 and 5xx that respect Retry-After; test with `MockTransport` returning a slow response,\n  a 429 and a success.\n- **Done when:** three parallel tool calls finish in roughly the time of the slowest one, the hanging\n  call fails at its deadline instead of waiting on the read timeout, and the 429 case succeeds after a\n  backoff, all shown by passing tests."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"client.stream POST\"] --> P{\"Free connection in pool?\"}\n    P -->|\"no, wait longer than pool timeout\"| PT[\"PoolTimeout\"]\n    P -->|\"yes, or freed in time\"| K{\"Keep-alive connection open?\"}\n    K -->|\"no\"| C[\"TCP and TLS connect, bounded by connect timeout\"]\n    K -->|\"yes\"| W[\"Write request body, bounded by write timeout\"]\n    C --> W\n    W --> R[\"Read next chunk, read timeout resets per chunk\"]\n    R -->|\"more chunks\"| R\n    R --> X[\"Exit context: connection back to pool\"]\n    D[\"Outer asyncio.timeout deadline\"] -.->|\"cancels any phase\"| R","caption":"Each phase of one streamed model or tool request has its own timeout; only the outer `asyncio.timeout`\nbounds the whole call."},"interview":[{"q":"Why use httpx instead of requests in an AI service?","a":"- **It has both sync and async clients with one API.** `httpx.AsyncClient` does not block the event loop,\n  so an async service can fan out to an LLM, a vector store and tools concurrently.\n- **It streams incrementally,** which LLM server-sent events need, and timeouts are on by default.\n- **Provider SDKs are built on it,** so passing your own `http_client` controls proxies, limits and hooks\n  for every SDK call."},{"q":"An agent step sometimes hangs for minutes when one tool is slow. How do you design the tool executor?","a":"- **Run the step's calls concurrently** with `asyncio.gather` on one shared `AsyncClient`, so the step\n  costs the slowest call rather than the sum.\n- **Give each call an `asyncio.timeout` deadline,** because a read timeout resets per chunk and never caps\n  a trickling response.\n- **Return failures as tool results,** catching `TimeoutError`, `httpx.HTTPStatusError` and\n  `httpx.RequestError`, so the model can choose another action instead of the run dying."},{"q":"A service sees bursts of `PoolTimeout` while the LLM provider reports normal latency. What is happening?","a":"- **Your pool is the bottleneck, not the provider.** By Little's law, 50 connections holding 8-second\n  streams carry about 6 requests per second, so a burst of 200 queues in the pool.\n- **A short `pool` timeout turns that queue into errors:** with `pool=2.0`, roughly 150 of 200 fail fast.\n- **Fix:** give long streams their own client and `Limits`, size `max_connections` from throughput times\n  duration, and put an explicit semaphore or job queue in front if you want visible queueing."},{"q":"When is the default httpx timeout wrong for LLM calls, and what do you set instead?","a":"- **The 5 s read default fails slow first bytes,** such as long prompts or reasoning models that think\n  before emitting anything.\n- **Set phases separately:** for example `httpx.Timeout(connect=5.0, read=60.0, write=10.0, pool=2.0)`,\n  with read covering worst-case time to first token.\n- **Add an outer deadline** with `asyncio.timeout(...)`, because no httpx phase bounds total duration."},{"q":"Where do retries belong for a call that gets HTTP 429?","a":"- **Not in the transport.** `HTTPTransport(retries=n)` retries only failed connects.\n- **In application code or the SDK:** Tenacity or the SDK's `max_retries`, with backoff and respect for\n  `Retry-After`.\n- **Watch the interaction:** the SDK's own timeout and retry settings apply on top of your httpx client."},{"q":"How do you test an agent that calls an LLM and tools over HTTP without the network?","a":"- **Inject `httpx.MockTransport(handler)`** into the same client factory used in production, so real\n  timeout, header and streaming code paths run while the handler replays fixed model and tool replies.\n- **For your own FastAPI app,** `httpx.ASGITransport(app=app)` sends requests straight into it in-process.\n- **This beats patching,** because tests exercise the actual client configuration rather than a stub."}],"tradeoffs":["**One API for sync and async vs async discipline.** Scripts, services and tests share code, but async only helps if every other call on the path is async too; one blocking call still stalls the loop and every agent run sharing it.","**Shared long-lived client vs lifecycle management.** Pooling lowers latency, at the cost of creating it at startup, closing it at shutdown, and not sharing it across event loops or forked processes.","**Per-phase timeouts vs a total budget.** They allow slow first tokens while failing fast on dead hosts, but never express a total budget; long streams and tool calls need an outer deadline as well.","**Parallel tool calls vs upstream load.** Running a step's calls concurrently cuts latency to the slowest call, but many agents doing it at once multiply load on shared tools; cap with `Limits` or a semaphore.","**HTTP/2 multiplexing vs load concentration.** It helps with many small calls to one host, but adds a dependency and can concentrate load on one backend behind a connection-level load balancer."],"pitfalls":["**Sizing `max_connections` for short calls, then routing long streams through it.** With the same short pool timeout, the symptom is bursts of `PoolTimeout` during spikes while the upstream reports normal latency. Fix: a separate client for streams, sized by Little's law.","**A new `AsyncClient` per request or per tool call.** Every call repeats the TLS handshake. Symptom: higher latency plus \"too many open files\" or ephemeral-port exhaustion under load. Fix: one shared client per upstream.","**Keeping the 5 s default read timeout for LLM calls.** Long prompts or reasoning models delay the first byte. Symptom: `ReadTimeout` only on big inputs (late agent steps with long transcripts) while small ones succeed.","**Assuming the read timeout caps total duration.** A slowly trickling stream runs far past your budget. Symptom: requests or agent steps hanging for minutes without any timeout firing.","**Expecting `HTTPTransport(retries=3)` to retry 429 or 503.** It only retries connection failures. Symptom: rate-limit errors surfacing straight to users.","**Abandoning a `client.send(request, stream=True)` response without `aclose()`.** For example on an early `break` or exception, connections leak. Symptom: `PoolTimeout` after a period of normal traffic. Fix: prefer `async with client.stream(...)`, or close in a `finally`."],"when":"- **Use when:** Python code makes HTTP calls in an AI system: direct calls to model servers, tool APIs,\n  webhooks, and as the `http_client` you pass to provider SDKs to control proxies, timeouts and limits.\n- **Sync or async:** `AsyncClient` inside async frameworks and agents; the sync client is fine for scripts\n  and batch jobs. Prefer `requests` only in legacy synchronous code where nothing else changes.\n- **Measure:** p50/p99 latency per upstream and per tool, time to first byte, timeout counts per phase,\n  and pool wait time. Pool wait rising while upstream latency is flat means your connection limit, not\n  the provider, is the bottleneck.\n- **Interviews:** expect questions on why a read timeout is not a deadline and why clients must be shared.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Creates one shared `AsyncClient` per upstream with explicit timeouts and limits, streams with context managers, and tests with `MockTransport` instead of patching.","architect":"Sets the timeout, retry and concurrency budgets for each dependency so that one slow model provider cannot exhaust connections for the whole service.","fde":"Configures proxies, custom CA bundles and mutual TLS through the client so SDK traffic works inside a customer's locked-down corporate network."},"check":{"q":"Your service streams completions through httpx with `timeout=30`. Short prompts work; 100k-token\nprompts fail with `ReadTimeout`, and occasionally a request hangs for ten minutes. Explain both.","a":"A single number sets every phase, so read is 30 s between chunks. With a 100k-token prompt the\nserver's prefill, plus any queueing on a busy server, can take longer than 30 s before the first byte,\nso the read timeout fires. The ten-minute\nhang is the opposite case: once tokens flow, each chunk resets the read timer, so a stream that keeps\ntrickling never trips it. Fix both by raising the read timeout to cover worst-case time to first token,\nand wrapping the whole call in `asyncio.timeout` for a total deadline, plus metrics on time to first\nbyte and total duration per request."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does httpx's read timeout actually bound?","options":["The total time from sending the request to receiving the full response","The maximum wait for the next chunk of data from the server","Only the time to establish the TCP and TLS connection","The time a request waits for a free pooled connection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Read timeouts reset whenever data arrives, so a slow but steady stream never trips them. Total deadlines need an outer timeout; connect and pool waits are separate phases."},{"q":"Why should an async service create one shared AsyncClient rather than one per request?","options":["AsyncClient objects cannot be garbage collected","The shared client's pool reuses keep-alive connections, avoiding repeated handshakes and socket exhaustion","Per-request clients cannot stream responses","httpx raises an error if more than one client exists per process"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Connection pooling lives in the client. A new client per call pays TCP and TLS setup every time and can run out of file descriptors under load, while streaming works either way."},{"q":"A team sets HTTPTransport(retries=3) and still sees 429 errors reach users. Why?","options":["The retries parameter only applies to synchronous clients","429 responses are retried, but without backoff they always fail again","Transport retries cover connection failures only, not HTTP status codes like 429","Retries are disabled when HTTP/2 is enabled"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The transport retries failed connects, not error responses. Retrying on 429 or 5xx with backoff and respect for Retry-After belongs in application code or the provider SDK."}]},{"key":"python-for-ai/jupyter","sources":["Official docs: Project Jupyter"],"why":"- **Much AI work is exploratory.** Look at 50 failing eval cases, try three prompt variants, plot score\n  distributions, inspect what the retriever returned or which tool an agent called.\n- **Scripts make every question start from zero.** Reloading a model, re-reading a dataset, re-calling an\n  API you pay for: seconds or minutes of setup dominate a two-second question, and output scrolls away in\n  a terminal.\n- **A plain REPL keeps state but not much else.** It loses history and cannot show tables, images or\n  charts well.\n- **Jupyter splits a stateful kernel from a front end.** Load the model or dataframe once, then ask\n  questions in small cells, each with output rendered inline and saved next to the code. The result\n  doubles as a record others can read.\n- **It trades some reproducibility for iteration speed.** Scripts with print statements are reproducible\n  but slow to iterate; knowing exactly what the notebook gave up is the skill.","how":"### Architecture\n| Piece | What it is | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Kernel** | A separate process (for Python, `ipykernel`, built on IPython) that executes code and holds all variables | Separate *because* the front end can crash, reconnect or be another program without losing state; other languages plug in the same way |\n| **Messaging protocol** | ZeroMQ sockets with a documented protocol: execute requests, streamed output, rich display data, interrupts | Why JupyterLab, the classic Notebook interface, VS Code and hosted services can all drive the same kernel |\n| **Server** | Jupyter Server | Manages kernels, files and the browser-facing API |\n| **Document** | An `.ipynb` is JSON: cells, each with source, an execution count and outputs (text, HTML, base64 images) | Outputs live *in* the file, so it reads without re-running, and can also carry data and secrets you did not mean to share |\n\n### State is the execution history, not the file\n- **The kernel's state is whatever cells you ran, in the order you ran them,** including cells since\n  edited or deleted.\n- **The file shows each cell's current text,** which may not be the code that produced the variables in\n  memory. For agents that includes the system prompt and tool list an agent object was built with.\n- **The visible clue:** execution counts (`[7]`, `[3]`) out of order.\n- **The only proof a notebook works:** **Restart Kernel and Run All** producing the same results.\n\n### Driving async agents from cells\n- **Top-level `await` works** *because* IPython kernels already run an event loop, so\n  `await agent.run(task)` or an async SDK call works directly in a cell.\n- **The same fact is why `asyncio.run()` fails** in a notebook with \"cannot be called from a running\n  event loop\". Agent code copied from a script needs its `asyncio.run(main())` replaced by `await main()`.\n- **Clients and sessions live as long as the kernel.** An HTTP client or MCP session opened in a cell\n  stays open until you close it or restart, which is convenient for exploration and a leak if forgotten.\n\n### Kernels, environments and the install trap\n- **The kernelspec picks the Python.** It is a small JSON file naming the interpreter to launch, not the\n  shell you started Jupyter from.\n- **Register a project environment as a kernel:** `python -m ipykernel install --user --name myenv`.\n- **`!pip` vs `%pip`.** `!pip` runs whatever `pip` is first on the server's `PATH`, which may belong to\n  another environment; `%pip` installs with the kernel's own interpreter, which is what you usually want.\n\n### Memory lives as long as the kernel\n- **Everything the kernel references survives until restart.**\n- **Output history holds references too.** IPython keeps every displayed result (`Out`, `_`, `__`), so a\n  large DataFrame or tensor you displayed stays referenced even after you `del` the variable.\n- **Re-running a \"load model\" cell makes a second copy** before the first can be freed. On a GPU that is\n  the classic notebook out-of-memory error: the first load fits, the re-run does not, and only a kernel\n  restart reliably gives the memory back.\n\n### Useful machinery\n- **Magics:** `%timeit` for micro-benchmarks; `%autoreload` to pick up edits to imported modules, such as\n  the agent package you are changing.\n- **Headless execution:** `jupyter nbconvert --execute` runs a notebook top to bottom; papermill injects\n  parameters and runs notebooks as batch jobs, which some teams use for scheduled reports.\n- **Version control helpers:** nbstripout removes outputs before commit; Jupytext pairs a notebook with a\n  plain `.py` file so diffs and reviews are readable.","example":"### Agent scenario: error analysis over agent traces\nIllustrative: 300 traces from an eval run of a support agent, one row per step, exported as JSONL. The\nnotebook answers \"where do failing runs go wrong?\" in a few cells.\n\n```python\n%load_ext autoreload\n%autoreload 2\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom agent_analysis.labels import first_bad_step     # logic lives in a tested module\n\nsteps = pd.read_json(\"runs/agent-eval-steps.jsonl\", lines=True)   # run_id, step, tool, is_error, passed\nruns = steps.groupby(\"run_id\").agg(passed=(\"passed\", \"first\"), n_steps=(\"step\", \"max\"),\n                                   tool_errors=(\"is_error\", \"sum\"))\nfailed = runs[~runs.passed]\nprint(len(failed), \"failed runs;\", failed.n_steps.median(), \"median steps\")\n\nbad = steps[steps.run_id.isin(failed.index)].groupby(\"run_id\").apply(first_bad_step)\nbad.tool.value_counts().head(5)                      # which tool the first wrong step used\n\nanswer = await support_agent.run(\"Is ticket 42 still open?\")   # top-level await: re-run one case live\n```\n\n| Question | Illustrative answer |\n|---|---|\n| Failed runs | 48 of 300 |\n| Failed runs with at least one tool error | 31 |\n| First bad step uses `search_docs` | 22 |\n| Next action | Rewrite the `search_docs` description and its error message, then re-run the eval set as a script |\n\n### Error analysis on 2,000 RAG answers, and the stale-cell trap\nIllustrative numbers from one eval run of a RAG step.\n\n| Cell | Does | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Loads eval results JSONL into pandas and, once, the embedding model | about 30 seconds |\n| 2 | Filters the 140 rows the judge scored as failures | seconds |\n| 3 | Shows ten of them: question, retrieved chunks, answer side by side | seconds |\n| 4 | Many failures involve tables, so adds a `has_table` flag by regex over the chunks; stores a cross-tab of document type against `has_table` for failing rows in `by_type` | seconds |\n| 5 | Plots `by_type` | seconds |\n\nEach question takes seconds because the model and data stay in memory. Then the trap:\n1. **You edit cell 2's filter** to include \"partial\" scores and re-run cells 2 and 5, but not cell 4.\n2. **`by_type` still holds counts from the old subset,** so the chart is silently wrong; nothing errors.\n3. **The file shows the new filter,** so a reviewer cannot tell.\n4. **Before sharing, you Restart and Run All:** cell 4 now runs on the new subset and the chart changes.\n   That habit makes the notebook trustworthy.\n5. **Finally, the `has_table` heuristic moves into `analysis/features.py` with a test,** and the notebook\n   imports it, so the logic outlives the exploration.\n\n```python\n%load_ext autoreload\n%autoreload 2\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom analysis.features import has_table      # logic lives in a module, not a cell\n\ndf = pd.read_json(\"runs/eval-2025-03.jsonl\", lines=True)\nfails = df[df.score < 3].assign(has_table=lambda d: d.chunks.map(has_table))\npd.crosstab(fails.doc_type, fails.has_table)\n```\n\n### CI check: was this report notebook run top to bottom?\nA pure-Python check for notebooks committed with outputs (curated reports such as a weekly agent eval\nsummary). Counts of 1..n in order are what Restart and Run All produces; anything else means hidden state.\n\n```python\nimport json, sys\n\ndef check(path: str) -> list[str]:\n    nb = json.load(open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    code = [c for c in nb[\"cells\"]                  # skip empty cells: they never get a count\n            if c[\"cell_type\"] == \"code\" and \"\".join(c[\"source\"]).strip()]\n    counts = [c.get(\"execution_count\") for c in code]\n    ran = [n for n in counts if n is not None]\n    problems = []\n    if ran != list(range(1, len(ran) + 1)):\n        problems.append(f\"execution counts {ran} are not 1..{len(ran)} in order\")\n    if len(ran) < len(counts):\n        problems.append(f\"{len(counts) - len(ran)} code cell(s) never ran\")\n    return problems\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    results = {p: check(p) for p in sys.argv[1:]}\n    for p, problems in results.items():\n        for msg in problems:\n            print(f\"{p}: {msg}\")\n    sys.exit(1 if any(results.values()) else 0)\n```\n\nA notebook whose counts read `[1, 7, 3]` fails with a clear message; one run cleanly passes. Do not run\nit on output-stripped notebooks, where every count is empty.","patterns":["**Explore traces in notebooks, decide with scripts.** Use a notebook to find failure patterns in agent traces, then re-run the comparison through the eval harness as a script before changing a prompt or tool. Track that every shipped agent change links to a reproducible eval run, not a notebook cell.","**Strip outputs at commit time.** Run `nbstripout --install` in the repo so a git filter removes outputs and execution counts from exploratory notebooks. Monitor with a secret scanner over the repository, since saved outputs (printed traces, tool results, API responses) are where keys and customer records leak.","**Pair notebooks with text files for review.** Use Jupytext paired formats (for example ipynb plus a percent-format `.py`) so pull requests diff readable code. Review the `.py`; regenerate the notebook from it.","**Re-execute reports headlessly before publishing.** Run `jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute` (or papermill with `-p` parameters for scheduled runs) in CI, so published numbers come from a clean kernel. Monitor run duration and failures like any batch job.","**Promote logic into a tested package.** Once a helper or tool is reused, move it to a module with tests and import it with `%autoreload 2` during exploration. Track how much logic lives only in cells; it should trend to zero for anything scheduled or served.","**One registered kernel per project environment.** Register each project's virtual environment with `python -m ipykernel install --user --name <project>` and install only with `%pip`. Watch for `ModuleNotFoundError` right after an install, the sign of a mismatched interpreter."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Improving an agent is mostly reading failures: which step went wrong,\n  which tool returned what, where the model looped. A notebook keeps hundreds of traces in memory so\n  each question about them takes seconds instead of a re-run.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Error analysis over exported traces and eval results, prompt and\n  tool-description iteration, first prototypes of a tool loop, and report notebooks re-executed in CI.\n- **What you control.** The kernel and environment, whether state is rebuilt (Restart and Run All),\n  which logic moves into tested modules, and what outputs get committed.\n- **What you can skip.** The messaging protocol and server internals. Know that state is execution\n  history, that top-level `await` works because the kernel already runs an event loop, and that saved\n  outputs can leak keys and customer data.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agent error analysis.** Load a few hundred exported traces into a dataframe once, then slice by\n  failing step, tool and error type in seconds per question.\n- **Prompt and tool-description iteration.** Try several variants of a system prompt or tool docstring\n  against the same 20 hard cases and compare outputs side by side.\n- **First tool-loop prototypes.** Use top-level `await` to call an async model client and tools cell by\n  cell before moving the loop into a tested module.\n- **Eval reports in CI.** A report notebook re-executed from a clean kernel turns eval results into\n  charts and tables reviewers can read.","mini_project":"**Error-analysis notebook over agent traces (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find the top failure modes of an agent from its traces, reproducibly.\n- **Steps:** export 50-200 agent runs as JSON lines (or generate them from a toy agent); load them into\n  a dataframe; tag each failed run by step and cause (wrong tool, bad arguments, loop, tool error);\n  chart counts by cause; move the loading and tagging code into a small module the notebook imports;\n  strip outputs before commit.\n- **Done when:** Restart and Run All reproduces the same table and chart, the top two failure modes each\n  have one concrete fix proposed, and the committed notebook holds no outputs or secrets."},"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Front end: JupyterLab, Notebook, VS Code\"] -->|\"execute request\"| S[\"Jupyter Server\"]\n    S -->|\"ZeroMQ messages\"| K[\"Kernel process: ipykernel\"]\n    K --> M[(\"Variables, traces and Out history in memory\")]\n    K -->|\"streamed output and display data\"| F\n    F -->|\"save\"| N[(\"ipynb JSON: cell source, counts, outputs\")]\n    M -.->|\"may differ after edits or out-of-order runs\"| N\n    R[\"Restart Kernel and Run All\"] -->|\"rebuilds state from cells in order\"| M","caption":"The kernel's memory comes from the execution history, while the `.ipynb` stores only current cell text\nand saved outputs; Restart and Run All is what makes the two agree before you trust an agent analysis."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a Jupyter kernel, and why is it a separate process from the front end?","a":"- **The kernel executes code and holds all variables;** for Python it is `ipykernel`, built on IPython.\n- **It is separate so state survives the front end,** which can crash, reconnect or be a different program\n  such as JupyterLab, VS Code or a hosted service, all speaking the same ZeroMQ messaging protocol.\n- **The cost is hidden state:** memory reflects what ran, not what the file shows."},{"q":"How would you use a notebook to find why an agent fails 16% of eval runs?","a":"- **Load traces once, one row per step,** with run ID, step number, tool, error flag and pass or fail, so\n  each question is a cheap pandas query over data in memory.\n- **Find the first bad step per failed run** and count by tool, which usually points at one tool's\n  description, arguments or error message.\n- **Then leave the notebook:** fix the tool, re-run the full eval set through the harness as a script, and\n  compare pass rates there, since notebook state cannot be trusted as evidence."},{"q":"Your agent works as a script but `asyncio.run(main())` fails in a notebook cell. Why, and what do you do?","a":"- **The kernel already runs an event loop,** and `asyncio.run()` refuses to start one inside a running loop.\n- **Use top-level `await main()`** in the cell instead; IPython supports it for exactly this reason.\n- **Avoid nested-loop patches,** which hide the problem, and close long-lived clients or sessions you\n  opened in cells before you restart."},{"q":"A notebook hits CUDA out-of-memory on a cell that worked an hour ago. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Suspect re-run loading cells.** Re-running \"load model\" creates a second copy before the first can be\n  freed.\n- **Suspect output history.** IPython keeps displayed results in `Out`, `_` and `__`, so a displayed tensor\n  stays referenced after `del`.\n- **Fix:** restart the kernel, which reliably frees the memory, load the model once in an early cell, and\n  avoid displaying large tensors."},{"q":"When would you not use a notebook for agent work?","a":"- **For anything scheduled, reused or served,** such as the agent loop itself or its eval harness,\n  because notebooks are hard to test, type-check and monitor; that logic belongs in a package the\n  notebook imports.\n- **For results others will rely on, unless re-run cleanly,** because out-of-order state can produce\n  numbers the visible code does not explain, such as a prompt that \"wins\" against a stale variant.\n- **Environment drift is a smaller version of the same risk:** `!pip` can install into another\n  interpreter (a `pip install` that succeeds, then `ModuleNotFoundError`), so use `%pip` and a registered\n  project kernel."},{"q":"How do you keep notebooks safe and reviewable in a team repository?","a":"- **Strip outputs on commit** with `nbstripout --install`, because saved outputs bloat diffs and can carry\n  keys or customer records.\n- **Pair with Jupytext** so reviews diff a readable `.py` file.\n- **Re-execute report notebooks in CI** with `jupyter nbconvert --execute` or papermill, so published numbers\n  come from a clean kernel."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast iteration vs hidden state.** Persistent kernel state makes iteration fast when setup is expensive, but results may not reproduce; the balance flips the moment the output informs a decision others rely on, such as shipping a new agent prompt.","**Readable saved outputs vs bloat and leaks.** Outputs saved in the file make notebooks readable without re-running, yet bloat diffs and can leak customer data or keys; strip them for code review, keep them only for curated reports.","**Exploration vs modular code.** Notebooks are excellent for exploration and teaching, but poor for modular, tested code; once logic is reused or scheduled, it belongs in a package the notebook imports.","**Cheap scheduled jobs vs operability.** Parameterized notebook execution turns analyses into repeatable jobs cheaply, but a scheduled notebook is harder to test, type-check and monitor than a script with the same logic."],"pitfalls":["**Trusting out-of-order execution.** Symptom: a colleague running the notebook top to bottom gets different numbers, or a `NameError` for a variable you deleted.","**Comparing agent variants built in different cells at different times.** Symptom: a prompt \"wins\" in the notebook but not in the eval harness, because one variant kept an old tool list or client. Fix: rebuild both agents in one cell after Restart and Run All, then confirm in a script.","**Committing outputs with API keys, tokens or customer records.** Symptom: secrets or personal data in the repository history and in rendered previews.","**Calling `asyncio.run()` in a cell.** It raises because the kernel's loop is already running. The usual hack, patching nested loops, hides the problem; use top-level `await` instead.","**Re-running a model-loading cell, or displaying large tensors, in a long session.** Symptom: CUDA out-of-memory on a cell that worked an hour ago, cleared only by restarting the kernel.","**Installing with `!pip` into a different interpreter than the kernel's.** Symptom: a successful install followed by `ModuleNotFoundError` in the next cell.","**Shipping a notebook as the production service.** Symptom: no tests, no error handling, and an outage when someone runs cells in a new order."],"when":"- **Use when:** exploring and communicating: reading agent traces, data inspection, error analysis on\n  eval results, prompt and tool-description iteration, quick model comparisons, and demos for\n  stakeholders.\n- **Move code out when:** it is reused, scheduled or needs tests, which includes every agent loop that\n  will serve users.\n- **Before trusting numbers:** treat nothing as final until Restart and Run All reproduces it, and pin the\n  environment so others can.\n- **Pair with tracking:** for tracking runs and reproducible evaluations, see the LLMOps lessons;\n  notebooks complement experiment tracking rather than replace it.\n- **Interviews:** expect to explain hidden state and how you keep notebook analysis honest.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Explores data and failure cases in notebooks, then promotes stable logic into tested modules the notebook imports, with outputs stripped before commit.","architect":"Sets the boundary: notebooks for exploration and reports, packages and pipelines for anything scheduled or served, plus rules on data allowed in saved outputs.","fde":"Uses a notebook to walk a customer through their own data and model results live, then hands over a cleaned, re-run version as the record of findings."},"check":{"q":"A colleague's notebook shows a 12-point accuracy improvement from a new prompt. What would you\ncheck before believing it, and why is a notebook specifically prone to this kind of error?","a":"I would restart the kernel and run all cells in order, because the kernel's state reflects the\nexecution history, not the file: an edited cell, a variable left over from an earlier experiment or a\nfilter applied to only one arm can produce a difference that the visible code does not explain. I would\nalso check that both prompts ran on the same rows, with the same model and settings, that the evaluation\nfunction is identical for both, and that the sample is large enough for a 12-point gap to be meaningful.\nThen I would move the comparison into a script or eval harness so it can be re-run."},"quiz":[{"q":"What determines the values of variables in a running Jupyter kernel?","options":["The current text of the cells, read top to bottom","The cells actually executed, in the order they were executed, including since-edited ones","The last saved version of the .ipynb file","Only the most recently executed cell"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The kernel is a live process that accumulates state from every execution. The file shows current cell text, which may differ from what produced the state, hence Restart and Run All."},{"q":"Why does asyncio.run() fail inside a standard IPython notebook cell?","options":["Notebooks do not support asynchronous code at all","The kernel uses threads instead of an event loop","The kernel already runs an event loop, and asyncio.run() refuses to start one inside a running loop","asyncio is not installed in the default kernel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"IPython kernels run their own event loop, which is also why top-level await works directly in a cell. asyncio.run() wants to create and own a new loop, so it raises."},{"q":"A team wants readable code reviews of notebooks without leaking data. Which practice best addresses both?","options":["Converting every notebook to PDF before committing","Committing notebooks with full outputs so reviewers can see results","Stripping outputs before commit and pairing notebooks with plain-text .py files","Storing notebooks only on a shared drive outside version control"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Outputs cause noisy diffs and can contain sensitive data. Stripping them, and pairing with a text representation such as Jupytext, gives clean diffs while keeping the notebook usable."}]}]});
